            },
            {
              q: "What passes through the Esophageal Hiatus?",
              a: "<b>At Level T10:</b><br>• Esophagus<br>• Vagal trunks (Anterior & Posterior)<br>• Esophageal branches of the left gastric vessels."
            },
            {
              q: "Define the Diaphragm and its role.",
              a: "The primary muscle of inspiration.<br>• <b>Contraction:</b> Moves downward (flattens), increasing thoracic volume and decreasing pressure, which draws air into the lungs."
            },
            {
              q: "What are the 3 major openings of the Diaphragm?",
              a: "<b>T8:</b> Caval opening (IVC, Right Phrenic nerve)<br><b>T10:</b> Esophageal hiatus (Esophagus, Vagus trunks)<br><b>T12:</b> Aortic hiatus (Aorta, Thoracic duct, Azygos vein)<br><i>Mnemonic: I 8 (ate) 10 eggs AT 12.</i>"
            },
            {
              q: "What is the nerve supply of the Diaphragm?",
              a: "<b>Motor & Central Sensory:</b> Phrenic Nerve (C3, C4, C5).<br><b>Peripheral Sensory:</b> Lower 6 Intercostal nerves.<br><i>Mnemonic: C3, 4, 5 keeps the diaphragm alive!</i>"
            },
            {
              q: "What is referred pain from the Diaphragm?",
              a: "Irritation of the central diaphragm (phrenic nerve) is often felt as <b>pain in the shoulder tip</b> (C3/C4 dermatome)."
            },
            {
              q: "Compare the two types of Hiatus Hernia.",
              a: "1. <b>Sliding (95%):</b> The gastroesophageal junction moves above the diaphragm. Common; causes GERD.<br>2. <b>Paraesophageal (Rolling):</b> The gastric fundus herniates next to the esophagus. GE junction stays in place. High risk of strangulation."
            },
            {
              q: "What is the Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve?",
              a: "A branch of the Vagus nerve (CN X).<br>• <b>Left:</b> Loops under the <b>Aortic Arch</b>.<br>• <b>Right:</b> Loops under the <b>Subclavian Artery</b>.<br>• <b>Clinical:</b> Injury (e.g., from surgery or apical lung tumor) causes <b>hoarseness</b>."
            },
            {
              q: "What are the Sympathetic Splanchnic Nerves?",
              a: "Nerves derived from the thoracic sympathetic trunk (T5–T12) that pass through the diaphragm to supply abdominal organs.<br>• <b>Greater:</b> T5–T9<br>• <b>Lesser:</b> T10–T11<br>• <b>Least:</b> T12"
            },
            {
              q: "What is the blood-air barrier in the lungs?",
              a: "The thin barrier across which gas exchange occurs.<br><b>Layers:</b> Alveolar epithelium (Type I pneumocyte) → Fused basement membranes → Capillary endothelium."
            },
            {
              q: "Compare Type I and Type II Pneumocytes.",
              a: "• <b>Type I:</b> Squamous; cover 95% of surface area; optimized for gas exchange.<br>• <b>Type II:</b> Cuboidal; produce <b>Surfactant</b>; act as stem cells to replace Type I cells."
            },
            {
              q: "What is Lung Compliance?",
              a: "The ease with which the lungs can be expanded.<br>• <b>Increased:</b> Emphysema (loss of elastic tissue).<br>• <b>Decreased:</b> Fibrosis, pulmonary edema, or lack of surfactant."
            },
            {
              q: "Define the V/Q Ratio.",
              a: "The ratio of alveolar ventilation (V) to pulmonary blood flow (Q).<br>• <b>Normal:</b> ~0.8<br>• <b>Mismatch:</b> 0 (Shunt — no ventilation) or ∞ (Dead space — no perfusion, e.g., Pulmonary Embolism)."
            },
            {
              q: "What is Boyle's Law and its relevance to breathing?",
              a: "<b>P1V1 = P2V2</b> (Pressure and Volume are inversely proportional).<br>During inspiration, increasing thoracic volume decreases pressure, causing air to flow into the lungs."
            },
            {
              q: "What is the embryological origin of the lungs?",
              a: "• Derived from <b>foregut endoderm</b> (Laryngotracheal diverticulum).<br>• Mesoderm gives rise to the cartilage, muscle, and connective tissue.<br>• Branching continues until age 8."
            },
            {
              q: "What are the 5 stages of lung development?",
              a: "1. <b>Embryonic</b> (4-7 wk): Lung buds form.<br>2. <b>Pseudoglandular</b> (7-16 wk): Conducting airways form.<br>3. <b>Canalicular</b> (16-26 wk): Respiratory bronchioles form; <b>respiration possible at end</b>.<br>4. <b>Saccular</b> (26 wk-birth): Terminal sacs form.<br>5. <b>Alveolar</b> (Birth-8 yr): Mature alveoli form."
            },
            {
              q: "What is Surfactant and why is it critical?",
              a: "Produced by <b>Type II pneumocytes</b>.<br>• <b>Function:</b> Reduces alveolar surface tension to prevent collapse (atelectasis).<br>• <b>Clinical:</b> Deficiency in premature infants leads to <b>Neonatal Respiratory Distress Syndrome (NRDS)</b>."
            },
            {
              q: "What is Congenital Diaphragmatic Hernia (CDH)?",
              a: "Failure of the pleuroperitoneal folds to close.<br>• <b>Most common:</b> Bochdalek hernia (posterolateral, usually left side).<br>• <b>Result:</b> Abdominal contents enter thorax, causing <b>pulmonary hypoplasia</b> (underdeveloped lungs)."
            },
            {
              q: "Describe Tracheoesophageal Fistula (TEF).",
              a: "An abnormal connection between the trachea and esophagus.<br>• <b>Classic presentation:</b> Excessive drooling, choking/cyanosis during feeding, and inability to pass an NG tube.<br>• Usually associated with <b>esophageal atresia</b>."
            },
            {
              q: "What are the fetal lung shunts and their adult remnants?",
              a: "1. <b>Foramen Ovale:</b> Shunts blood from RA to LA → <b>Fossa ovalis</b>.<br>2. <b>Ductus Arteriosus:</b> Shunts blood from Pulmonary Trunk to Aorta → <b>Ligamentum arteriosum</b>."
            },
            {
              q: "What forms the borders of a normal heart silhouette on CXR?",
              a: "<b>Right Border:</b> SVC and Right Atrium.<br><b>Left Border:</b> Aortic arch (knuckle), Pulmonary trunk, Left Auricle, and Left Ventricle."
            },
            {
              q: "What is the 'Silhouette Sign' on CXR?",
              a: "The loss of a normal anatomical border (like the heart or diaphragm) when adjacent lung tissue is filled with fluid/pus.<br><b>Example:</b> Loss of the right heart border indicates <b>Right Middle Lobe</b> consolidation."
            },
            {
              q: "What are the radiological signs of a Pleural Effusion?",
              a: "• <b>Blunting</b> of the costophrenic angles.<br>• <b>Meniscus sign</b> (curved upper border of fluid).<br>• Tracheal deviation <i>away</i> from the side of a large effusion."
            },
            {
              q: "What are the radiological signs of a Pneumothorax?",
              a: "• A visible <b>visceral pleural line</b>.<br>• <b>Absent lung markings</b> peripheral to the line.<br>• <b>Deep sulcus sign</b> (on supine films)."
            },
            {
              q: "What is Horner's Syndrome and its connection to the thorax?",
              a: "<b>Triad:</b> Ptosis (drooping eyelid), Miosis (constricted pupil), and Anhidrosis (lack of sweating).<br><b>Cause:</b> Damage to the sympathetic chain, often by a <b>Pancoast tumor</b> at the lung apex."
            },
            {
              q: "What is Superior Vena Cava (SVC) Syndrome?",
              a: "Obstruction of the SVC (often by lung cancer or lymphoma).<br><b>Symptoms:</b> Facial swelling (plethora), dilated neck/chest veins, and headache."
            },
            {
              q: "What is an Aortic Dissection?",
              a: "A tear in the aortic intima allowing blood to enter the media.<br><b>Symptom:</b> Sudden, 'tearing' chest pain radiating to the back.<br><b>CXR:</b> May show a <b>widened mediastinum</b>."
            },
            {
              q: "Match Myocardial Infarction (MI) territories to coronary arteries.",
              a: "• <b>Anterior Wall:</b> LAD (Left Anterior Descending).<br>• <b>Lateral Wall:</b> LCx (Left Circumflex).<br>• <b>Inferior Wall:</b> RCA (Right Coronary Artery)."
            },
            {
              q: "What is Coarctation of the Aorta?",
              a: "Narrowing of the aorta distal to the subclavian artery.<br><b>Classic Sign:</b> High BP in arms, low BP/weak pulses in legs. 'Rib notching' on CXR due to collateral circulation."
            },
            {
              q: "What is Patent Ductus Arteriosus (PDA)?",
              a: "Failure of the ductus arteriosus to close after birth.<br><b>Murmur:</b> Continuous 'machine-like' murmur.<br><b>Treatment:</b> Indomethacin (prostaglandin inhibitor) helps close it."
            },
            {
              q: "Differentiate Transudative vs. Exudative Pleural Effusion.",
              a: "<b>Transudate (Clear):</b> Due to pressure changes (e.g., Heart Failure, Cirrhosis). Low protein/LDH.<br><b>Exudate (Cloudy):</b> Due to inflammation/malignancy (e.g., Pneumonia, Cancer). High protein/LDH (meets <b>Light's Criteria</b>)."
            },
            {
              q: "What is a Chylothorax?",
              a: "Accumulation of lymph (chyle) in the pleural space.<br><b>Cause:</b> Injury to the <b>Thoracic Duct</b> (trauma or lymphoma).<br><b>Appearance:</b> Milky-white fluid with high triglycerides."
            },
            {
              q: "Define Lung Volumes: TV, IRV, ERV, and RV.",
              a: "• <b>TV (Tidal Volume):</b> Normal quiet breath (~500mL).<br>• <b>IRV:</b> Extra air inhaled after a normal breath.<br>• <b>ERV:</b> Extra air exhaled after a normal breath.<br>• <b>RV (Residual Volume):</b> Air left in lungs after maximal expiration (cannot be measured by spirometry)."
            },
            {
              q: "What are Lung Capacities: VC and TLC?",
              a: "• <b>VC (Vital Capacity):</b> TV + IRV + ERV (Max air you can move).<br>• <b>TLC (Total Lung Capacity):</b> VC + RV (Total air lungs can hold)."
            },
            {
              q: "What muscles are used for forced inspiration and expiration?",
              a: "<b>Forced Inspiration:</b> Scalenes, Sternocleidomastoid, Pectoralis minor.<br><b>Forced Expiration:</b> Abdominal muscles (Rectus abdominis, Obliques) and Internal intercostals."
            },
            {
              q: "Describe the cell types of the alveoli.",
              a: "• <b>Type I Pneumocytes:</b> Thin/flat; used for gas exchange.<br>• <b>Type II Pneumocytes:</b> Cuboidal; secrete surfactant and act as stem cells.<br>• <b>Alveolar Macrophages:</b> 'Dust cells' that clean up debris."
            },
            {
              q: "What are Club (Clara) cells?",
              a: "Non-ciliated cells found in the bronchioles.<br>• <b>Functions:</b> Secrete a component of surfactant, detoxify inhaled toxins, and act as stem cells for the bronchiolar lining."
            },
            {
              q: "What is the 'Crista Terminalis'?",
              a: "A vertical ridge in the right atrium separating the smooth part (sinus venarum) from the rough part (pectinate muscles)."
            },
            {
              q: "Which heart valve has only two cusps?",
              a: "<b>Mitral (Bicuspid) Valve</b>. The Tricuspid, Aortic, and Pulmonary valves all have three cusps."
            },
            {
              q: "What is the smallest functional unit of the lung?",
              a: "The <b>Acinus</b> (includes the respiratory bronchiole, alveolar ducts, and alveolar sacs)."
            },
            {
              q: "Where does the Left Superior Intercostal Vein drain?",
              a: "Into the <b>Left Brachiocephalic Vein</b>."
            },
            {
              q: "What is the clinical significance of the T4/T5 Plane (Transverse Thoracic Plane)?",
              a: "• Arch of Aorta starts and ends.<br>• Trachea bifurcates.<br>• Second rib attaches.<br>• Azygos vein enters the SVC."
            },
            {
              q: "What is the origin of the Thoracic Splanchnic Nerves?",
              a: "Sympathetic chain:<br>• <b>Greater:</b> T5–T9<br>• <b>Lesser:</b> T10–T11<br>• <b>Least:</b> T12"
            },
            {
              q: "What are the components of the Tetralogy of Fallot?",
              a: "1. <b>P</b>ulmonary stenosis<br>2. <b>R</b>ight ventricular hypertrophy<br>3. <b>O</b>verriding aorta<br>4. <b>V</b>entricular Septal Defect (VSD)<br><i>Mnemonic: <b>PROV</b>e it.</i>"
            },
            {
              q: "What are the boundaries of the Triangle of Auscultation?",
              a: "• <b>Trapezius</b> (Superior)<br>• <b>Latissimus Dorsi</b> (Inferior)<br>• <b>Medial border of the Scapula</b> (Lateral)<br><i>Note: This is the best place to listen to lung sounds as it has the least muscle cover.</i>"
            },
            {
              q: "Where is the most common site for a traumatic aortic rupture?",
              a: "The <b>Aortic Isthmus</b> (just distal to the origin of the left subclavian artery)."
            },
            {
              q: "Which specialized fibers conduct impulses directly to the ventricles?",
              a: "<b>Purkinje Fibers</b>."
            },
            {
              q: "What structures prevent the Mitral and Tricuspid valves from prolapsing?",
              a: "<b>Chordae tendineae</b> attached to <b>Papillary Muscles</b>."
            },
            {
              q: "What is the blood supply to the SA node in 60% of people?",
              a: "The <b>Right Coronary Artery (RCA)</b>."
            },
            {
              q: "Compare the Transverse and Oblique Pericardial Sinuses.",
              a: "• <b>Transverse:</b> Space behind the Aorta/Pulmonary trunk and in front of the SVC. (Used to clamp great vessels in surgery).<br>• <b>Oblique:</b> A blind reces behind the heart (Left Atrium) bounded by pulmonary veins."
            },
            {
              q: "What is Thoracic Outlet Syndrome (TOS)?",
              a: "Compression of the <b>brachial plexus</b> and/or <b>subclavian vessels</b> in the neck/shoulder area.<br>• <b>Causes:</b> Cervical rib, scalene muscle hypertrophy, or clavicle fracture."
            },
            {
              q: "What is the result of a Phrenic Nerve lesion?",
              a: "<b>Ipsilateral paralysis</b> of the diaphragm. On CXR, the affected side appears abnormally elevated (Eventration)."
            },
            {
              q: "What is the 'Cupula' of the pleura?",
              a: "The dome-shaped portion of the cervical parietal pleura that extends into the root of the neck, above the level of the 1st rib."
            }
