          cards: [
            { q: "What are the two types of pelvic inlets?", a: "Gynecoid (round/oval – female) and Android (heart-shaped – male)." },
            { q: "Boundaries of the pelvic inlet (pelvic brim)?", a: "Sacral promontory (posteriorly), arcuate line of ilium, pectineal line (pecten pubis), pubic symphysis (anteriorly)." },
            { q: "What is the diagonal conjugate?", a: "Distance from sacral promontory to inferior edge of pubic symphysis (~12.5 cm). Used clinically to estimate obstetric conjugate." },
            { q: "What is the obstetric (true) conjugate?", a: "Shortest AP diameter of pelvic inlet (~11 cm); from sacral promontory to posterior surface of pubic symphysis." },
            { q: "What is the interspinous diameter?", a: "Distance between the two ischial spines (~10 cm); the narrowest diameter of the pelvic outlet — clinically important in labor." },
            { q: "Greater pelvis vs. lesser pelvis?", a: "Greater pelvis = above pelvic brim (part of abdominal cavity). Lesser pelvis = below pelvic brim; contains pelvic organs." },
            { q: "What landmark separates greater from lesser pelvis?", a: "The pelvic brim (linea terminalis)." },
            { q: "Bones forming the acetabulum?", a: "Ilium (superior 2/5), ischium (posterior-inferior 2/5), pubis (anterior-inferior 1/5)." },
            { q: "What is the ischial spine's clinical significance?", a: "Landmark for pudendal nerve block; also marks level of ischial spine used in pudendal nerve anesthesia." },
            { q: "What muscles form the pelvic diaphragm?", a: "Levator ani (pubococcygeus, iliococcygeus, puborectalis) + Coccygeus." },
            { q: "Nerve supply of levator ani?", a: "Nerve to levator ani (S3, S4) from above + perineal branch of pudendal nerve from below." },
            { q: "What is the perineal body (central tendon of perineum)?", a: "Fibromuscular node between anal canal and vagina (female) or bulb of penis (male); convergence point of multiple perineal muscles." },
            { q: "What muscles attach to the perineal body?", a: "External anal sphincter, bulbospongiosus, superficial & deep transverse perineal, levator ani (pubococcygeus)." },
            { q: "What is the arcus tendineus?", a: "White line on obturator fascia; attachment of levator ani." },
            { q: "Obturator internus — nerve supply?", a: "Nerve to obturator internus (L5, S1, S2)." },
            { q: "Piriformis — origin, insertion, nerve supply?", a: "Origin: anterior sacrum (S2–S4). Insertion: greater trochanter. Nerve: S1, S2 (direct branches)." },
            { q: "What passes through the greater sciatic foramen above piriformis?", a: "Superior gluteal nerve and vessels." },
            { q: "What passes through the greater sciatic foramen below piriformis?", a: "Inferior gluteal N&V, sciatic nerve, pudendal N&V, posterior femoral cutaneous nerve, nerve to obturator internus, nerve to quadratus femoris." },
            { q: "What passes through the lesser sciatic foramen?", a: "Pudendal nerve and internal pudendal vessels (re-entering perineum), tendon of obturator internus." },
            { q: "Branches of the internal iliac artery (anterior division)?", a: "Umbilical (→ superior vesical), obturator, inferior vesical (male), uterine (female), vaginal (female), middle rectal, inferior gluteal, internal pudendal." },
            { q: "Branches of the internal iliac artery (posterior division)?", a: "Iliolumbar, lateral sacral, superior gluteal." },
            { q: "What is the medial umbilical ligament?", a: "Obliterated umbilical artery; remnant of fetal circulation; forms fold on anterior abdominal wall." },
            { q: "Uterine artery crosses which structure?", a: "Crosses ureter superiorly ('water under the bridge') — 2 cm lateral to cervix. Crucial surgical landmark." },
            { q: "What is the clinical significance of uterine artery–ureter relationship?", a: "Ureter can be ligated accidentally during hysterectomy when clamping uterine artery." },
            { q: "Ovarian artery — origin?", a: "Directly from abdominal aorta (L2); travels in suspensory ligament of ovary (infundibulopelvic ligament)." },
            { q: "Venous drainage of rectum?", a: "Superior rectal vein → inferior mesenteric vein (portal). Middle & inferior rectal veins → internal iliac → systemic. Site of portosystemic anastomosis." },
            { q: "What is the internal pudendal artery's course?", a: "Exits pelvis via greater sciatic foramen → wraps around ischial spine → enters perineum via lesser sciatic foramen → travels in pudendal canal (Alcock's canal)." },
            { q: "What is the pudendal nerve (S2, S3, S4)?", a: "Main nerve of the perineum. Branches: inferior rectal, perineal, dorsal nerve of penis/clitoris." },
            { q: "Where is the pudendal nerve blocked for anesthesia?", a: "At the ischial spine (transvaginally or transperineally)." },
            { q: "Pelvic splanchnic nerves — origin and function?", a: "S2–S4 (parasympathetic); erection, bladder contraction, rectal motility. Also called nervi erigentes." },
            { q: "Hypogastric nerve — function?", a: "Sympathetic (L1–L2 via superior hypogastric plexus); ejaculation, bladder neck closure, vasoconstriction." },
            { q: "Superior hypogastric plexus — location?", a: "Anterior to L5/S1, below aortic bifurcation; receives sympathetic fibers from lumbar splanchnics." },
            { q: "Inferior hypogastric plexus — contents?", a: "Mixed: sympathetic (hypogastric nerves) + parasympathetic (pelvic splanchnics); supplies pelvic viscera." },
            { q: "What nerves are at risk in a radical hysterectomy?", a: "Pelvic splanchnic nerves (parasympathetic) → bladder atony; sympathetic fibers → voiding dysfunction." },
            { q: "Femoral nerve — does it enter the pelvis?", a: "No. It runs in the groove between iliacus and psoas, exits under inguinal ligament lateral to femoral artery. Not a pelvic nerve." },
            { q: "Obturator nerve — origin and course?", a: "L2–L4; descends on medial border of psoas → exits via obturator foramen → supplies medial thigh adductors." },
            { q: "Lumbosacral trunk composition?", a: "L4 (partial) + L5; joins sacral plexus; lies on ala of sacrum." },
            { q: "What is the rectouterine pouch (pouch of Douglas)?", a: "Peritoneal recess between uterus/vagina and rectum; lowest point of peritoneal cavity in females. Site of fluid collection." },
            { q: "What is the vesicouterine pouch?", a: "Peritoneal recess between bladder and uterus; less deep than pouch of Douglas." },
            { q: "What is the retropubic space (space of Retzius)?", a: "Extraperitoneal space between pubic symphysis and bladder; contains fat, venous plexus. Accessed in retropubic prostatectomy." },
            { q: "Pararectal fossa — clinical relevance?", a: "Peritoneal recess lateral to rectum; can accumulate pus/blood; important in staging rectal cancer." },
            { q: "Broad ligament — layers and contents?", a: "Double fold of peritoneum. Contains: uterine tube (mesosalpinx), ovary (mesovarium), round ligament (mesometrium), uterine vessels, ureter." },
            { q: "Parts of the male urethra?", a: "Preprostatic (intramural), prostatic (longest – verumontanum here), membranous (shortest, sphincter), spongy/penile (longest overall)." },
            { q: "Where is the external urethral sphincter located in males?", a: "Surrounds membranous urethra; skeletal muscle; voluntary control; innervated by pudendal nerve (S2–S4)." },
            { q: "What is the verumontanum (seminal colliculus)?", a: "Elevation on posterior wall of prostatic urethra; site of ejaculatory duct openings and prostatic utricle." },
            { q: "Ejaculatory duct — formation and opening?", a: "Formed by union of ductus deferens + seminal vesicle duct; opens on verumontanum in prostatic urethra." },
            { q: "Zones of the prostate?", a: "Peripheral zone (70% – site of cancer), central zone (25% – ejaculatory ducts pass through), transition zone (5% – site of BPH)." },
            { q: "Nerve supply of erection vs. ejaculation?", a: "Erection: parasympathetic (S2–S4, pelvic splanchnic = nervi erigentes). Ejaculation: sympathetic (L1–L2, hypogastric nerve)." },
            { q: "Ductus deferens — arterial supply?", a: "Artery to ductus deferens (from inferior vesical or superior vesical artery)." },
            { q: "Seminal vesicles — location and function?", a: "Posterior to bladder, superior to prostate; produce ~70% of seminal fluid (fructose, prostaglandins); do NOT store sperm." },
            { q: "What is the prostatic venous plexus?", a: "Surrounds prostate; drains into internal iliac veins; communicates with vertebral venous plexus (Batson's plexus) → route of prostate cancer metastasis to vertebrae." },
            { q: "Waldeyer's fascia?", a: "Fascia attaching rectum to sacrum posteriorly; must be divided during abdominoperineal resection." },
            { q: "Parts of the uterus?", a: "Fundus, body, isthmus, cervix (supravaginal + vaginal portions)." },
            { q: "Ligaments supporting the uterus?", a: "Cardinal (transverse cervical/Mackenrodt's) — main support. Uterosacral. Round ligament (maintains anteversion). Pubocervical." },
            { q: "What is the cardinal (Mackenrodt's) ligament?", a: "Thickening of pelvic fascia; runs from cervix/upper vagina to lateral pelvic wall; MAIN support of uterus." },
            { q: "Round ligament — course and function?", a: "Uterine horn → inguinal canal → labium majus. Maintains anteversion. Contains no major vessels." },
            { q: "Blood supply of the ovary?", a: "Ovarian artery (from aorta) and ovarian branch of uterine artery; anastomose in mesovarium." },
            { q: "What is the suspensory ligament of the ovary?", a: "Peritoneal fold (infundibulopelvic ligament); contains ovarian artery, vein, lymphatics, nerves." },
            { q: "Ovarian ligament vs. suspensory ligament?", a: "Ovarian ligament = connects ovary to uterine horn (medially). Suspensory = connects ovary to lateral pelvic wall (contains ovarian vessels)." },
            { q: "Layers of the uterine wall?", a: "Perimetrium (serosa), myometrium (smooth muscle – thickest), endometrium (mucosa)." },
            { q: "Lymphatic drainage of the uterine body?", a: "Para-aortic lymph nodes (via ovarian vessels) + external iliac nodes." },
            { q: "Lymphatic drainage of the cervix?", a: "Internal iliac, external iliac, obturator, sacral nodes." },
            { q: "What is the vaginal fornix?", a: "Recess around cervix. Posterior fornix is deepest and adjacent to pouch of Douglas (clinical access point)." },
            { q: "What is a culdocentesis?", a: "Needle aspiration through posterior vaginal fornix into pouch of Douglas; detects blood (ectopic pregnancy) or pus." },
            { q: "Where does the sigmoid colon become the rectum?", a: "At the level of S3 vertebra (rectosigmoid junction)." },
            { q: "What are the transverse folds of the rectum (Houston's valves)?", a: "Three semilunar folds; middle one is most constant and marks anterior peritoneal reflection (~7 cm from anus)." },
            { q: "Arterial supply of the rectum?", a: "Superior rectal (inferior mesenteric), middle rectal (internal iliac), inferior rectal (internal pudendal)." },
            { q: "Peritoneal covering of the rectum?", a: "Upper 1/3: covered anteriorly and laterally. Middle 1/3: anteriorly only. Lower 1/3: no peritoneal covering (extraperitoneal)." },
            { q: "What is the dentate (pectinate) line?", a: "Junction of ectoderm and endoderm in anal canal; marks embryological boundary." },
            { q: "Differences above vs. below dentate line?", a: "Above: columnar epithelium, visceral innervation (ANS), superior rectal artery, portal drainage, internal hemorrhoids, lymph to internal iliac. Below: stratified squamous epithelium, somatic innervation (pudendal), inferior rectal artery, systemic drainage, external hemorrhoids, lymph to superficial inguinal." },
            { q: "What are anal columns (of Morgagni)?", a: "Longitudinal folds of mucosa in upper anal canal; separated by anal sinuses; anal valves connect them inferiorly." },
            { q: "Internal vs. external anal sphincter?", a: "Internal: involuntary smooth muscle, ANS (sympathetic contracts, parasympathetic relaxes). External: voluntary skeletal muscle, pudendal nerve (S2–S4)." },
            { q: "Puborectalis — function?", a: "Maintains anorectal angle (~90°); key for fecal continence; part of levator ani." },
            { q: "What is Waldeyer's fascia (presacral)?", a: "Presacral fascia attaching to posterior rectum; important in TME (total mesorectal excision) for rectal cancer." },
            { q: "What is the trigone of the bladder?", a: "Smooth triangular area between two ureteric orifices and internal urethral orifice; derived from mesonephric duct (mesoderm)." },
            { q: "Where is the ureter most vulnerable to surgical injury?", a: "Three sites: where it crosses common iliac artery, where uterine artery crosses over it, where it enters bladder (ureterovesical junction)." },
            { q: "What is the detrusor muscle?", a: "Three-layered smooth muscle of bladder wall; contracts during micturition; innervated by pelvic splanchnics (parasympathetic, S2–S4)." },
            { q: "Vesical venous plexus — drainage?", a: "Drains into internal iliac veins; in males, communicates with prostatic plexus." },
            { q: "Innervation of the bladder?", a: "Parasympathetic (S2–S4 via pelvic splanchnics): detrusor contraction. Sympathetic (T11–L2): relaxes detrusor, contracts internal sphincter. Somatic (pudendal, S2–S4): external sphincter." },
            { q: "Boundaries of the perineum?", a: "Anteriorly: pubic symphysis. Posteriorly: coccyx. Laterally: ischiopubic rami, sacrotuberous ligaments, ischial tuberosities." },
            { q: "What divides the perineum into two triangles?", a: "An imaginary transverse line connecting the two ischial tuberosities. Anterior = urogenital triangle. Posterior = anal triangle." },
            { q: "Contents of the anal triangle?", a: "Anal canal, external anal sphincter, ischiorectal (ischioanal) fossa." },
            { q: "What is the ischiorectal fossa?", a: "Fat-filled space lateral to anal canal; contains inferior rectal vessels and nerves; site of ischioanal abscess." },
            { q: "What is the pudendal canal (Alcock's canal)?", a: "Fascial sheath on lateral wall of ischiorectal fossa; contains pudendal nerve, internal pudendal artery and vein." },
            { q: "Layers of the scrotum (deep to superficial)?", a: "Skin → dartos muscle → external spermatic fascia → cremaster + cremasteric fascia → internal spermatic fascia → tunica vaginalis → tunica albuginea → testis." },
            { q: "What is Colles' fascia?", a: "Superficial perineal fascia (membranous layer) in the perineum; continuous with Scarpa's fascia of abdomen and dartos of scrotum." },
            { q: "What is the superficial perineal pouch?", a: "Space between Colles' fascia and perineal membrane; contains: erectile bodies (crura, bulb), perineal muscles (ischiocavernosus, bulbospongiosus, superficial transverse perineal), perineal nerves." },
            { q: "What is the deep perineal pouch?", a: "Between perineal membrane and superior fascia of UGD; contains: external urethral sphincter, deep transverse perineal muscle, bulbourethral (Cowper's) glands, dorsal nerve/artery of penis." },
            { q: "Cowper's (bulbourethral) glands — location and function?", a: "In deep perineal pouch; open into spongy urethra; produce pre-ejaculatory fluid (alkaline, lubricating)." },
            { q: "Arterial supply of the penis?", a: "Internal pudendal artery → dorsal artery of penis, deep artery of penis (artery of corpus cavernosum), artery of bulb." },
            { q: "What causes erection in males?", a: "Parasympathetic (S2–S4) → nitric oxide → relaxes smooth muscle in helicine arteries → engorgement of corpus cavernosum and spongiosum." },
            { q: "Layers of the spermatic cord?", a: "External spermatic fascia (from external oblique), cremasteric fascia (from internal oblique + transversus abdominis), internal spermatic fascia (from transversalis fascia)." },
            { q: "Contents of spermatic cord?", a: "Ductus deferens, testicular artery, pampiniform plexus, genital branch of genitofemoral nerve, cremasteric artery, artery to ductus deferens, lymphatics." },
            { q: "Cremasteric reflex — arc?", a: "Afferent: femoral branch of genitofemoral nerve (L1, L2). Efferent: genital branch → cremaster. Lost in UMN lesions above L1." },
            { q: "What is the gubernaculum?", a: "Guides testicular descent from abdomen into scrotum; becomes scrotal ligament in adults." },
            { q: "Processus vaginalis — significance?", a: "Peritoneal outpouching accompanying testicular descent; normally obliterates; patent processus → indirect inguinal hernia or hydrocele." },
            { q: "Parts of the vulva?", a: "Mons pubis, labia majora, labia minora, clitoris, vestibule (urethral + vaginal orifices), greater vestibular glands (Bartholin's), lesser vestibular glands." },
            { q: "Homologue of the penis in females?", a: "Clitoris (glans, body, two crura — no corpus spongiosum, no urethra)." },
            { q: "Homologue of the bulb of penis in females?", a: "Vestibular bulbs (bilateral)." },
            { q: "Greater vestibular glands (Bartholin's) — homologue and clinical significance?", a: "Homologue of Cowper's glands in males; open at 4 and 8 o'clock at vestibule; can form Bartholin's cyst/abscess." },
            { q: "What is the perineal membrane in females?", a: "Triangular fibrous sheet; pierced by urethra and vagina; less complete than in males." },
            { q: "What is an episiotomy?", a: "Surgical incision of perineum during childbirth; mediolateral direction preferred to avoid external anal sphincter and perineal body." },
            { q: "Nerve supply of the clitoris?", a: "Dorsal nerve of clitoris (branch of pudendal nerve, S2–S4)." },
            { q: "Lymph drainage of the clitoris and labia majora?", a: "Superficial inguinal lymph nodes." },
            { q: "What is a rectocele?", a: "Herniation of posterior vaginal wall (rectum bulges into vagina); due to weakened rectovaginal fascia/perineal body." },
            { q: "What is a cystocele?", a: "Herniation of anterior vaginal wall (bladder bulges into vagina); caused by weakened pubocervical fascia." },
            { q: "What causes urinary stress incontinence?", a: "Weakness of pelvic floor (levator ani) → bladder neck descends → external sphincter insufficient → leakage on increased intra-abdominal pressure." },
            { q: "Mechanism of a 4th-degree perineal tear?", a: "Extends through perineal body, external anal sphincter, and into rectal mucosa." },
            { q: "What is pelvic organ prolapse?", a: "Descent of pelvic organs (uterus, bladder, rectum) through vaginal canal; caused by levator ani/fascial failure." },
            { q: "Where does prostate cancer most commonly metastasize and why?", a: "Vertebral column; via Batson's vertebral venous plexus (valveless communication between prostatic and vertebral plexuses)." },
            { q: "What is a horseshoe abscess?", a: "Ischiorectal abscess crossing midline through deep post-anal space posterior to anal canal." },
            { q: "Why does blood from an ectopic pregnancy collect in the pouch of Douglas?", a: "It is the most dependent part of the peritoneal cavity in females; culdocentesis can diagnose it." },
            { q: "What is the clinical test for pudendal nerve damage?", a: "Loss of anal wink reflex (S2–S4); absent cutaneous sensation over perineum; impaired voluntary sphincter control." },
            { q: "What spinal level is responsible for the micturition reflex?", a: "S2–S4 (parasympathetic); pontine micturition center (PMC) coordinates it; damage above PMC → loss of voluntary control." },
            { q: "What structure gives rise to the trigone of the bladder?", a: "Mesonephric (Wolffian) duct; rest of bladder = urachus + allantois (endoderm)." },
            { q: "What is the urorectal septum?", a: "Mesodermal partition dividing the cloaca into the urogenital sinus (anterior) and anal canal (posterior)." },
            { q: "Homologue of labia majora in males?", a: "Scrotum (both derived from labioscrotal swellings)." },
            { q: "Homologue of labia minora in males?", a: "Ventral penile shaft skin / frenulum." },
            { q: "Müllerian (paramesonephric) duct derivatives in females?", a: "Uterine tubes, uterus, upper vagina (lower vagina from urogenital sinus)." },
            { q: "What is the gubernaculum homologue in females?", a: "Round ligament of uterus + ovarian ligament." },
            { q: "Indifferent gonad — when does sex differentiation begin?", a: "Week 7; SRY gene on Y chromosome triggers testicular differentiation → Sertoli cells produce AMH → Leydig cells produce testosterone." },
            { q: "Prostatic utricle — embryological remnant?", a: "Remnant of paramesonephric (Müllerian) duct in males; homologue of vagina." },
            { q: "Obstetric conjugate (true conjugate)?", a: "~11 cm (minimum for fetal head passage)." },
            { q: "Diagonal conjugate?", a: "~12.5 cm." },
            { q: "Interspinous diameter (narrowest outlet)?", a: "~10 cm." },
            { q: "Normal anorectal angle maintained by puborectalis?", a: "~90° (80–110°)." },
            { q: "Level of uterine artery crossing over ureter?", a: "2 cm lateral to cervix, at level of internal os." },
            { q: "Level of aortic bifurcation?", a: "L4 vertebral level." },
            { q: "Level of inferior mesenteric artery origin?", a: "L3." },
            { q: "Distance of peritoneal reflection from anus (anterior)?", a: "~7–9 cm anteriorly (marked by middle rectal fold of Houston)." }
          ]
        },
