            },
            {
              q: "What is the Arcuate Line?",
              a: "A landmark midway between the umbilicus and pubic symphysis.<br>• <b>Significance:</b> Marks the lower limit of the posterior rectus sheath.<br>• <b>Clinical:</b> Hematomas below this line can spread more freely."
            },
            {
              q: "What is the Linea Alba?",
              a: "A tough, avascular fibrous band running from the xiphoid to the pubic symphysis.<br>• <b>Clinical:</b> Often used for midline surgical incisions because it is avascular and provides a strong site for sutures."
            },
            {
              q: "What is the blood supply of the anterior abdominal wall?",
              a: "• <b>Superior Epigastric</b> (from Internal Thoracic)<br>• <b>Inferior Epigastric</b> (from External Iliac)<br>• <b>Lateral:</b> Lower intercostal, subcostal, and lumbar arteries."
            },
            {
              q: "What is the nerve supply of the anterior abdominal wall?",
              a: "• <b>T7–T11:</b> Intercostal nerves<br>• <b>T12:</b> Subcostal nerve<br>• <b>L1:</b> Iliohypogastric & Ilioinguinal nerves<br><br><b>Key Dermatomes:</b> T7 (Xiphoid), T10 (Umbilicus), L1 (Inguinal)."
            },
            {
              q: "Name the 5 umbilical folds on the internal abdominal wall.",
              a: "1. <b>Median umbilical fold:</b> Obliterated urachus.<br>2. <b>Medial umbilical folds (x2):</b> Obliterated umbilical arteries.<br>3. <b>Lateral umbilical folds (x2):</b> Inferior epigastric vessels (still patent)."
            },
            {
              q: "What is the Inguinal Ligament?",
              a: "The inferior free edge of the <b>External Oblique aponeurosis</b>.<br>• <b>Course:</b> ASIS to pubic tubercle.<br>• <b>Significance:</b> Forms the floor of the inguinal canal."
            },
            {
              q: "State the boundaries of the Inguinal Canal.",
              a: "• <b>Anterior:</b> EO aponeurosis (+ IO laterally)<br>• <b>Posterior:</b> Transversalis fascia (+ conjoint tendon medially)<br>• <b>Roof:</b> Arching fibers of IO & TA<br>• <b>Floor:</b> Inguinal ligament & lacunar ligament."
            },
            {
              q: "What are the contents of the Inguinal Canal?",
              a: "<b>Males:</b> Spermatic cord.<br><b>Females:</b> Round ligament of the uterus.<br><b>Both:</b> Ilioinguinal nerve (partially) and the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve."
            },
            {
              q: "Define the Deep and Superficial Inguinal Rings.",
              a: "• <b>Deep Ring:</b> An opening in the <b>transversalis fascia</b>, lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels.<br>• <b>Superficial Ring:</b> A triangular opening in the <b>EO aponeurosis</b>, superior to the pubic tubercle."
            },
            {
              q: "Compare Indirect and Direct Inguinal Hernias.",
              a: "<b>Indirect (Most Common):</b> Passes through the deep ring (lateral to inf. epigastric vessels). Usually congenital.<br><b>Direct:</b> Pushes through the posterior wall in Hesselbach’s triangle (medial to inf. epigastric vessels). Usually acquired (elderly)."
            },
            {
              q: "What are the boundaries of Hesselbach's Triangle?",
              a: "• <b>Lateral:</b> Inferior epigastric vessels<br>• <b>Medial:</b> Lateral border of Rectus abdominis<br>• <b>Inferior:</b> Inguinal ligament<br><i>Note: This is the site of <b>direct</b> inguinal hernias.</i>"
            },
            {
              q: "What is a Femoral Hernia?",
              a: "A hernia through the <b>femoral canal</b>, below the inguinal ligament.<br>• <b>Patient:</b> More common in females.<br>• <b>Risks:</b> High risk of strangulation due to narrow neck.<br>• <b>Site:</b> Below and lateral to the pubic tubercle."
            },
            {
              q: "List the layers of the Spermatic Cord (Outside to Inside).",
              a: "1. <b>External spermatic fascia</b> (from EO)<br>2. <b>Cremasteric muscle/fascia</b> (from IO)<br>3. <b>Internal spermatic fascia</b> (from Transversalis fascia)"
            },
            {
              q: "Describe the Cremasteric Reflex.",
              a: "Stroking the medial thigh causes the testis to elevate.<br>• <b>Afferent:</b> Femoral branch of genitofemoral nerve (L1/L2).<br>• <b>Efferent:</b> Genital branch of genitofemoral nerve.<br>• <b>Clinical:</b> Often absent in testicular torsion."
            },
            {
              q: "Differentiate between Parietal and Visceral Peritoneum pain.",
              a: "<b>Parietal:</b> Somatic innervation. Pain is <b>sharp, well-localized, and intense</b>.<br><b>Visceral:</b> Autonomic innervation. Pain is <b>dull, poorly localized (referred)</b>, and often midline."
            },
            {
              q: "List the Retroperitoneal organs (SAD PUCKER).",
              a: "<b>S:</b> Suprarenal glands<br><b>A:</b> Aorta & IVC<br><b>D:</b> Duodenum (2nd, 3rd, 4th parts)<br><b>P:</b> Pancreas (except tail)<br><b>U:</b> Ureters<br><b>C:</b> Colon (Ascending & Descending)<br><b>K:</b> Kidneys<br><b>E:</b> Esophagus<br><b>R:</b> Rectum"
            },
            {
              q: "What is the Greater Omentum?",
              a: "The 'policeman of the abdomen'.<br>• <b>Structure:</b> A double-layered fold of peritoneum hanging from the stomach's greater curvature.<br>• <b>Function:</b> Migrates to sites of inflammation/infection to wall them off."
            },
            {
              q: "What are the contents of the Lesser Omentum (Hepatoduodenal Ligament)?",
              a: "The <b>Portal Triad</b>:<br>1. Proper Hepatic Artery<br>2. Portal Vein<br>3. Common Bile Duct"
            },
            {
              q: "What is the Epiploic Foramen (of Winslow)?",
              a: "The opening between the greater and lesser sacs.<br><b>Anterior Boundary:</b> Hepatoduodenal ligament (portal triad).<br><b>Posterior Boundary:</b> IVC.<br><b>Clinical:</b> Pringle’s maneuver (clamping the triad here) stops hepatic bleeding."
            },
            {
              q: "Identify the most dependent spaces in the abdomen/pelvis.",
              a: "• <b>Supine (RUQ):</b> Hepatorenal recess (Morison’s Pouch).<br>• <b>Pelvis (Female):</b> Rectouterine pouch (Pouch of Douglas).<br>• <b>Pelvis (Male):</b> Rectovesical pouch."
            },
            {
              q: "What is the root of the Small Intestine Mesentery?",
              a: "A 15-cm-long diagonal line running from the duodenojejunal flexure (L2) to the right sacroiliac joint.<br>• <b>Contents:</b> SMA/SMV, lymphatics, and nerves."
            },
            {
              q: "What are the features of Peritonitis?",
              a: "Inflammation of the peritoneum.<br><b>Signs:</b> Board-like abdominal rigidity, rebound tenderness, and guarding.<br><b>Reason:</b> Irritation of the somatic parietal peritoneum."
            },
            {
              q: "Name the parts of the Stomach.",
              a: "Cardia, Fundus, Body, Pyloric Antrum, Pyloric Canal, and Pylorus (sphincter)."
            },
            {
              q: "Describe the blood supply to the Stomach.",
              a: "<b>Lesser Curvature:</b> Left gastric (Celiac) and Right gastric (Hepatic).<br><b>Greater Curvature:</b> Left gastroepiploic (Splenic) and Right gastroepiploic (Gastroduodenal).<br><b>Fundus:</b> Short gastrics (Splenic)."
            },
            {
              q: "What is the nerve supply of the Stomach?",
              a: "<b>Parasympathetic:</b> Vagus (CN X) (stimulates acid/motility).<br><b>Sympathetic:</b> T6–T9 via Celiac plexus (inhibits motility; carries pain)."
            },
            {
              q: "What is Hypertrophic Pyloric Stenosis?",
              a: "Hypertrophy of the pyloric sphincter in infants (~3–6 weeks old).<br><b>Features:</b> Projectile, non-bilious vomiting and an 'olive-shaped' mass in the epigastrium."
            },
            {
              q: "List the gastric secretory cells and their products.",
              a: "• <b>Parietal:</b> HCl & Intrinsic Factor.<br>• <b>Chief:</b> Pepsinogen.<br>• <b>G Cells:</b> Gastrin.<br>• <b>D Cells:</b> Somatostatin.<br>• <b>Mucous Cells:</b> Mucus."
            },
            {
              q: "What stimulates and inhibits HCl secretion in the Stomach?",
              a: "<b>Stimulators:</b> Gastrin, Acetylcholine (Vagus), and Histamine (H2 receptors).<br><b>Inhibitors:</b> Somatostatin and Prostaglandins."
            },
            {
              q: "What are the 4 parts of the Duodenum?",
              a: "1. <b>Superior:</b> Mobile; site of most ulcers.<br>2. <b>Descending:</b> Site of the major duodenal papilla (bile/pancreatic entry).<br>3. <b>Horizontal:</b> Crossed by SMA/SMV.<br>4. <b>Ascending:</b> Ends at the Ligament of Treitz."
            },
            {
              q: "Contrast the Jejunum and Ileum.",
              a: "<b>Jejunum:</b> LUQ, thicker wall, more vascular (longer vasa recta), fewer arterial arcades, no Peyer's patches.<br><b>Ileum:</b> RLQ, thinner wall, more arcades, short vasa recta, has <b>Peyer's patches</b> (lymphoid)."
            },
            {
              q: "State the 'Rule of 2s' for Meckel's Diverticulum.",
              a: "• 2% of the population.<br>• 2 inches long.<br>• 2 feet from the ileocecal valve.<br>• 2 types of ectopic tissue (Gastric/Pancreatic).<br>• Presents by age 2."
            },
            {
              q: "What are the distinguishing features of the Large Intestine?",
              a: "1. <b>Taeniae coli:</b> 3 longitudinal muscle bands.<br>2. <b>Haustra:</b> Sacculations of the wall.<br>3. <b>Epiploic appendages:</b> Fatty tags on the serosa."
            },
            {
              q: "Where are the watershed areas of the colon?",
              a: "1. <b>Splenic Flexure</b> (Griffith's Point): SMA/IMA boundary.<br>2. <b>Rectosigmoid Junction</b> (Sudeck's Point).<br><i>Clinical: These are the first areas to become ischemic during hypotension.</i>"
            },
            {
              q: "How do you identify McBurney's Point?",
              a: "1/3 of the way along a line from the ASIS to the umbilicus.<br>• <b>Significance:</b> Surface marking of the base of the appendix; site of maximal tenderness in appendicitis."
            },
            {
              q: "Describe the functional lobes of the Liver.",
              a: "Divided by <b>Cantlie’s Line</b> (from IVC to GB fossa).<br>• The liver is functionally split into 8 segments based on portal and biliary drainage (Couinaud segments)."
            },
            {
              q: "What is the dual blood supply of the Liver?",
              a: "1. <b>Portal Vein (75%):</b> Nutrient-rich, oxygen-poor.<br>2. <b>Hepatic Artery (25%):</b> Oxygen-rich.<br><i>Both enter via the Porta Hepatis.</i>"
            },
            {
              q: "List the 3 primary Portosystemic Anastomoses.",
              a: "1. <b>Esophagus:</b> Left gastric ↔ Azygos (Varices).<br>2. <b>Umbilicus:</b> Paraumbilical ↔ Epigastric (Caput medusae).<br>3. <b>Rectum:</b> Sup. Rectal ↔ Inf. Rectal (Hemorrhoids)."
            },
            {
              q: "Trace the path of Bile from the liver to the duodenum.",
              a: "Hepatic Ducts → Common Hepatic Duct (+ Cystic Duct) → Common Bile Duct (+ Pancreatic Duct) → Ampulla of Vater → Major Duodenal Papilla."
            },
            {
              q: "What is Calot's Triangle and why is it important?",
              a: "<b>Boundaries:</b> Cystic duct, Common hepatic duct, and Liver edge.<br><b>Contents:</b> Cystic artery.<br><b>Importance:</b> Must be carefully dissected during cholecystectomy to identify the artery."
            },
            {
              q: "What is Murphy's Sign?",
              a: "Inspiratory arrest during deep palpation of the RUQ.<br>• <b>Significance:</b> Suggests <b>acute cholecystitis</b> (inflamed gallbladder hits the examiner's hand)."
            },
            {
              q: "Identify the parts of the Pancreas.",
              a: "Head (in the C-loop of duodenum), Uncinate process, Neck, Body, and Tail (reaches the spleen)."
            },
            {
              q: "Differentiate between Acute Pancreatitis signs: Cullen's vs. Grey Turner's.",
              a: "• <b>Cullen’s:</b> Periumbilical bruising.<br>• <b>Grey Turner’s:</b> Flank (side) bruising.<br><i>Note: Both indicate severe necrotizing pancreatitis with retroperitoneal hemorrhage.</i>"
            },
            {
              q: "What is Kehr's Sign?",
              a: "Referred pain to the <b>left shoulder</b> following splenic rupture.<br><b>Mechanism:</b> Blood irritates the diaphragm, which shares the C3-C5 nerve supply (Phrenic) with the shoulder skin."
            },
            {
              q: "State the major branches of the Abdominal Aorta.",
              a: "• <b>Celiac Trunk:</b> T12<br>• <b>SMA:</b> L1<br>• <b>Renals:</b> L2<br>• <b>IMA:</b> L3<br>• <b>Bifurcation:</b> L4"
            },
            {
              q: "Name the branches of the Celiac Trunk.",
              a: "1. Left Gastric Artery<br>2. Splenic Artery<br>3. Common Hepatic Artery"
            },
            {
              q: "What structure drains into the Left Renal Vein but NOT the Right?",
              a: "The <b>Left Gonadal (Testicular/Ovarian) vein</b> and the Left Suprarenal vein.<br><i>Clinical: This explains why varicoceles are more common on the left side (Nutcracker effect).</i>"
            },
            {
              q: "Contrast the internal and external hemorrhoids.",
              a: "<b>Internal:</b> Above the pectinate line; painless (autonomic supply).<br><b>External:</b> Below the pectinate line; painful (somatic supply — inferior rectal nerve)."
            },
            {
              q: "What are the 3 main GI hormones and their primary triggers?",
              a: "1. <b>Gastrin:</b> Triggered by stomach distension/peptides.<br>2. <b>CCK:</b> Triggered by fatty acids/amino acids.<br>3. <b>Secretin:</b> Triggered by acid in the duodenum."
            },
            {
              q: "State the function of Cholecystokinin (CCK).",
              a: "• Stimulates pancreatic enzyme secretion.<br>• Stimulates gallbladder contraction.<br>• Relaxes the Sphincter of Oddi.<br>• Slows gastric emptying."
            },
            {
              q: "What is the function of Secretin?",
              a: "Stimulates the secretion of <b>bicarbonate-rich</b> pancreatic juice to neutralize stomach acid in the duodenum."
            },
            {
              q: "Describe the Pectinate (Dentate) Line significance.",
              a: "The transition between the endoderm (hindgut) and ectoderm (proctodeum).<br>• <b>Above:</b> Columnar epithelium, portal drainage, autonomic nerves.<br>• <b>Below:</b> Stratified squamous epithelium, systemic drainage, somatic nerves."
            },
            {
              q: "What are Brunner's Glands and where are they found?",
              a: "Found in the <b>Duodenal submucosa</b>.<br>• <b>Function:</b> Secrete alkaline mucus to protect the duodenum from acidic chyme."
            },
            {
              q: "What are Peyer's Patches?",
              a: "Lymphoid follicles found in the <b>Ileum lamina propria/submucosa</b>.<br>• <b>Function:</b> Immune surveillance; contain M cells that sample antigens."
            },
            {
              q: "Name the 3 pairs of salivary glands and their secretions.",
              a: "1. <b>Parotid:</b> Serous (mostly amylase).<br>2. <b>Submandibular:</b> Mixed.<br>3. <b>Sublingual:</b> Mucinous."
            },
            {
              q: "What is the function of Gastric Intrinsic Factor?",
              a: "Secreted by parietal cells; essential for the absorption of <b>Vitamin B12</b> in the terminal ileum."
            },
            {
              q: "Explain the 'Nutcracker Syndrome'.",
              a: "Compression of the <b>Left Renal Vein</b> between the SMA and the Aorta.<br>• <b>Result:</b> Hematuria, flank pain, and left-sided varicocele."
            },
            {
              q: "Where is the primary site of nutrient absorption in the small intestine?",
              a: "The <b>Jejunum</b>."
            },
            {
              q: "What is the function of Motilin?",
              a: "Produced in the small intestine; triggers <b>Migrating Motor Complexes (MMC)</b> to clear the gut between meals."
            },
            {
              q: "Define 'Gut Rotation'.",
              a: "The midgut herniates into the umbilical cord at week 6, rotates <b>270° counterclockwise</b> around the SMA, and returns to the abdomen at week 10."
            },
            {
              q: "What is the 'Water Under the Bridge' anatomical rule?",
              a: "The <b>Ureter</b> passes <b>under</b> the uterine artery (in females) or the ductus deferens (in males)."
            },
            {
              q: "Name the components of the Filtration Barrier in the kidney.",
              a: "1. Fenestrated capillary endothelium.<br>2. Glomerular basement membrane (GBM) — negatively charged.<br>3. Podocyte foot processes (slit diaphragm)."
            },
            {
              q: "What is the function of the Proximal Convoluted Tubule (PCT)?",
              a: "The workhorse of the nephron.<br>• Reabsorbs 100% of glucose/amino acids and 65-70% of Na+, Cl-, and water."
            },
            {
              q: "Describe the role of the Loop of Henle.",
              a: "Establishment of an <b>osmotic gradient</b> in the medulla.<br>• <b>Descending:</b> Permeable to water.<br>• <b>Ascending:</b> Impermeable to water; actively reabsorbs NaCl."
            },
            {
              q: "How does Aldosterone affect the kidney?",
              a: "Acts on the <b>Principal Cells</b> of the collecting duct to increase Na+ reabsorption and K+ secretion."
            },
            {
              q: "How does Antidiuretic Hormone (ADH/Vasopressin) work?",
              a: "Increases water permeability of the <b>Collecting Duct</b> by inserting <b>Aquaporin-2</b> channels."
            },
            {
              q: "What is the function of the Juxtaglomerular (JG) cells?",
              a: "Modified smooth muscle cells in the afferent arteriole that secrete <b>Renin</b> in response to low BP."
            },
            {
              q: "List the 3 zones of the Adrenal Cortex and their products.",
              a: "1. <b>G</b>lomerulosa: Mineraloids (Aldosterone)<br>2. <b>F</b>asciculata: Glucocorticoids (Cortisol)<br>3. <b>R</b>eticularis: Androgens (DHEA)<br><i>Mnemonic: <b>G-F-R</b> (Salt, Sugar, Sex).</i>"
            },
            {
              q: "What is the primary product of the Adrenal Medulla?",
              a: "Catecholamines (**Epinephrine** and Norepinephrine)."
            },
            {
              q: "Identify the muscles of the Pelvic Diaphragm.",
              a: "<b>Levator Ani</b> (Puborectalis, Pubococcygeus, Iliococcygeus) and <b>Coccygeus</b>."
            },
            {
              q: "What is the function of the Puborectalis muscle?",
              a: "Forms a 'sling' around the rectum; its relaxation is essential for defecation."
            },
            {
              q: "State the nerve supply of the Perineum.",
              a: "<b>Pudendal Nerve (S2–S4)</b>.<br><i>Mnemonic: S2, 3, 4 keeps the 'poop' off the floor.</i>"
            },
            {
              q: "Name the 3 parts of the Male Urethra.",
              a: "1. Prostatic<br>2. Membranous (thinnest/weakest part)<br>3. Spongy (Penile)"
            },
            {
              q: "Where is the most common site of Prostate Cancer?",
              a: "The <b>Peripheral Zone</b> (palpable on DRE)."
            },
            {
              q: "Where does Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia (BPH) usually occur?",
              a: "The <b>Transitional Zone</b> (surrounds the urethra; causes obstruction)."
            },
            {
              q: "Trace the path of Sperm from production to ejaculation.",
              a: "Seminiferous tubules → Epididymis → Vas deferens → Ejaculatory duct → Urethra.<br><i>Mnemonic: <b>SEVEN UP</b>.</i>"
            },
            {
              q: "What are the 3 ligaments that support the Ovaries and Uterus?",
              a: "1. <b>Suspensory ligament:</b> Contains ovarian vessels.<br>2. <b>Cardinal (Mackenrodt's):</b> Contains uterine vessels.<br>3. <b>Round ligament:</b> Remnant of gubernaculum; passes through inguinal canal."
            },
            {
              q: "Which part of the Fallopian tube is the most common site of fertilization?",
              a: "The <b>Ampulla</b>."
            },
            {
              q: "Identify the 4 parts of the Uterus.",
              a: "Fundus, Body, Isthmus, and Cervix."
            },
            {
              q: "What is the 'Point and Shoot' mnemonic for sexual response?",
              a: "• <b>P</b>oint: <b>P</b>arasympathetic (Erection - Pelvic splanchnic nerves).<br>• <b>S</b>hoot: <b>S</b>ympathetic (Emission/Ejaculation - Hypogastric nerves)."
            },
            {
              q: "What is the blood supply to the Ovaries/Testes?",
              a: "<b>Ovarian/Testicular arteries</b> (direct branches of the Abdominal Aorta at L2)."
            },
            {
              q: "What is a 'Varicocele'?",
              a: "Dilation of the pampiniform plexus of veins ('bag of worms'). More common on the left."
            },
            {
              q: "What is the significance of the Ischioanal (Ischiorectal) fossa?",
              a: "A large, fat-filled space lateral to the anal canal. High risk for <b>abscess formation</b> which can spread to the other side (horseshoe abscess)."
            },
            {
              q: "Define the Urogenital Triangle.",
              a: "The anterior half of the perineum; contains the external genitalia and the openings of the urethra (and vagina)."
            },
            {
              q: "Define the Anal Triangle.",
              a: "The posterior half of the perineum; contains the anus and ischioanal fossae."
            },
            {
              q: "What is the 'Pudendal Nerve Block' landmark?",
              a: "The <b>Ischial Spine</b>."
            },
            {
              q: "State the sympathetic and parasympathetic supply to the Hindgut.",
              a: "• <b>Sympathetic:</b> Lumbar splanchnic nerves (L1–L2).<br>• <b>Parasympathetic:</b> Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2–S4)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          topic: 'Neuroanatomy',
          icon: 'brain',
          cards: [
            {
              q: "What is the embryological origin of the CNS?",
              a: "<b>Neuroectoderm</b> (Neural tube)."
            },
            {
              q: "What is the most common site for a lumbar puncture (adults)?",
              a: "<b>L3–L4</b> or <b>L4–L5</b>.<br><i>Safe because the spinal cord typically ends at L1–L2.</i>"
            },
            {
              q: "What is the function of the Dorsal Column-Medial Lemniscus (DCML) pathway?",
              a: "• Fine touch<br>• Vibration<br>• Conscious proprioception"
            },
            {
              q: "Where are the first-order neuron cell bodies of the DCML located?",
              a: "In the <b>Dorsal Root Ganglion (DRG)</b>."
            },
            {
              q: "Where does the Lateral Spinothalamic tract decussate?",
              a: "In the <b>Anterior White Commissure</b>, at the level of entry into the spinal cord."
            },
            {
              q: "What are the clinical signs of a Lateral Corticospinal tract lesion?",
              a: "<b>Ipsilateral UMN signs</b> below the level of the lesion:<br>• Weakness<br>• Hyperreflexia<br>• Positive Babinski sign"
            },
            {
              q: "Describe the deficits in Brown-Séquard Syndrome (hemisection).",
              a: "• <b>Ipsilateral:</b> Loss of motor, vibration, and proprioception.<br>• <b>Contralateral:</b> Loss of pain and temperature (starting 1–2 levels below the lesion)."
            },
            {
              q: "What are the nerve roots of the Phrenic nerve?",
              a: "<b>C3, C4, C5</b> ('keeps the diaphragm alive')."
            },
            {
              q: "What is the clinical significance of the Artery of Adamkiewicz?",
              a: "It is the major blood supply to the <b>lower 2/3</b> of the spinal cord. Damage can lead to <b>Anterior Spinal Artery Syndrome</b>."
            },
            {
              q: "Which cranial nerve is the only one to exit the brainstem posteriorly?",
              a: "<b>CN IV</b> (Trochlear nerve)."
            },
            {
              q: "Which cranial nerve forms the Efferent limb of the Pupillary Light Reflex?",
              a: "<b>CN III</b> (Oculomotor nerve)."
            },
            {
              q: "What is the result of a CN VI (Abducens) lesion?",
              a: "<b>Medial strabismus</b> (inability to abduct the eye)."
            },
            {
              q: "Which muscle is innervated by CN IV?",
              a: "<b>Superior Oblique muscle</b> (moves the eye down and in)."
            },
            {
              q: "Which nucleus processes taste from the anterior 2/3 of the tongue?",
              a: "<b>Nucleus Solitarius</b> (carried by CN VII)."
            },
            {
              q: "Which nucleus provides motor innervation for swallowing (CN IX, X)?",
              a: "<b>Nucleus Ambiguus</b>."
            },
            {
              q: "What is the triad of Horner Syndrome?",
              a: "1. <b>Ptosis</b> (drooping eyelid)<br>2. <b>Miosis</b> (constricted pupil)<br>3. <b>Anhidrosis</b> (lack of sweating)"
            },
            {
