            {
              q: "What is the resting membrane potential of a neuron?",
              a: "-70 mV (inside negative relative to outside) | Maintained by: Na+/K+ ATPase (pumps 3 Na+ out, 2 K+ in) | High K+ inside, high Na+ outside | K+ leak channels — primary determinant of RMP | Cl- and large anions also contribute"
            },
            {
              q: "What is the Nernst equation and its significance?",
              a: "E = (61/z) × log([ion]out/[ion]in) | Calculates equilibrium potential for single ion | EK+ = -90 mV | ENa+ = +60 mV | ECl- = -70 mV | ECa2+ = +120 mV | RMP closer to EK+ because membrane is more permeable to K+ at rest"
            },
            {
              q: "What is the Goldman-Hodgkin-Katz equation?",
              a: "Accounts for multiple ions simultaneously | RMP determined by relative permeability of Na+, K+, Cl- | At rest — K+ permeability >> Na+ permeability | During AP — Na+ permeability >> K+ permeability | More complete than Nernst (single ion)"
