            {
              q: "What are voltage-gated Na+ channels and their states?",
              a: "3 states: Resting (closed, activatable) | Open (activated, Na+ rushes in) | Inactivated (closed, cannot open — absolute refractory period) | Activation gate — opens at -55 mV (threshold) | Inactivation gate — closes ~1 ms after opening | Blocked by tetrodotoxin (TTX), local anesthetics"
            },
            {
              q: "What are voltage-gated K+ channels?",
              a: "Slower than Na+ channels | Open during repolarization | Responsible for after-hyperpolarization (membrane goes below RMP) | Blocked by tetraethylammonium (TEA) | Contribute to relative refractory period"
            },
            {
              q: "What is the Na+/K+ ATPase pump?",
              a: "Electrogenic pump (net outward positive charge) | Pumps 3 Na+ out : 2 K+ in per cycle | Uses 1 ATP | Contributes ~-4 mV to RMP directly | Essential for maintaining ion gradients long-term | Inhibited by ouabain/cardiac glycosides (digoxin)"
            },
            {
              q: "What is threshold potential?",
              a: "~-55 mV for most neurons | Membrane potential at which AP is triggered | All-or-none phenomenon | Below threshold — graded potential only | At/above threshold — full AP generated regardless of stimulus strength | Determined by balance of inward Na+ and outward K+ currents"
            },
            {
              q: "What are the phases of the action potential?",
              a: "Resting (-70 mV) | Depolarization — Na+ channels open, Na+ rushes in, membrane → +30 mV | Repolarization — Na+ channels inactivate, K+ channels open, K+ exits | After-hyperpolarization — excess K+ efflux → below RMP | Return to resting — Na+/K+ pump restores gradients"
            },
            {
              q: "What is the absolute refractory period?",
              a: "Period during which NO stimulus can trigger another AP | Corresponds to Na+ channel inactivation | Lasts ~1–2 ms | Ensures unidirectional propagation of AP | Limits maximum firing frequency of neuron"
            },
            {
              q: "What is the relative refractory period?",
              a: "Period after absolute refractory period | Na+ channels recovering from inactivation | K+ channels still open (after-hyperpolarization) | A stronger-than-normal stimulus CAN trigger AP | Corresponds to after-hyperpolarization phase"
            },
            {
              q: "What is accommodation?",
              a: "Slow depolarization → Na+ channels inactivate before threshold reached → no AP | Rapid depolarization → AP generated | Clinically — why DC current causes single discharge but AC causes tetany | Also explains why slowly rising pressure may not cause pain"
            },
            {
              q: "What is saltatory conduction?",
              a: "AP jumps between nodes of Ranvier in myelinated fibers | Myelin acts as insulator — ion flow only at nodes | Faster + more energy efficient than continuous conduction | Velocity ∝ diameter + myelination | Lost in demyelinating diseases (MS, Guillain-Barré)"
