            { q: "The 6th Pharyngeal Arch Artery gives rise to?", a: "Pulmonary Arteries and the Ductus Arteriosus." },
            { q: "The Respiratory Diverticulum (lung bud) appears as an outgrowth of...?", a: "The Foregut (specifically the ventral wall of the Foregut)." },
            { q: "Embryologic origin of the epithelial lining of the Lungs and Trachea?", a: "Endoderm." },
            { q: "Embryologic origin of the cartilage, muscle, and connective tissue of the Lungs?", a: "Splanchnic Mesoderm." },
            { q: "Most common type of Tracheoesophageal Fistula (TEF)?", a: "Esophageal Atresia with a Distal Fistula (Type C)." },
            { q: "Four stages of Lung maturation in order?", a: "Pseudoglandular, Canalicular, Saccular, Alveolar." },
            { q: "During which stage of lung development does surfactant production begin?", a: "Saccular stage (starting around week 24–26)." },
            { q: "Cells responsible for secreting Surfactant?", a: "Type II Pneumocytes." },
            { q: "Embryologic origin of the GI tract lining?", a: "Endoderm." },
            { q: "The Foregut-Midgut transition occurs at which anatomical landmark?", a: "Just distal to the entrance of the Common Bile Duct into the Duodenum." },
            { q: "Midgut-Hindgut transition occurs at...?", a: "The distal third of the Transverse Colon." },
            { q: "The Midgut herniates into the umbilical cord during which week?", a: "Week 6 (Physiological Herniation)." },
            { q: "During the return of the Midgut to the abdomen, it rotates how many degrees?", a: "270 degrees counterclockwise around the Superior Mesenteric Artery (SMA)." },
            { q: "Failure of the Midgut to return to the abdomen results in?", a: "Omphalocele (covered by peritoneum/amnion)." },
            { q: "Extrusion of bowel through the abdominal wall (not covered by a sac)?", a: "Gastroschisis." },
            { q: "Embryologic cause of Meckel’s Diverticulum?", a: "Persistence of the Vitelline Duct (Yolk Stalk)." },
            { q: "Rule of 2s for Meckel’s Diverticulum?", a: "2% of population, 2 feet from ileocecal valve, 2 inches long, 2 types of ectopic tissue (gastric/pancreatic)." },
            { q: "Three successive kidney systems in the embryo?", a: "Pronephros, Mesonephros, Metanephros." },
            { q: "The definitive (permanent) kidney develops from which structure?", a: "Metanephros." },
            { q: "The Ureteric Bud gives rise to...?", a: "Ureter, Renal Pelvis, Calyces, and Collecting Ducts." },
            { q: "The Metanephric Blastema gives rise to...?", a: "Nephrons (Bowman’s capsule to Distal Convoluted Tubule)." },
            { q: "Potter Sequence is caused by...?", a: "Oligohydramnios (often due to Bilateral Renal Agenesis)." },
            { q: "Horseshoe Kidney is trapped under which artery during its 'ascent'?", a: "Inferior Mesenteric Artery (IMA)." },
            { q: "Embryologic origin of the Urinary Bladder (except the trigone)?", a: "Cloaca (Endoderm)." },
            { q: "The Mesonephric (Wolffian) Ducts develop into...?", a: "Male internal genitalia (SEED: Seminal vesicles, Epididymis, Ejaculatory duct, Ductus deferens)." },
            { q: "The Paramesonephric (Müllerian) Ducts develop into...?", a: "Female internal genitalia (Fallopian tubes, Uterus, upper 1/3 of Vagina)." },
            { q: "Substance that causes regression of Müllerian Ducts in males?", a: "Müllerian Inhibitory Substance (MIS/AMH) from Sertoli cells." },
            { q: "Which Pharyngeal Arch is associated with the Trigeminal Nerve (CN V)?", a: "1st Arch (Mandibular)." },
            { q: "Which Pharyngeal Arch is associated with the Facial Nerve (CN VII)?", a: "2nd Arch (Hyoid)." },
            { q: "The 3rd Pharyngeal Pouch gives rise to...?", a: "Thymus and Inferior Parathyroid Glands." },
            { q: "The 4th Pharyngeal Pouch gives rise to...?", a: "Superior Parathyroid Glands." },
            { q: "Embryologic origin of the Thyroid Gland?", a: "Foramen Cecum (floor of the pharynx) and descends via the Thyroglossal Duct." },
            { q: "DiGeorge Syndrome involves the failure of which pouches?", a: "3rd and 4th Pharyngeal Pouches." },
            { q: "Four structures that form the Diaphragm?", a: "Septum Transversum, Pleuroperitoneal membranes, Dorsal mesentery of esophagus, Body wall musculature." },
            { q: "Most common site of a Congenital Diaphragmatic Hernia?", a: "Left posterolateral side (Bochdalek hernia)." },
            { q: "Origin of the Adrenal Cortex?", a: "Mesoderm." },
            { q: "Origin of the Adrenal Medulla?", a: "Neural Crest Cells." },
            { q: "Derivative of the Allantois in the adult?", a: "Urachus (Median Umbilical Ligament)." },
            { q: "Derivative of the Umbilical Vein in the adult?", a: "Ligamentum Teres Hepatis." },
            { q: "Derivative of the Umbilical Arteries in the adult?", a: "Medial Umbilical Ligaments." },
            { q: "Embryologic origin of the Coronary Sinus?", a: "Left Horn of the Sinus Venosus." },
            { q: "Embryologic origin of the Smooth part of the Right Atrium (Sinus Venarum)?", a: "Right Horn of the Sinus Venosus." },
            { q: "The Primitive Atrium gives rise to what in the adult heart?", a: "The Trabeculated parts (auricles) of the Left and Right Atria." },
            { q: "Which structure divides the Atrioventricular Canal into right and left orifices?", a: "Endocardial Cushions." },
            { q: "Transposition of the Great Vessels is caused by a failure of what process?", a: "Failure of the Aorticopulmonary septum to spiral." },
            { q: "Total Anomalous Pulmonary Venous Return (TAPVR) occurs when?", a: "The Pulmonary veins fail to incorporate into the Left Atrium." },
            { q: "The Right Subclavian Artery is derived from which specific arch?", a: "Right 4th Pharyngeal Arch Artery." },
            { q: "The Celiac Artery is the artery of which embryologic gut division?", a: "Foregut." },
            { q: "The Superior Mesenteric Artery (SMA) is the artery of which gut division?", a: "Midgut." },
            { q: "The Inferior Mesenteric Artery (IMA) is the artery of which gut division?", a: "Hindgut." },
            { q: "Embryologic origin of the Liver, Gallbladder, and Pancreas?", a: "Endodermal outgrowths (buds) from the Foregut." },
            { q: "The Ventral Pancreatic Bud gives rise to...?", a: "Uncinate process and the inferior/posterior part of the Head of the Pancreas." },
            { q: "Annular Pancreas is caused by...?", a: "Abnormal migration of the Ventral Pancreatic Bud, encircling the Duodenum." },
            { q: "Hypertrophic Pyloric Stenosis presentation?", a: "Non-bilious projectile vomiting in a 2–6 week old infant; 'olive-shaped' mass in epigastrium." },
            { q: "Extrahepatic Biliary Atresia clinical hallmark?", a: "Progressive jaundice in the first two weeks of life and dark urine (conjugated hyperbilirubinemia)." },
            { q: "The 1st Pharyngeal Pouch develops into...?", a: "Epithelial lining of the Auditory Tube and Middle Ear cavity." },
            { q: "The 2nd Pharyngeal Pouch develops into...?", a: "Epithelial lining of the Palatine Tonsil crypts." },
            { q: "Derivatives of the 1st Pharyngeal Arch Cartilage (Meckel’s)?", a: "Malleus, Incus, and Mandible." },
            { q: "Derivatives of the 2nd Pharyngeal Arch Cartilage (Reichert’s)?", a: "Stapes, Styloid process, and Lesser horn of the Hyoid." },
            { q: "Treacher Collins Syndrome cause?", a: "1st Pharyngeal Arch neural crest cell migration failure (craniofacial deformities)." },
            { q: "Cleft Lip is a failure of fusion between...?", a: "Maxillary prominence and Medial Nasal prominence." },
            { q: "Cleft Palate is a failure of fusion between...?", a: "Lateral Palatine Shelves (or with the Primary Palate/Nasal Septum)." },
            { q: "Which embryonic structure serves as the 'inducer' for the Metanephric Blastema?", a: "Ureteric Bud." },
            { q: "Exstrophy of the Bladder is associated with a defect in...?", a: "Migration of Mesoderm into the infraumbilical abdominal wall." },
            { q: "The Gubernaculum in females becomes the...?", a: "Ovarian Ligament and Round Ligament of the Uterus." },
            { q: "The Processus Vaginalis normally obliterates to become the...?", a: "Tunica Vaginalis (persistence leads to Congenital Hydrocele or Indirect Inguinal Hernia)." },
            { q: "Hypospadias is a failure of fusion of the...?", a: "Urethral folds (ventral surface of the penis)." },
            { q: "Epispadias is associated with what other congenital defect?", a: "Bladder Exstrophy (opening on the dorsal surface of the penis)." },
            { q: "The Vestibule of the vagina is derived from...?", a: "Urogenital Sinus." },
            { q: "Embryologic origin of the Parafollicular (C) cells of the Thyroid?", a: "Ultimobranchial Body (derived from the 4th/5th Pharyngeal Pouch)." },
            { q: "The Anterior Lobe of the Pituitary arises from...?", a: "Surface Ectoderm (Rathke’s Pouch)." },
            { q: "The Posterior Lobe of the Pituitary arises from...?", a: "Neuroectoderm (Diencephalon)." },
            { q: "Embryologic origin of the Sclerotome?", a: "Somites (Mesoderm); forms vertebrae and ribs." },
            { q: "Embryologic origin of the Dermomyotome?", a: "Somites (Mesoderm); forms skeletal muscle and dermis." },
            { q: "Limb buds appear during which week of development?", a: "Week 4." },
            { q: "The Apical Ectodermal Ridge (AER) regulates...?", a: "Proximodistal growth of the limb." },
            { q: "The Zone of Polarizing Activity (ZPA) regulates...?", a: "Anteroposterior (radioulnar) patterning (via Sonic Hedgehog)." },
            { q: "Syndactyly (fused digits) is a failure of...?", a: "Apoptosis in the digital interzones." },
            { q: "The oxygen saturation is highest in which fetal vessel?", a: "Umbilical Vein (80% O2 saturation)." },
            { q: "The Ductus Venosus shunts blood from the...?", a: "Umbilical vein to the Inferior Vena Cava (bypassing the liver)." },
            { q: "The Foramen Ovale shunts blood from...?", a: "Right Atrium to the Left Atrium." },
            { q: "What physiological change causes the closure of the Foramen Ovale at birth?", a: "Decreased pulmonary vascular resistance increases Left Atrial pressure, pushing the septum primum against the septum secundum." },
            { q: "Which drug is used to close a Patent Ductus Arteriosus (PDA)?", a: "Indomethacin (Prostoglandin inhibitor)." },
            { q: "Which drug is used to keep a PDA open (e.g., in transposition)?", a: "Prostaglandin E1 (PGE1)." },
            { q: "Gastrulation occurs during which week?", a: "Week 3 (formation of the 3 germ layers)." },
            { q: "Definition of the 'Primitive Streak'?", a: "Linear band of thickened epiblast that initiates gastrulation." },
            { q: "Fate of the Trophoblast?", a: "Forms the embryonic part of the Placenta." },
            { q: "Monozygotic twins with 'Monochorionic/Diamniotic' sacs split at what stage?", a: "Blastocyst stage (Days 4–8)." },
            { q: "Most common site of Ectopic Pregnancy?", a: "Ampulla of the Fallopian Tube." },
            { q: "Primitive Hematopoiesis (weeks 3–8) occurs in the...?", a: "Yolk Sac." },
            { q: "Embryologic origin of the Amnion?", a: "Epiblast." },
            { q: "Structure that forms the 'maternal' component of the placenta?", a: "Decidua Basalis." },
            { q: "Structure that forms the 'fetal' component of the placenta?", a: "Chorion Frondosum." },
            { q: "Type of placenta in humans based on layers (e.g., how many layers separate fetal/maternal blood)?", a: "Hemochorial (maternal blood directly bathes fetal chorionic villi)." },
            { q: "Condition where the placenta attaches to the myometrium rather than the decidua?", a: "Placenta Accreta." },
            { q: "What is 'Placenta Previa'?", a: "The placenta develops in the lower uterine segment, covering the internal os of the cervix." },
            { q: "Function of the Syncytiotrophoblast?", a: "Secretes hCG to maintain the corpus luteum; invades the uterine wall." },
            { q: "Significance of the 'Primitive Streak' disappearing?", a: "Usually disappears by the end of week 4; persistence leads to Sacrococcygeal Teratoma." },
            { q: "Embryologic origin of the Neurocranium (flat bones of the skull)?", a: "Neural Crest Cells and Paraxial Mesoderm." },
            { q: "Type of ossification for the flat bones of the skull?", a: "Intramembranous Ossification." },
            { q: "Type of ossification for the long bones and base of the skull?", a: "Endochondral Ossification (cartilage precursor)." },
            { q: "Origin of the Sternum?", a: "Somatic Mesoderm in the ventral body wall (sternal bars)." },
            { q: "Origin of the Epidermis?", a: "Surface Ectoderm." },
            { q: "Origin of the Dermis?", a: "Mesoderm (Dermatome of somites and lateral plate mesoderm)." },
            { q: "What is 'Vernix Caseosa'?", a: "A fatty, white substance (sebum and dead epidermal cells) that protects fetal skin from amniotic fluid." },
            { q: "Failure of the 'Sternal Bars' to fuse results in?", a: "Ectopia Cordis (heart located outside the thorax)." },
            { q: "What is 'Esophageal Stenosis' usually caused by?", a: "Failure of recanalization of the esophagus during the 8th week." },
            { q: "Origin of the Spleen?", a: "Mesoderm of the dorsal mesogastrium (Note: It is NOT a foregut derivative)." },
            { q: "Which organ system is the last to reach functional maturity?", a: "Respiratory System." },
            { q: "'Wandering Spleen' is caused by?", a: "Failure of the dorsal mesentery (lienorenal ligament) to fuse with the posterior body wall." },
            { q: "Cause of 'Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia' (CAH)?", a: "Deficiency of enzymes (usually 21-hydroxylase) in the steroid synthesis pathway." },
            { q: "Cause of 'Polycystic Kidney Disease' (Autosomal Recessive)?", a: "Abnormal development of the collecting ducts." },
            { q: "'Urachal Cyst' location?", a: "Along the line between the umbilicus and the bladder (remnant of the allantois)." },
            { q: "Embryologic origin of the Mammary Glands?", a: "Downgrowths of surface ectoderm (Mammary Ridges/Milk Lines)." },
            { q: "Origin of the Tonsillar Crypts?", a: "Second Pharyngeal Pouch." }
          ]
        },
