            { q: "Nerve supply to the Ethmoid sinuses?", a: "Anterior and Posterior Ethmoidal nerves (V1)." },
            { q: "Nerve supply to the Maxillary sinus?", a: "Superior Alveolar nerves (V2)." },
            { q: "Structure that secretes Aqueous Humor?", a: "Ciliary Process." },
            { q: "Site of drainage for Aqueous Humor?", a: "Canal of Schlemm." },
            { q: "Primary lymphatic drainage of the Midline of the Lower Lip?", a: "Submental lymph nodes." },
            { q: "Which lymph nodes are known as the 'Sentinel nodes' of the head and neck?", a: "Deep Cervical Lymph Nodes (specifically the Jugulodigastric node)." },
            { q: "Boundaries of the 'Infratemporal Fossa'?", a: "Lateral: Ramus of mandible; Medial: Lateral pterygoid plate; Anterior: Posterior Maxilla." },
            { q: "Content of the Infratemporal Fossa?", a: "Muscles of mastication (medial/lateral pterygoids), Maxillary artery, Mandibular nerve (V3), Pterygoid venous plexus." },
            { q: "Clinical significance of the Pterygoid Venous Plexus?", a: "Communicates with the Cavernous Sinus; potential route for dental infections to reach the brain." },
            { q: "Nerve that provides parasympathetic fibers to the Parotid gland?", a: "Lesser Petrosal Nerve (branch of CN IX)." },
            { q: "Nerve that provides parasympathetic fibers to the Submandibular gland?", a: "Chorda Tympani (branch of CN VII)." },
            { q: "Nerve carrying taste from the Epiglottis?", a: "Internal Laryngeal Nerve (CN X)." },
            { q: "Ganglion associated with the Maxillary nerve (V2)?", a: "Pterygopalatine Ganglion." },
            { q: "Ganglion associated with the Mandibular nerve (V3)?", a: "Otic Ganglion." },
            { q: "Effect of Sympathetic stimulation on the eye?", a: "Mydriasis (dilation) and Superior Tarsal muscle contraction (eyelid elevation)." },
            { q: "Effect of Parasympathetic stimulation on the eye?", a: "Miosis (constriction) and Ciliary muscle contraction (accommodation)." },
            { q: "Sensory innervation of the Internal surface of the Tympanic Membrane?", a: "CN IX (Glossopharyngeal)." },
            { q: "Nerve passing through the middle ear across the handle of the Malleus?", a: "Chorda Tympani." },
            { q: "Muscle that opens the Eustachian Tube during swallowing?", a: "Tensor Veli Palatini (CN V3)." },
            { q: "Result of a lesion to the Superior Oblique muscle?", a: "Diplopia when looking down (e.g., walking downstairs) and compensatory head tilt." },
            { q: "Artery that supplies the retina (end artery)?", a: "Central Retinal Artery (branch of Ophthalmic)." },
            { q: "What is a 'Blowout Fracture' of the orbit?", a: "Fracture of the orbital floor (Maxilla), potentially entrapping the Inferior Rectus muscle." },
            { q: "Sign of an Inferior Rectus entrapment?", a: "Upward gaze palsy (inability to look up)." },
            { q: "Cause of 'Frey’s Syndrome'?", a: "Misdirected regrowth of auriculotemporal nerve fibers (parasympathetic to parotid) to sweat glands after parotid surgery (sweating while eating)." },
            { q: "Nerve at risk during a submandibular gland excision?", a: "Marginal Mandibular branch of the Facial Nerve." },
            { q: "Most common site of a Thyroglossal Duct Cyst?", a: "Midline of the neck, usually inferior to the hyoid bone; moves upward with tongue protrusion." },
            { q: "Location of a Branchial Cleft Cyst?", a: "Lateral neck, anterior to the Sternocleidomastoid muscle (remnant of 2nd pharyngeal cleft)." },
            { q: "Muscle responsible for the 'Gag Reflex' elevation of the palate?", a: "Levator Veli Palatini (CN X)." }
          ]
        },
        {
          topic: 'Histology',
          icon: 'microscope',
          cards: [
            { q: "Site of ribosomal RNA (rRNA) synthesis?", a: "Nucleolus." },
            { q: "Organelle responsible for post-translational modification and packaging?", a: "Golgi Apparatus." },
            { q: "Function of Smooth Endoplasmic Reticulum (SER) in hepatocytes?", a: "Detoxification of lipid-soluble drugs and steroid synthesis." },
            { q: "Which organelle is defective in Zellweger Syndrome?", a: "Peroxisome." },
            { q: "Protease-rich complex that degrades ubiquitin-tagged proteins?", a: "Proteasome." },
            { q: "Molecular motor for retrograde transport (toward nucleus)?", a: "Dynein." },
            { q: "Molecular motor for anterograde transport (away from nucleus)?", a: "Kinesin." },
            { q: "Epithelium lining the blood vessels (Endothelium)?", a: "Simple squamous." },
            { q: "Epithelium lining the Oropharynx and Esophagus?", a: "Stratified squamous non-keratinized." },
            { q: "Type of epithelium found in the Urinary Bladder (Urethelium)?", a: "Transitional epithelium." },
            { q: "Location of Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium?", a: "Trachea and large airways (Respiratory epithelium)." },
            { q: "Function of Tight Junctions (Zona Occludens)?", a: "Prevents paracellular movement of solutes." },
            { q: "Junctional complex that uses Cadherins and connects to Actin?", a: "Adherens Junction (Zonula Adherens)." },
            { q: "Junction that provides strong mechanical attachment via Intermediate Filaments?", a: "Desmosome (Macula Adherens)." },
            { q: "Protein defective in Pemphigus Vulgaris?", a: "Desmoglein (component of desmosomes)." },
            { q: "Structure that anchors the basal cell membrane to the basal lamina?", a: "Hemidesmosome." },
            { q: "Protein defective in Bullous Pemphigoid?", a: "Hemidesmosomal proteins (BP180/BP230)." },
            { q: "Major collagen type in Bone and Tendons?", a: "Type I Collagen." },
            { q: "Major collagen type in Hyaline and Elastic Cartilage?", a: "Type II Collagen." },
            { q: "Major collagen type in Reticular fibers (and skin/blood vessels)?", a: "Type III Collagen." },
            { q: "Collagen type defective in Alport Syndrome?", a: "Type IV Collagen (basement membrane)." },
            { q: "Cell responsible for synthesizing extracellular matrix in connective tissue?", a: "Fibroblast." },
            { q: "Tissue type found in the umbilical cord (Wharton's Jelly)?", a: "Mucoid connective tissue." },
            { q: "Location of Brown Adipose Tissue in adults?", a: "Very limited (interscapular, para-aortic); primary function is thermogenesis via Thermogenin." },
            { q: "Functional unit of contraction in skeletal muscle?", a: "Sarcomere (between two Z-lines)." },
            { q: "Protein that covers the myosin-binding site on actin at rest?", a: "Tropomyosin." },
            { q: "Calcium-binding protein in skeletal muscle?", a: "Troponin C." },
            { q: "Connective tissue layer surrounding a whole muscle?", a: "Epimysium." },
            { q: "Connective tissue layer surrounding an individual muscle fiber?", a: "Endomysium." },
            { q: "Histological feature unique to Cardiac Muscle?", a: "Intercalated Discs (contain gap junctions and desmosomes)." },
            { q: "Cells that myelinate axons in the CNS?", a: "Oligodendrocytes." },
            { q: "Cells that myelinate axons in the PNS?", a: "Schwann Cells." },
            { q: "Macrophages of the Central Nervous System?", a: "Microglia." },
            { q: "Cell that secretes osteoid (bone matrix)?", a: "Osteoblast." },
            { q: "Large, multinucleated cell derived from monocytes that resorbs bone?", a: "Osteoclast." },
            { q: "Small channels connecting Lacunae in compact bone?", a: "Canaliculi." },
            { q: "Type of bone formation involving a cartilage model?", a: "Endochondral ossification." },
            { q: "Most abundant White Blood Cell (WBC)?", a: "Neutrophil." },
            { q: "WBC with a kidney-shaped nucleus; precursor to macrophages?", a: "Monocyte." },
            { q: "WBC elevated in parasitic infections and allergic reactions?", a: "Eosinophil." },
            { q: "Precursor cell for Platelets?", a: "Megakaryocyte." },
            { q: "Cells in the stomach that secrete HCl and Intrinsic Factor?", a: "Parietal Cells." },
            { q: "Cells in the stomach that secrete Pepsinogen?", a: "Chief Cells." },
            { q: "Identifying histological feature of the Duodenum?", a: "Brunner’s Glands (submucosal)." },
            { q: "Identifying histological feature of the Ileum?", a: "Peyer’s Patches (lymphoid aggregates)." },
            { q: "Specialized cells in the Liver that store Vitamin A?", a: "Ito Cells (Hepatic Stellate Cells)." },
            { q: "Liver cells responsible for phagocytosis (macrophages)?", a: "Kupffer Cells." },
            { q: "Part of the Nephron where the Macula Densa is located?", a: "Distal Convoluted Tubule." },
            { q: "Cells in the lung that produce Surfactant?", a: "Type II Pneumocytes." },
            { q: "Identifying feature of the Thymus?", a: "Hassall’s Corpuscles." },
            { q: "Three layers of the Adrenal Cortex (outer to inner)?", a: "Zona Glomerulosa, Zona Fasciculata, Zona Reticularis." },
            { q: "Hormone produced by Zona Glomerulosa?", a: "Aldosterone (Mineralocorticoids)." },
            { q: "Cell type that predominates in the Islets of Langerhans (Pancreas)?", a: "Beta cells (produce Insulin)." },
            { q: "Epithelium of the Fallopian Tube?", a: "Simple columnar ciliated." },
            { q: "Layers of the Epidermis (deep to superficial)?", a: "Basale, Spinosum, Granulosum, Lucidum, Corneum." },
            { q: "Mechanoreceptor in the skin for fine/discriminative touch?", a: "Meissner Corpuscle." },
            { q: "Mechanoreceptor in the skin for deep pressure and vibration?", a: "Pacinian Corpuscle." },
            { q: "Appearance of 'Gitter cells' in the brain indicates?", a: "Microglial phagocytosis following an infarct." },
            { q: "Presence of Auer Rods in a blood smear suggests?", a: "Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML)." },
            { q: "What is a 'Clara Cell' (Club Cell)?", a: "Non-ciliated cell in bronchioles that secretes a component of surfactant." },
            { q: "Histological sign of Myocardial Infarction (12-24 hours)?", a: "Contraction bands and early neutrophilic infiltrate." },
            { q: "What are 'Negri Bodies'?", a: "Cytoplasmic inclusions in neurons indicative of Rabies." },
            { q: "What are 'Psammoma Bodies'?", a: "Laminated, calcified whorls seen in Papillary thyroid carcinoma, Meningioma, and Mesothelioma." },
            { q: "Definition of Metaplasia?", a: "Reversible change of one adult cell type to another (e.g., Barrett’s Esophagus)." },
            { q: "Identify the stain: Used for identifying Glycogen and basement membranes?", a: "PAS (Periodic Acid-Schiff)." },
            { q: "Identify the stain: Used for identifying Mycobacterium tuberculosis?", a: "Ziehl-Neelsen (Acid-fast stain)." },
            { q: "Space of Disse location?", a: "Between hepatocytes and sinusoids (contains Ito cells)." },
            { q: "Cell that forms the Blood-Testis Barrier?", a: "Sertoli Cell." },
            { q: "Location of the Podocytes?", a: "Visceral layer of Bowman's Capsule." },
            { q: "Epithelium of the Cornea?", a: "Stratified squamous non-keratinized." },
            { q: "Histology of the Spleen: White Pulp function?", a: "Immune response (contains T and B cells)." },
            { q: "Histology of the Spleen: Red Pulp function?", a: "Blood filtration (removes old RBCs)." },
            { q: "What is the 'Osteon' (Haversian System)?", a: "Structural unit of compact bone." },
            { q: "Difference between Volkmann’s and Haversian canals?", a: "Haversian are longitudinal; Volkmann’s are transverse (connect Haversian canals)." },
            { q: "Tissue in the Epiglottis?", a: "Elastic cartilage." },
            { q: "Histological change in Osteoporosis?", a: "Thinning of trabeculae (decreased bone mass) with normal mineralization." },
            { q: "Identify 'Reed-Sternberg' cells?", a: "Large, binucleated ('owl-eye') cells in Hodgkin Lymphoma." },
            { q: "What is 'Nissl Substance'?", a: "Rough Endoplasmic Reticulum in neurons." },
            { q: "Function of Myoepithelial cells?", a: "Contractile cells that help expel secretions from exocrine glands (e.g., sweat, mammary)." },
            { q: "Epithelium of the Gallbladder?", a: "Simple columnar with microvilli (no goblet cells)." },
            { q: "What is the 'Blood-Air Barrier' composed of?", a: "Type I pneumocyte, fused basement membranes, and endothelial cell." },
            { q: "Principal cell of the Parathyroid gland?", a: "Chief cell (secretes PTH)." },
            { q: "Cell responsible for 'Wallerian Degeneration'?", a: "Schwann cells and macrophages (clearing debris distal to nerve injury)." },
            { q: "Location of 'Purkinje Cells'?", a: "Cerebellum (middle layer of cortex)." },
            { q: "Identify the 'Juxtaglomerular Cells'?", a: "Modified smooth muscle cells of the afferent arteriole that secrete Renin." },
            { q: "What is the 'T-tubule' function?", a: "Conducts action potentials deep into the muscle fiber to the Sarcoplasmic Reticulum." },
            { q: "Structure that produces Melanin?", a: "Melanocyte (found in stratum basale)." },
            { q: "Tissue type in the Intervertebral Disc?", a: "Fibrocartilage." },
            { q: "Lining of the Paranasal Sinuses?", a: "Respiratory epithelium (pseudostratified ciliated columnar)." },
            { q: "Cells of the Anterior Pituitary: Acidophils produce?", a: "GH (Growth Hormone) and Prolactin." },
            { q: "Cells of the Anterior Pituitary: Basophils produce?", a: "B-FLAT (BSH, FSH, LH, ACTH, TSH)." },
            { q: "Identify 'Mallory Bodies'?", a: "Intermediate filament inclusions in hepatocytes (Alcoholic liver disease)." },
            { q: "Epithelium of the Endocervix?", a: "Simple columnar (mucus-secreting)." },
            { q: "Epithelium of the Exocervix?", a: "Stratified squamous non-keratinized." },
            { q: "What are 'C-cells' of the Thyroid?", a: "Parafollicular cells that secrete Calcitonin." },
            { q: "Structure of the Optic Nerve?", a: "It is a CNS tract (covered by meninges and myelinated by oligodendrocytes)." },
            { q: "Function of 'M-cells' in the GI tract?", a: "Antigen-sampling cells over Peyer’s Patches." },
            { q: "Identify 'Lewy Bodies'?", a: "Alpha-synuclein inclusions in neurons (Parkinson's Disease)." },
            { q: "Histology of a Sarcoidosis granuloma?", a: "Non-caseating (no central necrosis) with multinucleated giant cells." },
            { q: "Histology of a Tuberculosis granuloma?", a: "Caseating (central 'cheesy' necrosis)." },
            { q: "Core structure of a Cilia (Axoneme)?", a: "9+2 arrangement of microtubules." },
            { q: "Core structure of a Basal Body?", a: "9 triplets of microtubules (no central pair)." },
            { q: "Primary protein component of Microvilli?", a: "Actin filaments." },
            { q: "Difference between Cilia and Stereocilia?", a: "Cilia are motile (microtubules); Stereocilia are non-motile, long microvilli (actin), found in the epididymis and inner ear." },
            { q: "Functional role of the Glycocalyx?", a: "Cell recognition, attachment, and enzyme localization (e.g., brush border enzymes)." },
            { q: "Define 'Holocrine' secretion and give an example.", a: "The entire cell is discharged (e.g., Sebaceous glands)." },
            { q: "Define 'Apocrine' secretion and give an example.", a: "A portion of the apical cytoplasm is pinched off (e.g., Lactating mammary gland)." },
            { q: "Define 'Merocrine' (Eccrine) secretion and give an example.", a: "Exocytosis without loss of cell membrane (e.g., Parotid gland, most sweat glands)." },
            { q: "What does Hematoxylin stain and what color?", a: "Acidic structures (DNA/RNA in nucleus) purple/blue (Basophilic)." },
            { q: "What does Eosin stain and what color?", a: "Basic structures (Cytoplasmic proteins, collagen) pink/red (Acidophilic)." },
            { q: "Best stain for identifying elastic fibers in the aorta?", a: "Orcein or Verhoeff-Van Gieson (VVG)." },
            { q: "Best stain for identifying lipids in a frozen section?", a: "Sudan Black or Oil Red O." },
            { q: "Identify 'Argyrophilic' fibers.", a: "Reticular fibers (Type III Collagen) that stain black with Silver stains." },
            { q: "Histological appearance of the 'Osteoid Seam'?", a: "The narrow, unmineralized layer of new bone matrix on the surface of bone." },
            { q: "Identity of 'T-lymphocytes' in the lymph node?", a: "Located in the Paracortex." },
            { q: "Identity of 'B-lymphocytes' in the lymph node?", a: "Located in the Germinal Centers and Follicles of the Cortex." },
            { q: "Epithelium of the Prostatic Urethra?", a: "Transitional epithelium." },
            { q: "Epithelium of the Spongy (Penile) Urethra?", a: "Pseudostratified columnar (distally becomes stratified squamous)." },
            { q: "Secretory product of the Pinealocyte?", a: "Melatonin." },
            { q: "Identify 'Calcorporin' (Brain Sand) location.", a: "Calcified concretions in the Pineal Gland (visible on X-rays)." },
            { q: "Histological hallmark of 'Atrophy'?", a: "Decrease in cell size and number; presence of Autophagic vacuoles." },
            { q: "Histological hallmark of 'Hyperplasia'?", a: "Increase in the number of cells in an organ or tissue." },
            { q: "Histological hallmark of 'Dysplasia'?", a: "Disordered growth; loss of cellular uniformity and architectural orientation." },
            { q: "Distinction between 'White Pulp' and 'PALS' in the Spleen?", a: "PALS (Periarteriolar Lymphoid Sheaths) contain T-cells; follicles in the white pulp contain B-cells." },
            { q: "What are 'Juxtaglomerular (JG) cells' modified from?", a: "Smooth muscle cells of the Afferent Arteriole." }
          ]
        },
        {
          topic: 'Cell Biology',
          icon: 'dna',
          cards: [
            { q: "Composition of the plasma membrane?", a: "Phospholipid bilayer + cholesterol + proteins (integral & peripheral) + glycoproteins/glycolipids. Fluid mosaic model (Singer & Nicolson, 1972)." },
            { q: "Function of cholesterol in the plasma membrane?", a: "Stabilizes membrane fluidity — prevents crystallization at low temp, prevents excess fluidity at high temp. Fills gaps between phospholipids." },
            { q: "What determines membrane fluidity?", a: "Increased unsaturated fatty acids → more fluid. Increased cholesterol → stabilizes. Shorter fatty acid chains → more fluid." },
            { q: "Types of membrane proteins?", a: "Integral (transmembrane — span bilayer, removed only by detergents). Peripheral (attached to surface, removed by salt/pH changes)." },
            { q: "Simple diffusion vs. facilitated diffusion?", a: "Simple: no carrier, no energy, down gradient (O₂, CO₂, ethanol, steroids). Facilitated: carrier/channel protein, no energy, down gradient (glucose via GLUT, ions via channels)." },
            { q: "Primary active transport — example?", a: "Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase: pumps 3 Na⁺ out, 2 K⁺ in per ATP. Maintains resting membrane potential." },
            { q: "Secondary active transport — mechanism?", a: "Uses electrochemical gradient established by primary transport (no direct ATP). Symport (same direction) or antiport (opposite direction). Example: Na⁺/glucose symporter in intestine." },
            { q: "Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase — stoichiometry and significance?", a: "3 Na⁺ out : 2 K⁺ in per ATP hydrolyzed. Electrogenic (net positive charge out). Inhibited by ouabain/cardiac glycosides (digoxin)." },
            { q: "What is osmosis?", a: "Movement of water across a semipermeable membrane from low solute (high water) to high solute (low water) concentration. No energy required." },
            { q: "Aquaporins — function and clinical relevance?", a: "Water channel proteins (AQP1–AQP4). AQP2 in collecting duct regulated by ADH. Mutations → nephrogenic diabetes insipidus." },
            { q: "Endocytosis types?", a: "Phagocytosis (large particles, receptor-independent). Pinocytosis (fluid/small solutes). Receptor-mediated endocytosis (clathrin-coated pits, specific ligands — LDL, transferrin, hormones)." },
            { q: "What is receptor-mediated endocytosis?", a: "Ligand binds receptor in clathrin-coated pit → pit invaginates → endosome → ligand degraded in lysosome, receptor recycled to surface. Example: LDL uptake." },
            { q: "Familial hypercholesterolemia — mechanism?", a: "Defective LDL receptor → impaired receptor-mediated endocytosis of LDL → elevated plasma LDL/cholesterol → premature atherosclerosis." },
            { q: "Exocytosis — constitutive vs. regulated?", a: "Constitutive: continuous secretion (ECM proteins, membrane lipids). Regulated: triggered by signal/Ca²⁺ (neurotransmitters, hormones — insulin, digestive enzymes)." },
            { q: "Rough ER — functions?", a: "Synthesis of secretory proteins, membrane proteins, lysosomal proteins. Site of N-linked glycosylation and initial protein folding." },
            { q: "Smooth ER — functions?", a: "Lipid synthesis, steroid hormone synthesis, detoxification (CYP450 enzymes), Ca²⁺ storage (muscle = SR), glycogen metabolism." },
            { q: "Golgi apparatus — functions?", a: "Processing, sorting, packaging of proteins. O-linked glycosylation. Adds mannose-6-phosphate (M6P) to lysosomal enzymes. Ships to lysosomes, plasma membrane, or secretion." },
            { q: "What is the cis vs. trans face of the Golgi?", a: "Cis (receiving) face: receives vesicles from ER. Trans (shipping) face: dispatches vesicles to final destinations." },
            { q: "Mannose-6-phosphate (M6P) — significance?", a: "Targeting signal for lysosomal enzymes; added in Golgi; binds M6P receptor → directed to lysosomes. Absence → I-cell disease." },
            { q: "I-cell disease (Mucolipidosis II) — mechanism?", a: "Defective N-acetylglucosaminyl phosphotransferase → no M6P tag on lysosomal enzymes → enzymes secreted extracellularly instead of to lysosomes → lysosomal storage." },
            { q: "Lysosomes — contents and function?", a: "Contain ~60 acid hydrolases (proteases, lipases, nucleases, glycosidases) active at pH ~5. Degrade macromolecules from endocytosis, phagocytosis, autophagy." },
            { q: "Primary vs. secondary lysosome?", a: "Primary: newly formed from Golgi, contains enzymes not yet active. Secondary: fused with endosome/phagosome — active digestion occurring." },
            { q: "Peroxisomes — functions?", a: "β-oxidation of very long chain fatty acids (VLCFA), oxidation of amino acids, synthesis of bile acids and plasmalogens. Generate and neutralize H₂O₂ (catalase)." },
            { q: "Zellweger syndrome — mechanism?", a: "Defective peroxisome biogenesis (PEX gene mutations) → absent peroxisomes → accumulation of VLCFA → severe neurological damage, liver failure. Lethal in infancy." },
            { q: "Mitochondria — unique features?", a: "Double membrane. Own circular DNA (mtDNA, 37 genes). 2 membranes: outer (porins, permeable) + inner (cristae, impermeable — site of ETC and ATP synthase). Matrix contains TCA cycle enzymes." },
            { q: "Mitochondrial inheritance?", a: "Maternal (mtDNA inherited exclusively from mother's oocyte). Heteroplasmy = mixture of normal and mutant mtDNA in one cell." },
            { q: "What is the nucleolus?", a: "Dense region within nucleus; site of rRNA synthesis and ribosome subunit assembly. Prominent in rapidly dividing/secretory cells. Not membrane-bound." },
            { q: "Ribosome structure?", a: "Eukaryotic: 80S = 60S (28S+5.8S+5S rRNA + ~49 proteins) + 40S (18S rRNA + ~33 proteins). Prokaryotic: 70S (target of antibiotics — tetracycline, aminoglycosides, macrolides, chloramphenicol)." },
            { q: "Signal recognition particle (SRP) — function?", a: "Recognizes N-terminal signal sequence on nascent protein → arrests translation → docks ribosome on rough ER → cotranslational translocation into ER lumen." },
            { q: "Sites of ATP production in eukaryotes?", a: "Cytoplasm (glycolysis: 2 ATP net). Mitochondrial matrix (TCA cycle: GTP). Inner mitochondrial membrane (oxidative phosphorylation: ~32 ATP)." },
            { q: "Electron transport chain (ETC) complexes?", a: "Complex I (NADH dehydrogenase), II (succinate dehydrogenase), III (cytochrome bc1), IV (cytochrome c oxidase). Electrons passed → proton gradient → ATP synthase (Complex V)." },
            { q: "Which complex is inhibited by cyanide?", a: "Complex IV (cytochrome c oxidase). Binds Fe³⁺ of heme a₃ → blocks electron transfer to O₂ → histotoxic hypoxia." },
            { q: "Which complex is inhibited by rotenone?", a: "Complex I (NADH dehydrogenase). Also inhibited by amobarbital. Used as pesticide/fish poison." },
            { q: "What is an uncoupler of oxidative phosphorylation?", a: "Dissipates proton gradient without ATP synthesis → energy released as heat. Example: DNP (dinitrophenol — used illicitly for weight loss), thermogenin (UCP1 in brown fat)." },
            { q: "Thermogenin (UCP1) — location and function?", a: "Uncoupling protein in inner mitochondrial membrane of brown adipose tissue; dissipates proton gradient as heat; important in neonates and hibernating animals." },
            { q: "Heterochromatin vs. euchromatin?", a: "Heterochromatin: densely packed, transcriptionally inactive, stains dark (constitutive = always silent; facultative = Barr body). Euchromatin: loosely packed, transcriptionally active, stains light." },
            { q: "What is a Barr body?", a: "Inactivated X chromosome (facultative heterochromatin) in female somatic cells; appears as dense chromatin mass at nuclear periphery. Number = X chromosomes – 1." },
            { q: "Nucleosome structure?", a: "147 bp DNA wound ~1.75× around octamer of histone proteins (2× H2A, H2B, H3, H4). Linker DNA connects nucleosomes. H1 histone seals DNA on octamer." },
            { q: "Histone acetylation — effect on transcription?", a: "Acetylation of lysine residues on histones → neutralizes positive charge → loosens DNA-histone interaction → euchromatin → transcription ACTIVATED." },
            { q: "Histone methylation — effect?", a: "Context-dependent. H3K4me3 → active transcription. H3K27me3 → transcriptional repression. H3K9me3 → constitutive heterochromatin." },
            { q: "DNA methylation — effect on gene expression?", a: "Methylation of CpG islands (promoter regions) → gene silencing. Hypermethylation of tumor suppressor genes → cancer. Hypomethylation → proto-oncogene activation." },
            { q: "Nuclear pore complex — function?", a: "Regulates nucleocytoplasmic transport. Small molecules diffuse freely; large proteins/RNA require nuclear localization signal (NLS) and importins/exportins (Ran-GTP dependent)." },
            { q: "What is genomic imprinting?", a: "Epigenetic silencing of one parental allele based on parent of origin. Example: IGF2 (paternal expressed), H19 (maternal expressed). Errors → Prader-Willi / Angelman syndrome." },
            { q: "Phases of the cell cycle?", a: "G1 (growth, biosynthesis) → S (DNA synthesis) → G2 (preparation for division) → M (mitosis/meiosis). G0 = quiescent state." },
            { q: "What happens in each gap phase?", a: "G1: cell growth, organelle duplication, cyclin D/E synthesis. G2: cell checks DNA replication, cyclin B/A synthesis. Both are preparation phases." },
            { q: "Cyclins and CDKs — relationship?", a: "Cyclin = regulatory subunit (oscillates). CDK = catalytic subunit (constant). Active complex = cyclin-CDK; phosphorylates target proteins to drive cell cycle progression." },
            { q: "Key cyclin-CDK complexes?", a: "G1: Cyclin D-CDK4/6 (phosphorylates Rb). G1/S: Cyclin E-CDK2. S: Cyclin A-CDK2. M: Cyclin B-CDK1 (MPF = maturation promoting factor)." },
            { q: "What is the restriction point (R point)?", a: "Late G1 checkpoint; once passed, cell is committed to division regardless of mitogenic signals. Controlled by Rb phosphorylation status." },
            { q: "Retinoblastoma protein (Rb) — mechanism?", a: "Hypophosphorylated Rb binds E2F → blocks S phase entry. Cyclin D-CDK4/6 phosphorylates Rb → releases E2F → transcription of S-phase genes (DNA polymerase, thymidine kinase). Loss of Rb → uncontrolled proliferation." },
            { q: "G1/S checkpoint — what does it check?", a: "DNA integrity before replication. ATM/ATR kinases detect damage → activate Chk1/Chk2 → phosphorylate p53 → p21 (CDK inhibitor) → blocks Cyclin E-CDK2 → G1 arrest." },
            { q: "G2/M checkpoint — purpose?", a: "Ensures DNA is fully replicated and undamaged before mitosis. ATM/ATR → Chk1/Chk2 → inhibit Cdc25 phosphatase → CDK1 remains inactive → M-phase blocked." },
            { q: "Spindle assembly checkpoint (SAC) — mechanism?", a: "Monitors kinetochore attachment to spindle microtubules. Unattached kinetochores produce wait-anaphase signal (MCC complex inhibits APC/C) → prevents premature anaphase." },
            { q: "APC/C (Anaphase Promoting Complex/Cyclosome) — function?", a: "E3 ubiquitin ligase; activated at metaphase-anaphase transition; ubiquitinates securin (releases separase → cleaves cohesin) and cyclin B → triggers anaphase and mitotic exit." },
            { q: "p53 — 'guardian of the genome' — mechanism?", a: "Activated by DNA damage (ATM/ATR phosphorylation) → transcribes p21 (CDK inhibitor), GADD45 (DNA repair), BAX (apoptosis). Mutated in >50% of human cancers." },
            { q: "CDK inhibitors (CKIs) — examples?", a: "INK4 family: p16 (CDKN2A), p15, p18, p19 — inhibit CDK4/6. CIP/KIP family: p21 (CDKN1A), p27, p57 — inhibit multiple CDKs." },
            { q: "Stages of mitosis in order?", a: "Prophase → Prometaphase → Metaphase → Anaphase → Telophase → Cytokinesis." },
            { q: "What occurs in prophase?", a: "Chromatin condenses into chromosomes. Mitotic spindle begins forming from centrosomes. Nuclear envelope intact." },
            { q: "What occurs in prometaphase?", a: "Nuclear envelope breaks down. Spindle microtubules attach to kinetochores. Chromosomes begin moving." },
            { q: "What occurs in metaphase?", a: "Chromosomes align at metaphase plate (equatorial plane). All kinetochores attached to spindle. Spindle checkpoint active." },
            { q: "What occurs in anaphase?", a: "Sister chromatids separate (cohesin cleaved by separase). Chromatids pulled to opposite poles. Cell elongates." },
            { q: "What occurs in telophase?", a: "Nuclear envelope reforms around each set of chromosomes. Chromosomes decondense. Contractile ring forms for cytokinesis." },
            { q: "Cytokinesis — mechanism?", a: "Actomyosin contractile ring (actin + myosin II) constricts → cleavage furrow → two daughter cells. Driven by RhoA GTPase." },
            { q: "Centrosome — components and function?", a: "Two centrioles (9 triplet microtubules) + pericentriolar material. MTOC (microtubule organizing center). Duplicates in S phase. Nucleates spindle microtubules from γ-tubulin." },
            { q: "Key differences between mitosis and meiosis?", a: "Meiosis: 2 divisions, produces 4 haploid cells, recombination occurs, only in germ cells. Mitosis: 1 division, 2 diploid cells, no recombination, somatic cells." },
            { q: "What is crossing over (recombination)?", a: "Exchange of homologous DNA segments during prophase I (between non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes) at chiasmata. Creates genetic diversity." },
            { q: "Prophase I substages?", a: "Leptotene (chromosomes condense) → Zygotene (homologs pair, synaptonemal complex forms) → Pachytene (crossing over occurs) → Diplotene (synaptonemal complex dissolves, chiasmata visible) → Diakinesis (final condensation)." },
            { q: "What is the synaptonemal complex?", a: "Protein complex that holds homologous chromosomes together during prophase I (zygotene-pachytene); facilitates recombination. ZIP proteins, SYCP1–3." },
            { q: "What is nondisjunction?", a: "Failure of homologs (meiosis I) or sister chromatids (meiosis II or mitosis) to separate → aneuploidy. Trisomy 21 most commonly arises from meiosis I nondisjunction in the egg." },
            { q: "Meiosis I vs. Meiosis II division type?", a: "Meiosis I: reductional division (separates homologous chromosomes → haploid). Meiosis II: equational division (separates sister chromatids — like mitosis)." },
            { q: "Oogenesis — arrested stages?", a: "Primary oocyte arrested in prophase I (diplotene/dictyate) from fetal life until ovulation. Secondary oocyte arrested in metaphase II; completed only if fertilized." },
            { q: "Apoptosis vs. necrosis — key differences?", a: "Apoptosis: programmed, energy-dependent, cell shrinks, chromatin margination, apoptotic bodies, NO inflammation. Necrosis: uncontrolled, energy-independent, cell swells, membrane rupture, inflammatory response." },
            { q: "Intrinsic (mitochondrial) pathway of apoptosis?", a: "Stress/DNA damage → pro-apoptotic proteins (Bax, Bak) → mitochondrial outer membrane permeabilization (MOMP) → cytochrome c released → apoptosome (cytochrome c + Apaf-1 + caspase-9) → activates caspase-3 (executioner)." },
            { q: "Extrinsic (death receptor) pathway of apoptosis?", a: "FasL/TNF binds Fas/TNFR → recruits FADD → activates caspase-8 (initiator) → activates caspase-3 (executioner) → apoptosis." },
            { q: "Bcl-2 family — pro- vs. anti-apoptotic members?", a: "Anti-apoptotic: Bcl-2, Bcl-xL, Mcl-1 (inhibit MOMP). Pro-apoptotic: Bax, Bak (pore-forming), BH3-only proteins (Bad, Bid, Bim — activators/sensitizers)." },
            { q: "What is the apoptosome?", a: "Heptameric wheel-shaped complex: cytochrome c + Apaf-1 + caspase-9 (inactive) → activates caspase-9 → activates executioner caspase-3/7." },
            { q: "Caspases — initiator vs. executioner?", a: "Initiator: caspase-8 (extrinsic), caspase-9 (intrinsic) — activate executioners. Executioner: caspase-3, -6, -7 — cleave cellular proteins (PARP, lamin, actin)." },
            { q: "What is anoikis?", a: "Apoptosis triggered by loss of cell-ECM attachment (loss of integrin signaling); prevents metastasis. Cancer cells develop anoikis resistance." },
            { q: "What is autophagy?", a: " 'Self-eating' — lysosomal degradation of damaged organelles/proteins via autophagosomes. Survival mechanism during starvation. Regulated by mTOR (inhibits autophagy) and Beclin-1." },
            { q: "Pyroptosis — definition?", a: "Inflammatory programmed cell death via inflammasome activation → caspase-1 → IL-1β/IL-18 secretion + gasdermin D pore formation → cell lysis. Important in infection/sterile inflammation." },
            { q: "Necroptosis — definition?", a: "Programmed necrosis via RIPK1/RIPK3 → MLKL → plasma membrane disruption; inflammatory. Activated when caspase-8 is inhibited." },
            { q: "Classes of cell surface receptors?", a: "G protein-coupled receptors (GPCRs), receptor tyrosine kinases (RTKs), ligand-gated ion channels, cytokine receptors (JAK-STAT), enzyme-linked receptors (guanylyl cyclase)." },
            { q: "G protein cycle?", a: "Inactive Gα-GDP. Ligand → receptor → Gα exchanges GDP for GTP → dissociates from Gβγ → activates effector. Gα hydrolyzes GTP → GDP → reassociates with Gβγ → inactive." },
            { q: "Gs vs. Gi pathways?", a: "Gs: stimulates adenylyl cyclase → ↑cAMP → PKA activation. Gi: inhibits adenylyl cyclase → ↓cAMP. Cholera toxin locks Gs-on. Pertussis toxin locks Gi-off." },
            { q: "cAMP — second messenger pathway?", a: "Adenylyl cyclase → cAMP → activates PKA (protein kinase A) → phosphorylates serine/threonine residues on target proteins. Terminated by phosphodiesterase (converts cAMP → AMP)." },
            { q: "Gq pathway?", a: "Gq → activates phospholipase C-β (PLCβ) → cleaves PIP₂ → IP₃ + DAG. IP₃ → releases Ca²⁺ from ER. DAG → activates PKC." },
            { q: "Receptor tyrosine kinases (RTKs) — mechanism?", a: "Ligand binding → dimerization → autophosphorylation of tyrosine residues → recruits adaptor proteins (Grb2, SOS) → activates Ras → MAP kinase cascade → gene expression." },
            { q: "Ras — oncogene mechanism?", a: "Normally: GTPase activity terminates signaling. Oncogenic mutation (codon 12/13/61) → impaired GTPase → constitutively active Ras-GTP → continuous proliferation signal. Found in ~30% of cancers (K-Ras most common)." },
            { q: "JAK-STAT pathway — used by?", a: "Cytokine receptors (IL, IFN, EPO, GH, prolactin). JAK (Janus kinase) phosphorylates receptor → STAT dimerizes, translocates to nucleus → gene transcription." },
            { q: "PI3K-Akt-mTOR pathway?", a: "RTK → PI3K → PIP₃ → Akt (PKB) activation → mTOR → cell survival, growth, protein synthesis. PTEN phosphatase opposes PI3K. Loss of PTEN → overactive pathway → cancer." },
            { q: "What is second messenger?", a: "Intracellular signaling molecule produced in response to first messenger (ligand). Examples: cAMP, cGMP, IP₃, DAG, Ca²⁺, arachidonic acid." },
            { q: "Cholera toxin mechanism?", a: "ADP-ribosylates Gsα → locks in active (GTP-bound) state → constitutive adenylyl cyclase activation → ↑↑cAMP in intestinal epithelium → massive Cl⁻/water secretion → rice-water diarrhea." },
            { q: "Pertussis toxin mechanism?", a: "ADP-ribosylates Giα → prevents Gi activation → cannot inhibit adenylyl cyclase → ↑cAMP in respiratory epithelium → whooping cough pathogenesis." },
            { q: "Three components of the cytoskeleton?", a: "Microfilaments (actin, 7 nm), Intermediate filaments (8–12 nm), Microtubules (tubulin, 25 nm)." },
            { q: "Actin microfilaments — structure and functions?", a: "F-actin (filamentous) polymer of G-actin monomers. Functions: cell shape, motility, cytokinesis (contractile ring), microvilli, muscle contraction." },
            { q: "Intermediate filaments — types and locations?", a: "Vimentin (mesenchymal), Desmin (muscle), GFAP (astrocytes), Neurofilaments (neurons), Keratin (epithelial), Lamin (nuclear envelope). Provide structural stability." },
            { q: "Microtubule structure?", a: "13 protofilaments of α/β-tubulin heterodimers; hollow tube 25 nm diameter. Dynamic instability: GTP-tubulin polymerizes (+end); GTP hydrolysis → GDP-tubulin → depolymerization." },
            { q: "Drugs affecting microtubules?", a: "Stabilize (prevent depolymerization): Taxol (paclitaxel) — treat cancer. Destabilize (prevent polymerization): Colchicine (gout, blocks neutrophil migration), Vinca alkaloids (vincristine, vinblastine — cancer), Griseofulvin (fungal infections)." },
            { q: "Dynein vs. kinesin — direction of movement?", a: "Kinesin: moves cargo toward plus end of microtubule (anterograde, toward cell periphery). Dynein: moves toward minus end (retrograde, toward nucleus/MTOC)." },
            { q: "What is axonal transport?", a: "Anterograde (kinesin): neurotransmitter vesicles, organelles — soma to axon terminal. Retrograde (dynein): recycled membrane, neurotrophic factors, viruses (HSV, rabies, polio) — terminal to soma." },
            { q: "Kartagener syndrome (Primary ciliary dyskinesia)?", a: "Defective dynein arms in cilia → immotile cilia → bronchiectasis, sinusitis, situs inversus, male infertility (immotile sperm). AR inheritance." },
            { q: "Actin-related diseases?", a: "Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome: WASp protein (regulates actin branching via Arp2/3) defective → poor T cell activation. Lisfranc fracture: unrelated. Thymic actin dysregulation → immunodeficiency." },
            { q: "Types of cell junctions (apical to basal)?", a: "Tight junction (zonula occludens) → Adherens junction (zonula adherens) → Desmosome (macula adherens) → Gap junction. Hemidesmosomes attach cell to basement membrane." },
            { q: "Tight junctions — structure and function?", a: "Claudins + occludins seal intercellular space. Prevent paracellular diffusion (barrier function). Establish cell polarity (apical vs. basolateral). Absent in fenestrated capillaries." },
            { q: "Adherens junctions — components?", a: "E-cadherin (transmembrane, Ca²⁺-dependent) + catenins (α, β, p120) + actin. Mediate cell-cell adhesion. Loss of E-cadherin → epithelial-mesenchymal transition (EMT) in cancer." },
            { q: "Desmosomes — components and function?", a: "Desmoglein/desmocollin (cadherins) + desmoplakin + plakophilin + intermediate filaments (keratin). Spot-welds between cells; resist shear forces. Absent in pemphigus vulgaris." },
            { q: "Pemphigus vulgaris — mechanism?", a: "Autoantibodies against desmoglein-1 (superficial) and desmoglein-3 (deep) → desmosome destruction → acantholysis → intraepidermal blisters. Nikolsky sign positive." },
            { q: "Bullous pemphigoid — mechanism?", a: "Autoantibodies against BPAG1/BPAG2 (BP180) at hemidesmosomes → separation at dermal-epidermal junction → subepidermal blisters. Nikolsky sign negative." },
            { q: "Gap junctions — components and function?", a: "Connexins form connexons (hexamers); two connexons align → channel between cells. Allow direct passage of ions, small molecules (<1 kDa). Coordinate cardiac/smooth muscle, embryonic development." },
            { q: "Extracellular matrix (ECM) — major components?", a: "Collagen (tensile strength), Elastin (elasticity), Fibronectin (cell adhesion to ECM), Laminin (basement membrane), Proteoglycans (hydration/compression resistance), Hyaluronic acid." },
            { q: "Collagen synthesis — steps?", a: "Preprocollagen (RER) → signal peptide cleaved → hydroxylation of Pro/Lys (Vit C required) → glycosylation → triple helix formation (procollagen) → secretion → cleavage of propeptides → tropocollagen → fibril assembly." },
            { q: "Vitamin C deficiency (Scurvy) — collagen connection?", a: "Vitamin C required for prolyl/lysyl hydroxylase → without it, procollagen cannot form stable triple helix → weak collagen → perifollicular hemorrhage, poor wound healing, gum bleeding." },
            { q: "Ehlers-Danlos syndrome — mechanism?", a: "Defective collagen synthesis or processing (types I, III, V most common). Hyperextensible skin, hypermobile joints, easy bruising. Type IV (COL3A1 mutation) → vascular rupture." },
            { q: "Marfan syndrome — mechanism?", a: "FBN1 mutation (fibrillin-1) → defective microfibril scaffolding for elastin → ↑TGF-β activity → aortic aneurysm/dissection, tall stature, ectopia lentis, arachnodactyly. AD inheritance." },
            { q: "Integrins — function?", a: "Transmembrane receptors linking ECM (fibronectin, laminin, collagen) to intracellular actin cytoskeleton. Bidirectional signaling ('inside-out' and 'outside-in'). Important for cell migration, survival, adhesion." },
            { q: "Enzymes in DNA replication?", a: "Helicase (unwinds), Primase (RNA primer), DNA polymerase III (elongation, 5'→3'), DNA polymerase I (removes primer, fills gap), DNA ligase (seals nicks), Topoisomerase I/II (relieves supercoiling), SSB proteins (stabilize single strands)." },
            { q: "DNA polymerase — key properties?", a: "Synthesizes only 5'→3'. Requires primer (RNA). Has 3'→5' exonuclease (proofreading). Cannot initiate new strand de novo." },
            { q: "Leading vs. lagging strand?", a: "Leading: continuous synthesis toward replication fork (5'→3', one primer). Lagging: discontinuous — Okazaki fragments synthesized away from fork, each needing a primer; joined by ligase." },
            { q: "Okazaki fragments — size?", a: "~100–200 nucleotides in eukaryotes; ~1000–2000 in prokaryotes. Removed by RNase H / DNA Pol I, filled in, then ligated." },
            { q: "Telomeres — structure and function?", a: "TTAGGG repeats at chromosome ends. Protect against chromosome shortening. Maintained by telomerase (reverse transcriptase using RNA template). Absent in most somatic cells → telomere shortening → cellular senescence." },
            { q: "Telomerase — clinical significance?", a: "Active in stem cells, germ cells, cancer cells (85–90% of tumors). Enables unlimited replication (immortalization). Target of some anticancer strategies." },
            { q: "Origins of replication in eukaryotes?", a: "Multiple origins (ARS — autonomously replicating sequences) fired simultaneously → rapid replication of large genomes. Prokaryotes: single origin (oriC)." },
            { q: "RNA polymerases in eukaryotes?", a: "RNA Pol I: rRNA (28S, 18S, 5.8S — nucleolus). RNA Pol II: mRNA + most snRNA (nucleus). RNA Pol III: tRNA, 5S rRNA, small RNAs." },
            { q: "mRNA processing (pre-mRNA → mature mRNA)?", a: "5' 7-methylguanosine cap (added first) → intron removal by spliceosome → 3' poly-A tail added (~200 A residues). Cap and tail needed for nuclear export, ribosome binding, stability." },
            { q: "Spliceosome — components?", a: "snRNAs (U1, U2, U4, U5, U6) + snRNP proteins form spliceosome. Recognizes GU at 5' splice site and AG at 3' splice site. Intron removed as lariat structure." },
            { q: "Alternative splicing — significance?", a: "One gene → multiple mRNA/protein isoforms by including/excluding different exons. Dramatically expands proteome. Example: troponin isoforms, DSCAM (38,000 isoforms)." },
            { q: "Genetic code properties?", a: "Triplet, non-overlapping, comma-free, degenerate (redundant — multiple codons per amino acid), universal (with few exceptions), unambiguous (one codon = one AA)." },
            { q: "Start codon and initiator amino acid in eukaryotes?", a: "AUG → Methionine (Met). In prokaryotes: AUG → formyl-methionine (fMet). Methionine often cleaved post-translationally." },
            { q: "Stop codons?", a: "UAA (\"ochre\"), UAG (\"amber\"), UGA (\"opal/umber\"). No tRNA reads them; release factors (eRF1, eRF3) catalyze polypeptide release." },
            { q: "Translation initiation in eukaryotes?", a: "43S PIC (40S + eIF2-GTP-Met-tRNA) scans 5' cap → finds AUG → 60S joins → 80S ribosome. Kozak sequence context (ACCAUGG) optimal." },
            { q: "Antibiotics targeting translation?", a: "30S inhibitors: Aminoglycosides (misreading), Tetracyclines (block tRNA entry). 50S inhibitors: Chloramphenicol (peptidyl transferase), Macrolides (translocation), Linezolid (initiation), Clindamycin (translocation)." },
            { q: "Post-translational modifications?", a: "Signal peptide cleavage, glycosylation (N-linked in ER, O-linked in Golgi), phosphorylation, ubiquitination, acetylation, methylation, SUMOylation, GPI anchoring, disulfide bond formation." },
            { q: "Types of DNA damage and repair mechanisms?", a: "Single-base damage → Base excision repair (BER). Bulky adducts → Nucleotide excision repair (NER). Mismatches → Mismatch repair (MMR). Double-strand breaks → Homologous recombination (HR) or NHEJ." },
            { q: "Nucleotide excision repair (NER) — clinical significance?", a: "Repairs bulky adducts (UV-induced thymine dimers). Defect → Xeroderma pigmentosum (XP): extreme UV sensitivity, skin cancers, neurodegeneration. XPA–XPG proteins." },
            { q: "Mismatch repair (MMR) — clinical significance?", a: "MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2 proteins. Defects → Lynch syndrome (HNPCC): colorectal, endometrial, ovarian cancer. Microsatellite instability (MSI) marker." },
            { q: "Homologous recombination (HR) — when and key proteins?", a: "Repairs double-strand breaks in S/G2 phase (sister chromatid template available). Key proteins: BRCA1, BRCA2, RAD51. BRCA mutations → breast/ovarian cancer risk." },
            { q: "Non-homologous end joining (NHEJ) — characteristics?", a: "Repairs DSBs in G1 phase (no template). Error-prone (can cause deletions/mutations). Key proteins: Ku70/80, DNA-PKcs, Artemis, Ligase IV. Responsible for V(D)J recombination." },
            { q: "Base excision repair (BER) — mechanism?", a: "DNA glycosylase removes damaged base → AP site → AP endonuclease cleaves backbone → DNA polymerase β fills gap → ligase seals. Repairs oxidative damage (8-oxoguanine), deamination (uracil)." },
            { q: "Totipotent vs. pluripotent vs. multipotent?", a: "Totipotent: can form entire organism including placenta (fertilized egg, first 4 cells). Pluripotent: all cell types except placenta (inner cell mass → ESCs). Multipotent: limited lineages (HSCs, MSCs)." },
            { q: "What are induced pluripotent stem cells (iPSCs)?", a: "Somatic cells reprogrammed to pluripotency by introduction of transcription factors (Yamanaka factors: Oct4, Sox2, Klf4, c-Myc). Nobel Prize 2012 (Yamanaka + Gurdon)." },
            { q: "What is the Hayflick limit?", a: "~50–70 population doublings before replicative senescence in normal human somatic cells. Due to progressive telomere shortening. Cancer cells bypass via telomerase reactivation." },
            { q: "Hematopoietic stem cell (HSC) — location?", a: "Fetal: yolk sac → fetal liver → spleen → bone marrow. Adult: bone marrow (long bones in adults, axial skeleton predominantly). Express CD34, CD133, Lin⁻." },
            { q: "Proto-oncogene vs. oncogene?", a: "Proto-oncogene: normal gene promoting growth. Oncogene: mutated/overexpressed proto-oncogene that drives uncontrolled proliferation. Activation by point mutation, amplification, translocation." },
            { q: "Tumor suppressor genes — mechanism of inactivation?", a: "Two-hit hypothesis (Knudson): both alleles must be inactivated. First hit: germline or somatic mutation. Second hit: LOH, deletion, methylation. Examples: Rb, p53, APC, BRCA1/2." },
            { q: "Hallmarks of cancer (Hanahan & Weinberg)?", a: "Sustained proliferative signaling, evading growth suppressors, resisting cell death, enabling replicative immortality, inducing angiogenesis, activating invasion & metastasis. Added: reprogramming energy metabolism, evading immune destruction." },
            { q: "What is the Warburg effect?", a: "Cancer cells preferentially use aerobic glycolysis (not OXPHOS) even in presence of O₂ → produces lactate. Provides biosynthetic precursors for rapid cell growth. Basis of FDG-PET imaging." },
            { q: "Angiogenesis in tumors — key mediator?", a: "VEGF (vascular endothelial growth factor) — released by hypoxic tumor cells via HIF-1α. Drives new vessel formation. Target of bevacizumab (anti-VEGF antibody)." },
            { q: "What is HIF-1α?", a: "Hypoxia-inducible factor. In normoxia: prolyl hydroxylases hydroxylate HIF-1α → VHL ubiquitinates → proteasomal degradation. In hypoxia: hydroxylases inactive → HIF-1α stable → activates VEGF, EPO, glycolytic genes." },
            { q: "VHL mutation — consequence?", a: "Von Hippel-Lindau disease: VHL cannot ubiquitinate HIF-1α → constitutive HIF-1α activity → overexpression of VEGF → clear cell RCC, hemangioblastomas, pheochromocytoma." },
            { q: "Size of major cell components?", a: "Nucleus: 5–10 μm. Mitochondria: 1–10 μm. Lysosome: 0.1–1.2 μm. Ribosome: ~25 nm. Plasma membrane thickness: ~7.5 nm." },
            { q: "Human genome size?", a: "~3.2 billion base pairs (haploid). ~20,000–25,000 protein-coding genes. ~1.5% of genome is coding. ~98.5% non-coding (introns, regulatory, repetitive)." },
            { q: "Cell cycle duration (typical mammalian cell)?", a: "Total: ~24 hours. G1: ~11 hours. S: ~8 hours. G2: ~4 hours. M: ~1 hour." },
            { q: "ATP yield of complete glucose oxidation?", a: "Glycolysis: 2 ATP. TCA cycle: 2 GTP. Oxidative phosphorylation: ~28–30 ATP. Total: ~30–32 ATP per glucose (updated from classic 36–38)." },
            { q: "Eukaryotic vs. prokaryotic ribosome sizes?", a: "Eukaryotic: 80S (60S + 40S). Prokaryotic: 70S (50S + 30S). Mitochondrial: 55S." },
            { q: "Half-life of mRNA in mammalian cells?", a: "Varies widely: unstable mRNAs (c-fos, cytokines): minutes. Stable (globin): hours to days. AU-rich elements (AREs) in 3'UTR promote rapid degradation." }
          ]
        },
        {
          topic: 'Upper Limb',
          icon: 'hand',
          cards: [
            { q: "Most frequently fractured bone in the body?", a: "Clavicle (usually at the junction of its medial 2/3 and lateral 1/3)." },
            { q: "Primary function of the Serratus Anterior?", a: "Protraction and upward rotation of the scapula; holds scapula against the thoracic wall." },
            { q: "Nerve damaged in 'Winged Scapula'?", a: "Long Thoracic Nerve (C5–C7)." },
            { q: "Muscles of the Rotator Cuff (SITS)?", a: "Supraspinatus, Infraspinatus, Teres Minor, Subscapularis." },
            { q: "Most common rotator cuff muscle to be injured?", a: "Supraspinatus (often due to impingement under the acromion)." },
            { q: "Action of the Supraspinatus?", a: "Initiates abduction of the arm (first 15°)." },
            { q: "Action of the Infraspinatus and Teres Minor?", a: "Lateral (external) rotation of the arm." },
            { q: "Action of the Subscapularis?", a: "Medial (internal) rotation of the arm." },
            { q: "Contents of the Deltopectoral Triangle?", a: "Cephalic vein, Deltoid branch of the thoracoacromial artery." },
            { q: "Nerve innervating the Pectoralis Major?", a: "Medial and Lateral Pectoral nerves." },
            { q: "Nerve innervating the Pectoralis Minor?", a: "Medial Pectoral nerve (C8–T1)." },
            { q: "Root values of the Long Thoracic Nerve?", a: "C5, C6, C7 ('C5-6-7 reach for heaven')." },
            { q: "Artery that becomes the Axillary Artery at the lateral border of the 1st rib?", a: "Subclavian Artery." },
            { q: "Nerve most at risk in a mid-shaft humeral fracture?", a: "Radial Nerve (travels in the spiral/radial groove)." },
            { q: "Nerve most at risk in a surgical neck of the humerus fracture?", a: "Axillary Nerve." },
            { q: "Nerve most at risk in a supracondylar humeral fracture?", a: "Median Nerve." },
            { q: "Nerve most at risk in a medial epicondyle fracture?", a: "Ulnar Nerve." },
            { q: "Erb-Duchenne Palsy involves which roots?", a: "C5–C6 (Upper Trunk)." },
            { q: "Physical presentation of Erb-Duchenne Palsy?", a: "'Waitor's tip' hand (adducted, medially rotated arm, extended elbow)." },
            { q: "Klumpke Palsy involves which roots?", a: "C8–T1 (Lower Trunk)." },
            { q: "Physical presentation of Klumpke Palsy?", a: "'Claw hand' (total clawing of all digits due to loss of intrinsic hand muscles)." },
            { q: "Sensory innervation of the medial arm and axilla?", a: "Intercostobrachial Nerve (T2)." },
            { q: "Contents of the Quadrangular Space?", a: "Axillary nerve and Posterior circumflex humeral artery." },
            { q: "Boundaries of the Triangular Space?", a: "Teres major, Teres minor, Long head of triceps." },
            { q: "Primary flexor of the elbow?", a: "Brachialis (Biceps is a powerful supinator)." },
            { q: "Nerve innervating the anterior compartment of the arm?", a: "Musculocutaneous Nerve." },
            { q: "Muscle that pierces the Musculocutaneous Nerve?", a: "Coracobrachialis." },
            { q: "Nerve innervating the Brachioradialis?", a: "Radial Nerve (despite it being a flexor)." },
            { q: "Contents of the Cubital Fossa (Lateral to Medial)?", a: "Radial nerve, Biceps tendon, Brachial artery, Median nerve (TAN: Tendon, Artery, Nerve)." },
            { q: "Common origin for superficial flexors of the forearm?", a: "Medial Epicondyle (Common Flexor Origin)." },
            { q: "Common origin for superficial extensors of the forearm?", a: "Lateral Epicondyle (Common Extensor Origin)." },
            { q: "Nerve affected in 'Tennis Elbow' (Lateral Epicondylitis)?", a: "Radial nerve (specifically Extensor Carpi Radialis Brevis)." },
            { q: "Only forearm flexor not innervated by the Median nerve?", a: "Flexor Carpi Ulnaris (and the medial half of Flexor Digitorum Profundus)." },
            { q: "Nerve passing through the two heads of the Pronator Teres?", a: "Median Nerve." },
            { q: "Nerve passing through the two heads of the Flexor Carpi Ulnaris?", a: "Ulnar Nerve." },
            { q: "Structure that provides the majority of the blood supply to the superficial palmar arch?", a: "Ulnar Artery." },
            { q: "Contents of the Carpal Tunnel?", a: "4 tendons of FDS, 4 tendons of FDP, 1 tendon of FPL, and the Median nerve." },
            { q: "Most commonly fractured carpal bone?", a: "Scaphoid (risk of avascular necrosis)." },
            { q: "Most commonly dislocated carpal bone?", a: "Lunate." },
            { q: "Boundaries of the Anatomical Snuffbox?", a: "Abductor pollicis longus, Extensor pollicis brevis (lateral); Extensor pollicis longus (medial)." },
            { q: "Palpable artery in the Anatomical Snuffbox?", a: "Radial Artery." },
            { q: "Muscle responsible for opposition of the thumb?", a: "Opponens Pollicis (Median nerve)." },
            { q: "Nerve innervating the Adductor Pollicis?", a: "Ulnar Nerve (Deep branch)." },
            { q: "Innervation of the Lumbricals?", a: "1st and 2nd (Median nerve); 3rd and 4th (Ulnar nerve)." },
            { q: "Action of the Dorsal Interossei (DAB)?", a: "Abduction of fingers." },
            { q: "Action of the Palmar Interossei (PAD)?", a: "Adduction of fingers." },
            { q: "Clinical sign of Ulnar nerve injury at the wrist?", a: "'Ulnar claw' (hyperextension of MCP and flexion of IP joints in digits 4-5)." },
            { q: "Cause of 'Ape Hand' deformity?", a: "Median nerve injury (loss of thumb opposition)." },
            { q: "Difference between 'Hand of Benediction' and 'Ulnar Claw'?", a: "Hand of Benediction is seen only when attempting to make a fist (Median nerve); Ulnar Claw is seen at rest." },
            { q: "Primary lymphatic drainage of the lateral hand and forearm?", a: "Infraclavicular/Axillary nodes (via cephalic vein)." },
            { q: "Which ligament is most important for maintaining the stability of the Acromioclavicular (AC) joint?", a: "Coracoclavicular ligament (composed of Conoid and Trapezoid ligaments)." },
            { q: "Direction of the most common shoulder dislocation?", a: "Anteroinferior (associated with Axillary nerve injury)." },
            { q: "What is a Bankart lesion?", a: "Avulsion of the anterior-inferior glenoid labrum." },
            { q: "What is a Hill-Sachs lesion?", a: "Compression fracture of the posterolateral humeral head during dislocation." },
            { q: "Ligament that holds the head of the radius against the radial notch of the ulna?", a: "Annular ligament." },
            { q: "Pathology of 'Nursemaid’s Elbow'?", a: "Subluxation of the radial head through the annular ligament." },
            { q: "Which carpal bone is the first to ossify?", a: "Capitate." },
            { q: "Nerve supply to the Elbow joint?", a: "Musculocutaneous, Median, Ulnar, and Radial nerves (Hilton’s Law)." },
            { q: "Principal structure preventing superior displacement of the humeral head?", a: "Coracoacromial Arch (Coracoid process + Coracoacromial ligament + Acromion)." },
            { q: "Function of the Glenoid Labrum?", a: "Deepens the glenoid cavity to increase joint stability." },
            { q: "Branches of the 1st part of the Axillary Artery?", a: "Superior Thoracic Artery." },
            { q: "Branches of the 2nd part of the Axillary Artery?", a: "Thoracoacromial Artery and Lateral Thoracic Artery." },
            { q: "Branches of the 3rd part of the Axillary Artery?", a: "Subscapular Artery, Anterior and Posterior Circumflex Humeral Arteries." },
            { q: "Arteries involved in the Scapular Anastomosis?", a: "Suprascapular, Dorsal Scapular, and Circumflex Scapular arteries." },
            { q: "Clinical significance of the Scapular Anastomosis?", a: "Provides collateral circulation if the subclavian or axillary artery is ligated." },
            { q: "Origin of the Profunda Brachii (Deep Artery of the Arm)?", a: "Brachial Artery (distal to the Teres Major)." },
            { q: "Artery that travels with the Radial Nerve in the spiral groove?", a: "Profunda Brachii." },
            { q: "Vein used most commonly for venipuncture in the cubital fossa?", a: "Median Cubital Vein." },
            { q: "Nerve that accompanies the Cephalic Vein in the forearm?", a: "Lateral Antebrachial Cutaneous Nerve." },
            { q: "Nerve that accompanies the Basilic Vein in the forearm?", a: "Medial Antebrachial Cutaneous Nerve." },
            { q: "Result of Allen’s Test?", a: "Assesses the patency of the Ulnar and Radial arteries before arterial blood gas sampling." },
            { q: "Primary arterial supply to the Deep Palmar Arch?", a: "Radial Artery." },
            { q: "Muscle that separates the Ulnar Artery from the Ulnar Nerve at the wrist?", a: "Flexor Carpi Ulnaris." },
            { q: "Lymphatic drainage of the medial side of the hand/forearm?", a: "Cubital (supratrochlear) nodes, then to Lateral axillary nodes." },
            { q: "Result of Axillary Node Dissection on lymphatic drainage?", a: "Risk of Lymphedema in the upper limb." },
            { q: "Action of the Brachioradialis?", a: "Flexion of the elbow in the mid-prone position." },
            { q: "Muscle that initiates abduction from 15° to 90°?", a: "Deltoid (Axillary nerve)." },
            { q: "Muscles required for abduction above 90°?", a: "Serratus Anterior and Trapezius (upward rotation of scapula)." },
            { q: "What is 'Saturday Night Palsy'?", a: "Compression of the Radial Nerve in the axilla or spiral groove (presents with wrist drop)." },
            { q: "Clinical test for the Supraspinatus?", a: "'Empty Can' test (Jobe's test)." },
            { q: "Clinical test for the Serratus Anterior?", a: "Pushing against a wall (checks for winging of the scapula)." },
            { q: "What is 'Dupuytren’s Contracture'?", a: "Thickening and shrinking of the palmar aponeurosis (pulls 4th and 5th fingers into flexion)." },
            { q: "What is 'Mallet Finger'?", a: "Avulsion of the extensor tendon from the distal phalanx (inability to extend DIP joint)." },
            { q: "What is 'Boutonnière Deformity'?", a: "Central slip rupture; PIP flexion with DIP hyperextension." },
            { q: "What is 'Swan Neck Deformity'?", a: "PIP hyperextension with DIP flexion (common in Rheumatoid Arthritis)." },
            { q: "Nerve supply to the thenar eminence?", a: "Recurrent branch of the Median Nerve ('Million Dollar Nerve')." },
            { q: "Nerve supply to the hypothenar eminence?", a: "Deep branch of the Ulnar Nerve." },
            { q: "Action of the Opponens Digiti Minimi?", a: "Pulls the 5th metacarpal forward to deepen the hollow of the palm." },
            { q: "Nerve affected in 'Crutch Palsy'?", a: "Posterior cord of the brachial plexus (specifically Radial nerve)." },
            { q: "Site of ulnar nerve compression in the wrist?", a: "Guyon's Canal (Ulnar Tunnel)." },
            { q: "Fascia that envelopes the Pectoralis Minor and Subclavius?", a: "Clavipectoral Fascia." },
            { q: "Structure that pierces the Clavipectoral Fascia?", a: "Cephalic vein, Thoracoacromial artery, Lateral pectoral nerve." },
            { q: "Muscle that forms the floor of the Anatomical Snuffbox?", a: "Scaphoid and Trapezium." },
            { q: "Function of the Pronator Quadratus?", a: "Initiates pronation; deepest muscle of the anterior forearm." },
            { q: "Action of the Anconeus?", a: "Assists Triceps in elbow extension; abducts the ulna during pronation." },
            { q: "Sensory distribution of the Axillary Nerve?", a: "'Regimental Badge' area (over the lower part of the deltoid)." },
            { q: "Nerve that provides sensation to the palm (lateral 3.5 digits)?", a: "Palmar cutaneous branch of the Median Nerve (passes over the carpal tunnel)." },
            { q: "Why is sensation spared in the palm in Carpal Tunnel Syndrome?", a: "The palmar cutaneous branch arises proximal to and passes superficial to the flexor retinaculum." },
            { q: "Froment’s Sign indicates palsy of which nerve?", a: "Ulnar Nerve (tests Adductor Pollicis; patient uses FPL/Median nerve to compensate)." },
            { q: "Contents of the Triangular Interval?", a: "Radial Nerve and Profunda Brachii artery." },
            { q: "Which bony landmark is used to locate the second rib?", a: "Sternal Angle (Angle of Louis)." },
            { q: "Which tendon is most prominent on the anterior wrist during resisted flexion and adduction?", a: "Palmaris Longus (absent in about 15% of the population)." },
            { q: "The 'H-shaped' venous pattern in the cubital fossa is formed by which three veins?", a: "Cephalic, Basilic, and Median Cubital veins." },
            { q: "At what level does the Brachial Artery usually bifurcate into the Radial and Ulnar arteries?", a: "Neck of the radius (in the cubital fossa)." },
            { q: "Which nerve can be palpated posterior to the Medial Epicondyle?", a: "Ulnar Nerve ('Funny bone')." },
            { q: "What is a 'Colles Fracture'?", a: "Distal radius fracture with posterior (dorsal) displacement of the distal fragment ('Dinner fork deformity')." },
            { q: "What is a 'Smith Fracture'?", a: "Distal radius fracture with anterior (palmar) displacement ('Grasp-style' deformity)." },
            { q: "What is 'Fat Pad Sign' (Sail Sign) on an elbow X-ray?", a: "Indicates an occult fracture (often radial head in adults or supracondylar in children) causing joint effusion." },
            { q: "What is a 'Monteggia Fracture'?", a: "Fracture of the proximal third of the Ulna with dislocation of the proximal Radial head." },
            { q: "What is a 'Galeazzi Fracture'?", a: "Fracture of the distal third of the Radius with dislocation of the Distal Radioulnar Joint." },
            { q: "Which muscle forms the 'Mobile Wad of Three' in the lateral forearm?", a: "Brachioradialis, Extensor Carpi Radialis Longus, and Extensor Carpi Radialis Brevis." },
            { q: "What is the 'Extensor Expansion' (Dorsal Hood)?", a: "A specialized fibrous attachment on the back of the fingers where extensor tendons, lumbricals, and interossei insert." },
            { q: "Function of the Vincula Brevia and Longa?", a: "Small vascular bands that carry blood supply to the flexor tendons within the digital sheaths." },
            { q: "What structure forms the roof of the Carpal Tunnel?", a: "Flexor Retinaculum (Transverse Carpal Ligament)." },
            { q: "Which muscle is the primary supinator when the elbow is extended?", a: "Supinator muscle (Radial nerve)." },
            { q: "Which nerve is responsible for 'Power Grip' (e.g., holding a hammer)?", a: "Ulnar Nerve (controls most intrinsic hand muscles)." },
            { q: "Which nerve is responsible for 'Precision Grip' (e.g., holding a needle)?", a: "Median Nerve (controls the thenar muscles and lateral lumbricals)." },
            { q: "In a 'Claw Hand' deformity, why is the clawing more pronounced in a distal ulnar nerve lesion than a proximal one?", a: "'Ulnar Paradox': A distal lesion leaves the Flexor Digitorum Profundus intact, which pulls the IP joints into deeper flexion." },
            { q: "Nerve providing sensation to the 'Anatomical Snuffbox'?", a: "Superficial branch of the Radial Nerve." },
            { q: "What is 'Volkmann’s Ischemic Contracture'?", a: "Permanent shortening of forearm muscles (usually flexors) following a supracondylar fracture that causes brachial artery obstruction/compartment syndrome." },
            { q: "Common site of compression for the Posterior Interosseous Nerve (PIN)?", a: "Arcade of Frohse (superior border of the supinator muscle)." },
            { q: "What is 'Wartenberg’s Syndrome'?", a: "Compression of the superficial branch of the radial nerve, causing sensory loss/pain on the radial dorsum of the hand." },
            { q: "Nerve involved in 'Pronator Syndrome'?", a: "Median nerve compression between the two heads of the Pronator Teres." },
            { q: "What is the 'Space of Poirier'?", a: "An area of weakness in the floor of the carpal tunnel (over the lunate/capitate) where the lunate typically dislocates." },
            { q: "Nerve supply to the Pectoralis Major's clavicular vs. sternocostal heads?", a: "Clavicular: Lateral Pectoral Nerve; Sternocostal: Medial Pectoral Nerve." }
          ]
        },
        {
          topic: 'General Physiology',
          icon: 'activity',
          cards: [
            { q: "What is the primary component of the cell membrane bilayer?", a: "Phospholipids (amphipathic molecules)." },
            { q: "Which molecule regulates membrane fluidity in eukaryotes?", a: "Cholesterol." },
            { q: "Definition of 'Simple Diffusion'?", a: "Movement of solutes directly through the lipid bilayer down a concentration gradient without energy or carriers." },
            { q: "What characterizes 'Facilitated Diffusion'?", a: "Passive transport using a carrier protein or channel (e.g., GLUT transporters); exhibits saturation kinetics (Vmax)." },
            { q: "Primary Active Transport vs. Secondary Active Transport?", a: "Primary uses ATP directly (e.g., Na+/K+ pump); Secondary uses the electrochemical gradient established by primary transport (e.g., SGLT)." },
            { q: "Stoichiometry of the Na+/K+ ATPase pump?", a: "3 Na+ out, 2 K+ in (electrogenic)." },
            { q: "Typical intracellular vs. extracellular K+ concentration?", a: "Intracellular: ~140 mEq/L; Extracellular: ~4 mEq/L." },
            { q: "Typical intracellular vs. extracellular Na+ concentration?", a: "Intracellular: ~10–14 mEq/L; Extracellular: ~142 mEq/L." },
            { q: "Normal plasma osmolarity?", a: "~280–295 mOsm/L." },
            { q: "Definition of an 'Isotonic' solution?", a: "A solution with the same effective osmolarity as plasma; causes no net water movement across a cell membrane." },
            { q: "What happens to a cell placed in a Hypertonic solution?", a: "It shrinks (crenation) as water moves out." },
            { q: "Formula for Total Body Water (TBW)?", a: "60% of body weight in kilograms." },
            { q: "The '60-40-20 Rule' for fluid compartments?", a: "60% TBW, 40% Intracellular Fluid (ICF), 20% Extracellular Fluid (ECF)." },
            { q: "Two components of the ECF?", a: "Interstitial fluid (3/4 of ECF) and Plasma (1/4 of ECF)." },
            { q: "What determines the Resting Membrane Potential (RMP)?", a: "Diffusion potentials (mainly K+ 'leak' channels) and the Na+/K+ pump." },
            { q: "Phase of the action potential caused by Na+ influx?", a: "Depolarization." },
            { q: "Phase of the action potential caused by K+ efflux?", a: "Repolarization." },
            { q: "What is the 'Absolute Refractory Period'?", a: "Time during which a second action potential cannot be initiated, regardless of stimulus strength (due to inactivated Na+ channels)." },
            { q: "Effect of Myelination on conduction velocity?", a: "Increases velocity via saltatory conduction (jumping between Nodes of Ranvier)." },
            { q: "Ion responsible for triggering neurotransmitter release at the presynaptic terminal?", a: "Ca2+ (influx through voltage-gated channels)." },
            { q: "Primary excitatory neurotransmitter in the CNS?", a: "Glutamate." },
            { q: "Primary inhibitory neurotransmitter in the CNS?", a: "GABA (increases Cl- conductance)." },
            { q: "What is an EPSP?", a: "Excitatory Postsynaptic Potential; a local depolarization." },
            { q: "The 'Triad' in skeletal muscle consists of?", a: "One T-tubule and two terminal cisternae of the Sarcoplasmic Reticulum (SR)." },
            { q: "Role of Calcium in skeletal muscle contraction?", a: "Binds to Troponin C, moving Tropomyosin away from the actin-binding sites." },
            { q: "What causes the 'Power Stroke'?", a: "Release of Pi (inorganic phosphate) from the myosin head." },
            { q: "Mechanism of relaxation in muscle?", a: "Ca2+ is pumped back into the SR via SERCA (Sarco/Endoplasmic Reticulum Ca2+-ATPase)." },
            { q: "Multi-unit vs. Unitary (Single-unit) Smooth Muscle?", a: "Unitary muscles use gap junctions to contract as a syncytium (e.g., GI tract); Multi-unit act independently (e.g., Iris)." },
            { q: "Definition of 'Negative Feedback'?", a: "A reaction that causes a decrease in function in response to a stimulus (e.g., blood pressure regulation)." },
            { q: "Example of 'Positive Feedback' in physiology?", a: "Oxytocin in childbirth or the Na+ channel opening during an action potential." },
            { q: "Gs protein signaling pathway?", a: "Stimulates Adenylyl Cyclase -> increases cAMP -> activates Protein Kinase A (PKA)." },
            { q: "Gq protein signaling pathway?", a: "Activates Phospholipase C -> produces IP3 and DAG -> increases intracellular Ca2+ and activates Protein Kinase C (PKC)." },
            { q: "Second messenger for Nitric Oxide (NO)?", a: "cGMP." },
            { q: "Difference between 'Up-regulation' and 'Down-regulation'?", a: "Up-regulation increases receptor number in response to low hormone levels; Down-regulation decreases them in response to high levels." },
            { q: "What is the 'Nernst Equation' used for?", a: "Calculating the equilibrium potential for a single ion." },
            { q: "What is 'Total Peripheral Resistance' (TPR)?", a: "The total resistance to flow in the systemic circulation." },
            { q: "Starling Forces: Which force favors filtration out of the capillary?", a: "Capillary Hydrostatic Pressure (Pc)." },
            { q: "Starling Forces: Which force opposes filtration (favors reabsorption)?", a: "Plasma Colloid Osmotic Pressure (pi_p)." },
            { q: "What is 'Autophagy'?", a: "Regulated mechanism of the cell that disassembles unnecessary or dysfunctional components." },
            { q: "Primary buffer system in the ECF?", a: "Bicarbonate (HCO3-/CO2) buffer system." },
            { q: "What is 'Threshold Potential'?", a: "The membrane potential at which an action potential is inevitably fired (~ -55mV in many neurons)." },
            { q: "Function of the 'Relative Refractory Period'?", a: "A period following the absolute refractory period where an action potential can be fired, but only by a supra-threshold stimulus." },
            { q: "What is 'Accommodation' in a nerve cell?", a: "The rise in threshold potential when a nerve is slowly depolarized, caused by the closing of Na+ inactivation gates." },
            { q: "Effect of Hyperkalemia on Resting Membrane Potential (RMP)?", a: "It depolarizes the RMP (moves it closer to threshold), making the cell more excitable initially." },
            { q: "Effect of Hypocalcemia on nerve excitability?", a: "Increases excitability by lowering the threshold potential (causes tetany)." },
            { q: "What is the 'Neuromuscular Junction' (NMJ) neurotransmitter?", a: "Acetylcholine (ACh)." },
            { q: "Role of Acetylcholinesterase?", a: "Rapidly degrades ACh in the synaptic cleft to terminate the signal." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Botulinum Toxin?", a: "Blocks the release of ACh from presynaptic terminals, causing flaccid paralysis." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Myasthenia Gravis?", a: "Antibodies against post-synaptic nicotinic ACh receptors, leading to muscle weakness." },
            { q: "What are 'L-type' Calcium Channels in skeletal muscle?", a: "Dihydropyridine (DHP) receptors located in the T-tubules that act as voltage sensors." },
            { q: "What are 'Ryanodine Receptors' (RyR)?", a: "Calcium-release channels located on the Sarcoplasmic Reticulum membrane." },
            { q: "Definition of 'Isometric Contraction'?", a: "Muscle develops tension but does not shorten (length is constant)." },
            { q: "Definition of 'Isotonic Contraction'?", a: "Muscle shortens while maintaining constant tension." },
            { q: "What is 'Tetanization'?", a: "Continuous muscle contraction caused by high-frequency stimulation where individual twitches fuse." },
            { q: "Does smooth muscle contain Troponin?", a: "No, it uses Calmodulin as the calcium-binding protein." },
            { q: "Role of 'Myosin Light Chain Kinase' (MLCK)?", a: "Activated by the Ca2+-Calmodulin complex; it phosphorylates myosin heads to initiate contraction." },
            { q: "What is the 'Latch-bridge' mechanism?", a: "Allows smooth muscle to maintain high tension for long periods with very little ATP consumption." },
            { q: "Role of Myosin Phosphatase?", a: "Dephosphorylates myosin to induce relaxation in smooth muscle." },
            { q: "Primary neurotransmitter of all Preganglionic autonomic fibers?", a: "Acetylcholine (ACh)." },
            { q: "Neurotransmitter of most Sympathetic Postganglionic fibers?", a: "Norepinephrine (except for sweat glands, which use ACh)." },
            { q: "Receptor type found at the Sympathetic and Parasympathetic ganglia?", a: "Nicotinic (Nn)." },
            { q: "Alpha-1 (alpha1) receptor effect on blood vessels?", a: "Vasoconstriction (via Gq pathway)." },
            { q: "Beta-1 (beta1) receptor effect on the heart?", a: "Increases heart rate (chronotropy) and contractility (inotropy) via Gs pathway." },
            { q: "Beta-2 (beta2) receptor effect on the lungs?", a: "Bronchodilation (via Gs pathway)." },
            { q: "Parasympathetic effect on the Pupil?", a: "Miosis (constriction) via M3 receptors." },
            { q: "What is 'Solute Drag'?", a: "The movement of solutes along with water as it moves paracellularly (via bulk flow)." },
            { q: "Definition of 'Vmax' in carrier-mediated transport?", a: "The maximum rate of transport achieved when all carrier binding sites are saturated." },
            { q: "Difference between 'Endocytosis' and 'Exocytosis'?", a: "Endocytosis brings substances into the cell via vesicles; Exocytosis expels them." },
            { q: "What characterizes 'Pinocytosis'?", a: "'Cell drinking'; the non-specific ingestion of extracellular fluid and small solutes." },
            { q: "Primary cation in the Interstitial Fluid?", a: "Sodium (Na+)." },
            { q: "Primary anion in the Interstitial Fluid?", a: "Chloride (Cl-)." },
            { q: "Primary protein buffer inside the cells?", a: "Hemoglobin (in RBCs) and other intracellular proteins." },
            { q: "What is the 'Anion Gap' formula?", a: "Na+ - (Cl- + HCO3-)." },
            { q: "Normal Anion Gap value?", a: "8–12 mEq/L." },
            { q: "Significance of a 'High Anion Gap Metabolic Acidosis'?", a: "Indicates the addition of fixed acids (e.g., Lactic acid, Ketoacids) to the blood." },
            { q: "What is 'Steady State'?", a: "A condition where variables are constant over time, but energy may be required to maintain it (unlike equilibrium)." },
            { q: "Function of 'Gap Junctions' in Cardiac and Smooth muscle?", a: "Provide low-resistance electrical coupling between cells." },
            { q: "Which organelle is the 'Powerhouse' of the cell, providing ATP for physiology?", a: "Mitochondria." },
            { q: "Effect of 'Hypokalemia' on the T-wave of an EKG?", a: "Flat T-waves and presence of U-waves." },
            { q: "Effect of 'Hyperkalemia' on the T-wave of an EKG?", a: "Peaked T-waves." },
            { q: "Role of 'Clathrin' in transport?", a: "Protein that coats vesicles during receptor-mediated endocytosis." },
            { q: "What is 'Osmotic Pressure'?", a: "The pressure required to stop the inward flow of water across a semi-permeable membrane." },
            { q: "Van't Hoff's Law relates to?", a: "Calculating osmotic pressure based on solute concentration and temperature." },
            { q: "Definition of 'Tonicity'?", a: "The ability of an extracellular solution to make water move into or out of a cell by osmosis." },
            { q: "What is 'Phagocytosis'?", a: "'Cell eating'; ingestion of large particles like bacteria or cell debris by macrophages/neutrophils." },
            { q: "Effect of Insulin on cellular Potassium uptake?", a: "Increases K+ uptake by stimulating the Na+/K+ ATPase." },
            { q: "What is 'Up-regulation'?", a: "An increase in the number of receptors on a target cell, often in response to chronically low hormone levels." },
            { q: "What is 'Down-regulation'?", a: "A decrease in the number of receptors, often to protect the cell from overstimulation." },
            { q: "Function of the 'Sodium-Calcium Exchanger' (NCX)?", a: "Secondary active transport that uses the Na+ gradient to pump Ca2+ out of the cell." },
            { q: "How does 'Temperature' affect the rate of diffusion?", a: "Increased temperature increases the kinetic energy of molecules, increasing the diffusion rate." },
            { q: "Definition of 'Permeability'?", a: "The ease with which a substance can cross a cell membrane." },
            { q: "What determines 'Membrane Capacitance'?", a: "The ability of the lipid bilayer to store electrical charge." },
            { q: "Role of 'Cyclic AMP' (cAMP) as a second messenger?", a: "Activates Protein Kinase A (PKA) to phosphorylate target proteins." },
            { q: "Role of 'Inositol Trisphosphate' (IP3)?", a: "Binds to receptors on the ER to release stored Ca2+." },
            { q: "Role of 'Diacylglycerol' (DAG)?", a: "Activates Protein Kinase C (PKC) in the presence of Ca2+." },
            { q: "What is 'Spatial Summation'?", a: "Multiple presynaptic neurons firing simultaneously at different locations on a postsynaptic neuron." },
            { q: "What is 'Temporal Summation'?", a: "A single presynaptic neuron firing in rapid succession." },
            { q: "What is the 'Length-Tension Relationship' in muscle?", a: "Describes how the force of contraction depends on the initial overlap of actin and myosin filaments." },
            { q: "What is 'Oxygen Debt'?", a: "The extra oxygen required after exercise to restore metabolic systems to their resting state." },
            { q: "Difference between 'Fast-twitch' and 'Slow-twitch' muscle fibers?", a: "Fast-twitch (Type II) are for bursts of power (glycolytic); Slow-twitch (Type I) are for endurance (oxidative/myoglobin rich)." }
          ]
        },
        {
          topic: 'Lower Limb',
          icon: 'footprints',
          cards: [
            { q: "Parts of the hip bone?", a: "Ilium (superior), ischium (posteroinferior), pubis (anteroinferior). Fuse at acetabulum by age 15–17 at triradiate cartilage." },
            { q: "Borders of the acetabulum?", a: "Lunate surface (articular, C-shaped). Acetabular fossa (non-articular, contains fat pad). Acetabular notch (inferior gap) — bridged by transverse acetabular ligament." },
            { q: "What is the angle of inclination of the femoral neck?", a: "~126° (adult). >126° = coxa valga. <126° = coxa vara. Newborn ~150°." },
            { q: "What is the angle of anteversion of the femur?", a: "~15° anterior twist of femoral neck relative to shaft. Increased anteversion -> in-toeing. Decreased -> out-toeing." },
            { q: "Greater vs. lesser trochanter — muscle attachments?", a: "Greater trochanter: gluteus medius/minimus, piriformis, obturators, gemelli. Lesser trochanter: iliopsoas (psoas major + iliacus)." },
            { q: "What is the linea aspera?", a: "Prominent posterior ridge of femoral shaft. Attachments: adductors, vastus medialis/lateralis, short head biceps femoris, gluteus maximus (lateral lip)." },
            { q: "Parts of the tibia?", a: "Medial/lateral condyles, intercondylar eminence, tibial tuberosity (patellar ligament attachment), shaft, medial malleolus (distal)." },
            { q: "Why is the fibula not weight-bearing?", a: "Fibula bears only ~10% body weight. Non-weight-bearing long bone; primarily for muscle attachment and ankle stability." },
            { q: "Bones of the foot?", a: "Tarsals (7): calcaneus, talus, navicular, cuboid, 3 cuneiforms. Metatarsals (5). Phalanges (14): toes 2–5 = 3 each; hallux = 2." },
            { q: "What is the sustentaculum tali?", a: "Medial shelf of calcaneus; supports head of talus; groove for FHL tendon on inferior surface; supports spring (plantar calcaneonavicular) ligament." },
            { q: "Bones forming the ankle (talocrural) joint?", a: "Distal tibia (medial malleolus + tibial plafond) + distal fibula (lateral malleolus) form mortise. Talus (body) is the tenon." },
            { q: "Gluteus maximus — origin, insertion, action, nerve?", a: "Origin: ilium (posterior gluteal line), sacrum, coccyx, sacrotuberous ligament. Insertion: gluteal tuberosity + iliotibial band. Action: extension + lateral rotation of hip. Nerve: inferior gluteal (L5, S1, S2)." },
            { q: "Gluteus medius — origin, insertion, action, nerve?", a: "Origin: ilium (between anterior and posterior gluteal lines). Insertion: greater trochanter (lateral surface). Action: hip abduction; anterior fibers medially rotate. Nerve: superior gluteal (L4, L5, S1)." },
            { q: "Gluteus minimus — action and nerve?", a: "Hip abduction + medial rotation (more than medius). Superior gluteal nerve (L4, L5, S1). Deep to gluteus medius." },
            { q: "What is the Trendelenburg sign?", a: "Pelvis drops to contralateral side during single-leg stance when ipsilateral hip abductors (gluteus medius/minimus) are weak or superior gluteal nerve is damaged. Positive = pathological side is the stance leg." },
            { q: "Piriformis — origin, insertion, action, nerve?", a: "Origin: anterior sacrum (S2–S4). Insertion: greater trochanter (superior facet). Action: lateral rotation + abduction of hip. Nerve: direct branches S1, S2." },
            { q: "The six deep lateral rotators of the hip?", a: "Piriformis, Obturator internus, Obturator externus, Superior gemellus, Inferior gemellus, Quadratus femoris. (Mnemonic: POOGQS or 'Piece Of Pie, Old Gardener Quite Fortunate')" },
            { q: "Nerve to obturator internus supplies which muscles?", a: "Obturator internus + superior gemellus. L5, S1, S2." },
            { q: "Nerve to quadratus femoris supplies which muscles?", a: "Quadratus femoris + inferior gemellus. L4, L5, S1." },
            { q: "Tensor fasciae latae (TFL) — action and nerve?", a: "Flexion, medial rotation, abduction of hip; tenses iliotibial band. Superior gluteal nerve (L4, L5, S1)." },
            { q: "What is the iliotibial (IT) band?", a: "Thick lateral thickening of fascia lata; from iliac crest -> Gerdy's tubercle (lateral tibia). Resists knee varus, assists knee extension. IT band syndrome = lateral knee pain in runners." },
            { q: "Muscles of the anterior compartment of thigh?", a: "Iliopsoas (iliacus + psoas major), Sartorius, Quadriceps femoris (rectus femoris, vastus lateralis, vastus medialis, vastus intermedius), Pectineus." },
            { q: "Quadriceps femoris — common insertion and action?", a: "All four heads -> quadriceps tendon -> patella -> patellar ligament -> tibial tuberosity. Action: knee extension. Rectus femoris also flexes hip." },
            { q: "Which quadriceps muscle has a dual origin?", a: "Rectus femoris: straight head (anterior inferior iliac spine, AIIS) + reflected head (ilium above acetabulum). Only quadriceps crossing hip joint." },
            { q: "Femoral nerve — root values and branches in thigh?", a: "L2, L3, L4. Branches: nerve to pectineus, sartorius, quadriceps (anterior/posterior divisions), medial cutaneous nerve of thigh, intermediate cutaneous nerve of thigh, saphenous nerve (continues to leg/foot)." },
            { q: "Saphenous nerve — significance?", a: "Longest cutaneous nerve in body. Terminal branch of femoral nerve (L3, L4). Accompanies great saphenous vein. Supplies medial leg and foot. No motor component." },
            { q: "Femoral triangle — boundaries and contents?", a: "Boundaries: inguinal ligament (superior), sartorius (lateral), adductor longus (medial), iliopsoas + pectineus (floor). Contents (lateral to medial): Femoral Nerve, Artery, Vein, canal (Empty space — lymphatics). 'NAVE' or 'My Aunt Nibbles Vegetation.'" },
            { q: "Femoral canal — contents and significance?", a: "Medial compartment of femoral sheath; contains lymphatics + fat. Potential site for femoral hernia (more common in females). Femoral ring = its superior opening." },
            { q: "Femoral sheath — contents and layers?", a: "Three compartments (lateral to medial): Femoral artery, Femoral vein, Femoral canal. Femoral nerve is NOT within sheath (outside, lateral)." },
            { q: "Adductor (Hunter's) canal — boundaries and contents?", a: "Boundaries: vastus medialis (lateral), adductor longus/magnus (medial/posterior), sartorius (roof). Contents: femoral artery, femoral vein, saphenous nerve, nerve to vastus medialis. Femoral vessels exit via adductor hiatus -> become popliteal." },
            { q: "Iliopsoas — components and insertion?", a: "Psoas major (T12–L5 vertebral bodies/discs + transverse processes) + iliacus (iliac fossa) -> lesser trochanter. Action: hip flexion (most powerful). Nerve: femoral nerve (iliacus) + direct lumbar branches L1–L3 (psoas)." },
            { q: "Muscles of the medial compartment of thigh?", a: "Gracilis, Adductor longus, Adductor brevis, Adductor magnus, Obturator externus, Pectineus (dual — femoral + obturator)." },
            { q: "Obturator nerve — root values, course, distribution?", a: "L2, L3, L4. Descends medial to psoas -> obturator foramen -> divides into anterior and posterior divisions. Motor: medial thigh adductors. Cutaneous: small medial thigh area." },
            { q: "Adductor magnus — dual nerve supply?", a: "Adductor part (adductor hiatus): obturator nerve (L2–L4). Hamstring part (ischial tuberosity origin): tibial division of sciatic nerve (L4). Largest adductor." },
            { q: "Gracilis — insertion and clinical use?", a: "Pubic symphysis/ramus -> medial tibia (pes anserinus). Adducts hip, flexes + medially rotates knee. Used as free muscle flap (gracilis flap) for reconstructive surgery." },
            { q: "Pes anserinus — components?", a: "Common insertion of Sartorius + Gracilis + Semitendinosus on medial tibia. 'Goose's foot.' Pes anserinus bursitis = medial knee pain, common in obese/osteoarthritis patients." },
            { q: "Obturator nerve injury — deficit?", a: "Weakness of hip adduction; difficulty crossing legs. Sensory loss medial thigh. Can occur with pelvic fracture, obturator hernia, difficult labor." },
            { q: "Hamstring muscles?", a: "Biceps femoris (long + short head), Semitendinosus, Semimembranosus. All cross hip and knee except short head of biceps femoris (crosses knee only)." },
            { q: "Common origin of hamstrings?", a: "Ischial tuberosity (except short head of biceps femoris — linea aspera + lateral supracondylar line)." },
            { q: "Nerve supply of hamstrings?", a: "Tibial division of sciatic nerve (L5, S1, S2) — all hamstrings except short head of biceps femoris. Short head: common fibular (peroneal) division (L5, S1, S2)." },
            { q: "Semimembranosus vs. semitendinosus — distinguishing features?", a: "Semitendinosus: long round tendon in distal thigh (inserts pes anserinus). Semimembranosus: flat membranous tendon proximally (inserts medial tibial condyle + forms oblique popliteal ligament)." },
            { q: "What is the straight leg raise (SLR) test?", a: "Patient supine, examiner raises extended leg. Pain radiating below knee at <60° = positive -> suggests L4–S1 nerve root compression (disc herniation). Tests dural tension." },
            { q: "Muscles of the anterior leg compartment?", a: "Tibialis anterior, Extensor hallucis longus (EHL), Extensor digitorum longus (EDL), Fibularis (peroneus) tertius." },
            { q: "Nerve and artery of anterior leg compartment?", a: "Deep fibular (peroneal) nerve (L4, L5). Anterior tibial artery." },
            { q: "Actions of anterior compartment muscles?", a: "Dorsiflexion of foot (all). Tibialis anterior: inversion. EHL: extends hallux. EDL: extends toes 2–5. Peroneus tertius: eversion." },
            { q: "Deep fibular nerve injury — deficit?", a: "Foot drop (inability to dorsiflex) + inability to extend toes. Sensory loss: first web space (dorsum). Causes: tight cast, compartment syndrome, fibular head fracture, crossed-leg sitting." },
            { q: "Anterior compartment syndrome — mechanism?", a: "Increased pressure within anterior compartment -> compresses deep fibular nerve + anterior tibial artery -> ischemia -> severe pain with passive stretch -> emergency fasciotomy required." },
            { q: "Muscles of the lateral compartment?", a: "Fibularis (peroneus) longus, Fibularis (peroneus) brevis." },
            { q: "Nerve and action of lateral compartment?", a: "Superficial fibular (peroneal) nerve (L5, S1). Action: eversion of foot (primary). Secondary: plantarflexion." },
            { q: "Superficial fibular nerve injury — deficit?", a: "Loss of foot eversion. Sensory loss: dorsum of foot (except 1st web space). Cause: inversion ankle sprain (most common nerve injury with sprain)." },
            { q: "Fibularis longus — unique course?", a: "Crosses lateral ankle -> under foot in groove on cuboid -> obliquely across plantar foot -> inserts on medial cuneiform + base of 1st metatarsal. Supports transverse + lateral longitudinal arches." },
            { q: "Common fibular (peroneal) nerve — where is it vulnerable?", a: "Winds around fibular neck (head of fibula) — subcutaneous here. Vulnerable in: fibular head fracture, tight cast, prolonged squatting/crossing legs, knee dislocation." },
            { q: "Common fibular nerve injury — complete deficit?", a: "Foot drop + loss of eversion + sensory loss dorsum of foot + 1st web space. High-stepping gait (steppage gait)." },
            { q: "Muscles of superficial posterior compartment?", a: "Gastrocnemius (medial + lateral heads), Soleus, Plantaris. All insert via calcaneal (Achilles) tendon." },
            { q: "Muscles of deep posterior compartment?", a: "Popliteus, Tibialis posterior, Flexor digitorum longus (FDL), Flexor hallucis longus (FHL)." },
            { q: "Nerve and artery of posterior compartment?", a: "Tibial nerve (L4–S3). Posterior tibial artery." },
            { q: "Gastrocnemius vs. Soleus — origin differences?", a: "Gastrocnemius: two heads from femoral condyles (crosses knee). Soleus: soleal line of tibia + fibular head (does NOT cross knee). Both plantarflex ankle." },
            { q: "Clinical test distinguishing gastrocnemius vs. soleus tightness?", a: "Silfverskiöld test: if dorsiflexion improves with knee flexion -> gastrocnemius tightness (2-joint muscle). No change -> soleus tightness." },
            { q: "Achilles tendon — blood supply and vulnerable zone?", a: "Supplied by posterior tibial and fibular arteries. Watershed zone: 2–6 cm proximal to insertion — most common site of rupture (tendinopathy, rupture). Thompson test for rupture." },
            { q: "Thompson (Simmonds) test?", a: "Patient prone, calf squeezed. Normal = plantarflexion. Positive (no movement) = Achilles tendon rupture." },
            { q: "Tom, Dick, AND Harry — medial ankle structures?", a: "Posterior to medial malleolus (anterior to posterior): Tibialis posterior, flexor Digitorum longus, posterior tibial Artery & tibial Nerve, flexor Hallucis longus. (Tom, Dick, AN Harry)" },
            { q: "Popliteus — function?", a: "'Unlocking' extended knee by laterally rotating femur on fixed tibia (medially rotating tibia). Initiates knee flexion. Tibial nerve (L4, L5, S1)." },
            { q: "FHL vs. FDL — which is more important functionally?", a: "FHL: flexes hallux, powerful push-off (ballet dancers particularly susceptible to FHL tenosynovitis). FDL: flexes toes 2–5. Both invert foot and plantarflex ankle." },
            { q: "Layers of intrinsic foot muscles (plantar)?", a: "Layer 1 (superficial): Abductor hallucis, Flexor digitorum brevis, Abductor digiti minimi. Layer 2: Quadratus plantae, Lumbricals. Layer 3: Flexor hallucis brevis, Adductor hallucis, Flexor digiti minimi brevis. Layer 4 (deep): Plantar interossei (3), Dorsal interossei (4)." },
            { q: "Interossei — PAD vs. DAB?", a: "Plantar interossei ADduct toes (PAD). Dorsal interossei ABduct toes (DAB). Reference axis = 2nd toe." },
            { q: "Nerve supply of plantar foot?", a: "Medial plantar nerve (L4, L5): abductor hallucis, FDB, FHB, 1st lumbrical — 'LAFF' muscles. Lateral plantar nerve (S1, S2): remaining intrinsic muscles." },
            { q: "Medial plantar nerve — analogy to hand?", a: "Analogous to median nerve in hand (supplies thenar-equivalent muscles + lateral 3½ digits). Lateral plantar nerve approx ulnar nerve (hypothenar + most intrinsics)." },
            { q: "Arches of the foot?", a: "Medial longitudinal (highest — talus is keystone): calcaneus, talus, navicular, cuneiforms, metatarsals 1–3. Lateral longitudinal (flatter): calcaneus, cuboid, metatarsals 4–5. Transverse: bases of metatarsals + cuboid + cuneiforms." },
            { q: "Structures supporting the medial longitudinal arch?", a: "Spring ligament (plantar calcaneonavicular), plantar aponeurosis (windlass mechanism), tibialis posterior, FHL, FDB, intrinsic muscles." },
            { q: "What is plantar fasciitis?", a: "Inflammation of plantar aponeurosis at calcaneal origin. Pain with first steps in morning (post-static dyskinesia). Caused by overuse, flat feet, obesity. Calcaneal spur may be present." },
            { q: "Windlass mechanism?", a: "Dorsiflexion of toes -> tightens plantar aponeurosis -> raises medial longitudinal arch -> supinates foot -> stiffens foot for push-off." },
            { q: "Dorsal foot — nerve supply?", a: "Superficial fibular nerve: most of dorsum. Deep fibular nerve: 1st web space. Sural nerve: lateral dorsum. Saphenous nerve: medial dorsum." },
            { q: "Morton's neuroma — location and nerve?", a: "Perineural fibrosis of common digital nerve, most often between 3rd and 4th metatarsal heads (3rd interspace). Pain/numbness in toes 3–4. More common in women." },
            { q: "Femoral artery — origin and course?", a: "Continuation of external iliac artery at inguinal ligament midpoint. Descends in femoral triangle -> adductor canal -> passes through adductor hiatus -> becomes popliteal artery." },
            { q: "Profunda femoris (deep femoral artery) — branches?", a: "Largest branch of femoral artery. Gives: medial circumflex femoral artery (MCFA), lateral circumflex femoral artery (LCFA), 3–4 perforating arteries." },
            { q: "Which artery is the primary supply to femoral head in adults?", a: "Medial circumflex femoral artery (MCFA) via retinacular arteries. Fracture of femoral neck -> disrupts MCFA -> avascular necrosis of femoral head." },
            { q: "Cruciate anastomosis — components?", a: "Inferior gluteal artery + MCFA + LCFA (ascending branch) + 1st perforating artery. Collateral circulation around hip." },
            { q: "Trochanteric anastomosis — components?", a: "MCFA + LCFA + superior/inferior gluteal arteries. Supplies femoral head." },
            { q: "Popliteal artery — branches?", a: "Superior medial/lateral genicular, middle genicular, inferior medial/lateral genicular -> divides at lower border of popliteus into anterior tibial artery + tibioperoneal trunk (-> posterior tibial + fibular arteries)." },
            { q: "Anterior tibial artery — continuation?", a: "Passes through interosseous membrane -> becomes dorsalis pedis artery at ankle (felt between EHL and EDL tendons — dorsalis pedis pulse)." },
            { q: "Posterior tibial artery — continuation?", a: "Passes posterior to medial malleolus (posterior tibial pulse) -> enters foot -> divides into medial and lateral plantar arteries." },
            { q: "Dorsalis pedis pulse — location?", a: "Dorsum of foot, between tendons of EHL and EDL (extensor hallucis longus and extensor digitorum longus), lateral to EHL tendon." },
            { q: "Posterior tibial pulse — location?", a: "Posterior to medial malleolus, between medial malleolus and Achilles tendon." },
            { q: "Femoral pulse — location?", a: "Femoral triangle, at midinguinal point (midpoint between ASIS and pubic symphysis). NOT the inguinal ligament midpoint (which overlies femoral nerve)." },
            { q: "Geniculate anastomosis — significance?", a: "Network around knee (superior/inferior genicular + descending genicular + anterior tibial recurrent). Collateral circulation if popliteal artery is blocked. May be disrupted in popliteal artery injury." },
            { q: "Great (long) saphenous vein — course and tributaries?", a: "Begins dorsal venous arch -> anterior to medial malleolus -> medial leg -> medial knee -> medial thigh -> drains into femoral vein at saphenofemoral junction (4 cm below and lateral to pubic tubercle)." },
            { q: "Small (short) saphenous vein — course?", a: "Posterior to lateral malleolus -> ascends posterior midline of leg -> drains into popliteal vein at saphenopopliteal junction (posterior knee)." },
            { q: "What is the saphenofemoral junction?", a: "Where great saphenous vein joins femoral vein, 4 cm inferolateral to pubic tubercle (below inguinal ligament). Important in varicose vein surgery (Trendelenburg procedure)." },
            { q: "Perforating (communicating) veins — function?", a: "Connect superficial to deep venous systems. Valves direct flow from superficial -> deep. Valve failure -> superficial vein overload -> varicose veins." },
            { q: "Varicose veins — pathophysiology?", a: "Incompetent valves in perforating or saphenofemoral junction -> blood reflux -> superficial vein dilation -> tortuosity. Risk factors: pregnancy, obesity, prolonged standing." },
            { q: "What is DVT and why is it dangerous?", a: "Deep vein thrombosis (most common in calf veins, popliteal, femoral, iliac). Danger: proximal propagation -> pulmonary embolism. Virchow's triad: stasis, endothelial damage, hypercoagulability." },
            { q: "Homans' sign — reliability?", a: "Calf pain on dorsiflexion of foot. Historically used for DVT but poor sensitivity and specificity (~50%). Replaced by Wells score + D-dimer + Doppler ultrasound." },
            { q: "Type and movements of the hip joint?", a: "Synovial ball-and-socket joint. Movements: flexion/extension (sagittal), abduction/adduction (coronal), medial/lateral rotation (transverse), circumduction." },
            { q: "Ligaments of the hip joint?", a: "Iliofemoral (Y-ligament of Bigelow) — strongest, anterior. Pubofemoral — inferior/medial. Ischiofemoral — posterior. Ligament of head of femur (ligamentum teres) — transmits artery to femoral head (in children)." },
            { q: "Iliofemoral ligament — why is it important?", a: "Strongest ligament in body. Inverted Y-shape; limits hip extension -> allows humans to stand without muscular effort (passive standing). Anterior to hip joint." },
            { q: "Nerve supply of hip joint?", a: "Femoral nerve, obturator nerve, superior gluteal nerve, nerve to quadratus femoris. Hilton's Law: joint supplied by nerves to muscles acting on it." },
            { q: "Hilton's Law?", a: "A joint is innervated by branches of the nerves that supply the muscles acting on that joint. Clinical relevance: referred pain from hip joint felt in knee (obturator nerve distribution)." },
            { q: "Blood supply of femoral head — clinical importance?", a: "Adults: mainly MCFA via retinacular vessels. Ligamentum teres artery (from obturator) contributes in children. Femoral neck fracture -> AVN of femoral head (disrupts MCFA)." },
            { q: "Garden classification of femoral neck fractures?", a: "I: incomplete/valgus impacted. II: complete, non-displaced. III: complete, partially displaced. IV: complete, fully displaced. Grades III–IV -> high risk of AVN." },
            { q: "Posterior dislocation of hip — nerve at risk?", a: "Sciatic nerve. Femur driven posteriorly (e.g., dashboard injury) -> nerve stretched/compressed. Clinical: foot drop + sensory loss posterior thigh/leg/foot." },
            { q: "Anterior dislocation of hip — nerve at risk?", a: "Femoral nerve and/or obturator nerve. Less common than posterior." },
            { q: "What position does the hip assume in posterior dislocation?", a: "Flexion, adduction, internal rotation — leg appears shortened. (Anterior dislocation: extension, abduction, external rotation.)" },
            { q: "Type of joint and articulations of the knee?", a: "Synovial hinge joint (with rotation). Tibiofemoral (medial + lateral condyles) + patellofemoral joints. Most complex joint in body." },
            { q: "Ligaments of the knee?", a: "ACL (anterior cruciate), PCL (posterior cruciate), MCL (medial collateral/tibial), LCL (lateral collateral/fibular), patellar ligament, arcuate ligament, oblique popliteal ligament." },
            { q: "ACL — attachments and function?", a: "Anterior intercondylar area of tibia -> lateral femoral condyle (posterior aspect). Prevents anterior tibial translation + internal rotation of tibia. Taut in extension." },
            { q: "PCL — attachments and function?", a: "Posterior intercondylar area of tibia -> medial femoral condyle (anterior aspect). Prevents posterior tibial translation. Stronger than ACL. Taut in flexion." },
            { q: "Anterior drawer test vs. Lachman test?", a: "Both test ACL. Anterior drawer: knee at 90° flexion, tibia pulled forward. Lachman: knee at 20–30° flexion (more sensitive, preferred clinically). Positive = increased anterior translation." },
            { q: "Posterior drawer test?", a: "Knee at 90°, tibia pushed posteriorly. Positive = PCL rupture. PCL injury: posterior sag sign, posterior drawer." },
            { q: "MCL — attachments and injury?", a: "Medial femoral epicondyle -> medial tibial condyle. Resists valgus stress. Injured by valgus force (lateral blow to knee). MCL, ACL, medial meniscus together = 'unhappy triad.'" },
            { q: "LCL — attachments and function?", a: "Lateral femoral epicondyle -> fibular head. Resists varus stress. Cord-like (no capsular attachment). Injured less commonly (lateral blow = varus force)." },
            { q: "Unhappy (terrible) triad of O'Donoghue?", a: "ACL + MCL + medial meniscus injury. Caused by lateral blow to knee (valgus + external rotation). Common in football/soccer." },
            { q: "Medial vs. lateral meniscus — differences?", a: "Medial: C-shaped, attached to MCL (less mobile -> more frequently torn). Lateral: O-shaped, not attached to LCL (more mobile -> less torn). Both are fibrocartilage." },
            { q: "McMurray test?", a: "Flex knee fully -> extend while rotating leg. Pop/click/pain = meniscal tear. External rotation tests medial meniscus; internal rotation tests lateral meniscus." },
            { q: "What is the screw-home mechanism?", a: "During last 30° of knee extension, tibia externally rotates on femur (or femur internally rotates) -> 'locks' knee in full extension. Reversed (unlocked) by popliteus." },
            { q: "Bursae of the knee — clinical examples?", a: "Prepatellar bursa: 'housemaid's knee' (kneeling). Infrapatellar bursa: 'clergyman's knee' (deep: prayer position). Semimembranosus bursa: Baker's cyst (popliteal fossa, communicates with joint in adults)." },
            { q: "Baker's cyst (popliteal cyst) — mechanism?", a: "Herniation of synovial membrane through posterior capsule between semimembranosus and medial gastrocnemius. Associated with knee OA or rheumatoid arthritis. Rupture mimics DVT." },
            { q: "Patellar reflex — nerve root?", a: "L2, L3, L4 (predominantly L4). Tests femoral nerve. Stimulus: patellar tendon. Response: knee extension (quadriceps contraction)." },
            { q: "Talocrural (ankle) joint — type and movements?", a: "Synovial hinge joint. Mortise (tibia + fibula) + talus. Movements: dorsiflexion (anterior compartment, L4/L5) + plantarflexion (posterior compartment, S1/S2)." },
            { q: "Lateral ankle ligaments?", a: "Anterior talofibular ligament (ATFL) — most commonly injured in inversion sprain. Calcaneofibular ligament (CFL). Posterior talofibular ligament (PTFL) — strongest, rarely torn." },
            { q: "Medial ankle ligament?", a: "Deltoid ligament (strong, fan-shaped, 4 parts: tibionavicular, anterior/posterior tibiotalar, tibiocalcaneal). Resists eversion. Rarely torn (usually fibula fractures first)." },
            { q: "Inversion ankle sprain — most commonly injured ligament?", a: "ATFL (anterior talofibular ligament) first, then CFL. Mechanism: inversion + plantarflexion. Most common ligament injury in body." },
            { q: "Subtalar (talocalcaneal) joint — movements?", a: "Inversion/eversion of foot. Allows adaptation to uneven ground. Talus slides on calcaneus." },
            { q: "Transverse tarsal (Chopart's) joint?", a: "Talonavicular + calcaneocuboid joints. Line of separation in midfoot amputations. Important for foot inversion/eversion." },
            { q: "Lisfranc joint — clinical significance?", a: "Tarsometatarsal joints. Lisfranc injury: disruption (sprain/fracture-dislocation). Often missed on plain X-ray. Look for gap between 1st and 2nd metatarsal bases." },
            { q: "Sciatic nerve — root values, composition, course?", a: "L4, L5, S1, S2, S3. Largest nerve in body. Two components: tibial (medial) + common fibular (lateral), bound together. Exits via greater sciatic foramen below piriformis -> descends posterior thigh -> divides at apex of popliteal fossa." },
            { q: "Sciatic nerve injury — complete deficit?", a: "Loss of all knee flexion, all leg/foot movement; sensory loss entire leg and foot (except medial strip — saphenous nerve/femoral). Hip extension, abduction, adduction intact (gluteal, obturator nerves)." },
            { q: "Tibial nerve — branches and distribution?", a: "L4–S3. In leg: posterior compartment muscles. In foot: medial and lateral plantar nerves. Cutaneous: sural nerve (with common fibular contribution), medial calcaneal branches." },
            { q: "Tibial nerve injury — deficit?", a: "Loss of plantarflexion, toe flexion, inversion; intrinsic foot muscles. Calcaneovalgus foot (heel walking). Sensory loss: sole of foot. Cause: popliteal fossa trauma, tarsal tunnel syndrome." },
            { q: "Tarsal tunnel syndrome?", a: "Tibial nerve compressed under flexor retinaculum posterior to medial malleolus. Burning/tingling sole of foot (especially at night). Analogous to carpal tunnel syndrome." },
            { q: "Femoral nerve injury — deficit?", a: "Loss of knee extension (quadriceps) + hip flexion (iliacus). Loss of patellar reflex. Sensory loss anterior/medial thigh + medial leg (saphenous). Causes: psoas hematoma, pelvic fracture, inguinal region surgery." },
            { q: "Lateral cutaneous nerve of thigh — clinical relevance?", a: "L2, L3. Purely sensory. Exits under inguinal ligament near ASIS. Compression -> meralgia paresthetica (burning, numbness anterolateral thigh). Causes: obesity, tight belt, pregnancy." },
            { q: "Superior vs. inferior gluteal nerve — easy way to remember muscles?", a: "Superior gluteal: abductors (gluteus medius, minimus, TFL) — 'above = abductors.' Inferior gluteal: extensor (gluteus maximus) — 'inferior = extends.'" },
            { q: "Sural nerve — composition and distribution?", a: "Formed by medial sural cutaneous (from tibial) + lateral sural cutaneous (from common fibular). Supplies lateral leg + lateral dorsum of foot. Commonly used for nerve biopsy/graft." },
            { q: "Obturator nerve injury — causes?", a: "Obturator hernia (pressure on nerve), pelvic fracture, difficult labor, total hip replacement. Deficit: weakness adduction, medial thigh sensory loss." },
            { q: "Hip fractures — types and complications?", a: "Intracapsular (femoral neck): disrupts blood supply -> AVN, non-union. Extracapsular (intertrochanteric, subtrochanteric): better blood supply, heal better but still serious in elderly." },
            { q: "Piriformis syndrome?", a: "Piriformis muscle compresses sciatic nerve (variant: sciatic nerve passes through piriformis). Buttock pain + sciatica without disc herniation. FAIR test (flexion, adduction, internal rotation) provokes pain." },
            { q: "Compartment syndrome — 6 Ps?", a: "Pain (especially on passive stretch), Pressure (tense compartment), Paresthesia, Paralysis, Pallor, Pulselessness (late). Emergency: fasciotomy within 6 hours." },
            { q: "Popliteal artery injury — in what knee injury?", a: "Knee dislocation (especially posterior). Popliteal artery tethered proximally and distally -> stretched/torn. ABI (ankle-brachial index) <0.9 -> angiography + vascular repair." },
            { q: "Ottawa ankle rules?", a: "X-ray ankle if: bone tenderness at posterior tip/inferior 6 cm of fibula or tibia; OR inability to weight-bear (4 steps). X-ray foot if: tenderness at base of 5th metatarsal or navicular; OR inability to weight-bear." },
            { q: "Ottawa knee rules?", a: "X-ray knee if: age >=55, isolated patellar tenderness, fibular head tenderness, inability to flex to 90°, inability to weight-bear (4 steps)." },
            { q: "Foot drop — causes and gait?", a: "Deep fibular nerve / common fibular nerve / sciatic nerve / L4–L5 root damage. High-stepping gait (steppage gait) to clear foot. Slapping of foot on ground." },
            { q: "Meralgia paresthetica — nerve and treatment?", a: "Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve (L2, L3) entrapment at inguinal ligament near ASIS. Burning, tingling, numbness anterolateral thigh. Treatment: weight loss, loose clothing, local injection." },
            { q: "Pes planus (flat foot) vs. pes cavus?", a: "Pes planus: collapsed medial arch — often asymptomatic; causes: tibialis posterior dysfunction, ligamentous laxity. Pes cavus: excessively high arch — often neurological cause (CMT disease, Friedreich's ataxia)." },
            { q: "Charcot-Marie-Tooth (CMT) disease — foot findings?", a: "Hereditary motor and sensory neuropathy. Pes cavus + hammer toes + distal muscle wasting. Stork-leg appearance (peroneal muscle atrophy). AR/AD." },
            { q: "Patellofemoral pain syndrome (PFPS)?", a: "Anterior knee pain worse with stairs/prolonged sitting. Positive Clarke's sign (patellar grind test). Caused by lateral patellar maltracking. Treatment: VMO strengthening, patellar taping." },
            { q: "Iliotibial band syndrome?", a: "Friction of IT band over lateral femoral epicondyle during repetitive knee flexion/extension. Common in runners/cyclists. Lateral knee pain at 30° flexion (impingement zone). Noble compression test." },
            { q: "Femoral hernia — anatomical basis?", a: "Protrudes through femoral canal (medial femoral sheath), below inguinal ligament, lateral to pubic tubercle. More common in females. High risk of strangulation (femoral ring is rigid)." },
            { q: "What is claudication?", a: "Cramping leg pain during exercise, relieved by rest. Caused by arterial insufficiency (PAD) — usually superficial femoral artery at adductor hiatus. Ankle-brachial index (ABI) <0.9 diagnostic." },
            { q: "ATFL vs. deltoid — which tears in eversion injury?", a: "Eversion strains the deltoid ligament (medial). However, because the deltoid is so strong, eversion injuries more often fracture the fibula than tear the deltoid." },
            { q: "Femoral neck angle of inclination — normal vs. pathological?", a: "Normal: ~126°. Coxa valga: >135° (leg appears long, limping). Coxa vara: <120° (leg shorter, limping, Trendelenburg)." },
            { q: "Q angle of the knee — normal values?", a: "Angle between quadriceps line (ASIS to patella) and patellar tendon. Normal: ~14° males, ~17° females. Elevated Q angle -> patellar maltracking -> PFPS." },
            { q: "ABI (ankle-brachial index) — interpretation?", a: ">1.0: normal. 0.9–1.0: borderline. 0.7–0.9: mild PAD. 0.4–0.7: moderate. <0.4: severe (critical limb ischemia). >1.3: non-compressible vessels (diabetes, calcification)." },
            { q: "Dermatomes — lower limb key levels?", a: "L1: inguinal region. L2: anterior upper thigh. L3: anterior lower thigh/knee. L4: medial leg/ankle. L5: lateral leg + dorsal foot + 1st web space. S1: lateral foot + sole + posterior leg. S2–S4: perineum/posterior thigh." },
            { q: "Key reflexes — lower limb?", a: "Patellar reflex: L2–L4 (femoral nerve). Achilles reflex: S1–S2 (tibial nerve). Plantar reflex (Babinski): L5–S2 — normal = plantarflexion; UMN lesion = dorsiflexion (Babinski sign)." },
            { q: "Lumbar plexus roots?", a: "L1–L4. Gives: iliohypogastric (L1), ilioinguinal (L1), genitofemoral (L1, L2), lateral femoral cutaneous (L2, L3), femoral (L2–L4), obturator (L2–L4)." },
            { q: "Sacral plexus roots?", a: "L4–S4. Gives: sciatic (L4–S3), superior gluteal (L4, L5, S1), inferior gluteal (L5, S1, S2), pudendal (S2–S4), posterior femoral cutaneous (S1–S3)." },
            { q: "Sciatic nerve level of division?", a: "Usually at apex of popliteal fossa (~upper border of popliteal fossa). May divide higher (in thigh or even pelvis — variation). High division relevant in hip surgery." },
            { q: "Great saphenous vein — used for what procedures?", a: "Coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG), peripheral artery bypass, AV fistula for dialysis. Accessible, long, valved. Reversed or in-situ technique." }
          ]
        },
        {
          topic: 'Biochem Basics',
          icon: 'flask-conical',
          cards: [
            { q: "What is the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation?", a: "pH = pKa + log ([A-]/[HA]). Used to calculate pH of a buffer solution. At pH = pKa, [A-] = [HA] (50% dissociation)." },
            { q: "Km — what does it represent?", a: "Substrate concentration at which reaction velocity = 1/2 Vmax. Reflects enzyme-substrate affinity. Low Km = high affinity. NOT affected by enzyme concentration." },
            { q: "Vmax — what does it represent?", a: "Maximum reaction velocity when all enzyme active sites are saturated. Directly proportional to enzyme concentration. Reflects enzyme catalytic capacity (kcat)." },
            { q: "Competitive inhibition — effect on Km and Vmax?", a: "Km increases (apparent). Vmax unchanged. Inhibitor competes with substrate at active site. Overcome by increasing substrate concentration. Example: methotrexate inhibits DHFR." },
            { q: "Noncompetitive inhibition — effect on Km and Vmax?", a: "Vmax decreases. Km unchanged. Inhibitor binds allosteric site (not active site) regardless of substrate. Cannot be overcome by increasing substrate. Example: lead inhibits ALAD." },
            { q: "Uncompetitive inhibition — effect on Km and Vmax?", a: "Both Km and Vmax decrease proportionally. Inhibitor binds only enzyme-substrate complex. Lines parallel on Lineweaver-Burk plot." },
            { q: "What is a Lineweaver-Burk (double reciprocal) plot?", a: "Plot of 1/V vs. 1/[S]. Gives straight line: y-intercept = 1/Vmax, x-intercept = -1/Km, slope = Km/Vmax. Used to distinguish inhibition types." },
            { q: "What are the essential amino acids?", a: "PVT TIM HaLL — Phenylalanine, Valine, Tryptophan, Threonine, Isoleucine, Methionine, Histidine, Arginine (semi-essential), Leucine, Lysine. Cannot be synthesized; must come from diet." },
            { q: "Which amino acids are purely ketogenic?", a: "Leucine and Lysine. Only amino acids that cannot produce glucose. Converted to acetyl-CoA/acetoacetate." },
            { q: "Which amino acids are both glucogenic and ketogenic?", a: "Isoleucine, Phenylalanine, Tyrosine, Tryptophan, Threonine. (Mnemonic: 'I Pheel The Thrill' or FITTT)." },
            { q: "Zwitterion — definition?", a: "Amino acid form at physiological pH bearing both positive (NH3+) and negative (COO-) charges simultaneously. Net charge = 0 at isoelectric point (pI)." },
            { q: "Isoelectric point (pI) — calculation?", a: "pI = (pKa1 + pKa2) / 2. At pI, amino acid has no net charge and does not migrate in electric field. For acidic AAs: average of two lowest pKas. For basic AAs: average of two highest pKas." },
            { q: "Which amino acids have charged R groups at physiological pH?", a: "Positive (basic): Lysine (pKa ~10.5), Arginine (pKa ~12.5), Histidine (pKa ~6 — partially charged). Negative (acidic): Aspartate (pKa ~3.9), Glutamate (pKa ~4.1)." },
            { q: "Histidine — why clinically unique among charged amino acids?", a: "pKa ~6.0 — only amino acid that acts as a buffer at physiological pH (~7.4). Present in hemoglobin (bohr effect) and active sites of many enzymes." },
            { q: "Primary vs. secondary protein structure?", a: "Primary: amino acid sequence (peptide bonds). Secondary: local folding via hydrogen bonds — alpha helix (right-handed, 3.6 AA/turn) and beta-pleated sheet." },
            { q: "Tertiary vs. quaternary protein structure?", a: "Tertiary: 3D folding of single polypeptide via hydrophobic interactions, disulfide bonds, H-bonds, ionic bonds. Quaternary: multiple polypeptide subunits interacting." },
            { q: "Forces stabilizing tertiary protein structure?", a: "Hydrophobic interactions (dominant — nonpolar R groups bury in core), disulfide bonds (covalent, between cysteines), hydrogen bonds, ionic/electrostatic interactions, Van der Waals forces." },
            { q: "What is protein denaturation?", a: "Loss of 3D structure (secondary/tertiary/quaternary) without breaking peptide bonds. Caused by heat, pH extremes, detergents, urea, organic solvents. Primary structure intact." },
            { q: "What are chaperones (heat shock proteins)?", a: "Proteins that assist correct folding of other proteins; prevent aggregation. Examples: Hsp70 (binds newly synthesized polypeptides), Hsp90, GroEL/GroES (bacterial). ATP-dependent." },
            { q: "Prion diseases — mechanism?", a: "Normal PrPc (alpha-helix rich) converts to abnormal PrPsc (beta-sheet rich) conformation. PrPsc is protease-resistant, accumulates -> neurodegeneration. Examples: CJD, kuru, scrapie, fatal familial insomnia." },
            { q: "Collagen — primary structure feature?", a: "Repeating Gly-X-Y tripeptide. X often proline, Y often hydroxyproline or hydroxylysine. Glycine must be at every 3rd position (fits inside triple helix). Largest protein in body." },
            { q: "Steps of collagen synthesis in order?", a: "Translation (preprocollagen) -> Signal peptide cleaved -> Hydroxylation of Pro/Lys (Vit C, Fe2+ needed) -> Glycosylation -> Triple helix formation (procollagen) -> Secreted -> Propeptides cleaved (tropocollagen) -> Cross-linking by lysyl oxidase (Cu2+)." },
            { q: "Vitamin C deficiency — effect on collagen?", a: "Cannot hydroxylate proline/lysine -> unstable triple helix -> scurvy. Features: perifollicular hemorrhage, gum bleeding/swelling, poor wound healing, corkscrew hairs." },
            { q: "Type I collagen — location and associated disease?", a: "Bone, skin, tendon, dentin, cornea, late wound repair. Most abundant collagen. Defect -> osteogenesis imperfecta (brittle bones, blue sclerae, hearing loss)." },
            { q: "Type II collagen — location?", a: "Hyaline and elastic cartilage, vitreous humor, nucleus pulposus. Defect -> achondrogenesis type II, Stickler syndrome." },
            { q: "Type III collagen — location and associated disease?", a: "Skin, blood vessels, uterus, fetal tissue ('reticulin'). Defect -> Ehlers-Danlos type IV (vascular) -> spontaneous arterial/bowel rupture." },
            { q: "Type IV collagen — location and associated disease?", a: "Basement membranes. Network-forming (not fibrillar). Defect -> Alport syndrome (COL4A3/A4/A5 mutations -> hematuria, renal failure, sensorineural hearing loss, ocular abnormalities)." },
            { q: "Elastin — structure and features?", a: "Rich in glycine, proline, desmosine, isodesmosine (unique cross-links). Lacks regular repeating sequence. Cross-linking by lysyl oxidase (Cu2+). Degraded by elastase (inhibited by alpha1-antitrypsin)." },
            { q: "Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency — mechanism?", a: "Deficiency of alpha1-AT -> uninhibited elastase in lungs -> destruction of alveolar walls -> panacinar emphysema. Also: liver disease (misfolded protein accumulates in hepatocytes). PiZZ phenotype most severe." },
            { q: "What are the three major categories of enzyme cofactors?", a: "Coenzymes (organic, loosely bound — NAD+, FAD, CoA, PLP), prosthetic groups (organic, tightly/covalently bound — FAD in succinate DH, heme, biotin), metal ions (Mg2+, Zn2+, Fe2+, Cu2+, Se)." },
            { q: "NAD+ vs. NADP+ — primary roles?", a: "NAD+ (niacin/B3): primarily catabolic — accepts electrons in glycolysis, TCA, beta-oxidation. NADPH (from NADP+): primarily anabolic — used in fatty acid synthesis, cholesterol synthesis, antioxidant defense (glutathione reduction), P450 reactions." },
            { q: "FAD/FADH2 — derived from which vitamin?", a: "Riboflavin (Vitamin B2). FAD accepts 2 electrons + 2H+. Used in: succinate dehydrogenase (Complex II), alpha-ketoglutarate DH, pyruvate DH, beta-oxidation (acyl-CoA DH), ETF." },
            { q: "Coenzyme A — derived from which vitamin?", a: "Pantothenic acid (Vitamin B5). Carries acyl groups as thioesters. Forms acetyl-CoA (central metabolic intermediate), succinyl-CoA, malonyl-CoA, fatty acyl-CoA." },
            { q: "Pyridoxal phosphate (PLP) — derived from which vitamin and functions?", a: "Vitamin B6 (pyridoxine). Cofactor for: aminotransferases (transamination), decarboxylases (neurotransmitter synthesis — DOPA, serotonin, GABA, histamine), glycogen phosphorylase, ALA synthase (heme synthesis). Deficiency -> sideroblastic anemia, seizures (low GABA), peripheral neuropathy." },
            { q: "Biotin — function and clinical deficiency?", a: "Vitamin B7. Carboxylase cofactor (covalently attached): Pyruvate carboxylase, Acetyl-CoA carboxylase, Propionyl-CoA carboxylase, Methylcrotonyl-CoA carboxylase (mnemonic: PAMM). Deficiency: avidin (raw egg whites) binds biotin -> dermatitis, alopecia, neurological symptoms." },
            { q: "Thiamine (B1) — dependent enzymes?", a: "Pyruvate dehydrogenase (PDH), alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, transketolase (PPP), branched-chain alpha-keto acid dehydrogenase. Deficiency: Wernicke-Korsakoff (confusion, ataxia, ophthalmoplegia; memory loss), beriberi (wet = HF; dry = peripheral neuropathy), Leigh syndrome." },
            { q: "Glycolysis — net ATP yield from one glucose?", a: "Net 2 ATP (substrate-level phosphorylation). 2 NADH produced (cytoplasmic). 2 pyruvate produced. Occurs in cytoplasm. Anaerobic conditions: pyruvate -> lactate (regenerates NAD+)." },
            { q: "Substrate-level phosphorylation in glycolysis — which steps?", a: "Step 7: 1,3-BPG -> 3-PG by phosphoglycerate kinase (produces ATP). Step 10: PEP -> pyruvate by pyruvate kinase (produces ATP). Each occurs twice per glucose = 4 ATP total; net 2 ATP after investment." },
            { q: "Irreversible steps of glycolysis?", a: "Step 1: Hexokinase/glucokinase (glucose -> G6P). Step 3: Phosphofructokinase-1 (F6P -> F1,6-BP) — rate-limiting step. Step 10: Pyruvate kinase (PEP -> pyruvate)." },
            { q: "Phosphofructokinase-1 (PFK-1) — allosteric regulators?", a: "Activated by: AMP, ADP, fructose-2,6-bisphosphate (F2,6-BP — most potent). Inhibited by: ATP, citrate, H+. Rate-limiting enzyme of glycolysis." },
            { q: "Pyruvate kinase deficiency — consequence?", a: "Most common inherited RBC enzyme deficiency (after G6PD). Accumulation of 2,3-BPG -> right-shift of O2-Hgb curve. Hemolytic anemia — rigid RBCs (RBCs depend entirely on glycolysis). AR inheritance." },
            { q: "Glucokinase vs. hexokinase — differences?", a: "Glucokinase (liver/pancreatic beta-cells): high Km (low affinity), not saturated at normal glucose, not inhibited by G6P, induced by insulin. Hexokinase (all tissues): low Km (high affinity), inhibited by G6P, saturated at normal glucose." },
            { q: "Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex (PDH) — cofactors?", a: "Thiamine (B1), Lipoic acid, CoA (B5/pantothenate), FAD (B2), NAD+ (B3). Mnemonic: 'Tender Loving Care For Nancy.' Produces acetyl-CoA + NADH + CO2." },
            { q: "PDH regulation — activated vs. inhibited by?", a: "Activated by: pyruvate, ADP, NAD+, Ca2+ (exercise), insulin (via phosphatase). Inhibited by: acetyl-CoA, NADH, ATP (product inhibition), PDH kinase (phosphorylation)." },
            { q: "PDH deficiency — consequence?", a: "Cannot convert pyruvate -> acetyl-CoA -> pyruvate shunted to lactate -> lactic acidosis. Neurons cannot use glucose effectively. Treatment: ketogenic diet (bypasses PDH — fats -> acetyl-CoA directly). X-linked." },
            { q: "TCA cycle — products per acetyl-CoA turn?", a: "3 NADH, 1 FADH2, 1 GTP, 2 CO2. Net ATP from one turn: ~10 ATP (3 NADH x 2.5 + 1 FADH2 x 1.5 + 1 GTP = ~10)." },
            { q: "Rate-limiting enzyme of TCA cycle?", a: "Isocitrate dehydrogenase (isocitrate -> alpha-ketoglutarate). Activated by ADP, Ca2+. Inhibited by ATP, NADH." },
            { q: "Which TCA cycle enzymes require thiamine (B1)?", a: "Pyruvate dehydrogenase (technically feeds TCA), alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase (succinyl-CoA + NADH + CO2). Both require: TPP, lipoic acid, CoA, FAD, NAD+." },
            { q: "Anaplerotic reactions — definition and example?", a: "Reactions that replenish TCA cycle intermediates. Key example: pyruvate carboxylase (pyruvate -> oxaloacetate, biotin-dependent). Also: transamination of aspartate -> oxaloacetate." },
            { q: "Electron transport chain — which complexes pump protons?", a: "Complexes I, III, IV pump H+ from matrix to intermembrane space. Complex II (succinate DH) does NOT pump protons." },
            { q: "P/O ratio — definition?", a: "ATP produced per oxygen atom consumed. NADH: 2.5 ATP per NADH. FADH2: 1.5 ATP per FADH2. (Classic values: 3 and 2 are outdated but still appear in some sources.)" },
            { q: "Gluconeogenesis — where does it occur?", a: "Primarily liver (90%), kidney cortex (10%, important in prolonged fasting/acidosis). NOT in muscle (lacks glucose-6-phosphatase)." },
            { q: "Irreversible steps of gluconeogenesis (bypass reactions)?", a: "Pyruvate -> OAA: pyruvate carboxylase (biotin, mitochondria). OAA -> PEP: PEPCK (GTP). F1,6-BP -> F6P: fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase. G6P -> glucose: glucose-6-phosphatase (liver/kidney only)." },
            { q: "Why can't muscle perform gluconeogenesis?", a: "Lacks glucose-6-phosphatase -> cannot release free glucose into blood. Can perform glycolysis and glycogenolysis for own use only." },
            { q: "Cori cycle — what is it?", a: "Lactate produced by RBCs and exercising muscle -> travels to liver -> converted to glucose via gluconeogenesis -> returned to muscle. Net: transfers energy cost to liver (2 ATP consumed in muscle, 6 ATP used in liver)." },
            { q: "Glycogen structure — alpha-1,4 vs. alpha-1,6 linkages?", a: "Alpha-1,4: linear chain (glucose-glucose along chain). Alpha-1,6: branch points (every 8–12 residues). Branching increases solubility and number of non-reducing ends for rapid mobilization." },
            { q: "Glycogen synthase — substrate and regulation?", a: "Substrate: UDP-glucose (from glucose-1-phosphate + UTP via UDP-glucose pyrophosphorylase). Activated by glucose-6-phosphate, insulin (via dephosphorylation). Inhibited by glucagon/epinephrine (via PKA phosphorylation)." },
            { q: "Glycogen phosphorylase — regulation?", a: "Activated by: glucagon/epinephrine (via PKA -> phosphorylation), AMP (allosteric, muscle). Inhibited by: insulin (dephosphorylation), glucose (liver), ATP, G6P." },
            { q: "Von Gierke disease (GSD Type I)?", a: "Glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency -> cannot release glucose from liver -> severe fasting hypoglycemia, hepatomegaly, lactic acidosis, hyperlipidemia, hyperuricemia. AR." },
            { q: "Pompe disease (GSD Type II)?", a: "Lysosomal acid alpha-1,4-glucosidase (acid maltase) deficiency -> glycogen accumulates in lysosomes -> cardiomegaly, hypotonia, early death. Treatment: alglucosidase alfa (enzyme replacement). AR." },
            { q: "McArdle disease (GSD Type V)?", a: "Muscle glycogen phosphorylase deficiency -> cannot break down glycogen in muscle -> exercise intolerance, myoglobinuria, painful cramps. No rise in serum lactate with exercise. AR." },
            { q: "Pentose phosphate pathway (PPP) — functions?", a: "Produces NADPH (for reductive biosynthesis + antioxidant defense) and ribose-5-phosphate (for nucleotide synthesis). Occurs in cytoplasm. Active in: liver, RBCs, adrenal cortex, mammary gland, testes." },
            { q: "Rate-limiting enzyme of PPP?", a: "Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD). Produces 2 NADPH per glucose. Inhibited by NADPH (product inhibition)." },
            { q: "G6PD deficiency — mechanism and triggers?", a: "X-linked recessive. Cannot regenerate NADPH -> cannot reduce glutathione -> RBCs vulnerable to oxidative stress -> hemolytic anemia. Triggers: primaquine, dapsone, fava beans, infections, nitrofurantoin. Heinz bodies (denatured Hgb), bite cells on smear." },
            { q: "NADPH uses in the body?", a: "Fatty acid synthesis (FA synthase), cholesterol synthesis (HMG-CoA reductase), glutathione reduction (antioxidant), phagocyte respiratory burst (NADPH oxidase), cytochrome P450 reactions, nitric oxide synthesis." },
            { q: "Fatty acid synthesis — location and key enzyme?", a: "Cytoplasm (cytosol). Key enzyme: Acetyl-CoA carboxylase (ACC) — rate-limiting, adds CO2 to acetyl-CoA -> malonyl-CoA (biotin-dependent). Fatty acid synthase (FAS) builds the chain." },
            { q: "Acetyl-CoA carboxylase (ACC) — regulation?", a: "Activated by: citrate, insulin (dephosphorylation). Inhibited by: palmitoyl-CoA (product feedback), glucagon/epinephrine (via PKA phosphorylation), AMPK. Rate-limiting enzyme of fatty acid synthesis." },
            { q: "Beta-oxidation of fatty acids — location and products?", a: "Mitochondrial matrix. Each cycle removes 2 carbons as acetyl-CoA, produces 1 NADH + 1 FADH2. Net ATP from palmitate (16C): ~106 ATP." },
            { q: "Carnitine shuttle — purpose and steps?", a: "Long-chain fatty acids cannot cross inner mitochondrial membrane. Carnitine acyltransferase I (outer membrane, rate-limiting) transfers fatty acyl group to carnitine -> crosses inner membrane -> released as acyl-CoA in matrix. Inhibited by malonyl-CoA (prevents simultaneous synthesis + oxidation)." },
            { q: "Carnitine deficiency — presentation?", a: "Primary (OCTN2 mutations) or secondary (valproate, pivampicillin). Impaired fatty acid oxidation -> hypoketotic hypoglycemia, skeletal myopathy, cardiomyopathy, lipid accumulation. Treatment: L-carnitine supplementation." },
            { q: "Ketone body synthesis (ketogenesis) — where and substrate?", a: "Liver mitochondria only. Substrate: acetyl-CoA (from excess beta-oxidation). Products: acetoacetate, beta-hydroxybutyrate (transported to tissues), acetone (volatile, fruity breath)." },
            { q: "Which organ cannot use ketone bodies?", a: "Liver (lacks succinyl-CoA transferase/thiophorase = 3-oxoacid CoA transferase). RBCs also cannot (no mitochondria). Brain adapts to use ketones during starvation." },
            { q: "HMG-CoA — metabolic crossroads?", a: "In cytosol/ER: HMG-CoA reductase -> mevalonate -> cholesterol synthesis. In mitochondria: HMG-CoA lyase -> acetoacetate -> ketogenesis. Different compartments, different fates." },
            { q: "Cholesterol synthesis — rate-limiting enzyme and regulation?", a: "HMG-CoA reductase (HMG-CoA -> mevalonate). Inhibited by statins (competitive inhibitors). Inhibited by: cholesterol (via SREBP pathway), glucagon, cortisol. Activated by: insulin." },
            { q: "Statins — mechanism and side effects?", a: "Competitively inhibit HMG-CoA reductase -> ↓intracellular cholesterol -> ↑LDL receptor expression -> ↑LDL uptake from blood. Side effects: myopathy/rhabdomyolysis (↓CoQ10), hepatotoxicity, teratogenic." },
            { q: "Lipoprotein structure — general?", a: "Core: triglycerides + cholesterol esters. Shell: phospholipids + free cholesterol + apolipoproteins. Denser = less lipid, more protein. HDL > LDL > IDL > VLDL > chylomicrons (by density)." },
            { q: "Chylomicrons — origin, contents, function?", a: "Made in intestinal epithelium. Carry dietary (exogenous) triglycerides and cholesterol from gut to periphery. ApoB-48, ApoC-II, ApoE. ApoC-II activates lipoprotein lipase (LPL)." },
            { q: "VLDL — origin and function?", a: "Made in liver. Carries endogenous TGs and cholesterol to periphery. ApoB-100, ApoC-II, ApoE. Processed by LPL -> IDL -> LDL." },
            { q: "LDL — function and receptor?", a: "Delivers cholesterol to peripheral tissues. Taken up by LDL receptor (via ApoB-100) on cells. Elevated LDL -> atherosclerosis. Reduced by statins, PCSK9 inhibitors." },
            { q: "HDL — function and key apolipoprotein?", a: "Reverse cholesterol transport — picks up cholesterol from peripheral tissues -> liver. ApoA-I (activates LCAT — esterifies cholesterol). Protective against atherosclerosis." },
            { q: "Lipoprotein lipase (LPL) — activator and location?", a: "Located on capillary endothelium of adipose, heart, muscle. Activated by ApoC-II. Hydrolyzes TGs in chylomicrons and VLDL -> fatty acids released to tissues." },
            { q: "Familial hypercholesterolemia — mechanism?", a: "LDL receptor mutation (AD) -> impaired LDL uptake -> markedly elevated LDL. Homozygous: total cholesterol >700 mg/dL, MI in childhood, tendon xanthomas. Treated with statins + PCSK9 inhibitors." },
            { q: "Urea cycle — where does it occur?", a: "Partially in mitochondria (first two steps) + cytoplasm. Only in liver. Converts toxic NH3 -> urea (excreted by kidneys)." },
            { q: "Urea cycle steps in order?", a: "NH3 + CO2 -> carbamoyl phosphate (CPS-I, mitochondria) -> citrulline (+ ornithine) -> argininosuccinate -> arginine -> urea + ornithine. Ornithine is the carrier (recycled)." },
            { q: "Rate-limiting enzyme of urea cycle?", a: "Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I (CPS-I). Activated by N-acetylglutamate (NAG), which is synthesized from acetyl-CoA + glutamate; induced by arginine." },
            { q: "Hyperammonemia — causes and symptoms?", a: "Liver failure, urea cycle enzyme defects, organic acidemias. Symptoms: tremor, slurred speech, somnolence, cerebral edema, coma, death. NH3 -> glutamine in brain -> depletes alpha-ketoglutarate -> inhibits TCA." },
            { q: "Ornithine transcarbamylase (OTC) deficiency — features?", a: "Most common urea cycle defect. X-linked recessive. Orotic acid elevated (excess carbamoyl phosphate -> pyrimidine synthesis). Low BUN, elevated NH3. Treatment: low-protein diet, sodium benzoate/phenylbutyrate, arginine." },
            { q: "Phenylketonuria (PKU) — enzyme and mechanism?", a: "Phenylalanine hydroxylase (PAH) deficiency (or tetrahydrobiopterin/BH4 deficiency). Phenylalanine -> cannot form tyrosine -> accumulates -> phenylketones in urine. AR." },
            { q: "PKU — clinical features and treatment?", a: "Intellectual disability, seizures, musty/mousy urine odor, fair skin/hair/eyes (↓melanin), eczema. Treatment: phenylalanine-restricted diet + tyrosine supplementation + BH4 (sapropterin) for some variants." },
            { q: "Maple syrup urine disease (MSUD) — defect?", a: "Branched-chain alpha-keto acid dehydrogenase deficiency (thiamine-dependent). Accumulation of leucine, isoleucine, valine and their keto acids. Sweet/maple syrup smell urine. Neonatal encephalopathy, feeding difficulties. Treatment: restriction of BCAAs + thiamine." },
            { q: "Homocystinuria — causes and features?", a: "Cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency (most common) -> homocysteine accumulates. Features: Marfanoid habitus, downward lens subluxation (ectopia lentis), DVT/stroke, intellectual disability, osteoporosis. Treatment: B6 (many respond), B12, folate, low-methionine diet." },
            { q: "Alkaptonuria — defect and features?", a: "Homogentisate oxidase deficiency -> homogentisic acid accumulates. Features: dark urine on standing, ochronosis (dark pigment in connective tissue), arthritis. AR. Benign compared to other amino acid disorders." },
            { q: "Albinism — types and mechanism?", a: "Oculocutaneous albinism: tyrosinase deficiency (type 1) -> cannot convert tyrosine -> melanin. Features: absent pigmentation, photophobia, nystagmus, ↑skin cancer risk. Chediak-Higashi: albinism + immunodeficiency + giant granules." },
            { q: "Cystinuria — mechanism and treatment?", a: "Defective renal tubular transporter for dibasic amino acids (COLA: Cystine, Ornithine, Lysine, Arginine) -> cystine precipitates in urine -> recurrent kidney stones (hexagonal crystals, radiopaque). Treatment: hydration, alkalinization (cystine soluble at high pH), D-penicillamine." },
            { q: "Hartnup disease — mechanism and features?", a: "Defective neutral amino acid transporter (intestine + renal tubule) -> tryptophan not absorbed -> niacin deficiency -> pellagra-like symptoms (3 Ds: dermatitis, diarrhea, dementia). Treatment: high-protein diet, niacin supplementation." },
            { q: "Tyrosinemia type I — defect?", a: "Fumarylacetoacetase deficiency -> succinylacetone accumulation -> inhibits heme synthesis (ALA dehydratase) -> liver failure, renal tubular dysfunction (Fanconi syndrome), hepatocellular carcinoma. Treatment: NTBC (nitisinone) + tyrosine restriction." },
            { q: "De novo purine synthesis — key committed step?", a: "PRPP amidotransferase (PRPP + glutamine -> 5-phosphoribosylamine). Rate-limiting step. Inhibited by AMP, GMP (product feedback). Requires folate-derived cofactors (N10-formyl-THF)." },
            { q: "De novo pyrimidine synthesis — first step and key enzyme?", a: "Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase II (cytoplasmic, uses glutamine) -> carbamoyl phosphate + aspartate -> orotic acid -> UMP. Distinct from CPS-I (mitochondrial, uses NH3 — urea cycle)." },
            { q: "Orotic aciduria — types?", a: "Type 1: UMP synthase deficiency (OPRT + ODCase deficiency) -> orotic acid accumulates, megaloblastic anemia, growth retardation. Treatment: uridine. NOT hyperammonemia (urea cycle intact). Also seen in OTC deficiency (elevated orotic acid + hyperammonemia)." },
            { q: "Lesch-Nyhan syndrome — defect and features?", a: "HGPRT (hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase) deficiency -> cannot salvage purines -> ↑de novo synthesis -> ↑uric acid. Features: hyperuricemia, gout, intellectual disability, self-mutilation (biting fingers/lips), choreoathetosis. X-linked recessive." },
            { q: "Gout — biochemical mechanism?", a: "Hyperuricemia (↑uric acid from purine degradation via xanthine oxidase) -> monosodium urate crystals deposit in joints. Negatively birefringent needle-shaped crystals. Treatment: allopurinol (xanthine oxidase inhibitor), febuxostat, colchicine (acute)." },
            { q: "Allopurinol — mechanism and interactions?", a: "Structural analog of hypoxanthine; inhibits xanthine oxidase -> ↓uric acid production. Also inhibits metabolism of azathioprine/6-MP (both degraded by xanthine oxidase) -> toxic dose accumulation if combined. Dose reduction required." },
            { q: "Folate — biochemical role and deficiency effects?", a: "Tetrahydrofolate (THF) carries one-carbon units for purine synthesis and thymidylate synthesis (dUMP -> dTMP via thymidylate synthase). Deficiency: megaloblastic anemia (↓DNA synthesis), neural tube defects. No neurological symptoms (cf. B12 deficiency)." },
            { q: "Vitamin B12 — biochemical roles?", a: "Two reactions: (1) Methylcobalamin: methionine synthase (homocysteine + methyl-THF -> methionine + THF). (2) Adenosylcobalamin: methylmalonyl-CoA mutase (methylmalonyl-CoA -> succinyl-CoA). Deficiency: megaloblastic anemia + subacute combined degeneration of spinal cord + elevated homocysteine + elevated methylmalonic acid." },
            { q: "Folate trap — mechanism?", a: "B12 deficiency -> methionine synthase inactive -> methyl-THF cannot donate methyl group -> THF not regenerated -> folate 'trapped' as methyl-THF -> functional folate deficiency -> megaloblastic anemia (even with adequate folate intake)." },
            { q: "Methotrexate and trimethoprim — mechanisms?", a: "Methotrexate (cancer/autoimmune): inhibits dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR) -> ↓THF -> ↓purine/thymidylate synthesis. Trimethoprim (antibacterial): inhibits bacterial DHFR (100-1000x more selective for bacterial enzyme). Treat methotrexate toxicity with leucovorin (folinic acid — bypasses DHFR)." },
            { q: "5-fluorouracil (5-FU) — mechanism?", a: "Fluorouracil -> FdUMP -> irreversibly inhibits thymidylate synthase (with methyleneTHF) -> ↓dTMP -> ↓DNA synthesis. Treats colorectal, breast, skin cancer. Toxicity: myelosuppression, mucositis." },
            { q: "DNA structure — Watson-Crick base pairing rules?", a: "Adenine pairs with Thymine (2 hydrogen bonds). Guanine pairs with Cytosine (3 hydrogen bonds — stronger; GC content correlates with DNA melting temperature). In RNA: Uracil replaces Thymine (pairs with A)." },
            { q: "Chargaff's rules?", a: "In any ds-DNA: [A] = [T] and [G] = [C]. Therefore: purines = pyrimidines. A/T and G/C ratios constant within a species regardless of tissue." },
            { q: "Transitions vs. transversions — mutation types?", a: "Transition: purine -> purine or pyrimidine -> pyrimidine (A<->G, C<->T). Transversion: purine -> pyrimidine or vice versa (A/G <-> C/T). Transitions more common in spontaneous mutations." },
            { q: "What is a missense mutation?", a: "Single nucleotide change -> different amino acid incorporated. Example: HbS (sickle cell) — Glu -> Val at position 6 of beta-globin (GAG -> GTG)." },
            { q: "What is a nonsense mutation?", a: "Point mutation creates premature stop codon -> truncated, nonfunctional protein. Example: many thalassemia mutations, Duchenne MD (some cases)." },
            { q: "What is a frameshift mutation?", a: "Insertion or deletion of nucleotides (not a multiple of 3) -> disrupts reading frame -> abnormal protein from mutation onward + often premature stop. Example: most Duchenne MD mutations (exon deletions)." },
            { q: "Sickle cell anemia — molecular basis?", a: "Beta-globin gene: single nucleotide change (A->T, codon 6) -> Glu6Val. HbS polymerizes under low O2 -> sickling. AR. HbS protects against P. falciparum malaria (heterozygote advantage)." },
            { q: "HbA vs. HbA2 vs. HbF — compositions?", a: "HbA: alpha2beta2 (adult, 97%). HbA2: alpha2delta2 (adult, 2.5%). HbF: alpha2gamma2 (fetal; ↑in beta-thalassemia; higher O2 affinity than HbA — left shift)." },
            { q: "Oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve — right shift causes?", a: "Right shift (↓O2 affinity, ↑O2 delivery): ↑CO2, ↑H+ (↓pH), ↑temperature, ↑2,3-BPG. Mnemonic: 'CADET, face right.' (CO2, Acid, DPG, Exercise, Temperature)." },
            { q: "Bohr effect — definition?", a: "↑CO2/↑H+ in tissues -> promotes O2 release from hemoglobin (right shift). In lungs: ↓CO2/↑pH -> promotes O2 binding (left shift). Histidine residues of Hb act as proton acceptors." },
            { q: "What is 2,3-BPG and its significance?", a: "2,3-bisphosphoglycerate (2,3-BPG): produced by RBCs from 3-PG (Rapoport-Luebering shunt). Binds deoxyhemoglobin -> stabilizes T-state -> ↓O2 affinity -> ↑O2 delivery to tissues. ↑in: high altitude, chronic anemia, hypoxia." },
            { q: "Methemoglobin — definition and treatment?", a: "Hb with Fe3+ (oxidized) instead of Fe2+ -> cannot carry O2 -> functional anemia + left shift of remaining Hb. Causes: nitrites, dapsone, benzocaine. Treatment: methylene blue (NADPH-dependent) or vitamin C. Chocolate-colored blood." },
            { q: "Carbon monoxide poisoning — mechanism?", a: "CO binds Hb with 200-250x greater affinity than O2 -> carboxyhemoglobin -> left shift of O2 curve -> impaired O2 delivery. Cherry-red skin/mucosa. Treatment: 100% O2 (or hyperbaric O2)." },
            { q: "Hormone classifications — mechanism of action?", a: "Lipid-soluble (steroid hormones, thyroid hormone, Vitamin D, retinoids): diffuse through membrane -> intracellular receptors -> gene transcription. Water-soluble (peptides, catecholamines): bind surface receptors -> second messengers." },
            { q: "Nuclear receptors — mechanism?", a: "Ligand-binding causes conformational change -> receptor dimerizes -> binds hormone response elements (HREs) in DNA -> activates/represses gene transcription. Examples: glucocorticoid receptor, estrogen receptor, thyroid hormone receptor (TR), RAR, RXR, VDR." },
            { q: "G protein signaling — cAMP pathway key features?", a: "Gs -> adenylyl cyclase -> ↑cAMP -> PKA -> phosphorylates serine/threonine. Terminated by: phosphodiesterase (cAMP->AMP) and GTPase activity of Galpha. Amplification at each step." },
            { q: "Enzyme kinetics — what does kcat (turnover number) represent?", a: "Maximum number of substrate molecules converted to product per enzyme molecule per second when enzyme is fully saturated. kcat = Vmax/[E]total. High kcat = very efficient enzyme." },
            { q: "What is the catalytic efficiency of an enzyme?", a: "kcat/Km ratio — measures enzyme's efficiency at low substrate concentrations. Upper limit ~10^8–10^9 M^-1s^-1 (diffusion limit). Used to compare enzymes." },
            { q: "Allosteric enzymes — characteristics?", a: "Multiple subunits, sigmoidal (not hyperbolic) kinetics. Homotropic: substrate itself is allosteric activator (cooperativity — e.g., hemoglobin). Heterotropic: different molecule acts as activator/inhibitor. Do not follow simple Michaelis-Menten kinetics." },
            { q: "Hill coefficient (n) — significance?", a: "Measures cooperativity. n = 1: no cooperativity (Michaelis-Menten). n > 1: positive cooperativity (sigmoid curve — e.g., Hb, n approx 2.8). n < 1: negative cooperativity (rare)." },
            { q: "Ubiquitin-proteasome system — mechanism?", a: "Ubiquitin (small protein) covalently attached to target protein via E1 (activating), E2 (conjugating), E3 (ligating — specificity) enzymes. Polyubiquitinated protein -> 26S proteasome -> degraded. Regulates cell cycle, protein quality control, inflammation." },
            { q: "What is the signal peptide?", a: "N-terminal hydrophobic amino acid sequence (~15–30 AA) directing protein to ER for secretory/membrane pathway. Recognized by SRP -> co-translational translocation into ER lumen -> cleaved by signal peptidase." },
            { q: "What is a glycoprotein vs. proteoglycan?", a: "Glycoprotein: protein with attached oligosaccharides (branched, N- or O-linked). Proteoglycan: protein with glycosaminoglycan (GAG) chains (highly sulfated, linear, e.g., heparan sulfate, chondroitin sulfate). GAGs attract water -> compressive resistance in cartilage." },
            { q: "Lysosomal storage diseases — general mechanism?", a: "Deficiency of lysosomal hydrolase -> substrate accumulates within lysosomes -> progressive cellular dysfunction. Most AR. Substrate specificity determines tissue involvement." },
            { q: "Gaucher disease — defect, substrate, features?", a: "Glucocerebrosidase (beta-glucosidase) deficiency -> glucocerebroside accumulates in macrophages. Most common LSD. Features: hepatosplenomegaly, bone pain/fractures, pancytopenia, 'crumpled tissue paper' cells (Gaucher cells). No CNS in type I. Treatment: enzyme replacement (imiglucerase)." },
            { q: "Niemann-Pick disease — defect and features?", a: "Sphingomyelinase deficiency (types A, B) -> sphingomyelin accumulates in macrophages/neurons. Type A: severe CNS, cherry-red macula, hepatosplenomegaly, death by age 3. Type B: no CNS. Foam cells on biopsy." },
            { q: "Tay-Sachs disease — defect and features?", a: "Hexosaminidase A deficiency -> GM2 ganglioside accumulates in neurons. Progressive neurodegeneration, cherry-red macula, hyperacusis, no hepatosplenomegaly (cf. Niemann-Pick). Ashkenazi Jewish population. Onion-skin lysosomes on EM." },
            { q: "Fabry disease — defect, features, inheritance?", a: "Alpha-galactosidase A deficiency -> globotriaosylceramide (Gb3) accumulates in vascular endothelium. X-linked recessive. Features: angiokeratomas, acroparesthesias, renal failure, cardiomyopathy, corneal opacities (corneal verticillata). Treatment: enzyme replacement (agalsidase)." },
            { q: "Hurler syndrome (MPS I) — defect and features?", a: "Alpha-L-iduronidase deficiency -> heparan + dermatan sulfate accumulate. Features: coarse facies, corneal clouding, hepatosplenomegaly, intellectual disability, airway obstruction, gibbus deformity, cardiac disease. AR. Gargoylism." },
            { q: "Hunter syndrome (MPS II) — features and inheritance?", a: "Iduronate-2-sulfatase deficiency. X-linked recessive (only X-linked MPS). Similar to Hurler but milder: NO corneal clouding, behavior problems, slower progression. 'Hunter has no cloudy corneas, hunts at night.'" },
            { q: "I-cell disease — why is it called that?", a: "'Inclusion cell' disease. Lysosomes appear full of inclusions (undigested substrates). Cause: failure to add M6P targeting signal -> lysosomal enzymes secreted extracellularly. Clinically resembles Hurler but worse." },
            { q: "What is the respiratory burst in phagocytes?", a: "NADPH oxidase -> O2- (superoxide) -> H2O2 (by SOD) -> HOCl (by myeloperoxidase, Cl-). Kills bacteria. Chronic granulomatous disease: NADPH oxidase defect -> recurrent catalase-positive organism infections." },
            { q: "Reactive oxygen species (ROS) — antioxidant defenses?", a: "Superoxide dismutase (SOD): O2- -> H2O2. Catalase: H2O2 -> H2O + O2. Glutathione peroxidase: H2O2 + GSH -> H2O (requires NADPH from G6PD to regenerate GSH). Vitamin E, Vitamin C, beta-carotene also scavenge." },
            { q: "What is the Pasteur effect?", a: "In the presence of O2, oxidative phosphorylation is preferred over glycolysis -> glycolytic rate decreases. Mechanism: ↑ATP inhibits PFK-1. Absent in cancer cells (Warburg effect — prefer glycolysis even with O2)." },
            { q: "What are futile cycles (substrate cycles)?", a: "Simultaneous operation of opposing pathways (e.g., glycolysis + gluconeogenesis at same step) -> net ATP hydrolysis -> heat generation. Physiologically regulated to prevent futile cycling except in thermogenesis (brown fat)." }
          ]
        },
        {
          topic: 'General Embryology',
          icon: 'baby',
          cards: [
            { q: "Where do primordial germ cells (PGCs) originate?", a: "In the epiblast during the second week of development. They migrate to the yolk sac wall and then to the gonadal ridges by week 5." },
            { q: "At what stage is the primary oocyte arrested before puberty?", a: "Prophase of Meiosis I (Diplotene stage). Arrested by Oocyte Maturation Inhibitor (OMI)." },
            { q: "At what stage is the secondary oocyte arrested before fertilization?", a: "Metaphase of Meiosis II. Arrested for ~24 hours; completed only if fertilized." },
            { q: "What is 'Capacitation'?", a: "The period of conditioning in the female reproductive tract (~7 hours) where the glycoprotein coat and seminal plasma proteins are removed from the sperm's acrosome. Necessary for fertilization." },
            { q: "What is the 'Acrosome Reaction'?", a: "The release of enzymes (e.g., acrosin, hyaluronidase) from the sperm's acrosome upon binding to ZP3 on the zona pellucida. Allows penetration to the oocyte membrane." },
            { q: "Define 'Polyspermy' prevention.", a: "The cortical and zona reactions: release of lysosomal enzymes from oocyte granules makes the zona pellucida impermeable and changes oocyte membrane polarity once one sperm enters." },
            { q: "Most common site of fertilization?", a: "The ampulla of the uterine tube (widest part)." },
            { q: "What is a 'Morula'?", a: "A solid ball of 16 cells (blastomeres) formed about 3 days after fertilization. Enters the uterus." },
            { q: "When does implantation of the blastocyst typically begin?", a: "Around day 6 after fertilization. Occurs in the superior posterior wall of the uterine body." },
            { q: "What are the two layers of the Trophoblast?", a: "Cytotrophoblast (inner, mitotic layer) and Syncytiotrophoblast (outer, invasive, non-mitotic multinucleated mass)." },
            { q: "What are the two layers of the Bilaminar Germ Disc?", a: "Epiblast (dorsal, columnar cells) and Hypoblast (ventral, cuboidal cells). Formed in Week 2 ('Rule of Twos')." },
            { q: "What hormone is secreted by the Syncytiotrophoblast to maintain the Corpus Luteum?", a: "Human Chorionic Gonadotropin (hCG). Basis of pregnancy tests; maintains progesterone production." },
            { q: "What two cavities form during the second week?", a: "Amniotic cavity (within epiblast) and Yolk sac (Exocoelomic cavity, within hypoblast)." },
            { q: "Where is the most common site for an ectopic pregnancy?", a: "The uterine tube (specifically the ampulla). 95% of ectopics are tubal." },
            { q: "Define 'Gastrulation.'", a: "The process of converting the bilaminar disc into a trilaminar germ disc (Ectoderm, Mesoderm, Endoderm). Occurs in Week 3." },
            { q: "What structure initiates gastrulation?", a: "The formation of the Primitive Streak on the surface of the epiblast." },
            { q: "From which layer do all three germ layers ultimately derive?", a: "The Epiblast. Cells migrate through the primitive streak to displace hypoblast (endoderm) and form mesoderm." },
            { q: "What is the function of the Notochord?", a: "It acts as the basis for the axial skeleton (becomes nucleus pulposus) and induces the overlying ectoderm to form the neural plate." },
            { q: "What is a 'Sacrococcygeal Teratoma'?", a: "A tumor derived from remnants of the primitive streak; contains tissues from all three germ layers. Most common tumor in newborns." },
            { q: "What are the three subdivisions of the Intraembryonic Mesoderm?", a: "Paraxial (somites), Intermediate (urogenital), and Lateral Plate mesoderm (body wall/cavities)." },
            { q: "Derivatives of the Ectoderm?", a: "CNS, PNS, sensory epithelium (ear/nose/eye), epidermis, hair, nails, enamel of teeth, anterior pituitary." },
            { q: "Derivatives of the Neural Crest Cells (the '4th Germ Layer')?", a: "Craniofacial skeleton, melanocytes, adrenal medulla, Schwann cells, glial cells, meninges, odontoblasts, parafollicular C cells." },
            { q: "Derivatives of the Mesoderm?", a: "Muscle, cartilage, bone, dermis, circulatory system (heart/vessels), urogenital system (except bladder), spleen, adrenal cortex." },
            { q: "Derivatives of the Endoderm?", a: "Epithelial lining of GI tract, respiratory tract, urinary bladder, and parenchyma of thyroid, parathyroid, liver, and pancreas." },
            { q: "What determines the Cranio-Caudal axis of the embryo?", a: "Homeobox (HOX) genes. Regulated by Retinoic Acid concentration." },
            { q: "What components form the Placenta?", a: "Chorion frondosum (fetal part, derived from trophoblast/extraembryonic mesoderm) and Decidua basalis (maternal part, modified endometrium)." },
            { q: "How many arteries and veins are in a normal umbilical cord?", a: "Two umbilical arteries (carry deoxygenated blood to placenta) and one umbilical vein (carries oxygenated blood to fetus)." },
            { q: "Define 'Polyhydramnios.'", a: "Excess amniotic fluid (>1500 ml). Associated with GI atresias (cannot swallow), maternal diabetes, or anencephaly." },
            { q: "Define 'Oligohydramnios.'", a: "Decreased amniotic fluid (<400 ml). Associated with renal agenesis (Potter sequence — no fetal urine) or placental insufficiency." },
            { q: "What are the 'Tertiary Villi'?", a: "Chorionic villi that contain differentiated blood vessels (formed by end of Week 3). Ready for exchange." },
            { q: "When is the embryo most susceptible to teratogens?", a: "Weeks 3 to 8 (the period of organogenesis). Risk of major structural malformations." },
            { q: "Characteristic features of Fetal Alcohol Syndrome (FAS)?", a: "Maxillary hypoplasia, smooth philtrum, thin upper lip, intellectual disability, microcephaly." },
            { q: "What defect is caused by a failure of the neural folds to fuse?", a: "Neural Tube Defects (NTDs) like Anencephaly (cranial) or Spina Bifida (caudal)." },
            { q: "Supplementation of what vitamin prevents NTDs?", a: "Folic Acid (Vitamin B9). Recommended dose: 0.4 mg/day (higher if previous history)." },
            { q: "What is 'Situs Inversus'?", a: "Complete reversal of the position of internal organs. Often due to cilia dysfunction during gastrulation (Kartagener syndrome)." },
            { q: "Which signaling molecule is the 'Master Organizer' of the body axes?", a: "Sonic Hedgehog (SHH), which is secreted by the notochord and prechordal plate." },
            { q: "Which gene family is responsible for the identity of segments along the cranio-caudal axis?", a: "HOX (Homeobox) genes." },
            { q: "What is the role of Bone Morphogenetic Proteins (BMPs) in the early embryo?", a: "In the presence of BMP-4, the ectoderm becomes epidermis; if BMP-4 is inhibited (by Noggin/Chordin), the ectoderm becomes neural tissue." },
            { q: "Which factor is essential for establishing Left-Right asymmetry?", a: "Fibloblast Growth Factor 8 (FGF8) and the signaling molecule Serotonin (5-HT)." },
            { q: "What is the function of the Primitive Node (Hensen’s Node)?", a: "It acts as the 'Organizer,' secreting signaling molecules that induce gastrulation and neural tube formation." },
            { q: "How do Dizygotic (Fraternal) twins form?", a: "The simultaneous shedding of two oocytes and fertilization by two different spermatozoa. No genetic relation beyond siblings." },
            { q: "What is the placental arrangement for almost all Dizygotic twins?", a: "Dichorionic and Diamniotic (separate placentas and sacs)." },
            { q: "When does a split occur to result in Dichorionic/Diamniotic Monozygotic twins?", a: "At the 2-cell stage (within the first 3 days)." },
            { q: "When does a split occur to result in Monochorionic/Diamniotic Monozygotic twins?", a: "At the blastocyst stage (Days 4–8); this is the most common arrangement for identical twins (~65%)." },
            { q: "When does a split occur to result in Monochorionic/Monoamniotic twins?", a: "During the bilaminar germ disc stage (Days 8–13); high risk for umbilical cord entanglement." },
            { q: "What leads to Conjoined (Siamese) Twins?", a: "Partial splitting of the primitive streak or the embryonic disc after Day 13." },
            { q: "What is Twin-to-Twin Transfusion Syndrome (TTTS)?", a: "A complication in monochorionic pregnancies where vascular anastomoses in the placenta cause one twin (donor) to become anemic/small and the other (recipient) to become plethoric/large." },
            { q: "What does the Paraxial Mesoderm form?", a: "Somites (42–44 pairs), which further divide into sclerotome, myotome, and dermatome." },
            { q: "What does the Sclerotome derive?", a: "Vertebrae and ribs." },
            { q: "What does the Intermediate Mesoderm derive?", a: "The urogenital system (kidneys, gonads, and associated ducts)." },
            { q: "What are the two layers of the Lateral Plate Mesoderm?", a: "Somatic (parietal) layer (forms body wall) and Splanchnic (visceral) layer (forms organ walls)." },
            { q: "What forms the 'Body Cavity' (Intraembryonic Coelom)?", a: "The space between the somatic and splanchnic layers of the lateral plate mesoderm." },
            { q: "What is Hydatidiform Mole?", a: "Abnormal development of the trophoblast where the embryo is absent or non-viable; presents with 'snowstorm' ultrasound and extremely high hCG." },
            { q: "Difference between a Complete and Partial Mole?", a: "Complete: 46,XX (all paternal DNA, 'empty' egg). Partial: Triploid (69,XXY, 2 sperm + 1 egg) with some fetal parts." },
            { q: "What is VACTERL Association?", a: "A cluster of birth defects: Vertebral, Anal atresia, Cardiac, Tracheo-Esophageal fistula, Renal, and Limb defects." },
            { q: "What is Caudal Regression Syndrome?", a: "Failure of the lower spine to develop, often associated with poorly controlled maternal diabetes." },
            { q: "What is the significance of the Allantois?", a: "It is involved in early blood formation and becomes the Urachus (connecting the bladder to the umbilicus)." },
            { q: "What is a Patent Urachus?", a: "Failure of the urachus to close, causing urine to leak from the umbilicus." },
            { q: "What is the clinical marker for Down Syndrome in the first trimester?", a: "Increased Nuchal Translucency on ultrasound, low serum PAPP-A, and high free beta-hCG." },
            { q: "Which germ layer is responsible for the formation of the Adrenal Cortex?", a: "Mesoderm. (Note: Adrenal Medulla is Neural Crest/Ectoderm)." },
            { q: "Derivatives of Pharyngeal Arch 1 (Mandibular)?", a: "CN V3 (Trigeminal). Muscles of mastication, anterior 2/3 of tongue (sensation), malleus, incus, Meckel's cartilage." },
            { q: "Derivatives of Pharyngeal Arch 2 (Hyoid)?", a: "CN VII (Facial). Muscles of facial expression, stapes, styloid process, lesser horn of hyoid, Reichert's cartilage." },
            { q: "Derivatives of Pharyngeal Arch 3?", a: "CN IX (Glossopharyngeal). Stylopharyngeus muscle, greater horn of hyoid." },
            { q: "Derivatives of Pharyngeal Arch 4 & 6?", a: "CN X (Vagus). Arch 4: Superior laryngeal branch (cricothyroid, pharyngeal constrictors). Arch 6: Recurrent laryngeal branch (intrinsic muscles of larynx except cricothyroid)." },
            { q: "Derivatives of Pharyngeal Pouch 1?", a: "Epithelial lining of auditory (Eustachian) tube and middle ear cavity." },
            { q: "Derivatives of Pharyngeal Pouch 2?", a: "Epithelial lining of palatine tonsil crypts." },
            { q: "Derivatives of Pharyngeal Pouch 3?", a: "Inferior parathyroid glands and Thymus. (Migrate caudally)." },
            { q: "Derivatives of Pharyngeal Pouch 4?", a: "Superior parathyroid glands and Ultimobranchial body (parafollicular C cells of thyroid)." },
            { q: "What is DiGeorge Syndrome (22q11 deletion) in context of pouches?", a: "Failure of 3rd and 4th pouches to develop -> T-cell deficiency (thymic aplasia) and hypocalcemia (parathyroid aplasia)." },
            { q: "Derivative of Pharyngeal Cleft 1?", a: "External auditory meatus. (Clefts 2-4 disappear, forming cervical sinus; if persistent -> branchial cleft cyst)." },
            { q: "Aortic Arch 1 derivative?", a: "Part of maxillary artery." },
            { q: "Aortic Arch 3 derivative?", a: "Common carotid artery and proximal internal carotid artery." },
            { q: "Aortic Arch 4 derivative?", a: "On left: Arch of aorta. On right: Proximal right subclavian artery." },
            { q: "Aortic Arch 6 derivative?", a: "On left: Left pulmonary artery and Ductus arteriosus. On right: Right pulmonary artery." },
            { q: "Bulbus cordis gives rise to?", a: "Smooth parts (outflow tracts) of left and right ventricles (Conus arteriosus and Aortic vestibule)." },
            { q: "Truncus arteriosus gives rise to?", a: "Ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk." },
            { q: "Primitive atrium and ventricle give rise to?", a: "Trabeculated parts of the left and right atria and ventricles." },
            { q: "Sinus venosus (left and right horns) gives rise to?", a: "Left horn: Coronary sinus. Right horn: Smooth part of right atrium (Sinus venarum)." },
            { q: "What is the 'Foramen Secundum'?", a: "An opening in the septum primum that allows right-to-left shunting during fetal life." },
            { q: "Four stages of Lung Development?", a: "1. Pseudoglandular (Weeks 5-16), 2. Canalicular (Weeks 16-26), 3. Saccular (Weeks 26-Birth), 4. Alveolar (Birth-8 years)." },
            { q: "When does Surfactant production begin?", a: "Begins at Week 20-24 (canalicular stage); reaches adequate levels for survival by Week 32-34 (Type II pneumocytes)." },
            { q: "Midgut rotation — degrees and direction?", a: "270° counter-clockwise around the superior mesenteric artery (SMA)." },
            { q: "Physiological Herniation occurs when?", a: "Week 6: midgut herniates into umbilical cord (lack of space). Returns to abdomen by Week 10." },
            { q: "Failure of midgut to return to abdomen results in?", a: "Omphalocele (covered by peritoneum/amnion) vs. Gastroschisis (not covered, usually to the right of umbilicus)." },
            { q: "Derivatives of the Foregut?", a: "Esophagus, stomach, duodenum (1st/2nd parts), liver, gallbladder, pancreas. (Supply: Celiac trunk)." },
            { q: "Derivatives of the Midgut?", a: "Duodenum (3rd/4th parts), jejunum, ileum, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, proximal 2/3 of transverse colon. (Supply: SMA)." },
            { q: "Derivatives of the Hindgut?", a: "Distal 1/3 of transverse colon, descending colon, sigmoid colon, rectum, upper anal canal. (Supply: IMA)." },
            { q: "Metanephros gives rise to?", a: "Definitive kidney. Composed of Ureteric bud (collecting system) and Metanephric blastema (excretory system/nephrons)." },
            { q: "Ureteric bud derivatives?", a: "Ureter, renal pelvis, major/minor calyces, collecting ducts." },
            { q: "Metanephric blastema derivatives?", a: "Bowman's capsule, proximal tubule, Loop of Henle, distal tubule." },
            { q: "What causes 'Horseshoe Kidney'?", a: "Inferior poles of kidneys fuse; ascent is blocked by the Inferior Mesenteric Artery (IMA)." },
            { q: "Thyroid gland origin and descent?", a: "Originates at Foramen Cecum (tongue); descends via Thyroglossal Duct to its position in the neck." }
          ]
        },
        {
          topic: 'Reproductive System',
          icon: 'users',
          cards: [
            { q: "Indifferent gonad is formed by which week?", a: "Week 5 (from intermediate mesoderm + primordial germ cells migrating from yolk sac wall)." },
            { q: "Origin of primordial germ cells?", a: "Yolk sac wall (hindgut endoderm); migrate via dorsal mesentery to gonadal ridge by week 6." },
            { q: "SRY gene is located on which chromosome?", a: "Y chromosome (short arm); encodes Testis-Determining Factor (TDF)." },
            { q: "SRY gene product causes differentiation of what structure?", a: "Medullary sex cords -> testes (Sertoli + Leydig cells)." },
            { q: "Without SRY, the gonad defaults to?", a: "Ovary (cortical cords develop; medullary cords regress)." },
            { q: "Sertoli cells produce what hormone and what is its role?", a: "Anti-Müllerian Hormone (AMH) -> causes regression of Müllerian (paramesonephric) ducts in males." },
            { q: "Leydig cells produce what and when?", a: "Testosterone; begins week 8 (stimulated by hCG, later LH); causes Wolffian duct development." },
            { q: "Müllerian (Paramesonephric) ducts give rise to in females?", a: "Fallopian tubes, uterus, cervix, upper 1/3 of vagina." },
            { q: "Wolffian (Mesonephric) ducts give rise to in males (mnemonic)?", a: "SEED: Seminal vesicles, Epididymis, Ejaculatory duct, Ductus (vas) deferens." },
            { q: "What causes masculinization of external genitalia?", a: "DHT (Dihydrotestosterone) — converted from testosterone by 5alpha-reductase in peripheral tissues." },
            { q: "Genital tubercle develops into what in males vs. females?", a: "Males: Glans penis | Females: Clitoris." },
            { q: "Urogenital folds develop into?", a: "Males: ventral shaft of penis (corpus spongiosum covers urethra) | Females: labia minora." },
            { q: "Labioscrotal swellings develop into?", a: "Males: Scrotum | Females: Labia majora." },
            { q: "Urogenital sinus gives rise to?", a: "Urinary bladder, urethra, lower vagina (in females), prostate (in males)." },
            { q: "The lower 2/3 of vagina is derived from?", a: "Urogenital sinus (sinovaginal bulbs / vaginal plate)." },
            { q: "Hymen is the remnant of what?", a: "Junction between vaginal plate (urogenital sinus) and Müllerian duct derivatives." },
            { q: "5alpha-reductase deficiency presents as?", a: "46,XY with female external genitalia at birth -> virilization at puberty; testes present internally; no uterus/fallopian tubes." },
            { q: "Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome (AIS) — karyotype, gonads, phenotype?", a: "46,XY; testes (intra-abdominal); female external phenotype; no uterine/Müllerian structures; ↑ estrogen, ↑ testosterone, ↑ LH." },
            { q: "Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia (CAH) most common cause?", a: "21-hydroxylase deficiency -> ↑ androgens -> virilization of 46,XX female (ambiguous genitalia); salt-wasting (↓ aldosterone, ↓ cortisol)." },
            { q: "Müllerian Agenesis (Mayer-Rokitansky-Küster-Hauser syndrome) — key features?", a: "46,XX; absent uterus + upper vagina; normal ovaries; normal female phenotype; primary amenorrhea." },
            { q: "Gubernaculum in males guides descent of what?", a: "Testes from posterior abdominal wall into scrotum (weeks 26–32)." },
            { q: "Cryptorchidism — most common location of undescended testis?", a: "Inguinal canal; associated with ↑ risk of testicular carcinoma and infertility." },
            { q: "Processus vaginalis failure to close leads to?", a: "Congenital indirect inguinal hernia (or hydrocele)." },
            { q: "Seminiferous tubule is lined by what cells?", a: "Sertoli cells (sustentacular) + spermatogenic cells (spermatogonia -> primary spermatocyte -> secondary spermatocyte -> spermatid -> spermatozoa)." },
            { q: "Blood-testis barrier is formed by?", a: "Tight junctions between adjacent Sertoli cells; separates basal and adluminal compartments." },
            { q: "Function of Sertoli cells (list 5)?", a: "(1) Nourish developing sperm, (2) Blood-testis barrier, (3) Phagocytose residual bodies, (4) Secrete inhibin (inhibits FSH), (5) Secrete AMH (in fetal life), (6) Androgen-Binding Protein (ABP)." },
            { q: "Leydig (interstitial) cells are located where and secrete?", a: "Interstitium between seminiferous tubules; secrete testosterone (stimulated by LH)." },
            { q: "Spermatogenesis takes how long?", a: "~74 days (from spermatogonia to spermatozoa)." },
            { q: "Acrosome of sperm contains what and its function?", a: "Hydrolytic enzymes (hyaluronidase, acrosin, zona lysin); penetrate zona pellucida during fertilization." },
            { q: "Epididymis histology — cell type and function?", a: "Pseudostratified columnar epithelium with stereocilia; site of sperm maturation and storage; reabsorbs fluid." },
            { q: "Vas deferens histology?", a: "Thick smooth muscle (3 layers: inner longitudinal, middle circular, outer longitudinal); pseudostratified columnar epithelium with stereocilia." },
            { q: "Ovarian cortex contains?", a: "Follicles (primordial, primary, secondary, tertiary/Graafian) in stroma." },
            { q: "Ovarian medulla contains?", a: "Blood vessels, lymphatics, loose connective tissue, and hilar cells (analogous to Leydig cells)." },
            { q: "Primordial follicle consists of?", a: "Primary oocyte (arrested in prophase I) + single layer of flattened follicular cells." },
            { q: "Zona pellucida is formed when?", a: "During primary follicle stage; glycoprotein coat (ZP1, ZP2, ZP3) secreted by oocyte." },
            { q: "Granulosa cells secrete?", a: "Estrogen (from androgens via aromatase), inhibin, activin; form antrum fluid (follicular fluid)." },
            { q: "Theca interna cells secrete?", a: "Androgens (androstenedione, testosterone) -> converted to estrogen by granulosa cell aromatase." },
            { q: "Corpus luteum is formed from and secretes?", a: "Remnants of Graafian follicle after ovulation (luteinization of granulosa & theca cells); secretes progesterone + estrogen." },
            { q: "Corpus luteum of menstruation vs. pregnancy?", a: "Menstruation: degenerates -> corpus albicans (day 24) | Pregnancy: maintained by hCG for 8–10 weeks." },
            { q: "Uterine zones (endometrium layers)?", a: "(1) Functionalis (shed at menstruation — stratum compactum + spongiosum) | (2) Basalis (permanent; regenerates functionalis)." },
            { q: "Fallopian tube cell types?", a: "Ciliated columnar cells (transport ovum) + Peg (secretory) cells (nourishment)." },
            { q: "Site of fertilization?", a: "Ampulla of fallopian tube (lateral 1/3)." },
            { q: "Cervical mucus is thin/spinnbarkeit at what phase?", a: "Preovulatory/periovulatory (estrogen effect) — facilitates sperm penetration." },
            { q: "HPG axis — hypothalamus -> pituitary -> gonad sequence?", a: "GnRH (pulsatile) -> FSH + LH (anterior pituitary) -> gonads -> sex steroids + inhibin." },
            { q: "GnRH must be secreted in what pattern for normal reproduction?", a: "Pulsatile (continuous -> receptor downregulation -> ↓ FSH/LH — basis of GnRH agonist therapy)." },
            { q: "FSH acts on which cells in males and females?", a: "Males: Sertoli cells (spermatogenesis, ABP, inhibin) | Females: Granulosa cells (aromatase, estrogen, follicle growth)." },
            { q: "LH acts on which cells in males and females?", a: "Males: Leydig cells (testosterone) | Females: Theca cells (androgens) + triggers ovulation + forms corpus luteum." },
            { q: "Inhibin — source and function?", a: "Sertoli cells (males) / Granulosa cells (females); selectively inhibits FSH (not LH)." },
            { q: "Two-cell, two-gonadotropin theory of estrogen synthesis?", a: "LH -> theca cells -> androgens -> diffuse to granulosa cells -> FSH-stimulated aromatase -> estrogen." },
            { q: "Testosterone is converted to DHT by which enzyme and where?", a: "5alpha-reductase; in prostate, external genitalia, skin, and liver." },
            { q: "Testosterone is converted to estradiol by which enzyme and where?", a: "Aromatase; in adipose tissue, liver, adrenals, gonads, brain." },
            { q: "Menstrual cycle — average length and ovulation timing?", a: "28 days; ovulation on day 14 (LH surge 36h before); luteal phase always 14 days; follicular phase varies." },
            { q: "Hormonal changes at ovulation trigger?", a: "LH surge (positive feedback of rising estradiol on LH); causes ovulation ~36–40h after surge peak." },
            { q: "Progesterone rises in which phase?", a: "Luteal phase (days 14–28); secreted by corpus luteum; causes secretory endometrium, ↑ BBT 0.5°C, thick cervical mucus." },
            { q: "What causes menstruation?", a: "Corpus luteum degeneration -> ↓ progesterone + estrogen -> vasoconstriction of spiral arteries -> ischemia -> sloughing of functionalis." },
            { q: "Proliferative phase of endometrium — driven by?", a: "Estrogen; glands straight/narrow, stroma dense; days 5–14." },
            { q: "Secretory phase of endometrium — driven by?", a: "Progesterone (+ estrogen); glands tortuous/coiled, glycogen vacuoles; days 15–28; prepares for implantation." },
            { q: "hCG — produced by whom, acts on what, peaks when?", a: "Syncytiotrophoblast of developing embryo; acts on corpus luteum (mimics LH) -> maintains progesterone; peaks at 8–10 weeks." },
            { q: "Positive feedback of estrogen on LH occurs at what level of estrogen?", a: "Sustained estradiol >200 pg/mL for >50 hours triggers LH surge (preovulatory)." },
            { q: "Inhibin B vs. Inhibin A — timing?", a: "Inhibin B: follicular phase (from granulosa cells) | Inhibin A: luteal phase (from corpus luteum)." },
            { q: "Spermatogenesis requires what temperature?", a: "2–3°C below body temperature (~35°C); maintained by pampiniform plexus countercurrent heat exchange." },
            { q: "Capacitation of sperm occurs where and means what?", a: "Female reproductive tract; removal of glycoprotein coat + cholesterol from sperm membrane -> enables acrosome reaction and fertilization." },
            { q: "Acrosome reaction is triggered by?", a: "Binding of sperm to ZP3 of zona pellucida -> Ca2+ influx -> exocytosis of acrosomal enzymes." },
            { q: "Cortical reaction (zona reaction) — purpose?", a: "Fusion of cortical granules with oolemma after sperm entry -> hardens zona pellucida -> prevents polyspermy." },
            { q: "Secondary oocyte completes meiosis II when?", a: "After fertilization by sperm (completion triggered by sperm entry); arrested at metaphase II before fertilization." },
            { q: "Implantation occurs at what stage and where?", a: "Blastocyst stage; day 6–10; posterior wall of uterine body (most common); requires progesterone-primed secretory endometrium." },
            { q: "Placental hormones (list 4)?", a: "hCG, hPL (human placental lactogen), progesterone, estrogen (estriol = marker of fetal well-being)." },
            { q: "hPL (human placental lactogen) — function?", a: "Anti-insulin effect -> ↑ maternal blood glucose for fetus; promotes lipolysis; also called hCS (chorionic somatomammotropin)." },
            { q: "Relaxin — source and function?", a: "Corpus luteum + placenta; relaxes pubic symphysis, cervical ripening, inhibits uterine contractions." },
            { q: "Blood supply to testis?", a: "Testicular artery (from abdominal aorta at L2); venous drainage -> pampiniform plexus -> testicular vein (R: IVC; L: left renal vein)." },
            { q: "Lymphatic drainage of testis?", a: "Para-aortic (lumbar) lymph nodes (NOT inguinal)." },
            { q: "Lymphatic drainage of scrotum?", a: "Superficial inguinal lymph nodes." },
            { q: "Layers of the spermatic cord (outside-in)?", a: "External spermatic fascia (external oblique) -> Cremasteric muscle/fascia (internal oblique) -> Internal spermatic fascia (transversalis fascia) -> contents (vas deferens, testicular artery, pampiniform plexus, lymphatics, genital branch of genitofemoral nerve)." },
            { q: "Cremaster reflex — afferent and efferent?", a: "Afferent: femoral branch of genitofemoral nerve (L1-L2) | Efferent: genital branch of genitofemoral nerve (L1-L2); cremasteric muscle." },
            { q: "Prostate gland zones and associated pathology?", a: "Peripheral zone (70%) -> adenocarcinoma; Transitional zone (5%) -> BPH; Central zone (25%) -> least affected." },
            { q: "Prostate blood supply?", a: "Inferior vesical artery (from internal iliac)." },
            { q: "Seminal vesicles contribute what to semen?", a: "~60–70% of semen volume; fructose (energy for sperm), prostaglandins, fibrinogen, vitamin C." },
            { q: "Prostate gland contributes what to semen?", a: "~20–30% of volume; PSA (liquefies semen), acid phosphatase, zinc, citrate; slightly acidic." },
            { q: "Bulbourethral (Cowper's) glands — secretion and location?", a: "Pre-ejaculatory fluid (lubricant, neutralizes urethral acidity); located in deep perineal pouch." },
            { q: "Order of sperm pathway (from testis to urethra)?", a: "Seminiferous tubules -> Rete testis -> Efferent ductules -> Epididymis (head->body->tail) -> Vas deferens -> Ejaculatory duct -> Urethra (SEVEN UP: Seminiferous, Epididymis, Vas deferens, Ejaculatory duct, Nothing, Urethra, Penis)." },
            { q: "Ejaculatory duct is formed by union of?", a: "Vas deferens + duct of seminal vesicle; opens into prostatic urethra at verumontanum." },
            { q: "Vas deferens crosses what structure in inguinal canal?", a: "Crosses ureter at pelvic brim ('water under the bridge') — important in vasectomy and pelvic surgery." },
            { q: "Pudendal nerve innervates?", a: "Perineum, external genitalia, external anal sphincter, external urethral sphincter; S2-S4 ('S2-3-4 keep the genitals off the floor')." },
            { q: "Erection — nerve and neurotransmitter?", a: "Parasympathetic (pelvic splanchnic nerves S2-S4) -> NO -> ↑ cGMP -> smooth muscle relaxation -> vasodilation -> erection ('point — Parasympathetic')." },
            { q: "Ejaculation — nerve?", a: "Sympathetic (hypogastric nerve, L1-L2) -> emission (smooth muscle contraction of epididymis, vas deferens, seminal vesicles, prostate) + somatic pudendal nerve -> expulsion ('shoot — Sympathetic')." },
            { q: "Broad ligament contains?", a: "Fallopian tube (in mesosalpinx), ovarian ligament, round ligament, uterine vessels, parametrium connective tissue." },
            { q: "Round ligament — origin, path, insertion?", a: "From uterine cornu -> inguinal canal -> labia majora; maintains anteversion of uterus; no major neurovascular structures." },
            { q: "Cardinal (Mackenrodt's) ligament — function?", a: "Primary support of uterus against prolapse; from cervix/upper vagina to lateral pelvic wall; contains uterine vessels." },
            { q: "Uterosacral ligament — function?", a: "Supports uterus; from cervix -> sacrum; contains parasympathetic fibers (S2-S4)." },
            { q: "Ovarian ligament vs. suspensory ligament of ovary?", a: "Ovarian ligament: ovary -> uterus (within broad ligament) | Suspensory (infundibulopelvic) ligament: ovary -> pelvic wall; contains ovarian vessels, lymphatics, nerves." },
            { q: "Blood supply to ovary?", a: "Ovarian artery (from abdominal aorta, L2); venous drainage: R -> IVC; L -> left renal vein (same as testis)." },
            { q: "Blood supply to uterus?", a: "Uterine artery (branch of internal iliac); anastomoses with ovarian artery." },
            { q: "Uterine artery crosses ureter where?", a: "At the base of the broad ligament, 2 cm lateral to cervix ('water under the bridge' — ureter is under) — at risk in hysterectomy." },
            { q: "Lymphatic drainage of uterine body (fundus)?", a: "Para-aortic lymph nodes (like ovary/testis); also superficial inguinal via round ligament." },
            { q: "Lymphatic drainage of cervix?", a: "Internal and external iliac lymph nodes -> obturator lymph nodes." },
            { q: "Lymphatic drainage of vagina?", a: "Upper 1/3: internal iliac | Middle 1/3: internal iliac | Lower 1/3: superficial inguinal." },
            { q: "Perineal body (central tendon of perineum) — importance?", a: "Junction of 8+ muscles; damaged in perineal tears during childbirth; supports pelvic floor." },
            { q: "Ischiorectal (ischioanal) fossa — contents?", a: "Pudendal canal (Alcock's canal) containing pudendal nerve, internal pudendal artery/vein; filled with fat." },
            { q: "Urogenital triangle boundaries?", a: "Ischiopubic rami (anterior) + imaginary line between ischial tuberosities (posterior)." },
            { q: "Anal triangle boundaries?", a: "Ischial tuberosities (lateral) + coccyx (posterior) + same transverse line anteriorly." },
            { q: "Bartholin's glands (greater vestibular glands) — location and function?", a: "Posterolateral to vaginal orifice; homologue to Cowper's glands; produce lubrication; site of cyst/abscess formation." },
            { q: "Skene's glands (lesser vestibular / paraurethral glands)?", a: "Adjacent to urethral orifice; homologue to prostate; can be site of gonorrheal infection." },
            { q: "Uterine positions — most common normal position?", a: "Anteverted (fundus tilts anteriorly at cervix) + Anteflexed (fundus tilts anteriorly at body/cervix junction)." },
            { q: "Klinefelter syndrome — karyotype, findings, hormone levels?", a: "47,XXY; small testes, ↑ FSH/LH, ↓ testosterone, gynecomastia, infertility, tall stature; Barr body present; XXY = eXtra chromosome, needs eXtra support." },
            { q: "Turner syndrome — karyotype, findings, hormone levels?", a: "45,XO; streak ovaries, primary amenorrhea, ↑ FSH/LH (hypergonadotropic hypogonadism), webbed neck, shield chest, coarctation of aorta, lymphedema." },
            { q: "True hermaphrodite — karyotype and gonads?", a: "46,XX (most common) or mosaic; both ovarian and testicular tissue present (ovotestis)." },
            { q: "Hydatidiform mole — complete vs. partial?", a: "Complete: 46,XX (all paternal = androgenetic); no fetus; ↑↑↑ hCG; snowstorm on US; -> choriocarcinoma risk | Partial: 69,XXY (triploid); fetal parts may be present; ↑ hCG." },
            { q: "Ectopic pregnancy — most common site?", a: "Ampulla of fallopian tube (most common); presents with amenorrhea, unilateral pelvic pain, vaginal bleeding; ↑ hCG but slower-than-normal doubling." },
            { q: "PCOS — biochemical hallmarks?", a: "↑ LH:FSH ratio (>2:1), ↑ androgens, ↓ SHBG, insulin resistance, anovulation, oligomenorrhea, hirsutism, bilateral polycystic ovaries on US." },
            { q: "Precocious puberty — GnRH-dependent vs. independent?", a: "Dependent (central): ↑ GnRH pulsatility (tumor, idiopathic) | Independent (peripheral): autonomous sex steroid secretion (McCune-Albright, adrenal tumor, testotoxicosis) — low LH/FSH." },
            { q: "Which cancer marker is elevated in testicular germ cell tumors?", a: "AFP (yolk sac tumor) + beta-hCG (choriocarcinoma/mixed) + LDH (general marker); seminoma = beta-hCG only (mildly), AFP normal." },
            { q: "Prostate cancer — tumor marker and bone metastasis pattern?", a: "PSA; osteoblastic (sclerotic) bone metastases (↑ alkaline phosphatase); most commonly to lumbar spine." },
            { q: "Cervical cancer — most common HPV strains and Pap smear finding?", a: "HPV 16 (most common) and HPV 18; Pap: koilocytes (HPV cytopathic effect — perinuclear halo, raisinoid nuclei)." },
            { q: "Menopause — hormone changes?", a: "↓ Estrogen + progesterone, ↑ FSH (earliest marker), ↑ LH; FSH >25 mIU/mL = diagnostic; average age 51." },
            { q: "Main estrogen in premenopausal vs. postmenopausal women?", a: "Premenopausal: Estradiol (E2, from ovary) | Postmenopausal: Estrone (E1, from peripheral aromatization of androstenedione in fat)." },
            { q: "Asherman syndrome — cause and result?", a: "Intrauterine adhesions (synechiae) after D&C or uterine surgery -> secondary amenorrhea, infertility; normal FSH/LH (eugonadotropic)." },
            { q: "Sheehan syndrome — cause and presentation?", a: "Postpartum pituitary necrosis (ischemia due to massive hemorrhage) -> panhypopituitarism; first sign = failure of lactation (↓ prolactin)." },
            { q: "Kallmann syndrome — features?", a: "GnRH deficiency (olfactory bulb agenesis) -> anosmia + hypogonadotropic hypogonadism; low FSH/LH; KAL1 gene; males more affected." },
            { q: "Bicornuate uterus — embryologic cause?", a: "Incomplete fusion of Müllerian ducts; associated with recurrent pregnancy loss and preterm labor." },
            { q: "Varicocele — more common on which side and why?", a: "Left side; left testicular vein drains into left renal vein at 90° -> increased resistance -> venous dilation; 'bag of worms' on palpation; associated with infertility." },
            { q: "Which hormone maintains the corpus luteum in early pregnancy?", a: "hCG (human Chorionic Gonadotropin) — produced by syncytiotrophoblast; peaks at 8–10 weeks." },
            { q: "Which hormone is responsible for milk PRODUCTION?", a: "Prolactin (inhibited by dopamine from hypothalamus; estrogen stimulates prolactin but inhibits its action)." },
            { q: "Which hormone is responsible for milk EJECTION (let-down)?", a: "Oxytocin (from posterior pituitary); also stimulates uterine contractions (Ferguson reflex)." },
            { q: "Progesterone effects on the body (5 key effects)?", a: "(1) ↑ BBT, (2) Thick cervical mucus, (3) Secretory endometrium, (4) ↓ uterine motility, (5) ↑ respiratory rate (direct stimulant)." },
            { q: "Estrogen effects on reproductive tract (5 key effects)?", a: "(1) Proliferative endometrium, (2) Thin/spinnbarkeit cervical mucus, (3) ↑ LH surge (positive feedback), (4) ↑ SHBG, (5) ↑ coagulation factors." },
            { q: "Which gonadotropin is measured to confirm ovulation has occurred?", a: "Serum progesterone (day 21 of a 28-day cycle); >3 ng/mL suggests ovulation." },
            { q: "FSH on day 3 of cycle — clinical significance?", a: "Marker of ovarian reserve; ↑ FSH (>10 mIU/mL) = diminished ovarian reserve / approaching menopause." }
          ]
        },
        {
          topic: 'Renal System',
          icon: 'filter',
          cards: [
            { q: "What are the three successive kidney systems in embryological development?", a: "Pronephros (week 4, rudimentary, regresses) -> Mesonephros (week 4–8, functional briefly) -> Metanephros (week 5 onward, permanent kidney)." },
            { q: "Metanephros develops from which two components?", a: "(1) Ureteric bud (from mesonephric/Wolffian duct) -> ureter, renal pelvis, calyces, collecting ducts | (2) Metanephric mesoderm (blastema) -> nephrons (glomerulus to DCT)." },
            { q: "What induces the metanephric mesoderm to differentiate?", a: "Ureteric bud (via GDNF-RET signaling); metanephric mesoderm in turn induces ureteric bud branching." },
            { q: "Ureteric bud gives rise to which structures?", a: "Ureter, renal pelvis, major and minor calyces, collecting ducts." },
            { q: "Metanephric mesoderm (blastema) gives rise to which structures?", a: "Glomerulus (parietal + visceral epithelium), Bowman's capsule, PCT, Loop of Henle, DCT (up to — but not including — collecting duct)." },
            { q: "Kidneys ascend from the pelvis to the lumbar region due to?", a: "Differential growth of the body (not active migration); blood supply shifts from iliac -> aortic branches." },
            { q: "Horseshoe kidney — embryologic basis and clinical significance?", a: "Inferior poles fuse across midline during ascent -> trapped by inferior mesenteric artery (IMA); usually asymptomatic; ↑ risk of Wilms tumor, UPJ obstruction, renal calculi." },
            { q: "Renal agenesis (Potter sequence) — cause and features?", a: "Failure of ureteric bud to develop or interact with metanephric mesoderm; bilateral -> oligohydramnios -> Potter sequence: pulmonary hypoplasia, limb deformities, flat facies, death; unilateral -> compensatory hypertrophy." },
            { q: "Duplex collecting system — embryologic cause?", a: "Early bifurcation or two separate ureteric buds -> two ureters or bifid ureter; upper moiety obstructs, lower moiety refluxes ('lower goes lateral, upper goes medial' — Weigert-Meyer rule)." },
            { q: "Polycystic kidney disease — types and embryologic basis?", a: "ADPKD (PKD1/PKD2): failure of union between collecting tubules and nephrons | ARPKD: abnormal development of collecting ducts; cysts expand and compress normal parenchyma." },
            { q: "Urogenital sinus forms which urinary structures?", a: "Urinary bladder (from vesical part) and urethra; trigone of bladder = from absorbed mesonephric ducts." },
            { q: "Allantois remnant in adults?", a: "Median umbilical ligament (urachus); patent urachus -> urine from umbilicus; urachal cyst if middle remains; urachal sinus if opens at umbilicus only." },
            { q: "Renal cortex contains which structures?", a: "Renal corpuscles (glomerulus + Bowman's capsule), PCT, DCT, cortical collecting duct, macula densa." },
            { q: "Renal medulla contains which structures?", a: "Loop of Henle (thin descending + thin ascending + thick ascending), medullary collecting duct, vasa recta." },
            { q: "Filtration barrier of the glomerulus consists of (3 layers)?", a: "(1) Fenestrated capillary endothelium (charged barrier) -> (2) Glomerular basement membrane (GBM, type IV collagen + heparan sulfate — charge + size barrier) -> (3) Podocyte foot processes (slit diaphragm — nephrin, podocin)." },
            { q: "Mesangial cells — location and function?", a: "Between glomerular capillaries; phagocytic, structural support, regulate GFR by contraction (contain actin), produce cytokines; expanded in IgA nephropathy and diabetic nephropathy." },
            { q: "Podocytes — what type of cells and markers?", a: "Visceral epithelial cells of Bowman's capsule; highly differentiated; cannot regenerate; key proteins: nephrin (slit diaphragm), podocin, synaptopodin, WT1; effacement = nephrotic syndrome." },
            { q: "PCT histology — key features?", a: "Simple cuboidal epithelium with abundant microvilli (brush border = ↑ surface area) + many mitochondria (basolateral); most active reabsorption segment; acidophilic cytoplasm." },
            { q: "Thin descending loop of Henle histology?", a: "Simple squamous epithelium; highly permeable to water (AQP1), not solutes; no active transport." },
            { q: "Thick ascending loop of Henle (TAL) histology?", a: "Simple cuboidal/low columnar; no water permeability (no aquaporins); active NaCl reabsorption (NKCC2); generates medullary gradient; site of action of loop diuretics." },
            { q: "DCT histology vs. PCT?", a: "DCT: simple cuboidal, few/no microvilli (no brush border), less acidophilic than PCT, fewer mitochondria; site of aldosterone and PTH action." },
            { q: "Macula densa — location and function?", a: "Modified DCT cells adjacent to afferent arteriole; sense NaCl in tubular fluid -> regulate renin release and GFR (tubuloglomerular feedback)." },
            { q: "Juxtaglomerular (JG) cells — location and function?", a: "Modified smooth muscle cells of afferent arteriole; contain renin granules; release renin in response to ↓ BP, ↓ NaCl (via macula densa), beta1-adrenergic stimulation." },
            { q: "Juxtaglomerular apparatus (JGA) consists of?", a: "Macula densa + JG cells (granular cells) + extraglomerular mesangial cells (lacis cells)." },
            { q: "Collecting duct cell types?", a: "Principal cells (aldosterone-sensitive; Na+ reabsorption via ENaC, K+ secretion, ADH-sensitive AQP2) + Intercalated cells (Type A: H+ secretion via H+-ATPase; Type B: HCO3- secretion)." },
            { q: "Vasa recta — function?", a: "Straight capillaries parallel to loop of Henle; preserve medullary osmotic gradient by countercurrent exchange; supply medulla with blood." },
            { q: "Renal capsule is composed of?", a: "Dense fibrous connective tissue (collagen); invests the kidney; perirenal fat outside capsule." },
            { q: "GFR formula (Starling forces across glomerulus)?", a: "GFR = Kf x [(PGC − PBS) − (piGC − piBS)] where Kf = filtration coefficient; normal GFR approx 125 mL/min (180 L/day)." },
            { q: "Filtered load formula?", a: "Filtered load = GFR x Plasma concentration; e.g., glucose: 125 mL/min x 1 mg/mL = 125 mg/min." },
            { q: "Clearance formula?", a: "C = (U x V) / P; where U = urine concentration, V = urine flow rate, P = plasma concentration." },
            { q: "Inulin clearance measures?", a: "GFR (freely filtered, not reabsorbed or secreted = gold standard); creatinine clearance slightly overestimates GFR due to tubular secretion." },
            { q: "PAH (para-aminohippuric acid) clearance measures?", a: "Renal plasma flow (RPF); nearly 100% extracted in one pass; normal ~625 mL/min; RBF = RPF/(1−Hct) approx 1100 mL/min." },
            { q: "Filtration fraction (FF)?", a: "FF = GFR/RPF = 125/625 = ~0.20 (20%); ↑ FF -> ↑ oncotic pressure in peritubular capillaries -> ↑ reabsorption." },
            { q: "Afferent arteriole dilation vs. constriction effect on GFR?", a: "Dilation -> ↑ GFR; Constriction -> ↓ GFR (↓ hydrostatic pressure in glomerulus)." },
            { q: "Efferent arteriole dilation vs. constriction effect on GFR?", a: "Constriction -> ↑ GFR (↑ glomerular hydrostatic pressure); Dilation -> ↓ GFR; ACE inhibitors dilate efferent -> ↓ GFR." },
            { q: "Tubuloglomerular feedback (TGF) — mechanism?", a: "↑ NaCl delivery to macula densa -> release adenosine -> afferent arteriole constriction -> ↓ GFR; autoregulation mechanism." },
            { q: "Myogenic mechanism of renal autoregulation?", a: "↑ BP -> stretch of afferent arteriole smooth muscle -> vasoconstriction -> ↓ GFR; maintains GFR between MAP 80–180 mmHg." },
            { q: "Substances freely filtered at glomerulus (size/charge)?", a: "Molecular weight <7,000 Da freely filtered; negative charge = repelled by GBM (heparan sulfate); MW 7,000–70,000 = partial filtration; albumin (69,000 Da, negative) = minimal filtration." },
            { q: "Glucose renal threshold and Tm?", a: "Threshold ~180 mg/dL plasma glucose -> glucosuria; Tm (transport maximum) ~375 mg/min; splay = difference between threshold and Tm onset due to nephron heterogeneity." },
            { q: "PCT reabsorbs what percentage of filtered load?", a: "~67% of Na+, Cl-, K+, H2O; ~100% of glucose and amino acids; ~80–90% of HCO3-; ~50% of urea." },
            { q: "Primary driver of PCT reabsorption?", a: "Na+/K+-ATPase on basolateral membrane creates low intracellular Na+ -> Na+ enters from lumen via cotransporters (SGLT2 for glucose, NHE3 for H+)." },
            { q: "SGLT2 vs. SGLT1 location and capacity?", a: "SGLT2: early PCT, high capacity, low affinity (reabsorbs ~90% of filtered glucose) | SGLT1: late PCT, low capacity, high affinity; SGLT2 inhibitors (gliflozins) cause glucosuria." },
            { q: "Bulk of HCO3- reabsorption occurs where and how?", a: "PCT (~80–90%); NHE3 secretes H+ -> combines with filtered HCO3- -> H2CO3 -> CO2 + H2O (carbonic anhydrase IV, luminal) -> CO2 enters cell -> reformed as HCO3- (carbonic anhydrase II, cytosolic) -> exits basolaterally." },
            { q: "Loop of Henle — thin descending limb transport?", a: "Permeable to water (AQP1), not solutes; water exits into hypertonic medulla -> tubular fluid becomes hypertonic." },
            { q: "Loop of Henle — thick ascending limb (TAL) transport?", a: "NKCC2 (Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter, apical); K+ recycles back via ROMK; Cl- exits basolaterally; impermeable to water; furosemide blocks NKCC2." },
            { q: "Countercurrent multiplication — purpose?", a: "TAL actively reabsorbs NaCl (not water) -> medullary interstitium becomes hyperosmotic (up to 1200 mOsm/kg at papilla) -> enables water reabsorption in collecting duct under ADH." },
            { q: "DCT — main transporters and hormonal regulation?", a: "NCC (Na+-Cl- cotransporter, thiazide-sensitive); PTH -> ↑ Ca2+ reabsorption (via TRPV5); aldosterone acts on late DCT and CD principal cells." },
            { q: "Aldosterone mechanism of action on principal cells?", a: "Binds mineralocorticoid receptor -> ↑ ENaC (apical Na+ channel) synthesis + ↑ ROMK (K+ secretion) + ↑ Na+/K+-ATPase -> Na+ reabsorption, K+ secretion, H2O retention." },
            { q: "ADH (vasopressin) — source, receptor, mechanism?", a: "Posterior pituitary; V2 receptor on principal cells -> Gs -> ↑ cAMP -> PKA -> inserts AQP2 into apical membrane -> ↑ water reabsorption; also ↑ AQP3/4 on basolateral." },
            { q: "ADH release is stimulated by?", a: "↑ Plasma osmolality (osmoreceptors, hypothalamus) — most potent | ↓ Blood volume/pressure (baroreceptors) | Nausea, pain, stress, nicotine, opioids, hypoglycemia." },
            { q: "ADH release is inhibited by?", a: "↓ Plasma osmolality | ↑ Blood volume | Alcohol (ethanol), ANP, cold." },
            { q: "Diabetes insipidus — central vs. nephrogenic?", a: "Central: ↓ ADH production (trauma, tumor, surgery); responds to desmopressin | Nephrogenic: ↓ kidney response to ADH (Li+ toxicity, demeclocycline, V2 receptor mutation); does not respond to desmopressin; both have dilute polyuria + ↑ serum osmolality." },
            { q: "Urea recycling in the medulla — role?", a: "Collecting duct (inner medullary) reabsorbs urea via UT-A1/3 (ADH-stimulated) -> adds to medullary osmolality -> countercurrent multiplier; ~50% of filtered urea reabsorbed in PCT, rest varies with hydration." },
            { q: "Potassium handling in kidney?", a: "PCT: reabsorbs ~65% | TAL: reabsorbs ~25% (NKCC2) | DCT/CD: principal cells secrete K+ (via ROMK, BK channels); regulated by aldosterone and flow rate." },
            { q: "ANP (Atrial Natriuretic Peptide) — stimulus, mechanism, effects?", a: "Released by atrial stretch (↑ volume/pressure); ↑ cGMP -> ↓ renin, ↓ aldosterone, dilates afferent arteriole + constricts efferent -> ↑ GFR; ↑ Na+ excretion (natriuresis)." },
            { q: "RAAS — step-by-step sequence?", a: "↓ BP/volume or ↓ NaCl -> JG cells release renin -> cleaves angiotensinogen -> angiotensin I -> ACE (lung) -> angiotensin II -> (1) aldosterone release, (2) efferent constriction, (3) ADH release, (4) thirst, (5) Na+/H+ exchange in PCT." },
            { q: "Angiotensin II effects on kidney (4 major)?", a: "(1) Efferent arteriole constriction -> ↑ GFR (↑ FF) + ↑ peritubular oncotic pressure -> ↑ Na+ reabsorption | (2) ↑ aldosterone (adrenal cortex) | (3) ↑ ADH | (4) ↑ NHE3 in PCT -> ↑ HCO3- and Na+ reabsorption." },
            { q: "Henderson-Hasselbalch equation for blood pH?", a: "pH = 6.1 + log([HCO3-] / 0.03 x PCO2); normal pH 7.35–7.45; HCO3- = 24 mEq/L; PCO2 = 40 mmHg." },
            { q: "Four primary acid-base disorders?", a: "Metabolic acidosis (↓ HCO3-) | Metabolic alkalosis (↑ HCO3-) | Respiratory acidosis (↑ PCO2) | Respiratory alkalosis (↓ PCO2)." },
            { q: "Winter's formula (metabolic acidosis respiratory compensation)?", a: "Expected PCO2 = 1.5 x [HCO3-] + 8 ± 2; if actual PCO2 ≠ expected -> mixed disorder." },
            { q: "Anion gap formula and normal value?", a: "AG = Na+ − (Cl- + HCO3-); normal = 8–12 mEq/L; elevated AG = unmeasured anions (lactate, ketones, uremia, toxins)." },
            { q: "MUDPILES mnemonic (high AG metabolic acidosis causes)?", a: "Methanol, Uremia, Diabetic ketoacidosis, Propylene glycol, Isoniazid/Iron, Lactic acidosis, Ethylene glycol, Salicylates." },
            { q: "Normal AG metabolic acidosis (hyperchloremic) causes — HARDASS mnemonic?", a: "Hyperalimentation, Addison's disease, Renal tubular acidosis, Diarrhea, Acetazolamide, Spironolactone, Saline infusion." },
            { q: "Renal Tubular Acidosis Type 1 (distal) — defect and key feature?", a: "Defect in H+ secretion by alpha-intercalated cells (collecting duct) -> cannot acidify urine below pH 5.5 -> hypokalemia, nephrocalcinosis/renal stones (calcium phosphate)." },
            { q: "Renal Tubular Acidosis Type 2 (proximal) — defect and key feature?", a: "Defect in HCO3- reabsorption in PCT -> urine alkaline initially then acidifies once HCO3- depleted; hypokalemia; associated with Fanconi syndrome; urine pH <5.5 eventually." },
            { q: "Renal Tubular Acidosis Type 4 — defect and key feature?", a: "Hypoaldosteronism or aldosterone resistance -> ↓ H+ and K+ secretion -> hyperkalemia + metabolic acidosis; most common in diabetic nephropathy." },
            { q: "Calcium reabsorption along the nephron?", a: "PCT ~65% (passive, paracellular) | TAL ~25% (passive, paracellular) | DCT ~8% (active, transcellular, regulated by PTH/calcitriol via TRPV5) | CD ~2%." },
            { q: "PTH effects on kidney?", a: "↑ Ca2+ reabsorption (DCT), ↓ phosphate reabsorption (PCT via ↓ NaPi-IIa), ↑ 1alpha-hydroxylase (-> calcitriol), ↑ H+ secretion (-> mild alkalosis)." },
            { q: "Phosphate handling — main site and regulation?", a: "PCT reabsorbs ~85% via NaPi-IIa (Na+-phosphate cotransporter); PTH inhibits -> phosphaturia; FGF-23 also inhibits (in CKD -> phosphate retention)." },
            { q: "Free water clearance (CH2O) formula and interpretation?", a: "CH2O = V − Cosm; positive (>0) = dilute urine produced (excess free water excreted) | negative (<0) = concentrated urine (free water retained); ADH -> negative CH2O." },
            { q: "Maximum urine osmolality and minimum?", a: "Max: ~1200 mOsm/kg (with maximum ADH + intact medullary gradient) | Min: ~50 mOsm/kg (with no ADH, maximally dilute urine)." },
            { q: "Obligatory urine volume — why does it exist?", a: "Minimum ~500 mL/day; must excrete ~600 mOsm/day of solute; even maximally concentrated urine at 1200 mOsm/kg requires 500 mL." },
            { q: "SIADH — cause, lab findings, treatment?", a: "↑ ADH (ectopic — small cell lung ca, CNS disorders, drugs); hyponatremia + ↑ urine osmolality (>100 mOsm/kg) + ↑ urine Na+ (>20 mEq/L) + euvolemia; treat with fluid restriction ± vaptans (V2 antagonists)." },
            { q: "Kidneys are located at which vertebral levels?", a: "T12–L3; right kidney slightly lower than left (displaced by liver); hilum at L1 (transpyloric plane)." },
            { q: "Retroperitoneal structures of the kidney — what covers them anteriorly?", a: "Peritoneum covers anterior surface only; surrounded by perirenal (Gerota's) fat + renal fascia (Gerota's fascia) + pararenal fat." },
            { q: "Renal fascia (Gerota's fascia) — clinical importance?", a: "Encloses kidney + adrenal; open inferiorly (explains why perinephric abscess tracks inferiorly, not superiorly); used in radical nephrectomy." },
            { q: "Relations of right kidney (anterior)?", a: "Liver (superior), hepatorenal recess, duodenum (descending part — medial), right colic flexure (inferior), small intestine." },
            { q: "Relations of left kidney (anterior)?", a: "Stomach, spleen, tail of pancreas, left colic flexure, small intestine, splenic vessels." },
            { q: "Renal blood supply — sequence from aorta?", a: "Aorta -> Renal artery -> Segmental arteries (5, end arteries) -> Lobar arteries -> Interlobar arteries -> Arcuate arteries -> Interlobular (cortical radiate) arteries -> Afferent arterioles -> Glomerulus." },
            { q: "Why are renal segmental arteries clinically important?", a: "End arteries (no anastomoses) -> occlusion -> renal infarction of that segment; basis for partial nephrectomy." },
            { q: "Venous drainage of kidneys — asymmetry?", a: "Right renal vein -> IVC (short, direct) | Left renal vein -> crosses aorta anteriorly -> IVC (longer); left renal vein also receives left gonadal + left suprarenal vein." },
            { q: "Left renal vein compression by aorta — clinical syndrome?", a: "Nutcracker syndrome; left renal vein compressed between aorta and SMA -> hematuria, left flank pain, varicocele (males), pelvic congestion (females)." },
            { q: "Lymphatic drainage of kidneys?", a: "Para-aortic (lumbar) lymph nodes at level of renal hilum (L1-L2)." },
            { q: "Nerve supply of kidneys?", a: "Renal plexus (T10-L1 sympathetic fibers via celiac plexus + aorticorenal ganglion); kidney pain referred to T10-L1 dermatomes (flank, groin)." },
            { q: "Renal pelvis narrows to ureter at which structure?", a: "Ureteropelvic junction (UPJ) — most common site of obstruction in children; hydronephrosis." },
            { q: "Three natural constrictions of the ureter (sites of stone lodging)?", a: "(1) UPJ (ureteropelvic junction), (2) Pelvic brim (where ureter crosses iliac vessels), (3) UVJ (ureterovesical junction — narrowest, most common site of stone impaction)." },
            { q: "Ureter relations — where does it cross the gonadal vessels?", a: "Ureter passes posterior to gonadal vessels (gonadal vessels cross anterior to ureter) — 'water under the bridge'." },
            { q: "Ureter relation to uterine artery in females?", a: "Uterine artery crosses anterior and superior to ureter 2 cm lateral to cervix ('water under the bridge' — ureter is under); most common site of iatrogenic ureteral injury in hysterectomy." },
            { q: "Urinary bladder — trigone boundaries?", a: "Triangular area: two ureteral orifices (superolateral) + internal urethral orifice (inferior); mucosa smooth here (no rugae); derived from absorbed mesonephric ducts." },
            { q: "Detrusor muscle innervation (micturition)?", a: "Parasympathetic (S2-S4, pelvic splanchnic nerves) -> contraction; Sympathetic (T11-L2, hypogastric nerve) -> relaxation (storage); Somatic (pudendal nerve, S2-S4) -> external urethral sphincter (voluntary)." },
            { q: "Micturition reflex — sequence?", a: "Bladder fills -> stretch receptors -> afferent via pelvic nerve -> sacral micturition center (S2-S4) -> efferent parasympathetic -> detrusor contracts + internal sphincter relaxes -> voluntary relaxation of external sphincter (pudendal nerve) -> voiding." },
            { q: "Adrenal gland blood supply (3 arteries)?", a: "Superior suprarenal artery (from inferior phrenic) + Middle suprarenal artery (from aorta) + Inferior suprarenal artery (from renal artery); veins: right -> IVC; left -> left renal vein." },
            { q: "Nephrotic syndrome — definition and key features?", a: "Proteinuria >3.5 g/day, hypoalbuminemia, edema (periorbital, pitting), hyperlipidemia, lipiduria, hypercoagulability (loss of antithrombin III); 'nephrOtic = prOtein loss'." },
            { q: "Nephritic syndrome — definition and key features?", a: "Hematuria (RBC casts), oliguria, hypertension, azotemia, mild proteinuria; 'nephrItic = Inflammation'." },
            { q: "Minimal Change Disease (MCD) — presentation, EM finding, treatment?", a: "Most common nephrotic syndrome in children; EM: podocyte foot process effacement; LM: normal; IF: negative; responds to corticosteroids; associated with Hodgkin lymphoma." },
            { q: "Focal Segmental Glomerulosclerosis (FSGS) — associations?", a: "Most common nephrotic syndrome in adults (especially African Americans); associated with HIV (collapsing variant), heroin use, obesity, sickle cell; EM: foot process effacement + sclerosis; poor steroid response." },
            { q: "Membranous Nephropathy — associations and IF?", a: "Most common nephrotic in white adults; 'spike and dome' on EM (subepithelial deposits); granular IgG/C3 IF; associated with HBV, SLE (class V), drugs (NSAIDs, penicillamine), solid tumors (anti-PLA2R Ab = primary)." },
            { q: "Diabetic Nephropathy — histologic hallmark?", a: "Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules (nodular glomerulosclerosis); diffuse GBM thickening; mesangial expansion; earliest sign = microalbuminuria (30–300 mg/day)." },
            { q: "IgA Nephropathy (Berger's disease) — presentation and IF?", a: "Most common glomerulonephritis worldwide; gross hematuria following URI or GI infection (synpharyngitic); mesangial IgA deposits on IF; ↑ serum IgA; slowly progressive." },
            { q: "Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (PSGN) — presentation, IF, EM?", a: "Nephritic syndrome 2–4 weeks after Group A Strep pharyngitis/skin infection; 'starry sky' granular IgG/C3 IF (subepithelial humps on EM = 'lumpy bumpy'); ↓ complement (C3); self-limiting in children." },
            { q: "Rapidly Progressive (Crescentic) GN — types and IF?", a: "Type I: Anti-GBM (Goodpasture's) — linear IgG IF | Type II: Immune complex (SLE, PSGN) — granular IF | Type III: Pauci-immune (ANCA+: GPA/MPA) — negative IF; all have fibrin crescents compressing glomerulus." },
            { q: "Goodpasture syndrome — antibody target and presentation?", a: "Anti-GBM antibody (anti-type IV collagen, alpha3 chain); pulmonary hemorrhage + rapidly progressive GN; hemoptysis + hematuria; linear IgG on IF." },
            { q: "Lupus nephritis — most severe class and IF pattern?", a: "Class IV (diffuse proliferative GN) = most severe; 'wire loop' lesions; ↓ C3/C4; 'full house' IF (IgA, IgG, IgM, C3, C1q)." },
            { q: "Alport syndrome — gene, mutation, presentation?", a: "COL4A5 mutation (X-linked); defective type IV collagen in GBM; progressive hematuria, proteinuria, CKD; sensorineural deafness + ocular abnormalities (anterior lenticonus); EM: basket-weave (thinning + thickening) of GBM." },
            { q: "Thin Basement Membrane Disease — presentation?", a: "Asymptomatic persistent microscopic hematuria; EM: uniformly thin GBM (<200 nm); benign prognosis; COL4A3/4 heterozygous mutations." },
            { q: "Acute Tubular Necrosis (ATN) — most common cause of acute kidney injury in hospital?", a: "Ischemic (shock, hemorrhage) or nephrotoxic (aminoglycosides, cisplatin, myoglobin, contrast); muddy brown granular casts in urine; PCT most vulnerable (high O2 demand); tubular cells can regenerate." },
            { q: "Prerenal vs. intrinsic (ATN) azotemia — key lab differences?", a: "Prerenal: BUN:Cr >20, UNa <20, FeNa <1%, Uosm >500, concentrated urine | ATN: BUN:Cr <15, UNa >40, FeNa >2%, Uosm ~300, isosthenuric urine, muddy brown casts." },
            { q: "FeNa formula?", a: "FeNa = (UNa x PCr) / (PNa x UCr) x 100%; <1% = prerenal; >2% = intrinsic renal; unreliable if on diuretics (use FEurea instead)." },
            { q: "Renal papillary necrosis — associated conditions (mnemonic)?", a: "SADD: Sickle cell, Analgesic nephropathy (NSAIDs/phenacetin), Diabetes mellitus, Pyelonephritis (severe) / Obstruction; presents with flank pain + hematuria + sloughed papillae in urine." },
            { q: "Nephrolithiasis — most common stone type and risk factors?", a: "Calcium oxalate (75–80%); ↑ urine Ca2+, ↑ oxalate (Crohn's, spinach), ↓ urine citrate; envelope-shaped crystals; treat with thiazide diuretics (↓ urine Ca2+)." },
            { q: "Struvite (magnesium ammonium phosphate) stones — cause and appearance?", a: "Urease-producing bacteria (Proteus, Klebsiella, Pseudomonas) -> ↑ urine pH, ↑ ammonia; 'coffin lid' crystals; staghorn calculi; always infection-associated." },
            { q: "Uric acid stones — associations?", a: "Hyperuricemia (gout), myeloproliferative disorders, acidic urine (Lesch-Nyhan, Crohn's); radiolucent on X-ray (not seen on KUB); rhomboid/barrel-shaped crystals; treat with allopurinol + alkalization." },
            { q: "Cystine stones — cause?", a: "Cystinuria (autosomal recessive — defective transporter for COLA: Cystine, Ornithine, Lysine, Arginine in PCT/intestine); hexagonal crystals; treat with alkalization + D-penicillamine." },
            { q: "Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD) stages by GFR?", a: "G1: ≥90 (normal/high) | G2: 60–89 | G3a: 45–59 | G3b: 30–44 | G4: 15–29 | G5: <15 or dialysis." },
            { q: "CKD — metabolic consequences (5 key)?", a: "(1) ↑ BUN/Cr (uremia), (2) Metabolic acidosis (↓ H+ excretion), (3) Hyperkalemia, (4) Hyperphosphatemia + Hypocalcemia + ↑ PTH = renal osteodystrophy, (5) ↓ erythropoietin -> normocytic anemia." },
            { q: "Renal osteodystrophy — mechanism in CKD?", a: "↓ GFR -> phosphate retention -> ↑ FGF-23 -> ↓ 1alpha-hydroxylase -> ↓ calcitriol -> ↓ Ca2+ absorption -> ↑ PTH (secondary hyperparathyroidism) -> bone resorption; also ↓ calcitriol -> ↓ PTH receptor downregulation." },
            { q: "Wilms tumor (nephroblastoma) — associations and peak age?", a: "Most common renal tumor in children (peak 3–4 years); WT1 mutation; associated with WAGR syndrome (Wilms, Aniridia, Genitourinary anomalies, intellectual Retardation/Range) and Beckwith-Wiedemann; presents as abdominal mass + hematuria." },
            { q: "Renal cell carcinoma (RCC) — most common type and associations?", a: "Clear cell (75%) — VHL mutation (chromosome 3p); von Hippel-Lindau disease; smoking (strongest risk factor), obesity; presents with hematuria + flank pain + palpable mass (classic triad); paraneoplastic syndromes (EPO -> polycythemia, PTHrP, renin)." },
            { q: "RCC spread — classic metastatic route?", a: "Hematogenous (invades renal vein -> IVC); metastasizes to lung ('cannonball' lesions), bone, liver, adrenal; left varicocele = left renal vein occlusion by tumor." },
            { q: "Transitional cell carcinoma (urothelial) — most common location and risk factors?", a: "Renal pelvis/ureter/bladder (bladder = most common site); risk: smoking (most common), aniline dyes, cyclophosphamide, phenacetin, Schistosoma haematobium (squamous cell); painless hematuria." },
            { q: "Oncocytoma — features?", a: "Benign renal tumor; large eosinophilic cells packed with mitochondria; 'spoke-wheel' pattern on angiography; central scar; must distinguish from chromophobe RCC (both large eosinophilic cells)." },
            { q: "Angiomyolipoma — associations?", a: "Benign hamartoma (fat + muscle + vessels); associated with tuberous sclerosis (TSC1/TSC2); appears bright on CT (fat density); risk of retroperitoneal hemorrhage." },
            { q: "Acute Kidney Injury (AKI) — KDIGO definition?", a: "↑ Cr by ≥0.3 mg/dL within 48h OR ↑ Cr ≥1.5x baseline within 7 days OR urine output <0.5 mL/kg/hr for ≥6 hours." },
            { q: "Causes of postrenal AKI?", a: "Obstruction: BPH (most common in elderly men), bilateral ureteral stones, bladder cancer, urethral stricture, cervical cancer; bilateral obstruction needed for AKI (or unilateral in solitary kidney); renal ultrasound shows hydronephrosis." }
          ]
        },
        {
          topic: 'Respiratory System',
          icon: 'wind',
          cards: [
            { q: "Origin of the respiratory diverticulum (lung bud)?", a: "Foregut endoderm (ventral outgrowth)." },
            { q: "Germ layer origin of the lung parenchyma (epithelium)?", a: "Endoderm." },
            { q: "Germ layer origin of pulmonary connective tissue, cartilage, and smooth muscle?", a: "Splanchnic mesoderm." },
            { q: "Developmental defect in Tracheoesophageal Fistula (TEF)?", a: "Failure of the tracheoesophageal septum to partition the foregut. Most common: Esophageal atresia with distal TEF." },
            { q: "Five stages of lung development (in order)?", a: "Embryonic -> Pseudoglandular -> Canalicular -> Saccular -> Alveolar. (Mnemonic: 'Every Pulmonologist Can See Alveoli')." },
            { q: "When does surfactant production begin?", a: "Week 26 (Canalicular/Saccular transition). Reaches adequate levels by Week 34." },
            { q: "Primary cause of Neonatal Respiratory Distress Syndrome (NRDS)?", a: "Surfactant deficiency (mainly dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine/lecithin). Risk factors: prematurity, maternal diabetes, C-section." },
            { q: "Number of lobes in the Right vs. Left lung?", a: "Right: 3 lobes; Left: 2 lobes (+ lingula)." },
            { q: "Structure most likely to harbor an aspirated foreign body?", a: "Right main bronchus (wider, shorter, and more vertical than the left)." },
            { q: "Relationship of pulmonary artery to bronchus at the hilum?", a: "RALS: Right Anterior, Left Superior." },
            { q: "Cells responsible for secreting surfactant?", a: "Type II Pneumocytes. (Also act as stem cells for Type I cells)." },
            { q: "Primary role of Clara (Club) cells?", a: "Degrade toxins (P450 enzymes), secrete surfactant-like components, and act as reserve cells in bronchioles." },
            { q: "Formula for Minute Ventilation (Ve)?", a: "Ve = Tidal Volume (Vt) x Respiratory Rate (RR)." },
            { q: "Formula for Alveolar Ventilation (Va)?", a: "Va = (Tidal Volume - Dead Space) x Respiratory Rate." },
            { q: "Physiological effect of High Altitude on the Hemoglobin-Oxygen dissociation curve?", a: "Right shift (increased 2,3-BPG to facilitate O2 unloading)." },
            { q: "Effect of exercise on Pulmonary Vascular Resistance (PVR)?", a: "Decreased PVR (due to recruitment and distension of pulmonary vessels)." },
            { q: "Regional V/Q ratio difference in the lung?", a: "Apex: High V/Q (~3.0). Base: Low V/Q (~0.6). Note: Both ventilation and perfusion increase at the base, but perfusion increases more." },
            { q: "Classic PFT finding in Obstructive Lung Disease (COPD/Asthma)?", a: "Decreased FEV1/FVC ratio (< 70%). Increased Total Lung Capacity (TLC) due to air trapping." },
            { q: "Classic PFT finding in Restrictive Lung Disease (Fibrosis)?", a: "Normal or increased FEV1/FVC ratio; decreased TLC and Vital Capacity." },
            { q: "Pathognomonic histology finding in Asbestosis?", a: "Ferruginous bodies (asbestos fibers coated with iron). Found in sputum or biopsy." },
            { q: "Distinction between Transudate and Exudate (Light’s Criteria)?", a: "Exudate: Pleural protein/Serum protein > 0.5 OR Pleural LDH/Serum LDH > 0.6. Indicates inflammation." },
            { q: "Most common type of lung cancer in non-smokers?", a: "Adenocarcinoma (often peripheral location)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Albuterol?", a: "Beta2-agonist; increases cAMP to cause smooth muscle relaxation (bronchodilation)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Ipratropium?", a: "Muscarinic antagonist (M3 blocker); prevents bronchoconstriction mediated by the vagus nerve." },
            { q: "First-line treatment for an acute asthma exacerbation?", a: "Albuterol (SABA) + Systemic Corticosteroids (e.g., prednisone) + Oxygen." },
            { q: "Major side effect of Ethambutol (TB drug)?", a: "Optic neuritis (blurred vision, red-green color blindness)." },
            { q: "Drug used for smoking cessation that is a partial nicotinic agonist?", a: "Varenicline (Chantix)." },
            { q: "Component of the airway with the highest total cross-sectional area?", a: "Terminal bronchioles." },
            { q: "Component of the airway with the highest individual airway resistance?", a: "Medium-sized bronchi (Generation 4-7). Resistance is lowest in terminal bronchioles due to parallel arrangement." },
            { q: "Definition of Functional Residual Capacity (FRC)?", a: "Volume of air remaining in lungs after a normal tidal expiration (RV + ERV). Point where inward lung recoil equals outward chest wall recoil." },
            { q: "Shift of the Hb-O2 curve in Carbon Monoxide (CO) poisoning?", a: "Left shift (CO binds Hb with 240x affinity, making it harder for O2 to unload in tissues)." },
            { q: "Primary form of CO2 transport from tissues to lungs?", a: "Bicarbonate (HCO3-) in the plasma (70%)." },
            { q: "Enzyme that catalyzes CO2 + H2O <-> H2CO3?", a: "Carbonic Anhydrase (found in RBCs)." },
            { q: "The 'Chloride Shift' occurs to balance the exit of which ion from RBCs?", a: "Bicarbonate (HCO3-). Cl- enters the RBC to maintain electrical neutrality." },
            { q: "Response of pulmonary arterioles to alveolar hypoxia?", a: "Hypoxic Pulmonary Vasoconstriction (HPV). Shunts blood away from poorly ventilated areas to well-ventilated areas." },
            { q: "Primary central chemoreceptors respond to changes in?", a: "pH and PCO2 of the brain interstitial fluid. (H+ cannot cross blood-brain barrier, but CO2 can)." },
            { q: "Peripheral chemoreceptors (Carotid/Aortic bodies) primarily respond to?", a: "Decreased PO2 (< 60 mmHg), increased PCO2, and decreased pH." },
            { q: "Most common cause of Community-Acquired Pneumonia (CAP)?", a: "Streptococcus pneumoniae." },
            { q: "Pneumonia associated with 'currant jelly' sputum and alcoholics?", a: "Klebsiella pneumoniae. (Aspiration risk)." },
            { q: "Common cause of pneumonia in CF patients and 'hot tub' exposure?", a: "Pseudomonas aeruginosa." },
            { q: "Cause of 'Walking Pneumonia' with high titers of cold agglutinins?", a: "Mycoplasma pneumoniae." },
            { q: "Primary pneumonia associated with bird or bat droppings (Ohio/Mississippi River Valley)?", a: "Histoplasma capsulatum." },
            { q: "Silver-stain finding in Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia?", a: "Disc-shaped (crushed ping-pong ball) yeast. Treat with TMP-SMX." },
            { q: "Standard 4-drug treatment for active Tuberculosis?", a: "RIPE: Rifampin, Isoniazid, Pyrazinamide, Ethambutol." },
            { q: "Ghon Complex components in Primary TB?", a: "Ghon focus (subpleural parenchymal lesion) + ipsilateral hilar lymph node involvement/calcification." },
            { q: "Most common site for reactivation Tuberculosis?", a: "Apex of the lung (due to high O2 tension, favoring aerobic mycobacteria)." },
            { q: "Definition of Chronic Bronchitis?", a: "Productive cough for ≥ 3 months in ≥ 2 consecutive years. Hyperplasia of mucus-secreting glands." },
            { q: "Reid Index calculation (for Chronic Bronchitis)?", a: "Thickness of mucosal gland layer / Thickness of wall between epithelium and cartilage. Normal < 0.4." },
            { q: "Genetic cause of Panacinar Emphysema?", a: "Alpha1-antitrypsin deficiency. Usually involves lower lobes." },
            { q: "Location of Centriacinar Emphysema in smokers?", a: "Upper lobes." },
            { q: "Classic 'Charcot-Leyden crystals' in sputum indicate?", a: "Asthma (composed of eosinophil membrane protein, galectin-10)." },
            { q: "Curschmann spirals are found in which condition?", a: "Asthma (shed epithelium forming mucus plugs)." },
            { q: "Permanent dilation of bronchi due to chronic infection/inflammation?", a: "Bronchiectasis." },
            { q: "CT finding in Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis?", a: "Honeycombing and traction bronchiectasis (subpleural/basal predominance)." },
            { q: "Classic histology in Sarcoidosis?", a: "Non-caseating granulomas (containing Schumann bodies and Asteroid bodies). Stellate inclusions." },
            { q: "Lab finding strongly associated with Sarcoidosis?", a: "Elevated ACE levels and hypercalcemia (due to 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity in macrophages)." },
            { q: "Most common source of a Pulmonary Embolism?", a: "Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) from the proximal leg veins (e.g., femoral or popliteal veins)." },
            { q: "Classic ECG finding in Pulmonary Embolism?", a: "S1Q3T3 (S wave in Lead I, Q wave in Lead III, Inverted T in Lead III). More common is sinus tachycardia." },
            { q: "Definition of Cor Pulmonale?", a: "Right ventricular hypertrophy/failure due to pulmonary hypertension (not caused by left heart disease)." },
            { q: "Physical exam finding in Tension Pneumothorax?", a: "Tracheal deviation away from the affected side + hyperresonance + decreased breath sounds." },
            { q: "Appearance of Mesothelioma on imaging?", a: "Pleural thickening and calcified pleural plaques. Asbestos exposure is the primary risk." },
            { q: "Lung cancer associated with PTHrP (hypercalcemia)?", a: "Squamous Cell Carcinoma. ('sCa+mous')." },
            { q: "Lung cancer associated with SIADH or ACTH secretion?", a: "Small Cell (Oat Cell) Carcinoma." },
            { q: "Characteristic of Lambert-Eaton Syndrome in Small Cell Lung Cancer?", a: "Muscle weakness (proximal) that improves with use. Antibodies against presynaptic Ca2+ channels." },
            { q: "What does a 'Right Shift' in the Hb-O2 curve mean for O2 affinity?", a: "Decreased affinity (easier unloading in tissues). Caused by: ↑ CO2, ↑ H+, ↑ 2,3-BPG, ↑ Temp." },
            { q: "Main stimulus for breathing in a healthy person?", a: "PaCO2 (sensed as pH change in CNS)." },
            { q: "Calculation of the A-a Gradient?", a: "A-a Gradient = PAO2 - PaO2. Elevated in V/Q mismatch, diffusion defects, or shunts." },
            { q: "Lung volume that cannot be measured by simple spirometry?", a: "Residual Volume (RV), Functional Residual Capacity (FRC), and Total Lung Capacity (TLC)." },
            { q: "Drug of choice for prophylaxis in close contacts of H. influenzae type b meningitis?", a: "Rifampin." },
            { q: "Mechanism of N-acetylcysteine in CF patients?", a: "Cleaves disulfide bonds in mucus to liquefy secretions (mucolytic)." },
            { q: "Major risk factor for Neonatal Respiratory Distress Syndrome besides prematurity?", a: "Maternal diabetes (insulin inhibits surfactant production)." },
            { q: " 'Ivory white' pleural plaques are pathognomonic for?", a: "Asbestosis. Found on the diaphragm and parietal pleura." },
            { q: "Pancoast tumor can cause which neurological triad?", a: "Horner Syndrome (Ptosis, Miosis, Anhidrosis) due to compression of the sympathetic chain." },
            { q: "Typical O2 saturation and PaO2 in Carbon Monoxide poisoning?", a: "Normal PaO2 (O2 dissolved in plasma is fine), but decreased O2 saturation (O2 cannot bind Hb)." },
            { q: "Primary respiratory compensation for Metabolic Acidosis?", a: "Hyperventilation (blowing off CO2) to increase pH." },
            { q: "Winter’s Formula for predicted PCO2?", a: "PCO2 = 1.5 x [HCO3-] + 8 ± 2." },
            { q: "Primary cause of Respiratory Acidosis?", a: "Alveolar hypoventilation (e.g., airway obstruction, COPD, sedative overdose, MG)." },
            { q: "Primary cause of Respiratory Alkalosis?", a: "Hyperventilation (e.g., anxiety, high altitude, early salicylate poisoning, PE)." },
            { q: "Mixed acid-base disorder seen in Salicylate (Aspirin) overdose?", a: "Respiratory alkalosis (early stimulation of medullary center) and High Anion Gap Metabolic Acidosis (late)." },
            { q: "Definition of a 'Flail Chest'?", a: "≥ 3 adjacent ribs fractured in ≥ 2 places; causes paradoxical chest wall movement (moves in with inspiration)." },
            { q: "Treatment for a Tension Pneumothorax?", a: "Immediate needle decompression (2nd intercostal space, midclavicular line) followed by chest tube placement." },
            { q: "Definition of 'Dead Space'?", a: "Volume of air that does not participate in gas exchange. (Vd)." },
            { q: "Difference between Anatomical and Physiological Dead Space?", a: "Anatomical: Volume of conducting airways. Physiological: Anatomical + Alveolar dead space (wasted ventilation due to non-perfused alveoli)." },
            { q: "Effect of Pulmonary Embolism on Dead Space?", a: "Increases physiological dead space (ventilation without perfusion)." },
            { q: "Lung cancer associated with 'keratin pearls' and intercellular bridges?", a: "Squamous Cell Carcinoma." },
            { q: "Genetic mutation commonly associated with Adenocarcinoma in non-smokers?", a: "EGFR mutation or ALK translocation." },
            { q: "Characteristic cell type in Small Cell Lung Cancer on histology?", a: "Kulchitsky cells (small blue cells with scant cytoplasm). Neuroendocrine origin." },
            { q: "Neuroendocrine markers positive in Small Cell Lung Cancer?", a: "Chromogranin A, Neuron-specific enolase (NSE), and Synaptophysin." },
            { q: "Lung cancer that can present like pneumonia (filling alveolar spaces)?", a: "Adenocarcinoma in situ (formerly Bronchioloalveolar carcinoma). Presents with voluminous watery sputum." },
            { q: "The triad of Kartagener Syndrome?", a: "Situs inversus, Chronic sinusitis, and Bronchiectasis. (Primary Ciliary Dyskinesia)." },
            { q: "Pathophysiology of Kartagener Syndrome?", a: "Dynein arm defect leading to immotile cilia. Leads to infertility and recurrent infections." },
            { q: " 'Eggshell calcification' of hilar lymph nodes?", a: "Silicosis." },
            { q: "Occupational exposure associated with Silicosis?", a: "Sandblasting, foundries, and mining." },
            { q: "Primary risk associated with Silicosis?", a: "Increased susceptibility to Tuberculosis (silica impairs alveolar macrophage function)." },
            { q: "Pulmonary finding in Goodpasture Syndrome?", a: "Alveolar hemorrhage (hemoptysis) and Linear IgG deposits on basement membrane (Type II Hypersensitivity)." },
            { q: "Pulmonary finding in Wegener’s (Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis)?", a: "Necrotizing granulomas, vasculitis (upper/lower respiratory + renal), and c-ANCA (PR3-ANCA) positivity." },
            { q: "Therapeutic index concern for Theophylline?", a: "Narrow therapeutic index; metabolized by P450; causes cardiotoxicity (tachycardia) and seizures." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Guaifenesin?", a: "Expectorant; thins respiratory secretions by increasing volume and reducing viscosity." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Dextromethorphan?", a: "Antitussive (cough suppressant); antagonizes NMDA glutamate receptors." },
            { q: "Drug used to close a Patent Ductus Arteriosus (PDA)?", a: "Indomethacin (NSAID, reduces prostaglandins)." },
            { q: "Drug used to keep a PDA open?", a: "Alprostadil (Prostaglandin E1)." },
            { q: " 'Deep sulcus sign' on supine X-ray?", a: "Pneumothorax (air accumulating in the lateral costophrenic angle)." },
            { q: " 'Westermark sign' and 'Hampton’s Hump' on X-ray?", a: "Pulmonary Embolism. (Westermark = regional oligemia; Hampton's = wedge-shaped opacification)." },
            { q: " 'Chocolate-colored blood' indicates?", a: "Methemoglobinemia (Fe3+ cannot bind O2)." },
            { q: "Antidote for Methemoglobinemia?", a: "Methylene blue and Vitamin C." },
            { q: "Formula for Pulmonary Vascular Resistance (PVR)?", a: "PVR = (Ppulm_artery - Pleft_atrium) / Cardiac Output." },
            { q: "Lung sound: 'Dullness to percussion' + 'Decreased breath sounds'?", a: "Pleural effusion or atelectasis." },
            { q: "Lung sound: 'Dullness to percussion' + 'Increased breath sounds/fremitus'?", a: "Lobar pneumonia (consolidation provides better sound conduction)." },
            { q: "Classic histology finding in Hypersensitivity Pneumonitis?", a: "Poorly formed non-caseating granulomas and interstitial inflammation (reaction to inhaled organic dust like farmer's lung)." }
          ]
        },
        {
          topic: 'GI System',
          icon: 'utensils',
          cards: [
            { q: "Origin of the epithelial lining of the GI tract?", a: "Endoderm." },
            { q: "Origin of the smooth muscle and connective tissue of the GI tract?", a: "Splanchnic mesoderm." },
            { q: "Which GI structures are derived from the Foregut?", a: "Esophagus to proximal duodenum; liver, gallbladder, pancreas. (Supplied by Celiac Trunk)." },
            { q: "Artery supplying the Midgut?", a: "Superior Mesenteric Artery (SMA). (Distal duodenum to proximal 2/3 of transverse colon)." },
            { q: "Artery supplying the Hindgut?", a: "Inferior Mesenteric Artery (IMA). (Distal 1/3 of transverse colon to upper anal canal)." },
            { q: "Definition of physiological herniation?", a: "Midgut herniates through umbilical ring at week 6; returns and rotates 270 degrees counter-clockwise at week 10." },
            { q: "Failure of the pleuroperitoneal membrane to fuse results in?", a: "Congenital Diaphragmatic Hernia. (Usually left-sided, causes pulmonary hypoplasia)." },
            { q: "Result of failure of the ventral and dorsal pancreatic buds to fuse?", a: "Pancreas divisum (usually asymptomatic; common anomaly)." },
            { q: "Embryological cause of Annular Pancreas?", a: "Ventral pancreatic bud abnormally encircles the 2nd part of the duodenum; causes obstruction." },
            { q: "Persistence of the vitelline duct (omphalomesenteric duct) results in?", a: "Meckel’s Diverticulum. (True diverticulum; can contain ectopic gastric/pancreatic tissue)." },
            { q: "Four layers of the GI wall (inner to outer)?", a: "Mucosa (epithelium, lamina propria, muscularis mucosae), Submucosa, Muscularis externa, Adventitia/Serosa." },
            { q: "Location of Meissner’s (Submucosal) plexus?", a: "Submucosa (controls secretions and local blood flow)." },
            { q: "Location of Auerbach’s (Myenteric) plexus?", a: "Muscularis externa (located between inner circular and outer longitudinal layers; controls motility)." },
            { q: "Histology of the esophagus?", a: "Nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium." },
            { q: "Histology of the stomach and intestines?", a: "Simple columnar epithelium." },
            { q: "Unique histological feature of the Duodenum?", a: "Brunner’s Glands (located in submucosa; secrete alkaline mucus)." },
            { q: "Unique histological feature of the Ileum?", a: "Peyer’s Patches (lymphoid aggregates in submucosa/lamina propria)." },
            { q: "Which GI organs are Secondarily Retroperitoneal?", a: "Pancreas (except tail), Distal 2/3 of duodenum, Ascending/Descending colon, Rectum (upper parts)." },
            { q: "The 'Pringle Maneuver' compresses which ligament?", a: "Hepatoduodenal ligament (contains portal triad: hepatic artery, portal vein, common bile duct)." },
            { q: "Three structures of the Portal Triad?", a: "Proper hepatic artery, Portal vein, Common bile duct." },
            { q: "Effect of Gastrin on parietal cells?", a: "Increases H+ secretion (via CCK-B receptor/IP3-DAG and stimulation of ECL cells to release histamine)." },
            { q: "Source of Intrinsic Factor?", a: "Parietal cells (Stomach). Essential for Vitamin B12 absorption." },
            { q: "Function of Secretin?", a: "Released by S cells in duodenum; increases pancreatic HCO3- secretion; decreases gastric acid." },
            { q: "Hormone responsible for gallbladder contraction?", a: "Cholecystokinin (CCK). (Also increases pancreatic enzyme secretion and relaxes Sphincter of Oddi)." },
            { q: "Enzyme that activates trypsinogen to trypsin?", a: "Enterokinase (Enteropeptidase). Located on the duodenal brush border." },
            { q: "Primary site of Iron absorption?", a: "Duodenum (as Fe2+)." },
            { q: "Primary site of Folate absorption?", a: "Jejunum." },
            { q: "Primary site of Vitamin B12 absorption?", a: "Terminal Ileum (requires Intrinsic Factor)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of the 'Chloride Shift' in parietal cells?", a: "HCO3- exported to blood in exchange for Cl- entering cell (results in postprandial 'alkaline tide')." },
            { q: "Effect of Somatostatin on the GI tract?", a: "General inhibitory effect (decreases Gastrin, CCK, Secretin, Insulin, Glucagon, and gallbladder contraction)." },
            { q: "Definition of Achalasia?", a: "Failure of LES to relax due to loss of myenteric (Auerbach) plexus. Causes dysphagia to solids and liquids." },
            { q: " 'Bird's beak' appearance on barium swallow?", a: "Achalasia." },
            { q: "Linear mucosal lacerations at the gastroesophageal junction?", a: "Mallory-Weiss Syndrome (often from severe vomiting in alcoholics or bulimics)." },
            { q: "Definition of Barrett’s Esophagus?", a: "Metaplasia: Stratified squamous -> Nonciliated columnar with goblet cells (intestinal metaplasia) due to chronic GERD." },
            { q: "Risk of Barrett’s Esophagus?", a: "Esophageal Adenocarcinoma." },
            { q: "Most common cause of esophageal varices?", a: "Portal hypertension (usually due to liver cirrhosis)." },
            { q: "Type of gastritis caused by H. pylori?", a: "Chronic Antral Gastritis (Type B). Risk of peptic ulcers and MALT lymphoma." },
            { q: "Type of gastritis involving autoantibodies to parietal cells?", a: "Chronic Fundic Gastritis (Type A/Autoimmune). Associated with Pernicious Anemia." },
            { q: "Distinguishing feature of Duodenal Ulcer pain?", a: "Pain decreases with meals (leads to weight gain)." },
            { q: "Distinguishing feature of Gastric Ulcer pain?", a: "Pain increases with meals (leads to weight loss)." },
            { q: "Key HLA association in Celiac Disease?", a: "HLA-DQ2 and HLA-DQ8." },
            { q: "Biopsy finding in Celiac Disease?", a: "Villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, and intraepithelial lymphocytosis." },
            { q: "Dermatological manifestation of Celiac Disease?", a: "Dermatitis herpetiformis (IgA deposits at dermal papillae)." },
            { q: " 'String sign' on X-ray indicates?", a: "Crohn’s Disease (narrowing of terminal ileum due to transmural inflammation)." },
            { q: " 'Lead pipe' appearance of colon on imaging?", a: "Ulcerative Colitis (loss of haustra due to chronic inflammation)." },
            { q: "Crohn’s vs. UC: Which is always continuous from the rectum?", a: "Ulcerative Colitis." },
            { q: "Crohn’s vs. UC: Which has transmural inflammation and granulomas?", a: "Crohn’s Disease." },
            { q: "Common site for Volvulus in the elderly?", a: "Sigmoid colon." },
            { q: "Pathophysiology of Hirschsprung Disease?", a: "Failure of neural crest cell migration (absent ganglion cells in distal colon). Presents with failure to pass meconium." },
            { q: "Most common cause of small bowel obstruction?", a: "Adhesions (usually post-surgical)." },
            { q: "Markers for Hepatocellular injury?", a: "ALT and AST." },
            { q: "Markers for Cholestasis (obstructive)?", a: "Alkaline Phosphatase (ALP) and GGT." },
            { q: "AST:ALT ratio > 2:1 suggests?", a: "Alcoholic liver disease. ('ToAST to Alcohol')." },
            { q: "Classic triad of Hemochromatosis?", a: "Cirrhosis, Diabetes, and Skin hyperpigmentation ('Bronze Diabetes')." },
            { q: "Lab finding in Wilson’s Disease?", a: "Decreased ceruloplasmin; increased urinary copper; Kayser-Fleischer rings." },
            { q: " 'Onion skin' bile duct fibrosis on histology?", a: "Primary Sclerosing Cholangitis (PSC). Associated with Ulcerative Colitis." },
            { q: "Anti-mitochondrial antibodies (AMA) are positive in?", a: "Primary Biliary Cholangitis (PBC). (Autoimmune destruction of intrahepatic bile ducts)." },
            { q: "Most common cause of Acute Pancreatitis?", a: "Gallstones (40%) and Alcohol (30%)." },
            { q: "Lab marker with highest specificity for Acute Pancreatitis?", a: "Lipase. (Lasts longer than amylase)." },
            { q: "Trousseau sign (migratory thrombophlebitis) is associated with?", a: "Pancreatic Adenocarcinoma." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Omeprazole?", a: "Irreversible inhibition of H+/K+ ATPase (Proton Pump Inhibitor) in parietal cells." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Cimetidine/Ranitidine?", a: "H2 receptor antagonist (blocks histamine effect on acid secretion)." },
            { q: "Clinical use of Misoprostol in GI?", a: "Prevention of NSAID-induced gastric ulcers (PGE1 analog)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Metoclopramide?", a: "D2 receptor antagonist (prokinetic; increases gastric emptying)." },
            { q: "Side effect of Aluminum Hydroxide antacids?", a: "Constipation. ('Alu-minimum' amount of feces)." },
            { q: "Side effect of Magnesium Hydroxide antacids?", a: "Diarrhea. ('Mg' = Must Go)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Ondansetron?", a: "5-HT3 antagonist (antiemetic; centrally and peripherally active)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Sulfasalazine?", a: "Combination of sulfapyridine (antibacterial) and 5-ASA (anti-inflammatory; activated by colonic bacteria)." },
            { q: "Use of Lactulose in Hepatic Encephalopathy?", a: "Converted to lactic acid; traps NH3 as NH4+ in the gut (acidification) for excretion." },
            { q: " 'Rule of 2s' applies to which GI condition?", a: "Meckel’s Diverticulum (2 inches long, 2 feet from ileocecal valve, 2% of population, 2 years old, 2 types of ectopic tissue)." },
            { q: "Tumor marker for Pancreatic Cancer?", a: "CA 19-9." },
            { q: "Tumor marker for Hepatocellular Carcinoma?", a: "AFP (Alpha-fetoprotein)." },
            { q: "Most common site of a Gastrinoma?", a: "Pancreas or Duodenum (Zollinger-Ellison Syndrome; associated with MEN1)." },
            { q: "Define Courvoisier’s Sign?", a: "Palpable, nontender gallbladder + jaundice (strongly suggests pancreatic head tumor rather than stones)." },
            { q: "Initial treatment for portal hypertension-related ascites?", a: "Spironolactone (Aldosterone antagonist) + Sodium restriction." },
            { q: "Which polyp type has the highest malignant potential?", a: "Villous adenoma ('Villous is Villainous')." },
            { q: "Genetic mutation in FAP (Familial Adenomatous Polyposis)?", a: "APC gene mutation (Chromosome 5q). 100% progress to cancer." },
            { q: "Diagnosis for 'Apple core' lesion on barium enema?", a: "Colorectal Cancer (usually stenosing cancer in the descending colon)." },
            { q: "Primary enzyme deficiency in Lactose Intolerance?", a: "Lactase (brush border enzyme; splits lactose into glucose + galactose)." },
            { q: "Enzyme that conjugates bilirubin with glucuronic acid?", a: "UDP-glucuronosyltransferase (UGT). Located in hepatocytes." },
            { q: "Primary cause of Physiologic Jaundice of the Newborn?", a: "Immature UGT activity + increased RBC turnover + lack of gut bacteria to convert bilirubin." },
            { q: "Lab findings in Gilbert Syndrome?", a: "Unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia (mildly decreased UGT activity; triggered by stress/fasting/alcohol)." },
            { q: "Complete absence of UGT activity results in?", a: "Crigler-Najjar Syndrome, Type I (fatal if untreated; requires phototherapy/transplant)." },
            { q: "Autosomal recessive condition causing a 'black liver'?", a: "Dubin-Johnson Syndrome (defect in conjugated bilirubin transport into bile canaliculi)." },
            { q: "Distinction between Dubin-Johnson and Rotor Syndrome?", a: "Rotor Syndrome does NOT present with a black liver (milder transport defect)." },
            { q: "Anatomical landmark for an Indirect Inguinal Hernia?", a: "Lateral to the inferior epigastric artery (enters deep inguinal ring; covered by all layers of spermatic fascia)." },
            { q: "Anatomical landmark for a Direct Inguinal Hernia?", a: "Medial to the inferior epigastric artery (through Hesselbach’s triangle; covered only by external spermatic fascia)." },
            { q: "Pathophysiology of an Indirect Inguinal Hernia?", a: "Failure of the processus vaginalis to close (persistent communication)." },
            { q: "Three borders of Hesselbach’s Triangle?", a: "Rectus abdominis (medial), Inferior epigastric artery (lateral), Inguinal ligament (inferior)." },
            { q: "Most common type of hernia in females?", a: "Indirect Inguinal Hernia (though Femoral hernias are more common in females than in males)." },
            { q: "Hernia with the highest risk of incarceration?", a: "Femoral Hernia. (Enters below inguinal ligament, lateral to pubic tubercle)." },
            { q: "Classical presentation of Chronic Mesenteric Ischemia?", a: " 'Postprandial angina' (dull abdominal pain after eating) + weight loss (food fear)." },
            { q: "Most common site for Ischemic Colitis?", a: "Splenic flexure (watershed area between SMA and IMA) and rectosigmoid junction." },
            { q: "Condition associated with 'thumbprinting' on X-ray?", a: "Ischemic Colitis (due to mucosal edema/hemorrhage)." },
            { q: "Triad of portal hypertension?", a: "Caput medusae, splenomegaly, and esophageal varices." },
            { q: "Cause of Budd-Chiari Syndrome?", a: "Thrombosis or obstruction of the hepatic veins (leads to congestive hepatomegaly and ascites)." },
            { q: "Definition of Nutcracker Syndrome?", a: "Compression of the left renal vein between the SMA and the Aorta." },
            { q: "Definition of SMA Syndrome?", a: "Compression of the 3rd part of the duodenum between the SMA and the Aorta." },
            { q: "Most common cause of vitamin B12 deficiency in the US?", a: "Pernicious Anemia (autoimmune destruction of parietal cells/IF)." },
            { q: "Histology of Councilman bodies?", a: "Eosinophilic apoptotic hepatocytes (seen in Viral Hepatitis, Yellow Fever, or drug injury)." },
            { q: "Most common malignant liver tumor?", a: "Metastatic disease (from colon, pancreas, breast, or lung)." },
            { q: "Genetic defect in Hemochromatosis?", a: "HFE gene mutation (C282Y). Leads to unregulated iron absorption." },
            { q: "Lab finding for Wilson’s Disease (Kayser-Fleischer rings)?", a: "Low serum ceruloplasmin." }
          ]
        },
        {
          topic: 'Cardiovascular System',
          icon: 'heart',
          cards: [
            { q: "Embryologic origin of the ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk?", a: "Truncus arteriosus." },
            { q: "Embryologic origin of the smooth part (outflow tract) of left and right ventricles?", a: "Bulbus cordis." },
            { q: "Embryologic origin of the SVC?", a: "Right anterior cardinal vein and right common cardinal vein." },
            { q: "Embryologic origin of the coronary sinus?", a: "Left horn of the sinus venosus." },
            { q: "Which structure is responsible for partitioning the atria?", a: "Septum primum and septum secundum." },
            { q: "What structure failure causes a Patent Foramen Ovale (PFO)?", a: "Failure of septum primum and septum secundum to fuse after birth." },
            { q: "What cells are required for the formation of the aorticopulmonary septum?", a: "Neural crest cells. (Failure leads to Transposition, Tetralogy, or Truncus arteriosus)." },
            { q: "Shunt that bypasses the liver in fetal circulation?", a: "Ductus venosus (connects umbilical vein to IVC)." },
            { q: "Shunt that connects the pulmonary artery to the aorta in fetal circulation?", a: "Ductus arteriosus." },
            { q: "Post-natal remnant of the umbilical vein?", a: "Ligamentum teres hepatis (contained in the falciform ligament)." },
            { q: "Most posterior part of the heart (common cause of dysphagia if enlarged)?", a: "Left atrium. (Enlargement can also cause hoarseness via recurrent laryngeal nerve compression)." },
            { q: "Coronary artery that usually supplies the SA and AV nodes?", a: "Right Coronary Artery (RCA)." },
            { q: "Definition of 'Right Dominant' heart?", a: "Posterior Descending Artery (PDA) arises from the RCA (85% of population). Left dominant: PDA from Circumflex." },
            { q: "Primary artery supplying the interventricular septum (anterior 2/3)?", a: "Left Anterior Descending (LAD)." },
            { q: "Sequence of the cardiac conduction system?", a: "SA node -> Atria -> AV node -> Bundle of His -> Purkinje fibers -> Ventricles." },
            { q: "Formula for Cardiac Output (CO)?", a: "CO = Stroke Volume (SV) x Heart Rate (HR)." },
            { q: "Formula for Mean Arterial Pressure (MAP)?", a: "MAP = CO x TPR OR MAP = 2/3 Diastolic BP + 1/3 Systolic BP." },
            { q: "Three factors that increase Stroke Volume (SV)?", a: "Increased Preload, Increased Contractility, Decreased Afterload." },
            { q: "Effect of Verapamil on the Cardiac Action Potential?", a: "Decreases Phase 0 (upstroke) of the Pacemaker action potential (Calcium channel blocker)." },
            { q: "Definition of the 'Lucitropic' effect?", a: "Ability of the heart muscle to relax (influenced by Phospholamban phosphorylation via PKA)." },
            { q: "Component of the EKG representing atrial depolarization?", a: "P wave." },
            { q: "EKG representation of ventricular repolarization?", a: "T wave." },
            { q: "Physiological cause of the S2 split during inspiration?", a: "Increased venous return to the right heart delays pulmonic valve closure (P2)." },
            { q: "Murmur heard in Mitral Regurgitation?", a: "Holosystolic, high-pitched 'blowing' murmur at the apex; radiates to the axilla." },
            { q: "Murmur heard in Aortic Stenosis?", a: "Crescendo-decrescendo systolic ejection murmur (radiates to carotids). 'Pulsus parvus et tardus'." },
            { q: "Most common cause of sudden cardiac death in young athletes?", a: "Hypertrophic Obstructive Cardiomyopathy (HOCM). Autosomal dominant mutation in sarcomere proteins." },
            { q: "Histological hallmark of Rheumatic Heart Disease?", a: "Aschoff bodies (containing Anitschkow cells/caterpillar cells)." },
            { q: "Pathophysiology of Stable Angina?", a: "Atherosclerosis narrowing > 70% of the lumen; chest pain on exertion, relieved by rest or nitroglycerin." },
            { q: "Most specific lab marker for Myocardial Infarction?", a: "Troponin I (remains elevated for 7–10 days). CK-MB is useful for diagnosing re-infarction." },
            { q: "Complication 3–14 days post-MI involving the ventricular wall?", a: "Free wall rupture leading to cardiac tamponade." },
            { q: "Triad of Cardiac Tamponade (Beck’s Triad)?", a: "Hypotension, Distended neck veins, Muffled heart sounds." },
            { q: "Pathognomonic EKG finding for Cardiac Tamponade?", a: "Electrical alternans (alternating amplitude of QRS due to heart swinging in fluid)." },
            { q: "Most common cause of Subacute Bacterial Endocarditis?", a: "Viridans streptococci (low virulence, affects previously damaged valves)." },
            { q: "Bacteria associated with Endocarditis in IV drug users?", a: "Staphylococcus aureus (high virulence, often affects tricuspid valve)." },
            { q: "Name of the non-bacterial endocarditis associated with SLE?", a: "Libman-Sacks Endocarditis (verrucae on both sides of valve)." },
            { q: "Common cause of Left-sided Heart Failure?", a: "Ischemic heart disease, Hypertension, Aortic/Mitral valve disease." },
            { q: "Finding on physical exam for Left Heart Failure?", a: "Orthopnea, Paroxysmal Nocturnal Dyspnea (PND), Pulmonary edema (crackles)." },
            { q: "Primary mechanism of Right Heart Failure?", a: "Left heart failure (most common) or Cor pulmonale (lung disease)." },
            { q: "Definition of 'Isolated Systolic Hypertension'?", a: "Systolic > 140 with Diastolic < 90 (due to decreased arterial compliance in the elderly)." },
            { q: "Direction of the shunt in 'Cyanotic' (Right-to-Left) heart disease?", a: "Right to Left. The 5 T's: Tetralogy, Transposition, Truncus, Tricuspid atresia, TAPVR." },
            { q: "Four components of Tetralogy of Fallot?", a: "Pulmonary stenosis, Right Ventricular Hypertrophy, Overriding aorta, VSD. (PROV)." },
            { q: " 'Boot-shaped heart' on X-ray?", a: "Tetralogy of Fallot." },
            { q: "Condition associated with 'notching of the ribs' on X-ray?", a: "Coarctation of the aorta (postductal; due to collateral circulation via intercostal arteries)." },
            { q: "Congenital heart defect associated with Congenital Rubella?", a: "Patent Ductus Arteriosus (PDA)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of ACE inhibitors (e.g., Lisinopril)?", a: "Inhibits conversion of Angiotensin I to Angiotensin II; prevents Bradykinin breakdown." },
            { q: "Common side effect of ACE inhibitors?", a: "Dry cough and Angioedema (due to increased Bradykinin levels)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Digoxin?", a: "Inhibits Na+/K+ ATPase; increases intracellular Ca2+ (positive inotropy) and stimulates the vagus nerve (decreased HR)." },
            { q: "Drug used to treat Nitrate overdose (Methemoglobinemia)?", a: "Methylene blue." },
            { q: "Class of Antiarrhythmics that blocks Potassium channels?", a: "Class III (e.g., Amiodarone, Sotalol, Dofetilide)." },
            { q: "Major side effect of Statins?", a: "Myopathy (check CK) and Hepatotoxicity (check LFTs)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Hydralazine?", a: "Increases cGMP to cause smooth muscle relaxation (Predominantly an Arteriolar vasodilator)." },
            { q: "Drug of choice for Paroxysmal Supraventricular Tachycardia (PSVT)?", a: "Adenosine (very short half-life)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Ezetimibe?", a: "Prevents cholesterol absorption at the small intestine brush border (NPC1L1 protein)." },
            { q: "Cardiac side effect of Doxorubicin (chemotherapy)?", a: "Dilated Cardiomyopathy. (Prevent with Dexrazoxane)." },
            { q: " 'Water-hammer pulse' is associated with?", a: "Aortic Regurgitation." },
            { q: " 'Machine-like' continuous murmur?", a: "Patent Ductus Arteriosus (PDA)." },
            { q: " 'Egg-on-a-string' appearance on X-ray?", a: "Transposition of the Great Vessels." },
            { q: "Pericarditis occurring weeks after an MI?", a: "Dressler Syndrome (autoimmune reaction against necrotic myocardium)." },
            { q: " 'Nutmeg liver' is a sign of?", a: "Chronic passive congestion (due to Right Heart Failure)." },
            { q: "Normal mean Pulmonary Capillary Wedge Pressure (PCWP)?", a: "4-12 mmHg." },
            { q: "In which type of shock is PCWP increased?", a: "Cardiogenic Shock." },
            { q: "Effect of Nitroglycerin on Preload vs. Afterload?", a: "Predominantly decreases Preload (venodilation)." },
            { q: "Formula for Ejection Fraction (EF)?", a: "EF = (Stroke Volume / End Diastolic Volume) x 100. Normal > 55%." },
            { q: "Relationship between Resistance and Vessel Radius (Poiseuille’s Law)?", a: "Resistance is inversely proportional to the radius to the fourth power (R proportional to 1/r^4)." },
            { q: "Large-vessel vasculitis associated with jaw claudication and increased ESR?", a: "Giant Cell (Temporal) Arteritis. Risk of blindness." },
            { q: " 'Pulseless disease' in a young Asian female?", a: "Takayasu Arteritis." },
            { q: "Medium-vessel vasculitis associated with Hepatitis B and transmural inflammation?", a: "Polyarteritis Nodosa (PAN). (Nodular aneurysms on angiography)." },
            { q: "Diagnosis for a child with 'Strawberry tongue,' hand-foot edema, and coronary aneurysms?", a: "Kawasaki Disease. Treat with IVIG and Aspirin." },
            { q: "Vasculitis associated with asthma, eosinophilia, and p-ANCA?", a: "Churg-Strauss Syndrome (Eosinophilic Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis)." },
            { q: "Primary risk factor for Aortic Dissection?", a: "Hypertension. (Cystic medial necrosis)." },
            { q: "Genetic condition associated with Cystic Medial Necrosis and Aortic Dissection?", a: "Marfan Syndrome (FBN1 mutation)." },
            { q: "EKG hallmark of First-degree AV Block?", a: "Prolonged PR interval (> 200 ms), but every P wave is followed by a QRS." },
            { q: "Description of Mobitz Type I (Wenckebach) block?", a: "Progressive PR prolongation until a QRS is dropped. 'Longer, longer, longer, drop, then you have a Wenckebach'." },
            { q: "Delta wave on EKG and widened QRS indicates?", a: "Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) Syndrome. (Accessory pathway/Bundle of Kent)." },
            { q: "EKG finding for Hyperkalemia?", a: "Peaked T waves, widened QRS, and lost P waves." },
            { q: " 'Sawtooth' waves on EKG?", a: "Atrial Flutter." },
            { q: " 'Irregularly irregular' rhythm with no discernible P waves?", a: "Atrial Fibrillation. High risk of thromboembolism." },
            { q: "Cause of a wide, fixed split S2?", a: "Atrial Septal Defect (ASD)." },
            { q: "Heart sound associated with ventricular filling into a noncompliant/stiff ventricle?", a: "S4 ('Atrial kick'; heard in hypertrophy or stiff ventricles)." },
            { q: "Heart sound associated with rapid ventricular filling into a dilated/floppy ventricle?", a: "S3 (Ventricular gallop; heard in heart failure)." },
            { q: "Location to best auscultate the Mitral Valve?", a: "5th intercostal space, left midclavicular line (apex)." },
            { q: "Physical sign of 'Kussmaul sign'?", a: "Increase in JVP on inspiration (seen in constrictive pericarditis or restrictive cardiomyopathy)." },
            { q: " 'Bread and butter' pericarditis is seen in?", a: "Fibrinous pericarditis (post-MI or uremia)." },
            { q: "Most common primary cardiac tumor in adults?", a: "Myxoma (usually in the left atrium; 'ball-valve' obstruction)." },
            { q: "Most common primary cardiac tumor in children?", a: "Rhabdomyoma (associated with Tuberous Sclerosis)." },
            { q: "Definition of Buerger Disease (Thromboangiitis obliterans)?", a: "Segmental thrombosis/vasculitis in heavy smokers; leads to gangrene and autoamputation." },
            { q: "The 'opening snap' followed by a diastolic rumble indicates?", a: "Mitral Stenosis." },
            { q: "Paradoxical pulse (Pulsus paradoxus) definition?", a: "Drop in systolic BP > 10 mmHg during inspiration (seen in Tamponade, Asthma, COPD)." },
            { q: "Drug that causes 'Cinchonism' (headache, tinnitus)?", a: "Quinidine (Class IA antiarrhythmic)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Lidocaine as an antiarrhythmic?", a: "Class IB; selectively binds to inactivated Sodium channels in ischemic/depolarized tissue." },
            { q: "Formula for Net Filtration Pressure (Starling Forces)?", a: "Jv = Kf [(Pc - Pi) - sigma(pic - pii)]." },
            { q: "Primary cause of edema in Nephrotic Syndrome?", a: "Decreased plasma oncotic pressure (pic) due to hypoalbuminemia." },
            { q: "Primary cause of edema in Filariasis (Elephantiasis)?", a: "Impaired lymphatic drainage." },
            { q: "Effect of arteriolar constriction on capillary hydrostatic pressure (Pc)?", a: "Decreased Pc (protects capillaries from high pressure)." },
            { q: "Secretory source of Renin?", a: "Juxtaglomerular (JG) cells of the afferent arteriole." },
            { q: "Enzyme responsible for converting Angiotensin I to Angiotensin II?", a: "ACE (found primarily in the pulmonary vascular endothelium)." },
            { q: "Three primary actions of Angiotensin II?", a: "Vasoconstriction, Aldosterone release, and ADH release." },
            { q: "Site of action for Aldosterone?", a: "Principal cells of the collecting duct (inserts Na+/K+ pumps and ENaC)." },
            { q: "Effect of Atrial Natriuretic Peptide (ANP) on the kidney?", a: "Dilates afferent arteriole/constricts efferent (increases GFR) and promotes natriuresis." },
            { q: "Treatment for Digoxin toxicity?", a: "Digibind (anti-digoxin Fab fragments) and normalization of Potassium levels." },
            { q: "Class IV antiarrhythmic mechanism?", a: "Calcium Channel Blockers (Verapamil, Diltiazem); slows conduction through AV node." },
            { q: "Antiarrhythmic associated with 'Grey-blue' skin discoloration and pulmonary fibrosis?", a: "Amiodarone." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Sacubitril?", a: "Neprilysin inhibitor; prevents degradation of ANP and BNP (used in HFrEF with Valsartan)." },
            { q: "Reflex effect of Hydralazine that necessitates co-administration with beta-blockers?", a: "Reflex tachycardia." },
            { q: "Preferred hypertensive drug for a patient with Diabetes and Proteinuria?", a: "ACE inhibitor or ARB (reduces glomerular pressure)." },
            { q: "Definition of Takotsubo Cardiomyopathy?", a: " 'Broken heart syndrome'; apical ballooning of the left ventricle due to catecholamine surge." },
            { q: "Key difference between a True Aneurysm and a Pseudoaneurysm?", a: "True aneurysm involves all three layers of the vessel wall." },
            { q: "Anatomical location of an Aortic Dissection tear (Stanford Type A)?", a: "Ascending aorta (proximal to the brachiocephalic artery)." },
            { q: "Direction of the shunt in Eisenmenger Syndrome?", a: "Right-to-Left (reversal of a chronic Left-to-Right shunt due to pulmonary HTN)." },
            { q: "Most common cause of a Paradoxical Embolus?", a: "Patent Foramen Ovale (PFO) or ASD (allows DVT to cross to the arterial side)." },
            { q: " 'Flea-bitten' kidney appearance is associated with?", a: "Malignant Hypertension (hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis)." },
            { q: "Histology finding in Malignant Hypertension?", a: " 'Onion skinning' of arterioles." },
            { q: "Most common location of a Berry Aneurysm?", a: "Anterior communicating artery (Circle of Willis)." },
            { q: "Genetic association with Berry Aneurysms?", a: "ADPKD (Autosomal Dominant Polycystic Kidney Disease)." },
            { q: "Definition of Pulse Pressure?", a: "Systolic BP - Diastolic BP (proportional to Stroke Volume)." }
          ]
        },
        {
          topic: 'Endocrine System',
          icon: 'activity',
          cards: [
            { q: "Anterior pituitary (adenohypophysis) is derived from?", a: "Rathke's pouch <i>(ectoderm of roof of stomodeum \u2014 oral ectoderm)</i>; upgrowth meets downgrowth of diencephalon" },
            { q: "Posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis) is derived from?", a: "Downgrowth of neuroectoderm from floor of diencephalon <i>(brain)</i>; contains axon terminals of hypothalamic neurons, not secretory cells" },
            { q: "Craniopharyngioma \u2014 embryologic origin and features?", a: "Remnant of Rathke's pouch; <b>most common</b> suprasellar tumor in children; calcification <i>(machine oil fluid)</i>; bitemporal hemianopia; \u2191 intracranial pressure; cholesterol crystals" },
            { q: "Thyroid gland origin and migration path?", a: "Endoderm of floor of pharynx <i>(foramen cecum, base of tongue)</i> <b>\u2192</b> descends anterior to trachea <b>\u2192</b> final position by week 7; connected by thyroglossal duct <i>(normally obliterates)</i>" },
            { q: "Thyroglossal duct cyst \u2014 features and embryologic basis?", a: "Persistence of thyroglossal duct; midline neck cyst that moves with swallowing AND tongue protrusion; <b>most common</b> congenital neck mass; lined by respiratory/squamous epithelium; can contain ectopic thyroid tissue" },
            { q: "Foramen cecum of tongue \u2014 significance?", a: "Remnant of origin of thyroid descent; junction of anterior 2/3 and posterior 1/3 of tongue; no secretory function postnatally" },
            { q: "Ectopic thyroid \u2014 most common location?", a: "Base of tongue <i>(lingual thyroid)</i>; may be only thyroid tissue present \u2014 confirm with scan before removal" },
            { q: "Parathyroid glands \u2014 embryologic origins?", a: "Superior parathyroids: 4th pharyngeal pouch | Inferior parathyroids: 3rd pharyngeal pouch <i>(travel farther <b>\u2192</b> more variable position)</i>" },
            { q: "DiGeorge syndrome \u2014 embryologic defect and features?", a: "Failure of 3rd and 4th pharyngeal pouches to develop <b>\u2192</b> absent thymus <i>(T-cell deficiency)</i> + absent inferior and superior parathyroids <i>(hypocalcemia)</i>; 22q11.2 deletion; conotruncal heart defects; CATCH-22: Cardiac defects, Abnormal facies, Thymic aplasia, Cleft palate, Hypocalcemia, 22q11" },
            { q: "Adrenal cortex vs. medulla \u2014 embryologic origins?", a: "Cortex: mesoderm <i>(intermediate/lateral plate)</i> | Medulla: neural crest cells <i>(same origin as sympathetic ganglia)</i>; medullary cells = modified postganglionic sympathetic neurons" },
            { q: "Pancreatic endocrine cells \u2014 embryologic origin?", a: "Endoderm of pancreatic buds <i>(dorsal > ventral)</i>; islets of Langerhans develop from pancreatic ductal epithelium by week 12; beta cells producing insulin by week 10" },
            { q: "Fetal zone of adrenal cortex?", a: "Large inner zone present in fetus <b>\u2192</b> produces DHEA <b>\u2192</b> converted to estrogen by placenta; involutes after birth; makes fetal adrenal gland large at birth" },
            { q: "Anterior pituitary cell types and hormones (mnemonic)?", a: "GH FLAT: Gonadotrophs <i>(FSH/LH)</i>, corticotropH <i>(ACTH)</i>, Lactotrophs <i>(prolactin)</i>, Acidophils <i>(GH/prolactin = Somatotrophs + Lactotrophs)</i>, Thyrotrophs <i>(TSH)</i>; Basophils = ACTH, FSH, LH, TSH; Acidophils = GH, Prolactin" },
            { q: "Pars nervosa (posterior pituitary) contains which cells?", a: "Pituicytes <i>(glial support cells)</i> + axon terminals of hypothalamic neurons <i>(supraoptic nucleus <b>\u2192</b> ADH; paraventricular nucleus <b>\u2192</b> oxytocin)</i>; Herring bodies = storage granules in axon terminals" },
            { q: "Thyroid follicle \u2014 structure and function?", a: "Spherical follicle lined by follicular cells <i>(simple cuboidal <b>\u2192</b> columnar when active)</i>; lumen filled with colloid <i>(thyroglobulin)</i>; parafollicular <i>(C)</i> cells between follicles <b>\u2192</b> calcitonin" },
            { q: "Thyroid C cells (parafollicular cells) \u2014 origin and product?", a: "Neural crest origin; secrete calcitonin <i>(\u2193 serum Ca\u00b2\u207a by \u2193 osteoclast activity)</i>; basis of medullary thyroid carcinoma; stain with silver" },
            { q: "Parathyroid gland cell types?", a: "Chief cells <i>(most numerous; secrete PTH; clear cytoplasm)</i> + Oxyphil cells <i>(function unclear; pink/acidophilic; appear after puberty; many mitochondria)</i>" },
            { q: "Adrenal cortex zones and products (mnemonic)?", a: "GFR \u2014 Salt, Sugar, Sex <i>(outside-in)</i>: Zona Glomerulosa <i>(aldosterone \u2014 salt)</i> <b>\u2192</b> Zona Fasciculata <i>(cortisol, most of cortex \u2014 sugar)</i> <b>\u2192</b> Zona Reticularis <i>(androgens/DHEA \u2014 sex)</i>; \"GFR: the deeper you go, the sweeter it gets\"" },
            { q: "Zona glomerulosa \u2014 regulated by what and produces?", a: "Regulated by angiotensin II + hyperkalemia <i>(NOT ACTH primarily)</i>; produces aldosterone <i>(only zone with aldosterone synthase)</i>" },
            { q: "Zona fasciculata \u2014 histology and regulation?", a: "Clear <i>(lipid-laden)</i> cells arranged in cords; regulated by ACTH; produces cortisol <i>(glucocorticoid)</i> + some sex steroids" },
            { q: "Zona reticularis \u2014 regulation and products?", a: "Regulated by ACTH; produces DHEA and androstenedione <i>(weak androgens)</i>; compact cells with lipofuscin pigment" },
            { q: "Adrenal medulla \u2014 cell type and staining?", a: "Chromaffin cells <i>(modified postganglionic sympathetic neurons)</i>; stain brown with chromium salts <i>(chromaffin reaction)</i>; secrete epinephrine <i>(80%)</i> + norepinephrine <i>(20%)</i>; innervated by preganglionic sympathetic <i>(ACh)</i>" },
            { q: "Islets of Langerhans \u2014 cell types and products?", a: "Beta <i>(\u03b2)</i> cells \u2014 center \u2014 insulin + amylin <i>(60\u201370%)</i> | Alpha <i>(\u03b1)</i> cells \u2014 periphery \u2014 glucagon <i>(15\u201320%)</i> | Delta <i>(\u03b4)</i> cells \u2014 somatostatin <i>(5\u201310%)</i> | PP cells \u2014 pancreatic polypeptide | Epsilon <i>(\u03b5)</i> \u2014 ghrelin" },
            { q: "Pineal gland cell types and product?", a: "Pinealocytes <i>(secrete melatonin from tryptophan <b>\u2192</b> serotonin <b>\u2192</b> melatonin)</i>; astrocyte-like interstitial cells; calcifies with age <i>(\"brain sand\"/corpora arenacea)</i>; visible on skull X-ray as midline calcification" },
            { q: "Hypotahlamic-pituitary portal system \u2014 significance?", a: "Hypophyseal portal veins carry hypothalamic releasing/inhibiting hormones directly to anterior pituitary in high concentrations without systemic dilution; median eminence <b>\u2192</b> portal vessels <b>\u2192</b> anterior pituitary" },
            { q: "Hypothalamic releasing hormones and their targets (complete list)?", a: "TRH <b>\u2192</b> TSH + prolactin | CRH <b>\u2192</b> ACTH | GnRH <b>\u2192</b> LH + FSH | GHRH <b>\u2192</b> GH | Somatostatin <i>(GHIH)</i> <b>\u2192</b> \u2193GH + \u2193TSH | Dopamine <b>\u2192</b> \u2193Prolactin | MSH-releasing factor <b>\u2192</b> MSH" },
            { q: "Prolactin \u2014 unique regulation?", a: "Under tonic inhibition by dopamine <i>(prolactin-inhibiting factor)</i>; dopamine antagonists <i>(metoclopramide, antipsychotics)</i> <b>\u2192</b> hyperprolactinemia; TRH stimulates prolactin <i>(explains hyperprolactinemia in hypothyroidism)</i>" },
            { q: "Growth Hormone (GH) \u2014 direct vs. indirect effects?", a: "Direct: anti-insulin <i>(\u2191 lipolysis, \u2191 gluconeogenesis, \u2191 blood glucose)</i> | Indirect <i>(via IGF-1 from liver)</i>: \u2191 protein synthesis, \u2191 linear bone growth, \u2191 organ size, \u2191 muscle mass" },
            { q: "GH secretion \u2014 stimulated by?", a: "Sleep <i>(slow-wave)</i>, hypoglycemia, exercise, fasting, stress, ghrelin, arginine, GHRH; inhibited by somatostatin, IGF-1, hyperglycemia, obesity, GH itself" },
            { q: "Acromegaly \u2014 cause, findings, and diagnosis?", a: "GH-secreting pituitary adenoma <i>(after epiphyseal closure)</i>; coarsened facial features, large hands/feet, macroglossia, prognathism, \u2191 hat/ring/shoe size, DM, HTN, carpal tunnel; <b>diagnosis</b>: \u2191 IGF-1 + failure to suppress GH with oral glucose" },
            { q: "Gigantism vs. acromegaly?", a: "Gigantism: excess GH before epiphyseal closure <b>\u2192</b> tall stature + acromegalic features | Acromegaly: after closure <b>\u2192</b> no height increase, only soft tissue/bone widening" },
            { q: "ADH (Vasopressin) \u2014 osmoreceptors vs. baroreceptors?", a: "Osmoreceptors <i>(hypothalamus)</i>: respond to \u2191 osmolality <i>(>280 mOsm/kg)</i> \u2014 most sensitive | Baroreceptors <i>(carotid, aortic arch)</i>: respond to \u2193 blood volume/pressure \u2014 less sensitive but override osmolality in severe hypovolemia" },
            { q: "SIADH causes (mnemonic SIADH)?", a: "Surgery/Stress | Intracranial pathology <i>(meningitis, trauma, stroke)</i> | ADH-secreting tumor <i>(small cell lung ca)</i> | Drugs <i>(carbamazepine, SSRIs, chlorpropamide, cyclophosphamide)</i> | Hypothyroidism/HIV" },
            { q: "Oxytocin \u2014 source, stimuli, effects?", a: "Paraventricular nucleus <i>(primarily)</i>; released by nipple stimulation + cervical dilation <i>(Ferguson reflex)</i> + dehydration; effects: uterine contraction, milk ejection <i>(not production)</i>, pair bonding; positive feedback during labor" },
            { q: "Empty sella syndrome \u2014 cause?", a: "Herniation of subarachnoid space into sella turcica <i>(CSF)</i> <b>\u2192</b> compresses pituitary <b>\u2192</b> enlarged sella on imaging; primary <i>(idiopathic, obese women)</i> or secondary <i>(pituitary infarction/surgery)</i>; may have partial hypopituitarism" },
            { q: "Pituitary apoplexy \u2014 definition?", a: "Sudden hemorrhage or infarction of pituitary gland <i>(often into existing adenoma)</i>; severe headache + visual field defects + ophthalmoplegia + acute hypopituitarism; <b>emergency</b>" },
            { q: "Order of hormone loss in hypopituitarism?", a: "GH <i>(first, most sensitive)</i> <b>\u2192</b> LH/FSH <b>\u2192</b> TSH <b>\u2192</b> ACTH <b>\u2192</b> Prolactin <i>(last)</i>; PRL may \u2191 if stalk compression removes dopamine inhibition" },
            { q: "Steps of thyroid hormone synthesis (in order)?", a: "<i>(1)</i> I\u207b uptake by NIS <i>(Na\u207a/I\u207b symporter, basolateral)</i> <b>\u2192</b> <i>(2)</i> I\u207b oxidized to I\u2082 by thyroid peroxidase <i>(TPO)</i> \u2014 organification \u2014 <i>(3)</i> I\u2082 added to tyrosine residues on thyroglobulin <i>(MIT, DIT)</i> <b>\u2192</b> <i>(4)</i> Coupling: MIT+DIT=T3; DIT+DIT=T4 \u2014 <i>(5)</i> Proteolysis of thyroglobulin <b>\u2192</b> T3/T4 released into blood" },
            { q: "T3 vs. T4 \u2014 which is more active and why?", a: "T3 <i>(triiodothyronine)</i> is more potent <i>(3\u20135\u00d7 more active)</i>; T4 is prohormone converted to T3 peripherally by 5'-deiodinase <i>(liver, kidney, muscle)</i>; T4 has longer half-life <i>(~7 days vs. 1 day)</i>" },
            { q: "Reverse T3 (rT3) \u2014 formation and significance?", a: "T4 <b>\u2192</b> rT3 by 5-deiodinase <i>(inner ring)</i> instead of T3; metabolically inactive; elevated in sick euthyroid syndrome <i>(\u2191 rT3, \u2193 T3, normal/\u2193 TSH)</i>" },
            { q: "Thyroid hormone transport in blood?", a: ">99% bound to Thyroxine-Binding Globulin <i>(TBG, most)</i>, transthyretin, albumin; only free hormone is active; \u2191 TBG <i>(pregnancy, estrogen, OCP)</i> <b>\u2192</b> \u2191 total T4 but normal free T4" },
            { q: "Thyroid hormone mechanism of action?", a: "Lipophilic <b>\u2192</b> enters cell <b>\u2192</b> binds nuclear thyroid hormone receptor <i>(TR)</i> <b>\u2192</b> TR-hormone complex binds TRE <i>(thyroid response element)</i> <b>\u2192</b> gene transcription; T3 binds 10\u00d7 more avidly than T4" },
            { q: "Thyroid hormone physiologic effects (key 8)?", a: "<i>(1)</i> \u2191 BMR <i>(\u2191 Na\u207a/K\u207a-ATPase)</i>, <i>(2)</i> \u2191 cardiac output <i>(\u2191 HR + contractility \u2014 \u2191 \u03b2-receptor sensitivity)</i>, <i>(3)</i> \u2191 bone turnover, <i>(4)</i> \u2191 CNS development <i>(critical in fetal life)</i>, <i>(5)</i> \u2191 GH secretion, <i>(6)</i> \u2191 glycogenolysis + gluconeogenesis, <i>(7)</i> \u2191 lipid metabolism, <i>(8)</i> \u2191 gut motility" },
            { q: "Wolff-Chaikoff effect?", a: "Large doses of iodide transiently inhibit thyroid hormone synthesis <i>(\u2193 organification)</i>; protective auto-regulation; used in thyroid storm <b>treatment</b> <i>(saturated solution of potassium iodide \u2014 SSKI)</i>; lasts ~10 days then escapes" },
            { q: "Jod-Basedow phenomenon?", a: "Iodine excess <b>\u2192</b> hyperthyroidism in patients with pre-existing autonomous nodules or endemic goiter <i>(iodine deficiency background)</i>; opposite of Wolff-Chaikoff; seen with contrast dye in susceptible patients" },
            { q: "Graves' disease \u2014 antibody, pathogenesis, unique features?", a: "TSI/TRAb <i>(thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulin)</i> \u2014 IgG stimulates TSH receptor <b>\u2192</b> autonomous hyperthyroidism; unique: exophthalmos <i>(proptosis)</i>, pretibial myxedema, thyroid acropachy; diffuse goiter; <b>most common</b> hyperthyroidism in young women" },
            { q: "Hashimoto's thyroiditis \u2014 antibodies and histology?", a: "Anti-TPO <i>(most sensitive)</i> + anti-thyroglobulin antibodies; lymphocytic infiltration + germinal centers + H\u00fcrthle cell change <i>(oxyphilic metaplasia)</i>; <b>most common</b> cause of hypothyroidism in iodine-sufficient areas; \u2191 <b>risk</b> of B-cell lymphoma <i>(MALT)</i>" },
            { q: "Hypothyroidism features (key clinical findings)?", a: "Fatigue, cold intolerance, weight gain, constipation, bradycardia, dry skin, coarse hair, myxedema <i>(non-pitting, GAG accumulation)</i>, delayed DTRs, hyperlipidemia, macrocytic anemia <i>(\u2193 EPO + B12 absorption)</i>, menorrhagia" },
            { q: "Thyroid storm \u2014 precipitants and treatment?", a: "Precipitants: surgery, infection, trauma, contrast in hyperthyroid patient; <b>treatment</b>: PTU <i>(blocks synthesis + peripheral conversion)</i> <b>\u2192</b> KI/Lugol's <i>(Wolff-Chaikoff, give AFTER PTU)</i> <b>\u2192</b> \u03b2-blocker <i>(propranolol, \u2193 symptoms + T4\u2192T3 conversion)</i> <b>\u2192</b> steroids <i>(\u2193 peripheral conversion)</i> <b>\u2192</b> supportive" },
            { q: "PTU vs. Methimazole \u2014 differences?", a: "PTU: blocks TPO + blocks peripheral T4\u2192T3 conversion; used in 1st trimester pregnancy + thyroid storm | Methimazole: blocks TPO only; teratogenic <i>(aplasia cutis)</i> \u2014 avoid in 1st trimester; preferred 2nd/3rd trimester + non-pregnant adults; longer acting" },
            { q: "Subclinical vs. overt hypothyroidism?", a: "Subclinical: \u2191 TSH + normal free T4 <i>(asymptomatic/mild symptoms)</i> | Overt: \u2191 TSH + \u2193 free T4; treat overt; treat subclinical if TSH >10 or symptomatic" },
            { q: "Congenital hypothyroidism (Cretinism) \u2014 cause and features?", a: "Iodine deficiency <i>(most common worldwide)</i> or thyroid dysgenesis; features if untreated: intellectual disability, short stature, coarse facies, large tongue, umbilical hernia, constipation, prolonged jaundice; neonatal screen: TSH; treat immediately with levothyroxine" },
            { q: "De Quervain's (Subacute Granulomatous) Thyroiditis \u2014 cause and course?", a: "Viral <i>(postviral)</i>; painful thyroid, \u2191 ESR, jaw pain; triphasic: hyperthyroid <i>(release of stored hormone)</i> <b>\u2192</b> hypothroid <b>\u2192</b> euthyroid; \u2191 T4/T3 with \u2193 radioiodine uptake <i>(distinguishes from Graves')</i>; self-limiting; treat with numerical" },
            { q: "Riedel's thyroiditis \u2014 key feature?", a: "Fibrous replacement of thyroid <b>\u2192</b> \"woody/hard\" thyroid; mimics malignancy; associated with IgG4-related disease and retroperitoneal fibrosis; may cause hypothyroidism/compressive symptoms" },
            { q: "Thyroid cancer \u2014 most common type and prognosis?", a: "Papillary <i>(80%)</i> \u2014 best prognosis; Follicular <i>(10%)</i> \u2014 hematogenous spread; Medullary <i>(5%)</i> \u2014 C cells, calcitonin <b>marker</b>, MEN2A/2B; Anaplastic <i>(<2%)</i> \u2014 worst prognosis, rapidly fatal" },
            { q: "Papillary thyroid carcinoma \u2014 histologic features?", a: "Orphan Annie eye nuclei <i>(ground glass, empty-looking)</i>, Psammoma bodies <i>(dystrophic calcification)</i>, nuclear grooves, papillary architecture; spreads via lymphatics; excellent prognosis; RET/PTC and BRAF mutations" },
            { q: "Medullary thyroid carcinoma \u2014 marker and associations?", a: "Calcitonin <i>(tumor marker)</i>; amyloid stroma <i>(calcitonin deposits)</i>; RET proto-oncogene mutation; associated with MEN2A <i>(+ pheochromocytoma + parathyroid hyperplasia)</i> and MEN2B <i>(+ pheochromocytoma + mucosal neuromas + marfanoid)</i>" },
            { q: "PTH \u2014 stimulus for release and overall effect?", a: "\u2193 serum Ca\u00b2\u207a <i>(primary)</i> + \u2191 phosphate; net effect: \u2191 serum Ca\u00b2\u207a + \u2193 serum phosphate" },
            { q: "PTH effects on bone, kidney, and gut?", a: "Bone: \u2191 osteoclast activity <i>(via osteoblast RANK-L)</i> <b>\u2192</b> \u2191 Ca\u00b2\u207a + phosphate release | Kidney: \u2191 Ca\u00b2\u207a reabsorption <i>(DCT)</i>, \u2193 phosphate reabsorption <i>(PCT)</i>, \u2191 1\u03b1-hydroxylase <b>\u2192</b> \u2191 calcitriol | Gut: indirect via calcitriol <b>\u2192</b> \u2191 Ca\u00b2\u207a + phosphate absorption" },
            { q: "Calcitriol (1,25-(OH)\u2082D\u2083) \u2014 synthesis steps?", a: "Skin: UV <b>\u2192</b> cholesterol <b>\u2192</b> vitamin D3 <i>(cholecalciferol)</i> <b>\u2192</b> liver: 25-hydroxylase <b>\u2192</b> 25-<i>(OH)</i>D3 <b>\u2192</b> kidney <i>(1\u03b1-hydroxylase, stimulated by PTH, \u2193Ca, \u2193PO4)</i> <b>\u2192</b> 1,25-<i>(OH)</i>\u2082D\u2083 <i>(calcitriol, active form)</i>" },
            { q: "Calcitriol effects?", a: "\u2191 intestinal Ca\u00b2\u207a + phosphate absorption <i>(via calbindin)</i>, \u2191 renal Ca\u00b2\u207a + phosphate reabsorption, \u2191 bone mineralization <i>(at physiologic levels)</i>, feedback inhibits PTH" },
            { q: "Calcitonin \u2014 source, stimulus, effect?", a: "Parafollicular <i>(C)</i> cells of thyroid; stimulated by \u2191 Ca\u00b2\u207a; \u2193 osteoclast activity <b>\u2192</b> \u2193 serum Ca\u00b2\u207a + \u2193 phosphate; physiologically minor role in humans; used therapeutically in hypercalcemia + Paget's disease + osteoporosis" },
            { q: "Hyperparathyroidism Type 1 (Primary) \u2014 cause and labs?", a: "Parathyroid adenoma <i>(most common, 80%)</i> or hyperplasia or carcinoma; \u2191 PTH, \u2191 Ca\u00b2\u207a, \u2193 phosphate, \u2191 urine Ca\u00b2\u207a, \u2191 ALP; symptoms: bones <i>(osteitis fibrosa cystica)</i>, stones <i>(nephrolithiasis)</i>, groans <i>(GI: constipation, PUD)</i>, moans <i>(psychiatric)</i>" },
            { q: "Secondary hyperparathyroidism \u2014 cause and labs?", a: "Chronic hypocalcemia <i>(CKD most common, vitamin D deficiency)</i> <b>\u2192</b> compensatory \u2191 PTH; labs: \u2191 PTH, \u2193 or normal Ca\u00b2\u207a, \u2191 phosphate <i>(CKD)</i> or \u2193 phosphate <i>(VitD deficiency)</i>" },
            { q: "Tertiary hyperparathyroidism \u2014 definition?", a: "Autonomous PTH secretion after prolonged secondary hyperparathyroidism <i>(CKD)</i>; parathyroid becomes independent of Ca\u00b2\u207a feedback; \u2191 PTH + \u2191 Ca\u00b2\u207a <i>(unlike secondary)</i>; requires parathyroidectomy" },
            { q: "Hypoparathyroidism \u2014 causes and presentation?", a: "Post-thyroidectomy <i>(most common)</i>, DiGeorge, autoimmune; \u2193 PTH <b>\u2192</b> hypocalcemia + hyperphosphatemia; Chvostek's sign <i>(tap facial nerve \u2192 facial twitch)</i> + Trousseau's sign <i>(BP cuff \u2192 carpal spasm)</i> + tetany + prolonged QT" },
            { q: "Pseudohypoparathyroidism (PHP) Type 1a \u2014 defect and features?", a: "End-organ resistance to PTH <i>(Gs\u03b1 mutation, maternal allele \u2014 imprinting)</i>; \u2191 PTH + \u2193 Ca\u00b2\u207a + \u2191 phosphate; Albright's hereditary osteodystrophy: short stature, round facies, shortened 4th metacarpal, obesity, intellectual disability; \"SEABRIGHT bantam chicken\" sign" },
            { q: "Milk-alkali syndrome \u2014 cause?", a: "Excess Ca\u00b2\u207a + absorbable alkali <i>(antacids, milk)</i> <b>\u2192</b> hypercalcemia + metabolic alkalosis + renal failure; \u2193 PTH; third <b>most common</b> cause of hypercalcemia <i>(after primary HPT and malignancy)</i>" },
            { q: "Hypercalcemia of malignancy \u2014 mechanisms?", a: "PTHrP <i>(parathyroid hormone-related protein)</i> \u2014 <b>most common</b> <i>(squamous cell Ca of lung, breast, renal, bladder)</i> | Osteolytic metastases <i>(breast, MM)</i> | 1,25-<i>(OH)</i>\u2082D excess <i>(lymphoma \u2014 activated macrophages produce 1\u03b1-hydroxylase)</i>" },
            { q: "Steroidogenesis pathway \u2014 key enzyme for cortisol synthesis?", a: "Cholesterol <b>\u2192</b> pregnenolone <i>(via cholesterol desmolase/StAR)</i> <b>\u2192</b> progesterone <b>\u2192</b> 17\u03b1-hydroxyprogesterone <b>\u2192</b> 11-deoxycortisol <b>\u2192</b> cortisol <i>(via 11\u03b2-hydroxylase)</i>; rate-limiting step = cholesterol <b>\u2192</b> pregnenolone" },
            { q: "21-hydroxylase deficiency (most common CAH) \u2014 findings?", a: "\u2193 cortisol + \u2193 aldosterone + \u2191 17-OH progesterone + \u2191 androgens; virilization of 46,XX females; salt-wasting <i>(hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, hypotension)</i>; \u2191 ACTH <i>(no cortisol feedback)</i>; \u2191 renin" },
            { q: "11\u03b2-hydroxylase deficiency (CAH) \u2014 key difference from 21-hydroxylase?", a: "\u2193 cortisol + \u2191 androgens + \u2191 11-deoxycorticosterone <i>(mineralocorticoid effect)</i> <b>\u2192</b> hypertension <i>(\u2191 salt retention)</i> + hypokalemia; virilization of females; distinguishing feature = HTN <i>(vs. hypotension in 21-OH deficiency)</i>" },
            { q: "17\u03b1-hydroxylase deficiency (CAH) \u2014 findings?", a: "\u2193 cortisol + \u2193 sex steroids + \u2191 mineralocorticoids <i>(DOC)</i> <b>\u2192</b> HTN + hypokalemia; sexual infantilism in 46,XX <i>(no sex steroids)</i> + ambiguous genitalia/female phenotype in 46,XY; \u2191 ACTH" },
            { q: "Cortisol \u2014 physiologic effects (key 8)?", a: "<i>(1)</i> \u2191 gluconeogenesis + glycogenolysis <i>(\u2191 blood glucose)</i>, <i>(2)</i> \u2191 protein catabolism, <i>(3)</i> \u2191 lipolysis <i>(and redistribution)</i>, <i>(4)</i> \u2193 inflammation <i>(\u2193 PLA2, \u2193 prostaglandins, \u2193 cytokines)</i>, <i>(5)</i> \u2193 immune function, <i>(6)</i> \u2191 bone resorption, <i>(7)</i> \u2191 GFR, <i>(8)</i> fetal lung maturity <i>(surfactant)</i> + maintains vascular tone" },
            { q: "Cortisol \u2014 diurnal variation?", a: "Peaks in early morning <i>(6\u20138 AM, before waking)</i> <b>\u2192</b> lowest at midnight; disrupted in Cushing's syndrome <i>(loss of diurnal rhythm = most sensitive early test)</i>" },
            { q: "Cushing's syndrome \u2014 most common cause overall vs. most common endogenous cause?", a: "Overall: exogenous corticosteroids <i>(most common)</i> | Endogenous: Cushing's disease <i>(pituitary ACTH-secreting adenoma, ~70%)</i> | Other endogenous: ectopic ACTH <i>(small cell lung Ca, most common ectopic source)</i> + adrenal adenoma/carcinoma" },
            { q: "Cushing's syndrome features (key clinical)?", a: "Central obesity <i>(buffalo hump, moon facies, supraclavicular fat pad)</i>, purple striae <i>(>1 cm)</i>, proximal muscle weakness, thin skin/easy bruising, osteoporosis, DM, hypertension, hirsutism, menstrual irregularity, psychiatric symptoms, \u2193 immune function" },
            { q: "Cushing's syndrome diagnosis \u2014 step-by-step?", a: "Step 1: Confirm hypercortisolism <b>\u2192</b> 24h urine free cortisol OR late-night salivary cortisol OR overnight 1 mg dexamethasone suppression test <i>(\u2193 cortisol = normal)</i> | Step 2: \u2191 or \u2193 ACTH? <b>\u2192</b> If \u2191 ACTH: pituitary vs. ectopic <i>(high-dose dex suppression)</i> | CRH stimulation test | Inferior petrosal sinus sampling" },
            { q: "Dexamethasone suppression test \u2014 interpretation?", a: "Low-dose <i>(1 mg overnight or 2 mg/day \u00d7 2 days)</i>: suppresses cortisol in normal + adrenal adenoma fails | High-dose <i>(8 mg/day \u00d7 2 days)</i>: suppresses cortisol in Cushing's disease <i>(pituitary)</i> but NOT ectopic ACTH or adrenal tumor" },
            { q: "Nelson's syndrome?", a: "After bilateral adrenalectomy for Cushing's disease <b>\u2192</b> loss of cortisol feedback <b>\u2192</b> \u2191 ACTH + MSH from expanding pituitary adenoma <b>\u2192</b> hyperpigmentation + visual field defects; pituitary adenoma enlarges" },
            { q: "Primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison's disease) \u2014 cause and labs?", a: "Destruction of adrenal cortex <i>(autoimmune most common in developed world \u2014 anti-21-hydroxylase Ab; TB worldwide)</i>; \u2193 cortisol + \u2193 aldosterone + \u2191 ACTH <b>\u2192</b> hyperpigmentation <i>(\u2191 MSH from POMC)</i>; hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, hypotension, hypoglycemia, eosinophilia" },
            { q: "Secondary vs. tertiary adrenal insufficiency?", a: "Secondary: \u2193 ACTH <i>(pituitary disease)</i>; \u2193 cortisol + \u2193 androgens; aldosterone intact <i>(no hyperpigmentation, no hyperkalemia)</i> | Tertiary: \u2193 CRH <i>(hypothalamic disease or chronic exogenous steroid use \u2014 most common cause of secondary/tertiary AI)</i>" },
            { q: "Addisonian crisis \u2014 trigger and treatment?", a: "Physiologic stress in AI patient <i>(infection, surgery, trauma)</i>; severe hypotension, hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, hypoglycemia; treat with IV hydrocortisone <i>(stress dose)</i> + IV fluids + glucose; do not delay for <b>diagnostic</b> testing" },
            { q: "Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome?", a: "Bilateral adrenal hemorrhage <b>\u2192</b> acute adrenal crisis; <b>most common</b> cause: Neisseria meningitidis septicemia; DIC + hemorrhagic adrenal destruction; also Pseudomonas, anticoagulant therapy" },
            { q: "Aldosterone \u2014 effects on kidney, gut, salivary glands?", a: "\u2191 Na\u207a reabsorption <i>(ENaC)</i> + \u2191 K\u207a/H\u207a secretion <i>(H\u207a-ATPase)</i> in collecting duct; also \u2191 Na\u207a absorption in colon and salivary/sweat glands; net: \u2191 blood volume, \u2193 K\u207a, metabolic alkalosis" },
            { q: "Conn syndrome (Primary hyperaldosteronism) \u2014 cause, labs, treatment?", a: "Adrenal adenoma <i>(Conn's)</i> or bilateral adrenal hyperplasia; \u2191 aldosterone + \u2193 renin <i>(suppressed)</i> + hypertension + hypokalemia + metabolic alkalosis; headache, muscle weakness, polyuria; treat adenoma with surgery; hyperplasia with spironolactone/eplerenone" },
            { q: "Catecholamine synthesis pathway?", a: "Tyrosine <b>\u2192</b> DOPA <i>(tyrosine hydroxylase, rate-limiting)</i> <b>\u2192</b> Dopamine <i>(DOPA decarboxylase)</i> <b>\u2192</b> Norepinephrine <i>(dopamine \u03b2-hydroxylase, in vesicles)</i> <b>\u2192</b> Epinephrine <i>(PNMT, in cytosol, requires cortisol)</i>" },
            { q: "Why does adrenal medulla produce mostly epinephrine?", a: "PNMT <i>(phenylethanolamine N-methyltransferase)</i> requires high cortisol concentrations <i>(from zona fasciculata via portal blood flow within adrenal)</i> to convert NE <b>\u2192</b> epinephrine; medulla is bathed in cortisol" },
            { q: "Epinephrine vs. Norepinephrine \u2014 receptor preference?", a: "Epinephrine: \u03b21 = \u03b22 > \u03b11 <i>(vasodilation at low doses via \u03b22, vasoconstriction at high doses)</i> | NE: \u03b11 > \u03b21 >> \u03b22; NE <b>\u2192</b> reflex bradycardia <i>(\u2191 BP \u2192 baroreceptor \u2192 \u2193 HR)</i>" },
            { q: "Pheochromocytoma \u2014 rule of 10s?", a: "10% malignant, 10% bilateral, 10% extra-adrenal <i>(paraganglioma)</i>, 10% familial, 10% in children, 10% found incidentally; associated with MEN2A, MEN2B, VHL, NF1, SDH mutations" },
            { q: "Pheochromocytoma \u2014 symptoms and diagnosis?", a: "Episodic: headache <i>(most common)</i>, diaphoresis, palpitations, hypertension <i>(sustained or paroxysmal)</i>; <b>diagnosis</b>: 24h urine metanephrines + catecholamines <i>(most sensitive)</i> OR plasma free metanephrines; localize with CT/MRI <b>\u2192</b> MIBG scan" },
            { q: "Pheochromocytoma \u2014 preoperative treatment?", a: "\u03b1-blocker FIRST <i>(phenoxybenzamine \u2014 irreversible, or phentolamine)</i> <b>\u2192</b> then \u03b2-blocker <i>(propranolol)</i>; NEVER give \u03b2-blocker first <b>\u2192</b> unopposed \u03b1 <b>\u2192</b> hypertensive crisis" },
            { q: "Insulin \u2014 structure and synthesis?", a: "Polypeptide <i>(2 chains: A + B, connected by C-peptide)</i>; synthesized as preproinsulin <b>\u2192</b> proinsulin <i>(endoplasmic reticulum)</i> <b>\u2192</b> cleaved to insulin + C-peptide <i>(equimolar secretion)</i>; C-peptide = <b>marker</b> of endogenous insulin production" },
            { q: "Insulin secretion \u2014 primary stimulus and mechanism?", a: "\u2191 Blood glucose <b>\u2192</b> GLUT2 uptake <b>\u2192</b> glycolysis <b>\u2192</b> \u2191 ATP/ADP ratio <b>\u2192</b> closes K_ATP channel <b>\u2192</b> membrane depolarization <b>\u2192</b> opens voltage-gated Ca\u00b2\u207a channels <b>\u2192</b> Ca\u00b2\u207a influx <b>\u2192</b> exocytosis of insulin granules; also stimulated by amino acids, GLP-1, GIP, vagal stimulation, \u03b22 agonists" },
            { q: "Insulin \u2014 anabolic effects (key 6)?", a: "<i>(1)</i> \u2191 Glucose uptake <i>(GLUT4 in muscle/fat)</i>, <i>(2)</i> \u2191 glycogenesis, <i>(3)</i> \u2191 glycolysis, <i>(4)</i> \u2191 lipogenesis + \u2193 lipolysis, <i>(5)</i> \u2191 protein synthesis, <i>(6)</i> \u2191 K\u207a uptake into cells <i>(\u2193 serum K\u207a)</i>" },
            { q: "GLUT transporters \u2014 locations and regulation?", a: "GLUT1: RBCs, brain <i>(basal)</i> | GLUT2: liver, pancreatic \u03b2 cells, kidney, small intestine <i>(high Km, not saturable at normal glucose)</i> | GLUT3: neurons | GLUT4: muscle + adipose <i>(insulin-dependent, most important)</i> | GLUT5: fructose <i>(small intestine)</i>" },
            { q: "Glucagon \u2014 stimulus, source, effects?", a: "\u2193 Blood glucose + amino acids + stress; from \u03b1 cells; effects: \u2191 glycogenolysis, \u2191 gluconeogenesis, \u2191 lipolysis, \u2191 ketogenesis; acts via Gs <b>\u2192</b> \u2191 cAMP <b>\u2192</b> PKA; insulin opposes all glucagon actions" },
            { q: "Somatostatin \u2014 source and effects?", a: "Delta cells <i>(pancreas)</i> + D cells <i>(GI tract)</i> + hypothalamus <i>(GHIH)</i>; inhibits insulin + glucagon + GH + TSH + GI secretions <i>(gastrin, secretin, CCK)</i> + GI motility; octreotide = long-acting analog <i>(used in acromegaly, carcinoid, VIPoma, esophageal varices)</i>" },
            { q: "Incretin hormones \u2014 GLP-1 and GIP?", a: "GLP-1 <i>(glucagon-like peptide-1)</i>: from L cells <i>(ileum/colon)</i>; \u2191 glucose-dependent insulin release, \u2193 glucagon, \u2193 gastric emptying, \u2191 satiety | GIP <i>(gastric inhibitory peptide)</i>: from K cells <i>(duodenum/jejunum)</i>; \u2191 insulin release; both degraded by DPP-4; GLP-1 agonists = semaglutide, liraglutide" },
            { q: "Type 1 vs. Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus \u2014 key differences?", a: "T1DM: autoimmune <i>(anti-GAD, anti-islet cell, anti-insulin Ab)</i>, HLA-DR3/DR4, \u2193 insulin <i>(absolute)</i>, ketoacidosis-prone, thin patients | T2DM: insulin resistance + relative \u2193 insulin, genetic + lifestyle, obesity, \u2191 C-peptide <i>(early)</i>, no autoantibodies, hyperosmolar state" },
            { q: "Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) \u2014 pathophysiology?", a: "\u2193 Insulin <b>\u2192</b> \u2191 glucagon <b>\u2192</b> \u2191 lipolysis <b>\u2192</b> FFAs <b>\u2192</b> ketones <i>(acetoacetate, \u03b2-hydroxybutyrate)</i>; high AG metabolic acidosis; hyperglycemia <b>\u2192</b> osmotic diuresis <b>\u2192</b> dehydration; total body K\u207a depletion <i>(serum K\u207a may be normal/\u2191 initially due to acidosis \u2192 K\u207a shifts out)</i>" },
            { q: "Hyperosmolar Hyperglycemic State (HHS) vs. DKA?", a: "HHS <i>(T2DM)</i>: glucose >600 mg/dL, \u2191\u2191 serum osmolality <i>(>320)</i>, no/minimal ketosis, altered mental status, no significant acidosis; DKA <i>(T1DM)</i>: glucose ~300\u2013500, ketoacidosis <i>(pH <7.3)</i>, Kussmaul breathing, fruity breath; HHS has higher mortality" },
            { q: "Insulinoma \u2014 key features and diagnosis?", a: "Tumor of \u03b2 cells; hypoglycemia with \u2191 insulin + \u2191 C-peptide <i>(endogenous)</i> + \u2193 glucose; 72-hour fast <b>\u2192</b> hypoglycemia <b>\u2192</b> \u2191 insulin; no sulfonylurea in blood; <b>most common</b> pancreatic endocrine tumor; Rule of 3s: <3 cm, <3% malignant, >3 found in 10% cases" },
            { q: "Glucagonoma \u2014 key features?", a: "\u03b1 cell tumor; 4 Ds: Dermatitis <i>(necrolytic migratory erythema \u2014 pathognomonic)</i>, Diabetes <i>(\u2191 glucose)</i>, DVT, Depression; \u2191 glucagon; weight loss; hypoaminoacidemia" },
            { q: "VIPoma (Verner-Morrison syndrome)?", a: "Tumor secreting VIP; profuse watery diarrhea <i>(>3L/day)</i>, hypokalemia, achlorhydria <i>(WDHA syndrome)</i>; \u2191 VIP; inhibit with octreotide" },
            { q: "Zollinger-Ellison syndrome \u2014 cause and features?", a: "Gastrinoma <i>(G cell tumor, usually in duodenum/pancreas \u2014 gastrinoma triangle)</i>; \u2191\u2191 gastrin <b>\u2192</b> \u2191 HCl <b>\u2192</b> multiple peptic ulcers <i>(distal duodenum/jejunum)</i>, diarrhea, malabsorption; secretin stimulation test: paradoxical \u2191 gastrin; 25% MEN1 associated" },
            { q: "MEN1 \u2014 components and gene?", a: "3 Ps: Parathyroid hyperplasia <i>(most common, first to appear)</i> + Pituitary adenoma <i>(prolactinoma most common)</i> + Pancreatic endocrine tumors <i>(gastrinoma most common)</i>; MEN1 gene <i>(menin, chromosome 11q13)</i>; autosomal dominant" },
            { q: "MEN2A \u2014 components and gene?", a: "Medullary thyroid carcinoma <i>(MTC, 100%)</i> + Pheochromocytoma <i>(50%)</i> + Parathyroid hyperplasia <i>(20%)</i>; RET proto-oncogene mutation; prophylactic thyroidectomy in RET+ patients" },
            { q: "MEN2B \u2014 components?", a: "MTC + Pheochromocytoma + Mucosal neuromas <i>(lips, tongue, GI)</i> + Marfanoid habitus; NO parathyroid involvement; most aggressive MEN syndrome; RET mutation <i>(codon 918)</i>; earliest MTC onset" },
            { q: "Carcinoid syndrome \u2014 features, mediator, location?", a: "Flushing <i>(most common)</i>, diarrhea, bronchospasm, right-sided valvular lesions <i>(tricuspid regurgitation)</i>; mediator: serotonin <i>(5-HT)</i>; occurs when tumor metastasizes to liver <i>(bypasses portal degradation)</i> or is extraintestinal; \u2191 24h urine 5-HIAA; treat with octreotide" },
            { q: "Carcinoid tumor \u2014 most common location?", a: "GI tract <i>(appendix most common overall \u2014 rarely metastasize)</i> | Small intestine ileum <i>(most common to cause carcinoid syndrome)</i> | Lung <i>(bronchial)</i>" },
            { q: "Melatonin \u2014 synthesis pathway and function?", a: "Tryptophan <b>\u2192</b> serotonin <b>\u2192</b> N-acetylserotonin <b>\u2192</b> melatonin; secreted at night <i>(darkness)</i>; regulates circadian rhythm; \u2193 body temp, \u2193 GnRH <b>\u2192</b> \u2193 LH/FSH <i>(antigonadotropic)</i>; \u2191 in total blindness" },
            { q: "Leptin \u2014 source and function?", a: "Adipocytes; \u2191 with \u2191 fat mass <b>\u2192</b> acts on hypothalamus <i>(arcuate nucleus)</i> <b>\u2192</b> \u2191 POMC/CART <i>(anorexigenic)</i> + \u2193 NPY/AgRP <i>(orexigenic)</i> <b>\u2192</b> \u2193 appetite, \u2191 energy expenditure; leptin resistance in obesity <i>(like insulin resistance)</i>" },
            { q: "Ghrelin \u2014 source and function?", a: "Stomach <i>(P/D1 cells, fundus)</i>; only orexigenic gut hormone; \u2191 before meals, \u2193 after eating; stimulates GH secretion; acts on hypothalamic arcuate nucleus <b>\u2192</b> \u2191 NPY/AgRP <b>\u2192</b> \u2191 hunger; \u2191 in anorexia nervosa, Prader-Willi" },
            { q: "Erythropoietin (EPO) \u2014 source, stimulus, function?", a: "Peritubular fibroblasts <i>(interstitial cells)</i> of renal cortex; \u2191 secretion in response to \u2193 O\u2082 <i>(hypoxia via HIF-1\u03b1)</i> <b>\u2192</b> \u2191 RBC precursor proliferation + differentiation in bone marrow; \u2193 in CKD <b>\u2192</b> anemia; synthetic EPO <i>(darbepoetin)</i> for <b>treatment</b>" },
            { q: "Atrial Natriuretic Peptide (ANP) \u2014 stimulus and effects?", a: "Released by atrial cardiomyocytes in response to \u2191 atrial stretch <i>(\u2191 volume/pressure)</i>; acts via cGMP; \u2193 renin, \u2193 aldosterone, \u2193 ADH, \u2193 sympathetic tone, dilates afferent + constricts efferent arterioles <b>\u2192</b> \u2191 GFR + natriuresis; overall \u2193 BP + \u2193 volume" },
            { q: "BNP (B-type/Brain Natriuretic Peptide) \u2014 source and clinical use?", a: "Ventricular cardiomyocytes <i>(primarily)</i> in response to ventricular stretch; same effects as ANP; \u2191 BNP = <b>marker</b> of heart failure severity + ventricular dysfunction; also has prognostic value" },
            { q: "Renin \u2014 source, stimulus, mechanism?", a: "JG cells of kidney; stimulated by \u2193 BP <i>(baroreceptor)</i>, \u2193 NaCl <i>(macula densa)</i>, \u03b21-adrenergic stimulation; cleaves angiotensinogen <b>\u2192</b> angiotensin I; rate-limiting step of RAAS" },
            { q: "Vitamin D toxicity vs. deficiency?", a: "Deficiency: rickets <i>(children \u2014 bowing of legs, rachitic rosary)</i>, osteomalacia <i>(adults \u2014 soft bones, bone pain)</i>; \u2193 Ca\u00b2\u207a + \u2193 phosphate + \u2191 ALP + \u2191 PTH | Toxicity: hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, nephrolithiasis, renal failure; \u2191 Ca\u00b2\u207a + \u2191 phosphate + \u2193 PTH" },
            { q: "Thymus hormones and function?", a: "Thymosin, thymopoietin, thymulin; promote T-lymphocyte maturation and differentiation; maximal activity in childhood; involutes after puberty <i>(replaced by fat)</i>" },
            { q: "Insulin-like Growth Factor 1 (IGF-1) \u2014 source and regulation?", a: "Primarily liver; stimulated by GH; mediates most growth-promoting effects of GH <i>(somatomedin C)</i>; acts on bone <i>(\u2191 chondrocyte proliferation <b>\u2192</b> linear growth)</i>, muscle, organs; inhibited by malnutrition, liver disease; measured to screen/monitor acromegaly" }
          ]
        },
        {
          topic: 'Biochemistry (System Level)',
          icon: 'zap',
          cards: [
            { q: "Which amino acids are necessary for purine synthesis?", a: "Glycine, Aspartate, Glutamine." },
            { q: "Mechanism of 5-Fluorouracil (5-FU)?", a: "Inhibits thymidylate synthase <i>(decreases <i>dTMP</i>)</i>." },
            { q: "Enzyme deficient in Lesch-Nyhan syndrome?", a: "HGPRT <i>(He\u2019s Got Purine Recovery Trouble)</i>." },
            { q: "Type of mutation in Sickle Cell Anemia?", a: "Missense mutation <i>(Glutamic acid <b>\u2192</b> Valine)</i>." },
            { q: "Direction of DNA synthesis?", a: "5' to 3'." },
            { q: "Function of DNA Ligase?", a: "Joins Okazaki fragments <i>(catalyzes formation of phosphodiester bond)</i>." },
            { q: "Enzyme that elongates telomeres?", a: "Telomerase <i>(a reverse transcriptase)</i>." },
            { q: "Direction of protein synthesis (translation)?", a: "N-terminus to C-terminus." },
            { q: "Function of Chaperone proteins?", a: "Assist in proper folding of polypeptide chains." },
            { q: "Deficiency in I-cell disease?", a: "Phosphotransferase <i>(failure to Golgi-tag proteins with Mannose-6-Phosphate)</i>." },
            { q: "Rate-limiting enzyme of Glycolysis?", a: "<b>Rate-limiting</b> <b>enzyme</b> of Glycolysis? <b>\u2192</b> PFK-1 <i>(Phosphofructokinase-1)</i>." },
            { q: "Net yield of ATP and NADH from one glucose in glycolysis?", a: "2 ATP and 2 NADH." },
            { q: "Rate-limiting enzyme of Gluconeogenesis?", a: "<b>Rate-limiting</b> <b>enzyme</b> of Gluconeogenesis? <b>\u2192</b> Fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase." },
            { q: "Enzyme deficient in Essential Fructosuria?", a: "<b>enzyme</b> <b>deficient</b> in Essential Fructosuria? <b>\u2192</b> Fructokinase." },
            { q: "Enzyme deficient in Classic Galactosemia?", a: "<b>enzyme</b> <b>deficient</b> in Classic Galactosemia? <b>\u2192</b> Galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase <i>(GALT)</i>." },
            { q: "Rate-limiting enzyme of the Citric Acid (TCA) Cycle?", a: "<b>Rate-limiting</b> <b>enzyme</b> of the Citric Acid <i>(TCA)</i> Cycle? <b>\u2192</b> Isocitrate dehydrogenase." },
            { q: "Which TCA cycle enzyme requires B1, B2, B3, B5, and Lipoic acid?", a: "\u03b1-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase." },
            { q: "Rate-limiting enzyme of the Pentose Phosphate Pathway?", a: "<b>Rate-limiting</b> <b>enzyme</b> of the Pentose Phosphate Pathway? <b>\u2192</b> Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase <i>(G6PD)</i>." },
            { q: "Major product of the HMP shunt used for reductive biosynthesis?", a: "NADPH." },
            { q: "Rate-limiting enzyme of Glycogenolysis?", a: "<b>Rate-limiting</b> <b>enzyme</b> of Glycogenolysis? <b>\u2192</b> Glycogen phosphorylase." },
            { q: "Rate-limiting enzyme of Fatty Acid Synthesis?", a: "<b>Rate-limiting</b> <b>enzyme</b> of Fatty Acid Synthesis? <b>\u2192</b> Acetyl-CoA carboxylase <i>(ACC)</i>." },
            { q: "Rate-limiting enzyme of Fatty Acid Oxidation (\u03b2-oxidation)?", a: "<b>Rate-limiting</b> <b>enzyme</b> of Fatty Acid Oxidation <i>(\u03b2-oxidation)</i>? <b>\u2192</b> Carnitine acyltransferase I." },
            { q: "Systemic primary carnitine deficiency prevents transport of what?", a: "Systemic primary carnitine <b>deficiency</b> prevents transport of what? <b>\u2192</b> Long-chain fatty acids into the mitochondria." },
            { q: "Rate-limiting enzyme of Cholesterol Synthesis?", a: "<b>Rate-limiting</b> <b>enzyme</b> of Cholesterol Synthesis? <b>\u2192</b> HMG-CoA reductase." },
            { q: "Function of Apo B-100?", a: "Binds LDL receptor <i>(found on VLDL, IDL, LDL)</i>." },
            { q: "Function of Apo A-I?", a: "Activates LCAT <i>(found on HDL)</i>." },
            { q: "Deficiency in Familial Hypercholesterolemia (Type II)?", a: "<b>deficiency</b> in Familial Hypercholesterolemia <i>(Type II)</i>? <b>\u2192</b> Absent or defective LDL receptors." },
            { q: "Findings in Abetalipoproteinemia?", a: "Absent Chylomicrons, VLDL, LDL; Acanthocytes <i>(spur cells)</i>." },
            { q: "Rate-limiting enzyme of the Urea Cycle?", a: "<b>Rate-limiting</b> <b>enzyme</b> of the Urea Cycle? <b>\u2192</b> Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I." },
            { q: "Most common Urea Cycle disorder?", a: "<b>most common</b> Urea Cycle disorder? <b>\u2192</b> Ornithine transcarbamylase <i>(OTC)</i> <b>deficiency</b>." },
            { q: "Amino acid precursor to Serotonin and Niacin?", a: "Amino acid <b>precursor</b> to Serotonin and Niacin? <b>\u2192</b> Tryptophan." },
            { q: "Amino acid precursor to Tyrosine, Dopamine, and Melanin?", a: "Amino acid <b>precursor</b> to Tyrosine, Dopamine, and Melanin? <b>\u2192</b> Phenylalanine." },
            { q: "Enzyme deficient in Phenylketonuria (PKU)?", a: "<b>enzyme</b> <b>deficient</b> in Phenylketonuria <i>(PKU)</i>? <b>\u2192</b> Phenylalanine hydroxylase <i>(or BH4 cofactor)</i>." },
            { q: "\"Mousy\" or \"musty\" body odor is characteristic of?", a: "\"Mousy\" or \"musty\" body odor is <b>characteristic</b> of? <b>\u2192</b> Phenylketonuria <i>(PKU)</i>." },
            { q: "Enzyme deficient in Alkaptonuria?", a: "<b>enzyme</b> <b>deficient</b> in Alkaptonuria? <b>\u2192</b> Homogentisate oxidase." },
            { q: "Findings in Maple Syrup Urine Disease?", a: "Branched-chain \u03b1-ketoacid dehydrogenase <b>deficiency</b> <i>(Isoleucine, Leucine, Valine)</i>." },
            { q: "Vitamin required for pyruvate dehydrogenase activity?", a: "B1 <i>(Thiamine)</i>." },
            { q: "Vitamin deficiency causing Pellagra (Dermatitis, Dementia, Diarrhea)?", a: "Vitamin <b>deficiency</b> causing Pellagra <i>(Dermatitis, Dementia, Diarrhea)</i>? <b>\u2192</b> B3 <i>(Niacin)</i>." },
            { q: "Vitamin needed for hydroxylation of Proline in collagen synthesis?", a: "Vitamin C <i>(Ascorbic acid)</i>." },
            { q: "Most common vitamin deficiency in the US (especially in alcoholics)?", a: "<b>most common</b> vitamin <b>deficiency</b> in the US <i>(especially in alcoholics)</i>? <b>\u2192</b> Folate <i>(B9)</i>." },
            { q: "Neurological symptoms + Megaloblastic anemia suggest which deficiency?", a: "Neurological symptoms + Megaloblastic anemia suggest which <b>deficiency</b>? <b>\u2192</b> Vitamin B12 <i>(Cobalamin)</i>." },
            { q: "Vitamin that is a constituent of CoA?", a: "B5 <i>(Pantothenic acid)</i>." },
            { q: "Vitamin used to treat Cystic Acne?", a: "Vitamin A <i>(Retinoids)</i>." },
            { q: "Findings in Scurvy?", a: "Swollen gums, bruising, petechiae, poor wound healing <i>(collagen defect)</i>." },
            { q: "Rate-limiting enzyme of Heme synthesis?", a: "<b>Rate-limiting</b> <b>enzyme</b> of Heme synthesis? <b>\u2192</b> ALA synthase <i>(requires B6)</i>." },
            { q: "Enzyme inhibited by Lead in lead poisoning?", a: "<b>enzyme</b> inhibited by Lead in lead poisoning? <b>\u2192</b> Ferrochelatase and ALA dehydratase." },
            { q: "Condition caused by deficiency of Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase?", a: "Condition caused by <b>deficiency</b> of Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase? <b>\u2192</b> Porphyria cutanea tarda <i>(blistering skin photosensitivity)</i>." },
            { q: "Rate-limiting enzyme of De Novo Pyrimidine synthesis?", a: "<b>Rate-limiting</b> <b>enzyme</b> of De Novo Pyrimidine synthesis? <b>\u2192</b> Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase II." },
            { q: "Effect of Methotrexate on the nucleotide cycle?", a: "<b>Inhibits</b> dihydrofolate reductase <i>(decreases <i>dTMP</i>)</i>." },
            { q: "Second messenger used by Glucagon?", a: "cAMP." },
            { q: "Second messenger used by Insulin?", a: "Receptor tyrosine kinase <i>(MAP kinase pathway)</i>." },
            { q: "Net ATP yield from the Malate-Aspartate shuttle?", a: "32 ATP." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Cyanide poisoning?", a: "<b>Mechanism</b> of Cyanide poisoning? <b>\u2192</b> <b>Inhibits</b> Complex IV <i>(Cytochrome c oxidase)</i> in the ETC." },
            { q: "Effect of 2,4-Dinitrophenol (DNP) on the ETC?", a: "Uncoupling agent <i>(increases heat production, decreases ATP synthesis)</i>." },
            { q: "Enzyme deficient in Tay-Sachs disease?", a: "<b>enzyme</b> <b>deficient</b> in Tay-Sachs disease? <b>\u2192</b> Hexosaminidase A <i>(accumulates GM2 ganglioside)</i>." },
            { q: "Finding in Gaucher disease histology?", a: "\"Crinkled tissue paper\" macrophages." },
            { q: "Enzyme deficient in Niemann-Pick disease?", a: "<b>enzyme</b> <b>deficient</b> in Niemann-Pick disease? <b>\u2192</b> Sphingomyelinase <i>(\"No man picks his nose with his finger\")</i>." },
            { q: "Enzyme deficient in Von Gierke disease (GSD I)?", a: "<b>enzyme</b> <b>deficient</b> in Von Gierke disease <i>(GSD I)</i>? <b>\u2192</b> Glucose-6-phosphatase." },
            { q: "Key finding in McArdle disease (GSD V)?", a: "Muscle cramps and myoglobinuria with exercise; flat lactate curve." },
            { q: "Enzyme deficient in Pompe disease (GSD II)?", a: "<b>enzyme</b> <b>deficient</b> in Pompe disease <i>(GSD II)</i>? <b>\u2192</b> Lysosomal acid \u03b1-1,4-glucosidase." },
            { q: "Defect in Osteogenesis Imperfecta?", a: "Type I collagen synthesis." },
            { q: "Defect in Ehlers-Danlos Syndrome (Classic)?", a: "Type V collagen." },
            { q: "Defect in Menkes Disease?", a: "ATP7A <i>(impaired copper absorption/transport)</i>; affects Lysyl Oxidase." },
            { q: "Diagnosis for \"blue sclerae\" and multiple fractures?", a: "Osteogenesis Imperfecta." },
            { q: "Cofactor for all transamination reactions?", a: "B6 <i>(Pyridoxine)</i>." },
            { q: "Function of COMT and MAO?", a: "Degradation of catecholamines <i>(Epi, Norepi, Dopamine)</i>." },
            { q: "Rate-limiting enzyme of Ketogenesis?", a: "<b>Rate-limiting</b> <b>enzyme</b> of Ketogenesis? <b>\u2192</b> HMG-CoA synthase." },
            { q: "Molecule that inhibits the Carnitine shuttle?", a: "Malonyl-CoA." },
            { q: "Primary activator of PFK-1?", a: "Fructose-2,6-bisphosphate." },
            { q: "Enzyme that converts Ethanol to Acetaldehyde?", a: "Alcohol dehydrogenase." },
            { q: "Cofactor required for both Alcohol and Acetaldehyde dehydrogenase?", a: "NAD\u207a." },
            { q: "Metabolic state induced by a high NADH/NAD+ ratio in alcoholics?", a: "Lactic acidosis, fasting hypoglycemia, and ketoacidosis." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Disulfiram?", a: "<b>Mechanism</b> of Disulfiram? <b>\u2192</b> <b>Inhibits</b> acetaldehyde dehydrogenase <i>(causes acetaldehyde accumulation)</i>." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Fomepizole?", a: "<b>Mechanism</b> of Fomepizole? <b>\u2192</b> <b>Inhibits</b> alcohol dehydrogenase <i>(<b>antidote</b> for methanol/ethylene glycol)</i>." },
            { q: "End product of purine catabolism in humans?", a: "Uric acid." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Allopurinol?", a: "<b>Mechanism</b> of Allopurinol? <b>\u2192</b> <b>Inhibits</b> Xanthine oxidase <i>(decreases uric acid production)</i>." },
            { q: "Enzyme deficient in Adenosine Deaminase (ADA) deficiency?", a: "<b>enzyme</b> <b>deficient</b> in Adenosine Deaminase <i>(ADA)</i> <b>deficiency</b>? <b>\u2192</b> Adenosine deaminase <i>(causes SCID due to <i>dATP</i> toxicity in lymphocytes)</i>." },
            { q: "Primary fuel source for the brain during the first 3 days of starvation?", a: "Glucose <i>(from hepatic gluconeogenesis)</i>." },
            { q: "Primary fuel source for the brain after 3+ days of starvation?", a: "Ketone bodies <i>(acetoacetate and \u03b2-hydroxybutyrate)</i>." },
            { q: "Organ that lacks the enzyme Thiophorase (cannot use ketone bodies)?", a: "The Liver." },
            { q: "Major carrier of nitrogen from muscle to liver during exercise?", a: "Alanine <i>(Cahill cycle)</i>." },
            { q: "Major carrier of nitrogen from most tissues to the liver/kidneys?", a: "Glutamine." },
            { q: "Organelle responsible for \u03b2-oxidation of very-long-chain fatty acids (VLCFA)?", a: "Peroxisomes." },
            { q: "Defect in Zellweger Syndrome?", a: "Peroxisome biogenesis <i>(leads to accumulation of VLCFA)</i>." },
            { q: "Function of the Rough Endoplasmic Reticulum (RER)?", a: "Synthesis of exported proteins and N-linked oligosaccharide addition." },
            { q: "Function of the Smooth Endoplasmic Reticulum (SER)?", a: "Steroid synthesis and detoxification of drugs/toxins." },
            { q: "Which cell type is rich in SER?", a: "Hepatocytes and steroid-secreting cells <i>(Adrenal cortex, Gonads)</i>." },
            { q: "Protein that facilitates retrograde transport (Post-Golgi to ER)?", a: "COPI." },
            { q: "Protein that facilitates anterograde transport (ER to Golgi)?", a: "COPII." },
            { q: "Only enzyme of the TCA cycle located in the inner mitochondrial membrane?", a: "Succinate dehydrogenase <i>(Complex II)</i>." },
            { q: "High-energy bond formed in the TCA cycle via substrate-level phosphorylation?", a: "GTP <i>(produced by Succinyl-CoA synthetase)</i>." },
            { q: "Inhibitor of ATP Synthase (Complex V)?", a: "Oligomycin." },
            { q: "Complex of the ETC that does NOT pump protons (H+)?", a: "Complex II <i>(Succinate dehydrogenase)</i>." },
            { q: "\"Heinz bodies\" and \"Bite cells\" are seen in?", a: "G6PD <b>deficiency</b> <i>(oxidative stress)</i>." },
            { q: "Deficiency leading to \"Hypersegmented neutrophils\"?", a: "Vitamin B12 or Folate <b>deficiency</b> <i>(Megaloblastic anemia)</i>." },
            { q: "Biochemical defect in Albinism?", a: "Tyrosinase <b>deficiency</b> <i>(cannot convert Tyrosine to Melanin)</i>." },
            { q: "Characteristic finding in Cystinuria?", a: "Hexagonal cystine crystals in urine <i>(\"COLA\" stones: Cystine, Ornithine, Lysine, Arginine)</i>." },
            { q: "Defect in Hartnup Disease?", a: "<b>deficiency</b> of neutral amino acid transporters <i>(leads to Pellagra-like symptoms)</i>." },
            { q: "Molecule that serves as the primary methyl donor in most metabolic reactions?", a: "SAM <i>(S-adenosylmethionine)</i>." }
          ]
        },
        {
          topic: 'Biochemistry (Metabolism + Genetics)',
          icon: 'dna',
          cards: [
            { q: "First Law of Thermodynamics in biochemistry?", a: "Energy cannot be created or destroyed; \u0394G = \u0394H \u2212 T\u0394S; spontaneous reactions: \u0394G < 0 <i>(exergonic)</i>; non-spontaneous: \u0394G > 0 <i>(endergonic)</i>" },
            { q: "ATP \u2014 high-energy bond and hydrolysis \u0394G?", a: "Phosphoanhydride bonds <i>(between phosphates)</i>; \u0394G = \u22127.3 kcal/mol per bond; ATP <b>\u2192</b> ADP + Pi; energy coupling drives endergonic reactions" },
            { q: "Substrate-level phosphorylation vs. oxidative phosphorylation?", a: "Substrate-level: direct transfer of phosphate from substrate to ADP <i>(glycolysis: PGK + pyruvate kinase; TCA: succinyl-CoA synthetase)</i> | Oxidative: ATP synthase driven by proton gradient <i>(ETC)</i>; produces most ATP" },
            { q: "Michaelis-Menten equation and Km significance?", a: "v = Vmax[S] / <i>(Km + [S])</i>; Km = [S] at \u00bd Vmax; low Km = high affinity; high Km = low affinity; Km does not change with competitive inhibition <i>(Vmax unchanged)</i>; Km \u2191 with competitive inhibitor" },
            { q: "Competitive vs. noncompetitive inhibition \u2014 effect on Km and Vmax?", a: "Competitive: \u2191 Km, Vmax unchanged <i>(overcome by \u2191 substrate)</i> | Noncompetitive: Km unchanged, \u2193 Vmax <i>(cannot overcome)</i> | Uncompetitive: \u2193 Km, \u2193 Vmax <i>(both decrease proportionally)</i>" },
            { q: "Allosteric enzymes \u2014 characteristics?", a: "Sigmoid kinetics <i>(cooperative binding)</i>; not obeying Michaelis-Menten; have regulatory subunits + catalytic subunits; activators <b>\u2192</b> T<b>\u2192</b>R state shift <i>(\u2191 affinity)</i>; inhibitors <b>\u2192</b> R<b>\u2192</b>T shift; not plotted on standard Lineweaver-Burk" },
            { q: "Rate-limiting enzyme \u2014 definition?", a: "Slowest step in a pathway; most regulated <i>(allosteric, covalent modification, hormonal)</i>; determines overall flux; usually irreversible; examples: PFK-1 <i>(glycolysis)</i>, pyruvate dehydrogenase <i>(pyruvate <b>\u2192</b> acetyl-CoA)</i>, HMG-CoA reductase <i>(cholesterol <b>synthesis</b>)</i>" },
            { q: "Covalent modification of enzymes \u2014 examples?", a: "Phosphorylation <i>(kinases: PKA, PKC; phosphatases remove)</i>; glycosylation; acetylation; ubiquitination <i>(proteasomal <b>degradation</b>)</i>; proteolytic cleavage <i>(zymogens <b>\u2192</b> active enzymes: trypsinogen <b>\u2192</b> trypsin)</i>" },
            { q: "Cofactors vs. coenzymes \u2014 sources?", a: "Cofactors: inorganic ions <i>(Zn\u00b2\u207a, Fe\u00b2\u207a, Mg\u00b2\u207a, Cu\u00b2\u207a)</i> | Coenzymes: organic molecules, mostly B vitamins; NAD\u207a/NADH <i>(B3/niacin)</i>, FAD/FADH\u2082 <i>(B2/riboflavin)</i>, CoA <i>(B5/pantothenate)</i>, TPP <i>(B1/thiamine)</i>, PLP <i>(B6/pyridoxine)</i>, biotin <i>(B7)</i>, folate <i>(B9)</i>, B12" },
            { q: "Glycolysis \u2014 net products from 1 glucose?", a: "2 pyruvate + 2 ATP <i>(net)</i> + 2 NADH + 2 H\u2082O; occurs in cytoplasm; anaerobic; 10 steps" },
            { q: "Irreversible steps of glycolysis (regulated enzymes)?", a: "<i>(1)</i> Hexokinase/Glucokinase <i>(glucose <b>\u2192</b> G6P)</i> | <i>(2)</i> PFK-1 \u2014 <b>Rate-limiting</b> <i>(F6P <b>\u2192</b> F1,6BP)</i> | <i>(3)</i> Pyruvate kinase <i>(PEP <b>\u2192</b> pyruvate)</i>" },
            { q: "PFK-1 \u2014 allosteric activators and inhibitors?", a: "<b>Activated</b> by: AMP, ADP, F2,6BP <i>(most potent activator \u2014 insulin \u2191 via PFK-2)</i>, Pi | <b>Inhibited</b> by: ATP, citrate, H\u207a <i>(acidosis)</i>, glucagon <i>(\u2193 F2,6BP)</i>" },
            { q: "Hexokinase vs. Glucokinase \u2014 key differences?", a: "Hexokinase: all tissues, low Km <i>(high affinity)</i>, low capacity, <b>Inhibited</b> by G6P, not induced by insulin | Glucokinase <i>(GK)</i>: liver + \u03b2 cells, high Km <i>(low affinity, acts only when glucose high)</i>, high capacity, not <b>Inhibited</b> by G6P, induced by insulin \u2014 glucose sensor" },
            { q: "Pyruvate kinase \u2014 regulation?", a: "<b>Activated</b> by F1,6BP <i>(feedforward)</i> | <b>Inhibited</b> by ATP, acetyl-CoA, alanine, glucagon <i>(phosphorylation <b>\u2192</b> inactive)</i>; <b>deficiency</b> <b>\u2192</b> hemolytic anemia <i>(RBCs rely on glycolysis only)</i>" },
            { q: "Fate of pyruvate under aerobic vs. anaerobic conditions?", a: "Aerobic: pyruvate <b>\u2192</b> acetyl-CoA <i>(pyruvate dehydrogenase, mitochondria)</i> | Anaerobic: pyruvate <b>\u2192</b> lactate <i>(lactate dehydrogenase, cytoplasm; regenerates NAD\u207a)</i>" },
            { q: "Pyruvate Dehydrogenase Complex (PDC) \u2014 cofactors (mnemonic)?", a: "The Liking Couple Finds Adventure Nightly: Thiamine <i>(B1/TPP)</i>, Lipoic acid, CoA <i>(B5)</i>, FAD <i>(B2)</i>, NAD\u207a <i>(B3)</i>; converts pyruvate <b>\u2192</b> acetyl-CoA + CO\u2082 + NADH; irreversible; in mitochondrial matrix" },
            { q: "PDC regulation \u2014 activators and inhibitors?", a: "<b>Activated</b> by: \u2191 ADP, \u2191 NAD\u207a, \u2191 CoA, \u2191 Ca\u00b2\u207a <i>(exercise)</i>, insulin <i>(<b>Activates</b> phosphatase <b>\u2192</b> dephosphorylates <b>\u2192</b> active PDC)</i> | <b>Inhibited</b> by: \u2191 ATP, \u2191 NADH, \u2191 acetyl-CoA, \u2191 fatty acids; PDC kinase phosphorylates <b>\u2192</b> inactive" },
            { q: "PDC deficiency \u2014 presentation?", a: "\u2191 Pyruvate + \u2191 lactate + \u2191 alanine <i>(pyruvate + glutamate <b>\u2192</b> alanine via ALT)</i>; lactic acidosis; neurodegeneration; treat with ketogenic diet <i>(bypasses PDC)</i>; <b>X-linked</b> <i>(PDHA1 gene, E1\u03b1 subunit)</i>" },
            { q: "TCA cycle \u2014 net products per acetyl-CoA?", a: "3 NADH + 1 FADH\u2082 + 1 GTP + 2 CO\u2082; occurs in mitochondrial matrix; 8 steps; <b>Rate-limiting</b> step = isocitrate dehydrogenase" },
            { q: "TCA cycle \u2014 regulated enzymes (3 irreversible)?", a: "<i>(1)</i> Citrate synthase <i>(oxaloacetate + acetyl-CoA <b>\u2192</b> citrate)</i> | <i>(2)</i> Isocitrate dehydrogenase <i>(<b>Rate-limiting</b>; isocitrate <b>\u2192</b> \u03b1-KG; <b>Activated</b> by ADP/Ca\u00b2\u207a, <b>Inhibited</b> by NADH/ATP)</i> | <i>(3)</i> \u03b1-KG dehydrogenase <i>(\u03b1-KG <b>\u2192</b> succinyl-CoA; same cofactors as PDC)</i>" },
            { q: "TCA anaplerotic reactions \u2014 purpose?", a: "Replenish TCA intermediates; pyruvate <b>\u2192</b> oxaloacetate <i>(pyruvate carboxylase, biotin)</i>; propionyl-CoA <b>\u2192</b> succinyl-CoA; amino acids <b>\u2192</b> \u03b1-KG, succinyl-CoA, fumarate, oxaloacetate; essential when intermediates are siphoned off for <b>synthesis</b>" },
            { q: "Electron Transport Chain \u2014 complexes and their actions?", a: "Complex I <i>(NADH dehydrogenase)</i>: NADH <b>\u2192</b> NAD\u207a, pumps 4H\u207a | Complex II <i>(succinate dehydrogenase)</i>: FADH\u2082 <b>\u2192</b> FAD, does NOT pump H\u207a | Complex III <i>(cytochrome bc1)</i>: pumps 4H\u207a | Complex IV <i>(cytochrome c oxidase)</i>: O\u2082 <b>\u2192</b> H\u2082O, pumps 2H\u207a; electrons flow: I/II <b>\u2192</b> ubiquinone <i>(CoQ)</i> <b>\u2192</b> III <b>\u2192</b> cytochrome c <b>\u2192</b> IV" },
            { q: "ATP yield from complete oxidation of 1 glucose?", a: "~30\u201332 ATP; 2 ATP <i>(glycolysis substrate-level)</i> + 2 GTP <i>(TCA)</i> + 2.5 ATP/NADH \u00d7 10 NADH = 25 + 1.5 ATP/FADH\u2082 \u00d7 2 FADH\u2082 = 3; total ~32 ATP <i>(modern estimate vs. old 36\u201338)</i>" },
            { q: "ETC inhibitors \u2014 site of action?", a: "Rotenone/Amytal: Complex I | Antimycin A: Complex III | Cyanide/CO/Azide: Complex IV <i>(binds Fe\u00b3\u207a of cytochrome a3)</i> | Oligomycin: ATP synthase <i>(Complex V)</i>" },
            { q: "Uncouplers of oxidative phosphorylation?", a: "Dissipate proton gradient as heat without generating ATP; 2,4-DNP <i>(dinitrophenol)</i> \u2014 industrial poison; aspirin overdose <i>(salicylate)</i>; thermogenin <i>(UCP1 in brown adipose tissue \u2014 physiologic, generates heat in newborns/hibernators)</i>; \u2191 O\u2082 consumption + \u2191 heat + \u2193 ATP" },
            { q: "Gluconeogenesis \u2014 substrates and location?", a: "Substrates: lactate <i>(Cori cycle)</i>, alanine <i>(glucose-alanine cycle)</i>, glycerol <i>(from lipolysis)</i>, propionyl-CoA <i>(odd-chain FA)</i>, glucogenic amino acids; primarily liver <i>(90%)</i> + kidney <i>(10%)</i>; stimulated by glucagon + cortisol; <b>Inhibited</b> by insulin" },
            { q: "Irreversible steps of gluconeogenesis (bypasses glycolysis)?", a: "<i>(1)</i> Pyruvate <b>\u2192</b> OAA <i>(pyruvate carboxylase, biotin, mitochondria)</i> <b>\u2192</b> OAA <b>\u2192</b> PEP <i>(PEPCK, GTP, cytoplasm)</i> | <i>(2)</i> F1,6BP <b>\u2192</b> F6P <i>(FBPase-1)</i> | <i>(3)</i> G6P <b>\u2192</b> glucose <i>(glucose-6-phosphatase, ER, liver/kidney only)</i>" },
            { q: "Cori cycle \u2014 purpose?", a: "Lactate <i>(from anaerobic glycolysis in RBCs, muscle)</i> <b>\u2192</b> liver <b>\u2192</b> gluconeogenesis <b>\u2192</b> glucose <b>\u2192</b> back to muscle/RBCs; costs 6 ATP net <i>(liver uses oxidative phosphorylation to drive gluconeogenesis)</i>; transfers metabolic burden from muscle to liver" },
            { q: "Glucose-alanine cycle \u2014 purpose?", a: "Muscle: pyruvate + glutamate <b>\u2192</b> alanine <i>(ALT)</i> <b>\u2192</b> blood <b>\u2192</b> liver; liver: alanine <b>\u2192</b> pyruvate + glutamate <i>(ALT)</i> <b>\u2192</b> gluconeogenesis + urea cycle; non-toxic nitrogen transport from muscle to liver" },
            { q: "Glycogen synthesis \u2014 key enzyme and cofactor?", a: "Glycogen synthase <i>(<b>Rate-limiting</b>; adds glucose via \u03b1-1,4 bonds)</i>; UDP-glucose is <b>Activated</b> <b>precursor</b>; glycogenin = primer protein; branching <b>enzyme</b> creates \u03b1-1,6 branches <i>(every 8\u201312 residues)</i>; <b>Activated</b> by insulin + glucose-6-phosphate; <b>Inhibited</b> by glucagon/epinephrine <i>(via phosphorylation <b>\u2192</b> inactive)</i>" },
            { q: "Glycogenolysis \u2014 key enzymes?", a: "Glycogen phosphorylase <i>(<b>Rate-limiting</b>; cleaves \u03b1-1,4 bonds <b>\u2192</b> G1P; <b>Activated</b> by glucagon/epinephrine via PKA phosphorylation, AMP, Ca\u00b2\u207a; <b>Inhibited</b> by insulin, glucose, ATP)</i> + Debranching <b>enzyme</b> <i>(\u03b1-1,6 glucosidase \u2014 cleaves branch points <b>\u2192</b> free glucose)</i>" },
            { q: "Glycogen storage diseases \u2014 most important examples?", a: "Type I <i>(Von Gierke)</i>: G6Pase <b>deficiency</b> <b>\u2192</b> \u2191 glycogen in liver/kidney; severe hypoglycemia, lactic acidosis, hyperuricemia, hyperlipidemia | Type II <i>(Pompe)</i>: \u03b1-1,4-glucosidase <i>(acid maltase)</i> <b>deficiency</b> <b>\u2192</b> lysosomal glycogen; cardiomegaly, hypotonia; \"Pompe's trashes the Pump <i>(heart)</i>\" | Type V <i>(McArdle)</i>: muscle glycogen phosphorylase; exercise intolerance, myoglobinuria, no \u2191 lactate with exercise" },
            { q: "Pentose Phosphate Pathway (PPP) \u2014 products and significance?", a: "NADPH + ribose-5-phosphate; occurs in cytoplasm; no ATP produced; NADPH used for: glutathione reduction <i>(\u2191 in RBCs)</i>, fatty acid <b>synthesis</b>, cholesterol <b>synthesis</b>, cytochrome P450; ribose-5-P used for nucleotide <b>synthesis</b>; important in RBCs, liver, adrenal, mammary gland" },
            { q: "G6PD deficiency \u2014 pathophysiology and triggers?", a: "<b>X-linked</b>; \u2193 PPP <b>\u2192</b> \u2193 NADPH <b>\u2192</b> \u2193 reduced glutathione <b>\u2192</b> RBCs susceptible to oxidative stress <b>\u2192</b> hemolytic anemia; triggers: primaquine, dapsone, fava beans, sulfonamides, infections; Heinz bodies <i>(denatured Hb)</i> + bite cells on smear; <b>most common</b> enzymopathy worldwide" },
            { q: "Fructose metabolism \u2014 key enzymes and deficiency diseases?", a: "Fructokinase: fructose <b>\u2192</b> F1P <i>(liver, kidney, intestine)</i>; <b>deficiency</b> = essential fructuria <i>(benign, fructose in urine)</i> | Aldolase B: F1P <b>\u2192</b> DHAP + glyceraldehyde; <b>deficiency</b> = hereditary fructose intolerance <i>(HFI)</i>: F1P accumulates <b>\u2192</b> <b>Inhibits</b> glycogenolysis + gluconeogenesis <b>\u2192</b> severe hypoglycemia + liver/kidney damage; treat by eliminating fructose/sucrose/sorbitol" },
            { q: "Galactose metabolism \u2014 key enzymes and deficiency diseases?", a: "Galactokinase: galactose <b>\u2192</b> Gal-1P; <b>deficiency</b> = galactosemia type II <i>(mild: galactitol accumulates <b>\u2192</b> cataracts)</i> | Gal-1P uridyltransferase: Gal-1P + UDP-glucose <b>\u2192</b> UDP-galactose + G1P; <b>deficiency</b> = classic galactosemia <i>(severe: Gal-1P accumulates <b>\u2192</b> jaundice, liver failure, E. coli sepsis, intellectual disability, cataracts, ovarian failure)</i>; treat with eliminating galactose/lactose" },
            { q: "Sorbitol pathway \u2014 significance?", a: "Glucose <b>\u2192</b> sorbitol <i>(aldose reductase, NADPH)</i> <b>\u2192</b> fructose <i>(sorbitol dehydrogenase, NAD\u207a)</i>; in hyperglycemia: excess sorbitol accumulates <i>(cell impermeable)</i> <b>\u2192</b> osmotic damage <b>\u2192</b> cataracts, retinopathy, neuropathy, nephropathy in diabetes; lens, Schwann cells, kidneys have high aldose reductase" },
            { q: "Fatty acid synthesis \u2014 location, substrates, key enzyme?", a: "Cytoplasm <i>(cytosol)</i>; acetyl-CoA + NADPH <i>(from PPP)</i> + HCO\u2083\u207b; acetyl-CoA carboxylase <i>(ACC, <b>Rate-limiting</b>; biotin; <b>Activated</b> by citrate/insulin; <b>Inhibited</b> by palmitoyl-CoA/glucagon/epinephrine)</i>; fatty acid synthase <i>(FAS)</i> complex adds 2C units as malonyl-CoA; product = palmitate <i>(16C)</i>" },
            { q: "How does acetyl-CoA exit mitochondria for FA synthesis?", a: "Cannot cross inner mitochondrial membrane directly; OAA + acetyl-CoA <b>\u2192</b> citrate <i>(citrate synthase)</i> <b>\u2192</b> citrate transported out <b>\u2192</b> citrate lyase in cytoplasm regenerates acetyl-CoA + OAA; OAA <b>\u2192</b> malate <b>\u2192</b> pyruvate <i>(generates NADPH via malic <b>enzyme</b>)</i>" },
            { q: "\u03b2-oxidation of fatty acids \u2014 location and products per cycle?", a: "Mitochondrial matrix; each cycle: 1 acetyl-CoA + 1 NADH + 1 FADH\u2082; even-chain 16C palmitate <b>\u2192</b> 8 acetyl-CoA + 7 NADH + 7 FADH\u2082 <b>\u2192</b> ~106 ATP; odd-chain FAs <b>\u2192</b> propionyl-CoA <b>\u2192</b> methylmalonyl-CoA <b>\u2192</b> succinyl-CoA <i>(B12-dependent)</i>; very long chain FAs <b>\u2192</b> peroxisomal \u03b2-oxidation first" },
            { q: "Carnitine shuttle \u2014 function and clinical relevance?", a: "Long-chain FAs <i>(>12C)</i> require carnitine transport into mitochondria; carnitine acyltransferase I <i>(outer membrane, <b>Rate-limiting</b>, <b>Inhibited</b> by malonyl-CoA)</i> + carnitine acyltransferase II <i>(inner membrane)</i>; carnitine <b>deficiency</b> <b>\u2192</b> inability to oxidize LCFAs <b>\u2192</b> myopathy + hypoglycemia; primary carnitine <b>deficiency</b> <i>(SLC22A5 <b>mutation</b>)</i>" },
            { q: "Ketone body synthesis \u2014 where, from what, and products?", a: "Liver mitochondria; from acetyl-CoA <i>(excess during fasting/DKA)</i>; HMG-CoA synthase <i>(mitochondrial)</i> <b>\u2192</b> HMG-CoA <b>\u2192</b> acetoacetate <b>\u2192</b> \u03b2-hydroxybutyrate <i>(by \u03b2-hydroxybutyrate dehydrogenase, NADH)</i> + acetone <i>(spontaneous)</i>; liver cannot use ketones <i>(no succinyl-CoA transferase/thiophorase)</i>" },
            { q: "Ketone body utilization \u2014 tissues and enzyme?", a: "Brain <i>(after 3+ days fasting)</i>, heart, kidney, skeletal muscle; \u03b2-hydroxybutyrate <b>\u2192</b> acetoacetate <b>\u2192</b> acetoacetyl-CoA <i>(via succinyl-CoA transferase/thiophorase, absent in liver)</i> <b>\u2192</b> 2 acetyl-CoA <b>\u2192</b> TCA; preferred fuel in prolonged fasting" },
            { q: "Malonyl-CoA \u2014 significance in fat metabolism?", a: "Product of ACC <i>(ACC adds CO\u2082 to acetyl-CoA)</i>; substrate for FA <b>synthesis</b>; <b>Inhibits</b> carnitine acyltransferase I <b>\u2192</b> prevents FA entry into mitochondria <i>(prevents futile cycle of <b>synthesis</b> + simultaneous oxidation)</i>; \u2191 in fed state <i>(insulin <b>\u2192</b> \u2191 ACC)</i> <b>\u2192</b> \u2193 \u03b2-oxidation" },
            { q: "Lipoprotein lipase (LPL) vs. Hormone-sensitive lipase (HSL)?", a: "LPL: endothelial surface of capillaries; cleaves TG in chylomicrons + VLDL <b>\u2192</b> FFA + glycerol <b>\u2192</b> taken up by tissues; <b>Activated</b> by insulin + apo-CII; <b>Inhibited</b> by apo-CIII | HSL: adipocyte cytoplasm; cleaves stored TG; <b>Activated</b> by glucagon/epinephrine <i>(PKA phosphorylation)</i>; <b>Inhibited</b> by insulin" },
            { q: "Chylomicron \u2014 composition, synthesis site, function?", a: "Synthesized in intestinal epithelium; carries dietary <i>(exogenous)</i> TG + cholesterol; apo-B48 <i>(structural)</i>, apo-CII <i>(LPL activator)</i>, apo-E <i>(receptor-mediated uptake of remnants)</i>; TG delivered to peripheral tissues <b>\u2192</b> chylomicron remnant <b>\u2192</b> taken up by liver <i>(apo-E receptor)</i>" },
            { q: "VLDL, IDL, LDL, HDL \u2014 function summary?", a: "VLDL: liver <b>\u2192</b> carries endogenous TG to periphery; apo-B100 | IDL: VLDL remnant | LDL: IDL <b>\u2192</b> LDL by hepatic lipase; carries cholesterol to periphery; apo-B100; taken up by LDL receptor <i>(liver/peripheral)</i> | HDL: liver + intestine; reverse cholesterol transport; apo-A1; LCAT esterifies cholesterol" },
            { q: "Familial hypercholesterolemia \u2014 defect and findings?", a: "LDL receptor <b>mutation</b> <i>(autosomal dominant)</i>; \u2191\u2191 LDL; heterozygous: LDL 2\u00d7 normal, premature CAD; homozygous: LDL 5\u00d7 normal, CAD in childhood, tendon xanthomas <i>(Achilles)</i>, xanthelasma, corneal arcus; treat with statins <i>(\u2191 LDL-R expression)</i>" },
            { q: "Familial hypertriglyceridemia?", a: "\u2191 VLDL production; \u2191 TG; pancreatitis risk; eruptive xanthomas; \u2191 risk of CAD; treat with fibrates <i>(\u2191 LPL via PPAR\u03b1)</i>, niacin, omega-3 FA" },
            { q: "Abetalipoproteinemia \u2014 defect and findings?", a: "Defect in MTP <i>(microsomal triglyceride transfer protein)</i> <b>\u2192</b> cannot assemble apo-B-containing lipoproteins <i>(chylomicrons, VLDL)</i> <b>\u2192</b> \u2193 fat absorption; acanthocytes <i>(spiked RBCs)</i>, fat malabsorption, fat-soluble vitamin <b>deficiency</b> <i>(A, D, E, K)</i>, spinocerebellar ataxia, retinitis pigmentosa; treat with high-dose vitamins A + E" },
            { q: "Cholesterol synthesis \u2014 rate-limiting step and drug target?", a: "HMG-CoA <b>\u2192</b> mevalonate <i>(HMG-CoA reductase, <b>Rate-limiting</b>)</i>; statins inhibit HMG-CoA reductase; <b>Activated</b> by insulin; <b>Inhibited</b> by glucagon, cholesterol <i>(SREBP pathway)</i>, statins; occurs in cytoplasm/ER; liver is primary site" },
            { q: "Bile acid synthesis \u2014 primary vs. secondary?", a: "Primary: cholesterol <b>\u2192</b> cholic acid + chenodeoxycholic acid <i>(liver, <b>Rate-limiting</b>: 7\u03b1-hydroxylase)</i>; conjugated with glycine/taurine <b>\u2192</b> bile salts | Secondary: gut bacteria deconjugate <b>\u2192</b> deoxycholic acid + lithocholic acid; 95% enterohepatic circulation <i>(reabsorbed in terminal ileum)</i>" },
            { q: "Sphingolipid structure and synthesis?", a: "Sphingosine + fatty acid = ceramide <i>(backbone)</i>; ceramide + phosphocholine = sphingomyelin; ceramide + sugar = glycosphingolipid <i>(cerebrosides, gangliosides)</i>; <b>synthesis</b> begins with palmitoyl-CoA + serine <b>\u2192</b> sphinganine; all contain ceramide core" },
            { q: "Sphingolipidoses \u2014 key examples (enzyme deficiency \u2192 accumulated substrate)?", a: "Gaucher's: glucocerebrosidase <b>\u2192</b> glucocerebroside; Niemann-Pick: sphingomyelinase <b>\u2192</b> sphingomyelin; Tay-Sachs: Hex-A <i>(\u03b2-hexosaminidase A)</i> <b>\u2192</b> GM2 ganglioside; Fabry's: \u03b1-galactosidase A <b>\u2192</b> ceramide trihexoside; Krabbe: galactocerebrosidase <b>\u2192</b> galactocerebroside; Metachromatic leukodystrophy: arylsulfatase A <b>\u2192</b> cerebroside sulfate; all AR except Fabry's <i>(<b>X-linked</b>)</i>" },
            { q: "Gaucher disease \u2014 types and treatment?", a: "<b>most common</b> lysosomal storage disease; glucocerebrosidase <b>deficiency</b> <b>\u2192</b> glucocerebroside in macrophages; Type 1 <i>(<b>most common</b>, no CNS)</i>: Ashkenazi Jewish, hepatosplenomegaly, bone pain, \"Erlenmeyer flask\" deformity, pancytopenia; treat with <b>enzyme</b> replacement <i>(imiglucerase)</i> or substrate reduction <i>(miglustat)</i>" },
            { q: "Tay-Sachs vs. Niemann-Pick \u2014 key distinguishing features?", a: "Tay-Sachs: Hex-A <b>deficiency</b> <b>\u2192</b> GM2 ganglioside; cherry-red macula + progressive neurodegeneration; NO hepatosplenomegaly; \"onion skin\" lysosomes | Niemann-Pick: sphingomyelinase <b>\u2192</b> sphingomyelin; cherry-red macula + progressive neurodegeneration + HEPATOSPLENOMEGALY; \"foamy\" histiocytes; both AR, Ashkenazi Jewish" },
            { q: "Prostaglandin synthesis pathway?", a: "Membrane phospholipids <b>\u2192</b> arachidonic acid <i>(phospholipase A2, blocked by corticosteroids)</i> <b>\u2192</b> PGG2/PGH2 <i>(COX-1/COX-2, blocked by NSAIDs/aspirin)</i> <b>\u2192</b> prostaglandins + thromboxane + prostacyclin; lipoxygenase pathway <b>\u2192</b> leukotrienes <i>(blocked by zileuton)</i>" },
            { q: "Essential amino acids (mnemonic)?", a: "PVT TIM HaLL: Phenylalanine, Valine, Tryptophan, Threonine, Isoleucine, Methionine, Histidine, Leucine, Lysine; conditionally essential: arginine, glutamine, cysteine, tyrosine <i>(in certain conditions)</i>" },
            { q: "Transamination \u2014 key enzymes and cofactor?", a: "AST <i>(aspartate aminotransferase)</i> and ALT <i>(alanine aminotransferase)</i>; cofactor = PLP <i>(pyridoxal phosphate, B6)</i>; transfers amino group from amino acid to \u03b1-ketoglutarate <b>\u2192</b> glutamate + new \u03b1-keto acid; reversible; key in gluconeogenesis <i>(alanine <b>\u2192</b> pyruvate)</i> and urea cycle" },
            { q: "Urea cycle \u2014 location, purpose, rate-limiting step?", a: "Starts in mitochondria <i>(CPS-I, ornithine transcarbamylase)</i> + cytoplasm <i>(argininosuccinate synthetase, argininosuccinate lyase, arginase)</i>; disposes of NH\u2083 <i>(toxic)</i>; <b>Rate-limiting</b>: CPS-I <i>(carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I)</i>; requires N-acetylglutamate <i>(allosteric activator of CPS-I)</i>; net: 2 NH\u2083 + CO\u2082 <b>\u2192</b> urea + fumarate" },
            { q: "Urea cycle mnemonic?", a: "Ordinarily, Careless Crappers Are Also Frivolous About Urination: Ornithine <b>\u2192</b> Citrulline <i>(+ carbamoyl-P)</i> <b>\u2192</b> Argininosuccinate <i>(+ aspartate)</i> <b>\u2192</b> Arginine <i>(+ fumarate released)</i> <b>\u2192</b> Urea + Ornithine <i>(arginase)</i>" },
            { q: "Hyperammonemia \u2014 causes and symptoms?", a: "Urea cycle <b>enzyme</b> <b>deficiency</b> <i>(<b>most common</b>: OTC <b>deficiency</b>, <b>X-linked</b>)</i> | liver failure | organic acidemias; symptoms: encephalopathy, asterixis, cerebral edema, coma; \u2191 glutamine in CSF <i>(NH\u2083 + glutamate <b>\u2192</b> glutamine in brain, depletes \u03b1-KG <b>\u2192</b> \u2193 TCA)</i>; treat: \u2193 protein, sodium benzoate/phenylbutyrate <i>(alternative nitrogen excretion)</i>, lactulose <i>(liver failure)</i>, dialysis" },
            { q: "OTC (Ornithine Transcarbamylase) deficiency \u2014 key features?", a: "<b>most common</b> urea cycle defect; <b>X-linked</b> recessive; \u2191 orotic acid <i>(carbamoyl-P <b>\u2192</b> pyrimidines <b>\u2192</b> orotic acid accumulates)</i> + \u2191 NH\u2083 + \u2193 BUN; distinguishes from CPS-I <b>deficiency</b> <i>(no orotic aciduria)</i>; presents in neonates or stress in carriers" },
            { q: "Phenylketonuria (PKU) \u2014 enzyme, accumulated substance, findings?", a: "Phenylalanine hydroxylase <b>deficiency</b> <i>(or BH4 <b>deficiency</b>)</i>; Phe <b>\u2192</b> cannot convert to tyrosine; Phe <b>\u2192</b> phenylpyruvate, phenylacetate, phenyllactate; \u2193 tyrosine <i>(\u2193 melanin <b>\u2192</b> fair skin/hair/eyes)</i>; musty/mousy odor; intellectual disability, seizures, eczema; treat: \u2193 Phe diet + \u2191 tyrosine; maternal PKU <b>\u2192</b> teratogenic to fetus <i>(microcephaly, cardiac defects)</i>" },
            { q: "Alkaptonuria \u2014 enzyme and features?", a: "Homogentisate oxidase <b>deficiency</b>; tyrosine metabolism; homogentisic acid accumulates <b>\u2192</b> excreted in urine <i>(turns black on standing = ochronosis)</i>; dark urine, arthritis <i>(ochronotic arthropathy <b>\u2192</b> homogentisate deposits in cartilage/connective tissue)</i>, benign; AR" },
            { q: "Maple Syrup Urine Disease (MSUD) \u2014 enzyme and features?", a: "Branched-chain \u03b1-keto acid dehydrogenase <i>(BCKAD)</i> <b>deficiency</b>; cannot catabolize Leucine, Isoleucine, Valine <i>(BCAAs)</i>; sweet maple syrup urine odor; severe neurodegeneration, feeding difficulty, seizures in neonates; \u2191 BCAAs + \u2191 branched-chain keto acids; treat: restrict BCAAs; requires thiamine <i>(B1)</i> cofactor" },
            { q: "Homocystinuria \u2014 causes and features?", a: "Cystathionine synthase <b>deficiency</b> <i>(<b>most common</b>)</i> <b>\u2192</b> \u2191 homocysteine + \u2191 methionine; OR MTHFR <b>deficiency</b> / B12 or folate <b>deficiency</b> <b>\u2192</b> \u2191 homocysteine + \u2193 methionine; clinical: Marfanoid habitus, downward lens dislocation <i>(ectopia lentis \u2014 vs. Marfan: upward)</i>, intellectual disability, osteoporosis, \u2191 DVT/stroke risk; treat: \u2193 methionine + \u2191 cysteine + B6 <i>(if responsive)</i> + betaine + folate/B12" },
            { q: "Cystinuria \u2014 defect and features?", a: "Defective renal PCT transporter for COLA <i>(Cystine, Ornithine, Lysine, Arginine)</i>; AR; cystine stones <i>(hexagonal crystals)</i>; recurrent urolithiasis; treat with hydration, alkalization, D-penicillamine <i>(chelates cystine)</i>; not confused with cystinosis <i>(lysosomal cystine transport defect)</i>" },
            { q: "Hartnup disease \u2014 defect and features?", a: "Defective neutral amino acid transporter in intestine/renal PCT <i>(tryptophan \u2193 absorption)</i>; \u2193 tryptophan <b>\u2192</b> \u2193 niacin <i>(B3)</i> <b>\u2192</b> pellagra-like <i>(photosensitive rash, ataxia, psychiatric symptoms)</i>; treat with niacin supplementation; exacerbated by dietary protein <b>deficiency</b>" },
            { q: "Amino acids that are purely ketogenic?", a: "Leucine and Lysine <i>(LL = only ketogenic)</i>; cannot be used for gluconeogenesis; <b>\u2192</b> acetyl-CoA + acetoacetyl-CoA" },
            { q: "Amino acid carbon skeletons \u2192 TCA entry points?", a: "<b>\u2192</b> Pyruvate: Ala, Ser, Gly, Cys, Thr | <b>\u2192</b> Acetyl-CoA/Acetoacetyl-CoA: Leu, Lys, Phe, Trp, Tyr, Ile | <b>\u2192</b> \u03b1-KG: Glu, Gln, Pro, Arg, His | <b>\u2192</b> Succinyl-CoA: Val, Ile, Met, Thr | <b>\u2192</b> Fumarate: Phe, Tyr | <b>\u2192</b> OAA: Asp, Asn" },
            { q: "One-carbon metabolism \u2014 methyl donor and carriers?", a: "SAM <i>(S-adenosylmethionine)</i> = primary methyl donor <i>(methylates DNA, proteins, neurotransmitters, creatine, phosphatidylcholine)</i>; THF <i>(tetrahydrofolate, B9)</i> = carries one-carbon units <i>(as N5,N10-methylene-THF for dTMP synthesis)</i>; B12 = methyl-B12 transfers methyl from N5-methyl-THF to homocysteine <b>\u2192</b> methionine <i>(links folate and B12 cycles)</i>" },
            { q: "Folate trap (B12 deficiency)?", a: "B12 <b>deficiency</b> <b>\u2192</b> cannot convert N5-methyl-THF to THF <b>\u2192</b> folate trapped as N5-methyl-THF <b>\u2192</b> functional folate <b>deficiency</b> <b>\u2192</b> \u2193 dTMP <b>synthesis</b> <b>\u2192</b> megaloblastic anemia <i>(same as folate <b>deficiency</b>)</i>; B12 also needed for propionyl-CoA <b>\u2192</b> methylmalonyl-CoA <b>\u2192</b> succinyl-CoA <i>(methylmalonyl-CoA mutase)</i>; \u2191 methylmalonic acid = specific to B12 <b>deficiency</b> <i>(not folate)</i>" },
            { q: "Nitric oxide (NO) synthesis?", a: "Arginine <b>\u2192</b> NO + citrulline <i>(NO synthase, NOS; requires NADPH, O\u2082, BH4, FAD, FMN, calmodulin)</i>; eNOS <i>(endothelial)</i>: vasodilation <i>(\u2191 cGMP <b>\u2192</b> smooth muscle relaxation)</i>; nNOS <i>(neuronal)</i>: neurotransmission; iNOS <i>(inducible)</i>: macrophage cytotoxicity; <b>Inhibited</b> by L-NAME <i>(NOS inhibitor)</i>" },
            { q: "Creatine synthesis pathway?", a: "Arginine + glycine <b>\u2192</b> guanidinoacetate <i>(kidney)</i> <b>\u2192</b> SAM methylates <b>\u2192</b> creatine <i>(liver)</i> <b>\u2192</b> phosphocreatine in muscle <i>(creatine kinase)</i> <b>\u2192</b> phosphocreatine + ADP <b>\u2192</b> creatine + ATP <i>(rapid energy in muscle)</i>; creatinine = spontaneous, irreversible <b>degradation</b> of creatine phosphate; excreted by kidney <i>(marker of muscle mass + GFR)</i>" },
            { q: "Purine synthesis \u2014 de novo vs. salvage?", a: "De novo: builds purine ring on ribose-5-phosphate <i>(from PPP)</i>; requires glutamine, glycine, aspartate, THF, CO\u2082; <b>Rate-limiting</b> <b>enzyme</b>: PRPP amidotransferase <i>(glutamine PRPP <b>\u2192</b> 5-phosphoribosylamine)</i>; <b>Inhibited</b> by purine end products <i>(AMP, GMP)</i> | Salvage: recycles free purines; HGPRT <i>(hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase)</i> + APRT" },
            { q: "Lesch-Nyhan syndrome \u2014 defect and features?", a: "HGPRT <b>deficiency</b> <i>(<b>X-linked</b> recessive)</i>; cannot salvage hypoxanthine + guanine <b>\u2192</b> \u2191 PRPP <b>\u2192</b> \u2191 de novo purine <b>synthesis</b> <b>\u2192</b> \u2191 uric acid; features: hyperuricemia <i>(gout)</i>, intellectual disability, choreoathetosis, self-mutilation <i>(biting lips/fingers)</i>, aggression; treat with allopurinol <i>(\u2193 uric acid)</i> but neurologic symptoms persist" },
            { q: "Pyrimidine synthesis \u2014 de novo pathway?", a: "Starts with carbamoyl phosphate <i>(CPS-II, cytoplasm, uses glutamine \u2014 unlike CPS-I which uses NH\u2083 in urea cycle)</i> <b>\u2192</b> carbamoyl phosphate + aspartate <b>\u2192</b> orotic acid <b>\u2192</b> UMP <i>(via UMP synthase = OPRT + OMP decarboxylase)</i> <b>\u2192</b> UDP <b>\u2192</b> UTP <b>\u2192</b> CTP; <b>Rate-limiting</b>: CPS-II" },
            { q: "Orotic aciduria (type I) \u2014 cause and treatment?", a: "UMP synthase <b>deficiency</b> <i>(OPRT or OMP decarboxylase)</i>; \u2191 orotic acid in urine; megaloblastic anemia <i>(\u2193 pyrimidines <b>\u2192</b> \u2193 DNA <b>synthesis</b>)</i> that does NOT respond to B12 or folate; no hyperammonemia <i>(vs. OTC <b>deficiency</b> which has hyperammonemia + orotic aciduria)</i>; treat with uridine supplementation <i>(bypasses block)</i>" },
            { q: "Purine catabolism \u2192 end product?", a: "Purines <i>(AMP, GMP)</i> <b>\u2192</b> hypoxanthine/guanine <b>\u2192</b> xanthine <i>(xanthine oxidase)</i> <b>\u2192</b> uric acid <i>(excreted in urine)</i>; xanthine oxidase <b>Inhibited</b> by allopurinol <i>(treated as substrate <b>\u2192</b> alloxanthine, irreversible inhibitor)</i> and febuxostat; rasburicase <i>(recombinant uricase)</i> converts uric acid <b>\u2192</b> allantoin" },
            { q: "Gout \u2014 pathophysiology and treatment?", a: "\u2191 Uric acid <b>\u2192</b> monosodium urate crystals deposit in joints <i>(negatively birefringent, needle-shaped)</i> + soft tissue <i>(tophi)</i>; triggered by alcohol, purine-rich foods, dehydration, thiazide diuretics; acute: colchicine + NSAIDs + corticosteroids; chronic: allopurinol or febuxostat <i>(\u2193 uric acid production)</i> or probenecid <i>(\u2191 uric acid excretion \u2014 blocks reabsorption)</i>" },
            { q: "Thymidylate synthesis \u2014 enzyme and clinical relevance?", a: "Thymidylate synthase: dUMP <b>\u2192</b> dTMP <i>(uses N5,N10-methylene-THF <b>\u2192</b> DHF; regenerated by DHFR)</i>; <b>Inhibited</b> by 5-FU <i>(fluorouracil \u2014 suicide inhibitor via FdUMP)</i> and methotrexate <i>(<b>Inhibits</b> DHFR <b>\u2192</b> \u2193 THF <b>\u2192</b> \u2193 dTMP)</i>; both cause \u2193 DNA <b>synthesis</b> <b>\u2192</b> cancer/rapidly dividing cells" },
            { q: "Thiamine (B1) \u2014 function, deficiency diseases?", a: "Cofactor as TPP for: PDC <i>(pyruvate <b>\u2192</b> acetyl-CoA)</i>, \u03b1-KG dehydrogenase <i>(TCA)</i>, BCKAD <i>(MSUD)</i>, transketolase <i>(PPP)</i>; <b>deficiency</b>: Wernicke's encephalopathy <i>(confusion, ataxia, ophthalmoplegia \u2014 thiamine first!)</i>, Korsakoff's <i>(anterograde amnesia, confabulation)</i>, Beriberi <i>(wet: high-output HF; dry: peripheral neuropathy)</i>; \u2191 pyruvate + lactate" },
            { q: "Riboflavin (B2) \u2014 function and deficiency?", a: "FAD/FMN <i>(electron carriers in ETC, \u03b2-oxidation, TCA, complex I/II)</i>; <b>deficiency</b>: corneal vascularization, cheilosis <i>(lip cracking)</i>, glossitis <i>(magenta tongue)</i>, angular stomatitis, normocytic anemia; \"C\" symptoms: Corneal vascularization, Cheilosis, glossitis <i>(Crimson)</i>" },
            { q: "Niacin (B3) \u2014 function and deficiency/excess?", a: "NAD\u207a/NADH + NADP\u207a/NADPH <i>(electron carriers)</i>; derived from tryptophan <i>(requires B6, B2, iron)</i>; <b>deficiency</b>: Pellagra \u2014 4 Ds: Diarrhea, Dermatitis <i>(sun-exposed)</i>, Dementia, Death; excess: flushing <i>(prostaglandin-mediated, \u2193 with aspirin)</i>, hyperglycemia, hyperuricemia, hepatotoxicity; used pharmacologically to \u2191 HDL, \u2193 TG" },
            { q: "Pantothenic acid (B5) \u2014 function?", a: "Component of CoA <i>(coenzyme A)</i> and ACP <i>(acyl carrier protein)</i>; essential for fatty acid <b>synthesis</b>, \u03b2-oxidation, TCA <i>(succinyl-CoA)</i>, amino acid metabolism; <b>deficiency</b> rare <i>(\"burning feet\" syndrome in severe malnutrition)</i>" },
            { q: "Pyridoxine (B6) \u2014 function and deficiency?", a: "PLP <i>(pyridoxal phosphate)</i> cofactor for: transamination <i>(AST, ALT)</i>, decarboxylation reactions <i>(DOPA <b>\u2192</b> dopamine; histidine <b>\u2192</b> histamine; tryptophan <b>\u2192</b> serotonin; GABA <b>synthesis</b>)</i>, glycogen phosphorylase, cystathionine synthase, ALA synthase <i>(heme)</i>; <b>deficiency</b>: sideroblastic anemia <i>(\u2193 heme)</i>, peripheral neuropathy, glossitis, convulsions <i>(\u2193 GABA)</i>; isoniazid <b>\u2192</b> B6 <b>deficiency</b> <i>(INH-B6 complex excreted)</i>" },
            { q: "Biotin (B7) \u2014 function and deficiency?", a: "Cofactor for carboxylases <i>(add CO\u2082)</i>: Pyruvate carboxylase <i>(pyruvate <b>\u2192</b> OAA)</i>, ACC <i>(acetyl-CoA <b>\u2192</b> malonyl-CoA)</i>, Propionyl-CoA carboxylase, MCC; <b>deficiency</b>: dermatitis, alopecia, enteritis; caused by raw egg whites <i>(avidin binds biotin)</i> or antibiotic use; mnemonic: Biotin = CO\u2082 Binding" },
            { q: "Folate (B9) \u2014 function, deficiency, and sources?", a: "THF carries one-carbon units; essential for purine <b>synthesis</b> <i>(C2, C8)</i> + dTMP <b>synthesis</b> <i>(thymidylate synthase)</i>; <b>deficiency</b>: megaloblastic anemia <i>(\u2191 MCV, hypersegmented neutrophils)</i> + neural tube defects <i>(spina bifida, anencephaly \u2014 supplement periconceptionally)</i>; NO neurologic symptoms <i>(vs. B12)</i>; found in leafy greens; destroyed by cooking; \u2191 homocysteine but normal methylmalonic acid" },
            { q: "Cobalamin (B12) \u2014 function, deficiency, and source?", a: "Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase <i>(propionyl-CoA pathway, requires adenosyl-B12)</i> + methionine synthase <i>(homocysteine <b>\u2192</b> methionine, requires methyl-B12)</i>; <b>deficiency</b>: megaloblastic anemia + subacute combined <b>degradation</b> <i>(posterior + lateral spinal cord \u2014 proprioception, vibration, UMN signs)</i>, \u2191 methylmalonic acid + \u2191 homocysteine; source: animal products only; intrinsic factor <i>(parietal cells)</i> required for ileal absorption; pernicious anemia = anti-IF antibodies" },
            { q: "Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) \u2014 function and deficiency?", a: "Antioxidant; cofactor for proline/lysine hydroxylase <i>(collagen <b>synthesis</b> \u2014 post-translational)</i>; enhances iron absorption <i>(reduces Fe\u00b3\u207a <b>\u2192</b> Fe\u00b2\u207a)</i>; <b>deficiency</b>: Scurvy \u2014 perifollicular hemorrhage, bleeding gums, poor wound healing, corkscrew hairs, hemarthrosis; \u2193 proline/lysine hydroxylation <b>\u2192</b> defective collagen <i>(type I)</i>" },
            { q: "Vitamin A (retinol) \u2014 function and deficiency/toxicity?", a: "Vision <i>(retinal = 11-cis-retinal combines with opsin <b>\u2192</b> rhodopsin)</i>; epithelial differentiation <i>(retinoic acid <b>\u2192</b> RAR <b>\u2192</b> gene expression)</i>; immune function; antioxidant; <b>deficiency</b>: night blindness <i>(earliest)</i>, xerophthalmia, Bitot's spots, immune dysfunction; toxicity <i>(fat-soluble)</i>: teratogenic <i>(cleft palate, cardiac defects)</i>, pseudotumor cerebri, hepatotoxicity, alopecia, dry skin" },
            { q: "Vitamin D \u2014 function and deficiency/toxicity?", a: "Calcitriol <b>\u2192</b> \u2191 intestinal Ca\u00b2\u207a + phosphate absorption; \u2191 renal reabsorption; \u2191 bone mineralization; <b>deficiency</b>: rickets <i>(children)</i>, osteomalacia <i>(adults)</i>; toxicity: hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, nephrolithiasis; fat-soluble; synthesized in skin <i>(UV)</i> + dietary; stored in fat" },
            { q: "Vitamin E (tocopherol) \u2014 function and deficiency?", a: "Antioxidant <i>(protects PUFA membranes from oxidation)</i>; scavenges free radicals; <b>deficiency</b> <i>(rare, fat malabsorption)</i>: hemolytic anemia, spinocerebellar ataxia, peripheral neuropathy, loss of proprioception/vibration <i>(mimics B12 but \u2191 methylmalonic acid only in B12)</i>; similar symptoms to Friedreich's ataxia" },
            { q: "Vitamin K \u2014 function, deficiency, and antagonists?", a: "Cofactor for \u03b3-carboxylation of Glu residues on clotting factors II, VII, IX, X + protein C, S, Z <i>(glutamate carboxylase)</i>; <b>deficiency</b>: \u2191 PT + \u2191 PTT <i>(factors II, VII, IX, X affected)</i>, normal bleeding time, hemorrhagic disease of newborn; warfarin antagonizes vitamin K <i>(blocks VKOR, \u2193 carboxylation)</i>; fat-soluble; found in leafy greens + gut bacteria" },
            { q: "Zinc \u2014 function and deficiency?", a: "Cofactor for >300 enzymes <i>(carbonic anhydrase, carboxypeptidase, alcohol dehydrogenase, DNA/RNA polymerase, alkaline phosphatase)</i>; <b>deficiency</b>: acrodermatitis enteropathica, delayed wound healing, anosmia, ageusia, hypogonadism, growth retardation, immune dysfunction; \u2193 in acrodermatitis enteropathica <i>(ZIP4 transporter defect)</i>" },
            { q: "DNA structure \u2014 key features?", a: "Double helix <i>(Watson-Crick)</i>; antiparallel strands <i>(5'<b>\u2192</b>3' and 3'<b>\u2192</b>5')</i>; base pairing: A=T <i>(2 H-bonds)</i>, G\u2261C <i>(3 H-bonds)</i>; major groove <i>(protein binding)</i> + minor groove; B-form helix <i>(physiologic)</i>; sugar = deoxyribose; backbone: phosphodiester bonds" },
            { q: "Chargaff's rules?", a: "In any double-stranded DNA: %A = %T and %G = %C; A + G = T + C <i>(purines = pyrimidines)</i>; \u2191 GC content <b>\u2192</b> higher melting temperature <i>(Tm)</i> <b>\u2192</b> more heat needed to denature <i>(3 H-bonds vs. 2)</i>" },
            { q: "DNA replication \u2014 key enzymes (eukaryotic)?", a: "Helicase <i>(unwinds dsDNA at origin)</i>; Primase <i>(synthesizes RNA primer)</i>; DNA polymerase \u03b1 <i>(primase activity + initiates)</i>; DNA polymerase \u03b4 <i>(leading strand + lagging strand \u2014 Okazaki fragments)</i>; DNA polymerase \u03b5 <i>(leading strand)</i>; DNA polymerase \u03b2 <i>(base excision repair)</i>; RNase H <i>(removes RNA primers)</i>; DNA ligase <i>(seals nicks)</i>; Topoisomerase I/II <i>(relieve supercoiling \u2014 II creates double-strand break)</i>" },
            { q: "DNA replication \u2014 leading vs. lagging strand?", a: "Leading strand: continuous <b>synthesis</b> 5'<b>\u2192</b>3' <i>(in direction of replication fork)</i> | Lagging strand: discontinuous <i>(Okazaki fragments, ~100\u2013200 nt in eukaryotes, 1000\u20132000 in prokaryotes)</i>; each fragment requires RNA primer; joined by DNA ligase after primer removal" },
            { q: "DNA repair mechanisms \u2014 types and associated diseases?", a: "Base excision repair <i>(BER)</i>: glycosylase removes damaged base; DNA pol \u03b2 fills; mismatch repair <i>(MMR)</i>: MSH2/MLH1 recognize mismatches; defect <b>\u2192</b> Lynch syndrome <i>(HNPCC)</i> | Nucleotide excision repair <i>(NER)</i>: removes bulky adducts <i>(UV-induced pyrimidine dimers)</i>; defect <b>\u2192</b> Xeroderma pigmentosum | Homologous recombination: BRCA1/2; defect <b>\u2192</b> hereditary breast/ovarian cancer | NHEJ: Ku proteins; error-prone; G1 phase" },
            { q: "Xeroderma pigmentosum \u2014 defect and features?", a: "Defective NER <i>(cannot repair UV-induced pyrimidine dimers \u2014 CPDs, 6-4 photoproducts)</i>; AR; extreme UV sensitivity, freckling, multiple skin cancers <i>(basal cell, squamous cell, melanoma)</i> from childhood; neurologic <b>degradation</b>; \u2191 risk of ocular melanoma" },
            { q: "Telomeres \u2014 structure and telomerase?", a: "Repetitive TTAGGG sequences at chromosome ends; prevent <b>degradation</b> + chromosome fusion; shorten with each replication <i>(end replication problem)</i>; telomerase <i>(reverse transcriptase + RNA template)</i> maintains length in stem cells, germ cells, cancer cells; absent/inactive in most somatic cells <b>\u2192</b> cellular senescence" },
            { q: "RNA types and functions?", a: "mRNA <i>(messenger)</i>: carries genetic code from DNA to ribosome | tRNA <i>(transfer)</i>: adaptor \u2014 anticodon matches codon; carries amino acid; cloverleaf structure; CCA-3' end <i>(amino acid attachment)</i> | rRNA <i>(ribosomal)</i>: structural + catalytic <i>(ribozyme)</i> \u2014 28S <i>(large, peptidyl transferase)</i>, 18S <i>(small, decoding)</i> in eukaryotes | snRNA: splicing | miRNA/siRNA: gene silencing" },
            { q: "Transcription \u2014 steps and key enzymes?", a: "Initiation: RNA pol II binds promoter <i>(TATA box, -25 to -30; CAAT box, -80; GC box)</i> via transcription factors <i>(TFIID binds TATA; TFIIH = helicase)</i> | Elongation: RNA pol II synthesizes 5'<b>\u2192</b>3' | Termination: polyadenylation signal <i>(AAUAAA)</i> <b>\u2192</b> cleavage + poly-A tail added; RNA pol I <i>(rRNA)</i>, II <i>(mRNA)</i>, III <i>(tRNA, 5S rRNA)</i>" },
            { q: "mRNA processing (pre-mRNA \u2192 mRNA)?", a: "5' 7-methylguanosine cap <i>(added co-transcriptionally; protects from exonucleases, ribosome recognition)</i> | Splicing <i>(spliceosomes, snRNPs \u2014 U1, U2, U4, U5, U6; removes introns, ligates exons)</i> | 3' poly-A tail <i>(150\u2013250 A residues; stability, nuclear export)</i> | All occur in nucleus before export via nuclear pore" },
            { q: "Genetic code features?", a: "Triplet codons <i>(64 total)</i>; degenerate/redundant <i>(multiple codons <b>\u2192</b> same AA \u2014 wobble at 3rd position)</i>; unambiguous <i>(each codon <b>\u2192</b> only one AA)</i>; non-overlapping; universal <i>(with exceptions: mitochondria)</i>; start codon: AUG <i>(methionine)</i>; stop codons: UAA, UAG, UGA <i>(\"U Are Away, U Are Gone, U Go Away\")</i>" },
            { q: "Translation \u2014 initiation, elongation, termination?", a: "Initiation: 43S complex <i>(40S + Met-tRNA + eIFs)</i> scans for AUG <b>\u2192</b> 60S joins <b>\u2192</b> 80S; GTP required | Elongation: aminoacyl-tRNA enters A site <b>\u2192</b> peptide bond <i>(peptidyl transferase = 28S rRNA)</i> <b>\u2192</b> translocation <i>(EF-2/EF-G, GTP)</i> <b>\u2192</b> A site free | Termination: stop codon <b>\u2192</b> release factors <b>\u2192</b> polypeptide released; <b>Inhibited</b> by diphtheria toxin <i>(ADP-ribosylates EF-2)</i>, ricin <i>(depurinates 28S rRNA)</i>" },
            { q: "Antibiotics targeting prokaryotic translation (30S vs. 50S)?", a: "30S <i>(small subunit)</i>: Aminoglycosides <i>(streptomycin \u2014 misreading)</i>, Tetracyclines <i>(block tRNA entry)</i> | 50S <i>(large subunit)</i>: Chloramphenicol <i>(<b>Inhibits</b> peptidyl transferase)</i>, Macrolides <i>(erythromycin \u2014 blocks translocation)</i>, Linezolid, Clindamycin | Note: prokaryotes = 70S <i>(30S + 50S)</i>; eukaryotes = 80S <i>(40S + 60S)</i>" },
            { q: "Mutations \u2014 types and effects?", a: "Silent <i>(synonymous)</i>: same AA <i>(wobble)</i>; Missense: different AA <i>(sickle cell: Glu<b>\u2192</b>Val in HbS)</i>; Nonsense: premature stop codon <b>\u2192</b> truncated protein; Frameshift: insertion/deletion <i>(not in multiples of 3)</i> <b>\u2192</b> altered reading frame <b>\u2192</b> usually nonsense downstream; Transition: purine<b>\u2194</b>purine or pyrimidine<b>\u2194</b>pyrimidine | Transversion: purine<b>\u2194</b>pyrimidine" },
            { q: "Sickle cell disease \u2014 mutation and molecular basis?", a: "Point <b>mutation</b> in \u03b2-globin gene <i>(HBB)</i>: codon 6, Glu <i>(GAG)</i> <b>\u2192</b> Val <i>(GTG)</i>; Val is hydrophobic <b>\u2192</b> HbS polymerizes under low O\u2082 <b>\u2192</b> sickling; HbSS = disease; HbAS = trait <i>(protective vs. malaria)</i>; \u2193 solubility of deoxy-HbS; diagnose with Hb electrophoresis + sickling test; treat with hydroxyurea <i>(\u2191 HbF)</i>" },
            { q: "Epigenetics \u2014 DNA methylation and histone modification?", a: "DNA methylation: CpG islands <i>(5-methylcytosine by DNMT)</i>; \u2191 methylation <b>\u2192</b> gene silencing; methylated = condensed chromatin <i>(heterochromatin)</i> | Histone acetylation <i>(HAT)</i>: removes positive charge <b>\u2192</b> loosens DNA-histone interaction <b>\u2192</b> euchromatin <b>\u2192</b> active transcription | Histone deacetylation <i>(HDAC)</i>: condenses <b>\u2192</b> silencing | Histone methylation: context-dependent <i>(H3K4me = active; H3K9me = inactive)</i>" },
            { q: "Imprinting \u2014 definition and examples?", a: "Epigenetic silencing of one parental allele <i>(methylation)</i>; monoallelic expression based on parental origin; Prader-Willi: paternal 15q11-q13 deletion <i>(or maternal UPD15)</i> <b>\u2192</b> loss of paternal SNRPN expression; Angelman: maternal 15q11-q13 deletion <i>(or paternal UPD15)</i> <b>\u2192</b> loss of maternal UBE3A expression; Beckwith-Wiedemann: loss of maternal imprinting at 11p15 <b>\u2192</b> \u2191 IGF-2" },
            { q: "Prader-Willi vs. Angelman syndrome \u2014 distinguish?", a: "Prader-Willi: paternal deletion 15q11 <i>(or maternal UPD)</i>; hyperphagia, obesity, hypogonadism, intellectual disability, short stature, hypotonia at birth; \"HHHO: Happy, Hypotonia, Hyperphagia, Obesity\" | Angelman: maternal deletion 15q11 <i>(or paternal UPD)</i>; severe intellectual disability, absent speech, seizures, happy/laughing demeanor, ataxia; \"happy puppet syndrome\"" },
            { q: "X-inactivation (Lyonization) \u2014 mechanism?", a: "Random inactivation of one X chromosome in each somatic cell of females <i>(day 16 of development)</i>; XIST gene <i>(on Xi)</i> produces XIST RNA <b>\u2192</b> coats Xi <b>\u2192</b> heterochromatin <i>(Barr body)</i>; inactivation is random but clonal; some genes escape inactivation <i>(pseudoautosomal region + ~15% of X genes)</i>; females = mosaics" },
            { q: "Modes of inheritance \u2014 autosomal dominant features?", a: "Vertical transmission; affects every generation; 50% risk if one parent affected; male = female equally; often structural proteins/receptors <i>(gain of function or haploinsufficiency)</i>; examples: Huntington's, Marfan's, NF1/2, ADPKD, familial hypercholesterolemia, achondroplasia, hereditary spherocytosis" },
            { q: "Autosomal recessive features?", a: "Horizontal transmission; 25% risk if both parents carriers; often <b>enzyme</b> deficiencies; consanguinity \u2191 risk; carriers usually unaffected; examples: CF, PKU, galactosemia, glycogen storage diseases, sickle cell, <b>most common</b> lysosomal storage diseases, Wilson's, hemochromatosis" },
            { q: "X-linked recessive features?", a: "Males affected <i>(hemizygous)</i>; females carriers <i>(may have mild symptoms \u2014 lyonization)</i>; no father-to-son transmission; affected father <b>\u2192</b> all daughters carriers; examples: Hemophilia A/B, G6PD <b>deficiency</b>, Duchenne/Becker MD, Fabry's, Lesch-Nyhan, color blindness, Bruton's agammaglobulinemia, Hunter syndrome" },
            { q: "Mitochondrial inheritance features?", a: "Maternal inheritance <i>(mitochondria from oocyte)</i>; all children of affected mother may be affected; heteroplasmy <i>(variable expression)</i>; examples: MERRF <i>(myoclonic epilepsy, ragged-red fibers)</i>, MELAS <i>(mitochondrial encephalomyopathy, lactic acidosis, stroke-like episodes)</i>, Leber's hereditary optic neuropathy <i>(LHON)</i>" },
            { q: "Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium \u2014 equation and assumptions?", a: "p\u00b2 + 2pq + q\u00b2 = 1; p + q = 1; p\u00b2 = AA, 2pq = Aa <i>(carriers)</i>, q\u00b2 = aa <i>(affected)</i>; assumes: large population, random mating, no <b>mutation</b>, no selection, no migration; carrier frequency for AR disease: if q\u00b2 = 1/10,000 <b>\u2192</b> q = 1/100 <b>\u2192</b> p \u2248 1 <b>\u2192</b> carrier = 2pq \u2248 2/100 = 1/50" },
            { q: "Anticipation \u2014 definition and examples?", a: "Disease presents earlier and more severely in successive generations due to expansion of trinucleotide repeats; examples: Huntington's <i>(CAG, HTT gene)</i>, Myotonic dystrophy <i>(CTG, DMPK gene)</i>, Fragile X <i>(CGG, FMR1 gene)</i>, Friedreich's ataxia <i>(GAA, frataxin gene \u2014 no anticipation, AR)</i>" },
            { q: "Fragile X syndrome \u2014 mutation and features?", a: "CGG repeat expansion <i>(>200 repeats)</i> in FMR1 gene <i>(Xq27.3)</i> <b>\u2192</b> methylation <b>\u2192</b> gene silencing <b>\u2192</b> \u2193 FMRP <i>(needed for synaptic plasticity)</i>; <b>X-linked</b> dominant <i>(premutation carriers: 55\u2013200 repeats)</i>; features: macroorchidism, large ears/jaw/forehead, intellectual disability <i>(<b>most common</b> inherited intellectual disability)</i>, autism, mitral valve prolapse; premutation: fragile X-associated tremor/ataxia syndrome <i>(FXTAS)</i> in males + premature ovarian failure in females" },
            { q: "Huntington's disease \u2014 mutation, gene, inheritance, features?", a: "CAG expansion <i>(>36 repeats)</i> in HTT gene <i>(chromosome 4p)</i>; <b>Autosomal</b> dominant; trinucleotide repeat encodes polyglutamine <i>(polyQ)</i> in huntingtin protein <b>\u2192</b> toxic gain of function <b>\u2192</b> neuronal death <i>(striatum/caudate first)</i>; features: chorea, psychiatric <i>(depression, personality change first)</i>, dementia; onset typically 30\u201350 years; paternal anticipation <i>(CAT repeats more unstable in spermatogenesis)</i>" },
            { q: "Chromosomal deletions \u2014 microdeletion syndromes (key examples)?", a: "5p deletion <i>(Cri-du-chat)</i>: cat-like cry, intellectual disability, microcephaly | 7q11.23 deletion <i>(Williams)</i>: elfin facies, supravalvular aortic stenosis, intellectual disability, hypercalcemia, friendly/musical | 15q11-q13 deletion: Prader-Willi <i>(paternal)</i> or Angelman <i>(maternal)</i> | 17p11.2 <i>(Smith-Magenis)</i>: self-injurious behavior | 22q11.2 <i>(DiGeorge/velocardiofacial)</i>" },
            { q: "Trisomy 21 (Down syndrome) \u2014 genetics, features, associations?", a: "Trisomy 21 <i>(95% nondisjunction, maternal age-related; 4% Robertsonian translocation \u2014 not age-related; 1% mosaic)</i>; features: intellectual disability, flat facies, epicanthal folds, single palmar crease, sandal-gap toe, hypotonia, Brushfield spots; associations: \u2191 AML/ALL <i>(especially AML-M7)</i>, Alzheimer's <i>(chromosome 21 = APP gene)</i>, Hirschsprung's, duodenal atresia, AVSD, hypothyroidism; maternal serum: \u2193 AFP, \u2193 uE3, \u2191 \u03b2-hCG, \u2191 inhibin A" },
            { q: "Trisomy 18 (Edwards) vs. Trisomy 13 (Patau) \u2014 key features?", a: "Trisomy 18 <i>(Edwards)</i>: PRINCE Edward = Prominent occiput, Rocker-bottom feet, Intellectual disability, Nondisjunction, Clenched fists <i>(overlapping fingers)</i>, cardiac defects <i>(VSD)</i>; most die <1 year | Trisomy 13 <i>(Patau)</i>: Holoprosencephaly, Microphthalmia, cleft lip/Palate, Polydactyly, cardiac defects; most die <1 year; both \u2191 with maternal age" },
            { q: "Robertsonian translocation \u2014 definition and clinical relevance?", a: "Fusion of two acrocentric chromosomes <i>(13, 14, 15, 21, 22)</i> at centromere with loss of short arms; <b>most common</b>: t<i>(14;21)</i>; carrier has 45 chromosomes but normal phenotype; offspring risk of Down syndrome <i>(14;21 carrier mother: 10\u201315% risk; father: 2\u20135% risk)</i>; cannot have children with trisomy 21 via nondisjunction mechanism" },
            { q: "Mosaicism \u2014 definition and clinical significance?", a: "Two or more genetically distinct cell lines in one individual; arises from mitotic error after fertilization; milder phenotype than full trisomy; examples: mosaic Down syndrome <i>(milder)</i>, McCune-Albright <i>(somatic GNAS <b>mutation</b> \u2014 caf\u00e9-au-lait spots, precocious puberty, polyostotic fibrous dysplasia)</i>" },
            { q: "FISH (Fluorescence In Situ Hybridization) \u2014 principle and uses?", a: "Fluorescent DNA probe hybridizes to complementary chromosomal sequence; detects deletions/duplications/translocations not visible on karyotype; uses: DiGeorge <i>(22q11)</i>, Williams <i>(7q11)</i>, Prader-Willi/Angelman <i>(15q11)</i>, Philadelphia chromosome t<i>(9;22)</i>; does not require dividing cells <i>(can use interphase cells)</i>" },
            { q: "PCR (Polymerase Chain Reaction) \u2014 steps and requirements?", a: "Denaturation <i>(94\u00b0C \u2014 separate strands)</i> <b>\u2192</b> Annealing <i>(50\u201365\u00b0C \u2014 primers bind)</i> <b>\u2192</b> Extension <i>(72\u00b0C \u2014 Taq polymerase synthesizes)</i>; requires: DNA template, primers <i>(2)</i>, Taq polymerase <i>(heat-stable)</i>, dNTPs, Mg\u00b2\u207a, buffer; exponential amplification <i>(2\u207f copies after n cycles)</i>; uses: diagnosis, forensics, prenatal testing, pathogen detection" },
            { q: "Southern vs. Northern vs. Western blotting?", a: "Southern: DNA <i>(detect specific DNA sequences \u2014 RFLP, gene <b>mutation</b>s)</i> | Northern: RNA <i>(detect specific mRNA \u2014 gene expression)</i> | Western: Protein <i>(detect specific proteins \u2014 antibody-based)</i>; mnemonic: SNoW DRoP \u2014 Southern=DNA, Northern=RNA, Western=Protein" },
            { q: "CRISPR-Cas9 \u2014 mechanism?", a: "Guide RNA <i>(gRNA)</i> directs Cas9 nuclease to complementary DNA sequence <b>\u2192</b> Cas9 creates double-strand break <b>\u2192</b> repaired by NHEJ <i>(error-prone <b>\u2192</b> gene knockout)</i> or HDR <i>(with template <b>\u2192</b> precise edit)</i>; revolutionized gene editing; used in treatment of sickle cell disease <i>(\u2191 HbF by disrupting BCL11A)</i>" },
            { q: "Oncogenes vs. tumor suppressor genes?", a: "Oncogenes <i>(proto-oncogene <b>\u2192</b> oncogene)</i>: gain-of-function <b>mutation</b>, one allele sufficient <i>(dominant)</i>; promote cell growth; examples: RAS <i>(point <b>mutation</b> \u2014 colon, lung)</i>, MYC <i>(translocation/amplification)</i>, HER2 <i>(amplification)</i>, BCR-ABL <i>(CML, t(9;22))</i>, RET <i>(MEN2)</i> | Tumor suppressors: loss-of-function <i>(recessive \u2014 Knudson two-hit)</i>; brake cell growth; examples: RB <i>(retinoblastoma)</i>, TP53 <i>(<b>most common</b> in human cancers)</i>, BRCA1/2, APC, VHL, PTEN, NF1/2" },
            { q: "p53 \u2014 function and consequences of loss?", a: "\"Guardian of the genome\"; G1/S checkpoint guardian; <b>Activated</b> by DNA damage <b>\u2192</b> \u2191 p21 <i>(CDK inhibitor <b>\u2192</b> cell cycle arrest)</i> <b>\u2192</b> DNA repair or apoptosis <i>(\u2191 BAX, \u2193 BCL-2)</i>; TP53 mutated in >50% of human cancers; Li-Fraumeni syndrome <i>(germline TP53 <b>mutation</b>)</i> <b>\u2192</b> multiple cancers; MDM2 normally ubiquitinates p53 <b>\u2192</b> <b>degradation</b>" },
            { q: "Cell cycle phases and checkpoints?", a: "G1 <i>(growth, <b>synthesis</b> of proteins)</i> <b>\u2192</b> S <i>(DNA <b>synthesis</b>, 2N <b>\u2192</b> 4N)</i> <b>\u2192</b> G2 <i>(proofreading, growth)</i> <b>\u2192</b> M <i>(mitosis \u2014 PMAT: prophase, metaphase, anaphase, telophase)</i>; G0 = quiescent; Checkpoints: G1/S <i>(p53/RB \u2014 major restriction point)</i>, G2/M <i>(cyclin B-CDK1)</i>, Spindle assembly checkpoint <i>(M phase \u2014 MAD/BUB proteins)</i>; G0 cells: neurons, skeletal muscle, cardiac muscle" },
            { q: "CDKs and Cyclins \u2014 key pairs?", a: "Cyclin D-CDK4/6: G1 progression <i>(phosphorylates RB <b>\u2192</b> releases E2F <b>\u2192</b> S phase genes)</i>; <b>Inhibited</b> by p16 <i>(CDKN2A)</i> and p21/p27 | Cyclin E-CDK2: G1<b>\u2192</b>S transition | Cyclin A-CDK2: S phase | Cyclin B-CDK1 <i>(MPF)</i>: G2<b>\u2192</b>M; CDK inhibitors: p21 <i>(\u2191 by p53)</i>, p27, p57, p16 <i>(\u2193 in many cancers)</i>" },
            { q: "Retinoblastoma gene (RB) \u2014 function and two-hit hypothesis?", a: "RB protein <i>(hypophosphorylated = active = growth suppressor)</i> sequesters E2F transcription factor <b>\u2192</b> prevents S phase entry; CDK4/6-cyclin D phosphorylates RB <b>\u2192</b> releases E2F <b>\u2192</b> cell cycle entry; two hits needed: sporadic <i>(both somatic)</i> vs. hereditary <i>(one germline + one somatic <b>\u2192</b> bilateral, earlier onset)</i>; also lost in osteosarcoma, small cell lung ca, SCLC, bladder ca" },
            { q: "Philadelphia chromosome \u2014 translocation and disease?", a: "t<i>(9;22)</i><i>(q34;q11)</i>; BCR-ABL fusion gene <b>\u2192</b> constitutively active tyrosine kinase <b>\u2192</b> uncontrolled proliferation; CML <i>(> 95%)</i>; also ALL <i>(poor prognosis)</i>; treated with imatinib <i>(BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase inhibitor \u2014 first targeted cancer therapy)</i>; \u2191 WBC <i>(granulocytes, basophils)</i>, \u2193 LAP score" }
          ]
        },
        {
          topic: 'Musculoskeletal',
          icon: 'bone',
          cards: [
            { q: "Root of the Phrenic Nerve?", a: "C3, C4, C5 (<i>keeps the diaphragm alive</i>)." },
            { q: "Presentation of Erb\u2019s Palsy (\"Waiter\u2019s tip\")?", a: "Adducted, medially rotated arm, extended elbow (<i>C5-C6 root injury</i>)." },
            { q: "Presentation of Klumpke Palsy?", a: "Total claw hand (<i>C8-T1 root injury</i>)." },
            { q: "Nerve compressed in \"Saturday Night Palsy\"?", a: "<b>Radial nerve</b> (at the <i>spiral groove</i>)." },
            { q: "Nerve responsible for thumb opposition?", a: "<b>Median nerve</b> (<i>Recurrent branch</i>)." },
            { q: "Muscle group innervated by the Obturator nerve?", a: "Thigh adductors." },
            { q: "Nerve responsible for sensation between the 1st and 2nd toes?", a: "<b>Deep Peroneal nerve</b>." },
            { q: "Nerve injured in a Baker\u2019s Cyst?", a: "<b>Tibial nerve</b>." },
            { q: "Most common direction of a Shoulder Dislocation?", a: "Anterior." },
            { q: "Most common direction of a Hip Dislocation?", a: "Posterior (presents with <i>shortened, internally rotated leg</i>)." },
            { q: "Anatomical structure injured in \"Gamekeeper\u2019s Thumb\"?", a: "<b>Ulnar collateral ligament</b> of the thumb." },
            { q: "Contents of the Femoral Triangle (Lateral to Medial)?", a: "<i>NAVL</i>: Nerve, Artery, Vein, Lymphatics." },
            { q: "Contents of the Femoral Sheath?", a: "<b>Femoral Artery</b>, Vein, and Canal (<i>does NOT contain the Femoral Nerve</i>)." },
            { q: "Primary cell type in the Stratum Corneum?", a: "Keratinocytes (<i>corneocytes</i>)." },
            { q: "Role of Melanocytes?", a: "Produce melanin; derived from <b>Neural Crest cells</b>." },
            { q: "Most common skin cancer?", a: "<b>Basal Cell Carcinoma</b>." },
            { q: "Skin cancer associated with arsenic exposure and \"keratin pearls\"?", a: "<b>Squamous Cell Carcinoma</b>." },
            { q: "ABCDE criteria for Melanoma?", a: "Asymmetry, Border irregularity, Color variation, Diameter > 6 mm, Evolution." },
            { q: "Finding in Pemphigus Vulgaris?", a: "IgG against <b>Desmoglein</b>; <i>skin sloughs off</i> (positive Nikolsky sign)." },
            { q: "Finding in Bullous Pemphigoid?", a: "IgG against <b>Hemidesmosomes</b>; <i>tense blisters</i> (negative Nikolsky sign)." },
            { q: "Pathogenesis of Acne Vulgaris?", a: "Excess sebum/androgens, follicular hyperkeratosis, <i>Cutibacterium acnes colonization</i>." },
            { q: "Type of collagen in Bone?", a: "<i>Type I</i>." },
            { q: "Type of collagen in Cartilage?", a: "<i>Type II</i>." },
            { q: "Function of Calcitonin in bone metabolism?", a: "Inhibits <b>osteoclasts</b> (<i>decreases serum Calcium</i>)." },
            { q: "Effect of PTH on Bone?", a: "Stimulates <b>Osteoblasts</b> to release <b>RANK-L</b>, which activates <b>Osteoclasts</b>." },
            { q: "Histology of Gout: Yellow or Blue when perpendicular to light?", a: "Blue (<i>Yellow when parallel</i>)." },
            { q: "HLA association in Rheumatoid Arthritis?", a: "HLA-DR4 (<i>Four walls in a Rheum</i>)." },
            { q: "Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) most specific antibody?", a: "Anti-Smith (<i>Anti-dsDNA</i> is also specific but reflects disease activity)." },
            { q: "Lab finding in Polymyalgia Rheumatica?", a: "Significantly elevated ESR and CRP with <i>normal CK</i>." },
            { q: "Association of Polymyalgia Rheumatica?", a: "Giant Cell (<i>Temporal</i>) Arteritis." },
            { q: "Key finding in Fibromyalgia?", a: "Chronic widespread pain at specific <i>tender points</i>; normal inflammatory markers." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Allopurinol vs. Febuxostat?", a: "Both are <i>Xanthine Oxidase inhibitors</i>." },
            { q: "Drug used for chronic Gout that increases uric acid excretion (uricosuric)?", a: "<b>Probenecid</b>." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Leflunomide?", a: "Inhibits dihydroorotate dehydrogenase (prevents <i>pyrimidine synthesis</i>)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Denosumab?", a: "Monoclonal antibody against <b>RANK-L</b> (inhibits <b>osteoclast</b> maturation)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Teriparatide?", a: "Recombinant <b>PTH</b> analog (stimulates <b>osteoblasts</b> when given <i>pulsatile</i>)." },
            { q: "Root of the Long Thoracic Nerve?", a: "C5, C6, C7 (<i>keeps the wings from heaven</i>)." },
            { q: "Clinical sign of Long Thoracic Nerve injury?", a: "<i>Winged scapula</i>." },
            { q: "Nerve injured in a midshaft humerus fracture?", a: "<b>Radial nerve</b>." },
            { q: "Nerve injured in a surgical neck humerus fracture?", a: "<b>Axillary nerve</b>." },
            { q: "Muscle responsible for the first 15\u00b0 of arm abduction?", a: "<b>Supraspinatus</b> (Suprascapular nerve)." },
            { q: "Muscle responsible for arm abduction from 15\u00b0 to 90\u00b0?", a: "<b>Deltoid</b> (<b>Axillary nerve</b>)." },
            { q: "Structures passing through the Carpal Tunnel?", a: "<b>Median nerve</b> and <i>9 tendons</i> (4 FDP, 4 FDS, 1 FPL)." },
            { q: "Nerve responsible for the \"Claw Hand\" (at rest)?", a: "<b>Ulnar nerve</b> (<i>distal lesion</i>)." },
            { q: "Most commonly fractured carpal bone?", a: "<b>Scaphoid</b> (risk of <i>avascular necrosis</i>)." },
            { q: "Muscle group responsible for \"Tennis Elbow\" (Lateral Epicondylitis)?", a: "Extensor muscles of the forearm." },
            { q: "Most common site of a Disc Herniation?", a: "<i>L4-L5</i> or <i>L5-S1</i>." },
            { q: "Nerve responsible for Foot Drop?", a: "<b>Common Peroneal</b> (Fibula) nerve." },
            { q: "Action of the Iliohypogastric nerve?", a: "Sensory to <i>suprapubic region</i>; motor to transverse abdominis." },
            { q: "Nerve injured in \"Trendelenburg Gait\"?", a: "<b>Superior Gluteal nerve</b> (lesion <i>contralateral</i> to the side that drops)." },
            { q: "The \"Unhappy Triad\" of knee injuries?", a: "ACL, MCL, and Medial Meniscus (though Lateral Meniscus is often more common in acute trauma)." },
            { q: "Nerve responsible for the Patellar Reflex?", a: "<b>Femoral nerve</b> (L2-L4)." },
            { q: "Main muscle of hip flexion?", a: "<b>Iliopsoas</b>." },
            { q: "Primary muscle of hip extension?", a: "<b>Gluteus maximus</b>." },
            { q: "Function of Osteoblasts?", a: "Build bone (secrete collagen and catalyze mineralization)." },
            { q: "Cell type derived from the monocyte/macrophage lineage in bone?", a: "<b>Osteoclasts</b>." },
            { q: "Primary neurotransmitter at the Neuromuscular Junction (NMJ)?", a: "Acetylcholine (ACh)." },
            { q: "Role of Calcium in skeletal muscle contraction?", a: "Binds to Troponin C to move Tropomyosin off the actin-binding site." },
            { q: "Type of muscle fiber that is \"Red\" and mitochondria-rich?", a: "Type 1 (<i>Slow twitch</i>; \"<i>1 slow red ox</i>\")." },
            { q: "Type of muscle fiber that is \"White\" and anaerobic?", a: "Type 2 (<i>Fast twitch</i>)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Osteocyte communication?", a: "Gap junctions within <i>canaliculi</i>." },
            { q: "Mode of ossification for long bones?", a: "Endochondral ossification (<i>cartilage model first</i>)." },
            { q: "Mode of ossification for flat bones (skull, clavicle)?", a: "Intramembranous ossification." },
            { q: "Classic X-ray finding for Osteosarcoma?", a: "<i>Codman triangle</i> or <i>Sunburst pattern</i>." },
            { q: "Most common benign bone tumor?", a: "<b>Osteochondroma</b>." },
            { q: "Translocation associated with Ewing Sarcoma?", a: "t(11;22)." },
            { q: "Histology of Ewing Sarcoma?", a: "<i>Small blue cell tumor</i>; \"<i>Onion skin</i>\" periosteal reaction." },
            { q: "Lab findings in Osteoporosis?", a: "<i>Normal Ca, PO4, PTH</i>, and ALP." },
            { q: "Pathophysiology of Osteomalacia/Rickets?", a: "<i>Vitamin D deficiency</i> leading to defective mineralization of osteoid." },
            { q: "Lab findings in Paget Disease of Bone?", a: "<i>Isolated elevated ALP</i> (Normal Ca, PO4, PTH)." },
            { q: "Bone disease characterized by \"Mosaic\" bone pattern?", a: "<b>Paget Disease</b> of Bone." },
            { q: "Antibody most specific for Rheumatoid Arthritis?", a: "Anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide (<b>Anti-CCP</b>)." },
            { q: "Classic hand deformities in Rheumatoid Arthritis?", a: "<i>Ulnar deviation</i>, <i>Boutonniere</i>, and <i>Swan-neck</i> deformities." },
            { q: "Joints spared in Rheumatoid Arthritis?", a: "DIP (Distal Interphalangeal) joints." },
            { q: "Hand joints affected in Osteoarthritis?", a: "DIP (<i>Heberden nodes</i>) and PIP (<i>Bouchard nodes</i>)." },
            { q: "HLA association for Sero-negative Spondyloarthropathies?", a: "HLA-B27." },
            { q: "The \"Can't see, can't pee, can't climb a tree\" triad?", a: "Reactive Arthritis (Uveitis, Urethritis, Arthritis)." },
            { q: "Antibody associated with Diffuse Scleroderma?", a: "<b>Anti-Scl-70</b> (Anti-DNA topoisomerase I)." },
            { q: "Antibody associated with CREST Syndrome?", a: "<b>Anti-centromere</b> antibody." },
            { q: "Most common joint affected by Gout?", a: "MTP joint of the great toe (<i>Podagra</i>)." },
            { q: "Shape and birefringence of Gout crystals?", a: "<i>needle-shaped</i>; <i>negatively birefringent</i> (<i>Yellow when parallel</i> to light)." },
            { q: "Shape and birefringence of Pseudogout (CPPD) crystals?", a: "<i>rhomboid-shaped</i>; <i>weakly positively birefringent</i> (<i>Blue when parallel</i>)." },
            { q: "Pathophysiology of Myasthenia Gravis?", a: "Autoantibodies against <i>postsynaptic</i> <b>ACh receptors</b>." },
            { q: "Pathophysiology of Lambert-Eaton Syndrome?", a: "Autoantibodies against <i>presynaptic</i> voltage-gated Calcium channels." },
            { q: "Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy inheritance?", a: "<b>X-linked recessive</b> (deletion of <b>Dystrophin</b> gene)." },
            { q: "Physical exam sign of Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy?", a: "<i>Gower sign</i> (using hands to \"climb up\" legs to stand)." },
            { q: "Key histological finding in Dermatomyositis?", a: "<i>Perifascicular atrophy</i>." },
            { q: "Dermatological signs of Dermatomyositis?", a: "<b>Gottron papules</b> and <b>Heliotrope rash</b>." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Methotrexate?", a: "Dihydrofolate reductase inhibitor (decreases DNA synthesis)." },
            { q: "Common side effect of Colchicine?", a: "<i>Diarrhea</i> (inhibits microtubule polymerization)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Etanercept?", a: "<i>TNF-alpha decoy receptor</i>." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Alendronate (Bisphosphonate)?", a: "Pyrophosphate analog; induces apoptosis in <b>osteoclasts</b>." },
            { q: "Side effect of Bisphosphonates?", a: "Corrosive <i>esophagitis</i> and <i>Osteonecrosis of the jaw</i>." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Succinylcholine?", a: "<i>depolarizing</i> neuromuscular blocker (ACh receptor agonist)." },
            { q: "Antidote for Malignant Hyperthermia?", a: "<b>Dantrolene</b>." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Celecoxib?", a: "Reversible, selective COX-2 inhibitor." },
            { q: "\"Bamboo spine\" on X-ray?", a: "<b>Ankylosing Spondylitis</b>." },
            { q: "Most common cause of Osteomyelitis?", a: "<i>Staphylococcus aureus</i>." },
            { q: "Cause of Osteomyelitis in Sickle Cell patients?", a: "<i>Salmonella</i>." },
            { q: "Difference between Osteopetrosis and Osteoporosis?", a: "<b>Osteopetrosis</b> is thick, dense bone (<b>osteoclast</b> failure); <b>Osteoporosis</b> is porous, thin bone." },
            { q: "\"Pencil-in-cup\" deformity on X-ray?", a: "<b>Psoriatic Arthritis</b>." },
            { q: "Receptor on Osteoclasts that stimulates bone resorption?", a: "<b>RANK</b>." },
            { q: "Secreted by Osteoblasts to act as a \"decoy receptor\" for RANK-L?", a: "<b>Osteoprotegerin</b> (<b>OPG</b>)." },
            { q: "Effect of Estrogen on bone remodeling?", a: "Inhibits <b>Osteoclasts</b> by increasing <b>OPG</b> production and inducing <b>Osteoclast</b> apoptosis." },
            { q: "Primary mineral component of bone matrix?", a: "<b>Hydroxyapatite</b> [Ca10(PO4)6(OH)2]." },
            { q: "Ligament that prevents hyperextension of the vertebral column?", a: "<b>Anterior Longitudinal Ligament</b>." },
            { q: "Part of the intervertebral disc that herniates?", a: "<b>Nucleus pulposus</b>." },
            { q: "Strongest ligament in the human body (located in the hip)?", a: "<b>Iliofemoral ligament</b> (<i>Bigelow ligament</i>)." },
            { q: "Muscle that passes through the greater sciatic foramen?", a: "<b>Piriformis</b>." },
            { q: "Nerve that can be compressed by the Piriformis muscle?", a: "<b>Sciatic nerve</b>." },
            { q: "Genetic defect in Achondroplasia?", a: "Constitutive activation of FGFR3 (inhibits chondrocyte proliferation)." },
            { q: "Inheritance pattern of Achondroplasia?", a: "Autosomal Dominant (80% are de novo mutations)." },
            { q: "Pathophysiology of Osteopetrosis (\"Marble Bone Disease\")?", a: "<b>Carbonic Anhydrase II</b> deficiency (prevents acidic environment for bone resorption)." },
            { q: "Physical sign of Vitamin D deficiency in children?", a: "<i>rachitic rosary</i> (prominence of costochondral junctions)." },
            { q: "Antibody associated with Drug-Induced Lupus?", a: "<b>Anti-histone antibody</b>." },
            { q: "Drugs commonly causing Drug-Induced Lupus?", a: "<i>SHIPP</i>: Sulfonamides, Hydralazine, Isoniazid, Procainamide, Phenytoin." },
            { q: "\"Pantry\" or \"Heliotrope\" rash is pathognomonic for?", a: "<b>Dermatomyositis</b>." },
            { q: "Type of hypersensitivity in Myasthenia Gravis?", a: "Type II Hypersensitivity." },
            { q: "Most common cause of a \"Cold Abscess\" in the spine?", a: "<b>Pott Disease</b> (<i>Tuberculosis</i> of the vertebrae)." },
            { q: "Most common cause of Pyogenic Arthritis in sexually active young adults?", a: "<i>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</i>." },
            { q: "\"Shepherd\u2019s crook\" deformity of the femur is seen in?", a: "<b>Fibrous Dysplasia</b>." },
            { q: "Difference between a Sprain and a Strain?", a: "Sprain = <i>Ligament</i>; Strain = <i>Muscle/Tendon</i>." },
            { q: "\"Sunburst\" appearance on X-ray is classic for?", a: "<b>Osteosarcoma</b>." },
            { q: "Skin layer containing Melanocytes and Merkel cells?", a: "<b>Stratum basale</b>." },
            { q: "Location of the \"Stem cells\" of the skin?", a: "<b>Stratum basale</b>." },
            { q: "Definition of the Auspitz sign?", a: "<i>Pinpoint bleeding</i> after scaling off a Psoriatic plaque." },
            { q: "Histological feature of Psoriasis?", a: "<b>Acanthosis</b> (epidermal hyperplasia) and <b>Parakeratosis</b> (nuclei in <b>stratum corneum</b>)." }
          ]
        },
        {
          topic: 'Genetics',
          icon: 'microscope',
          cards: [
            { q: "Purines vs. Pyrimidines (structures)?", a: "<b>Purines</b> (A, G): 2 rings. <b>Pyrimidines</b> (C, T, U): 1 ring. (Mnemonic: <i>PURe As Gold</i>; <i>CUT the PY</i>)" },
            { q: "Bond type between base pairs in DNA?", a: "Hydrogen bonds (3 between G-C, 2 between A-T)." },
            { q: "Definition of a Nucleosome?", a: "<b>DNA</b> wrapped twice around a histone octamer (2 each of H2A, H2B, H3, H4)." },
            { q: "Charge of Histones and DNA?", a: "<b>Histones</b>: Positive (Arginine/Lysine). <b>DNA</b>: Negative (Phosphate group)." },
            { q: "Effect of Histone Acetylation?", a: "Relaxes <b>DNA</b> coiling, permitting transcription (<i>Euchromatin</i>)." },
            { q: "Effect of DNA Methylation at CpG islands?", a: "Silences gene expression (\"<i>Methylation Makes DNA Mute</i>\")." },
            { q: "Function of DNA Helicase?", a: "Unwinds <b>DNA</b> template at the replication fork." },
            { q: "Direction of DNA Polymerase proofreading (exonuclease activity)?", a: "3' to 5'." },
            { q: "Probability of an offspring being affected in Autosomal Dominant (AD) inheritance (one affected parent)?", a: "50%." },
            { q: "Classic example of an Autosomal Dominant disorder with \"delayed age of onset\"?", a: "<b>Huntington Disease</b>." },
            { q: "Probability of an offspring being a carrier in Autosomal Recessive (AR) inheritance (two carrier parents)?", a: "50% (25% affected, 25% unaffected)." },
            { q: "What is Pleiotropy?", a: "One gene mutation results in multiple seemingly unrelated phenotypic effects." },
            { q: "Definition of Allelic Heterogeneity?", a: "Different mutations at the same locus produce the same phenotype (e.g., beta-thalassemia)." },
            { q: "Definition of Locus Heterogeneity?", a: "Mutations at different loci produce the same phenotype (e.g., Albinism)." },
            { q: "What is Heteroplasmy?", a: "Presence of both normal and mutated mtDNA, resulting in variable expression in mitochondrial diseases." },
            { q: "Inheritance pattern that never shows male-to-male transmission?", a: "X-linked Recessive." },
            { q: "Genetic defect in Cystic Fibrosis?", a: "Autosomal recessive mutation in <b>CFTR</b> gene (usually <i>delta F508</i> on Chromosome 7)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Fragile X Syndrome?", a: "Trinucleotide repeat expansion (<i>CGG</i>) in the <b>FMR1</b> gene." },
            { q: "Physical findings in Fragile X?", a: "Post-pubertal <i>macroorchidism</i>, long face with large ears, autism." },
            { q: "Cause of Prader-Willi Syndrome?", a: "Maternal imprinting with Paternal gene deletion/mutation (Chromosome 15)." },
            { q: "Cause of Angelman Syndrome?", a: "Paternal imprinting with Maternal gene deletion/mutation (Chromosome 15)." },
            { q: "Trinucleotide repeat in Huntington Disease?", a: "<i>CAG</i> (\"<i>Caudate Acetylcholine GABA</i>\" are decreased)." },
            { q: "Trinucleotide repeat in Myotonic Dystrophy?", a: "<i>CTG</i>." },
            { q: "Common feature of Mitochondrial Myopathies on muscle biopsy?", a: "\"<i>Ragged red fibers</i>.\"" },
            { q: "Most common cause of Down Syndrome (Trisomy 21)?", a: "Meiotic <i>non-disjunction</i> (associated with advanced maternal age)." },
            { q: "Chromosomal finding in Edwards Syndrome?", a: "<b>Trisomy 18</b> (\"<b>Edwards</b>\" = <i>Eighteen</i>)." },
            { q: "Chromosomal finding in Patau Syndrome?", a: "<b>Trisomy 13</b> (\"<b>Patau</b>\" = <i>Puberty at 13</i>)." },
            { q: "Clinical features of Patau Syndrome?", a: "Cleft lip/palate, holoprosencephaly, polydactyly, microphthalmia." },
            { q: "Karyotype of Klinefelter Syndrome?", a: "47, XXY." },
            { q: "Karyotype of Turner Syndrome?", a: "45, XO." },
            { q: "Physical findings in Turner Syndrome?", a: "Short stature, webbed neck, streak ovaries, coarctation of the aorta." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Cri-du-chat Syndrome?", a: "Congenital microdeletion of short arm of chromosome 5 (5p-)." },
            { q: "Common findings in DiGeorge Syndrome?", a: "<i>CATCH-22</i>: Cardiac defects, Abnormal facies, Thymic hypoplasia, Cleft palate, Hypocalcemia (22q11 deletion)." },
            { q: "Karyotype of Jacob Syndrome?", a: "47, XYY (presents with tall stature, acne, and learning disability)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of G6PD Deficiency inheritance?", a: "X-linked Recessive." },
            { q: "Genetics of Wilson Disease?", a: "<i>ATP7B</i> mutation on Chromosome 13 (Autosomal Recessive)." },
            { q: "Defect in von Hippel-Lindau (VHL) disease?", a: "VHL gene (tumor suppressor) on Chromosome 3." },
            { q: "Name of the syndrome: 45, XO?", a: "<b>Turner Syndrome</b>." },
            { q: "Name of the syndrome: 47, XXY?", a: "<b>Klinefelter Syndrome</b>." },
            { q: "Most common cause of intellectual disability in males?", a: "<b>Fragile X Syndrome</b>." },
            { q: "Genetics of Williams Syndrome?", a: "Microdeletion of long arm of chromosome 7 (includes elastin gene)." },
            { q: "Characteristics of Williams Syndrome?", a: "<i>elfin</i> facies, extreme friendliness with strangers, cardiovascular problems (supravalvular aortic stenosis)." },
            { q: "Hardy-Weinberg Equation for allele frequency?", a: "p + q = 1" },
            { q: "Hardy-Weinberg Equation for phenotype/genotype frequency?", a: "p^2 + 2pq + q^2 = 1" },
            { q: "What does 2pq represent in Hardy-Weinberg?", a: "Heterozygote (carrier) frequency." },
            { q: "Purpose of PCR (Polymerase Chain Reaction)?", a: "Rapidly amplify specific <b>DNA</b> sequences." },
            { q: "Purpose of Southern Blot?", a: "Detect specific <b>DNA</b> sequences. (Mnemonic: <i>SNoW DRoP</i>\u2014Southern/<b>DNA</b>, Northern/RNA, Western/Protein)" },
            { q: "Purpose of FISH (Fluorescence In Situ Hybridization)?", a: "Direct visualization of chromosomal anomalies (microdeletions, translocations) at the molecular level." },
            { q: "What is Robertsonian Translocation?", a: "Fusion of two acrocentric chromosomes (13, 14, 15, 21, 22) at the centromere." },
            { q: "Genetic defect in Marfan Syndrome?", a: "<i>FBN1</i> mutation (<i>Fibrillin-1</i>) on Chromosome 15." },
            { q: "Genetic defect in Achondroplasia?", a: "Constitutive activation of <i>FGFR3</i>." },
            { q: "Mode of inheritance for G6PD Deficiency?", a: "X-linked Recessive." },
            { q: "Mode of inheritance for Hemophilia A and B?", a: "X-linked Recessive." },
            { q: "What is Anticipation?", a: "Increased severity or earlier onset of disease in succeeding generations (common in trinucleotide repeats)." },
            { q: "\"Lyonization\" refers to what process?", a: "X-inactivation (formation of Barr bodies)." },
            { q: "The p arm of a chromosome refers to?", a: "The short arm (\"petite\")." },
            { q: "Function of Single-strand binding proteins (SSBPs)?", a: "Prevent <b>DNA</b> strands from reannealing during replication." },
            { q: "Role of Primase in DNA replication?", a: "Makes an RNA primer on which <b>DNA polymerase</b> III can initiate replication." },
            { q: "Which DNA polymerase in prokaryotes has 5' to 3' exonuclease activity?", a: "<b>DNA Polymerase</b> I (removes the RNA primer)." },
            { q: "Definition of a \"Silent\" mutation?", a: "Nucleotide change that codes for the same amino acid (often a \"<i>wobble</i>\" position)." },
            { q: "Definition of a \"Nonsense\" mutation?", a: "Nucleotide change resulting in a premature Stop codon (<i>UAG, UAA, UGA</i>)." },
            { q: "Effect of a \"Frameshift\" mutation?", a: "Insertion or deletion of nucleotides (not a multiple of 3), changing the reading frame." },
            { q: "Type of repair deficient in Xeroderma Pigmentosum?", a: "Nucleotide Excision Repair (inability to repair pyrimidine dimers from UV light)." },
            { q: "Type of repair deficient in Hereditary Nonpolyposis Colorectal Cancer (HNPCC/Lynch Syndrome)?", a: "Mismatch Repair." },
            { q: "Sequence of the \"Start\" codon?", a: "AUG (Codes for Methionine in eukaryotes; fMet in prokaryotes)." },
            { q: "Sequence of the three \"Stop\" codons?", a: "UAA, UAG, UGA (\"<i>U Are Away, U Are Gone, U Go Away</i>\")." },
            { q: "Function of the TATA box?", a: "Eukaryotic promoter sequence that binds RNA polymerase II and transcription factors." },
            { q: "Three post-transcriptional modifications of mRNA?", a: "5' Capping, 3' Polyadenylation (Poly-A tail), and Splicing of introns." },
            { q: "Function of the Spliceosome?", a: "Removes introns and joins exons; mediated by <i>snRNPs</i> (\"<i>snurps</i>\")." },
            { q: "Antibodies against snRNPs (Anti-Smith) are highly specific for?", a: "<b>Systemic Lupus Erythematosus</b> (<b>SLE</b>)." },
            { q: "What is Alternative Splicing?", a: "Producing different proteins from the same gene by including/excluding different exons." },
{ q: "Function of Aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase?", a: "Matches and attaches the correct amino acid to its tRNA (requires ATP)." },
            { q: "Inheritance of most enzyme deficiencies?", a: "Autosomal Recessive." },
            { q: "Inheritance of most structural protein deficiencies?", a: "Autosomal Dominant." },
            { q: "Genetic defect in Familial Hypercholesterolemia?", a: "Defective LDL receptor (Autosomal Dominant)." },
            { q: "Genetic defect in Hereditary Hemochromatosis?", a: "HFE gene mutation (usually C282Y) leading to <i>iron overload</i>." },
            { q: "Diagnosis for intellectual disability, \"fair\" skin/hair, and \"musty\" odor?", a: "<b>Phenylketonuria</b> (<b>PKU</b>)." },
            { q: "Inheritance of Hunter Syndrome vs. Hurler Syndrome?", a: "<b>Hunter Syndrome</b> is X-linked Recessive; <b>Hurler Syndrome</b> is Autosomal Recessive." },
            { q: "Function of the p53 gene?", a: "Tumor suppressor; induces <i>p21</i> and stalls cell cycle at G1/S to repair <b>DNA</b> or induce apoptosis." },
            { q: "Genetic condition associated with germline p53 mutation?", a: "<b>Li-Fraumeni Syndrome</b> (multiple early-onset cancers)." },
            { q: "Translocation associated with Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML)?", a: "t(9;22) — <i>The Philadelphia Chromosome</i> (<i>BCR-ABL</i> fusion)." },
            { q: "Translocation associated with Burkitt Lymphoma?", a: "t(8;14) — <i>c-myc</i> activation." },
            { q: "Translocation associated with Follicular Lymphoma?", a: "t(14;18) — <i>bcl-2</i> activation (inhibits apoptosis)." },
            { q: "Genetic defect in Neurofibromatosis Type 1?", a: "<b>NF1</b> gene on Chromosome 17." },
            { q: "Genetic defect in Neurofibromatosis Type 2?", a: "<b>NF2</b> gene on Chromosome 22 (\"22 for Type 2\")." },
            { q: "Function of the RB1 gene?", a: "Tumor suppressor that inhibits <i>E2F</i>; prevents G1 → S transition." },
            { q: "Definition of Uniparental Disomy?", a: "Offspring receives two copies of a chromosome from one parent and zero from the other." },
            { q: "What is Germline Mosaicism?", a: "Mutation occurs in a precursor egg or sperm cell, affecting some offspring but not the parent's somatic cells." },
            { q: "Definition of Variable Expressivity?", a: "Patients with the same genotype have varying degrees of phenotypic severity (e.g., <b>NF1</b>)." },
            { q: "Definition of Incomplete Penetrance?", a: "Not all individuals with a mutant genotype show the mutant phenotype." },
            { q: "Formula for the \"Carrier Frequency\" of an AR disease if the prevalence is 1/10,000?", a: "q^2 = 1/10,000 → q = 1/100. Carrier frequency (<i>2pq</i>) is approx 2 x 1/100 = 1/50." },
            { q: "Karyotype of Jacob Syndrome?", a: "47, XYY (presents with tall stature, acne, and learning disability)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of G6PD Deficiency inheritance?", a: "X-linked Recessive." },
            { q: "Genetics of Wilson Disease?", a: "<i>ATP7B</i> mutation on Chromosome 13 (Autosomal Recessive)." },
            { q: "Characteristics of Williams Syndrome?", a: "<i>elfin</i> facies, extreme friendliness with strangers, cardiovascular problems (supravalvular aortic stenosis)." },
            { q: "Purpose of a \"Western Blot\"?", a: "To detect specific proteins in a tissue sample or extract." },
            { q: "Definition of Polymorphism?", a: "A genetic variant that occurs in at least 1% of the population." },
            { q: "Clinical significance of the CYP2D6 polymorphism?", a: "Affects the metabolism of many drugs (e.g., Codeine, antidepressants); patients can be \"poor\" or \"ultra-rapid\" metabolizers." },
            { q: "Genetic test used to detect chromosomal imbalances (deletions/duplications) that are too small for a standard karyotype?", a: "Microarray (Comparative Genomic Hybridization)." },
            { q: "Genetic defect in Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome?", a: "Mutation in the <i>WASP</i> gene (X-linked Recessive); triad of Eczema, Thrombocytopenia, and Immunodeficiency." },
            { q: "Genetic defect in Bruton Agammaglobulinemia?", a: "Defect in Bruton Tyrosine Kinase (<i>BTK</i> gene) on the X chromosome." },
            { q: "Most common chromosomal cause of spontaneous abortion in the first trimester?", a: "Autosomal Trisomy (Trisomy 16 is the most common specifically, though rarely seen live-born)." },
            { q: "Chromosomal location of the \"Alpha-globin\" genes?", a: "Chromosome 16." },
            { q: "Chromosomal location of the \"Beta-globin\" genes?", a: "Chromosome 11." },
            { q: "Genetic cause of McCune-Albright Syndrome?", a: "G-protein signaling mutation (mosaicism); presents with cafe-au-lait spots, polyostotic fibrous dysplasia, and precocious puberty." },
            { q: "Function of Homeobox (Hox) genes?", a: "Code for transcription factors that guide the segmental organization of the embryo in a cranio-caudal direction." },
            { q: "Result of a mutation in a Hox gene?", a: "Appendages appearing in the wrong locations (<i>homeotic transformation</i>)." },
            { q: "Part of the cell cycle where \"p53\" and \"Rb\" primarily act?", a: "<i>G1</i> to S phase transition." },
            { q: "Cell type that remains in G0 (permanent)?", a: "Neurons, skeletal muscle, cardiac muscle, and RBCs." },
            { q: "Cell type that enters G1 from G0 only when stimulated (stable/quiescent)?", a: "Hepatocytes and Lymphocytes." },
            { q: "Definition of Genomic Imprinting?", a: "One allele is transcriptionally inactivated (silenced) during gametogenesis depending on the parent of origin." },
            { q: "Mechanism of MicroRNAs (miRNAs)?", a: "Small non-coding RNA molecules that post-transcriptionally regulate gene expression by targeting mRNA for degradation or translational repression." },
            { q: "Definition of Codominance?", a: "Both alleles are expressed in the phenotype of a heterozygote (e.g., Blood groups A, B, AB)." },
            { q: "Purpose of a \"Northern Blot\"?", a: "To detect specific sequences of RNA to study gene expression." },
            { q: "Purpose of a \"Western Blot\"?", a: "To detect specific proteins in a tissue sample or extract." },
            { q: "Chromosomal location of the \"Alpha-globin\" genes?", a: "Chromosome 16." },
            { q: "Chromosomal location of the \"Beta-globin\" genes?", a: "Chromosome 11." },
            { q: "Definition of \"Loss of Heterozygosity\"?", a: "When a patient inherits one mutant tumor suppressor gene and the second, normal allele is later mutated or lost (<i>Knudson</i>\u2019s \"<i>Two-Hit</i>\" Hypothesis)." }
          ]
        }
      ],
      'year2': [
        {
          topic: 'General Pharmacology',
          icon: 'pill',
          cards: [
            { q: "Definition of Bioavailability (<i>F</i>)?", a: "The fraction of administered drug that reaches systemic circulation unchanged." },
            { q: "Bioavailability of an intravenous (IV) drug?", a: "100% (<i>F</i> = 1)." },
            { q: "Formula for Volume of Distribution (<i>V</i><sub>d</sub>)?", a: "<i>V</i><sub>d</sub> = (amount of drug in the body) / (plasma drug concentration)." },
            { q: "Characteristics of drugs with a low <i>V</i><sub>d</sub>?", a: "Large/charged molecules; often plasma protein-bound (remains in vascular compartment)." },
            { q: "Characteristics of drugs with a high <i>V</i><sub>d</sub>?", a: "Small lipophilic molecules; distributed into tissues and fat." },
            { q: "Formula for Clearance (<i>CL</i>)?", a: "<i>CL</i> = (0.693 &times; <i>V</i><sub>d</sub>) / <i>t</i><sub>1/2</sub> or <i>CL</i> = (rate of elimination) / (plasma concentration)." },
            { q: "Definition of Half-life (<i>t</i><sub>1/2</sub>)?", a: "The time required to reduce the plasma concentration of a drug by 50%." },
            { q: "How many half-lives are required to reach steady-state concentration (<i>C</i><sub>ss</sub>)?", a: "Approximately 4 to 5 half-lives." },
            { q: "Formula for Loading Dose (<i>LD</i>)?", a: "<i>LD</i> = (<i>C</i><sub>p</sub> &times; <i>V</i><sub>d</sub>) / <i>F</i> (<i>C</i><sub>p</sub> = target plasma concentration)." },
            { q: "Formula for Maintenance Dose (<i>MD</i>)?", a: "<i>MD</i> = (<i>C</i><sub>p</sub> &times; <i>CL</i> &times; &tau;) / <i>F</i> (&tau; = dosage interval)." },
            { q: "Primary site of drug metabolism?", a: "<b>Liver.</b>" },
            { q: "Primary site of drug excretion?", a: "<b>Kidney.</b>" },
            { q: "Zero-order elimination definition?", a: "A constant <b>amount</b> of drug is eliminated per unit time (independent of concentration)." },
            { q: "Examples of drugs with Zero-order elimination?", a: "<b>PEA:</b> <b>P</b>henytoin, <b>E</b>thanol, <b>A</b>spirin (at high doses)." },
            { q: "First-order elimination definition?", a: "A constant <b>fraction</b> of drug is eliminated per unit time (proportional to concentration)." },
            { q: "Which elimination type shows a linear decrease on a plasma concentration vs. time graph?", a: "Zero-order." },
            { q: "Effect of urinary acidification on weak base excretion?", a: "Increases excretion (traps the drug in its ionized, polar form)." },
            { q: "Agent used to acidify urine?", a: "Ammonium chloride (NH<sub>4</sub>Cl)." },
            { q: "Effect of urinary alkalinization on weak acid (e.g., Aspirin) excretion?", a: "Increases excretion." },
            { q: "Agent used to alkalinize urine?", a: "Sodium bicarbonate (NaHCO<sub>3</sub>)." },
            { q: "Purpose of Phase I metabolism?", a: "Reduction, oxidation, hydrolysis via <b>Cytochrome P450</b> to yield slightly polar, water-soluble metabolites." },
            { q: "Key enzyme system in Phase I metabolism?", a: "Cytochrome P450 (CYP450)." },
            { q: "Purpose of Phase II metabolism?", a: "Conjugation (Glucuronidation, Acetylation, Sulfonation) to yield very polar, inactive metabolites." },
            { q: "Phase II reaction that Geriatric patients retain longest?", a: "Conjugation (<b>Glucuronidation</b>)." },
            { q: "Impact of 'Fast Acetylators' vs 'Slow Acetylators'?", a: "Slow acetylators have increased side effects from drugs like <b>Isoniazid</b> or <b>Procainamide</b> due to decreased metabolism." },
            { q: "Definition of a Prodrug?", a: "An inactive compound that is metabolized into a pharmacologically active drug." },
            { q: "Definition of Efficacy (<i>V</i><sub>max</sub>)?", a: "The maximal effect a drug can produce regardless of dose." },
            { q: "Definition of Potency (<i>EC</i><sub>50</sub>)?", a: "The amount of drug needed to produce a given effect (usually 50% of peak)." },
            { q: "Effect of a Competitive Antagonist on <i>V</i><sub>max</sub> and <i>K</i><sub>m</sub>?", a: "No change in <i>V</i><sub>max</sub>; Increases <i>K</i><sub>m</sub> (decreases potency)." },
            { q: "Effect of a Non-competitive Antagonist on <i>V</i><sub>max</sub> and <i>K</i><sub>m</sub>?", a: "Decreases <i>V</i><sub>max</sub>; No change in <i>K</i><sub>m</sub>." },
            { q: "Definition of an Irreversible Antagonist?", a: "Binds covalently to the receptor; cannot be displaced by increasing agonist concentration." },
            { q: "Definition of a Partial Agonist?", a: "Acts at the same site as a full agonist but with lower maximal efficacy." },
            { q: "Therapeutic Index (<i>TI</i>) formula?", a: "<i>TI</i> = <i>TD</i><sub>50</sub> / <i>ED</i><sub>50</sub> (Toxic dose / Effective dose)." },
            { q: "Clinical significance of a low <i>TI</i>?", a: "Narrow window between effective and toxic dose; requires frequent monitoring (e.g., Warfarin, Digoxin, Lithium)." },
            { q: "Common CYP450 Inducers?", a: "<b>GP Craves Rifampin St. Barbs:</b> Griseofulvin, Phenytoin, Carbamazepine, Rifampin, St. John’s Wort, Barbiturates, Chronic alcohol." },
            { q: "Common CYP450 Inhibitors?", a: "<b>SICK GARA:</b> Sodium Valproate, Isoniazid, Cimetidine, Ketoconazole, Erythromycin, Grapefruit juice, Amiodarone, Ritonavir." },
            { q: "Effect of CYP450 Inhibitors on co-administered drugs?", a: "Decreases metabolism, leading to increased drug levels and potential toxicity." },
            { q: "Effect of CYP450 Inducers on co-administered drugs?", a: "Increases metabolism, leading to increased drug levels and subtherapeutic effects." },
            { q: "Primary neurotransmitter for Pre-ganglionic Autonomic fibers?", a: "Acetylcholine (ACh)." },
            { q: "Receptor type at Autonomic Ganglia?", a: "Nicotinic (N<sub>n</sub>)." },
            { q: "Neurotransmitter for Parasympathetic Post-ganglionic fibers?", a: "Acetylcholine (ACh)." },
            { q: "Neurotransmitter for Sympathetic Post-ganglionic fibers (except sweat glands)?", a: "Norepinephrine (NE)." },
            { q: "Receptor type on skeletal muscle motor endplate?", a: "Nicotinic (N<sub>m</sub>)." },
            { q: "Rate-limiting step in Catecholamine synthesis?", a: "Tyrosine hydroxylase (converts Tyrosine to DOPA)." },
            { q: "Enzyme that degrades NE in the presynaptic nerve terminal?", a: "Monoamine Oxidase (MAO)." },
            { q: "Antidote for Acetaminophen toxicity?", a: "<b>N-acetylcysteine</b> (restores Glutathione)." },
            { q: "Antidote for Benzodiazepine overdose?", a: "Flumazenil." },
            { q: "Antidote for Opioid overdose?", a: "Naloxone." },
            { q: "Antidote for Heparin toxicity?", a: "Protamine sulfate." },
            { q: "Antidote for Warfarin toxicity (immediate)?", a: "Fresh Frozen Plasma (FFP) or Prothrombin Complex Concentrate (PCC)." },
            { q: "Antidote for Carbon Monoxide poisoning?", a: "100% O<sub>2</sub> or Hyperbaric O<sub>2</sub>." },
            { q: "Antidote for Cyanide poisoning?", a: "Nitrite + Sodium Thiosulfate, or Hydroxocobalamin." },
            { q: "Antidote for Lead poisoning?", a: "EDTA, Dimercaprol, Succimer (in children)." },
            { q: "Antidote for Organophosphate (nerve gas) poisoning?", a: "Atropine + Pralidoxime (2-PAM)." },
            { q: "Antidote for Iron toxicity?", a: "Deferoxamine." },
            { q: "Antidote for Methanol or Ethylene Glycol poisoning?", a: "Fomepizole (inhibits Alcohol Dehydrogenase)." },
            { q: "Antidote for Digoxin toxicity?", a: "Digoxin-specific Fab fragments." },
            { q: "Drug associated with Gray Baby Syndrome?", a: "Chloramphenicol." },
            { q: "Drug associated with Red Man Syndrome?", a: "Vancomycin." },
            { q: "Drug associated with Disulfiram-like reactions?", a: "Metronidazole, certain Cephalosporins, Griseofulvin." },
            { q: "Classic drugs causing Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)?", a: "Anticonvulsants (Lamotrigine, Phenytoin), Sulfonamides, Penicillin, Allopurinol." },
            { q: "Drug associated with Tendon Rupture?", a: "Fluoroquinolones (e.g., Ciprofloxacin)." },
            { q: "Drug associated with Ototoxicity and Nephrotoxicity?", a: "Aminoglycosides, Cisplatin, Loop diuretics." },
            { q: "Drug class causing 'Cinchonism' (tinnitus, headache)?", a: "Quinidine, Quinine." },
            { q: "Drug associated with Gingival Hyperplasia?", a: "Phenytoin, Calcium Channel Blockers (Verapamil, Nifedipine), Cyclosporine." },
            { q: "Common drugs causing Drug-Induced Lupus?", a: "<b>SHIPPE:</b> Sulfa drugs, Hydralazine, Isoniazid, Procainamide, Phenytoin, Etanercept." },
            { q: "G<sub>q</sub> receptor mechanism?", a: "Activates Phospholipase C &rarr; &uarr; IP<sub>3</sub>, DAG, Ca<sup>2+</sup>." },
            { q: "G<sub>s</sub> receptor mechanism?", a: "Activates Adenylate Cyclase &rarr; &uarr; cAMP &rarr; Protein Kinase A." },
            { q: "G<sub>i</sub> receptor mechanism?", a: "Inhibits Adenylate Cyclase &rarr; &darr; cAMP." },
            { q: "Receptors using G<sub>q</sub> pathway?", a: "<b>HAV 1 M&M:</b> H<sub>1</sub>, &alpha;<sub>1</sub>, V<sub>1</sub>, M<sub>1</sub>, M<sub>3</sub>." },
            { q: "Receptors using G<sub>s</sub> pathway?", a: "&beta;<sub>1</sub>, &beta;<sub>2</sub>, &beta;<sub>3</sub>, D<sub>1</sub>, V<sub>2</sub>, H<sub>2</sub>." },
            { q: "Receptors using G<sub>i</sub> pathway?", a: "<b>MAD 2s:</b> M<sub>2</sub>, &alpha;<sub>2</sub>, D<sub>2</sub>." },
            { q: "FDA Pregnancy Category X definition?", a: "Studies demonstrate fetal abnormalities; risks clearly outweigh benefits." },
            { q: "Phase I Clinical Trial focus?", a: "Safety and Pharmacokinetics (Small group of healthy volunteers)." },
            { q: "Phase II Clinical Trial focus?", a: "Efficacy and Optimal Dosing (Small group of patients with disease)." },
            { q: "Phase III Clinical Trial focus?", a: "Comparison to Standard of Care (Large group of patients)." },
            { q: "Phase IV Clinical Trial focus?", a: "Post-marketing surveillance for rare side effects." },
            { q: "Primary effect of &alpha;<sub>1</sub> receptor activation?", a: "Vasoconstriction, increased peripheral resistance, mydriasis, and increased internal urethral sphincter tone." },
            { q: "Primary effect of &alpha;<sub>2</sub> receptor activation?", a: "Inhibition of NE, ACh, and insulin release; decreased aqueous humor production." },
            { q: "Primary effect of &beta;<sub>1</sub> receptor activation?", a: "Increased heart rate (chronotropy), increased contractility (inotropy), and increased renin release." },
            { q: "Primary effect of &beta;<sub>2</sub> receptor activation?", a: "Vasodilation, bronchodilation, increased lipolysis, and decreased uterine tone (tocolysis)." },
            { q: "Primary effect of &beta;<sub>3</sub> receptor activation?", a: "Increased lipolysis and thermogenesis; relaxation of the detrusor muscle in the bladder." },
            { q: "Effect of &alpha;<sub>1</sub> activation on the eye?", a: "Contraction of the pupillary dilator muscle (Mydriasis)." },
            { q: "Dominant receptor on the heart?", a: "&beta;<sub>1</sub>." },
            { q: "Dominant receptor on bronchial smooth muscle?", a: "&beta;<sub>2</sub>." },
            { q: "Location of M<sub>1</sub> receptors?", a: "CNS and enteric nervous system." },
            { q: "Location and effect of M<sub>2</sub> receptors?", a: "Heart (atria/nodes); decreases heart rate and atrial contractility." },
            { q: "Effect of M<sub>3</sub> activation on the eye?", a: "Contraction of the pupillary sphincter (Miosis) and ciliary muscle (Accommodation)." },
            { q: "Effect of M<sub>3</sub> activation on the GI/GU tract?", a: "Increased peristalsis, increased secretions, and contraction of the detrusor muscle (voiding)." },
            { q: "Effect of M<sub>3</sub> activation on blood vessels?", a: "Vasodilation via Nitric Oxide (NO) release (though vessels lack PSNS innervation)." },
            { q: "Result of Nicotinic (N<sub>n</sub>) receptor stimulation at the adrenal medulla?", a: "Release of Epinephrine and Norepinephrine into the blood." },
            { q: "Signal pathway for &alpha;<sub>1</sub>, &alpha;<sub>2</sub>, &beta;<sub>1</sub>, &beta;<sub>2</sub> (in order)?", a: "<i>q, i, s, s</i> (QISS)." },
            { q: "Signal pathway for M<sub>1</sub>, M<sub>2</sub>, M<sub>3</sub> (in order)?", a: "<i>q, i, q</i> (QIQ)." },
            { q: "Receptors that increase cAMP levels?", a: "&beta;<sub>1</sub>, &beta;<sub>2</sub>, &beta;<sub>3</sub>, D<sub>1</sub>, V<sub>2</sub>, H<sub>2</sub>." },
            { q: "Receptors that decrease cAMP levels?", a: "M<sub>2</sub>, &alpha;<sub>2</sub>, D<sub>2</sub>." },
            { q: "Receptors that increase IP<sub>3</sub>/DAG (Ca<sup>2+</sup>)?", a: "&alpha;<sub>1</sub>, M<sub>1</sub>, M<sub>3</sub>, H<sub>1</sub>, V<sub>1</sub>." },
            { q: "Epinephrine's effect at low doses?", a: "Primarily &beta; effects (vasodilation, bronchodilation)." },
            { q: "Epinephrine's effect at high doses?", a: "Primarily &alpha; effects (vasoconstriction)." },
            { q: "What is 'Epinephrine Reversal'?", a: "Administration of an &alpha;-blocker before Epinephrine, causing a net decrease in BP due to unopposed &beta;<sub>2</sub> action." },
            { q: "Primary use of Isoproterenol?", a: "Electrophysiologic evaluation of tachyarrhythmias (pure &beta;<sub>1</sub> = &beta;<sub>2</sub> agonist)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Cocaine as a sympathomimetic?", a: "Inhibits NET (Norepinephrine Transporter), preventing reuptake of NE into the presynaptic neuron." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Amphetamines?", a: "Displaces stored NE from vesicles into the synapse via NET reversal." },
            { q: "Why are &beta;-blockers contraindicated in Cocaine use?", a: "Leads to 'unopposed &alpha;<sub>1</sub> stimulation,' causing severe hypertensive crisis." },
            { q: "Selective &beta;<sub>1</sub> blockers (AmeBA)?", a: "Atenolol, Metoprolol, Esmolol, Bisoprolol, Acebutolol." },
            { q: "Non-selective &alpha; and &beta; blockers?", a: "Carvedilol and Labetalol." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Neostigmine?", a: "Reversible AChE (Acetylcholinesterase) inhibitor; does not cross the BBB." },
            { q: "Clinical use of Edrophonium?", a: "Historically used to diagnose Myasthenia Gravis (Tensilon test)." },
            { q: "Symptoms of Organophosphate poisoning?", a: "<b>DUMBBELLS:</b> Diarrhea, Urination, Miosis, Bronchospasm, Bradycardia, Emesis, Lacrimation, Lethargy, Salivation." },
            { q: "Treatment for Atropine overdose?", a: "Physostigmine (crosses the BBB)." },
            { q: "Contraindications for Atropine?", a: "Acute Angle-Closure Glaucoma, BPH, Hyperthermia." },
            { q: "Calculation for Drug Bioavailability (<i>F</i>)?", a: "<i>F</i> = AUC<sub>oral</sub> / AUC<sub>IV</sub> (AUC = Area Under the Curve)." },
            { q: "Relationship between <i>V</i><sub>d</sub> and Half-life?", a: "As <i>V</i><sub>d</sub> increases, half-life (<i>t</i><sub>1/2</sub>) increases." },
            { q: "Relationship between Clearance (<i>CL</i>) and Half-life?", a: "As <i>CL</i> increases, half-life (<i>t</i><sub>1/2</sub>) decreases." },
            { q: "Loading dose correction for renal/hepatic failure?", a: "Loading dose remains the same (it depends on <i>V</i><sub>d</sub>, not <i>CL</i>)." },
            { q: "Maintenance dose correction for renal/hepatic failure?", a: "Maintenance dose must be decreased (it depends on <i>CL</i>)." },
            { q: "How many half-lives to eliminate &gt;90% of a drug?", a: "3.3 half-lives (90%) to 4 half-lives (93.75%)." },
            { q: "Drug causing Cyanide toxicity via metabolism?", a: "Nitroprusside (at high doses or prolonged use)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Nitrites in treating Cyanide poisoning?", a: "Oxidizes Hemoglobin to Methemoglobin, which has a high affinity for Cyanide (sequesters it)." },
            { q: "Side effect of Thiazide diuretics on electrolytes?", a: "<b>HyperGLUC:</b> Hyperglycemia, Hyperlipidemia, Hyperuricemia, Hypercalcemia." },
            { q: "Drug class associated with 'Flash Pulmonary Edema'?", a: "Loop Diuretics (often used to treat it, but high doses of NSAIDs can worsen the underlying failure)." },
            { q: "Side effect of ACE inhibitors?", a: "<b>CATCHH:</b> Cough, Angioedema, Teratogen, Creatinine increase, Hyperkalemia, Hypotension." },
            { q: "Definition of Synergism?", a: "The combined effect of two drugs is greater than the sum of their individual effects (1+1=3)." },
            { q: "Definition of Potentiation?", a: "A drug with no effect on its own increases the effect of another drug (0+1=2)." },
            { q: "Definition of Tachyphylaxis?", a: "Acute, rapid decrease in drug response after repeated administration (e.g., Nitroglycerin, MDMA)." },
            { q: "How to prevent Nitroglycerin tachyphylaxis?", a: "Provide a 'drug-free interval' (typically 8&ndash;12 hours at night)." },
            { q: "Effect of Thalidomide on the fetus?", a: "Phocomelia (limb defects)." },
            { q: "Effect of Tetracyclines on the fetus?", a: "Discolored teeth and inhibited bone growth." },
            { q: "Effect of Aminoglycosides on the fetus?", a: "Ototoxicity (CN VIII damage)." },
            { q: "Effect of Isotretinoin (Vitamin A) on the fetus?", a: "Severe CNS, craniofacial, and cardiac defects." },
            { q: "Effect of Warfarin on the fetus?", a: "Bone/cartilage deformities and fetal hemorrhage (heparin is preferred in pregnancy)." },
            { q: "Up-regulation occurs when?", a: "Chronic use of an antagonist (body creates more receptors to compensate)." },
            { q: "Down-regulation occurs when?", a: "Chronic use of an agonist (body removes receptors to avoid overstimulation)." },
            { q: "What happens to the Dose-Response curve during Desensitization?", a: "It shifts to the right (higher dose needed for same effect)." },
            { q: "Spare Receptors definition?", a: "Receptors that exist in excess of those required to produce a maximal response." },
            { q: "How do you detect Spare Receptors?", a: "If <i>EC</i><sub>50</sub> &lt; <i>K</i><sub>d</sub> (the concentration for half-maximal effect is less than the concentration for half-maximal binding)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Passive Diffusion?", a: "Movement of drug down its concentration gradient; no energy or carrier required." },
            { q: "Type of transport that requires ATP and moves against a gradient?", a: "Active Transport." },
            { q: "Type of transport that uses a carrier but no energy (down-gradient)?", a: "Facilitated Diffusion." },
            { q: "Property of drugs that can cross the Blood-Brain Barrier (BBB)?", a: "Small, non-polar, and highly lipid-soluble." },
            { q: "Primary protein responsible for drug efflux (pumping drugs out of cells)?", a: "P-glycoprotein (MDR1)." },
            { q: "Consequence of P-glycoprotein expression in cancer cells?", a: "Multi-drug resistance (pumping chemotherapeutic agents out of the cell)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of the Insulin receptor?", a: "Receptor Tyrosine Kinase (RTK) &rarr; MAP-kinase pathway." },
            { q: "Mechanism of the ANP/BNP receptors?", a: "Membrane-bound Guanylate Cyclase &rarr; &uarr; cGMP &rarr; Protein Kinase G (PKG)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Nitric Oxide (NO)?", a: "Diffuses into cell &rarr; Soluble Guanylate Cyclase &rarr; &uarr; cGMP." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Steroid/Thyroid hormone receptors?", a: "Intracellular/Nuclear receptors that act as transcription factors to alter gene expression." },
            { q: "Receptors that utilize the JAK/STAT pathway?", a: "<b>PIGGLy:</b> Prolactin, Immunomodulators, Growth hormone, G-CSF, Leptin." },
            { q: "Definition of Dissociation Constant (<i>K</i><sub>d</sub>)?", a: "The concentration of drug at which 50% of receptors are occupied." },
            { q: "In a Lineweaver-Burk plot, what does the x-intercept represent?", a: "-1/<i>K</i><sub>m</sub>." },
            { q: "In a Lineweaver-Burk plot, what does the y-intercept represent?", a: "1/<i>V</i><sub>max</sub>." },
            { q: "Effect of a Non-competitive inhibitor on a Lineweaver-Burk plot?", a: "x-intercept is unchanged; y-intercept shifts upward (<i>V</i><sub>max</sub> decreases)." },
            { q: "Effect of a Competitive inhibitor on a Lineweaver-Burk plot?", a: "y-intercept is unchanged; x-intercept shifts right/closer to zero (<i>K</i><sub>m</sub> increases)." },
            { q: "Quantal Dose-Response curve vs. Graded Dose-Response curve?", a: "Quantal = 'All or none' response; Graded = Incremental response (e.g., BP change)." },
            { q: "Antidote for Arsenic poisoning?", a: "Dimercaprol or Succimer." },
            { q: "Antidote for Copper toxicity (Wilson’s Disease)?", a: "Penicillamine or Trientine." },
            { q: "Antidote for &beta;-blocker overdose?", a: "Glucagon (increases cAMP via a non-adrenergic pathway)." },
            { q: "Antidote for Theophylline toxicity?", a: "&beta;-blockers (to treat tachyarrhythmias) and Activated Charcoal." },
            { q: "Treatment for Salicylate (Aspirin) overdose?", a: "Sodium Bicarbonate (alkalinizes urine to trap the acid)." },
            { q: "Antidote for Amphetamine overdose?", a: "Ammonium Chloride (acidifies urine to trap the base)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Carbon Monoxide toxicity?", a: "Competitive inhibition of O<sub>2</sub> binding to hemoglobin + left shift of the dissociation curve." },
            { q: "Drug causing 'Cushing-like' symptoms?", a: "Corticosteroids (Prednisone)." },
            { q: "Drug causing Diabetes Insipidus?", a: "Lithium or Demeclocycline." },
            { q: "Drug causing Fanconi Syndrome?", a: "Expired Tetracyclines or Ifosfamide." },
            { q: "Drug causing Hemorrhagic Cystitis?", a: "Cyclophosphamide." },
            { q: "Agent used to prevent Cyclophosphamide-induced Hemorrhagic Cystitis?", a: "Mesna." },
            { q: "Drug causing Grayish skin discoloration (Blue-man syndrome)?", a: "Amiodarone." },
            { q: "Drug class causing 'First-dose Syncope'?", a: "&alpha;<sub>1</sub>-blockers (e.g., Prazosin)." },
            { q: "Drug causing Pulmonary Fibrosis?", a: "<b>BAMB:</b> Bleomycin, Amiodarone, Methotrexate, Busulfan." },
            { q: "Definition of the Therapeutic Window?", a: "The range of drug doses which can treat disease effectively without having toxic effects." },
            { q: "Formula for the Extraction Ratio (<i>ER</i>)?", a: "<i>ER</i> = (<i>C</i><sub>in</sub> - <i>C</i><sub>out</sub>) / <i>C</i><sub>in</sub>." },
            { q: "Difference between Redistribution and Elimination?", a: "Redistribution = drug moving from organs to fat/muscle; Elimination = metabolism/excretion." },
            { q: "Reason for the short duration of action of Thiopental?", a: "Rapid redistribution from the brain to skeletal muscle and fat." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Clonidine?", a: "Centrally acting &alpha;<sub>2</sub>-agonist; decreases sympathetic outflow." },
            { q: "Clinical use of Methyldopa?", a: "Hypertension in pregnancy." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Metyrosine?", a: "Inhibits Tyrosine Hydroxylase (stops catecholamine synthesis); used for Pheochromocytoma." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Reserpine?", a: "Inhibits VMAT (blocks storage of NE/Dopamine in vesicles)." },
            { q: "Which Autonomic drug causes 'Pupillary Block' or worsens Glaucoma?", a: "Epinephrine (due to mydriasis; only in closed-angle)." },
            { q: "Drug of choice for hypertensive emergency?", a: "Nitroprusside, Fenoldopam, or Labetalol." },
            { q: "Most common Phase II reaction?", a: "Glucuronidation." },
            { q: "Why are neonates susceptible to Chloramphenicol toxicity?", a: "They lack UDP-glucuronosyltransferase (cannot perform Glucuronidation)." },
            { q: "Effect of Sulfonamides on Bilirubin in neonates?", a: "Displacement of bilirubin from albumin &rarr; Kernicterus." },
            { q: "Enzyme inhibited by Disulfiram?", a: "Acetaldehyde dehydrogenase." },
            { q: "Henderson-Hasselbalch Relationship?", a: "pH - p<i>K</i><sub>a</sub> = log([non-protonated] / [protonated])." },
            { q: "Formula for Volume of Distribution (<i>V</i><sub>d</sub>) involving body weight?", a: "<i>V</i><sub>d</sub> = <i>V</i><sub>area</sub> &times; Weight (often expressed as L/kg)." },
            { q: "Clearance (<i>CL</i>) in terms of Renal Blood Flow?", a: "<i>CL</i> = Extraction Ratio &times; Renal Blood Flow." },
            { q: "Consequence of Succinylcholine administration in a patient with Pseudocholinesterase deficiency?", a: "Prolonged apnea/paralysis (inability to degrade the drug)." },
            { q: "Risk of using Primaquine or Rasburicase in a patient with G6PD deficiency?", a: "Hemolytic anemia (oxidative stress)." },
            { q: "Genetic marker associated with hypersensitivity to Abacavir?", a: "HLA-B*5701." },
            { q: "Genetic marker associated with SJS risk from Carbamazepine in Asian populations?", a: "HLA-B*1502." },
            { q: "Effect of CYP2D6 'Ultra-rapid metabolism' on Codeine?", a: "Increased conversion to Morphine, leading to potential opioid toxicity." },
            { q: "Consequence of Thiopurine S-methyltransferase (TPMT) deficiency?", a: "Increased toxicity when treated with 6-Mercaptopurine or Azathioprine." },
            { q: "Mechanism of 'Red Wine/Cheese Effect' with MAO Inhibitors?", a: "Tyramine displaces stored NE, causing a hypertensive crisis." },
            { q: "Drug combination causing 'Serotonin Syndrome'?", a: "Combination of SSRIs, SNRIs, MAOIs, TCAs, Tramadol, or Triptans." },
            { q: "Physical exam findings in Serotonin Syndrome?", a: "Hyperreflexia, clonus, tremors, and autonomic instability." },
            { q: "Physical exam findings in Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome (NMS)?", a: "'Lead-pipe' rigidity, hyperthermia, and elevated Creatine Kinase (CK)." },
            { q: "Treatment for Malignant Hyperthermia?", a: "Dantrolene (blocks RyR1 Calcium release)." },
            { q: "Classic presentation of Lead poisoning in children?", a: "Abdominal pain, constipation, and cognitive impairment (encephalopathy)." },
            { q: "Classic presentation of Lead poisoning in adults?", a: "Foot drop/wrist drop (peripheral neuropathy), nephropathy, and 'Lead lines' on gums." },
            { q: "Microscopic finding in Lead poisoning (Blood smear)?", a: "Basophilic stippling." },
            { q: "Source of Mercury poisoning and its primary symptom?", a: "Seafood (methylmercury); results in ataxia, paresthesias, and visual field loss." },
            { q: "Classic breath odor in Arsenic poisoning?", a: "Garlic breath." },
            { q: "EKG finding in Tricyclic Antidepressant (TCA) overdose?", a: "QRS prolongation (due to Sodium channel blockade)." },
            { q: "Treatment for QRS prolongation in TCA overdose?", a: "Sodium Bicarbonate (increases extracellular Na<sup>+</sup> to overcome blockade)." },
            { q: "Catecholamine that increases Systolic BP but decreases Diastolic BP?", a: "Epinephrine (low-dose) or Isoproterenol." },
            { q: "Catecholamine that increases Systolic BP, Diastolic BP, and causes Reflex Bradycardia?", a: "Norepinephrine (&alpha;<sub>1</sub> &gt; &alpha;<sub>2</sub> &gt; &beta;<sub>1</sub>)." },
            { q: "Reflex response to a pure &alpha;<sub>1</sub> agonist (e.g., Phenylephrine)?", a: "Increase in BP leading to a decrease in Heart Rate (Reflex Bradycardia)." },
            { q: "Reflex response to a pure &beta;<sub>2</sub> agonist (e.g., Albuterol)?", a: "Decrease in BP leading to an increase in Heart Rate (Reflex Tachycardia)." },
            { q: "Definition of an 'Orphan Drug'?", a: "A drug developed to treat a rare medical condition (affecting &lt;200,000 people in the US)." },
            { q: "Definition of 'Compassionate Use'?", a: "Providing an experimental drug to a patient with a life-threatening illness outside of a clinical trial." },
            { q: "What is the 'Double-Blind' study design?", a: "Neither the patient nor the researcher knows who is receiving the treatment vs. the placebo." },
            { q: "Primary goal of the FDA?", a: "To ensure the safety, efficacy, and security of drugs, biological products, and medical devices." },
            { q: "Effect of gastric emptying rate on drug absorption?", a: "Faster emptying generally increases the rate of absorption." },
            { q: "What is 'Enterohepatic Circulation'?", a: "Drugs excreted in bile are reabsorbed from the intestine back into the portal blood, prolonging their action." },
            { q: "Drug that classically undergoes significant First-Pass metabolism?", a: "Nitroglycerin (given sublingually to avoid it)." },
            { q: "Primary plasma protein that binds Acidic drugs?", a: "Albumin." },
            { q: "Primary plasma protein that binds Basic drugs?", a: "&alpha;<sub>1</sub>-acid glycoprotein." },
            { q: "Mechanism of cellular injury in Acetaminophen toxicity?", a: "Depletion of Glutathione leading to high levels of the toxic metabolite NAPQI." },
            { q: "How do NSAIDs decrease the efficacy of Loop Diuretics?", a: "NSAIDs inhibit Prostaglandin synthesis which is required to support the action of diuretics." },
            { q: "What is 'Physiologic Antagonism'?", a: "Two agonists acting at different receptors to produce opposite effects (e.g., Histamine vs. Epinephrine)." },
            { q: "Drug class that causes 'Redistribution' as its primary mechanism of action termination?", a: "Highly lipid-soluble IV anesthetics (e.g., Thiopental, Propofol)." },
            { q: "If a drug has a half-life of 10 hours, how much remains after 30 hours?", a: "12.5% (after 3 half-lives)." },
            { q: "Formula for Renal Clearance (<i>CL</i><sub>r</sub>)?", a: "<i>CL</i><sub>r</sub> = (<i>U</i> &times; <i>V</i>) / <i>P</i> (<i>U</i> = urine conc, <i>V</i> = urine flow rate, <i>P</i> = plasma conc)." },
            { q: "Formula for <i>V</i><sub>d</sub> if 500mg is given and plasma concentration is 5mg/L?", a: "100L (500 / 5)." },
            { q: "Definition of an Inverse Agonist?", a: "A ligand that binds to the same receptor as an agonist but induces a response opposite to it." },
            { q: "Concept of 'Constitutive Activity'?", a: "The ability of a receptor to exert an effect even in the absence of a bound ligand." },
            { q: "Definition of the 'Ceiling Effect'?", a: "The point at which increasing the dose of a drug no longer increases the therapeutic effect." },
            { q: "Intrinsic Activity (&alpha;) value for a Full Agonist?", a: "&alpha; = 1." },
            { q: "Intrinsic Activity (&alpha;) value for a Competitive Antagonist?", a: "&alpha; = 0." },
            { q: "Definition of 'Volume of Distribution at Steady State' (<i>V</i><sub>ss</sub>)?", a: "The volume when the rate of drug entry into circulation equals the rate of elimination." },
            { q: "Definition of 'Clearance' in terms of Extraction Ratio (<i>ER</i>)?", a: "<i>CL</i> = <i>Q</i> &times; <i>ER</i> (where <i>Q</i> is blood flow)." },
            { q: "Function of Arrestin proteins?", a: "Mediate receptor desensitization and internalization of GPCRs." },
            { q: "Definition of 'Bioequivalence'?", a: "Two drug products show comparable bioavailability (rate and extent) at the same dose." },
            { q: "What is a 'Black Box Warning'?", a: "Strict warning in labeling for evidence of a serious hazard." },
            { q: "Definition of an 'Idiosyncratic Drug Reaction'?", a: "An unusual drug response, often genetically determined, unrelated to known mechanism." },
            { q: "What are 'Auto-receptors'?", a: "Receptors on the presynaptic terminal that respond to the neurotransmitter released by that same terminal (negative feedback)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of 'Botulinum Toxin'?", a: "Cleaves SNARE proteins, preventing fusion of ACh vesicles (inhibits release)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of 'Latrotoxin' (Black Widow Venom)?", a: "Triggers massive, explosive release of Acetylcholine." },
            { q: "Definition of 'Therapeutic Index' (<i>TI</i>) vs. 'Certain Safety Factor' (<i>CSF</i>)?", a: "<i>TI</i> = <i>TD</i><sub>50</sub>/<i>ED</i><sub>50</sub>; <i>CSF</i> = <i>LD</i><sub>1</sub>/<i>ED</i><sub>99</sub> (CSF is more conservative)." },
            { q: "Drug combination causing severe hypotension via cGMP synergy?", a: "Sildenafil + Nitrates." }
          ]
        },
        {
          topic: 'Biostatistics and Epidemiology',
          icon: 'activity',
          cards: [
            { q: "Purpose of a Case-Control study?", a: "To compare a group with a disease to a group without disease to look for prior exposures; it is retrospective." },
            { q: "Defining feature of a Cohort study?", a: "Groups are defined by exposure status and followed over time to see who develops the disease." },
            { q: "Primary measure of association in Case-Control studies?", a: "Odds Ratio (OR)." },
            { q: "Primary measure of association in Cohort studies?", a: "Relative Risk (RR)." },
            { q: "Study type that provides a 'snapshot' of prevalence?", a: "Cross-sectional study." },
            { q: "Only study design that can prove causality?", a: "Randomized Clinical Trial (RCT) when properly randomized and controlled." },
            { q: "Definition of an Ecological Study?", a: "Data are analyzed at the population level rather than the individual level." },
            { q: "What is the 'Ecological Fallacy'?", a: "Erroneously applying population-level data to specific individuals." },
            { q: "Phase I Clinical Trial focus?", a: "Safety, toxicity, and pharmacokinetics in a small group of healthy volunteers." },
            { q: "Phase II Clinical Trial focus?", a: "Efficacy, optimal dosing, and side effects in a small group of patients with the disease." },
            { q: "Phase III Clinical Trial focus?", a: "Comparison of the new treatment against the current standard of care." },
            { q: "Phase IV Clinical Trial focus?", a: "Post-marketing surveillance for rare or long-term adverse effects." },
            { q: "Definition of Incidence?", a: "Number of new cases in a population over a specific period of time." },
            { q: "Definition of Prevalence?", a: "Total number of existing cases (new + old) in a population at a specific point in time." },
            { q: "Formula for Prevalence in terms of Incidence?", a: "Prevalence &asymp; Incidence &times; Duration." },
            { q: "Impact of a new drug that prolongs life but doesn't cure on prevalence?", a: "Prevalence increases." },
            { q: "Impact of a highly effective vaccine on incidence?", a: "Incidence decreases." },
            { q: "Definition of Case-Fatality Rate?", a: "Percentage of people diagnosed with a disease who die from it." },
            { q: "Calculation for Sensitivity?", a: "TP / (TP + FN)." },
            { q: "Clinical utility of a highly sensitive test?", a: "Used for screening to rule out disease (SNOUT)." },
            { q: "Calculation for Specificity?", a: "TN / (TN + FP)." },
            { q: "Clinical utility of a highly specific test?", a: "Used for confirmation to rule in disease (SPIN)." },
            { q: "Definition of Positive Predictive Value (PPV)?", a: "Probability that a person with a positive test actually has the disease." },
            { q: "How does increasing disease prevalence affect PPV?", a: "PPV increases." },
            { q: "How does increasing disease prevalence affect NPV?", a: "NPV decreases." },
            { q: "Formula for Attributable Risk (AR)?", a: "Incidence<sub>exposed</sub> - Incidence<sub>unexposed</sub>." },
            { q: "Formula for Relative Risk (RR)?", a: "Incidence<sub>exposed</sub> / Incidence<sub>unexposed</sub>." },
            { q: "Interpretation of RR = 1?", a: "No association between exposure and outcome." },
            { q: "Interpretation of RR > 1?", a: "Exposure is associated with increased risk of outcome." },
            { q: "Formula for Number Needed to Treat (NNT)?", a: "1 / Absolute Risk Reduction (ARR)." },
            { q: "Formula for Number Needed to Harm (NNH)?", a: "1 / Attributable Risk (AR)." },
            { q: "Definition of Selection Bias?", a: "Non-random assignment of participants to study groups." },
            { q: "What is Berkson Bias?", a: "Selection bias created by choosing hospitalized patients as the control group." },
            { q: "Definition of Recall Bias?", a: "Subjects with a disease remember past exposures more accurately than healthy controls." },
            { q: "What is the Hawthorne Effect?", a: "Participants change their behavior because they know they are being studied." },
            { q: "Definition of Lead-Time Bias?", a: "Early detection is confused with increased survival, though the disease progression is unchanged." },
            { q: "Definition of Length-Time Bias?", a: "Screening tests are more likely to detect slow-progressing cases than rapidly fatal ones." },
            { q: "What is a Confounder?", a: "An extraneous variable that is associated with both the exposure and the outcome." },
            { q: "Technique to eliminate confounding in the design phase?", a: "Randomization, matching, or restriction." },
            { q: "Technique to eliminate confounding in the analysis phase?", a: "Stratification or multivariate regression." },
            { q: "Characteristics of a Normal Distribution?", a: "Mean = Median = Mode; bell-shaped and symmetric." },
            { q: "Percentage of data within 1 Standard Deviation (SD) in a normal curve?", a: "68%." },
            { q: "Percentage of data within 2 Standard Deviations (SD) in a normal curve?", a: "95%." },
            { q: "Percentage of data within 3 Standard Deviations (SD) in a normal curve?", a: "99.7%." },
            { q: "Direction of the tail in a Positively Skewed distribution?", a: "Tail points toward the right (higher values)." },
            { q: "Order of Mean, Median, and Mode in Positive Skew?", a: "Mean > Median > Mode." },
            { q: "Definition of Null Hypothesis (H<sub>0</sub>)?", a: "Statement that there is no difference or association between groups." },
            { q: "Definition of Type I Error (&alpha;)?", a: "Rejecting the null hypothesis when it is actually true (False Positive)." },
            { q: "Definition of Type II Error (&beta;)?", a: "Failing to reject the null hypothesis when it is actually false (False Negative)." },
            { q: "Definition of Statistical Power?", a: "1 - &beta;; the probability of rejecting the null hypothesis when it is truly false." },
            { q: "How to increase Statistical Power?", a: "Increase sample size, increase effect size, or increase &alpha;." },
            { q: "Definition of a Confidence Interval (CI)?", a: "A range of values that is likely to contain the true population parameter." },
            { q: "Meaning of a 95% CI for RR that includes 1.0?", a: "The results are not statistically significant (p > 0.05)." },
            { q: "Meaning of a 95% CI for a Mean Difference that includes 0?", a: "The results are not statistically significant (p > 0.05)." },
            { q: "When to use a T-test?", a: "Comparing the means of two groups." },
            { q: "When to use ANOVA?", a: "Comparing the means of three or more groups." },
            { q: "When to use a Chi-square test?", a: "Comparing percentages or proportions between categorical groups." },
            { q: "Purpose of a Pearson Correlation Coefficient (r)?", a: "Measures the strength and direction of a linear relationship between two continuous variables." },
            { q: "Range of the Correlation Coefficient (r)?", a: "Between -1 and +1." },
            { q: "What does r<sup>2</sup> represent?", a: "The proportion of variance in one variable explained by the other." },
            { q: "Definition of Primary Prevention?", a: "Preventing disease before it occurs (e.g., vaccination)." },
            { q: "Definition of Secondary Prevention?", a: "Early detection and treatment of asymptomatic disease (e.g., Pap smear)." },
            { q: "Definition of Tertiary Prevention?", a: "Reducing disability from established disease (e.g., physical therapy after stroke)." },
            { q: "Definition of Quaternary Prevention?", a: "Protecting patients from unnecessary or harmful medical interventions." },
            { q: "Standard for 'Informed Consent'?", a: "Disclosure, Understanding, Capacity, and Voluntariness." },
            { q: "Top tier of the Evidence Pyramid?", a: "Systematic Reviews and Meta-analyses of RCTs." },
            { q: "Definition of Intention-to-Treat analysis?", a: "Analyzing participants in the groups they were originally assigned, regardless of whether they finished the treatment." },
            { q: "Benefit of Intention-to-Treat analysis?", a: "Preserves randomization and avoids bias from dropouts." },
            { q: "Definition of Meta-analysis?", a: "Statistical method that pools data from multiple studies to increase power." },
            { q: "What is a Forest Plot?", a: "A graphical representation used in meta-analyses to show the results of individual studies and the pooled estimate." },
            { q: "Definition of Infant Mortality Rate?", a: "Number of deaths under age 1 per 1,000 live births." },
            { q: "Most common cause of post-neonatal infant mortality in developed countries?", a: "Sudden Infant Death Syndrome (SIDS) and congenital anomalies." },
            { q: "Definition of Maternal Mortality Ratio?", a: "Number of maternal deaths per 100,000 live births." },
            { q: "Definition of Standardized Mortality Ratio (SMR)?", a: "Observed Deaths / Expected Deaths." },
            { q: "Definition of Reliability (Precision)?", a: "The consistency and reproducibility of a test." },
            { q: "Definition of Validity (Accuracy)?", a: "The ability of a test to measure what it is intended to measure." },
            { q: "Relationship between Random Error and Reliability?", a: "Increased random error decreases reliability." },
            { q: "Relationship between Systematic Error (Bias) and Validity?", a: "Increased systematic error decreases validity." },
            { q: "Definition of Internal Validity?", a: "The extent to which the results of a study are correct for the sample of people being studied." },
            { q: "Definition of External Validity (Generalizability)?", a: "The extent to which study results can be applied to other populations." },
            { q: "What is a Survival Curve (Kaplan-Meier)?", a: "A graph showing the proportion of subjects surviving over time." },
            { q: "Definition of Hazard Ratio (HR)?", a: "The relative risk of an event happening at any given point in time." },
            { q: "Difference between Z-score and T-score?", a: "Z-score uses population standard deviation; T-score uses sample standard deviation (used when n < 30)." },
            { q: "Definition of Standard Error of the Mean (SEM)?", a: "SD / sqrt(n); measures the dispersion of sample means around the population mean." },
            { q: "What happens to SEM as sample size increases?", a: "SEM decreases." },
            { q: "What is a Bimodal Distribution?", a: "A distribution with two peaks, often suggesting two different underlying populations." },
            { q: "Definition of Nesting in a study?", a: "A case-control study conducted within a large cohort study." },
            { q: "Advantage of a Crossover Study?", a: "Subjects serve as their own controls, reducing confounding." },
            { q: "Purpose of a Washout Period in crossover trials?", a: "To ensure the effects of the first treatment are gone before starting the second." },
            { q: "What is Effect Modification?", a: "When the magnitude of the effect of an exposure varies across levels of another variable (not a bias)." },
            { q: "Difference between Confounding and Effect Modification?", a: "Confounding is a nuisance to be eliminated; Effect Modification is a natural phenomenon to be described." },
            { q: "Sensitivity", a: "a / (a + c)." },
            { q: "Specificity", a: "d / (b + d)." },
            { q: "PPV", a: "a / (a + b)." },
            { q: "NPV", a: "d / (c + d)." },
            { q: "Odds Ratio (OR)", a: "(a/c) / (b/d) = ad / bc." },
            { q: "Relative Risk (RR)", a: "[a / (a + b)] / [c / (c + d)]." },
            { q: "Attributable Risk (AR)", a: "[a / (a + b)] - [c / (c + d)]." },
            { q: "Absolute Risk Reduction (ARR)", a: "Control Group Risk - Treatment Group Risk." },
            { q: "Relative Risk Reduction (RRR)", a: "ARR / Control Group Risk." },
            { q: "Number Needed to Treat (NNT)", a: "1 / ARR." },
            { q: "Standard Deviation (SD) Rule", a: "1 SD = 68%, 2 SD = 95%, 3 SD = 99.7%." },
            { q: "Definition of a Factorial Study Design?", a: "A study that tests the effect of two or more interventions simultaneously using different combinations of treatments." },
            { q: "What is a Time-to-Event analysis?", a: "A statistical method (like Kaplan-Meier) used to analyze the time until a specific outcome occurs." },
            { q: "Definition of Selection Bias specifically in the context of the Healthy Worker Effect?", a: "A bias where workers usually exhibit lower death rates than the general population because those who are ill are often excluded from employment." },
            { q: "What is Observer Bias?", a: "When the investigator's prior knowledge of the hypothesis or participant status influences how they record data." },
            { q: "Definition of Ascertainment Bias?", a: "A type of selection bias where certain individuals are more likely to be included in a study than others (e.g., only those with severe symptoms)." },
            { q: "What is Non-differential Misclassification?", a: "When errors in classifying exposure or disease occur equally across all groups, typically biasing results toward the null (RR = 1)." },
            { q: "What is Differential Misclassification?", a: "When errors in classification occur more frequently in one group than another, which can bias results in either direction." },
            { q: "Definition of Face Validity?", a: "The extent to which a test appears, 'on its face,' to measure what it is supposed to measure." },
            { q: "Definition of Criterion Validity?", a: "The extent to which a measure agrees with a gold standard." },
            { q: "Definition of a Random Variable?", a: "A numerical outcome of a random phenomenon." },
            { q: "Relationship between the Mean and Median in a Negatively Skewed distribution?", a: "Mean < Median < Mode." },
            { q: "Tail direction in a Negatively Skewed distribution?", a: "The tail points toward the left (lower values)." },
            { q: "Definition of Kurtosis?", a: "A measure of the 'tailedness' or peakedness of a probability distribution." },
            { q: "What is Standard Normal Distribution?", a: "A normal distribution with a mean of 0 and a standard deviation of 1." },
            { q: "What is a P-value?", a: "The probability of obtaining results at least as extreme as the observed results, assuming the null hypothesis is true." },
            { q: "Significance of a P-value < 0.05?", a: "There is less than a 5% probability that the observed result occurred by chance alone." },
            { q: "When to use a Paired T-test?", a: "When comparing two means from the same group at different times (e.g., before and after treatment)." },
            { q: "When to use a Two-sample (Independent) T-test?", a: "When comparing the means of two independent, unrelated groups." },
            { q: "Purpose of Linear Regression?", a: "To model the relationship between a dependent variable and one or more independent variables using a straight line." },
            { q: "Purpose of Logistic Regression?", a: "To model the probability of a binary (yes/no) outcome based on one or more independent variables." },
            { q: "What is Coefficient of Variation?", a: "The ratio of the standard deviation to the mean (SD / Mean), used to compare precision across different scales." },
            { q: "Definition of Reliability in the context of Test-Retest?", a: "The degree to which a test gives the same result when administered to the same person under the same conditions." },
            { q: "What is Inter-rater Reliability?", a: "The degree of agreement among different observers or 'raters.'" },
            { q: "Formula for Kappa Statistic?", a: "A measure used to assess inter-rater reliability while accounting for the amount of agreement that could occur by chance." },
            { q: "Definition of Yield in a screening program?", a: "The amount of previously unrecognized disease that is diagnosed and brought to treatment as a result of screening." },
            { q: "Impact of a Parallel testing strategy on sensitivity?", a: "Increases sensitivity and NPV (you only need one positive test to be 'positive')." },
            { q: "Impact of a Serial testing strategy on specificity?", a: "Increases specificity and PPV (you must test positive on all tests to be 'positive')." },
            { q: "Definition of Crude Birth Rate?", a: "Number of live births per 1,000 population in a given year." },
            { q: "Definition of Fertility Rate?", a: "Number of live births per 1,000 women of childbearing age (usually 15–44)." },
            { q: "Definition of Neonatal Mortality Rate?", a: "Number of deaths from birth to 28 days per 1,000 live births." },
            { q: "Definition of Perinatal Mortality Rate?", a: "Number of stillbirths plus neonatal deaths (under 7 days) per 1,000 total births." },
            { q: "Definition of Attack Rate?", a: "The proportion of an exposed population that develops a disease during an outbreak (New Cases / Population at Risk)." },
            { q: "What is the Secondary Attack Rate?", a: "The proportion of people who develop a disease after being exposed to a primary case." },
            { q: "Definition of Qualitative Research?", a: "Research focused on exploring phenomena through non-numerical data like interviews and observations." },
            { q: "Definition of Case Series?", a: "A descriptive study that tracks several patients with a known exposure or a similar outcome." },
            { q: "What is Triangulation in research?", a: "Using multiple methods or data sources to develop a comprehensive understanding of a phenomenon." },
            { q: "What is the Belmont Report?", a: "A document outlining the three fundamental ethical principles for human subjects research: Respect for Persons, Beneficence, and Justice." },
            { q: "Definition of Institutional Review Board (IRB)?", a: "An independent committee that reviews research involving human subjects to ensure ethical standards are met." },
            { q: "What is Clinical Significance?", a: "Whether the observed effect is large enough to be important in clinical practice, regardless of statistical significance." },
            { q: "Difference between Efficacy and Effectiveness?", a: "Efficacy is how a drug performs in ideal (trial) conditions; effectiveness is how it performs in real-world clinical settings." },
            { q: "What is a Gold Standard in diagnostic testing?", a: "The best available diagnostic test used to determine the true disease state." },
            { q: "Definition of Publication Bias?", a: "The tendency for journals to publish studies with positive results more often than those with negative or inconclusive results." },
            { q: "Tool used to detect Publication Bias?", a: "A Funnel Plot." },
            { q: "Definition of a Run-in Period in an RCT?", a: "A period before randomization where all participants receive a placebo or active drug to identify non-adherents or those with early side effects." },
            { q: "Benefit of a Run-in Period?", a: "It increases the study's power by ensuring the final randomized participants are more likely to comply with the protocol." },
            { q: "Definition of Equipoise?", a: "The ethical requirement that there is genuine uncertainty about which treatment in a trial is superior." },
            { q: "What is a Composite Endpoint?", a: "A primary outcome measure that combines multiple individual events into a single value." },
            { q: "Risk of using Composite Endpoints?", a: "A significant result might be driven by the least important component." },
            { q: "Definition of a Superiority Trial?", a: "A study designed to prove that a new treatment is statistically better than a placebo or current standard." },
            { q: "What is a Censored Observation in survival analysis?", a: "Data from a participant who left the study or reached the end of follow-up without experiencing the event." },
            { q: "Definition of Median Survival Time?", a: "The time at which 50% of the population has experienced the event and 50% have not." },
            { q: "Difference between Probability and Odds?", a: "Probability is favorable outcomes / total outcomes; Odds is favorable / unfavorable." },
            { q: "Relationship between RR and OR in rare diseases?", a: "When the disease is rare, the odds ratio approximates relative risk." },
            { q: "Definition of Standardized Mortality Ratio (SMR)?", a: "The ratio of observed deaths to expected deaths, adjusted for age/sex." },
            { q: "Interpretation of an SMR of 150?", a: "The study population has a 50% higher mortality rate than expected." },
            { q: "Definition of Cluster?", a: "An unusual aggregation of health events in time and place." },
            { q: "What is the Ecological Fallacy?", a: "Making an inference about an individual based on aggregate data from a group." },
            { q: "Definition of Lead-Time in the context of screening?", a: "The amount of time by which a diagnosis is moved forward because of a screening test." },
            { q: "Definition of Critical Point in the natural history of a disease?", a: "A point before which treatment is effective and after which it may be less effective." },
            { q: "What makes a disease suitable for screening?", a: "A long detectable preclinical phase and effective early treatment." },
            { q: "Impact of Overdiagnosis on survival statistics?", a: "It artificially inflates survival rates because cases that would never progress are counted." },
            { q: "What is a Type III Error?", a: "Correctly rejecting the null hypothesis but for the wrong reason." },
            { q: "Impact of a smaller alpha on beta?", a: "Decreasing alpha increases the risk of beta." },
            { q: "Definition of Effect Size?", a: "A quantitative measure of the magnitude of an effect." },
            { q: "What is Post-hoc Analysis?", a: "Statistical analyses specified after data are seen, increasing Type I error risk." },
            { q: "Definition of Data Cleaning?", a: "Identifying and correcting errors, inconsistencies, and outliers in a dataset." },
            { q: "What is Imputation?", a: "Replacing missing data with estimated values to preserve sample size." },
            { q: "Definition of Concomitant Variables?", a: "Variables measured at the start of a study that may be used as covariates." },
            { q: "Purpose of a Sensitivity Analysis in a study?", a: "To determine how different assumptions affect the results." },
            { q: "Definition of Bayes' Theorem in clinical diagnosis?", a: "Calculating disease probability based on prior probability and new data." },
            { q: "What is a Bernoulli Trial?", a: "An experiment with exactly two possible outcomes, success or failure." },
            { q: "Definition of Variance?", a: "The average of squared differences from the mean." },
            { q: "Relationship between SD and Variance?", a: "Standard deviation is the square root of variance." },
            { q: "What is a Z-score?", a: "A value's relationship to the mean measured in standard deviations." },
            { q: "Meaning of a Z-score of +2.0?", a: "The value is two standard deviations above the mean." },
            { q: "What is Multicollinearity?", a: "When predictor variables are highly correlated in multiple regression." },
            { q: "Definition of Stepwise Regression?", a: "An automatic method for selecting predictor variables." },
            { q: "What is R<sup>2</sup>?", a: "The percentage of variation in the dependent variable explained by the model." },
            { q: "Interpretation of an R<sup>2</sup> of 0.85?", a: "85% of the variance in the outcome is explained by the model." },
            { q: "When are Non-parametric tests used?", a: "When data are not normally distributed or are ranked/ordinal." },
            { q: "Non-parametric alternative to the Independent T-test?", a: "Wilcoxon Rank-Sum test (or Mann-Whitney U test)." },
            { q: "Non-parametric alternative to the Paired T-test?", a: "Wilcoxon Signed-Rank test." },
            { q: "Non-parametric alternative to ANOVA?", a: "Kruskal-Wallis test." },
            { q: "Definition of Concurrent Validity?", a: "A new test is compared to an established test at the same time." },
            { q: "Definition of Construct Validity?", a: "The degree to which a test measures the construct it claims to measure." },
            { q: "What is Content Validity?", a: "The extent to which a measurement represents all facets of a construct." },
            { q: "Definition of Sensitivity Analysis?", a: "Redoing the analysis under different assumptions to verify robustness." },
            { q: "Definition of Heterogeneity in meta-analysis?", a: "Variation in outcomes between studies beyond what chance alone would produce." },
            { q: "Statistical test used to measure Heterogeneity?", a: "Cochrane's Q or the I<sup>2</sup> statistic." },
            { q: "Interpretation of I<sup>2</sup> > 50%?", a: "Substantial heterogeneity between studies." },
            { q: "Difference between Fixed Effects and Random Effects models?", a: "Fixed assumes one true effect size; random allows the effect to vary across studies." },
            { q: "What is a Funnel Plot used for?", a: "To visually assess publication bias in a meta-analysis." },
            { q: "Definition of Harmonized Mortality Rate?", a: "A rate adjusted to compare countries or time periods fairly." },
            { q: "What is the Proportional Mortality Ratio (PMR)?", a: "Deaths from a specific cause divided by all deaths in the same population." },
            { q: "Definition of Life Table analysis?", a: "A method to calculate survival or death probabilities over intervals." },
            { q: "What is Competing Risk?", a: "An event that prevents the primary event of interest from occurring." },
            { q: "Definition of Informed Refusal?", a: "A patient’s decision to refuse recommended treatment after being fully informed." },
            { q: "What is Standardization?", a: "Methods used to remove differences in population structure when comparing mortality rates." },
            { q: "Definition of Secular Trends?", a: "Long-term changes in disease frequency over years or decades." },
            { q: "Definition of Point-Source Outbreak?", a: "An outbreak where all cases are exposed to the same source over a brief period." },
            { q: "Definition of Propagated Outbreak?", a: "An outbreak that spreads from person to person." },
            { q: "Definition of Herd Immunity Threshold?", a: "The proportion of a population that must be immune to stop disease spread: 1 - 1/R<sub>0</sub>." },
            { q: "Definition of Utility in health economics?", a: "A value between 0 (death) and 1 (perfect health) assigned to a health state." },
            { q: "How is QALY calculated?", a: "Years of life gained &times; Utility Weight." },
            { q: "Definition of Cost-Effectiveness Analysis?", a: "A comparison of the costs and health outcomes of interventions." },
            { q: "Definition of Cost-Benefit Analysis?", a: "A study where costs and outcomes are both measured in monetary terms." },
            { q: "What is an Incremental Cost-Effectiveness Ratio (ICER)?", a: "The difference in cost between two interventions divided by the difference in effect." },
            { q: "Definition of Measurement Bias?", a: "Systematic error arising from inaccurate measurement." },
            { q: "What is Contamination Bias?", a: "When the control group inadvertently receives the treatment being studied." },
            { q: "What is Compliance Bias?", a: "When adherence differs between study groups." },
            { q: "Definition of Co-intervention Bias?", a: "When one group receives additional treatments that affect the main intervention." },
            { q: "What is a Type IV Error?", a: "Correctly identifying statistical significance but interpreting the result incorrectly." },
            { q: "Definition of Interquartile Range (IQR)?", a: "The distance between Q1 and Q3, representing the middle 50% of data." },
            { q: "What is a Box-and-Whisker Plot used to show?", a: "Median, quartiles, and outliers in a dataset." },
            { q: "Definition of Outlier?", a: "An observation that lies an abnormal distance from other values in a sample." },
            { q: "What is Skewness?", a: "A measure of asymmetry in a probability distribution." },
            { q: "Definition of Kurtosis (Leptokurtic)?", a: "A distribution with a high peak and heavy tails." },
            { q: "Definition of Kurtosis (Platykurtic)?", a: "A distribution that is relatively flat with thin tails." },
            { q: "Definition of Endemic?", a: "Habitual presence of a disease in a geographic area." },
            { q: "Definition of Epidemic?", a: "Disease occurrence clearly in excess of normal expectancy." },
            { q: "Definition of Pandemic?", a: "A worldwide epidemic." },
            { q: "What is Generation Time?", a: "The interval between infection receipt and maximal communicability." },
            { q: "Definition of Incubation Period?", a: "Time from infection to onset of illness." },
            { q: "What is a Carrier?", a: "Someone who harbors an organism but is not clinically ill." },
            { q: "Formula for Number Needed to Screen (NNS)?", a: "1 / (Incidence &times; Absolute Risk Reduction)." },
            { q: "What is the Rule of Three?", a: "If no events occur in N subjects, the 95% upper confidence limit for event rate is 3/N." },
            { q: "Definition of SMR interpretation?", a: "SMR > 1 means more deaths than expected; SMR < 1 means fewer deaths than expected." },
            { q: "What is Effect Size (Cohen's d)?", a: "A standardized difference between two means: small 0.2, medium 0.5, large 0.8." },
            { q: "Definition of Intra-class Correlation (ICC)?", a: "A statistic used for measurements within grouped units." },
            { q: "Definition of Cost-Utility Analysis?", a: "A cost-effectiveness analysis where outcomes are measured in QALYs." },
            { q: "What is a Decision Tree?", a: "A visual map of diagnostic or treatment pathways." },
            { q: "Definition of a Markov Model?", a: "A model simulating disease progression through health states." },
            { q: "What is Discounting?", a: "Adjusting future costs and benefits to present value." },
            { q: "Definition of Time Trade-Off (TTO)?", a: "Choosing between longer life in poor health or shorter life in perfect health." },
            { q: "What is a Pragmatic Trial?", a: "A trial designed to show real-world effectiveness." },
            { q: "Definition of Explanatory Trial?", a: "A trial designed to determine efficacy under ideal conditions." },
            { q: "What is Block Randomization?", a: "A method to ensure nearly equal group sizes during randomization." },
            { q: "Definition of Stratified Randomization?", a: "Randomizing within subgroups to balance important variables." },
            { q: "What is Adaptive Design?", a: "A trial that allows planned changes based on interim data." },
            { q: "Definition of ITT vs. PP analysis?", a: "ITT includes everyone randomized; PP includes only those who completed treatment as assigned." },
            { q: "Why is ITT usually preferred over PP?", a: "Preserves randomization and provides a more realistic estimate of effectiveness." },
            { q: "What is Standard Error of the Proportion?", a: "A measure of variability of a proportion across samples." },
            { q: "What is Power of the Test in terms of Beta?", a: "1 - beta." },
            { q: "What is Interaction in a statistical model?", a: "When the effect of one variable changes depending on another." },
            { q: "What is a Nominal Scale?", a: "Data categorized into groups with no inherent order." },
            { q: "What is an Ordinal Scale?", a: "Data categorized into ordered groups with unequal intervals." },
            { q: "What is an Interval Scale?", a: "Numerical data with equal intervals but no true zero." },
            { q: "What is a Ratio Scale?", a: "Numerical data with equal intervals and a true zero." },
            { q: "Which measure of central tendency is most appropriate for Nominal data?", a: "Mode." },
            { q: "Which measure of central tendency is best for skewed Interval/Ratio data?", a: "Median." },
            { q: "Definition of Criterion-Related Validity?", a: "How well a measure predicts an outcome based on another measure." },
            { q: "What is Predictive Validity?", a: "How well a test predicts what it is designed to predict." },
            { q: "Definition of Discriminant Validity?", a: "The degree a test does not correlate with different constructs." },
            { q: "What is Convergent Validity?", a: "The degree to which two related measures actually correlate." },
            { q: "Definition of Responsiveness?", a: "A clinical instrument's ability to detect meaningful change over time." },
            { q: "What is Berkson’s Paradox?", a: "A selection bias in hospital-based studies causing spurious associations." },
            { q: "Definition of Neyman Bias?", a: "Missing patients who die early or recover quickly, leaving survivors only." },
            { q: "What is Migration Bias?", a: "Bias introduced when participants move in or out of the study area." },
            { q: "Definition of Voluntarism Bias?", a: "Volunteers differ systematically from non-volunteers." },
            { q: "When to use Fisher’s Exact Test?", a: "When sample size is small and Chi-square assumptions do not hold." },
            { q: "What is McNemar’s Test?", a: "A test for paired nominal data comparing two related proportions." },
            { q: "What is the Type I Error Rate in multiple comparisons?", a: "The chance of at least one false positive increases with more tests." },
            { q: "What is the Bonferroni Correction?", a: "Dividing alpha by the number of tests to control false positives." },
            { q: "Definition of Spearman’s Rank Correlation?", a: "A non-parametric measure of the relationship between ranked variables." },
            { q: "What is the Herd Effect?", a: "Indirect protection for the unimmunized when enough people are immune." },
            { q: "What is Passive Surveillance?", a: "Health authority staff rely on voluntary or mandated reports from providers." },
            { q: "What is Active Surveillance?", a: "Staff actively seek cases by contacting facilities or labs." },
            { q: "What is Sentinel Surveillance?", a: "Monitoring a selected group to identify broader trends." },
            { q: "Definition of Utilitarianism in Public Health?", a: "Taking actions that provide the greatest good for the greatest number." },
            { q: "Definition of Negative Study?", a: "A study that fails to reject the null hypothesis." },
            { q: "What is a Pilot Study?", a: "A small preliminary study conducted before a full-scale trial." },
            { q: "Definition of Post-hoc Power Analysis?", a: "Calculating power after the study; generally discouraged." },
            { q: "What is a Nomogram?", a: "A graphical calculating device for estimate post-test probability." },
            { q: "Definition of Clinical Practice Guidelines?", a: "Statements to help practitioners and patients make care decisions." },
            { q: "Definition of Grey Literature?", a: "Research not published in traditional journals, like reports or abstracts." },
            { q: "Why is Grey Literature important?", a: "To reduce publication bias by including negative or null studies." },
            { q: "What is a Gold Standard?", a: "The best available diagnostic test used to determine true disease state." },
            { q: "What is Verification Bias?", a: "When test results influence whether the confirmatory gold standard is performed." },
            { q: "Definition of Spectrum Bias?", a: "Evaluating a test in an unusually healthy or sick population, affecting performance." },
            { q: "Definition of a Forest Plot?", a: "A graphical display of study estimates and confidence intervals in a meta-analysis." },
            { q: "What does the Diamond at the bottom of a Forest Plot represent?", a: "The pooled estimate of all included studies." },
            { q: "Definition of Heterogeneity in Meta-analysis?", a: "The degree to which study outcomes vary more than expected by chance." },
            { q: "What is the I<sup>2</sup> Statistic?", a: "A measure of heterogeneity in a meta-analysis." },
            { q: "Interpretation of an I<sup>2</sup> of 75% or greater?", a: "High heterogeneity; studies may be too different to pool meaningfully." },
            { q: "Definition of a Harmonized Mortality Rate?", a: "A rate adjusted to compare different populations or time periods." },
            { q: "What is the Proportional Mortality Ratio (PMR)?", a: "The number of deaths from a specific cause divided by total deaths." },
            { q: "Definition of Life Table analysis?", a: "A method to calculate survival probabilities at each age or interval." },
            { q: "What is Competing Risk?", a: "An event that prevents the occurrence of the primary event of interest." },
            { q: "Definition of Informed Refusal?", a: "A patient’s decision to refuse treatment after being fully informed." },
            { q: "What is Standardization?", a: "Methods used to remove the effects of population differences when comparing rates." },
            { q: "Definition of Secular Trends?", a: "Long-term changes in disease frequency over years or decades." },
            { q: "Definition of Point-Source Outbreak?", a: "An outbreak where all cases are exposed to the same source over a brief period." },
            { q: "Definition of Propagated Outbreak?", a: "An outbreak that spreads person to person." },
            { q: "Definition of Herd Immunity Threshold?", a: "The proportion immune needed to stop spread: 1 - 1/R<sub>0</sub>." },
            { q: "Definition of Utility in health economics?", a: "A value between 0 and 1 assigned to a health state." },
            { q: "How is QALY calculated?", a: "Years of Life gained &times; Utility Weight." },
            { q: "Definition of Cost-Effectiveness Analysis?", a: "Comparing costs and health outcomes of interventions." },
            { q: "Definition of Cost-Benefit Analysis?", a: "A study where both costs and outcomes are measured in monetary terms." },
            { q: "What is an Incremental Cost-Effectiveness Ratio (ICER)?", a: "The difference in cost between two interventions divided by the difference in effect." },
            { q: "Definition of Measurement Bias?", a: "Systematic error arising from inaccurate measurement." },
            { q: "What is Contamination Bias?", a: "When control participants inadvertently receive the treatment being studied." },
            { q: "What is Compliance Bias?", a: "When adherence differs systematically between study groups." },
            { q: "Definition of Co-intervention Bias?", a: "When one group receives additional treatments that confound the primary intervention." },
            { q: "What is a Type IV Error?", a: "Correctly identifying a statistically significant result but giving an incorrect interpretation." },
            { q: "Definition of Multiple Linear Regression?", a: "A method to model one continuous dependent variable and multiple independent variables." },
            { q: "What is a Dummy Variable in regression?", a: "A numeric variable representing categorical data (e.g., 0 for male, 1 for female)." },
            { q: "Definition of Residuals in regression?", a: "The difference between the observed value and the predicted value." },
            { q: "Purpose of Cox Proportional Hazards Regression?", a: "To investigate the association between survival time and predictor variables." },
            { q: "Definition of Years of Potential Life Lost (YPLL)?", a: "A measure of premature mortality, subtracting age at death from standard life expectancy." },
            { q: "Definition of Disability-Adjusted Life Year (DALY)?", a: "A measure of overall disease burden from ill-health, disability, or early death." },
            { q: "Difference between DALY and QALY?", a: "DALY measures health loss; QALY measures health gained." },
            { q: "Definition of a Case-Crossover Study?", a: "A study where the case serves as its own control during a different time period." },
            { q: "Definition of Direct Causality?", a: "A factor causes disease without intermediate steps." },
            { q: "Definition of Indirect Causality?", a: "A factor causes disease through one or more intermediate steps." },
            { q: "What is a Necessary Cause?", a: "A factor that must be present for disease to occur." },
            { q: "What is a Sufficient Cause?", a: "A factor that inevitably produces disease." },
            { q: "Definition of Biological Plausibility?", a: "The association is consistent with medical knowledge." },
            { q: "Definition of Dose-Response Relationship?", a: "Risk increases with increasing exposure." },
            { q: "Definition of Temporal Relationship in Causality?", a: "The exposure must occur before disease." },
            { q: "Definition of Incidence Density?", a: "An incidence measure with person-time at risk in the denominator." },
            { q: "What is Lead-time?", a: "The time between screening detection and symptomatic diagnosis." },
            { q: "Definition of Overdiagnosis?", a: "Diagnosis of disease that would never have caused symptoms or death." },
            { q: "Primary goal of Randomization?", a: "Ensure known and unknown confounders are distributed equally." },
            { q: "Definition of Double-blinding?", a: "Neither participants nor researchers know treatment assignments." },
            { q: "Purpose of Triple-blinding?", a: "Participants, researchers, and analysts all remain unaware of group assignments." },
            { q: "Null Value for OR or RR?", a: "1.0." },
            { q: "Null Value for Absolute Risk Reduction?", a: "0." },
            { q: "What is Publication Bias?", a: "The tendency to publish positive results more often than negative or null findings." },
            { q: "Tool used to detect Publication Bias?", a: "A Funnel Plot." },
            { q: "Definition of a Run-in Period in an RCT?", a: "A period before randomization to identify non-adherent or sensitive participants." },
            { q: "Benefit of a Run-in Period?", a: "It increases study power by ensuring participants are likely to comply." },
            { q: "Definition of Equipoise?", a: "Ethical uncertainty about which treatment is superior." },
            { q: "What is a Composite Endpoint?", a: "A single outcome that combines multiple individual events." },
            { q: "Risk of using Composite Endpoints?", a: "A significant result may be driven by the least important component." },
            { q: "Definition of a Superiority Trial?", a: "A study designed to show that a new treatment is better than a standard treatment." },
            { q: "What is a Censored Observation?", a: "Data from a participant who leaves without experiencing the event." },
            { q: "Definition of Median Survival Time?", a: "The time at which 50% of subjects have had the event." },
            { q: "Difference between Probability and Odds?", a: "Probability = favorable/total; odds = favorable/unfavorable." },
            { q: "Relationship between RR and OR in rare diseases?", a: "OR approximates RR when the disease is rare." },
            { q: "Definition of Standardized Mortality Ratio (SMR)?", a: "Observed / Expected deaths adjusted for population structure." },
            { q: "Interpretation of an SMR of 150?", a: "50% higher mortality than expected." },
            { q: "Definition of Cluster?", a: "An unusual aggregation of health events in time and space." },
            { q: "What is the Ecological Fallacy?", a: "Inferring individual risk from aggregate population data." },
            { q: "Definition of Lead-Time?", a: "The amount of time by which a diagnosis is moved earlier by screening." },
            { q: "Definition of Critical Point?", a: "A point in the disease course before which treatment is more effective." },
            { q: "What makes a disease suitable for screening?", a: "A long detectable preclinical phase and an effective early treatment." },
            { q: "Impact of Overdiagnosis on survival statistics?", a: "It artificially inflates survival rates." },
            { q: "What is a Type III Error?", a: "Correctly rejecting the null but for the wrong reason." },
            { q: "Impact of a smaller alpha on beta?", a: "Making alpha smaller increases beta." },
            { q: "Definition of Effect Size?", a: "A quantitative measure of the magnitude of an effect." },
            { q: "What is Post-hoc Analysis?", a: "Analysis after seeing data, increasing false-positive risk." },
            { q: "Definition of Data Cleaning?", a: "Finding and correcting errors before analysis." },
            { q: "What is Imputation?", a: "Replacing missing values with estimates." },
            { q: "Definition of Concomitant Variables?", a: "Variables measured at baseline used as covariates." },
            { q: "Purpose of a Sensitivity Analysis?", a: "To test how different assumptions affect results." },
            { q: "Definition of Bayes' Theorem?", a: "Updating disease probability using prior probability and new evidence." },
            { q: "What is a Bernoulli Distribution?", a: "A distribution for a binary success/failure trial." },
            { q: "When is a Poisson Distribution used?", a: "For counting events in a fixed interval." },
            { q: "Definition of Central Limit Theorem?", a: "Large samples of means tend to a normal distribution." },
            { q: "What is a Confidence Level?", a: "The probability that the CI contains the true population parameter." },
            { q: "What is Standard Error of the Difference?", a: "The variability of the difference between two sample means." },
            { q: "Definition of Degrees of Freedom in a T-test?", a: "Usually n - 1; independent pieces of information used." },
            { q: "Purpose of a Power Analysis?", a: "Estimate sample size needed to detect an effect." },
            { q: "Definition of Pre-test Probability?", a: "The probability of disease before testing." },
            { q: "Definition of Post-test Probability?", a: "The probability of disease after knowing the test result." },
            { q: "What is a Nomogram?", a: "A graphical calculator for post-test probability." },
            { q: "Formula for Pre-test Odds?", a: "Prevalence / (1 - Prevalence)." },
            { q: "Formula for Post-test Odds?", a: "Pre-test Odds x Likelihood Ratio." },
            { q: "What is a ROC Curve?", a: "A graph of sensitivity vs. 1 - specificity for different cutoffs." },
            { q: "What does AUC represent?", a: "The overall accuracy of a diagnostic test." },
            { q: "What is Information Bias?", a: "Systematic error in how exposure or outcome data are obtained." },
            { q: "Definition of Surveillance Bias?", a: "Closer monitoring of one group leads to higher outcome detection." },
            { q: "What is Protopathic Bias?", a: "When an early symptom causes the exposure, e.g., taking aspirin for cancer pain." },
            { q: "Definition of Attrition Bias?", a: "Unequal loss of participants between groups causing bias." },
            { q: "What is Verification Bias?", a: "When initial test results influence whether the confirmatory test is done." },
            { q: "Definition of a Non-inferiority Trial?", a: "A trial designed to show a new treatment is not much worse than an existing one." },
            { q: "Definition of Multiple Linear Regression?", a: "A method modeling a continuous outcome with multiple predictors." },
            { q: "What is a Dummy Variable?", a: "A numeric variable representing categorical categories in regression." },
            { q: "Definition of Residuals?", a: "The difference between observed and predicted values." },
            { q: "Purpose of Cox Proportional Hazards Regression?", a: "To investigate survival time relationships with predictors." },
            { q: "Definition of Years of Potential Life Lost (YPLL)?", a: "Years lost due to premature death compared to standard life expectancy." },
            { q: "Definition of Disability-Adjusted Life Year (DALY)?", a: "Years lost due to ill-health, disability, or early death." },
            { q: "Difference between DALY and QALY?", a: "DALY measures health loss; QALY measures health benefit." },
            { q: "Definition of a Case-Crossover Study?", a: "A study where cases act as their own controls over different times." },
            { q: "Definition of Direct Causality?", a: "A factor causes disease without intermediate steps." },
            { q: "Definition of Indirect Causality?", a: "A factor causes disease through intermediate steps." },
            { q: "What is a Necessary Cause?", a: "A factor that must be present for the disease to occur." },
            { q: "What is a Sufficient Cause?", a: "A factor that inevitably produces disease if present." },
            { q: "Definition of Biological Plausibility?", a: "Association consistent with known biology." },
            { q: "Definition of Dose-Response Relationship?", a: "The risk of disease increases as exposure increases." },
            { q: "Definition of Temporal Relationship?", a: "Exposure must occur before disease." },
            { q: "Definition of Incidence Density?", a: "Incidence using person-time rather than people." },
            { q: "What is Lead-time?", a: "Time between screening detection and symptomatic diagnosis." },
            { q: "Definition of Overdiagnosis?", a: "Diagnosis of disease that would never have caused harm." },
            { q: "Primary goal of Randomization?", a: "To distribute confounders evenly across groups." },
            { q: "Definition of Double-blinding?", a: "Neither participants nor researchers know group assignment." },
            { q: "Purpose of Triple-blinding?", a: "Participants, researchers, and analysts are all unaware." },
            { q: "Null Value for OR or RR?", a: "1.0." },
            { q: "Null Value for Absolute Risk Reduction?", a: "0." },
            { q: "What is Publication Bias?", a: "The tendency to publish positive findings more often." },
            { q: "Tool used to detect Publication Bias?", a: "A Funnel Plot." },
            { q: "Definition of a Run-in Period?", a: "A pre-randomization phase to screen participants." },
            { q: "Benefit of a Run-in Period?", a: "It improves compliance and study power." },
            { q: "Definition of Equipoise?", a: "Genuine uncertainty about which treatment is superior." },
            { q: "What is a Composite Endpoint?", a: "A single outcome combining multiple events." },
            { q: "Risk of Composite Endpoints?", a: "Results may be driven by the least important event." },
            { q: "Definition of a Superiority Trial?", a: "A trial designed to show one intervention is better than another." },
            { q: "What is a Censored Observation?", a: "A participant who leaves without having the event." },
            { q: "Definition of Median Survival Time?", a: "The time when 50% of subjects have had the event." },
            { q: "Difference between Probability and Odds?", a: "Probability = favorable/total; odds = favorable/unfavorable." },
            { q: "Relationship between RR and OR in rare diseases?", a: "OR approximates RR when the disease is rare." },
            { q: "Definition of Standardized Mortality Ratio (SMR)?", a: "Observed deaths divided by expected deaths." },
            { q: "Interpretation of an SMR of 150?", a: "50% higher mortality than expected." },
            { q: "Definition of Cluster?", a: "An unusual aggregation of health events in time and place." },
            { q: "What is the Ecological Fallacy?", a: "Inferring individual risk from population data." },
            { q: "Definition of Lead-Time?", a: "How much earlier diagnosis is made by screening." },
            { q: "Definition of Critical Point?", a: "The point in disease where early treatment is still effective." },
            { q: "What makes a disease suitable for screening?", a: "A long preclinical phase and effective early treatment." },
            { q: "Impact of Overdiagnosis on survival statistics?", a: "It makes survival look better without changing mortality." },
            { q: "What is a Type III Error?", a: "Correctly rejecting the null for the wrong question." },
            { q: "Impact of smaller alpha on beta?", a: "Lower alpha increases beta." },
            { q: "Definition of Effect Size?", a: "The magnitude of an experimental effect." },
            { q: "What is Post-hoc Analysis?", a: "Analysis after the data are known, raising Type I error risk." },
            { q: "Definition of Data Cleaning?", a: "Fixing errors and inconsistencies before analysis." },
            { q: "What is Imputation?", a: "Replacing missing data with estimated values." },
            { q: "Definition of Concomitant Variables?", a: "Baseline variables used as covariates." },
            { q: "Purpose of a Sensitivity Analysis?", a: "To test result stability under different assumptions." },
            { q: "Definition of Bayes' Theorem?", a: "Updating probability using prior knowledge and new evidence." },
            { q: "What is a Bernoulli Trial?", a: "An experiment with two possible outcomes." },
            { q: "Definition of Variance?", a: "The average squared distance from the mean." },
            { q: "Relationship between SD and Variance?", a: "SD is square root of variance." },
            { q: "What is a Z-score?", a: "Distance from the mean in SD units." },
            { q: "Meaning of a Z-score of +2.0?", a: "Two SDs above the mean." },
            { q: "What is Multicollinearity?", a: "High correlation among predictors in a regression model." },
            { q: "Definition of Stepwise Regression?", a: "A method that adds or removes predictors automatically." },
            { q: "What is R<sup>2</sup>?", a: "The proportion of variance explained by a model." },
            { q: "Interpretation of R<sup>2</sup> = 0.85?", a: "85% of variance is explained by the model." },
            { q: "When are Non-parametric tests used?", a: "When data are not normally distributed or are ordinal." },
            { q: "Alternative to Independent T-test?", a: "Wilcoxon Rank-Sum test." },
            { q: "Alternative to Paired T-test?", a: "Wilcoxon Signed-Rank test." },
            { q: "Alternative to ANOVA?", a: "Kruskal-Wallis test." },
            { q: "Definition of Concurrent Validity?", a: "Comparison to an established test at the same time." },
            { q: "Definition of Construct Validity?", a: "How well a test measures the intended construct." },
            { q: "What is Content Validity?", a: "Coverage of all aspects of the construct." },
            { q: "Definition of Sensitivity Analysis?", a: "Redoing analysis under different assumptions." },
            { q: "Definition of Heterogeneity?", a: "Variation in outcomes between studies." },
            { q: "Test for Heterogeneity?", a: "Cochrane's Q or I<sup>2</sup>." },
            { q: "Interpretation of I<sup>2</sup> > 50%?", a: "Substantial heterogeneity." },
            { q: "Difference between Fixed and Random Effects?", a: "Fixed assumes one true effect; random allows variation." },
            { q: "What is a Funnel Plot?", a: "A plot used to detect publication bias." },
            { q: "Definition of Harmonized Mortality Rate?", a: "A rate adjusted for fair population comparisons." },
            { q: "What is PMR?", a: "Deaths from one cause divided by total deaths." },
            { q: "Definition of Life Table analysis?", a: "A method for calculating survival probabilities." },
            { q: "What is Competing Risk?", a: "An event that blocks the primary outcome." },
            { q: "Definition of Informed Refusal?", a: "Refusing treatment after understanding risks." },
            { q: "What is Standardization?", a: "Removing population differences when comparing rates." },
            { q: "Definition of Secular Trends?", a: "Long-term changes in disease frequency." },
            { q: "Definition of Point-Source Outbreak?", a: "Exposure to a common source over a short time." },
            { q: "Definition of Propagated Outbreak?", a: "Person-to-person spread of disease." },
            { q: "What is Herd Immunity Threshold?", a: "1 - 1/R<sub>0</sub>." },
            { q: "What is Utility?", a: "Value assigned to a health state from 0 to 1." },
            { q: "How is QALY calculated?", a: "Years of Life gained x Utility Weight." },
            { q: "What is Cost-Effectiveness Analysis?", a: "Comparing costs with health outcomes." },
            { q: "What is Cost-Benefit Analysis?", a: "Measuring both costs and outcomes in money." },
            { q: "What is ICER?", a: "Cost difference divided by effect difference." },
            { q: "What is Measurement Bias?", a: "Systematic error from measurement inaccuracy." },
            { q: "What is Contamination Bias?", a: "When controls unintentionally receive the intervention." },
            { q: "What is Compliance Bias?", a: "Different adherence rates across study groups." },
            { q: "What is Co-intervention Bias?", a: "Additional treatments alter the primary effect." },
            { q: "What is a Type IV Error?", a: "Correct statistical finding with incorrect interpretation." },
            { q: "What is Multiple Linear Regression?", a: "Modeling a continuous outcome with multiple predictors." },
            { q: "What is a Dummy Variable?", a: "A numeric code for categorical data." },
            { q: "What are Residuals?", a: "Differences between observed and predicted values." },
            { q: "What is Cox Regression?", a: "Modeling survival time with predictor variables." },
            { q: "What is YPLL?", a: "Years of life lost due to premature death." },
            { q: "What is DALY?", a: "Years lost from disability and early death." },
            { q: "How does DALY differ from QALY?", a: "DALY measures loss; QALY measures benefit." },
            { q: "What is a Case-Crossover Study?", a: "Cases act as their own controls at different times." },
            { q: "What is Direct Causality?", a: "Disease caused without intermediate steps." },
            { q: "What is Indirect Causality?", a: "Disease caused through intermediate steps." },
            { q: "What is a Necessary Cause?", a: "A required factor for disease occurrence." },
            { q: "What is a Sufficient Cause?", a: "A factor that inevitably causes disease." },
            { q: "What is Biological Plausibility?", a: "Consistency with known biology." },
            { q: "What is Dose-Response?", a: "Greater exposure produces greater effect." },
            { q: "What is Temporal Relationship?", a: "Exposure precedes disease." },
            { q: "What is Incidence Density?", a: "Incidence measured per person-time at risk." },
            { q: "What is Lead-time?", a: "The time gain from screening." },
            { q: "What is Overdiagnosis?", a: "Identifying disease that would not harm." },
            { q: "What is Randomization?", a: "Equal distribution of confounders." },
            { q: "What is Double-blinding?", a: "Neither participants nor investigators know assignments." },
            { q: "What is Triple-blinding?", a: "Participants, researchers, and analysts are unaware." },
            { q: "Null Value for OR/RR?", a: "1.0." },
            { q: "Null Value for ARR?", a: "0." },
            { q: "What is Publication Bias?", a: "Publishing positive results more often." },
            { q: "What is a Funnel Plot?", a: "A tool to detect publication bias." },
            { q: "What is a Run-in Period?", a: "A phase to identify non-adherers ahead of randomization." },
            { q: "What is Equipoise?", a: "Ethical uncertainty about treatment superiority." },
            { q: "What is a Composite Endpoint?", a: "A combined outcome measure." },
            { q: "What is a Type III Error?", a: "Correctly rejecting H0 for the wrong reason." },
            { q: "What is NNS?", a: "Number needed to screen to prevent one adverse event." },
            { q: "What is the Rule of Three?", a: "If no events occur in N subjects, upper 95% confidence limit is 3/N." },
            { q: "What is SMR?", a: "Observed / expected deaths." },
            { q: "What does SMR > 1 mean?", a: "More deaths than expected." },
            { q: "What does SMR < 1 mean?", a: "Fewer deaths than expected." },
            { q: "What is Cohen's d?", a: "A standardized difference between means." },
            { q: "What is ICC?", a: "A measure of similarity within groups." },
            { q: "What is Cost-Utility Analysis?", a: "Cost-effectiveness using QALYs." },
            { q: "What is a Decision Tree?", a: "A visual map of decision paths and outcomes." },
            { q: "What is a Markov Model?", a: "A health-state progression model over time." },
            { q: "What is Discounting?", a: "Adjusting future values to the present.", a: "" }
          ]
        },
        {
          topic: 'Endocrine Pharmacology',
          icon: 'pill',
          cards: [
            { q: "MOA of Octreotide and Lanreotide?", a: "Somatostatin analogs (long-acting); bind somatostatin receptors (SSTR) → inhibit GH, glucagon, insulin, and LH release. <b>Clinical Uses:</b> Acromegaly, VIPoma, Gastrinoma. <b>Side Effects:</b> Hyperglycemia, gallstones." },
            { q: "Clinical use of Desmopressin (dDAVP) beyond Central DI?", a: "<b>Mechanism:</b> Synthetic vasopressin analog; stimulates V2 receptors on endothelial cells → increases release of Factor VIII and von Willebrand Factor (vWF). <b>Clinical Uses:</b> Von Willebrand disease, Hemophilia A, Nocturnal Enuresis. <b>Route:</b> IV, intranasal, or SC; avoid in Type 2B vWD." },
            { q: "Preferred treatment for Acromegaly in a patient resistant to somatostatin analogs?", a: "<b>Pegvisomant</b> (GH receptor antagonist); directly blocks GH signaling at peripheral tissues." },
            { q: "MOA of Cabergoline and Bromocriptine in Hyperprolactinemia?", a: "<b>Dopamine (D2) agonists;</b> inhibit prolactin secretion from the anterior pituitary. <b>Cabergoline:</b> Preferred (longer half-life, better efficacy). <b>Bromocriptine:</b> More clinical data in pregnancy." },
            { q: "Primary side effect of Oxytocin administration?", a: "<b>Water intoxication</b> (due to ADH-like effect → hyponatremia) and <b>uterine rupture</b> (from excessive uterine contractions)." },
            { q: "MOA of Conivaptan and Tolvaptan?", a: "<b>ADH (Vasopressin V2) receptor antagonists</b> (Vaptans); used in SIADH to increase free water clearance and raise serum sodium." },
            { q: "Drug of choice for Thyroid Hormone Replacement?", a: "<b>Levothyroxine (T₄);</b> preferred over Liothyronine (T₃) due to: longer half-life (~7 days), easier monitoring, and peripheral conversion to active T₃ as needed." },
            { q: "MOA of Propylthiouracil (PTU) and Methimazole?", a: "<b>Inhibit Thyroid Peroxidase (TPO);</b> block iodine organification and coupling of MIT/DIT to form T₃ and T₄." },
            { q: "Unique peripheral MOA of Propylthiouracil (PTU) not shared by Methimazole?", a: "<b>Inhibits 5'-deiodinase;</b> blocks peripheral conversion of T₄ → T₃, providing additional benefit in thyroid storm management." },
            { q: "Black Box Warning for Propylthiouracil (PTU)?", a: "<b>Severe hepatotoxicity/acute liver failure</b> (rare but serious); monitor LFTs regularly." },
            { q: "Preferred Thionamide in the 1st Trimester of pregnancy?", a: "<b>PTU</b> (Methimazole is <b>teratogenic</b>; associated with Aplasia Cutis and other congenital anomalies)." },
            { q: "Most serious common adverse effect of Thionamides (PTU/Methimazole)?", a: "<b>Agranulocytosis</b> (~0.1-0.5% incidence); requires monitoring: obtain CBC if fever or sore throat develops." },
            { q: "MOA of Lugol's Solution (Potassium Iodide) in hyperthyroidism?", a: "<b>Wolff-Chaikoff effect:</b> High-dose iodide acutely inhibits thyroid hormone release and reduces thyroid vascularity. Used in thyroid storm after PTU/Methimazole (prevents T₄→T₃ conversion)." },
            { q: "Common use for Beta-blockers (e.g., Propranolol) in Thyroid Storm?", a: "<b>Control sympathetic symptoms</b> (tachycardia, tremor, anxiety) + <b>inhibits peripheral T₄ to T₃ conversion</b> (via inhibition of Type 1 deiodinase)." },
            { q: "Rapid-acting Insulin analogs?", a: "<b>Lispro, Aspart, Glulisine;</b> onset 10-15 minutes; peak 1-2 hours. Mnemonic: \"<b>NO LAG</b>\"" },
            { q: "Long-acting (basal) Insulin analogs?", a: "<b>Glargine (Lantus):</b> 24-hour duration. <b>Detemir (Levemir):</b> 16-24 hours, binds albumin. <b>Degludec (Tresiba):</b> >42 hours (most stable glucose)." },
            { q: "First-line oral agent for Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus?", a: "<b>Metformin (Biguanide);</b> effective, safe, inexpensive, and provides cardiovascular/weight benefits." },
            { q: "MOA of Metformin?", a: "<b>Inhibits hepatic gluconeogenesis</b> (Primary) + <b>increases peripheral glucose uptake.</b> Mechanism: AMPK activation → ↓ mTOR. Does <i>not</i> cause hypoglycemia." },
            { q: "Most serious adverse effect of Metformin?", a: "<b>Lactic Acidosis</b> (Metformin-Associated Lactic Acidosis, MALA); contraindicated in: eGFR <30, acute illness, hypoxia, contrast dye use." },
            { q: "MOA of Sulfonylureas (e.g., Glipizide, Glyburide)?", a: "<b>Close K⁺ channels</b> in pancreatic Beta-cells → cell depolarization → Ca²⁺ influx → <b>Insulin release.</b> Causes hypoglycemia." },
            { q: "Difference between 1st and 2nd generation Sulfonylureas?", a: "<b>1st gen:</b> Acetohexamide, Tolbutamide (short half-life, Disulfiram-like reactions). <b>2nd gen:</b> Glipizide, Glyburide (more potent, longer-acting, fewer interactions)." },
            { q: "MOA of Pioglitazone and Rosiglitazone (TZDs)?", a: "<b>Activate PPAR-γ</b> (nuclear receptor) → increase insulin sensitivity and adiponectin production → ↓ insulin resistance in liver/muscle." },
            { q: "Major side effects of Thiazolidinediones (TZDs)?", a: "<b>Weight gain, fluid retention (edema), HF exacerbation,</b> and <b>increased fracture risk</b> in women. Also ↑ bladder cancer risk (Pioglitazone)." },
            { q: "MOA of GLP-1 Agonists (e.g., Exenatide, Liraglutide, Semaglutide)?", a: "<b>Activate GLP-1 receptors →</b> glucose-dependent ↑ insulin secretion, ↓ glucagon, slow gastric emptying. <b>Benefits:</b> Weight loss, cardioprotection, GI side effects." },
            { q: "MOA of DPP-4 Inhibitors (e.g., Sitagliptin)?", a: "<b>Inhibit Dipeptidyl Peptidase-4</b> → ↑ endogenous GLP-1 levels → glucose-dependent insulin secretion. <b>Advantage:</b> No hypoglycemia." },
            { q: "MOA of SGLT2 Inhibitors (e.g., Canagliflozin, Empagliflozin, Dapagliflozin)?", a: "<b>Inhibit Sodium-Glucose Cotransporter-2</b> in the Proximal Convoluted Tubule (PCT) → ↓ glucose reabsorption → glucosuria. <b>Benefits:</b> HF reduction, weight loss." },
            { q: "Common side effects of SGLT2 Inhibitors?", a: "<b>Glucosuria, UTIs, vaginal candidiasis, euglycemic DKA</b> (most serious), and ↑ risk of amputation (older agents). Monitor: Blood glucose, renal function." },
            { q: "MOA of Acarbose and Miglitol?", a: "<b>Alpha-glucosidase inhibitors;</b> delay intestinal glucose absorption by inhibiting brush-border enzymes (sucrase, maltase, lactase)." },
            { q: "Treatment for severe hypoglycemia in an unconscious patient (non-IV access)?", a: "<b>Intramuscular Glucagon</b> (1 mg IM). Alternative: IV Dextrose if IV access available." },
            { q: "MOA of Glucocorticoids?", a: "<b>Inhibit Phospholipase A₂</b> → ↓ arachidonic acid → ↓ prostaglandins/leukotrienes. Also <b>inhibit NF-κB</b> (transcription factor for inflammatory mediators)." },
            { q: "Result of chronic Glucocorticoid use on the HPA axis?", a: "<b>Adrenal atrophy</b> due to suppression of ACTH; requires slow taper to restore function. Risk of acute adrenal insufficiency if stopped abruptly." },
            { q: "MOA of Spironolactone in Endocrine therapy?", a: "<b>Aldosterone receptor antagonist</b> (primary) + <b>weak androgen receptor antagonist</b> → blocks testosterone effects. <b>Uses:</b> Primary hyperaldosteronism, PCOS-related hirsutism." },
            { q: "MOA and use of Metyrapone?", a: "<b>Inhibits 11β-hydroxylase</b> → ↓ cortisol → ↑ ACTH (via feedback). <b>Diagnostic:</b> Metyrapone stimulation test for ACTH reserve. <b>Therapeutic:</b> Cushing's syndrome." },
            { q: "MOA of Ketoconazole in Cushing's Syndrome?", a: "<b>Inhibits multiple P450 enzymes</b> (including desmolase, 17α-hydroxylase) → blocks all steroid synthesis. <b>Risk:</b> Hepatotoxicity; requires LFT monitoring." },
            { q: "MOA of Mifepristone in an Endocrine context?", a: "<b>Competitive Progesterone and Glucocorticoid receptor antagonist.</b> <b>Uses:</b> Medical abortion (with Misoprostol) and hyperglycemia control in Cushing's syndrome." },
            { q: "Drug used to treat Primary Hyperaldosteronism (Conn Syndrome)?", a: "<b>Spironolactone</b> (first-line) or <b>Eplerenone</b> (more selective, fewer gynecomastia)." },
            { q: "MOA of Bisphosphonates (e.g., Alendronate, Risedronate)?", a: "<b>Pyrophosphate analogs;</b> bind hydroxyapatite in bone → inhibit osteoclast H⁺-ATPase → ↓ bone resorption (anti-resorptive)." },
            { q: "Serious adverse effect of oral Bisphosphonates?", a: "<b>Corrosive esophagitis</b> (severe erosions); patient must stay upright for ≥30 minutes after dosing. IV forms avoid this." },
            { q: "MOA of Teriparatide?", a: "<b>Recombinant PTH (1-34) analog;</b> when given <i>pulsatile,</i> stimulates osteoblasts > osteoclasts → net bone formation (anabolic). Unique: only FDA-approved anabolic agent." },
            { q: "MOA of Denosumab?", a: "<b>Monoclonal antibody against RANKL</b> (Receptor Activator of NF-κB Ligand) → inhibits osteoclast maturation and survival. Superior to bisphosphonates in many cases." },
            { q: "MOA of Cinacalcet?", a: "<b>Calcimimetic;</b> sensitizes Ca²⁺-sensing receptors (CaSR) on parathyroid gland to <i>lower</i> setpoint → ↓ PTH secretion. Used in: Secondary hyperparathyroidism, hypercalcemia of malignancy." },
            { q: "Use of Sevelamer?", a: "<b>Phosphate binder</b> (non-calcium, non-metal); used in Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD) to treat hyperphosphatemia. Also ↓ LDL cholesterol." },
            { q: "MOA of Clomiphene?", a: "<b>SERM (Selective Estrogen Receptor Modulator);</b> antagonist at estrogen receptors in hypothalamus → blocks feedback inhibition → ↑ FSH/LH → ovulation induction." },
            { q: "MOA of Tamoxifen vs. Raloxifene in bone?", a: "<b>Both are agonists</b> at bone estrogen receptors → prevent osteoporosis. But: <b>Tamoxifen = agonist in uterus</b> (↑ endometrial cancer); <b>Raloxifene = antagonist in uterus</b> (safer)." },
            { q: "MOA of Anastrozole and Letrozole?", a: "<b>Aromatase inhibitors;</b> block the enzyme aromatase → ↓ peripheral conversion of androgens (Androstenedione, Testosterone) to estrogens. Used in ER+ breast cancer." },
            { q: "MOA of Finasteride?", a: "<b>5α-reductase inhibitor;</b> blocks conversion of Testosterone → Dihydrotestosterone (DHT). <b>Uses:</b> BPH, male pattern baldness. <b>Side effect:</b> Sexual dysfunction." },
            { q: "MOA of Flutamide?", a: "<b>Competitive inhibitor</b> at androgen receptors (blocks DHT/testosterone signaling). <b>Use:</b> Prostate cancer. <b>Given with:</b> GnRH agonist to prevent initial testosterone flare." },
            { q: "MOA of Leuprolide (Continuous vs. Pulsatile)?", a: "<b>Pulsatile:</b> GnRH agonist → ↑ FSH/LH (used for infertility). <b>Continuous:</b> GnRH antagonist (downregulates receptors, flare-like effect) → ↓ FSH/LH/testosterone (prostate cancer, endometriosis)." },
            { q: "Side effect of Sildenafil when combined with Nitrates?", a: "<b>Severe, potentially fatal hypotension;</b> both ↑ cGMP → excessive vasodilation. <i>Absolute contraindication.</i>" },
            { q: "MOA of Tesamorelin?", a: "<b>GHRH analog;</b> stimulates GH release. Used to treat HIV-associated lipodystrophy (reduce abdominal fat)." },
            { q: "Clinical use of Mecasermin?", a: "<b>Recombinant IGF-1;</b> used in children with growth failure due to severe IGF-1 deficiency (GH-resistant); requires SQ administration." },
            { q: "Primary side effect of Mecasermin?", a: "<b>Hypoglycemia</b> (IGF-1 has insulin-like effects on glucose uptake); requires careful monitoring and carbohydrate intake." },
            { q: "MOA of Ganirelix and Cetrorelix?", a: "<b>GnRH antagonists;</b> directly suppress GnRH receptors. Used in controlled ovarian stimulation to prevent premature LH surge (unlike GnRH agonists)." },
            { q: "Comparison: Half-life of Desmopressin vs. Vasopressin?", a: "<b>Vasopressin:</b> ~6 minutes (non-selective: V1/V2). <b>Desmopressin:</b> 8-20 hours (V2-selective); much higher V2:V1 selectivity ratio." },
            { q: "Effect of Amiodarone on thyroid function?", a: "<b>Dual effect:</b> Can cause <b>hypothyroidism</b> (Wolff-Chaikoff effect, iodide content) or <b>hyperthyroidism</b> (Jod-Basedow effect or destructive thyroiditis); monitor TSH closely." },
            { q: "Why is Methimazole preferred over PTU in most adults?", a: "<b>Longer half-life</b> (~6 hours vs 1.5 hours) → once-daily dosing, better compliance. <b>Lower hepatotoxicity risk</b> vs PTU. <b>PTU reserved for</b> 1st trimester pregnancy." },
            { q: "MOA of Anion Inhibitors (Perchlorate, Thiocyanate)?", a: "<b>Competitive inhibition</b> of the sodium-iodide symporter (NIS) → ↓ iodine uptake into thyroid. Rarely used today." },
            { q: "Major risk of using Radioactive Iodine (I-131) in Graves' disease?", a: "<b>Permanent hypothyroidism</b> (develops in ~50% within 10 years); requires lifelong T₄ replacement and TSH monitoring." },
            { q: "MOA of Liotrix?", a: "<b>Combination hormone:</b> 4:1 mixture of synthetic T₄ and T₃; provides both immediate (T₃) and sustained (T₄) effects. Rarely used (less stable dosing than T₄ monotherapy)." },
            { q: "Which Diabetes drug class is associated with 'Euglycemic DKA'?", a: "<b>SGLT2 Inhibitors</b> (e.g., Empagliflozin); causes DKA with <i>normal</i> blood glucose (↓ insulin → lipolysis, ketosis despite euglycemia)." },
            { q: "MOA of Pramlintide?", a: "<b>Amylin analog;</b> decreases gastric emptying + ↓ glucagon secretion + ↑ satiety. <b>Use:</b> Both Type 1 and Type 2 DM; must be injected immediately before meals." },
            { q: "Specific clinical timing for Pramlintide administration?", a: "<b>Immediately before meals;</b> given as separate injection from insulin. Reduces postprandial glucose excursions." },
            { q: "Which sulfonylureas have the highest risk of hypoglycemia in the elderly?", a: "<b>Long-acting agents:</b> Glyburide (chlorpropamide, tolazamide); prolonged duration → cumulative hypoglycemia risk. Safer: short-acting (Glipizide)." },
            { q: "Effect of Metformin on Vitamin B12?", a: "<b>Decreases B12 absorption</b> (malabsorption, possibly via Ca²⁺-dependent mechanism); long-term use → B12 deficiency. <b>Monitor:</b> Annual B12 levels; consider supplementation." },
            { q: "MOA of Meglitinides (Repaglinide, Nateglinide)?", a: "<b>Rapid K⁺ channel closure</b> (similar to sulfonylureas) but <b>much shorter duration</b> (~2-4 hours). <b>Use:</b> Postprandial glucose control." },
            { q: "Common side effect of Alpha-glucosidase inhibitors (Acarbose)?", a: "<b>GI upset:</b> Flatulence, diarrhea, abdominal bloating (due to fermentation of undigested carbohydrates by colonic bacteria)." },
            { q: "Contraindication for GLP-1 Agonists (e.g., Liraglutide)?", a: "<b>Personal or family history of Medullary Thyroid Carcinoma (MTC) or MEN 2;</b> GLP-1 agonists may promote MTC growth (black box warning)." },
            { q: "Benefit of GLP-1 Agonists and SGLT2 Inhibitors beyond glucose control?", a: "<b>Cardioprotective benefits:</b> ↓ cardiovascular events, heart failure hospitalization, mortality. <b>Additional:</b> Weight loss, blood pressure reduction." },
            { q: "MOA of Mitotane?", a: "<b>Adrenolytic drug;</b> selectively destroys adrenocortical cells via formation of reactive metabolites. <b>Use:</b> Inoperable adrenal cortical carcinoma. <b>Monitoring:</b> Plasma levels, cortisol, DHEA-S." },
            { q: "Effect of Spironolactone on the heart in HF?", a: "<b>Reduces cardiac remodeling and mortality</b> in HFrEF (heart failure with reduced ejection fraction); prevents aldosterone-mediated fibrosis." },
            { q: "MOA of Eplerenone vs. Spironolactone?", a: "<b>Eplerenone:</b> Selective aldosterone receptor antagonist (no progesterone or androgen receptor affinity). <b>Advantage:</b> No gynecomastia or sexual dysfunction." },
            { q: "Drug of choice for primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison's)?", a: "<b>Hydrocortisone</b> (glucocorticoid replacement) + <b>Fludrocortisone</b> (mineralocorticoid replacement). Doses: ~15-25 mg/day cortisol-equivalent." },
            { q: "Difference between Prednisone and Prednisolone?", a: "<b>Prednisone:</b> Prodrug converted to <i>Prednisolone</i> by hepatic 11β-HSD. In liver disease, use Prednisolone directly." },
            { q: "MOA of Danazol?", a: "<b>Synthetic androgen;</b> partial agonist at androgen receptors. <b>Uses:</b> Endometriosis, hereditary angioedema. <b>Side effects:</b> Virilization (hirsutism, acne, deepened voice)." },
            { q: "Adverse effects of Danazol?", a: "<b>Weight gain, hirsutism, acne, voice deepening, decreased HDL,</b> clitoral enlargement, and liver dysfunction (virilization)." },
            { q: "MOA of Fulvestrant?", a: "<b>Pure estrogen receptor antagonist (SERD);</b> binds ER and promotes its degradation. <b>Use:</b> ER+ breast cancer resistant to other agents." },
            { q: "MOA of Exemestane?", a: "<b>Irreversible (suicide) aromatase inhibitor;</b> covalently inactivates aromatase. <b>Use:</b> Postmenopausal breast cancer." },
            { q: "MOA of Mifepristone (RU-486)?", a: "<b>Progesterone receptor antagonist;</b> leads to decidual breakdown and pregnancy termination. <b>Given with:</b> Misoprostol (PGE1 analog) for uterine contractions." },
            { q: "MOA of Misoprostol in medical abortion?", a: "<b>PGE1 analog;</b> causes uterine muscle contraction to expel the fetus. Also used to prevent NSAID-induced ulcers." },
            { q: "MOA of Bicalutamide and Enzalutamide?", a: "<b>Nonsteroidal competitive inhibitors</b> of androgen receptor; block testosterone/DHT signaling. <b>Use:</b> Prostate cancer (better CNS penetration than Bicalutamide)." },
            { q: "MOA of Abiraterone?", a: "<b>Inhibits 17α-hydroxylase/17,20-lyase (CYP17A1);</b> blocks androgen synthesis in testes and adrenal glands. <b>Given with:</b> Prednisone (to counteract mineralocorticoid excess from CYP17 inhibition)." },
            { q: "MOA of Minoxidil (Topical)?", a: "<b>Direct arteriolar vasodilator;</b> opens K⁺ channels → ↑ blood flow to hair follicles. <b>Use:</b> Androgenetic alopecia (requires continuous use)." },
            { q: "MOA of Calcitonin (Salmon)?", a: "<b>Directly inhibits osteoclast bone resorption</b> via calcitonin receptors. <b>Uses:</b> Paget's disease of bone, acute hypercalcemia, osteoporosis. <b>Note:</b> Tachyphylaxis develops." },
            { q: "MOA of Gallium Nitrate?", a: "<b>Inhibits bone resorption</b> by osteoclasts (mechanism unclear). <b>Use:</b> Cancer-related hypercalcemia resistant to hydration/bisphosphonates. <b>Risk:</b> Nephrotoxicity." },
            { q: "MOA of Plicamycin (Mithramycin)?", a: "<b>RNA synthesis inhibitor</b> → lowers calcium by inhibiting osteoclasts. <b>Use:</b> Hypercalcemia (rarely used due to hepatotoxicity, thrombocytopenia)." },
            { q: "Electrolyte abnormality caused by Cinacalcet?", a: "<b>Hypocalcemia;</b> by suppressing PTH → ↓ serum calcium. Requires monitoring of serum calcium and phosphate." },
            { q: "Difference in MOA: Ergocalciferol vs. Cholecalciferol?", a: "<b>Ergocalciferol (D₂):</b> Plant-derived (yeast/fungi). <b>Cholecalciferol (D₃):</b> Animal/skin-derived; more potent and longer-acting (preferred)." },
            { q: "MOA of Calcitriol?", a: "<b>Active 1,25-(OH)₂D₃;</b> binds vitamin D receptor (VDR, intracellular) → ↑ intestinal absorption of calcium and phosphate → ↑ osteoclast maturation. Used in: Hypoparathyroidism, CKD." },
            { q: "Treatment for SIADH in a patient who cannot tolerate Vaptans?", a: "<b>Demeclocycline</b> (tetracycline antibiotic); acts as ADH antagonist in renal tubule to induce nephrogenic DI." },
            { q: "Side effect of Demeclocycline?", a: "<b>Nephrogenic Diabetes Insipidus</b> (polyuria, polydipsia) and <b>photosensitivity</b> (sun-exposed skin reactions)." },
            { q: "MOA of Octreotide in variceal bleeding?", a: "<b>Induces splanchnic vasoconstriction</b> → ↓ portal venous pressure → stops bleeding. <b>Given with:</b> Endoscopic band ligation." },
            { q: "MOA of Pasireotide?", a: "<b>Somatostatin analog</b> with <b>high affinity for SSTR5</b> (vs. octreotide's SSTR2/SSTR5); specifically used for Cushing's disease (ACTH-secreting adenomas express SSTR5)." },
            { q: "Most common side effect of Alprostadil (PGE1) for ED?", a: "<b>Penile pain</b> (burning) and <b>priapism</b> (risk of fibrosis if untreated). <b>Also:</b> Systemic hypotension if absorbed." },
            { q: "MOA of Ulipristal?", a: "<b>Selective progesterone receptor modulator (SPRM);</b> competitive antagonist at PR. <b>Use:</b> Emergency contraception (up to 120 hours post-intercourse)." },
            { q: "Drug for Hyperprolactinemia that is safe in pregnancy?", a: "<b>Bromocriptine;</b> more extensive clinical data/safety history than Cabergoline in pregnancy. Pregnancy rates higher with dopamine agonists." },
            { q: "Primary difference in MOA between Degarelix and Leuprolide?", a: "<b>Degarelix:</b> GnRH antagonist → immediate ↓ testosterone (no flare). <b>Leuprolide:</b> GnRH agonist → initial ↑ (flare), then ↓ testosterone." },
            { q: "Why is Flutamide given with Leuprolide?", a: "To prevent the <b>testosterone flare</b> phenomenon during the first week of Leuprolide therapy; competitive AR blockade." },
            { q: "Drug used to close a Patent Ductus Arteriosus (PDA)?", a: "<b>Indomethacin</b> (NSAID); inhibits prostaglandin E₁ synthesis → PDA closure. <b>Note:</b> Less effective in older infants." },
            { q: "Drug used to keep a PDA open?", a: "<b>Alprostadil (PGE1);</b> maintains ductal patency in neonates with PDA-dependent congenital heart disease." },
            { q: "MOA of Cyproterone acetate?", a: "<b>Progestin + androgen receptor antagonist;</b> blocks testosterone/DHT action. <b>Uses:</b> Hirsutism, severe acne, prostate cancer." },
            { q: "What drug is a 'Calcimimetic'?", a: "<b>Cinacalcet;</b> sensitizes calcium-sensing receptors to lower set-point for PTH suppression." },
            { q: "What drug is a 'Selective Estrogen Receptor Downregulator' (SERD)?", a: "<b>Fulvestrant;</b> ER antagonist that promotes ER protein degradation." },
            { q: "Which endocrine hormones signal via the cAMP/PKA pathway?", a: "<b>FLAT ChAMP:</b> FSH, LH, ACTH, TSH, Calcitonin, hCG, ADH (V2 receptor), MSH, PTH, Glucagon." },
            { q: "Which endocrine hormones signal via the IP3/DAG (Gq) pathway?", a: "<b>GOAT HAG:</b> GnRH, Oxytocin, ADH (V1 receptor), TRH, Histamine (H1), Angiotensin II, Gastrin." },
            { q: "Which endocrine hormones use Intracellular/Nuclear receptors?", a: "<b>PET CAT on TV:</b> Progesterone, Estrogen, Testosterone, Cortisol, Aldosterone, T₃/T₄, Vitamin D." },
            { q: "MOA of IGF-1 and Insulin signaling?", a: "<b>Receptor Tyrosine Kinase;</b> autophosphorylation → MAPK and PI3K/Akt pathways → gene transcription, glucose uptake, protein synthesis." },
            { q: "MOA of GH and Prolactin signaling?", a: "<b>JAK/STAT pathway</b> (non-receptor tyrosine kinase); STAT1/STAT3 phosphorylation → gene transcription." },
            { q: "Which DM medication class increases the risk of bladder cancer?", a: "<b>Pioglitazone (TZD);</b> increased risk noted in epidemiologic studies (FDA warning)." },
            { q: "Only non-insulin antidiabetic drug approved for Type 1 DM?", a: "<b>Pramlintide (Amylin analog);</b> approved as adjunct to insulin in both Type 1 and Type 2 DM." },
            { q: "MOA of Diazoxide in the pancreas?", a: "<b>K⁺ channel opener;</b> hyperpolarizes Beta-cells → inhibits insulin release. <b>Use:</b> Insulinoma (symptomatic hypoglycemia)." },
            { q: "Which SGLT2 inhibitor is specifically known for reducing HF hospitalization?", a: "<b>Empagliflozin</b> and <b>Dapagliflozin;</b> shown in EMPA-REG and DAPA-HF trials to ↓ HF hospitalization regardless of diabetes status." },
            { q: "Main metabolic difference between Glucagon and Epinephrine?", a: "<b>Glucagon:</b> Does NOT affect muscle glycogen (muscle lacks glucagon receptors) → cannot increase blood glucose from muscle. <b>Epinephrine:</b> Activates muscle glycogenolysis." },
            { q: "MOA of Trilostane?", a: "<b>Inhibits 3β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase;</b> blocks conversion of Pregnenolone → Progesterone. <b>Use:</b> Cushing's syndrome (veterinary, limited human use)." },
            { q: "Effect of Spironolactone on 17α-hydroxylase?", a: "<b>Weak inhibition</b> of CYP17 → contributes to anti-androgenic effects (gynecomastia, sexual dysfunction)." },
            { q: "Why is Fludrocortisone the drug of choice for mineralocorticoid replacement?", a: "<b>Highest mineralocorticoid-to-glucocorticoid potency ratio</b> (250:10) among available steroids; minimal glucocorticoid effects." },
            { q: "Diagnostic use of Cosyntropin?", a: "<b>Synthetic ACTH (1-24);</b> used in ACTH stimulation test to distinguish primary (↑ cortisol response fails) from secondary (preserved response) adrenal insufficiency." },
            { q: "Glucocorticoid with the highest anti-inflammatory potency and zero salt-retention?", a: "<b>Dexamethasone;</b> ~25-30× potency of hydrocortisone with negligible mineralocorticoid activity." },
            { q: "MOA of Strontium Ranelate?", a: "<b>Dual action:</b> ↑ osteoblast bone formation + ↓ osteoclast resorption. <b>Use:</b> Postmenopausal osteoporosis (non-US market mainly)." },
            { q: "MOA of Romosozumab?", a: "<b>Sclerostin inhibitor</b> (monoclonal antibody); increases osteoblast Wnt signaling → ↑ bone formation + ↓ resorption. <b>Fastest bone-building agent.</b>" },
            { q: "Unique side effect of Alendronate besides esophagitis?", a: "<b>Osteonecrosis of the Jaw (ONJ)</b> and <b>atypical subtrochanteric fractures</b> (rare but serious; long-term use)." },
            { q: "MOA of Cinacalcet in Tertiary Hyperparathyroidism?", a: "<b>↑ Ca²⁺-sensing receptor sensitivity</b> → ↓ PTH in post-kidney transplant patients with persistent hyperparathyroidism." },
            { q: "Most common electrolyte toxicity of Vitamin D overdose?", a: "<b>Hypercalcemia + hypercalciuria;</b> leads to polyuria, renal stones, metastatic calcification." },
            { q: "MOA of the 'Mini-pill' (Progestin-only)?", a: "<b>Thickens cervical mucus</b> (prevents sperm penetration) + <b>thins endometrium</b> (↓ implantation). <b>Advantage:</b> Safe in lactation." },
            { q: "Component of OCPs responsible for inhibiting the LH surge?", a: "<b>Estrogen;</b> suppresses FSH/LH release via negative feedback. Progestin acts at hypothalamus but estrogen controls the LH surge." },
            { q: "Effect of OCPs on SHBG (Sex Hormone Binding Globulin)?", a: "<b>Estrogen ↑↑ SHBG</b> → ↓ free testosterone → improves acne, hirsutism. (Progestin-only decreases SHBG.)" },
            { q: "MOA of Letrozole in PCOS?", a: "<b>Aromatase inhibitor;</b> decreases estrogen → ↑ FSH (via feedback) → induces ovulation. <b>Advantage over Clomiphene:</b> No antiestrogenic effects on endometrium." },
            { q: "Interaction between Rifampin and OCPs?", a: "<b>Rifampin induces CYP3A4</b> → ↑ OCP metabolism → ↓ efficacy. <b>Management:</b> Higher-dose OCP or alternative contraception." },
            { q: "MOA of Drospirenone (found in Yaz/Yasmin)?", a: "<b>Progestin with anti-mineralocorticoid and anti-androgenic activity</b> (spironolactone analog); helps with water retention and acne." },
            { q: "MOA of Sermorelin?", a: "<b>GHRH analog;</b> stimulates GH secretion from anterior pituitary. <b>Use:</b> Diagnostic test for GH reserve (stimulation test) or therapeutic GH augmentation." },
            { q: "How does Somatostatin inhibit TSH release?", a: "Via <b>Gi-coupled receptors</b> on pituitary thyrotropes → ↓ cAMP → inhibits TSH secretion." },
            { q: "Common side effect of Octreotide related to the biliary system?", a: "<b>Biliary sludge and gallstones</b> (cholestasis); due to inhibition of cholecystokinin (CCK) → ↓ gallbladder contractility." },
            { q: "Effect of GH on adipose tissue?", a: "<b>Lipolytic:</b> GH → ↑ hormone-sensitive lipase → ↑ free fatty acids. This creates the 'insulin-antagonistic' metabolic state." },
            { q: "Drug of choice for nephrogenic DI induced by Lithium?", a: "<b>Amiloride</b> (K⁺-sparing diuretic); blocks Li⁺ entry into collecting duct cells via ENaC (epithelial Na⁺ channel)." },
            { q: "MOA of Metyrapone in a 'Metyrapone Test'?", a: "<b>Blocks 11β-hydroxylase</b> → ↓↓ cortisol → ↑↑ ACTH (if HPA axis intact). <b>Measures:</b> Pituitary ACTH reserve in response to low cortisol." },
            { q: "Why is Methimazole avoided in the 1st trimester of pregnancy?", a: "<b>Risk of congenital anomalies:</b> Choanal atresia, esophageal atresia, aplasia cutis. <b>PTU preferred</b> (despite hepatotoxicity risk, lower trans-placental passage)." },
            { q: "MOA of Ketoconazole's effect on Leydig cells?", a: "<b>Inhibits 17,20-desmolase (CYP17A1);</b> blocks conversion of 17-OH-Pregnenolone → DHEA → Testosterone." },
            { q: "Major advantage of Eplerenone over Spironolactone?", a: "<b>Higher selectivity</b> for mineralocorticoid receptors; lacks progesterone and androgen receptor affinity → no gynecomastia, no sexual dysfunction." },
            { q: "MOA of Ospemifene?", a: "<b>SERM</b> selective for vaginal tissue; agonist effect on local estrogen receptors. <b>Use:</b> Dyspareunia (vaginal atrophy) in postmenopausal women." },
            { q: "Treatment for Hyperprolactinemia that also treats Parkinson's?", a: "<b>Bromocriptine;</b> dopamine D2 agonist → suppresses prolactin and treats dopamine deficiency in Parkinson's." },
            { q: "Which class of DM drugs is contraindicated in Gastroparesis?", a: "<b>GLP-1 Agonists and Pramlintide;</b> both slow gastric emptying → worsen symptoms in gastroparesis." },
            { q: "Treatment for GH-producing pituitary adenoma if surgery fails?", a: "<b>Octreotide</b> (somatostatin analog, SSTR2 agonist) or <b>Pegvisomant</b> (GH receptor antagonist)." },
            { q: "MOA of Triptorelin?", a: "<b>GnRH agonist;</b> similar to Leuprolide. <b>Use:</b> Continuous administration for chemical castration in prostate cancer." },
            { q: "MOA of Lixisenatide and Albiglutide?", a: "<b>GLP-1 receptor agonists</b> (incretin mimetics); similar mechanism to Exenatide/Liraglutide." },
            { q: "Which DM drug class can cause 'Fractures in women' as a side effect?", a: "<b>Thiazolidinediones (TZDs)</b> like Pioglitazone; ↓ osteoblast function → ↑ fracture risk (especially hip, ankle)." },
            { q: "MOA of Miglitol vs. Acarbose?", a: "<b>Both are alpha-glucosidase inhibitors;</b> <b>Miglitol:</b> absorbed systemically (can measure in plasma). <b>Acarbose:</b> not absorbed (undergoes metabolism in GI tract)." },
            { q: "Why is Metformin withheld before giving IV radiocontrast?", a: "<b>Risk of acute renal failure</b> post-contrast (radio-opaque dyes are nephrotoxic) → ↓ metformin clearance → <b>Metformin-Associated Lactic Acidosis (MALA).</b> Hold 48 hours post-contrast." },
            { q: "Effect of Pioglitazone on Lipid profiles?", a: "<b>↑ HDL, ↓ Triglycerides</b> (more favorable than Rosiglitazone which ↑ LDL). Overall favorable lipid effect." },
            { q: "MOA of Linagliptin?", a: "<b>DPP-4 inhibitor;</b> unique because it is excreted via bile (no renal metabolism) → safe in renal failure without dose adjustment." },
            { q: "MOA of Ipodate and Iopanoic acid?", a: "<b>Radiopaque contrast media</b> that also inhibit 5'-deiodinase → ↓ peripheral T4 → T3 conversion (similar to PTU). Used in thyroid storm." },
            { q: "Treatment for 'Amiodarone-induced Thyrotoxicosis' Type 1?", a: "<b>Thionamides (PTU or Methimazole);</b> blocks thyroid hormone synthesis in Graves-like reaction." },
            { q: "Treatment for 'Amiodarone-induced Thyrotoxicosis' Type 2?", a: "<b>Glucocorticoids;</b> suppresses destructive thyroiditis (iodine-induced inflammation of thyroid)." },
            { q: "MOA of Natpara?", a: "<b>Recombinant human PTH (1-84);</b> full-length PTH. <b>Use:</b> Hypocalcemia in hypoparathyroidism (given SC once daily)." },
            { q: "Effect of Thiazide diuretics on Calcium?", a: "<b>↑ renal calcium reabsorption</b> in distal tubule → ↑ serum calcium. Used to prevent calcium stone recurrence." },
            { q: "Effect of Loop diuretics on Calcium?", a: "<b>↓ renal calcium reabsorption</b> (inhibit Mg²⁺/Ca²⁺ reabsorption in thick ascending limb). Mnemonic: <b>\"Loops Lose Calcium.\"</b>" },
            { q: "MOA of Megestrol acetate?", a: "<b>Progestin;</b> stimulates appetite and weight gain. <b>Use:</b> Anorexia and cachexia in AIDS and cancer patients." },
            { q: "Side effect of Flutamide often monitored with LFTs?", a: "<b>Hepatotoxicity</b> (cholestatic jaundice, acute liver failure); rare but serious. Requires monthly LFT monitoring." },
            { q: "MOA of Finasteride in BPH?", a: "<b>Reduces prostate volume</b> by blocking DHT synthesis (5α-reductase inhibitor); takes 6-12 months for maximal effect." },
            { q: "MOA of Degarelix?", a: "<b>GnRH antagonist;</b> provides immediate suppression of testosterone <i>without</i> initial flare (unlike agonists)." },
            { q: "Preferred treatment for PCOS-related Hirsutism?", a: "<b>Spironolactone</b> (aldosterone antagonist, weak AR antagonist) or <b>OCPs</b> (↑ SHBG → ↓ free testosterone)." },
            { q: "MOA of Fludrocortisone?", a: "<b>Potent mineralocorticoid analog</b> with some glucocorticoid activity. <b>Use:</b> Mineralocorticoid replacement in adrenal insufficiency." },
            { q: "Drug used to treat Pheochromocytoma before surgery?", a: "<b>Phenoxybenzamine</b> (irreversible, non-selective alpha-blocker); prevents hypertensive crisis during catecholamine release." },
            { q: "Why must Alpha-blockers be given BEFORE Beta-blockers in Pheochromocytoma?", a: "<b>To prevent hypertensive crisis:</b> If beta-blocked first, unopposed alpha-stimulation → severe hypertension. Alpha first → vasodilation; then beta for tachycardia." },
            { q: "MOA of Aminoglutethimide?", a: "<b>Inhibits conversion of Cholesterol → Pregnenolone</b> (inhibits CYP11A1, side-chain cleavage enzyme) → blocks <i>all</i> steroid synthesis." },
            { q: "Major side effect of Metyrapone?", a: "<b>Hirsutism</b> (due to shunting of 11-deoxycortisol precursors toward androgen synthesis pathway)." },
            { q: "MOA of Pitocin?", a: "<b>Synthetic Oxytocin;</b> binds oxytocin receptors → ↑ uterine muscle contractility. <b>Uses:</b> Labor induction, augmentation, postpartum hemorrhage." },
            { q: "Drug of choice for Central Diabetes Insipidus?", a: "<b>Desmopressin (dDAVP);</b> synthetic V2-selective vasopressin analog (intranasal, IV, or SC)." },
            { q: "Why is Vasopressin used in Septic Shock?", a: "<b>V1 receptor agonist</b> → potent peripheral vasoconstriction; vasopressor effect maintained even when endogenous levels fail." },
            { q: "MOA of Atosiban?", a: "<b>Oxytocin receptor antagonist;</b> blocks uterine muscle contraction. <b>Use:</b> Tocolytic to inhibit preterm labor." },
            { q: "MOA of Goserelin?", a: "<b>GnRH agonist;</b> similar to Leuprolide. <b>Use:</b> Prostate cancer, endometriosis (continuous release implant)." },
            { q: "Effect of Estrogen on Thyroid Binding Globulin (TBG)?", a: "<b>↑↑ TBG synthesis;</b> leads to higher Total T4 and Total T3 (though Free T4/T3 remain normal in euthyroid patients). Important in OCPs." },
            { q: "Which drug class is known to cause 'Blue-green vision' (Cyanopsia)?", a: "<b>PDE-5 inhibitors</b> (Sildenafil, Tadalafil); inhibit PDE-6 in the retina → disrupts photoreceptor cGMP signaling." },
            { q: "MOA of Teduglutide?", a: "<b>GLP-2 analog;</b> enhances intestinal blood flow and mucosal growth. <b>Use:</b> Short Bowel Syndrome to improve nutrient absorption." },
            { q: "Drug of choice for medical management of Prolactinoma?", a: "<b>Cabergoline</b> (preferred over Bromocriptine due to: higher efficacy, better tolerability, fewer side effects)." },
            { q: "MOA of Tranexamic Acid in heavy menstrual bleeding?", a: "<b>Antifibrinolytic;</b> inhibits plasminogen activation → ↓ fibrinolysis → stabilizes clots → ↓ bleeding." },
            { q: "MOA of Clomiphene on the Pituitary?", a: "<b>Blocks estrogen's negative feedback</b> on hypothalamus/pituitary → ↑ GnRH, FSH, LH → stimulates ovulation." },
            { q: "MOA of Sermorelin (diagnostic)?", a: "<b>GHRH analog;</b> used in GHRH stimulation test to assess pituitary GH secretory capacity." },
            { q: "Which somatostatin analog is most effective for Cushing's Disease?", a: "<b>Pasireotide;</b> has much higher affinity for SSTR5 (vs. Octreotide's SSTR2/5), which is prevalent in ACTH-secreting pituitary adenomas." },
            { q: "MOA of Mecasermin rinfabate?", a: "<b>Complex of recombinant IGF-1 + IGFBP-3;</b> extends IGF-1 half-life (from ~10 min to ~20 hrs). <b>Use:</b> Growth failure from severe IGF-1 deficiency." },
            { q: "MOA of the 'Escaped' Wolff-Chaikoff effect?", a: "<b>Downregulation of the sodium-iodide symporter (NIS);</b> reduces iodine entry into thyroid after prolonged high iodide exposure, preventing excessive hormone synthesis." },
            { q: "What is the 'Jod-Basedow' phenomenon?", a: "<b>Iodine-induced hyperthyroidism;</b> occurs when excess iodine given to patient with multinodular goiter or latent Graves' disease → autonomous hormone synthesis." },
            { q: "Effect of glucocorticoids on thyroid hormone metabolism?", a: "<b>↓ peripheral conversion of T4 → T3</b> (inhibit 5'-deiodinase); also slightly ↓ TSH. Used in thyroid storm management." },
            { q: "Management of Myxedema Coma?", a: "<b>IV Levothyroxine (T4, high-dose)</b> + <b>IV Liothyronine (T3)</b> + <b>Stress-dose Glucocorticoids</b> (hydrocortisone). Airway protection as needed." },
            { q: "Which class of DM drugs is associated with 'Fournier's Gangrene'?", a: "<b>SGLT2 Inhibitors</b> (e.g., Empagliflozin, Canagliflozin, Dapagliflozin); rare but serious infection of perineum/genitalia." },
            { q: "MOA of 'Bromocriptine QR' in Type 2 DM?", a: "<b>Resets circadian dopaminergic tone</b> in hypothalamus → ↓ hepatic glucose production + improves insulin sensitivity (unique mechanism vs. other antidiabetics)." },
            { q: "Which DM medication is associated with 'Cholestatic Hepatitis'?", a: "<b>Sulfonylureas</b> (specifically 1st generation like Chlorpropamide); rare hepatotoxicity risk." },
            { q: "Interaction between Salicylates (Aspirin) and Sulfonylureas?", a: "<b>Salicylates displace sulfonylureas from plasma proteins</b> → ↑ free drug → ↑↑ hypoglycemia risk. Monitor glucose closely." },
            { q: "MOA of Welchol (Colesevelam) in DM therapy?", a: "<b>Bile acid sequestrant;</b> activates TGR5 or reduces FXR signaling → ↓ glucose levels. Also ↓ LDL cholesterol." },
            { q: "MOA of Mitotane's selective toxicity?", a: "Metabolized by adrenal mitochondria into reactive acyl chloride → causes focal degeneration of fasciculata and reticularis zones." },
            { q: "Enzyme inhibited by Aminoglutethimide?", a: "<b>CYP11A1</b> (P450scc, Side-chain cleavage enzyme/Desmolase); first step in steroid synthesis." },
            { q: "MOA of LCI699 (Osilodrostat)?", a: "<b>Potent 11β-hydroxylase inhibitor;</b> used for Cushing's disease to lower cortisol. More selective than Ketoconazole." },
            { q: "Why is Dexamethasone used in the 'Dexamethasone Suppression Test'?", a: "It provides potent negative feedback to pituitary ACTH BUT <i>does not</i> interfere with plasma cortisol measurement (unlike hydrocortisone)." },
            { q: "MOA of Denosumab in Giant Cell Tumor of Bone?", a: "<b>Inhibits RANKL</b> (expressed by stromal cells) → prevents osteoclast-mediated bone destruction (unique off-label use)." },
            { q: "MOA of Sodium Fluoride in bone?", a: "<b>↑ osteoblast proliferation</b> + incorporation into bone crystals; however, new bone is often brittle and paradoxically prone to fractures." },
            { q: "Management of Paget's Disease of Bone?", a: "<b>Bisphosphonates</b> (first-line, most potent) or <b>Calcitonin</b> (if bisphosphonate intolerant); reduces bone turnover." },
            { q: "MOA of Calcipotriene?", a: "<b>Vitamin D analog;</b> used topically to treat psoriasis by inhibiting keratinocyte proliferation." },
            { q: "MOA of Toremifene?", a: "<b>SERM</b> similar to Tamoxifen; ER antagonist in breast tissue. <b>Use:</b> Postmenopausal women with ER+ metastatic breast cancer." },
            { q: "MOA of Bazedoxifene (used in Duavee)?", a: "<b>SERM</b> paired with conjugated estrogens; treats menopausal symptoms while protecting endometrium (antagonist in uterus)." },
            { q: "MOA of Degarelix vs. Cetrorelix?", a: "<b>Degarelix:</b> Prostate cancer (continuous long-term suppression). <b>Cetrorelix:</b> Infertility (short-term, prevents LH surge)." },
            { q: "Common side effect of Aromatase Inhibitors (Anastrozole) compared to Tamoxifen?", a: "<b>↑ osteoporosis and joint pain</b> (arthralgias); due to <i>complete</i> estrogen depletion vs. Tamoxifen's partial agonism." },
            { q: "MOA of Indomethacin as a tocolytic?", a: "<b>Non-selective COX inhibitor;</b> ↓ prostaglandin synthesis → inhibits uterine contractions." },
            { q: "Side effect of Indomethacin on the fetus when used as a tocolytic?", a: "<b>Premature closure of Ductus Arteriosus</b> (limits O2 delivery in utero) and <b>Oligohydramnios</b> (↓ renal blood flow)." },
            { q: "MOA of Nifedipine as a tocolytic?", a: "<b>DHP-Calcium Channel Blocker;</b> blocks Ca²⁺ influx into myometrial smooth muscle → relaxation." },
            { q: "MOA of Tesamorelin?", a: "<b>GHRH analog;</b> stimulates GH release. <b>Use:</b> HIV-associated lipodystrophy (reduce abdominal fat accumulation)." },
            { q: "Drug used to treat nocturnal enuresis by increasing water reabsorption?", a: "<b>Desmopressin (dDAVP);</b> V2 receptor agonist → ↑ aquaporin-2 channels in collecting duct → ↑ water reabsorption." },
            { q: "MOA of V2 receptor activation in the collecting duct?", a: "<b>Gs-coupled → ↑ cAMP → PKA phosphorylation → insertion of Aquaporin-2 water channels</b> into apical membrane." },
            { q: "MOA of V1 receptor activation in vascular smooth muscle?", a: "<b>Gq-coupled → ↑ IP3/DAG → ↑ intracellular Ca²⁺ → Vasoconstriction</b> (peripheral vasoconstriction)." },
            { q: "Which endocrine drug is famously associated with 'Disulfiram-like reactions' with alcohol?", a: "<b>Chlorpropamide</b> (1st generation Sulfonylurea); accumulates alcohol → acetaldehyde buildup → flushing, nausea." },
            { q: "Which DM drug's metabolism is affected by CYP2C9 polymorphism?", a: "<b>Sulfonylureas</b> (e.g., Glimepiride); slow metabolizers (Poor Metabolizers) have significantly ↑ risk of prolonged hypoglycemia." },
            { q: "Effect of Cholestyramine on Levothyroxine absorption?", a: "<b>Significantly ↓ absorption;</b> Levothyroxine must be separated by ≥4 hours from bile acid sequestrants." },
            { q: "Why is Methimazole preferred for 'Thyroid Storm' after initial stabilization?", a: "<b>Longer duration of action</b> (~6 hours vs 1.5 hours for PTU) + faster intra-thyroidal action once T4→T3 conversion is controlled." },
            { q: "MOA of Estrogen's effect on total T4 levels?", a: "<b>↑ Thyroid-Binding Globulin (TBG) synthesis</b> in liver → ↑ Total T4 / Total T3 (though Free T4 remains normal in euthyroid patients)." },
            { q: "Why does chronic Glucocorticoid therapy cause 'Steroid Diabetes'?", a: "<b>↑ hepatic gluconeogenesis</b> + <b>↓ peripheral glucose uptake</b> (insulin resistance); direct insulin antagonism." },
            { q: "Effect of Glucocorticoids on Osteoprotegerin (OPG)?", a: "<b>↓ OPG + ↑ RANKL expression</b> → ↑ osteoclast activity → ↓ bone density (glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis)." },
            { q: "MOA of Abiraterone-induced mineralocorticoid excess?", a: "Inhibition of CYP17 → buildup of upstream mineralocorticoid precursors (e.g., <b>Deoxycorticosterone</b>) → mineralocorticoid excess; treated with <b>Prednisone</b> co-administration." },
            { q: "MOA of Menotropins (hMG)?", a: "<b>Purified FSH + LH</b> from urine of postmenopausal women; used for ovulation induction in infertility." },
            { q: "MOA of Urofollitropin?", a: "<b>Purified FSH (recombinant or urine-derived);</b> used for ovulation induction in PCOS or assisted reproduction." },
            { q: "What is 'Ovarian Hyperstimulation Syndrome' (OHSS)?", a: "<b>Life-threatening complication:</b> Ovarian enlargement, ascites, hemoconcentration, thrombosis; mediated by VEGF from stimulated follicles. Treat: IVF pause, fluid resuscitation." },
            { q: "Drug used to treat 'Endometriosis-associated pain' via GnRH antagonism?", a: "<b>Elagolix</b> (oral GnRH antagonist); suppresses gonadotropins → ↓ estrogen without flare." },
            { q: "MOA of Calcitriol vs. Calcifediol?", a: "<b>Calcifediol:</b> 25-(OH)D (storage form). <b>Calcitriol:</b> 1,25-(OH)₂D₃ (active form); used when kidney function impaired." },
            { q: "Why is Calcitriol preferred in patients with end-stage renal disease (ESRD)?", a: "<b>ESRD patients lack 1α-hydroxylase</b> in kidney → cannot convert 25-(OH)D to active Calcitriol; must be given exogenously." },
            { q: "MOA of Denosumab's effect on the immune system?", a: "<b>RANKL expressed on T-lymphocytes;</b> Denosumab may ↑ risk of serious infections (cellulitis, osteomyelitis) - requires monitoring." },
            { q: "MOA of Raloxifene's effect on LDL cholesterol?", a: "<b>Acts as estrogen agonist in liver</b> → ↑ LDL receptor expression → ↓ LDL cholesterol." },
            { q: "Absolute contraindication for OCPs in women >35?", a: "<b>Smoking >15 cigarettes/day;</b> dramatically ↑ risk of thromboembolism and stroke." },
            { q: "Why is the 'Mini-pill' (Progestin-only) preferred for lactating women?", a: "<b>Estrogen inhibits milk production;</b> progestin-only does not affect quantity/quality of breast milk." },
            { q: "Contraindication for Bisphosphonates?", a: "<b>Hypocalcemia</b> (must be corrected first) and esophageal strictures/achalasia." },
            { q: "Which endocrine drug is famously associated with 'Yellow-halo vision'?", a: "<i>Not primarily endocrine:</i> <b>Digoxin</b> (cardiac glycoside); often tested alongside endocrine electrolyte disturbances (Hypokalemia)." },
            { q: "MOA of Bromocriptine in treating Type 2 DM?", a: "<b>Quick-Release (QR) formulation;</b> acts on hypothalamus to modulate circadian dopaminergic tone → ↓ hepatic glucose production + ↑ insulin sensitivity." },
            { q: "MOA of Estrogen's cardioprotective effect?", a: "<b>↑ HDL, ↓ LDL, induces vasodilation</b> via ↑ Nitric Oxide (NO) production; improves endothelial function." },
            { q: "Why do Glucocorticoids cause 'Neutrophilia' while being immunosuppressive?", a: "<b>↓ adhesion molecule expression</b> on neutrophils → 'demargination' from vessel walls into circulation → apparent neutrophilia (not true ↑ production)." },
            { q: "MOA of the interaction between Spironolactone and ACE Inhibitors?", a: "<b>Synergistic ↓ aldosterone effect</b> → additive K⁺ retention → <b>hyperkalemia risk;</b> requires monitoring." },
            { q: "Effect of Thyroid hormone on the heart's Beta-1 receptors?", a: "<b>Upregulation of Beta-1 receptors</b> (↑ number and sensitivity) → ↑ tachycardia and contractility (explains thyroid storm symptoms)." },
            { q: "Which DM drug class is known to ↓ Plasminogen Activator Inhibitor-1 (PAI-1)?", a: "<b>Thiazolidinediones (TZDs);</b> ↓ PAI-1 → improved fibrinolysis → potential cardiovascular benefits." },
            { q: "MOA of 'Somatopause' treatment?", a: "<b>GH or GHRH analog therapy</b> in elderly; increases lean muscle mass, ↓ fat (though clinically controversial for routine use)." },
            { q: "How do Glucocorticoids affect synthesis of Lipocortin-1 (Annexin A1)?", a: "<b>↑ synthesis</b> of Lipocortin-1 → inhibits Phospholipase A₂ → ↓ arachidonic acid release → anti-inflammatory." },
            { q: "Compare the half-lives: Methimazole vs. PTU?", a: "<b>Methimazole:</b> ~6 hours (once-daily dosing acceptable). <b>PTU:</b> ~1.5 hours (requires 2-3 doses/day)." },
            { q: "Primary difference in indication: Alprostadil vs. Misoprostol?", a: "<b>Alprostadil (PGE1):</b> PDA maintenance, erectile dysfunction. <b>Misoprostol:</b> NSAID ulcer prevention, medical abortion." },
            { q: "Comparison: Potency of Dexamethasone vs. Hydrocortisone?", a: "<b>Dexamethasone:</b> ~25-30× more potent with negligible mineralocorticoid activity. <b>Hydrocortisone:</b> ~1× potency with significant mineralocorticoid effect." },
            { q: "Which Endocrine drug causes 'Red-man syndrome' if infused too quickly?", a: "<b>None;</b> but be careful: 'Red-man' (Vancomycin), 'Flushing' (Niacin), 'Hot Flashes' (Tamoxifen/Raloxifene) are distinct." },
            { q: "MOA of Levothyroxine-induced cardiac arrhythmias?", a: "<b>Excessive T4 mimics hyperthyroidism</b> → ↑ myocardial O₂ demand + ↑ sympathetic sensitivity → atrial fibrillation risk." },
            { q: "Side effect of Octreotide on the Exocrine Pancreas?", a: "<b>Steatorrhea</b> (fatty stools); due to inhibition of pancreatic enzyme secretion." },
            { q: "Drug used to treat 'Osteoporosis' that carries risk of Venous Thromboembolism (VTE)?", a: "<b>SERMs</b> like Raloxifene; acts as estrogen agonist in vasculature → ↑ VTE risk (~3× vs placebo)." }
          ]
        },
        {
          topic: 'Clinical Toxicology',
          icon: 'flask',
          cards: [
            { q: "Immediate priority in any acute poisoning?", a: "<b>Stabilization of ABCs</b> (Airway, Breathing, Circulation). This takes precedence over decontamination or history." },
            { q: "Formula for calculating the Anion Gap (AG)?", a: "$\\text{AG} = \\text{Na}^+ - (\\text{Cl}^- + \\text{HCO}_3^-)$<br><b>Normal range:</b> 8–12 mEq/L. Each increase of 1 indicates organic acid accumulation." },
            { q: "Most effective timeframe for Activated Charcoal administration?", a: "<b>Within 1 hour of ingestion</b> is optimal (up to 4 hours may be considered, especially if slow-release formulation)." },
            { q: "Substances poorly adsorbed by Activated Charcoal? (Mnemonic: PHAILS)", a: "<b>Pesticides, Hydrocarbons, Acids/Alkali, Iron, Lithium, Solvents</b> (also Alcohols, Glycols). These require alternative decontamination methods." },
            { q: "Indications for Whole Bowel Irrigation (WBI)?", a: "<b>Body packers,</b> Iron, Lithium, or sustained-release/enteric-coated tablets. Not indicated for most overdoses." },
            { q: "Primary complication of Gastric Lavage?", a: "<b>Aspiration pneumonia</b> or <b>esophageal perforation.</b> Rarely performed now; Activated Charcoal is preferred." },
            { q: "Antidote for Acetaminophen toxicity?", a: "<b>N-acetylcysteine (NAC);</b> available IV or oral. Must be given early for maximum efficacy." },
            { q: "Mechanism of NAC in Acetaminophen overdose?", a: "<b>Restores glutathione (GSH) stores</b> and acts as a glutathione substitute → prevents oxidative damage and hepatotoxicity." },
            { q: "Timeframe for optimal NAC efficacy?", a: "<b>Within 8 hours of ingestion</b> is most effective (Peak hepatotoxicity risk 48–72 hours if untreated)." },
            { q: "Tool used to predict hepatotoxicity after acute Acetaminophen ingestion?", a: "<b>Rumack-Matthew Nomogram;</b> plots serum acetaminophen level vs. time to determine if treatment is needed." },
            { q: "Classic Toxidrome: Mydriasis, tachycardia, hyperthermia, dry skin, urinary retention?", a: "<b>Anticholinergic Toxidrome</b> (Atropine, Tricyclic Antidepressants, Antihistamines). Remember: \"<b>Dry as a bone, Red as a beet, Mad as a hatter.</b>\"" },
            { q: "Classic Toxidrome: Miosis, bradycardia, diaphoresis, salivation, lacrimation?", a: "<b>Cholinergic Toxidrome</b> (Organophosphates, Carbamates). Excessive acetylcholine at both muscarinic and nicotinic receptors." },
            { q: "Cholinergic mnemonic: DUMBELS?", a: "<b>Diarrhea, Urination, Miosis, Bradycardia/Bronchospasm, Emesis, Lacrimation, Salivation.</b> Helps recall muscarinic cholinergic signs." },
            { q: "Antidote for Anticholinergic toxicity (if severe CNS symptoms)?", a: "<b>Physostigmine</b> (cholinesterase inhibitor); crosses BBB → treats central anticholinergic effects." },
            { q: "Contraindication for Physostigmine?", a: "<b>Suspected Tricyclic Antidepressant (TCA) overdose</b> (increases risk of asystole due to synergistic effects)." },
            { q: "Treatment for Organophosphate poisoning (Muscarinic vs. Nicotinic)?", a: "<b>Atropine</b> (treats Muscarinic effects) + <b>Pralidoxime (2-PAM)</b> (treats Nicotinic/muscle fasciculations if given before AChE 'aging')." },
            { q: "First-line treatment for Beta-blocker overdose?", a: "<b>Glucagon</b> (3–10 mg IV bolus, then infusion); increases cAMP via non-adrenergic G-protein pathway." },
            { q: "Treatment for Calcium Channel Blocker (CCB) toxicity (Refractory)?", a: "<b>High-dose Insulin Euglycemic Therapy (HIET)</b>; insulin improves myocardial contractility and systemic perfusion." },
            { q: "ECG finding classic for Digoxin toxicity?", a: "<b>'Scooped' ST segments</b> (Digoxin effect) or any arrhythmia (PVCs most common). Pathognomonic finding." },
            { q: "Electrolyte abnormality that worsens Digoxin toxicity?", a: "<b>Hypokalemia</b> (K⁺ and Digoxin compete for Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase); also Hypomagnesemia, Hypercalcemia." },
            { q: "Definitive treatment for severe Digoxin toxicity?", a: "<b>Digoxin-specific antibody fragments (DigiFab);</b> neutralizes free Digoxin in serum." },
            { q: "Antidote for Heparin reversal?", a: "<b>Protamine sulfate</b> (positively charged, binds negatively charged heparin)." },
            { q: "Antidote for Warfarin (Acute/Life-threatening)?", a: "<b>Fresh Frozen Plasma (FFP)</b> or <b>Prothrombin Complex Concentrate (PCC)</b> (preferred; has higher concentration of factors)." },
            { q: "Antidote for Warfarin (Non-emergent)?", a: "<b>Vitamin K (Phytonadione);</b> takes 12–24 hours to work; slower but safer." },
            { q: "Toxic metabolite of Methanol?", a: "<b>Formic acid</b> (metabolized via alcohol dehydrogenase → formaldehyde → formic acid)." },
            { q: "Characteristic organ damage in Methanol poisoning?", a: "<b>Retinal injury (blindness)</b> and <b>Basal Ganglia necrosis</b> (putamen most affected); permanent blindness can result." },
            { q: "Toxic metabolite of Ethylene Glycol?", a: "<b>Oxalic acid</b> (via glycolic acid intermediate); causes severe organ toxicity." },
            { q: "Characteristic renal finding in Ethylene Glycol poisoning?", a: "<b>Calcium oxalate crystals in urine</b> (envelope-shaped/monohydrate form). Pathognomonic finding." },
            { q: "Preferred inhibitor of Alcohol Dehydrogenase in Methanol/Ethylene Glycol poisoning?", a: "<b>Fomepizole</b> (4-methylpyrazole); blocks toxic metabolite formation; preferred over ethanol (more selective)." },
            { q: "Why is Ethanol used if Fomepizole is unavailable?", a: "<b>Higher affinity for Alcohol Dehydrogenase</b> than toxic alcohols → competes for enzyme → prevents toxic metabolite formation (outdated but still effective)." },
            { q: "Alcohol ingestion with high Osmolal Gap but NO Anion Gap acidosis?", a: "<b>Isopropyl Alcohol</b> (produces acetone; NOT an organic acid); causes intoxication without acidosis." },
            { q: "Classic triad of Wernicke's Encephalopathy?", a: "<b>Ataxia, Ophthalmoplegia (CN VI palsy), Confusion</b> from thiamine deficiency (common in chronic alcoholics)." },
            { q: "Antidote for Benzodiazepine overdose?", a: "<b>Flumazenil</b> (competitive GABA-A antagonist); rapidly reverses CNS depression." },
            { q: "Major risk of Flumazenil use in chronic Benzodiazepine users?", a: "<b>Precipitation of seizures</b> (acute reversal of tolerance); contraindicated if chronic use suspected." },
            { q: "Opioid withdrawal symptoms?", a: "<b>Mydriasis, piloerection (cold turkey), lacrimation, diarrhea, yawning, insomnia, muscle aches.</b> Uncomfortable but not life-threatening." },
            { q: "Treatment for Cocaine-induced chest pain (Class of drug to avoid)?", a: "<b>Avoid Beta-blockers</b> (risk of unopposed α-adrenergic stimulation → coronary vasospasm); use Benzodiazepines + Vasodilators." },
            { q: "Life-threatening complication of MDMA (Ecstasy)?", a: "<b>Hyponatremia</b> (from SIADH + excessive water intake) and <b>Hyperthermia</b> (serotonergic effects on thermoregulation)." },
            { q: "Characteristic 'nystagmus' in PCP (Phencyclidine) intoxication?", a: "<b>Rotary (or vertical/horizontal) nystagmus;</b> highly specific for PCP; also dissociation, ataxia." },
            { q: "Antidote for Cyanide poisoning (classic triple-kit)?", a: "<b>Amyl nitrite, Sodium nitrite, Sodium thiosulfate.</b> Creates Sulfmethemoglobin to bind CN⁻; older method." },
            { q: "Modern preferred Cyanide antidote (turns urine red)?", a: "<b>Hydroxocobalamin</b> (Vitamin B₁₂ₐ); binds CN⁻ → cyanocobalamin (excreted in urine, pink/red); safer, no hypotension." },
            { q: "Treatment for Lead poisoning (Adult vs. Pediatric)?", a: "<b>Adult:</b> Succimer (oral, preferred) or EDTA. <b>Pediatric:</b> Succimer (oral) or Dimercaprol + EDTA (if severe)." },
            { q: "Classic radiographic finding in pediatric Lead poisoning?", a: "<b>Lead lines</b> at the metaphyses of long bones (radiopaque bands). Also: Basophilic stippling on blood smear." },
            { q: "Antidote for Acute Iron poisoning?", a: "<b>Deferoxamine</b> (chelates Fe³⁺); produces <b>vin-rosé (pink-red) colored urine</b> when Fe-deferoxamine complex is excreted." },
            { q: "Antidote for Arsenic or Mercury poisoning?", a: "<b>Dimercaprol (BAL)</b> or <b>Succimer (DMSA).</b> Both are sulfhydryl-containing chelators." },
            { q: "Antidote for Copper toxicity (Wilson's Disease)?", a: "<b>Penicillamine</b> (chelator) or <b>Zinc acetate</b> (reduces copper absorption)." },
            { q: "First-line treatment for Black Widow spider bite (latrodectism)?", a: "<b>Benzodiazepines and Opioids</b> (for muscle pain/spasms); <b>Antivenom</b> if severe systemic effects." },
            { q: "Characteristic skin finding of Brown Recluse bite?", a: "<b>'Red, white, and blue' lesion</b> (central white necrosis → blue-purple surrounding area → red outer rim); loxoscelism (tissue necrosis)." },
            { q: "Indication for CroFab antivenom in Pit Viper bites?", a: "<b>Progressive swelling</b> beyond one joint or <b>systemic coagulopathy</b> (PT prolongation, thrombocytopenia)." },
            { q: "Management of Carbon Monoxide (CO) poisoning?", a: "<b>100% O₂</b> (reduces CO-Hb half-life from ~4 hrs to ~1 hr); <b>Hyperbaric O₂</b> if severe/LOC/cardiac complications." },
            { q: "Color of blood in Methemoglobinemia?", a: "<b>Chocolate-colored blood</b> (dark brown); does not turn bright red with aeration (key finding)." },
            { q: "Treatment for Methemoglobinemia?", a: "<b>Methylene Blue</b> (IV) if symptomatic; reduces Met-Hb back to normal Hb (not effective in G6PD deficiency)." },
            { q: "Triad of Serotonin Syndrome?", a: "<b>1. Autonomic instability</b> (HTN, tachycardia), <b>2. Altered mental status</b> (agitation, confusion), <b>3. Neuromuscular irritability</b> (hyperreflexia, clonus)." },
            { q: "Antidote for Serotonin Syndrome?", a: "<b>Cyproheptadine</b> (5-HT₂ antagonist); stops serotonin-mediated effects at the receptor." },
            { q: "Key difference between Serotonin Syndrome and Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome (NMS)?", a: "<b>NMS:</b> Lead-pipe rigidity, fever, bradykinesia. <b>Serotonin Syndrome:</b> Hyperreflexia/clonus (more common), GI symptoms." },
            { q: "Treatment for NMS?", a: "<b>Dantrolene</b> (directly relaxes muscle) or <b>Bromocriptine</b> (dopamine agonist); stop antipsychotic immediately." },
            { q: "ECG hallmark of TCA toxicity?", a: "<b>Widened QRS complex</b> (>120 ms) due to Na⁺ channel blockade; indicates serious toxicity." },
            { q: "Treatment for TCA-induced QRS widening?", a: "<b>Sodium Bicarbonate ($\\text{NaHCO}_3$)</b>; increases serum pH → decreases drug's Na⁺ channel affinity." },
            { q: "Treatment for Lithium toxicity (Severe)?", a: "<b>Hemodialysis</b> (Li⁺ is water-soluble and not protein-bound; effectively removed by dialysis)." },
            { q: "Isoniazid (INH) toxicity triad?", a: "<b>Seizures, Acidosis, Coma</b> (INH depletes Pyridoxine via competitive inhibition)." },
            { q: "Antidote for Isoniazid-induced seizures?", a: "<b>Pyridoxine (Vitamin B₆);</b> give gram-for-gram to the amount of INH ingested (e.g., 5g INH → 5g Pyridoxine)." },
            { q: "Salicylate poisoning (Early vs. Late acid-base status)?", a: "<b>Early:</b> Respiratory Alkalosis (salicylates stimulate respiratory center). <b>Late:</b> Mixed AG Metabolic Acidosis + Respiratory Alkalosis." },
            { q: "Effect of Salicylates on the mitochondria?", a: "<b>Uncoupling of oxidative phosphorylation</b> → ↓ ATP → hyperthermia, pulmonary edema, metabolic acidosis." },
            { q: "Why is Sodium Bicarbonate used in Salicylate poisoning?", a: "<b>To alkalinize urine</b> (Ion Trapping): keeps salicylate ionized → prevents renal reabsorption → enhances urinary excretion." },
            { q: "Classic finding in Theophylline toxicity?", a: "<b>Severe hypokalemia</b> and <b>seizures</b>; causes tachycardia, arrhythmias, and tremor." },
            { q: "Toxicity of Sulfonylureas (Management)?", a: "<b>Octreotide</b> (somatostatin analog); inhibits insulin release from pancreatic Beta cells." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Carbon Monoxide toxicity?", a: "<b>Left-shift of the Oxygen-Hemoglobin dissociation curve</b> (inhibits O₂ unloading at tissues); carboxyhemoglobin (CO-Hb) has 200-250× higher affinity than O₂." },
            { q: "Mushroom toxin causing delayed hepatic failure (Amanita phalloides)?", a: "<b>α-amanitin</b> (inhibits RNA polymerase II → blocks protein synthesis); causes fatal hepatotoxicity in <24-48 hrs." },
            { q: "Antidote for Valproic acid-induced hyperammonemia?", a: "<b>L-carnitine</b> (propionyl-CoA is conjugated to carnitine → excreted; bypasses the accumulation)." },
            { q: "What causes 'Red Man Syndrome' and how to treat?", a: "<b>Vancomycin</b> (rapid infusion) causes non-immunologic mast cell degranulation → histamine release. <b>Treatment:</b> Slow infusion + Antihistamines (Diphenhydramine)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Cyanide's cellular toxicity?", a: "<b>Inhibits Cytochrome c Oxidase (Complex IV)</b> in the electron transport chain → blocks aerobic respiration → tissue hypoxia." },
            { q: "Characteristic breath odor of Arsenic/Organophosphates?", a: "<b>Garlic-like odor</b> (from volatile arsine or phosphine compounds)." },
            { q: "Breath odor of Cyanide?", a: "<b>Bitter almonds</b> (not detectable by all people due to genetic variation in smell perception)." },
            { q: "Management of Hydrofluoric Acid skin burns?", a: "<b>Topical Calcium Gluconate gel</b> (topical or intradermal) to bind fluoride ions and prevent systemic toxicity." },
            { q: "Drug used to 'trap' weakly acidic drugs in urine?", a: "<b>Sodium Bicarbonate</b> (Alkalinization); keeps drug ionized → prevents reabsorption (e.g., Salicylates, Barbiturates)." },
            { q: "Drug used to 'trap' weakly basic drugs in urine?", a: "<b>Ammonium Chloride</b> (Acidification) — though <i>rarely used</i> clinically due to metabolic risks." },
            { q: "Classic feature of Ciguatera fish poisoning?", a: "<b>Temperature reversal</b> (paradoxical dysesthesia: cold feels hot, hot feels cold); also GI symptoms, neurologic effects." },
            { q: "Ethylene Glycol vs. Methanol (Commonality)?", a: "<b>Both cause:</b> High Anion Gap Acidosis + High Osmolal Gap; treated similarly with Fomepizole/Ethanol." },
            { q: "Most common cause of death in TCA toxicity?", a: "<b>Arrhythmias</b> (from Na⁺ channel blockade → QRS widening → ventricular dysrhythmias)." },
            { q: "Visual 'halos' or yellow-green vision?", a: "<b>Digoxin toxicity</b> (Xanthopsia); Van Gogh-like visual disturbances due to effects on retinal cones." },
            { q: "Treatment for Body Packers (leaking drug packets)?", a: "<b>Surgical intervention</b> if packets are obstructed or leaking; NOT WBI or activated charcoal (risk of rupture)." },
            { q: "Antidote for Beta-blockers if Glucagon fails?", a: "<b>High-dose Insulin</b> or <b>Intravenous Lipid Emulsion (ILE);</b> lipophilic drugs sequestered in lipid 'sink'." },
            { q: "Which metal toxicity causes 'Mad Hatter' symptoms?", a: "<b>Mercury</b> (Erethism mercurialis): tremor, behavioral changes, irritability, personality changes; 'mad as a hatter'." },
            { q: "Management of extrapyramidal symptoms (EPS) from Metoclopramide?", a: "<b>Diphenhydramine</b> or <b>Benztropine</b> (anticholinergic agents reverse dystonia/akathisia)." },
            { q: "Why is Glucose given WITH Thiamine in alcoholics?", a: "<b>Thiamine BEFORE Glucose</b> to prevent Wernicke's precipitation: glucose without thiamine → worsening of CNS damage." },
            { q: "'Rice-water stools' and QTc prolongation?", a: "<b>Arsenic poisoning</b> (causes gastroenteritis with watery diarrhea and cardiac arrhythmias)." },
            { q: "Methemoglobinemia inducer (Classic antibiotic)?", a: "<b>Dapsone</b> or <b>Sulfonamides</b> (oxidize Fe²⁺ to Fe³⁺)." },
            { q: "Treatment for Cocaine-induced hypertensive crisis?", a: "<b>Phentolamine</b> (α-blocker) or <b>Benzodiazepines;</b> <i>avoid</i> Beta-blockers (unopposed α-stimulation)." },
            { q: "Characteristic CBC finding in chronic Lead poisoning?", a: "<b>Basophilic stippling</b> on blood smear (precipitated ribosomes in RBCs)." },
            { q: "Characteristic bone marrow finding in Sideroblastic anemia (Lead)?", a: "<b>Ringed sideroblasts</b> (iron-laden mitochondria in erythroid precursors)." },
            { q: "Drug-induced Lupus (common toxins)?", a: "<b>Procainamide, Hydralazine, Isoniazid (HIP);</b> causes positive ANA and anti-histone antibodies." },
            { q: "Tinnitus and hyperpnea?", a: "<b>Salicylate (Aspirin) toxicity;</b> tinnitus is early sign; hyperpnea reflects respiratory alkalosis." },
            { q: "Treatment for severe Methanol toxicity if Fomepizole fails?", a: "<b>Hemodialysis</b> and <b>Folate</b> (to help convert formate → CO₂)." },
            { q: "Treatment for severe Ethylene Glycol toxicity if Fomepizole fails?", a: "<b>Hemodialysis</b> and <b>Thiamine/Pyridoxine</b> (cofactors for detoxification pathways)." },
            { q: "Gray Baby Syndrome cause?", a: "<b>Chloramphenicol</b> (in neonates with immature liver enzymes); causes cardiovascular collapse, gray discoloration." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Local Anesthetic systemic toxicity (LAST)?", a: "<b>Sodium channel blockade;</b> treat with <b>Lipid Emulsion</b> (binds lipophilic local anesthetic → removes from heart)." },
            { q: "First-line for Chlorine gas inhalation?", a: "<b>Humidified oxygen</b> and <b>nebulized Sodium Bicarbonate</b> (neutralizes acidic chlorine)." },
            { q: "'Dry as a bone, Red as a beet, Mad as a hatter'?", a: "<b>Anticholinergic Toxidrome</b> mnemonic; includes: Dilated pupils, Hyperthermia, Tachycardia, Dry skin." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Cocaine's effect on neurotransmitters?", a: "<b>Inhibits reuptake</b> of <b>Norepinephrine, Dopamine, and Serotonin</b> → ↑ synaptic levels → sympathomimetic effects." },
            { q: "Organophosphate mnemonic: SLUDGE?", a: "<b>Salivation, Lacrimation, Urination, Defecation, GI distress, Emesis</b> (muscarinic cholinergic effects)." },
            { q: "Antidote for Isoniazid (Primary CNS risk)?", a: "<b>Pyridoxine (Vitamin B₆);</b> replaces INH-consumed cofactor." },
            { q: "'Mees' lines' (transverse white bands on nails)?", a: "<b>Arsenic poisoning</b> (horizontal white bands on fingernails appear weeks after acute exposure)." },
            { q: "Difference between Cocaine and Amphetamine pupils?", a: "<b>Both cause Mydriasis,</b> but <b>Cocaine:</b> Shorter duration, localized vasoconstriction. <b>Amphetamine:</b> Longer duration, systemic stimulation." },
            { q: "Management of Serotonin Syndrome hyperthermia?", a: "<b>External cooling</b> and <b>Benzodiazepines</b> (not Antipyretics like Acetaminophen, which are ineffective)." },
            { q: "Most common ECG abnormality in CO poisoning?", a: "<b>Sinus tachycardia;</b> ST-depression suggests underlying ischemia." },
            { q: "Gold standard for measuring Carboxyhemoglobin?", a: "<b>Arterial/Venous Blood Gas with Co-oximetry</b> (Pulse Ox is <i>unreliable</i> in CO poisoning)." },
            { q: "Why is pulse oximetry normal in Methemoglobinemia?", a: "Pulse Ox often 'reads' Met-Hb as ~85%, <i>regardless of true saturation</i> (due to light absorption at two wavelengths)." },
            { q: "Fatal dose of Acetaminophen (Adult)?", a: "Approx. <b>150 mg/kg</b> or <b>>7.5–10 grams</b> (highly variable based on timing and metabolism)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of action of Pralidoxime (2-PAM)?", a: "<b>Regenerates active Acetylcholinesterase</b> by removing the phosphate group from the enzyme (if given <b>before 'aging'</b> occurs)." },
            { q: "Why is Atropine given BEFORE Pralidoxime in Organophosphate poisoning?", a: "To prevent transient worsening of symptoms; 2-PAM has weak transient AChE inhibition effects." },
            { q: "Antidote for Magnesium Sulfate toxicity?", a: "<b>Calcium Gluconate</b> (antagonizes neuromuscular blockade from excess Mg²⁺)." },
            { q: "Specific antidote for Hydrofluoric acid systemic toxicity?", a: "<b>Intravenous Calcium</b> and <b>Magnesium</b> (HF causes profound hypocalcemia/hypomagnesemia → cardiac arrhythmias)." },
            { q: "Treatment for Bromide toxicity (Bromism)?", a: "<b>Sodium Chloride</b> (Chloride competes with Bromide for renal reabsorption → enhances excretion)." },
            { q: "Antidote for Caffeine or Theophylline-induced tachyarrhythmias?", a: "<b>Esmolol</b> (short-acting Beta-blocker); preferred for rapid reversibility." },
            { q: "Formula for Serum Osmolality?", a: "$\\text{Osmolality} = 2(\\text{Na}^+) + \\frac{\\text{Glucose}}{18} + \\frac{\\text{BUN}}{2.8}$" },
            { q: "Formula for Osmolal Gap?", a: "$\\text{Osmolal Gap} = \\text{Measured Osmolality} - \\text{Calculated Osmolality}$<br><b>Normal:</b> < 10 mOsm/kg. Elevated = unmeasured solutes (toxic alcohols)." },
            { q: "Causes of 'Normal Anion Gap' Metabolic Acidosis (HARDUP)?", a: "<b>Hyperalimentation, Acetazolamide, Renal Tubular Acidosis, Diarrhea, Ureteroenteric fistula, Pancreatic fistula.</b>" },
            { q: "Co-oximetry finding in Methemoglobinemia?", a: "<b>Absorption peak at 630 nm</b> (characteristic wavelength); allows quantitative measurement of Met-Hb." },
            { q: "What does 'Double Gap' refer to in Toxicology?", a: "<b>Simultaneous presence of Anion Gap + Osmolal Gap</b> (Typical of Methanol/Ethylene Glycol poisoning)." },
            { q: "Toxin in Foxglove (Digitalis purpurea)?", a: "<b>Cardiac glycosides</b> (Digoxin-like effect); similar ECG changes and toxicity." },
            { q: "Toxin in Jimson Weed (Datura stramonium)?", a: "<b>Scopolamine/Atropine</b> (causes Anticholinergic toxidrome); 'Datura high' historically used as drug." },
            { q: "Poison Hemlock (Conium maculatum) mechanism?", a: "<b>Nicotinic receptor antagonist</b> (causes ascending paralysis similar to curare); tremor, salivation, then paralysis." },
            { q: "Clinical effect of Water Hemlock (Cicutoxin)?", a: "<b>Status epilepticus</b> (GABA antagonist); severe seizures refractory to benzodiazepines." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Ricin (Castor beans)?", a: "<b>Inhibits the 60S ribosomal subunit</b> → stops protein synthesis → cellular death; highly toxic." },
            { q: "Management of Jellyfish stings (Box jellyfish)?", a: "<b>Acetic acid (Vinegar)</b> to deactivate nematocysts; avoid fresh water (causes nematocyst firing)." },
            { q: "'Monday Disease' in dynamite factory workers?", a: "<b>Nitroglycerin exposure</b>; weekend withdrawal causes vasospasm and chest pain (tolerance/dependence)." },
            { q: "Most common cause of death in Fire victims (besides burns)?", a: "<b>Combined Cyanide and Carbon Monoxide poisoning</b> (from incomplete combustion of polymers)." },
            { q: "Vinyl Chloride chronic exposure risk?", a: "<b>Hepatic Angiosarcoma</b> (rare but aggressive malignancy); used in plastics manufacturing." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Paraquat (herbicide) toxicity?", a: "<b>Redox cycling produces reactive oxygen species (ROS)</b> → progressive Pulmonary Fibrosis (fatal)." },
            { q: "Ethylene Dibromide primary organ toxicity?", a: "<b>Severe hepatic necrosis and nephrotoxicity</b>; fumigant exposure." },
            { q: "Most common cause of pediatric poisoning death (supplement)?", a: "<b>Iron</b> (from prenatal vitamins containing Fe²⁺/Fe³⁺); highly toxic in young children." },
            { q: "Management of a child who swallowed a single 'button battery' (Asymptomatic, in stomach)?", a: "<b>Wait and watch</b> (repeat X-ray); if in esophagus, it is a surgical emergency (causes liquefactive necrosis)." },
            { q: "Toxic dose of Salicylates in children?", a: "$>150 \\text{ mg/kg}$ (lower threshold than adults due to immature metabolism)." },
            { q: "Why is Oil of Wintergreen dangerous in small amounts for children?", a: "<b>Contains concentrated Methyl Salicylate</b> (1 tsp ≈ 21 adult aspirins); can cause fatal toxicity in infants." },
            { q: "Earliest sign of Ethanol withdrawal?", a: "<b>Tremulousness</b> ('The Shakes') at 6–12 hours; progresses to hallucinations, seizures, delirium tremens." },
            { q: "Timeframe for Delirium Tremens (DTs)?", a: "<b>48–96 hours</b> after last drink; most serious withdrawal manifestation (hallucinations, autonomic instability)." },
            { q: "Treatment for Alcohol withdrawal seizures?", a: "<b>Benzodiazepines</b> (Lorazepam, Diazepam); Phenytoin is ineffective in alcohol withdrawal." },
            { q: "Neonatal Abstinence Syndrome (NAS) symptoms?", a: "<b>High-pitched cry, tremors, sneezing, diarrhea, tachypnea</b> (hyperreflexia, irritability); appears 24–72 hrs after birth." },
            { q: "GHB (Gamma-Hydroxybutyrate) withdrawal hallmark?", a: "<b>Severe agitation and autonomic instability</b> (similar to severe EtOH withdrawal); extremely difficult to manage." },
            { q: "Preferred route for NAC (IV vs Oral)?", a: "<b>Both effective;</b> IV preferred for: vomiting, hepatic failure, or pregnancy (faster onset, bypasses GI)." },
            { q: "Indication for Urinary Alkalinization in Phenobarbital overdose?", a: "<b>Enhances elimination</b> of weak acids (Phenobarbital is ionized at high pH → prevented reabsorption)." },
            { q: "Why avoid Ipecac syrup in modern toxicology?", a: "<b>Risk of aspiration</b> and interference with Activated Charcoal/Endoscopy; rarely used now." },
            { q: "Dose-limiting toxicity of Deferoxamine?", a: "<b>ARDS</b> (Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome) with prolonged infusions (>24 hrs); pulmonary toxicity." },
            { q: "Primary treatment for Hydrocarbon aspiration?", a: "<b>Supportive care;</b> avoid Gastric Lavage (↑ risk of aspiration)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Pyridoxine in Ethylene Glycol poisoning?", a: "<b>Diverts Glyoxylic acid → Glycine</b> (non-toxic) instead of Oxalate (toxic)." },
            { q: "'Saturday Night Palsy' in a toxic patient?", a: "<b>Radial nerve compression</b> due to prolonged immobilization while intoxicated (e.g., 'passed out')." },
            { q: "Mechanism of 'Red Man Syndrome' from Vancomycin?", a: "<b>Non-immunologic (Direct) mast cell degranulation</b> → histamine release; not a true IgE allergy." },
            { q: "Management of Valproic Acid-induced hyperammonemic encephalopathy?", a: "<b>L-carnitine supplementation</b> (enhances carnitine conjugation of excess metabolites)." },
            { q: "Classic ECG finding in Citalopram/Escitalopram overdose?", a: "<b>QTc prolongation</b> (risk of Torsades de Pointes); can be fatal." },
            { q: "Management of Dystonic reaction from Antipsychotics?", a: "<b>Diphenhydramine (IV/IM)</b> or <b>Benztropine</b> (anticholinergic rapidly reverses dystonia)." },
            { q: "Why is Glucose alone insufficient in an alcoholic with hypoglycemia?", a: "<b>Must give Thiamine FIRST</b> to prevent Wernicke's Encephalopathy (glucose precipitates encephalopathy if thiamine depleted)." },
            { q: "Drug of choice for hypertensive crisis caused by MAO-Inhibitors + Tyramine?", a: "<b>Phentolamine</b> (α-blocker; directly dilates vessels); <i>avoid</i> Beta-blockers (unopposed α-stimulation)." },
            { q: "Propofol Infusion Syndrome (PRIS) hallmarks?", a: "<b>Metabolic acidosis, rhabdomyolysis, hyperkalemia, cardiac failure</b> (from lipid emulsion infusion; rare but fatal)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Methemoglobinemia caused by Nitrites?", a: "<b>Oxidizes Ferrous iron (Fe²⁺) to Ferric iron (Fe³⁺)</b> in hemoglobin → cannot bind O₂." },
            { q: "High-yield source of Acrylonitrile poisoning?", a: "<b>Burning plastics and synthetic rubber</b> (presents similarly to Cyanide); blocks aerobic respiration." },
            { q: "Treatment for Hydrogen Sulfide ($H_2S$) poisoning?", a: "<b>Nitrites</b> (to create Sulfmethemoglobin) and <b>100% Oxygen;</b> remove from exposure immediately." },
            { q: "Toxic element in 'Magic Shaving Powder' or older depilatories?", a: "<b>Barium</b> (causes profound hypokalemia and paralysis); historical toxin." },
            { q: "Cause of 'Metal Fume Fever'?", a: "<b>Inhalation of Zinc Oxide fumes</b> (common in welding); self-limited illness with flulike symptoms." },
            { q: "Toxin in Pufferfish (Fugu)?", a: "<b>Tetrodotoxin</b> (one of the most toxic nonprotein substances known); lethal dose ~1 mg/kg." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Tetrodotoxin?", a: "<b>Blocks voltage-gated Sodium channels</b> → prevents action potential initiation → paralysis." },
            { q: "Scombroid poisoning cause?", a: "<b>Histamine ingestion</b> from improperly refrigerated fish (Tuna, Mahi-mahi); bacteria produce histamine from histidine." },
            { q: "Scombroid poisoning presentation?", a: "<b>Flushed skin, peppery taste in mouth, headache, diarrhea</b> (mimic of allergic reaction); treat with Antihistamines." },
            { q: "Toxin in 'Death Cap' mushrooms (Amanita phalloides)?", a: "<b>Amatoxin</b> (inhibits RNA polymerase II) → fatal hepatotoxicity; no antidote." },
            { q: "'Cat's eye' (Mydriasis) + High Anion Gap Acidosis?", a: "<b>Methanol poisoning</b> (specific combination of signs)." },
            { q: "Hypocalcemia + High Anion Gap Acidosis?", a: "<b>Ethylene Glycol poisoning</b> (Calcium is consumed by oxalate crystals)." },
            { q: "Seizures + High Anion Gap Acidosis (Refractory to BZDs)?", a: "<b>Isoniazid (INH) toxicity</b> (depletes Pyridoxine → seizures)." },
            { q: "Normal AG Acidosis + Nephrolithiasis + Hypokalemia?", a: "<b>Topiramate toxicity</b> (Carbonic Anhydrase inhibition); causes RTA-like picture." },
            { q: "Indication for Hemodialysis in Salicylate poisoning?", a: "<b>Level > 100 mg/dL (acute),</b> altered mental status, or renal failure." },
            { q: "Indication for Hemodialysis in Lithium poisoning?", a: "<b>Level > 4.0 mEq/L (acute)</b> or <b>> 2.5 mEq/L (chronic with symptoms).</b>" },
            { q: "When is Multi-dose Activated Charcoal (MDAC) indicated?", a: "<b>For 'ABCD-Q':</b> Theophylline, Phenobarbital, Carbamazepine, Dapsone, Quinine (enterohepatic circulation drugs)." },
            { q: "Maximum duration for NAC infusion (Standard IV protocol)?", a: "<b>21 hours</b> (total of 3 bags: 150 mg/kg loading dose, then 2 maintenance doses)." },
            { q: "Primary risk of performing Gastric Lavage on a Hydrocarbon ingestion?", a: "<b>Chemical pneumonitis</b> (aspiration risk outweighs benefit); supportive care preferred." },
            { q: "Most common cause of late-onset death in Paraquat ingestion?", a: "<b>Progressive Pulmonary Fibrosis</b> (develops over days/weeks); untreatable once established." },
            { q: "Why should you avoid 'Vigorous' suctioning in a Caustic ingestion?", a: "<b>Risk of esophageal perforation;</b> gentle suctioning only if airway threatened." },
            { q: "Drug used to treat Serotonin Syndrome when BZDs fail?", a: "<b>Cyproheptadine</b> (5-HT₂ antagonist; stops serotonin-mediated effects)." },
            { q: "Name of the scale used to assess Opioid withdrawal?", a: "<b>COWS</b> (Clinical Opiate Withdrawal Scale); helps standardize assessment and guide treatment." },
            { q: "Treatment for Black Widow spider bite with severe muscle spasms?", a: "<b>Benzodiazepines and Antivenom</b> (if available/severe); pain control essential." },
            { q: "Classic feature of Ergotism?", a: "<b>Intense vasoconstriction</b> leading to 'St. Anthony's Fire' (burning pain and gangrene); from Ergot fungus on rye." },
            { q: "The 'Antidote' for Beta-blockers that acts via Myocardial G-protein?", a: "<b>Glucagon</b> (bypasses β-adrenergic receptor → activates G-protein directly)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Octreotide in Sulfonylurea overdose?", a: "<b>Somatostatin analog</b> that suppresses Insulin release from pancreatic Beta cells." },
            { q: "Zero-order kinetics definition?", a: "<b>Constant amount</b> of drug is eliminated per unit time (independent of concentration); e.g., Ethanol, high-dose Salicylates, Phenytoin." },
            { q: "First-order kinetics definition?", a: "<b>Constant fraction</b> of drug is eliminated per unit time (concentration-dependent); Most drugs follow this." },
            { q: "Volume of Distribution ($V_d$) formula?", a: "$V_d = \\frac{\\text{Total amount of drug in body}}{\\text{Plasma drug concentration}}$" },
            { q: "How does $V_d$ affect hemodialysis efficacy?", a: "<b>Large $V_d$ (> 1 L/kg)</b> = poorly dialyzable (drug 'hidden' in tissues). <b>Small $V_d$</b> = dialyzable." },
            { q: "Definition of 'Enterohepatic Circulation' in toxicology?", a: "<b>Drugs excreted in bile are reabsorbed in small intestine</b> (e.g., TCAs, Phenobarbital); targeted by Multi-dose Activated Charcoal." },
            { q: "Specific ECG change in Phenothiazine (Antipsychotic) overdose?", a: "<b>Blunting of T-waves and prominent U-waves</b> (can lead to QTc prolongation)." },
            { q: "ECG sign of hyperkalemia (Common in Digoxin/Renal tox)?", a: "<b>Peaked T-waves, PR prolongation, eventually 'Sine Wave' pattern</b> (widened QRS → flattened P)." },
            { q: "Brugada Pattern (Pseudo-right bundle branch block) associated with which toxicity?", a: "<b>Sodium Channel Blocker overdose</b> (e.g., Cocaine, TCAs, Flecainide); high mortality risk." },
            { q: "'Osborn Wave' (J-wave) on ECG?", a: "<b>Characteristic of Hypothermia</b> (often in intoxicated 'found down' patients); distinctive hump at junction." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Cyanogen Chloride or Hydrogen Cyanide?", a: "<b>Arrests aerobic respiration</b> by inhibiting Cytochrome c Oxidase; blocks entire electron transport chain." },
            { q: "Nerve agent (Sarin, VX) mechanism?", a: "<b>Irreversible inhibition of Acetylcholinesterase</b> (more potent than organophosphates); causes fatal cholinergic crisis." },
            { q: "Difference between Carbamate and Organophosphate poisoning?", a: "<b>Carbamate:</b> Reversible binding, no 'aging'; 2-PAM usually not required. <b>OP:</b> Irreversible → 2-PAM essential." },
            { q: "Primary cause of death in Cholinergic crisis?", a: "<b>'The Killer Bs': Bronchospasm and Bronchorrhea</b> → respiratory failure (ARDS)." },
            { q: "Pregnancy category of ACE Inhibitors/ARBs?", a: "<b>Teratogenic (Category X/D);</b> causes fetal renal dysgenesis and skull hypoplasia (especially 2nd/3rd trimester)." },
            { q: "Preferred treatment for Lead poisoning in a pregnant patient?", a: "<b>Chelation generally avoided</b> unless maternal life at risk; consult specialists (Succimer is often used if necessary)." },
            { q: "Fetal Alcohol Syndrome (FAS) facial features?", a: "<b>Smooth philtrum, thin upper lip, short palpebral fissures</b> (microcephaly, intellectual disability, cardiac defects)." },
            { q: "Is NAC safe to give in pregnancy for Acetaminophen overdose?", a: "<b>Yes;</b> crosses placenta and protects fetus; benefits outweigh minimal risks." },
            { q: "'Red wheals' or 'flagellate dermatitis' after eating mushrooms?", a: "<b>Shiitake Mushroom dermatitis</b> (irritant contact dermatitis from lentinan)." },
            { q: "Treatment for Methemoglobinemia in patients with G6PD deficiency?", a: "<b>Avoid Methylene Blue</b> (↑ risk of hemolysis); use Vitamin C or Exchange Transfusion." },
            { q: "Definition of the 'Window Period' in Acetaminophen ingestion?", a: "<b>The time after initial GI symptoms resolve but BEFORE liver enzymes (ALT/AST) spike;</b> patient may appear well." },
            { q: "Why is Calcium Chloride preferred over Calcium Gluconate in a code situation?", a: "<b>Calcium Chloride provides 3× more elemental calcium</b> and does not require hepatic metabolism (faster onset)." },
            { q: "Management of Beta-blocker/CCB overdose (the 'Lipid Sink' theory)?", a: "<b>Intravenous Lipid Emulsion (ILE)</b> to sequester lipophilic drugs from target organs." },
            { q: "Mechanism of N-acetylcysteine in non-Acetaminophen liver failure?", a: "<b>Improves systemic hemodynamics</b> and oxygen delivery to tissues; may improve outcomes." },
            { q: "What toxin causes 'Phossy Jaw' (bone necrosis)?", a: "<b>White Phosphorus</b> (industrial exposure); causes osteonecrosis of jaw." },
            { q: "Mechanism of 'Body Stuffers' vs. 'Body Packers'?", a: "<b>Stuffers:</b> Swallow small amounts in panic (↑ rupture risk). <b>Packers:</b> Professional transport (well-wrapped, ↓ immediate risk)." },
            { q: "Name of the criteria used to diagnose Serotonin Syndrome?", a: "<b>Hunter Criteria</b> (combination of: GI, Autonomic, Neuromuscular, or Mental status changes after serotonergic drug use)." },
            { q: "How to treat Valproic Acid overdose with metabolic crisis?", a: "<b>Hemodialysis and L-carnitine</b> (addresses both toxin removal and metabolic abnormalities)." },
            { q: "The 'Tox-Handshake': Four things to check in every poisoned patient?", a: "<b>1. Mental Status</b> (Glasgow Coma Scale), <b>2. Pupil Size</b> (miosis/mydriasis), <b>3. Skin Moisture/Temperature</b> (diaphoresis/flushed), <b>4. Vital Signs.</b>" },
            { q: "Definition of 'The Toxic Window'?", a: "<b>The time between drug ingestion and onset of organ damage</b> where intervention (like NAC for APAP) is most effective." },
            { q: "Most common cause of falsely elevated Pulse Oximetry reading?", a: "<b>Carbon Monoxide poisoning</b> (Carboxyhemoglobin is misread as Oxyhemoglobin by standard pulse ox)." },
            { q: "Most common cause of falsely LOW Pulse Oximetry reading (around 85%)?", a: "<b>Methemoglobinemia</b> (Met-Hb absorbs light differently; pulse ox 'clips' at ~85%)." },
            { q: "Formula for 'Clearing' a drug via Hemodialysis?", a: "<b>High water solubility, low molecular weight (<500 Da), low protein binding, small Volume of Distribution ($V_d$).</b>" },
            { q: "Mechanism of 'Ion Trapping' in the urine?", a: "<b>Adjusting urine pH</b> to keep toxin ionized (charged) → prevents reabsorption across renal tubule." },
            { q: "Why is Sodium Bicarbonate ($NaHCO_3$) given in Sodium Channel Blocker toxicity (e.g., TCAs)?", a: "<b>Increases extracellular Na⁺ and serum pH</b> → decreases drug's Na⁺ channel affinity; also hypertonicity helps." },
            { q: "Dose of Glucagon for Beta-blocker overdose?", a: "<b>3–10 mg IV bolus,</b> followed by continuous infusion (significantly higher than hypoglycemia doses)." },
            { q: "Target Blood Glucose in High-Dose Insulin Euglycemic Therapy (HIET)?", a: "<b>100–200 mg/dL</b> (requires concurrent concentrated Dextrose infusion to prevent hypoglycemia)." },
            { q: "Primary side effect of IV Methylene Blue?", a: "<b>Serotonin Syndrome</b> (it is a potent MAO-inhibitor); can be life-threatening if combined with SSRIs." },
            { q: "First-line treatment for Isoniazid (INH) induced status epilepticus?", a: "<b>Pyridoxine (Vitamin B₆)</b> given gram-for-gram to the amount of INH ingested." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Hydrofluoric Acid (HF) toxicity?", a: "<b>Liquefactive necrosis</b> and profound systemic Hypocalcemia/Hypomagnesemia from Fluoride ion binding." },
            { q: "Treatment for 'Phosgene' gas inhalation?", a: "<b>Supportive care (O₂/Rest);</b> there is NO specific antidote; pulmonary edema is main complication." },
            { q: "Classic symptom of 'Aconitine' (Monkshood) poisoning?", a: "<b>Perioral numbness</b> and fatal ventricular arrhythmias ('The Queen of Poisons'); no antidote." },
            { q: "Mechanism of 'Strychnine' poisoning?", a: "<b>Antagonizes Glycine receptors</b> in spinal cord → removes inhibitory tone → 'risus sardonicus' and tetanic convulsions." },
            { q: "Clinical triad of 'Ergotism'?", a: "<b>1. Severe burning pain (St. Anthony's Fire),</b> <b>2. Muscle spasms,</b> <b>3. Gangrene.</b>" },
            { q: "'Meth Mouth' pathophysiology?", a: "<b>Combination of: drug-induced xerostomia (dry mouth), poor hygiene, teeth grinding (bruxism).</b>" },
            { q: "Primary cause of death in 'Bath Salts' (Synthetic Cathinones) intoxication?", a: "<b>Excited Delirium</b> leading to Hyperthermia and Rhabdomyolysis; profound sympathomimetic effects." },
            { q: "'Krokodil' (Desomorphine) characteristic clinical feature?", a: "<b>Severe skin necrosis and gangrene</b> at injection sites (from toxic byproducts like Phosphorus compounds)." },
            { q: "Management of Gamma-Hydroxybutyrate (GHB) overdose?", a: "<b>Supportive care;</b> patients often 'wake up' suddenly with <b>extreme agitation</b> (the characteristic 'G-sleep')." },
            { q: "'Fetal Hydantoin Syndrome' cause?", a: "<b>Maternal use of Phenytoin</b> during pregnancy." },
            { q: "Classic presentation of 'Fetal Hydantoin Syndrome'?", a: "<b>Microcephaly, cleft lip/palate, hypoplastic nails/phalanges</b> (intrauterine growth restriction)." },
            { q: "Management of a pregnant patient with CO poisoning?", a: "<b>Lower threshold for Hyperbaric Oxygen ($HBO_2$)</b> because Fetal Hb has <i>higher</i> affinity for CO than maternal Hb." },
            { q: "Why is Ceftriaxone avoided in neonates?", a: "<b>Displaces Bilirubin from albumin</b> → ↑ risk of Kernicterus (bilirubin encephalopathy)." },
            { q: "Next best step for a patient with a 'Scooped ST' and heart block?", a: "<b>Check Potassium level</b> and order <b>Digoxin level</b> (rules out Digoxin toxicity)." },
            { q: "Next best step for an unconscious patient with 'Pinpoint Pupils'?", a: "<b>Trial of Naloxone</b> and check bedside Glucose (classic opioid presentation)." },
            { q: "Next best step for a patient with QRS > 120ms after an unknown ingestion?", a: "<b>Administer Sodium Bicarbonate ($NaHCO_3$)</b> (presumed Na⁺ channel blocker like TCA)." },
            { q: "Next best step in a symptomatic 'Body Packer'?", a: "<b>Emergency surgical consultation</b> (NOT WBI or Charcoal); risk of packet rupture." },
            { q: "'Blue Line' on the gingival margin (Burton's Line)?", a: "<b>Lead poisoning</b> (dark-gray or blue discoloration of gum line)." },
            { q: "'Acrodynia' (Painful, pink/red hands and feet)?", a: "<b>Mercury poisoning</b> (common in children; autonomic symptoms, pink discoloration)." },
            { q: "Horizontal AND Vertical Nystagmus?", a: "<b>Highly suggestive of Phencyclidine (PCP) intoxication</b> (specific finding)." },
            { q: "'Cherry Red' skin color (Post-mortem)?", a: "<b>Carbon Monoxide or Cyanide poisoning</b> (bright red discoloration from carboxyhemoglobin or pink from cyanosis relief)." },
            { q: "Antidote for Valproic Acid?", a: "<b>L-Carnitine</b> (supplementation, cofactor replacement)." },
            { q: "Antidote for Sulfonylureas?", a: "<b>Octreotide</b> (inhibits insulin secretion)." },
            { q: "Antidote for Black Widow bite?", a: "<b>Latrodectus antivenom</b> (Equine-derived); or Benzodiazepines/Opioids for symptom control." },
            { q: "Antidote for Calcium Channel Blockers?", a: "<b>Calcium, Glucagon, High-dose Insulin, Lipid Emulsion</b> (multimodal therapy for refractory cases)." },
            { q: "Contraindication for Flumazenil in an unknown overdose?", a: "<b>Potential for intractable seizures</b> if co-ingested pro-convulsant (like TCAs); use caution in mixed ingestions." },
            { q: "Why is Acidification of urine (using Ammonium Chloride) no longer recommended?", a: "<b>Worsens systemic acidosis</b> and ↑ risk of rhabdomyolysis-induced renal failure." },
            { q: "Why are Magnesium-containing cathartics avoided in renal failure patients?", a: "<b>Risk of severe hypermagnesemia</b> and neuromuscular blockade; use sodium-based cathartics instead." },
            { q: "Differential for 'Hyperthermia and Rigidity'?", a: "<b>Serotonin Syndrome, Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome (NMS), Malignant Hyperthermia (anesthesia), Salicylate toxicity.</b>" },
            { q: "Differential for 'Miosis' (Pinpoint Pupils)?", a: "<b>Opioids, Organophosphates (Cholinergics), Clonidine, Sedative-hypnotics</b> (sometimes)." },
            { q: "Differential for 'Mydriasis' (Dilated Pupils)?", a: "<b>Sympathomimetics (Cocaine/Amphetamines), Anticholinergics, Opioid withdrawal.</b>" },
            { q: "Toxin causing 'Vertical Nystagmus' specifically?", a: "<b>Phencyclidine (PCP)</b> or <b>Wernicke's Encephalopathy</b> (from thiamine deficiency)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Digoxin at the cellular level?", a: "<b>Inhibits the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase pump</b> → ↑ intracellular Ca²⁺ → positive inotropic effect (at therapeutic doses)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Clonidine toxicity?", a: "<b>Alpha-2 adrenergic agonist;</b> causes 'Central Sympatholytic' effect (mimics opioid overdose with CNS/respiratory depression)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Batrachotoxin (from Poison Dart Frogs)?", a: "<b>Irreversibly opens Sodium channels</b> → uncontrolled depolarization → paralysis and arrhythmias." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Botulinum Toxin?", a: "<b>Cleaves SNARE proteins</b> → prevents Acetylcholine release at neuromuscular junction → flaccid paralysis." },
            { q: "Definition of 'Chain of Custody' in toxicology screen?", a: "<b>Documented, chronological history</b> of seizure, custody, and analysis of sample to ensure no tampering." },
            { q: "Most common 'False Positive' on Urine Drug Screen (UDS) for Amphetamines?", a: "<b>Pseudoephedrine, Bupropion, or Selegiline</b> (cross-reactive on immunoassay)." },
            { q: "Most common 'False Positive' for Opioids on UDS?", a: "<b>Poppy seeds or Rifampin</b> (can produce positive opioid metabolite screens)." },
            { q: "Why does standard UDS often miss Fentanyl or Oxycodone?", a: "<b>Standard screens detect natural opiates (morphine/codeine);</b> synthetic opioids require specific assays." },
            { q: "'Boiled Lobster' appearance of the skin?", a: "<b>Boric Acid poisoning</b> (erythematous skin with 'boiled' appearance)." },
            { q: "Management of 'Button Battery' in Esophagus vs. Stomach?", a: "<b>Esophagus:</b> Emergency endoscopic removal. <b>Stomach:</b> Observation (repeat X-ray) if asymptomatic." },
            { q: "Mechanism of 'Metformin-Associated Lactic Acidosis' (MALA)?", a: "<b>Inhibits mitochondrial respiration</b> → shift toward anaerobic metabolism → lactic acid accumulation." },
            { q: "Preferred treatment for QRS widening in Arsenic overdose?", a: "<b>Sodium Bicarbonate</b> (same principle as TCA overdose; blocks cardiac Na⁺ channels)." },
            { q: "Toxin found in 'Apricot Pits' or 'Laetrile'?", a: "<b>Amygdalin</b> (metabolized to Cyanide); historically promoted as cancer 'cure' (ineffective & dangerous)." },
            { q: "Hallmark of Ciguatera poisoning (Fish)?", a: "<b>Reversal of hot/cold temperature sensation</b> (paradoxical dysesthesia); also neurologic/GI symptoms." }
          ]
        },
        {
          topic: 'GI Pharmacology',
          icon: 'pill',
          cards: [
            { q: "Mechanism of action (MOA) of H₂-receptor antagonists (e.g., Cimetidine, Famotidine)?", a: "<b>Reversibly block H₂ receptors</b> → <b>↓</b> H⁺ secretion by parietal cells." },
            { q: "MOA of Proton Pump Inhibitors (PPIs) (e.g., Omeprazole, Lansoprazole)?", a: "<b>Irreversibly inhibit H⁺/K⁺ ATPase</b> in stomach parietal cells." },
            { q: "Specific side effects of Cimetidine?", a: "<b>Cytochrome P-450 inhibitor;</b> antiandrogenic effects (gynecomastia, impotence); crosses blood-brain barrier (confusion)." },
            { q: "Which PPI/H₂RA is known to decrease the renal excretion of creatinine?", a: "<b>Cimetidine.</b>" },
            { q: "MOA of Misoprostol in GI protection?", a: "<b>PGE₁ analog;</b> <b>↑</b> production of gastric mucous barrier and <b>↓</b> acid secretion." },
            { q: "Primary clinical use of Misoprostol in GI?", a: "<b>Prevention of NSAID-induced gastric ulcers.</b>" },
            { q: "Contraindication for Misoprostol?", a: "<b>Pregnancy</b> (it is an abortifacient/causes uterine contractions)." },
            { q: "MOA of Sucralfate?", a: "<b>Binds to ulcers/erosions,</b> providing physical protection (requires acidic environment to polymerize)." },
            { q: "MOA of Bismuth subsalicate (Pepto-Bismol)?", a: "<b>Coats ulcers,</b> stimulates mucosal protective factors; has antimicrobial effects against <i>H. pylori.</i>" },
            { q: "Common harmless side effect of Bismuth?", a: "<b>Black tongue and black stools</b> (may mimic melena)." },
            { q: "Adverse effect associated with Aluminum hydroxide antacids?", a: "<b>Constipation</b> ('Alumin-um' = 'Minimum' stools)." },
            { q: "Adverse effect associated with Magnesium hydroxide antacids?", a: "<b>Diarrhea</b> ('Magnesi-um' = 'Must go' to the bathroom)." },
            { q: "Which antacid is associated with 'Milk-alkali syndrome'?", a: "<b>Calcium carbonate</b> (hypercalcemia, metabolic alkalosis, renal insufficiency)." },
            { q: "Standard 'Triple Therapy' for H. pylori?", a: "<b>PPI + Amoxicillin</b> (or Metronidazole) <b>+ Clarithromycin.</b>" },
            { q: "Standard 'Quadruple Therapy' for H. pylori?", a: "<b>PPI + Bismuth + Metronidazole + Tetracycline.</b>" },
            { q: "MOA of Ondansetron (Zofran)?", a: "<b>5-HT₃ antagonist;</b> blocks vagal afferents and central (Chemoreceptor Trigger Zone - CTZ)." },
            { q: "Major ECG side effect of Ondansetron?", a: "<b>QT interval prolongation</b> (risk of Torsades de Pointes)." },
            { q: "MOA of Metoclopramide?", a: "<b>D₂ receptor antagonist</b> in CTZ; also a prokinetic (stimulates 5-HT₄ receptors)." },
            { q: "Neurological side effects of Metoclopramide?", a: "<b>Extrapyramidal symptoms</b> (dystonia, parkinsonism, tardive dyskinesia)." },
            { q: "Contraindications for Metoclopramide?", a: "<b>Small bowel obstruction, Parkinson's disease.</b>" },
            { q: "MOA of Aprepitant?", a: "<b>Substance P antagonist;</b> blocks NK₁ (neurokinin-1) receptors in the brain." },
            { q: "Which class of antiemetic is best for chemotherapy-induced vomiting?", a: "<b>5-HT₃ antagonists</b> (Ondansetron) and <b>NK₁ antagonists</b> (Aprepitant)." },
            { q: "MOA of Scopolamine for motion sickness?", a: "<b>Antimuscarinic</b> (M₁ antagonist)." },
            { q: "MOA of Promethazine/Prochlorperazine?", a: "<b>D₂ and H₁ antagonists</b> (used for severe N/V)." },
            { q: "MOA of Psyllium and Methylcellulose?", a: "<b>Bulk-forming laxatives;</b> absorb water to soften stool and increase bulk." },
            { q: "MOA of Magnesium citrate and Polyethylene glycol (PEG)?", a: "<b>Osmotic laxatives;</b> draw water into the bowel lumen." },
            { q: "MOA of Senna and Bisacodyl?", a: "<b>Stimulant laxatives;</b> enteric nerve stimulation → colonic contraction." },
            { q: "MOA of Docusate?", a: "<b>Stool softener;</b> acts as a surfactant to allow water and lipids into stool." },
            { q: "MOA of Loperamide?", a: "<b>μ-opioid receptor agonist;</b> slows gut motility (poor CNS penetration, low addiction potential)." },
            { q: "MOA of Diphenoxylate?", a: "<b>μ-opioid receptor agonist;</b> often combined with Atropine to discourage abuse." },
            { q: "MOA of Octreotide in GI?", a: "<b>Somatostatin analog;</b> inhibits secretion of splanchnic hormones (gastrin, CCK, VIP, insulin)." },
            { q: "Primary clinical use of Octreotide in GI emergency?", a: "<b>Acute variceal bleeds</b> (decreases splanchnic blood flow)." },
            { q: "MOA of Sulfasalazine?", a: "<b>Combination of Sulfapyridine</b> (antibacterial) and <b>5-aminosalicylic acid</b> (anti-inflammatory) activated by gut bacteria." },
            { q: "Active component of Sulfasalazine that treats IBD?", a: "<b>5-aminosalicylic acid (5-ASA / Mesalamine).</b>" },
            { q: "Common side effects of the Sulfapyridine component?", a: "<b>Reversible oligospermia, malaise,</b> sulfonamide toxicity." },
            { q: "MOA of Infliximab and Adalimumab in IBD?", a: "<b>Anti-TNF-α monoclonal antibodies.</b>" },
            { q: "Necessary screening before starting Infliximab?", a: "<b>Latent Tuberculosis (TB) screen</b> (risk of reactivation)." },
            { q: "MOA of Vedolizumab?", a: "<b>Integrin antagonist (α₄β₇);</b> gut-specific anti-inflammatory." },
            { q: "MOA of Azathioprine/6-Mercaptopurine (6-MP)?", a: "<b>Purine synthesis inhibitors;</b> used for IBD maintenance." },
            { q: "Dangerous drug interaction with Azathioprine?", a: "<b>Allopurinol</b> (inhibits Xanthine Oxidase → <b>↑</b> toxic 6-MP levels)." },
            { q: "MOA of Erythromycin as a prokinetic?", a: "<b>Stimulates motilin receptors.</b>" },
            { q: "Clinical use for Erythromycin in GI?", a: "<b>Gastroparesis</b> (especially diabetic)." },
            { q: "MOA of Orlistat?", a: "<b>Inhibits gastric and pancreatic lipase</b> → <b>↓</b> dietary fat absorption." },
            { q: "Primary side effect of Orlistat?", a: "<b>Steatorrhea,</b> decreased absorption of fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K)." },
            { q: "MOA of Ursodeoxycholic acid (Ursodiol)?", a: "<b>Bile acid;</b> reduces cholesterol secretion and <b>↑</b> bile flow (used to dissolve gallstones)." },
            { q: "Treatment for Wilson's Disease?", a: "<b>Penicillamine or Trientine</b> (Copper chelators)." },
            { q: "Treatment for Hepatic Encephalopathy (to ↓ Ammonia)?", a: "<b>Lactulose and Rifaximin.</b>" },
            { q: "MOA of Lactulose in Hepatic Encephalopathy?", a: "<b>Converted to lactic acid by bacteria</b> → acidifies gut → converts NH₃ to NH₄⁺ (trapping)." },
            { q: "MOA of Pancrelipase?", a: "<b>Replacement of lipase, amylase, and protease</b> for pancreatic insufficiency (Cystic Fibrosis, chronic pancreatitis)." },
            { q: "MOA of Lubiprostone?", a: "<b>Activates Type 2 chloride channels</b> in the gut; used for chronic constipation/IBS-C." },
            { q: "Effect of Gastrin on parietal cells?", a: "<b>Stimulates H⁺ secretion</b> via CCK-B receptors and ECL cells (<b>↑</b> histamine)." },
            { q: "Effect of Somatostatin on the GI tract?", a: "<b>Inhibitory;</b> <b>↓</b> gastric acid, pepsinogen, pancreatic/biliary secretions, and gallbladder contraction." },
            { q: "MOA of Linaclotide?", a: "<b>Guanylate cyclase-C agonist</b> → <b>↑</b> cGMP → <b>↑</b> Cl⁻ and HCO₃⁻ secretion into lumen." },
            { q: "First-line drug for primary biliary cholangitis?", a: "<b>Ursodeoxycholic acid (Ursodiol).</b>" },
            { q: "MOA of Alosetron?", a: "<b>5-HT₃ antagonist</b> used specifically for severe IBS-D in women." },
            { q: "Black box warning for Alosetron?", a: "<b>Ischemic colitis.</b>" },
            { q: "Drug used to prevent esophageal variceal bleeding (prophylaxis)?", a: "<b>Non-selective beta-blockers</b> (Propranolol, Nadolol)." },
            { q: "MOA of Sulfasalazine-induced anemia?", a: "<b>Interferes with Folate absorption.</b>" },
            { q: "Why use Metronidazole in Crohn's disease?", a: "<b>Treats perianal disease and abscesses.</b>" },
            { q: "Drug used for localized distal Ulcerative Colitis?", a: "<b>Mesalamine enemas or suppositories.</b>" },
            { q: "Antidote for Acetaminophen toxicity?", a: "<b>N-acetylcysteine</b> (replenishes glutathione)." },
            { q: "Treatment for Iron overdose (acute)?", a: "<b>Deferoxamine.</b>" },
            { q: "GI side effect of chronic Lead poisoning?", a: "<b>Colicky abdominal pain</b> ('Lead colic')." },
            { q: "Treatment for Arsenic poisoning?", a: "<b>Dimercaprol.</b>" },
            { q: "Effect of Opioids on the Sphincter of Oddi?", a: "<b>Spasm/contraction</b> (can worsen biliary colic; Meperidine is traditionally preferred)." },
            { q: "MOA of Teduglutide?", a: "<b>GLP-2 analog;</b> enhances intestinal villus growth in Short Bowel Syndrome." },
            { q: "Use of Cholestyramine in GI?", a: "<b>Bile acid sequestrant;</b> used for bile acid-induced diarrhea (e.g., after ileal resection)." },
            { q: "MOA of Telotristat?", a: "<b>Tryptophan hydroxylase inhibitor;</b> <b>↓</b> serotonin production in Carcinoid Syndrome." },
            { q: "MOA of Eluxadoline?", a: "<b>μ and κ opioid agonist and δ antagonist;</b> used for IBS-D." },
            { q: "Effect of Glucagon on the esophagus?", a: "<b>Relaxes smooth muscle</b> (lower esophageal sphincter); used to dislodge food boluses." },
            { q: "MOA of Simethicone?", a: "<b>Antifoaming agent;</b> breaks up gas bubbles (anti-flatulent)." },
            { q: "MOA of Mineral oil?", a: "<b>Lubricant laxative;</b> softens stool and coats the intestine." },
            { q: "Danger of Mineral oil aspiration?", a: "<b>Lipid pneumonia.</b>" },
            { q: "Drug that causes 'Grey baby syndrome' but is used for GI infections abroad?", a: "<b>Chloramphenicol.</b>" },
            { q: "MOA of Fidaxomicin?", a: "<b>Inhibits RNA polymerase;</b> used specifically for <i>C. difficile</i> infection." },
            { q: "First-line treatment for C. difficile (current guidelines)?", a: "<b>Oral Vancomycin or Fidaxomicin.</b>" },
            { q: "MOA of Obeticholic acid?", a: "<b>FXR (Farnesoid X receptor) agonist;</b> used for primary biliary cholangitis." },
            { q: "Side effect of high-dose Calcium carbonate?", a: "<b>Rebound acid hypersecretion.</b>" },
            { q: "MOA of Prucalopride?", a: "<b>Selective 5-HT₄ receptor agonist;</b> used for chronic idiopathic constipation." },
            { q: "MOA of Methylnaltrexone?", a: "<b>Peripheral μ-opioid receptor antagonist;</b> treats opioid-induced constipation without crossing BBB." },
            { q: "Use of Secretin in GI testing?", a: "<b>Stimulates pancreatic HCO₃⁻ secretion;</b> used to diagnose Gastrinoma (Zollinger-Ellison)." },
            { q: "Response of a Gastrinoma to Secretin injection?", a: "<b>Paradoxic increase in Serum Gastrin.</b>" },
            { q: "MOA of Cholecystokinin (CCK)?", a: "<b>Stimulates gallbladder contraction and pancreatic enzyme secretion.</b>" },
            { q: "Therapeutic use of CCK octapeptide (Sincalide)?", a: "<b>Used in HIDA scans</b> to stimulate the gallbladder." },
            { q: "MOA of Vasopressin in GI hemorrhage?", a: "<b>Potent splanchnic vasoconstrictor</b> (less preferred than Octreotide)." },
            { q: "Effect of Somatostatin on Glucagon and Insulin?", a: "<b>Inhibits both.</b>" },
            { q: "Common GI side effect of Methotrexate (used in IBD)?", a: "<b>Mucositis and oral ulcers.</b>" },
            { q: "GI side effect of Mycophenolate mofetil?", a: "<b>Severe diarrhea.</b>" },
            { q: "Interaction between PPIs and Clopidogrel?", a: "<b>PPIs</b> (especially Omeprazole) may inhibit CYP2C19 → <b>↓</b> activation of Clopidogrel." },
            { q: "Drug used to treat Portal Hypertension in Cirrhosis?", a: "<b>Propranolol</b> (non-selective beta-blocker)." },
            { q: "MOA of Rifaximin in Hepatic Encephalopathy?", a: "<b>Poorly absorbed antibiotic;</b> <b>↓</b> ammonia-producing bacteria in the gut." },
            { q: "Antibiotic associated with Pseudomembranous colitis?", a: "<b>Clindamycin</b> (classic), but also Amoxicillin and Cephalosporins." },
            { q: "Treatment for VIPoma?", a: "<b>Octreotide.</b>" },
            { q: "What vitamin should be supplemented in patients on Sulfasalazine?", a: "<b>Folate</b> (Vitamin B₉)." },
            { q: "MOA of Naloxegol?", a: "<b>Peripheral μ-opioid receptor antagonist</b> (PEGylated Naloxone) for opioid-induced constipation." },
            { q: "Major GI side effect of NSAIDs?", a: "<b>Inhibition of PGE₂</b> → gastric ulcers and bleeding." },
            { q: "GI side effect of chronic Alcohol use?", a: "<b>Gastritis, Pancreatitis, and Cirrhosis.</b>" },
            { q: "Which drug is used for bowel prep before colonoscopy?", a: "<b>Polyethylene glycol (PEG).</b>" },
            { q: "Side effect of PPIs regarding bone health?", a: "<b>↑</b> risk of fractures (due to <b>↓</b> Calcium absorption)." },
            { q: "Risk of PPIs regarding infections?", a: "<b>↑</b> risk of <i>C. difficile</i> and Pneumonia (due to loss of acid barrier)." },
            { q: "35yo with GERD, wants a drug that doesn't cause gynecomastia. Which H₂RA?", a: "<b>Famotidine</b> (or Ranitidine, though mostly withdrawn)." },
            { q: "Patient with Gastroparesis needs a drug to help with gastric emptying.", a: "<b>Metoclopramide or Erythromycin.</b>" },
            { q: "Patient with severe Travelers' Diarrhea. First line anti-motility?", a: "<b>Loperamide.</b>" },
            { q: "Best drug for symptomatic relief of Esophageal Spasm?", a: "<b>Nitrates or Calcium Channel Blockers.</b>" },
            { q: "Medication that causes 'painless' black tongue?", a: "<b>Bismuth subsalicylate.</b>" },
            { q: "MOA of Linaclotide vs Lubiprostone?", a: "<b>Linaclotide</b> (Guanylate cyclase-C); <b>Lubiprostone</b> (Chloride channel activator)." },
            { q: "Which antibiotic is contraindicated in pregnancy due to 'discolored teeth' but used in Quadruple H. pylori therapy?", a: "<b>Tetracycline.</b>" },
            { q: "MOA of Tegaserod?", a: "<b>5-HT₄ partial agonist</b> (used for IBS-C; restricted use due to CV risks)." },
            { q: "Most effective long-term therapy for Barrett's Esophagus symptoms?", a: "<b>PPIs.</b>" },
            { q: "MOA of Entyvio (Vedolizumab)?", a: "<b>α₄β₇ Integrin blocker.</b>" },
            { q: "MOA of Propranolol/Nadolol in preventing variceal hemorrhage?", a: "<b>β₂ blockade</b> → splanchnic vasoconstriction (unopposed α) → <b>↓</b> portal venous flow." },
            { q: "Drug used to treat Hepatorenal Syndrome (HRS) by causing systemic vasoconstriction?", a: "<b>Terlipressin</b> (Vasopressin analog) + Albumin." },
            { q: "MOA of Rifaximin in preventing Hepatic Encephalopathy recurrence?", a: "<b>Non-absorbable antibiotic</b> that kills ammonia-producing enteric bacteria." },
            { q: "Why is Neomycin rarely used now for Hepatic Encephalopathy despite being effective?", a: "<b>Risk of ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity.</b>" },
            { q: "MOA of Nitazoxanide?", a: "<b>Interferes with PFOR enzyme</b> (anaerobic metabolism); used for Cryptosporidium in immunocompetent patients." },
            { q: "MOA of Ribavirin (used in Hep C)?", a: "<b>Lethal hypermutation,</b> inhibition of RNA polymerase, and inhibition of IMPDH." },
            { q: "Significant side effect of Ribavirin?", a: "<b>Hemolytic anemia and severe teratogenicity.</b>" },
            { q: "MOA of Sofosbuvir?", a: "<b>NS5B RNA-dependent RNA polymerase inhibitor</b> (HCV)." },
            { q: "MOA of Simeprevir?", a: "<b>NS3/4A protease inhibitor</b> (HCV)." },
            { q: "MOA of Ledipasvir?", a: "<b>NS5A inhibitor</b> (HCV)." },
            { q: "MOA of Ustekinumab (Stelara) in Crohn's?", a: "<b>Monoclonal antibody against IL-12 and IL-23.</b>" },
            { q: "MOA of Tofacitinib in Ulcerative Colitis?", a: "<b>JAK inhibitor</b> (Janus Kinase)." },
            { q: "Which IBD drug is associated with a risk of Progressive Multifocal Leukoencephalopathy (PML)?", a: "<b>Natalizumab</b> (blocks α₄-integrin; crosses BBB)." },
            { q: "How does Vedolizumab differ from Natalizumab in safety?", a: "<b>Vedolizumab is gut-specific (α₄β₇);</b> it does not cross the BBB or cause PML." },
            { q: "MOA of Cyclosporine in 'rescue therapy' for Ulcerative Colitis?", a: "<b>Calcineurin inhibitor;</b> inhibits IL-2 transcription." },
            { q: "Major side effects of Cyclosporine?", a: "<b>Nephrotoxicity, Gingival hyperplasia, Hirsutism</b> ('the three H's')." },
            { q: "MOA of Miglustat in Gaucher Disease (GI-related metabolic)?", a: "<b>Glucosylceramide synthase inhibitor</b> (<b>↓</b> substrate formation)." },
            { q: "MOA of Sevelamer?", a: "<b>Non-absorbable phosphate binder;</b> used in GI tract for CKD patients." },
            { q: "MOA of Lanthanum carbonate?", a: "<b>Phosphate binder</b> (used for hyperphosphatemia)." },
            { q: "MOA of Crofelemer?", a: "<b>Chloride channel inhibitor;</b> used for HIV-associated non-infectious diarrhea." },
            { q: "Drug used to treat refractory Celiac disease?", a: "<b>Azathioprine or Budesonide</b> (locally acting steroid)." },
            { q: "MOA of Budesonide's 'local' effect in IBD?", a: "<b>High first-pass metabolism in the liver</b> limits systemic exposure." },
            { q: "Drug used for the 'Urea Breath Test'?", a: "<b>¹³C-labeled Urea.</b>" },
            { q: "Treatment for Methanol or Ethylene Glycol ingestion (GI/metabolic)?", a: "<b>Fomepizole</b> (inhibits alcohol dehydrogenase)." },
            { q: "Treatment for Amanita phalloides (mushroom) poisoning?", a: "<b>Silibinin (Silybin) or Penicillin G.</b>" },
            { q: "GI side effect of Colchicine?", a: "<b>Severe diarrhea</b> (disrupts rapidly dividing GI mucosal cells)." },
            { q: "Drug causing 'Crystalline nephropathy' if not hydrated during GI infection treatment?", a: "<b>Sulfadiazine or Acyclovir.</b>" },
            { q: "Mechanism of 'Red Man Syndrome' with Vancomycin?", a: "<b>Direct Histamine release from mast cells</b> (not a true allergy)." },
            { q: "Classic GI side effect of Metronidazole?", a: "<b>Disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol</b> (flushing, tachycardia)." },
            { q: "MOA of Pasireotide?", a: "<b>Somatostatin analog</b> with high affinity for SSTR₅; used in Cushing's or specialized neuroendocrine GI tumors." },
            { q: "MOA of Resins (e.g., Cholestyramine) in Pruritus?", a: "<b>Binds bile acids in the gut,</b> preventing their reabsorption and deposition in skin (common in PBC)." },
            { q: "MOA of Chenodeoxycholic acid?", a: "<b>Bile acid</b> that suppresses hepatic synthesis of cholesterol (stone dissolution)." },
            { q: "MOA of Nitroglycerin paste in GI?", a: "<b>Relaxes the internal anal sphincter</b> (used for anal fissures)." },
            { q: "MOA of Dicyclomine/Hyoscyamine?", a: "<b>Antispasmodics (Anticholinergics)</b> used for IBS-related cramping." },
            { q: "Why avoid Loperamide in patients with C. difficile or high fever?", a: "<b>Risk of inducing Toxic Megacolon.</b>" },
            { q: "Standard drug treatment for Whipple Disease?", a: "<b>Ceftriaxone</b> (initially) followed by Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (TMP-SMX) for 1 year." },
            { q: "MOA of Ezetimibe in the GI tract?", a: "<b>Inhibits NPC1L1 transporter</b> → <b>↓</b> cholesterol absorption at the brush border." },
            { q: "MOA of Sacrosidase?", a: "<b>Enzyme replacement</b> for Congenital Sucrase-Isomaltase Deficiency (CSID)." },
            { q: "First-line treatment for Achalasia (pharmacologic)?", a: "<b>Nifedipine (CCB) or Nitrospray</b> (rarely used; usually surgery/botox)." },
            { q: "MOA of Botulinum toxin in Achalasia?", a: "<b>Inhibits ACh release</b> at the Lower Esophageal Sphincter → relaxation." },
            { q: "MOA of Exenatide/Liraglutide (GI-hormone based)?", a: "<b>GLP-1 agonists;</b> <b>↓</b> gastric emptying and <b>↑</b> insulin." },
            { q: "GI side effect of GLP-1 agonists?", a: "<b>Nausea, vomiting,</b> and potential risk of Pancreatitis." },
            { q: "MOA of Sitagliptin/Linagliptin?", a: "<b>DPP-4 inhibitors;</b> prevent the breakdown of endogenous GLP-1." },
            { q: "Drug used to treat Gastrinoma when surgery is not possible?", a: "<b>High-dose PPIs.</b>" },
            { q: "Which drug inhibits the absorption of Fat-Soluble vitamins?", a: "<b>Orlistat and Bile acid sequestrants</b> (Cholestyramine)." },
            { q: "MOA of Obeticholic acid in Primary Biliary Cholangitis?", a: "<b>FXR agonist</b> → <b>↓</b> bile acid synthesis and <b>↑</b> bile acid transport out of hepatocytes." },
            { q: "Most common side effect of Obeticholic acid?", a: "<b>Severe pruritus</b> (itching)." },
            { q: "MOA of Pancrelipase administration?", a: "<b>Supplies lipase, protease, and amylase;</b> must be taken with meals to digest macronutrients." },
            { q: "Why are PPIs often co-administered with Pancrelipase?", a: "<b>To prevent gastric acid</b> from denaturing the supplemental enzymes before they reach the duodenum." },
            { q: "Drug of choice for treating Biliary Colic (acute pain)?", a: "<b>NSAIDs</b> (e.g., Ketorolac) are often superior to opioids for biliary pain." },
            { q: "MOA of Rifaximin in Travelers' Diarrhea?", a: "<b>Inhibits bacterial RNA synthesis</b> (non-absorbable, active against <i>E. coli</i>)." },
            { q: "MOA of Nitazoxanide in Giardia or Cryptosporidium?", a: "<b>Interferes with the PFOR</b> (pyruvate:ferredoxin oxidoreductase) enzyme-dependent electron transfer reaction." },
            { q: "Treatment for Whipple's Disease (long-term)?", a: "<b>Ceftriaxone</b> (2 weeks) followed by TMP-SMX (1 year)." },
            { q: "MOA of Bezlotoxumab?", a: "<b>Human monoclonal antibody</b> that binds <i>C. difficile</i> toxin B." },
            { q: "Clinical use of Bezlotoxumab?", a: "<b>Prevention of C. difficile recurrence</b> in high-risk patients." },
            { q: "MOA of Somatostatin analogs in dumping syndrome?", a: "<b>Slows gastric emptying</b> and inhibits the release of insulin and GI hormones." },
            { q: "MOA of Glycerol phenylbutyrate in Urea Cycle Disorders (GI/Metabolic)?", a: "<b>Nitrogen scavenger;</b> helps eliminate ammonia via non-urea pathways." },
            { q: "MOA of Chenodiol?", a: "<b>Naturally occurring bile acid</b> that <b>↓</b> biliary cholesterol secretion (used for radiolucent gallstones)." },
            { q: "First-line pharmacotherapy for Hemochromatosis (if phlebotomy is not an option)?", a: "<b>Deferoxamine</b> (IV) or <b>Deferasirox</b> (Oral) – Iron chelators." },
            { q: "MOA of Miglustat?", a: "<b>Inhibits glucosylceramide synthase;</b> used for Type 1 Gaucher disease." },
            { q: "MOA of Palonosetron?", a: "<b>Second-generation 5-HT₃ receptor antagonist</b> with a longer half-life (40 hours)." },
            { q: "MOA of Dexamethasone in emesis?", a: "<b>Glucocorticoid;</b> used as an adjunct to 5-HT₃ antagonists to <b>↑</b> antiemetic efficacy." },
            { q: "MOA of Amifostine in GI radiation?", a: "<b>Cytoprotective agent;</b> scavenges free radicals to reduce xerostomia (dry mouth) and proctitis." },
            { q: "Treatment for Radiation Proctitis (topical)?", a: "<b>Sucralfate enemas or Sucralfate paste.</b>" },
            { q: "MOA of Cannabinoids (Dronabinol) in GI?", a: "<b>Agonist at CB₁ receptors</b> in the vomiting center; also stimulates appetite." },
            { q: "MOA of Plecanatide?", a: "<b>Guanylate cyclase-C (GC-C) agonist</b> (similar to Linaclotide) for Chronic Idiopathic Constipation." },
            { q: "MOA of Tenapanor?", a: "<b>NHE3 (Sodium-Hydrogen Exchanger 3) inhibitor;</b> <b>↓</b> sodium absorption in the gut → <b>↑</b> luminal water." },
            { q: "MOA of Alvimopan?", a: "<b>Peripherally acting μ-opioid receptor antagonist;</b> used specifically for post-operative ileus." },
            { q: "MOA of Pyridostigmine in Ogilvie Syndrome (Acute Colonic Pseudo-obstruction)?", a: "<b>Acetylcholinesterase inhibitor;</b> <b>↑</b> parasympathetic activity to stimulate colonic contraction." },
            { q: "Primary side effect of Pyridostigmine to watch for in GI patients?", a: "<b>Excessive salivation and Bradycardia</b> (cholinergic crisis)." },
            { q: "Why is Metoclopramide avoided in bowel obstruction?", a: "<b>Prokinetic effect against a physical obstruction</b> can lead to perforation." },
            { q: "Which GI drug can cause 'Cinchonism' (tinnitus, headache, dizziness)?", a: "<b>Quinidine</b> (occasionally used for GI-related protozoal infections, though rare now)." },
            { q: "MOA of Sulfasalazine in decreasing inflammation?", a: "<b>Inhibits prostaglandin synthesis and leukotriene synthesis</b> (via the 5-ASA moiety)." },
            { q: "Which H₂RA has the most drug-drug interactions?", a: "<b>Cimetidine</b> (potent P450 inhibitor)." },
            { q: "MOA of Octreotide in Chylothorax/Chylous ascites?", a: "<b>↓</b> intestinal fat absorption and <b>↓</b> triglyceride content in lymph." },
            { q: "A patient on long-term PPIs develops a fracture. What is the biochemical reason?", a: "<b>↓</b> Gastric acidity → <b>↓</b> solubility of Calcium Carbonate → <b>↓</b> intestinal Calcium absorption." },
            { q: "Why is Magnesium Hydroxide usually combined with Aluminum Hydroxide in commercial antacids?", a: "<b>To offset their side effects</b> (Magnesium causes diarrhea; Aluminum causes constipation)." },
            { q: "Which antiemetic class is best for 'Delayed' chemotherapy-induced nausea (2–5 days post-chemo)?", a: "<b>NK₁ receptor antagonists</b> (e.g., Aprepitant)." },
            { q: "Which drug is used for 'rescue' in severe, steroid-refractory Ulcerative Colitis?", a: "<b>Infliximab or Cyclosporine.</b>" },
            { q: "MOA of Bismuth in the 'Quadruple Therapy' for H. pylori?", a: "<b>Direct antimicrobial effect</b> + prevents bacterial adhesion to gastric epithelium." },
            { q: "Effect of Somatostatin on Splanchnic blood flow?", a: "<b>Potent vasoconstriction</b> (via inhibition of vasodilator hormones like Glucagon)." },
            { q: "Which drug blocks the enzyme that converts Testosterone to DHT, but is also a P450 inhibitor used in GI?", a: "<b>Cimetidine</b> (though it primarily blocks the androgen receptor, it has complex endocrine side effects)." },
            { q: "MOA of Misoprostol in preventing ulcers vs. its use in obstetrics?", a: "<b>Both involve EP₃ receptor agonism;</b> GI → <b>↓</b> cAMP in parietal cells; OB → <b>↑</b> Calcium in uterine smooth muscle." },
            { q: "Why does Loperamide not cause CNS opioid effects (euphoria/analgesia)?", a: "<b>It is a substrate for the P-glycoprotein efflux pump,</b> which pumps it out of the brain." },
            { q: "MOA of Senna-induced 'Melanosis Coli'?", a: "<b>Anthraquinone-induced apoptosis</b> of colonic epithelial cells → pigment-laden macrophages in the lamina propria." },
            { q: "Drug for pruritus in a patient with Primary Biliary Cholangitis?", a: "<b>Cholestyramine</b> (binds bile acids) or <b>Rifampin</b> (induces enzymes to metabolize pruritogens)." },
            { q: "Treatment for a patient with Wilson's Disease who cannot tolerate Penicillamine?", a: "<b>Trientine</b> (another chelator) or <b>Zinc</b> (blocks intestinal Copper absorption via Metallothionein)." },
            { q: "Which drug used for IBD requires testing for Thiopurine Methyltransferase (TPMT) activity?", a: "<b>Azathioprine / 6-Mercaptopurine</b> (Low TPMT → <b>↑</b> risk of life-threatening myelosuppression)." },
            { q: "MOA of Methotrexate in Crohn's Disease?", a: "<b>Inhibits Dihydrofolate Reductase (DHFR)</b> → <b>↓</b> DNA synthesis; also <b>↑</b> Adenosine (anti-inflammatory)." },
            { q: "MOA of Natalizumab's GI benefit?", a: "<b>Blocks α₄-integrin</b> → prevents leukocyte adhesion and migration into gut tissue." }
          ]
        },
        {
          topic: 'General Pathology',
