                                      "a": "Inhibition of thyroid peroxidase (TPO)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Effect of Amiodarone on the thyroid?",
                                      "a": "Can cause either hyperthyroidism or hypothyroidism (due to high iodine content)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cause of Thyroid Storm?",
                                      "a": "Stress, infection, or surgery in a patient with untreated hyperthyroidism."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Jod-Basedow phenomenon?",
                                      "a": "Iodine-induced hyperthyroidism in a patient with a multinodular goiter."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Wolff-Chaikoff effect?",
                                      "a": "High iodine levels inhibiting thyroid hormone synthesis (protective)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Ectopic thyroid tissue location?",
                                      "a": "Base of the tongue (Lingual thyroid)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Remnant of the descent of the thyroid gland?",
                                      "a": "Thyroglossal duct cyst (midline neck mass)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Branchial cleft cyst location vs. Thyroglossal?",
                                      "a": "Lateral neck vs. Midline."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Relationship between Calcium and Albumin?",
                                      "a": "Total calcium drops by 0.8 for every 1.0 decrease in albumin."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Psammoma body mnemonics (PSaMMoma)?",
                                      "a": "Papillary thyroid, Serous ovary, Meningioma, Mesothelioma."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary location of the \"Sertoli-cell only\" equivalent in endocrine?",
                                      "a": "Not applicable, but used to differentiate primary vs secondary failure."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Treatment for <b>Prolactinoma</b>?",
                                      "a": "Dopamine agonists (Cabergoline, Bromocriptine)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Relationship between Prolactin and GnRH?",
                                      "a": "Prolactin inhibits GnRH secretion."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cause of hypopituitarism in adults?",
                                      "a": "Non-secreting pituitary adenoma (compression)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cause of hypopituitarism in children?",
                                      "a": "Craniopharyngioma."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Origin of Craniopharyngioma?",
                                      "a": "Rathke pouch remnants."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Classic imaging appearance of Craniopharyngioma?",
                                      "a": "Calcified, cystic mass with \"machinery oil\" fluid."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Diagnosis for patient with hypercalcemia and low urinary calcium?",
                                      "a": "Familial Hypocalciuric Hypercalcemia (FHH)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Defect in FHH?",
                                      "a": "Defective G-protein coupled calcium-sensing receptors (CaSR)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Effect of 11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency?",
                                      "a": "Hypertension (due to 11-deoxycorticosterone) and virilization."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Effect of 17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency?",
                                      "a": "Hypertension and primary amenorrhea (low sex hormones)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Source of Aldosterone?",
                                      "a": "Zona Glomerulosa."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Source of Cortisol?",
                                      "a": "Zona Fasciculata."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Source of Androgens?",
                                      "a": "Zona Reticularis."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mnemonic for Adrenal layers (G-F-R)?",
                                      "a": "Salt (Glomerulosa), Sugar (Fasciculata), Sex (Reticularis)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Major side effect of Spironolactone in males?",
                                      "a": "Gynecomastia."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of Graves exophthalmos?",
                                      "a": "Fibroblast proliferation and GAG deposition triggered by <b>TSH</b>-receptor antibodies."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Effect of pregnancy on Total T<sub>4</sub>?",
                                      "a": "Increases due to increased Thyroid Binding Globulin (TBG)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Best indicator of thyroid function status?",
                                      "a": "<b>TSH</b> level."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Precursor of <b>ACTH</b> and MSH?",
                                      "a": "Pro-opiomelanocortin (POMC)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Histological appearance of Pituitary Adenoma?",
                                      "a": "Monomorphism (cells look identical) and absence of a reticulin network."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Difference between Pituitary Adenoma and Hyperplasia?",
                                      "a": "Adenomas lack reticulin; Hyperplasia preserves the reticulin framework."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cause of Pituitary Apoplexy?",
                                      "a": "Sudden hemorrhage into a pre-existing pituitary adenoma."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Classic presentation of Pituitary Apoplexy?",
                                      "a": "Sudden \"thunderclap\" headache, visual loss, and cardiovascular collapse."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common location of ectopic <b>ACTH</b> production?",
                                      "a": "Small cell carcinoma of the lung or bronchial carcinoids."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Distinction between Central and Nephrogenic <b>Diabetes Insipidus</b> via Desmopressin?",
                                      "a": "Central DI responds to Desmopressin (urine osmolality increases); Nephrogenic does not.\n\nThyroid & Parathyroid Fine Points"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of the \"Cold Nodule\" on thyroid scan?",
                                      "a": "Area of thyroid that does not take up radioactive iodine; higher risk for malignancy."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Significance of the BRAF mutation in thyroid cancer?",
                                      "a": "Strongly associated with <b>Papillary Thyroid Carcinoma</b> and poor prognosis."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Histological feature of <b>Graves Disease</b>?",
                                      "a": "Tall, columnar follicular cells with \"scalloping\" of the colloid."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Cause of Goiter in Iodine Deficiency?",
                                      "a": "Low T<sub>4</sub> leads to high <b>TSH</b>, which causes hypertrophy and hyperplasia of follicular cells."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Plummar Syndrome?",
                                      "a": "Toxic Multinodular Goiter (hyperfunctioning nodules independent of <b>TSH</b>)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Difference between Toxic Multinodular Goiter and Graves?",
                                      "a": "Toxic MNG lacks exophthalmos and pretibial myxedema."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cause of Hypocalcemia (overall)?",
                                      "a": "Hypoalbuminemia (though \"true\" ionized calcium remains normal)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary lab finding in Pseudohypoparathyroidism?",
                                      "a": "High <b>PTH</b> but low Calcium (due to end-organ resistance to <b>PTH</b>)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Genetic defect in Albright Hereditary Osteodystrophy?",
                                      "a": "Defective Gs-protein alpha subunit (GNAS1 mutation)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Physical phenotype of Albright Hereditary Osteodystrophy?",
                                      "a": "Short stature and shortened 4th/5th metacarpals.\n\nAdrenal Cortex & Medulla Nuances"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Impact of chronic Ketoconazole use on the Adrenal gland?",
                                      "a": "Inhibits desmolase/steroid synthesis, leading to adrenal insufficiency."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "High-dose Dexamethasone suppression test: No suppression of <b>ACTH</b>/Cortisol?",
                                      "a": "Suggests Ectopic <b>ACTH</b> production (e.g., Small Cell Lung Cancer)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "High-dose Dexamethasone suppression test: Suppression of Cortisol?",
                                      "a": "Confirms <b>Cushing Disease</b> (Pituitary Adenoma)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Cause of Primary Pigmented Nodular Adrenocortical Disease?",
                                      "a": "Part of Carney Complex (PRKAR1A mutation)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Spironolactone vs. Eplerenone in <b>Conn Syndrome</b>?",
                                      "a": "Eplerenone is more selective and lacks the anti-androgenic side effects (gynecomastia)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Diagnostic use of MIBG scan?",
                                      "a": "Localization of extra-adrenal Pheochromocytomas (Paragangliomas)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Pre-operative management of <b>Pheochromocytoma</b>?",
                                      "a": "Alpha-blockade (Phenoxybenzamine) followed by Beta-blockade."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Why must Alpha-blockade precede Beta-blockade in <b>Pheochromocytoma</b>?",
                                      "a": "To prevent hypertensive crisis from unopposed alpha-1 stimulation.\n\nDiabetes & Pancreatic Neuroendocrine Tumors"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Common HLA alleles for Type 1 DM?",
                                      "a": "HLA-DR3 and HLA-DR4."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Histological finding in early Type 1 DM islets?",
                                      "a": "Insulitis (lymphocytic infiltration)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Pathogenesis of Non-enzymatic Glycosylation (NEG) in Diabetes?",
                                      "a": "Glucose attaches to proteins, forming Advanced Glycation End-products (AGEs)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of Sorbitol damage in Diabetics?",
                                      "a": "Glucose converted to Sorbitol via Aldose Reductase, causing osmotic swelling in Schwann cells and Lens."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Classic presentation of Glucagonoma?",
                                      "a": "Necrolytic migratory erythema (rash), diabetes, and anemia."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Presentation of Somatostatinoma?",
                                      "a": "Diabetes, steatorrhea, and gallstones (due to inhibition of CCK and insulin)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Clinical syndrome of VIPoma?",
                                      "a": "WDHA Syndrome: Watery Diarrhea, Hypokalemia, Achlorhydria.\n\nSystemic Endocrine & MEN Syndromes"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Carcinoid Syndrome?",
                                      "a": "Flushing, diarrhea, and right-sided heart valve lesions."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Why are Carcinoid heart lesions only right-sided?",
                                      "a": "Lung MAO (Monoamine Oxidase) metabolizes serotonin before it reaches the left heart."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Urinary marker for Carcinoid Syndrome?",
                                      "a": "5-HIAA (5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Common trigger for Carcinoid Syndrome symptoms?",
                                      "a": "Alcohol or emotional stress causing hormone release."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Association of \"Brown Tumors\"?",
                                      "a": "Osteitis fibrosa cystica in Hyperparathyroidism."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Liddle Syndrome mimic?",
                                      "a": "Hyperaldosteronism presentation but with low Aldosterone/Renin (gain of function of ENaC)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Syndrome of Apparent Mineralocorticoid Excess (AME) cause?",
                                      "a": "Deficiency of 11-beta-HSD2 (often from licorice), allowing Cortisol to activate Mineralocorticoid receptors."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Difference between <b>MEN 2A</b> and 2B?",
                                      "a": "2A includes Hyperparathyroidism; 2B includes Mucosal Neuromas and Marfanoid habitus."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Common genetic screening for families of MEN 2 patients?",
                                      "a": "Testing for RET proto-oncogene mutations."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Prophylactic treatment for RET mutation carriers in MEN 2?",
                                      "a": "Prophylactic thyroidectomy (due to 100% risk of <b>Medullary Thyroid Carcinoma</b>)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Histological characteristic of an Adrenal Adenoma?",
                                      "a": "Well-circumscribed yellow mass with cells resembling the normal zona fasciculata."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common site of ectopic adrenal tissue?",
                                      "a": "Along the path of gonadal descent (e.g., retroperitoneum, ovaries, testes)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Histological appearance of Adrenocortical Carcinoma?",
                                      "a": "Large, invasive mass with areas of necrosis, hemorrhage, and cystic change."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Distinction between Adrenal Adenoma and Carcinoma?",
                                      "a": "Carcinomas are typically larger (>100g), show vascular invasion, and have higher mitotic rates."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Effect of 11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency on Renin levels?",
                                      "a": "Low Renin (due to hypertension caused by 11-deoxycorticosterone)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Underlying cause of Adrenal Crisis in Addison patients?",
                                      "a": "Any form of acute stress (infection, trauma, surgery) that increases cortisol demand."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "\"<b>Zellballen</b>\" pattern on histology is characteristic of?",
                                      "a": "<b>Pheochromocytoma</b> (nests of cells surrounded by vascular stroma)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Immunohistochemical stain positive in <b>Pheochromocytoma</b>?",
                                      "a": "Chromogranin and Synaptophysin."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "S100 stain utility in <b>Pheochromocytoma</b>?",
                                      "a": "Highlights the sustenacular cells surrounding the <b>Zellballen</b> nests.\n\nThyroid & Parathyroid: Nuance & Morphology"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of the \"Tall Cell Variant\" of Papillary Carcinoma?",
                                      "a": "Aggressive variant with height of cells at least twice their width and abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Significance of the RET/PTC rearrangement?",
                                      "a": "Genetic driver commonly found in <b>Papillary Thyroid Carcinoma</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Follicular Thyroid Carcinoma spread pattern?",
                                      "a": "Hematogenous (unlike Papillary, which is primarily lymphatic)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Pathogenesis of Secondary Hyperparathyroidism in Renal Failure?",
                                      "a": "Hyperphosphatemia leads to Hypocalcemia, which chronically stimulates <b>PTH</b> secretion."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Tertiary Hyperparathyroidism definition?",
                                      "a": "Autonomous <b>PTH</b> secretion following long-standing secondary hyperparathyroidism."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Major cause of \"Hungry Bone Syndrome\"?",
                                      "a": "Sudden drop in <b>PTH</b> after parathyroidectomy, causing rapid calcium uptake by bones."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Result of <b>PTH</b> on Renal Phosphorus handling?",
                                      "a": "Decreases phosphate reabsorption in the proximal convoluted tubule (Phosphate Trashing)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Histology of Parathyroid Adenoma?",
                                      "a": "Predominantly chief cells with a rim of normal parathyroid tissue and absence of stromal fat.\n\nPancreatic Islets & Neuroendocrine Nuances"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Relationship between Obesity and Insulin Resistance?",
                                      "a": "Increased adipokines (e.g., Resistin) and free fatty acids interfere with insulin signaling."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary cause of Polyuria in Diabetes?",
                                      "a": "Osmotic diuresis resulting from glucosuria."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Diagnostic threshold for HbA<sub>1c</sub> in <b>Diabetes Mellitus</b>?",
                                      "a": "Greater than or equal to 6.5%."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Insular\" amyloidosis?",
                                      "a": "Deposition of Amylin (IAPP) in the islets, characteristic of Type 2 DM."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common location of a Gastrinoma?",
                                      "a": "The \"Gastrinoma Triangle\" (Duodenum, pancreas, and porta hepatis)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Clinical finding in VIPoma (WDHA Syndrome)?",
                                      "a": "Watery Diarrhea, Hypokalemia, and Achlorhydria.\n\nPituitary & Hypothalamic Specifics"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Hormone deficiency sequence in Progressive Hypopituitarism?",
                                      "a": "GH is usually lost first, followed by LH/FSH, then <b>TSH</b> and <b>ACTH</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Kallmann Syndrome?",
                                      "a": "Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism with anosmia due to failure of GnRH neurons to migrate."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Effect of Craniopharyngioma on the Pituitary Stalk?",
                                      "a": "Compression can cause Hyperprolactinemia (loss of dopamine inhibition)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Histology of \"Adamantinomatous\" Craniopharyngioma?",
                                      "a": "Squamous epithelium, \"wet\" keratin, and calcifications.\n\nIntegrated Endocrine & Rare Syndromes"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Autoimmune Polyendocrine Syndrome Type 1 (APS-1)?",
                                      "a": "Triad of Hypoparathyroidism, Adrenal insufficiency, and Chronic Mucocutaneous Candidiasis."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Genetic defect in APS-1?",
                                      "a": "Mutation in the AIRE (Autoimmune Regulator) gene."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Major endocrine risk in patients with Cowden Syndrome?",
                                      "a": "Increased risk of Follicular Thyroid Carcinoma (PTEN mutation)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "McCune-Albright Syndrome endocrine manifestations?",
                                      "a": "Precocious puberty, hyperthyroidism, and GH-secreting adenomas."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Physical triad of McCune-Albright Syndrome?",
                                      "a": "Polyostotic fibrous dysplasia, Caf\u00e9-au-lait spots, and autonomous endocrine hyperfunction."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Metabolic cause of \"Thyroidization\" of the kidney?",
                                      "a": "Chronic Pyelonephritis (islets of thyroid-like tissue in renal parenchyma; distinct from thyroid pathology)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Difference between \"Primary\" and \"Secondary\" Hyperaldosteronism Renin levels?",
                                      "a": "Primary has Low Renin; Secondary has High Renin."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Cause of Secondary Hyperaldosteronism?",
                                      "a": "Decreased renal perfusion (e.g., Renal Artery Stenosis, Heart Failure).\n\nHigh-Yield Laboratory Interferences"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Effect of high Biotin intake on Thyroid Function Tests?",
                                      "a": "Can falsely elevate T<sub>4</sub>/T<sub>3</sub> and falsely lower <b>TSH</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Why is <b>C-peptide</b> useful in Hypoglycemia workup?",
                                      "a": "Distinguishes endogenous insulin production (high <b>C-peptide</b>) from exogenous insulin injection (low <b>C-peptide</b>)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Lab profile of Factitious Hyperthyroidism?",
                                      "a": "Low <b>TSH</b>, High T<sub>4</sub>, and Low Thyroglobulin (due to exogenous ingestion)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cause of a \"Thyroid Storm\"?",
                                      "a": "Acute stress (infection, surgery, or trauma) in a patient with pre-existing <b>Graves disease</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Effect of Pregnancy on Total T<sub>4</sub> vs. Free T<sub>4</sub>?",
                                      "a": "Total T<sub>4</sub> increases (due to high TBG), but Free T<sub>4</sub> remains normal (eustates)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Significance of \"Cold\" vs \"Hot\" nodules on RAIU scan?",
                                      "a": "Cold nodules are non-functional and more likely to be malignant; Hot nodules are functional and usually benign."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Histological feature of a Hurthle Cell?",
                                      "a": "Large follicular cell with abundant, granular, eosinophilic cytoplasm and numerous mitochondria."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Condition where Hurthle Cells are most prominently seen?",
                                      "a": "<b>Hashimoto Thyroiditis</b> and Hurthle Cell Adenoma/Carcinoma."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common location for an ectopic Parathyroid gland?",
                                      "a": "Within the Thymus (due to shared origin from the 3rd pharyngeal pouch)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Pathogenesis of Calciphylaxis?",
                                      "a": "Systemic calcification of small vessels leading to skin necrosis, often seen in end-stage renal disease/secondary hyperparathyroidism."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Difference between Osteitis Fibrosa Cystica and Renal Osteodystrophy?",
                                      "a": "Osteitis Fibrosa Cystica is specific to high <b>PTH</b>; Renal Osteodystrophy is the broader bone disease seen in chronic kidney failure.\n\nAdrenal & Pituitary: Advanced Diagnostics"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of the Metyrapone stimulation test?",
                                      "a": "Blocks 11-beta-hydroxylase; a normal response is an increase in <b>ACTH</b> and 11-deoxycortisol."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Utility of the Metyrapone test?",
                                      "a": "To evaluate the integrity of the entire Hypothalamic-Pituitary-Adrenal (HPA) axis."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Histological finding in the pituitary of a patient with <b>Cushing Syndrome</b>?",
                                      "a": "Crooke Hyaline Change (accumulation of intermediate filaments in <b>ACTH</b>-producing cells)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary difference between Lymphocytic Hypophysitis and Pituitary Adenoma?",
                                      "a": "Lymphocytic Hypophysitis is an autoimmune inflammation (often postpartum) that can mimic a mass on MRI."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Genetic mutation associated with <b>MEN 1</b>?",
                                      "a": "Mutation in the MENIN tumor suppressor gene."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common pituitary tumor in <b>MEN 1</b>?",
                                      "a": "<b>Prolactinoma</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Relationship between Pituitary <b>Gigantism</b> and the Cardiovascular system?",
                                      "a": "Often leads to heart failure due to concentric left ventricular hypertrophy and cardiomyopathy.\n\nMetabolic & Pancreatic: The \"Grey Zones\""
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of \"Amyloidosis\" in Type 2 Diabetes?",
                                      "a": "Co-secretion of Islet Amyloid Polypeptide (Amylin) with insulin leads to its deposition in islets."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Distinction between Diabetic Ketoacidosis (<b>DKA</b>) and Hyperosmolar Hyperglycemic State (<b>HHS</b>)?",
                                      "a": "<b>DKA</b> (Type 1) has ketones and acidosis; <b>HHS</b> (Type 2) has extreme dehydration and higher glucose (>600 mg/dL)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary reason for the \"Dawn Phenomenon\" in Diabetics?",
                                      "a": "Early morning rise in blood sugar due to the physiological surge of Growth Hormone and Cortisol."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of the \"Somogyi Effect\"?",
                                      "a": "Rebound hyperglycemia in the morning in response to an undiagnosed hypoglycemic episode during the night."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Diagnostic use of the Glucagon Stimulation Test?",
                                      "a": "Used to diagnose <b>Pheochromocytoma</b> (triggers catecholamine release) or growth hormone deficiency."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Hormone responsible for the \"Incretin Effect\"?",
                                      "a": "GLP-1 (Glucagon-like peptide-1) and GIP, which increase insulin secretion after oral glucose.\n\nNeuroendocrine & Rare Syndromes"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Paraganglioma?",
                                      "a": "A tumor of the extra-adrenal sympathetic or parasympathetic ganglia (the \"extra-adrenal <b>pheochromocytoma</b>\")."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common location for a Paraganglioma?",
                                      "a": "The Organ of Zuckerkandl (near the origin of the inferior mesenteric artery)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Components of Carney Complex?",
                                      "a": "Myxomas (heart/skin), hyperpigmentation (lentigines), and endocrine overactivity (adrenal/pituitary)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Characteristic of Von Hippel-Lindau (VHL) syndrome in Endocrine?",
                                      "a": "High risk for <b>Pheochromocytoma</b> (often bilateral)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Difference in <b>Pheochromocytoma</b> risk between <b>MEN 2A</b> and 2B?",
                                      "a": "The risk is roughly equal (~50%), but 2B presents with a more aggressive thyroid component."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Histological \"Salt and Pepper\" chromatin is a classic description of?",
                                      "a": "Neuroendocrine tumors (e.g., Carcinoid, Pancreatic NETs, <b>Medullary Thyroid Carcinoma</b>)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common hormone secreted by a Pancreatic Neuroendocrine Tumor (PanNET)?",
                                      "a": "Insulin (Insulinoma)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is the \"Rule of 10s\" for Insulinomas?",
                                      "a": "Unlike <b>Pheochromocytoma</b>, only 10% of Insulinomas are malignant (most are benign)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cause of death in patients with <b>Acromegaly</b>?",
                                      "a": "Heart failure (due to dilated cardiomyopathy)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of hypercalcemia in Sarcoidosis?",
                                      "a": "1-alpha-hydroxylase activity in granulomatous macrophages converts Vitamin D to its active form (1,25-OH)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Histological appearance of the thyroid in Riedel Thyroiditis?",
                                      "a": "Dense hyalinized fibrosis and a chronic inflammatory infiltrate."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Effect of chronic Lithium use on the thyroid?",
                                      "a": "Inhibition of thyroid hormone release, commonly causing hypothyroidism."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of the \"Pendred Syndrome\"?",
                                      "a": "Genetic disorder causing sensorineural hearing loss and goiter due to iodine transport defects."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary difference in laboratory findings between 21-hydroxylase and 11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency?",
                                      "a": "21-hydroxylase deficiency presents with hypotension; 11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency presents with hypertension."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Why does 17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency cause hypertension?",
                                      "a": "Accumulation of 11-deoxycorticosterone, which has mineralocorticoid activity."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cause of primary adrenal insufficiency in children?",
                                      "a": "Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia (<b>CAH</b>)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Distinguishing feature of an Adrenal Myelolipoma?",
                                      "a": "A benign, non-functioning tumor composed of mature adipose tissue and hematopoietic elements.\n\nPediatric & Developmental Endocrine Pathology"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Presentation of a Lingual Thyroid?",
                                      "a": "A mass at the base of the tongue; may be the only functional thyroid tissue present."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cause of congenital hypothyroidism worldwide?",
                                      "a": "Thyroid dysgenesis (aplasia, hypoplasia, or ectopic gland)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Effect of maternal gestational diabetes on the newborn?",
                                      "a": "Islet cell hyperplasia in the fetus leading to neonatal hypoglycemia after birth."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Classic cardiac defect associated with infants of diabetic mothers?",
                                      "a": "Transposition of the Great Arteries or Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy (septal hypertrophy)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is the \"Water-Clear Cell\" hyperplasia?",
                                      "a": "A rare form of parathyroid hyperplasia where cells have abundant clear cytoplasm.\n\nFinishing Touches: \"Buzzword\" Review"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "\"<b>Psammoma bodies</b>\" in the thyroid?",
                                      "a": "<b>Papillary Thyroid Carcinoma</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "\"Amyloid stroma\" in the thyroid?",
                                      "a": "<b>Medullary Thyroid Carcinoma</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "\"<b>Orphan Annie</b> Eye\" nuclei?",
                                      "a": "<b>Papillary Thyroid Carcinoma</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "\"Rock-hard, fixed thyroid\"?",
                                      "a": "Riedel Thyroiditis (or Anaplastic Carcinoma, but Riedel is the classic \"hard\" association)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "\"Pot-bellied, pale, puffy-faced infant with a protruding tongue\"?",
                                      "a": "Cretinism (Congenital Hypothyroidism)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "\"Scalloped colloid\"?",
                                      "a": "<b>Graves Disease</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "\"Hurthle cells and lymphoid follicles with germinal centers\"?",
                                      "a": "<b>Hashimoto Thyroiditis</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "\"<b>Zellballen</b>\" nests?",
                                      "a": "<b>Pheochromocytoma</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "\"<b>Homer-Wright Rosettes</b>\"?",
                                      "a": "<b>Neuroblastoma</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "\"<b>Kimmelstiel-Wilson</b> nodules\"?",
                                      "a": "Diabetic Nephropathy."
                            }
                  ]
        },
