                                      "q": "Unique ion current responsible for the <b>SA node</b> \"pacemaker\" potential?",
                                      "a": "I<sub>f</sub> (funny current) sodium channels."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Normal PR interval duration?",
                                      "a": "120 to 200 milliseconds."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Effect of carotid sinus massage on the heart?",
                                      "a": "Increases baroreceptor firing, increasing vagal tone to slow the HR.\n\nPart 3: Pathology - Valvular & Structural"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Classic triad of <b>Aortic Stenosis</b> symptoms?",
                                      "a": "Syncope, Angina, and Dyspnea (SAD)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Murmur description for <b>Aortic Stenosis</b>?",
                                      "a": "Crescendo-decrescendo systolic ejection murmur."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Murmur associated with <b>Mitral Regurgitation</b>?",
                                      "a": "Holosystolic, high-pitched \"blowing\" murmur at the apex."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cause of Mitral Stenosis?",
                                      "a": "Chronic <b>Rheumatic Heart Disease</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Murmur associated with an Atrial Septal Defect (<b>ASD</b>)?",
                                      "a": "Mid-systolic murmur with a wide, fixed split S2."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Physical exam finding in Coarctation of the Aorta?",
                                      "a": "Brachial-femoral pulse delay and rib notching on X-ray."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Four components of <b>Tetralogy of Fallot</b>?",
                                      "a": "Pulmonary stenosis, RVH, Overriding aorta, <b>VSD</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Pathognomonic histological finding in <b>Rheumatic Heart Disease</b>?",
                                      "a": "<b>Aschoff bodies</b> (containing Anitschkow cells).\n\nPart 4: Ischemic Heart Disease & Heart Failure"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Unstable Angina?",
                                      "a": "Angina at rest or increasing in frequency/severity without enzyme elevation."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "EKG finding in Prinzmetal (variant) angina?",
                                      "a": "Transient ST-segment elevation."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Gold standard biomarker for myocardial infarction (MI)?",
                                      "a": "Cardiac <b>Troponin</b> I or T."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "MI biomarker that returns to normal first (useful for re-infarction)?",
                                      "a": "<b>CK-MB</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cause of death in the first 24 hours post-MI?",
                                      "a": "Arrhythmia (usually Ventricular Fibrillation)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Timing of free wall rupture or papillary muscle rupture post-MI?",
                                      "a": "3 to 14 days."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Name of the friction rub-associated pericarditis occurring weeks after an MI?",
                                      "a": "<b>Dressler Syndrome</b> (Autoimmune)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Heart Failure with Preserved Ejection Fraction (<b>HFpEF</b>)?",
                                      "a": "Diastolic dysfunction (impaired filling) with <b>EF</b> &ge;q 50%."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Key compensatory mechanism in Chronic Heart Failure?",
                                      "a": "Activation of the Renin-Angiotensin-Aldosterone System (RAAS)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Interpretation of an elevated B-type Natriuretic Peptide (<b>BNP</b>)?",
                                      "a": "Indicates ventricular wall stretch/volume overload.\n\nPart 5: Cardiomyopathies & Inflammatory Disease"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cause of Dilated Cardiomyopathy?",
                                      "a": "Idiopathic (or Alcohol, Beriberi, <i>Coxsackie B</i>, Chagas)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Inheritance pattern of Hypertrophic Obstructive Cardiomyopathy (<b>HOCM</b>)?",
                                      "a": "Autosomal Dominant (Sarcomere protein mutations)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "<b>Beck's Triad</b> for <b>Cardiac Tamponade</b>?",
                                      "a": "Hypotension, Distended neck veins, Muffled heart sounds."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Classic EKG finding in <b>Cardiac Tamponade</b>?",
                                      "a": "Electrical alternans."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "<b>Kussmaul sign</b>?",
                                      "a": "Increase in JVP on inspiration (seen in constrictive pericarditis)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common organism in Subacute Bacterial Endocarditis?",
                                      "a": "<i>Viridans group streptococci</i>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common organism in Acute Endocarditis (IV drug users)?",
                                      "a": "<i>Staphylococcus aureus</i>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Physical signs of <b>infective endocarditis</b> on the hands/eyes?",
                                      "a": "<b>Osler nodes</b>, <b>Janeway lesions</b>, and <b>Roth spots</b>.\n\nPart 6: Vascular & Hypertension"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Hypertensive Emergency?",
                                      "a": "BP >180/120 mmHg with evidence of end-organ damage."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Characteristic of Monckeberg Sclerosis?",
                                      "a": "Medial calcific sclerosis that does not obstruct blood flow."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Histological appearance of malignant hypertension in vessels?",
                                      "a": "Hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis (\"onion skinning\")."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Classic presentation of <b>Aortic Dissection</b>?",
                                      "a": "Sudden onset \"tearing\" chest pain radiating to the back."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most important risk factor for <b>Aortic Dissection</b>?",
                                      "a": "Hypertension."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "<b>Giant Cell Arteritis</b> is associated with which systemic condition?",
                                      "a": "Polymyalgia Rheumatica."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Classic triad of <b>Wegener</b>\u2019s (Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis)?",
                                      "a": "Nasopharynx, Lung, and Kidney involvement."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Vascular disease associated with \"String of Beads\" on angiography?",
                                      "a": "Fibromuscular Dysplasia.\n\nPart 7: Pharmacology"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of action of Statins?",
                                      "a": "HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Side effects of ACE Inhibitors?",
                                      "a": "Cough, Angioedema, Hyperkalemia (CATCH)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Preferred drug for hypertensive emergency in pregnancy?",
                                      "a": "Hydralazine or Labetalol."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of Class I Antiarrhythmics?",
                                      "a": "Sodium (Na<sup>+</sup>) channel blockers."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of Class III Antiarrhythmics (e.g., Amiodarone)?",
                                      "a": "Potassium (K<sup>+</sup>) channel blockers (prolong repolarization)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Common side effect of Dihydropyridine Calcium Channel Blockers (e.g., Amlodipine)?",
                                      "a": "Peripheral edema."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Reversal agent for Heparin?",
                                      "a": "Protamine sulfate."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Reversal agent for Warfarin?",
                                      "a": "Vitamin K (delayed) or Fresh Frozen Plasma (rapid)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Clinical use of Digoxin?",
                                      "a": "Heart failure and Atrial Fibrillation (slows AV conduction)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Characteristic EKG change of Digoxin toxicity?",
                                      "a": "\"Scooped\" ST segments."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Drug of choice for paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia (PSVT)?",
                                      "a": "Adenosine.\n\nPart 8: Clinical Skills & Diagnostics"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Significance of a Fourth Heart Sound (S4)?",
                                      "a": "Atrial kick into a stiff ventricle (e.g., hypertrophy)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Significance of a Third Heart Sound (S3)?",
                                      "a": "Rapid ventricular filling in a dilated ventricle (e.g., HF)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "EKG criteria for Left Ventricular Hypertrophy?",
                                      "a": "Deep S in V1 + Tall R in V5 > 35mm."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Appearance of \"Water-bottle\" heart on Chest X-ray?",
                                      "a": "Large pericardial effusion."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Diagnosis for \"Boot-shaped heart\" on X-ray?",
                                      "a": "<b>Tetralogy of Fallot</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Meaning of \"Pulsus Paradoxus\"?",
                                      "a": "Drop in systolic BP > 10 mmHg during inspiration.\n\nPart 9: Advanced Topics (Braunwald/Lilly Focus)"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary mechanism of Prinzmetal Angina?",
                                      "a": "Coronary artery vasospasm."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Pathophysiology of \"Stunned Myocardium\"?",
                                      "a": "Reversible contractile dysfunction after temporary ischemia."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Characteristic of \"Hibernating Myocardium\"?",
                                      "a": "Chronic ischemia leading to downregulated function, reversible with revascularization."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Key hemodynamic difference between restrictive cardiomyopathy and constrictive pericarditis?",
                                      "a": "Restrictive CM usually has higher <b>BNP</b> and pulmonary pressures."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "EKG finding in Wolff-Parkinson-White (<b>WPW</b>) syndrome?",
                                      "a": "Short PR interval and Delta wave (slurred upstroke of QRS)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Major criteria for <b>Jones criteria</b> (Rheumatic Fever)?",
                                      "a": "Joints (polyarthritis), Carditis, Nodules, Erythema marginatum, Sydenham chorea."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common primary cardiac tumor in adults?",
                                      "a": "<b>Atrial Myxoma</b> (usually left atrium)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common primary cardiac tumor in children?",
                                      "a": "<b>Rhabdomyoma</b> (associated with Tuberous Sclerosis)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of Nitrate tolerance?",
                                      "a": "Depletion of sulfhydryl groups required for NO release."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cause of sudden cardiac death in young athletes?",
                                      "a": "Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy.\n\nPart 10: Summary & Integration"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Effect of inspiration on Right-sided murmurs?",
                                      "a": "Increases intensity (due to increased venous return)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Effect of Valsalva maneuver on <b>HOCM</b> murmur?",
                                      "a": "Increases intensity (decreased <b>preload</b>)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Effect of Squatting on <b>HOCM</b> murmur?",
                                      "a": "Decreases intensity (increased <b>preload</b> and <b>afterload</b>)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "First-line treatment for stable chronic angina?",
                                      "a": "Beta-blockers."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Drug used to close a Patent Ductus Arteriosus (<b>PDA</b>)?",
                                      "a": "Indomethacin (NSAID)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Drug used to keep a <b>PDA</b> open?",
                                      "a": "Prostaglandin E1."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definitive treatment for Third-degree (Complete) Heart Block?",
                                      "a": "Permanent pacemaker."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "\"Thumbprint\" sign on abdominal X-ray suggests?",
                                      "a": "Ischemic colitis (often secondary to low CO or vascular disease)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common site of Myocardial Infarction?",
                                      "a": "Left Anterior Descending (<b>LAD</b>) artery (\"<b>Widowmaker</b>\")."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Complication of high-flow oxygen in a patient with COPD and <b>cor pulmonale</b>?",
                                      "a": "CO2 retention and respiratory acidosis (loss of hypoxic drive)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of <b>Brugada Syndrome</b>?",
                                      "a": "Autosomal dominant disorder with pseudo-right bundle branch block and ST elevations in V1-V3; risk of V-fib."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Distinguishing feature of Mobitz Type I (Wenckebach) AV block?",
                                      "a": "Progressive PR interval lengthening until a QRS is dropped."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Distinguishing feature of Mobitz Type II AV block?",
                                      "a": "Dropped QRS complexes without PR interval lengthening; usually involves His-Purkinje system."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Treatment of choice for <b>Torsades de Pointes</b>?",
                                      "a": "Intravenous Magnesium Sulfate."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cause of <b>Long QT Syndrome</b>?",
                                      "a": "Congenital mutations in K<sup>+</sup> or Na<sup>+</sup> channels (e.g., Romano-Ward or Jervell and Lange-Nielsen)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "EKG finding in Multifocal Atrial Tachycardia (MAT)?",
                                      "a": "At least 3 different P-wave morphologies; often seen in COPD patients."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of the \"<b>Ashman Phenomenon</b>\"?",
                                      "a": "A long R-R interval followed by a short R-R interval, often causing an aberrant QRS (usually RBBB) in A-fib.\n\nPart 12: Congenital Heart Disease (Detailed)"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Direction of shunt in <b>Eisenmenger Syndrome</b>?",
                                      "a": "Right-to-Left (reversal of a chronic Left-to-Right shunt)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Common cardiac defect in Down Syndrome?",
                                      "a": "Atrioventricular (AV) canal defect."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Cardiac defect associated with Turner Syndrome?",
                                      "a": "Bicuspid aortic valve or Preductal Coarctation of the aorta."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Neonatal presentation of Transposition of the Great Vessels?",
                                      "a": "Early cyanosis (blue baby) that is not corrected by supplemental oxygen."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Pathophysiology of <b>Ebstein Anomaly</b>?",
                                      "a": "Displaced tricuspid valve leaflets into the right ventricle (\"atrialization\" of the RV)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Maternal drug exposure associated with <b>Ebstein Anomaly</b>?",
                                      "a": "Lithium."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "X-ray finding in Total Anomalous Pulmonary Venous Return (TAPVR)?",
                                      "a": "\"Snowman\" sign (dilated vertical vein and SVC).\n\nPart 13: Clinical Management & Guidelines"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "First-line treatment for acute pericarditis?",
                                      "a": "NSAIDs and Colchicine."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Recommended door-to-balloon time for STEMI?",
                                      "a": "Within 90 minutes."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Contraindication for Nitrate use in MI?",
                                      "a": "Right Ventricular infarction or recent Phosphodiesterase inhibitor (Viagra) use."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Goal LDL for a patient with established ASCVD (Very High Risk)?",
                                      "a": "Less than 55 mg/dL."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Indication for SGLT2 inhibitors in Cardiology?",
                                      "a": "Heart failure (both HFrEF and <b>HFpEF</b>), regardless of diabetes status."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Class of drugs that improve mortality in HFrEF?",
                                      "a": "ACEi/ARNI, Beta-blockers, Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists (MRA), and SGLT2 inhibitors."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Major side effect of Hydralazine?",
                                      "a": "Drug-induced Lupus Erythematosus.\n\nPart 14: Rare & High-Yield Pathologies"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Typical patient demographic for <b>Takotsubo</b> Cardiomyopathy?",
                                      "a": "Post-menopausal women following extreme emotional or physical stress."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Cardiac complication of <b>Kawasaki Disease</b>?",
                                      "a": "Coronary artery aneurysms."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Diagnostic test for Syphilitic Aneurysm?",
                                      "a": "Tree-barking of the aorta (endarteritis of vasa vasorum)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Common cause of Myocarditis in South America?",
                                      "a": "<i>Trypanosoma cruzi</i> (Chagas Disease)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Histological finding in Giant Cell Myocarditis?",
                                      "a": "Extensive necrosis with multinucleated giant cells and lymphocytic infiltrate."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Classic EKG finding in Pulmonary Embolism?",
                                      "a": "S1Q3T3 (Deep S in I, Q wave in III, inverted T in III).\n\nPart 15: Pressure-Volume Loops & Curves"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Effect of increased <b>Afterload</b> on the Pressure-Volume loop?",
                                      "a": "Increased peak systolic pressure and decreased Stroke Volume (narrower loop)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Effect of increased Inotropy on the Pressure-Volume loop?",
                                      "a": "Increased Stroke Volume and decreased End-Systolic Volume (loop shifts left)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Point on the PV loop where the Mitral Valve opens?",
                                      "a": "Bottom left corner (following isovolumetric relaxation)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Point on the PV loop where the Aortic Valve closes?",
                                      "a": "Top left corner.\n\nPart 16: Physical Exam Nuances (Chizner/Lilly)"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Heart sound heard in Mitral Stenosis?",
                                      "a": "Opening Snap (OS) followed by a mid-diastolic rumble."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Significance of a shortened A2-OS interval?",
                                      "a": "Indicates more severe Mitral Stenosis."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Murmur of Pulmonic Regurgitation?",
                                      "a": "Graham Steell murmur (high-pitched, decrescendo diastolic murmur)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Character of the pulse in Aortic Regurgitation?",
                                      "a": "Water-hammer pulse (Corrigan pulse) or bounding pulses."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Pulsus Alternans is a sign of?",
                                      "a": "Severe Left Ventricular systolic failure."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Pulsus Bisferiens is seen in which conditions?",
                                      "a": "Aortic Regurgitation or Hypertrophic Obstructive Cardiomyopathy."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Effect of Handgrip on <b>Aortic Stenosis</b> murmur?",
                                      "a": "Decreases intensity (increased <b>afterload</b> decreases flow across the valve)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Effect of Handgrip on <b>Mitral Regurgitation</b> murmur?",
                                      "a": "Increases intensity (increased <b>afterload</b> increases backward flow).\n\nPart 17: Summary Statistics & Trivia"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cause of heart failure worldwide?",
                                      "a": "Coronary Artery Disease."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cause of constrictive pericarditis in developing countries?",
                                      "a": "<i>Tuberculosis</i>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Percentage of people with a Patent Foramen Ovale (PFO)?",
                                      "a": "Approximately 25%."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common site of an Aortic Aneurysm?",
                                      "a": "Abdominal Aorta (infrarenal)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Major risk factor for Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm (AAA)?",
                                      "a": "Tobacco smoking."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Standard screening for AAA?",
                                      "a": "One-time ultrasound in men aged 65\u201375 who have ever smoked."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Classic EKG progression in STEMI?",
                                      "a": "Hyperacute T-waves &rarr; ST elevation &rarr; Q waves &rarr; T-wave inversion."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common complication of Mitral Valve Prolapse?",
                                      "a": "<b>Mitral Regurgitation</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "\"Early peaking\" systolic murmur suggests?",
                                      "a": "Mild <b>Aortic Stenosis</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "\"Late peaking\" systolic murmur suggests?",
                                      "a": "Severe <b>Aortic Stenosis</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Apoprotein that mediates extrahepatic uptake of LDL?",
                                      "a": "Apo B-100."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary function of Apo A-I?",
                                      "a": "Activates LCAT to catalyze cholesterol esterification in HDL (Reverse cholesterol transport)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Deficiency in Familial Hypercholesterolemia (Type IIa)?",
                                      "a": "Absent or defective LDL receptors."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Physical finding pathognomonic for Hypertriglyceridemia?",
                                      "a": "Eruptive xanthomas."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Physical finding pathognomonic for Type II Hyperlipidemia?",
                                      "a": "Tendon xanthomas (especially Achilles)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Key enzyme inhibited by Fibrates?",
                                      "a": "They activate PPAR-&alpha; to increase LPL activity."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary side effect of Niacin treatment?",
                                      "a": "Cutaneous flushing (mediated by prostaglandins).\n\nPart 19: Metabolic & Endocrine Effects on the Heart"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Cardiac effect of Hyperthyroidism?",
                                      "a": "Increased B1-receptor expression leading to tachycardia and high-output heart failure."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cardiac manifestation of Sarcoidosis?",
                                      "a": "Restrictive cardiomyopathy or sudden cardiac death due to AV block."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "\"Orphan Annie eye\" nuclei in a thyroid met are unrelated, but what is the cardiac \"Orphan\" sign?",
                                      "a": "There is none; however, \"Soldier\u2019s Heart\" refers to Autonomic Dystonia (Da Costa's Syndrome)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Effect of Hypocalcemia on the EKG?",
                                      "a": "QT interval prolongation."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Effect of Hyperkalemia on the EKG?",
                                      "a": "Peaked T-waves, dropped P-waves, and widened QRS (sine wave)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Cardiovascular complication of Acromegaly?",
                                      "a": "Biventricular hypertrophy and cardiomyopathy.\n\nPart 20: Advanced Pathology & Morphology (Robbins Focus)"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of a \"Stary\" lesion in atherosclerosis?",
                                      "a": "A fatty streak (the earliest visible lesion)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common site of a Syphilitic (Luetic) Aneurysm?",
                                      "a": "Ascending Aorta and Aortic Arch."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Histological term for debris-filled plaque in a coronary artery?",
                                      "a": "Atheroma with a necrotic core (cholesterol clefts)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Difference between a \"True\" and \"False\" (Pseudo) aneurysm?",
                                      "a": "A true aneurysm involves all three layers of the arterial wall."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary location of a \"Nutmeg Liver\"?",
                                      "a": "Central veins (due to Right Heart Failure/Passive Congestion)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Pathophysiology of Cardiac Amyloidosis?",
                                      "a": "Deposition of misfolded proteins (AL or TTR) in the myocardium."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Cardiac finding in Carcinoid Syndrome?",
                                      "a": "Endocardial fibrosis of the Right Heart (Tricuspid/Pulmonic) valves.\n\nPart 21: High-Yield Physics & Formulas"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Formula for Stroke Volume?",
                                      "a": "<b>EDV</b> - <b>ESV</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Formula for Ejection Fraction?",
                                      "a": "(<b>SV</b> / <b>EDV</b>) &times; 100."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Relationship between Wall Stress and Pressure (Law of Laplace)?",
                                      "a": "Wall Stress = (Pressure &times; Radius) / (2 &times; Wall Thickness)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Effect of increased radius on wall stress?",
                                      "a": "Increases wall stress (why dilated hearts fail)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Factor that determines the \"Velocity of Shortening\" in muscle?",
                                      "a": "<b>Afterload</b> (inversely proportional).\n\nPart 22: Clinical Exam & \"Buzzwords\""
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "\"Double apical impulse\" is a hallmark of?",
                                      "a": "Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy (<b>HOCM</b>)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "\"Machine-like\" murmur heard throughout the cardiac cycle?",
                                      "a": "Patent Ductus Arteriosus (<b>PDA</b>)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "\"Early diastolic blowing murmur\" at the left sternal border?",
                                      "a": "Aortic Regurgitation."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "\"Mid-systolic click\" followed by a murmur?",
                                      "a": "Mitral Valve Prolapse (MVP)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What maneuver makes an MVP click occur earlier?",
                                      "a": "Standing or Valsalva (decreased <b>preload</b>)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Significance of the \"Cannon A-wave\" in JVP?",
                                      "a": "Right atrium contracting against a closed tricuspid valve (Complete Heart Block/V-tach)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "\"Delta wave\" on EKG?",
                                      "a": "Wolff-Parkinson-White (<b>WPW</b>) Syndrome."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "\"Osborn Wave\" (J-wave) on EKG?",
                                      "a": "Hypothermia.\n\nPart 23: Integration (Pharmacology + Pathology)"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Class of antiarrhythmics that exhibit \"Use-Dependence\"?",
                                      "a": "Class IC (Flecainide, Propafenone)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Antiarrhythmic drug known for causing pulmonary fibrosis?",
                                      "a": "Amiodarone."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Antiarrhythmic drug known for causing Cinchonism (tinnitus/headache)?",
                                      "a": "Quinidine."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of Ezetimibe?",
                                      "a": "Prevents cholesterol absorption at the small intestine brush border."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Preferred treatment for Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy symptoms?",
                                      "a": "Beta-blockers or Non-dihydropyridine Calcium Channel Blockers (Verapamil)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Drug used to decrease mortality in African American patients with HFrEF?",
                                      "a": "Isosorbide Dinitrate + Hydralazine (BiDil)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary risk of using Sodium Nitroprusside?",
                                      "a": "Cyanide toxicity."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cause of myocarditis in the USA?",
                                      "a": "<i>Adenovirus</i> and <i>Coxsackie B</i> virus."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Formula for Pulmonary Vascular Resistance (<b>PVR</b>)?",
                                      "a": "<b>PVR</b> = (MPAP - <b>PCWP</b>) / CO."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Normal value for Pulmonary Capillary Wedge Pressure (<b>PCWP</b>)?",
                                      "a": "4 to 12 mmHg."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Condition where <b>PCWP</b> is elevated?",
                                      "a": "Left heart failure or Mitral Stenosis."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Condition where Mean Pulmonary Artery Pressure is high but <b>PCWP</b> is normal?",
                                      "a": "Pulmonary Arterial Hypertension (Group 1)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Effect of a Left-to-Right shunt on Oxygen Saturation in the Right Atrium?",
                                      "a": "Increased O2 saturation in the Right Atrium (characteristic of <b>ASD</b>).\n\nPart 25: Advanced Anatomy & Radiology"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Vessel most likely to be compressed by a Left Atrial enlargement?",
                                      "a": "Esophagus (causing dysphagia) or Left Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve (causing hoarseness/Ortner Syndrome)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common location for a Traumatic Aortic Rupture?",
                                      "a": "Aortic Isthmus (just distal to the origin of the left subclavian artery)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "\"Egg-on-a-string\" appearance on Chest X-ray?",
                                      "a": "Transposition of the Great Vessels."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "\"Rib notching\" on X-ray is due to collateral circulation through which vessels?",
                                      "a": "Intercostal arteries (seen in Coarctation of the Aorta).\n\nPart 26: Specific Clinical Syndromes (Braunwald/Chizner)"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of <b>Heyde Syndrome</b>?",
                                      "a": "Association between <b>Aortic Stenosis</b> and GI bleeding from colonic angiodysplasia."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Classic EKG finding in <b>Wellens Syndrome</b>?",
                                      "a": "Deeply inverted or biphasic T-waves in V2-V3 (signifies critical proximal <b>LAD</b> stenosis)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Cardiac Index?",
                                      "a": "<b>Cardiac Output</b> divided by Body Surface Area (BSA)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common site of origin for Atrial Flutter?",
                                      "a": "Cavotricuspid isthmus in the Right Atrium."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary treatment for Symptomatic Bradycardia?",
                                      "a": "Atropine.\n\nPart 27: Pathology Tidbits (Robbins)"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"<b>Cor Pulmonale</b>\"?",
                                      "a": "Right ventricular hypertrophy and failure resulting from primary pulmonary hypertension or lung disease."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cause of a Paradoxical Embolus?",
                                      "a": "Patent Foramen Ovale (PFO) or <b>ASD</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Microscopic finding in Viral Myocarditis?",
                                      "a": "Lymphocytic infiltrate with focal myocyte necrosis."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Histological hallmark of Monckeberg Medial Calcific Sclerosis?",
                                      "a": "Ring-like calcifications in the media of medium-sized arteries; no lumen narrowing.\n\nPart 28: Critical Pharmacology Nuances"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Drug of choice for Beta-blocker overdose?",
                                      "a": "Glucagon (increases cAMP in myocytes via non-beta receptors)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of Ranolazine?",
                                      "a": "Inhibits the late inward sodium current, reducing diastolic wall tension and O2 consumption."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Why are ACE inhibitors contraindicated in Bilateral Renal Artery Stenosis?",
                                      "a": "They inhibit efferent arteriole constriction, causing a sharp drop in GFR and acute renal failure."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Main toxicity of Nitroprusside in patients with renal failure?",
                                      "a": "Thiocyanate toxicity (presents as psychosis, seizures, or delirium)."
                            }
                  ]
        },
        {
                  "topic": "Renal/Urinary Pathology",
                  "icon": "droplet",
                  "cards": [
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of <b>Nephrotic Syndrome</b>?",
                                      "a": "Massive proteinuria (> 3.5 g/day), hypoalbuminemia, edema, and hyperlipidemia."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cause of <b>Nephrotic Syndrome</b> in children?",
                                      "a": "<b>Minimal Change Disease</b> (<b>MCD</b>)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Light microscopy finding in <b>Minimal Change Disease</b>?",
                                      "a": "Normal-appearing glomeruli."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Electron microscopy finding diagnostic for <b>Minimal Change Disease</b>?",
                                      "a": "Effacement (flattening) of podocyte foot processes."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "First-line treatment for <b>Minimal Change Disease</b>?",
                                      "a": "Corticosteroids (Prednisone)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cause of <b>Nephrotic Syndrome</b> in African Americans and Hispanics?",
                                      "a": "<b>Focal Segmental Glomerulosclerosis</b> (<b>FSGS</b>)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Secondary causes associated with <b>FSGS</b>?",
                                      "a": "HIV infection, sickle cell disease, and heroin abuse."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Key light microscopy finding in <b>FSGS</b>?",
                                      "a": "Segmental sclerosis and hyalinosis in some, but not all, glomeruli."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Characteristic EM finding in <b>FSGS</b>?",
                                      "a": "Diffuse effacement of foot processes (similar to <b>MCD</b> but with structural damage)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "<b>Nephrotic syndrome</b> associated with HBV, HCV, and NSAIDs?",
                                      "a": "<b>Membranous Nephropathy</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Pathognomonic \"Spike and Dome\" appearance on silver stain?",
                                      "a": "<b>Membranous Nephropathy</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Immunofluorescence (IF) pattern for <b>Membranous Nephropathy</b>?",
                                      "a": "Granular IgG and C3 along the basement membrane."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Major autoantibody associated with primary <b>Membranous Nephropathy</b>?",
                                      "a": "Anti-phospholipase A2 receptor (PLA2R) antibody."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Defining clinical triad of <b>Nephritic Syndrome</b>?",
                                      "a": "Hypertension, hematuria (RBC casts), and mild-to-moderate proteinuria."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Classic presentation of <b>Poststreptococcal Glomerulonephritis</b> (<b>PSGN</b>)?",
                                      "a": "Tea-colored urine 2\u20133 weeks after a skin or throat infection."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "\"Lumpy-bumpy\" granular IF pattern in <b>PSGN</b>?",
                                      "a": "Subepithelial deposits of IgG, IgM, and C3."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Electron microscopy hallmark of <b>PSGN</b>?",
                                      "a": "Large subepithelial \"humps.\""
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cause of glomerulonephritis worldwide?",
                                      "a": "<b>IgA Nephropathy</b> (<b>Berger Disease</b>)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Clinical timing of <b>IgA Nephropathy</b> hematuria?",
                                      "a": "Occurs concurrently with or days after an upper respiratory infection."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Systemic vasculitis involving IgA deposits, purpura, and abdominal pain?",
                                      "a": "<b>Henoch-Sch\u00f6nlein Purpura</b> (<b>HSP</b>)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Histological finding in <b>Rapidly Progressive Glomerulonephritis</b> (<b>RPGN</b>)?",
                                      "a": "Crescents in Bowman\u2019s space consisting of fibrin and macrophages."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of <b>Goodpasture Syndrome</b>?",
                                      "a": "Type II hypersensitivity; antibodies against alpha-3 chain of Type IV collagen."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Linear IF pattern on glomerular basement membrane?",
                                      "a": "<b>Goodpasture Syndrome</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Nephritic condition presenting with hemoptysis and hematuria?",
                                      "a": "<b>Goodpasture Syndrome</b> (or <b>Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis</b>)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Differentiation of Wegener\u2019s (<b>GPA</b>) from Goodpasture?",
                                      "a": "<b>GPA</b> is c-ANCA positive and lacks linear GBM deposits (pauci-immune)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "\"Tram-track\" appearance on Light Microscopy?",
                                      "a": "<b>Membranoproliferative Glomerulonephritis</b> (<b>MPGN</b>)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Association for <b>MPGN</b> Type I?",
                                      "a": "Hepatitis B and C infections."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Pathophysiology of <b>MPGN</b> Type II (Dense Deposit Disease)?",
                                      "a": "C3 nephritic factor stabilizes C3 convertase, leading to persistent complement activation."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "<b>Alport Syndrome</b> inheritance and defect?",
                                      "a": "X-linked dominant; defect in Type IV collagen."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Clinical triad of <b>Alport Syndrome</b>?",
                                      "a": "Hereditary nephritis (hematuria), sensorineural deafness, and ocular disturbances."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "\"Basket-weave\" appearance on EM?",
                                      "a": "<b>Alport Syndrome</b>.\n\nII. Tubulointerstitial and Vascular Diseases"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cause of <b>Acute Kidney Injury</b> (<b>AKI</b>) in hospitalized patients?",
                                      "a": "<b>Acute Tubular Necrosis</b> (<b>ATN</b>)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Three phases of <b>Acute Tubular Necrosis</b>?",
                                      "a": "Prodromal (initiation), Oliguric (maintenance), and Polyuric (recovery)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Pathognomonic urinary cast in <b>ATN</b>?",
                                      "a": "Granular \"Muddy Brown\" casts."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Key lab finding in <b>ATN</b>: <b>FENA</b>?",
                                      "a": "<b>FENA</b> > 2% (inability to reabsorb sodium)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Nephrotoxic agents causing <b>ATN</b>?",
                                      "a": "Aminoglycosides, radiocontrast dye, lead, and ethylene glycol."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Specific site of tubular injury in ischemic <b>ATN</b>?",
                                      "a": "Straight segment of proximal tubule and thick ascending limb of Henle."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "\"Eosinophils in urine\" suggests which diagnosis?",
                                      "a": "<b>Acute Interstitial Nephritis</b> (<b>AIN</b>)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Common drug triggers for <b>Acute Interstitial Nephritis</b>?",
                                      "a": "Penicillins, NSAIDs, sulfonamides, and diuretics (The \"P\" drugs)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Classic triad of <b>Acute Interstitial Nephritis</b>?",
                                      "a": "Fever, rash, and eosinophilia."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "<b>Renal Papillary Necrosis</b> is commonly seen in which patients?",
                                      "a": "Sickle cell disease/trait, Diabetes Mellitus, Chronic Analgesic use, and Pyelonephritis."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Gross appearance of the kidney in Chronic Pyelonephritis?",
                                      "a": "Coarse, asymmetric corticomedullary scarring and blunted calyces."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "\"Thyroidization\" of the kidney on histology?",
                                      "a": "Chronic Pyelonephritis (tubules filled with eosinophilic casts resembling thyroid follicles)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Difference between Hyaline and Hyperplastic Arteriolosclerosis?",
                                      "a": "Hyaline is from chronic HTN/Diabetes; Hyperplastic (\"onion skin\") is from Malignant HTN."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Renal pathology associated with Diffuse Cortical Necrosis?",
                                      "a": "Obstetric catastrophes (e.g., abruptio placentae) and septic shock.\n\nIII. Cystic and Congenital Diseases"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Inheritance and age of onset for <b>ADPKD</b>?",
