                            {
                                      "q": "Defining feature of a Contusion?",
                                      "a": "Bruising caused by blunt force that ruptures small blood vessels without breaking the skin."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Distinguishing Suicidal vs. Homicidal cut-throat wounds?",
                                      "a": "Suicide often shows \"hesitation marks\"; Homicide often shows deep, singular, or jagged defense-related cuts."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of death in hanging?",
                                      "a": "Cerebral ischemia due to compression of carotid arteries (rather than airway obstruction)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Post-mortem finding in Hypothermia?",
                                      "a": "Wischnewski spots (multiple small hemorrhages in the gastric mucosa)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Diatom Test\"?",
                                      "a": "Detection of silica-shelled algae in bone marrow to support a diagnosis of drowning."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Forensic finding in Shaken Baby Syndrome?",
                                      "a": "Retinal hemorrhages, subdural hematoma, and diffuse axonal injury."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Characteristics of high-voltage electrical burns?",
                                      "a": "Small entry/exit points with massive deep tissue destruction."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Significance of \"Blow-back\" in forensics?",
                                      "a": "Blood and tissue found inside the barrel of a gun in contact shots."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Sequence of color changes in a bruise?",
                                      "a": "Red/Purple (Hb) -> Blue/Black -> Green (Biliverdin) -> Yellow/Brown (Bilirubin/Hemosiderin)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Timeframe for \"fixed\" <b>Livor Mortis</b>?",
                                      "a": "Usually after 8\u201312 hours; it no longer blanches with pressure."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary feature of Pulmonary Thromboembolism at autopsy?",
                                      "a": "Lines of Zahn (alternating layers of platelets/fibrin and RBCs) in the clot."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Distinguishing Premortem vs. Postmortem clots?",
                                      "a": "Premortem clots are friable and have Lines of Zahn; Postmortem clots are \"chicken fat\" (yellow) or \"currant jelly\" (red)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Common fracture site in motor vehicle accidents (pedestrian vs car)?",
                                      "a": "\"Bumper fracture\" (tibial/fibular fractures at the level of the car bumper)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary chemical change used for post-mortem interval (PMI) in the eye?",
                                      "a": "Increasing Potassium (K<sup>+</sup>) concentration in the vitreous humor."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Triage\" in forensic mass disasters?",
                                      "a": "Prioritizing victims based on the severity of injuries and likelihood of survival."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Finding in \"Coupe\" vs \"Contre-coupe\" brain injury?",
                                      "a": "Coupe is at the site of impact; Contre-coupe is on the opposite side (common in falls)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Forensic significance of a \"Dicing\" pattern?",
                                      "a": "Small, squared-off abrasions caused by shattered tempered side-glass in car accidents."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most reliable method for identifying a skeletonized body?",
                                      "a": "Dental records or DNA analysis."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "<b>Characteristics of Skeletal remains:</b> Female Pelvis?",
                                      "a": "Wider sub-pubic angle (> 90&deg;), wider sciatic notch, and oval-shaped pelvic inlet."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "<b>Characteristics of Skeletal remains:</b> Male Pelvis?",
                                      "a": "Narrow sub-pubic angle (< 90&deg;), heart-shaped pelvic inlet."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"<b>Exsanguination</b>\"?",
                                      "a": "Death due to extreme blood loss (usually > 40% of blood volume)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Key finding in Heat Stroke?",
                                      "a": "Body temperature > 40\u00b0C (104\u00b0F) and anhidrosis (lack of sweating)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Sign of a \"Graze Wound\"?",
                                      "a": "Bullet moves tangential to the skin, causing an elongated abrasion without entering the body."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Significance of \"Soot\" in the airway?",
                                      "a": "Indicates the person was alive and breathing during a fire (Antemortem burn)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common toxicological finding in forensic autopsies?",
                                      "a": "Ethanol (Alcohol)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Rule for calculating blood alcohol clearance?",
                                      "a": "Average rate is 15\u201320 mg/dL per hour."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cause of Pelvic Inflammatory Disease (PID)?",
                                      "a": "Chlamydia trachomatis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Fitz-Hugh-Curtis Syndrome?",
                                      "a": "Inflammation of the liver capsule (\"violin-string\" adhesions) following PID."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Classic histological finding in Syphilitic Chancre (Primary)?",
                                      "a": "Endarteritis obliterans with a plasma cell-rich infiltrate."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Identifying feature of Lymphogranuloma Venereum (LGV)?",
                                      "a": "Necrotizing granulomatous inflammation of inguinal lymph nodes (stellate abscesses)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Causative agent of \"Strawberry Cervix\"?",
                                      "a": "Trichomonas vaginalis."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cause of nipple discharge (bloody or serous)?",
                                      "a": "Intraductal papilloma."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Distinction between Intraductal Papilloma and Papillary Carcinoma?",
                                      "a": "Papilloma has a double layer of cells (epithelial and myoepithelial); Carcinoma lacks the myoepithelial layer."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Risk factor for Fat <b>Necrosis</b> of the breast?",
                                      "a": "Trauma (presents as a fixed, firm mass mimicking cancer)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Defining feature of Sclerosing Adenosis?",
                                      "a": "Increased acini and intralobular fibrosis, often associated with calcifications."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common benign tumor of the female breast?",
                                      "a": "Fibroadenoma."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Characteristic of Phyllodes Tumor?",
                                      "a": "\"Leaf-like\" projections on histology with increased stromal cellularity."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common location for breast cancer?",
                                      "a": "Upper outer quadrant."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Defining histological feature of Invasive Ductal Carcinoma (IDC)?",
                                      "a": "Nest and cords of tumor cells in a dense, desmoplastic stroma."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Hallmark of Invasive Lobular Carcinoma?",
                                      "a": "\"Single-file\" pattern of cells due to loss of E-cadherin."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Clinical presentation of Inflammatory Breast Cancer?",
                                      "a": "Peau d'orange (orange peel skin) due to lymphatic obstruction by tumor emboli."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Pathogenesis of Balanitis Xerotica Obliterans?",
                                      "a": "Lichen sclerosus of the glans penis; presents as white patches and can cause phimosis."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Peyronie Disease?",
                                      "a": "Fibrous plaque within the tunica albuginea leading to abnormal penile curvature."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Distinguishing feature of Bowen Disease (Penis)?",
                                      "a": "Carcinoma in situ presenting as a leukoplakic (white) plaque on the shaft."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Erythroplasia of Queyrat?",
                                      "a": "Carcinoma in situ presenting as a red plaque on the glans."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Tumor marker for <b>Choriocarcinoma</b> in males?",
                                      "a": "Beta-hCG (may cause gynecomastia due to LH-like activity)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cause of scrotal swelling in newborns?",
                                      "a": "Congenital <b>hydrocele</b> (incomplete closure of processus vaginalis)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Spermatocele?",
                                      "a": "Cystic dilation of the efferent ductules containing sperm."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Chain of Custody\"?",
                                      "a": "The chronological documentation of the seizure, control, and analysis of physical evidence."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is \"Post-mortem Redistribution\"?",
                                      "a": "The change in drug concentrations after death as drugs diffuse from organs into the blood."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of an \"Incised Wound\" vs. \"Stab Wound\"?",
                                      "a": "Incised is longer than it is deep; Stab is deeper than it is long."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is a \"D\u00e9fense Wound\" on the hands?",
                                      "a": "Cuts on the palmar surface or ulnar borders, indicating an attempt to grab a weapon or shield the body."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Appearance of a \"Star-shaped\" (Stellate) entry wound?",
                                      "a": "Contact GSW over a bony prominence (e.g., the skull) due to expanding gases."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Muzzle Imprint\"?",
                                      "a": "An abrasion mirroring the gun barrel's end, seen in hard-contact wounds."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Typical color of <b>Livor Mortis</b> in <b>Cyanide</b> poisoning?",
                                      "a": "Bright cherry-red or pink."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Typical color of <b>Livor Mortis</b> in Nitrite or Chlorate poisoning?",
                                      "a": "Chocolate brown (due to methemoglobinemia)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is \"Somatice Death\"?",
                                      "a": "The irreversible cessation of the functions of the brain, heart, and lungs."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is \"Molecular Death\"?",
                                      "a": "The death of individual tissues and cells, occurring hours after somatic death."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Kiernan\u2019s Sign\" (Forensics)?",
                                      "a": "Hemorrhage into the intermediate lobe of the pituitary in cases of hypothermia."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Difference between \"Primary\" and \"Secondary\" Blast Injuries?",
                                      "a": "Primary is due to the pressure wave (affects hollow organs); Secondary is due to flying debris/shrapnel."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is \"Tertiary\" Blast Injury?",
                                      "a": "Injury resulting from the victim being thrown by the blast wind against a hard surface."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Common autopsy finding in anaphylactic shock?",
                                      "a": "Laryngeal edema and mucus plugging of the bronchi."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Manner of Death: Undetermined\"?",
                                      "a": "Used when there is insufficient information or conflicting evidence to choose a specific manner."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Exhumation\"?",
                                      "a": "The legal disinterment of a body for re-examination or identification."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is \"Petrifaction\" in forensic entomology?",
                                      "a": "The use of insect life cycles (e.g., blowflies) to estimate the time since death."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Characteristic of \"Exit Wounds\" in GSW?",
                                      "a": "Irregular shape, absence of an abrasion ring, and no soot or stippling."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Diatom Test\" significance?",
                                      "a": "If diatoms are found in closed circulation (bone marrow/brain), it suggests the person drowned while alive."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Finding in \"Garrotting\"?",
                                      "a": "Form of strangulation using a cord or wire, often tightened with a stick."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Indicator of \"Manual Strangulation\"?",
                                      "a": "Fingernail abrasions on the neck and bruising of the deep neck muscles."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is the \"Boxer\u2019s Posture\" (Pugilistic Attitude)?",
                                      "a": "Flexion of limbs in fire victims due to heat-induced coagulation of muscle proteins."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Difference between \"Antemortem\" and \"Postmortem\" burns?",
                                      "a": "Antemortem burns show a red line of inflammation (vital reaction) and soot in the lungs."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Neglect\" in geriatric/pediatric forensics?",
                                      "a": "Failure of a caregiver to provide necessary food, clothing, shelter, or medical care."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is a \"Choke\" in a shotgun?",
                                      "a": "The partial constriction of the bore at the muzzle to control the spread of the shot."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Significance of \"Wadding\" in shotgun wounds?",
                                      "a": "It can enter the wound at close range and help determine the distance of fire."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"<b>Sudden Infant Death Syndrome</b>\" (SIDS)?",
                                      "a": "Sudden death of an infant < 1 year that remains unexplained after a thorough investigation/autopsy."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Common finding in \"Air Embolism\"?",
                                      "a": "Frothy blood in the right ventricle; usually diagnosed by opening the heart under water."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary finding in \"Fat Embolism Syndrome\"?",
                                      "a": "Petechial rash, respiratory distress, and CNS dysfunction after a long-bone fracture."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Asphyxia\"?",
                                      "a": "Interference with the uptake or utilization of oxygen."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Forensic significance of the \"Hyoid Bone\"?",
                                      "a": "Fractures are highly suggestive of manual strangulation in adults."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Timeframe for \"Gastric Emptying\" in forensics?",
                                      "a": "Light meals usually leave the stomach in 2 hours; heavy meals in 4-6 hours (highly variable)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is \"Tache Noire\"?",
                                      "a": "A reddish-brown horizontal band on the eyeball when the eyes remain open after death."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Difference between \"Self-inflicted\" and \"Homicidal\" stab wounds?",
                                      "a": "Self-inflicted are often clustered in a reachable area (e.g., left chest) and may have hesitation marks."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is \"Simulated Suicide\"?",
                                      "a": "A homicide made to look like a suicide by the perpetrator (e.g., moving the body or weapon)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Indicators of \"High-Velocity\" rifle wounds?",
                                      "a": "Massive tissue destruction and \"explosive\" exit wounds due to cavitation."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is \"Casper\u2019s Dictum\"?",
                                      "a": "A body decomposes in air twice as fast as in water and eight times as fast as in earth."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Define \"Negative Autopsy\"?",
                                      "a": "An autopsy where no gross or microscopic cause of death can be found (e.g., some arrhythmias)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Medicolegal Autopsy\"?",
                                      "a": "An autopsy performed under the authority of the state to determine cause and manner of death."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Common finding in \"Drowning in Salt Water\"?",
                                      "a": "Pulmonary edema and hemoconcentration (due to osmotic shift of fluid into lungs)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Common finding in \"Drowning in Fresh Water\"?",
                                      "a": "Hemodilution, hemolysis, and potential hyperkalemia."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Whiplash Injury\"?",
                                      "a": "Cervical strain/sprain caused by sudden hyperextension followed by hyperflexion."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Key feature of \"Scald Burns\"?",
                                      "a": "Caused by hot liquids; characterized by \"tidemark\" patterns and absence of singed hair."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is \"Pressure Pallor\"?",
                                      "a": "Areas of the skin that remain pale because they were compressed against a surface during <b>livor mortis</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Significance of \"Toxicology Screening\" in Forensic Pathology?",
                                      "a": "To identify poisons or drugs that may have caused or contributed to death."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Bolus Death\"?",
                                      "a": "Choking on a large piece of food, leading to sudden airway obstruction and vagal inhibition."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Forensic finding in \"Electrocution\"?",
                                      "a": "Joule burn (entry mark) showing a crater with raised, pale edges."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is \"Post-mortem Lividity\" (another name for)?",
                                      "a": "<b>Livor mortis</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common location for \"Defense Wounds\"?",
                                      "a": "Palmar surface of hands and outer aspect of forearms."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Defining feature of \"Slash\" wounds?",
                                      "a": "Sharp force injury where length is greater than depth."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is \"Smudging\" in GSW?",
                                      "a": "The deposit of soot around the entry wound, typical of close-range fire."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is \"Stippling\" (Tattooing)?",
                                      "a": "Small punctate abrasions from unburned powder, typical of intermediate-range GSW."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Range for \"Intermediate-range\" GSW stippling?",
                                      "a": "Usually between 1 centimeter to 1 meter (approx. 3 feet)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Indicator of \"Hard Contact\" GSW?",
                                      "a": "Muzzle imprint and \"blowback\" of tissue into the barrel."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Laceration\" vs \"Incision\"?",
                                      "a": "Lacerations are blunt force (ragged edges, tissue bridges); Incisions are sharp force (clean edges, no bridges)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Abraded Skin\"?",
                                      "a": "Superficial injury where the epidermis is removed by friction."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Ecchymosis\"?",
                                      "a": "A medical term for a bruise or contusion."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cause of death in \"House Fires\"?",
                                      "a": "<b>Carbon monoxide</b> poisoning/smoke inhalation (not thermal burns)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Percentage of COHb usually fatal in a healthy adult?",
                                      "a": "Greater than 50-60%."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Characteristics of \"Exit Wounds\" vs \"Entry Wounds\"?",
                                      "a": "Entry has an abrasion collar; Exit is usually larger and more irregular without a collar."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of a Brenner Tumor?",
                                      "a": "An ovarian surface epithelial tumor containing bladder-like transitional epithelium."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Histological finding in a Sertoli-Leydig cell tumor?",
                                      "a": "Reinke crystals (pink, rod-shaped cytoplasmic inclusions)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common location for an Ectopic Pregnancy?",
                                      "a": "The ampulla of the fallopian tube."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Define \"Decidualization\" of the stroma?",
                                      "a": "Transformation of endometrial stroma into plump, secretory cells under the influence of progesterone."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is \"Arias-Stella Reaction\"?",
                                      "a": "Endometrial changes (hyperchromatic, enlarged nuclei) associated with pregnancy or ectopic pregnancy."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Placenta Accreta?",
                                      "a": "Improper implantation of the placenta directly onto the myometrium without intervening decidua."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Placenta Percreta?",
                                      "a": "Placental invasion through the entire myometrium and into the serosa or adjacent organs (e.g., bladder)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cause of Postpartum Hemorrhage?",
                                      "a": "Uterine atony."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Characteristic of HELLP Syndrome?",
                                      "a": "Hemolysis, Elevated Liver enzymes, and Low Platelets (a severe form of Preeclampsia)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Vulvar Lichen Sclerosus?",
                                      "a": "Thinning of the epidermis and \"parchment-like\" vulvar skin with a slight risk of Squamous Cell Carcinoma."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Lichen Simplex Chronicus?",
                                      "a": "<b>Hyperplasia</b> of vulvar squamous epithelium due to chronic scratching; no increased risk of cancer."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Pathogenesis of \"Chocolate Cysts\"?",
                                      "a": "Blood-filled cysts in the ovary caused by repeated cyclic hemorrhage of ectopic endometrial tissue."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Typical presentation of <b>Sarcoma Botryoides</b>?",
                                      "a": "A \"bunch of grapes\" mass protruding from the vagina of a child."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common benign tumor of the male breast?",
                                      "a": "Gynecomastia."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary risk factor for Male Breast Cancer?",
                                      "a": "BRCA2 mutation and Klinefelter Syndrome."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of <b>Extramammary Paget Disease</b>?",
                                      "a": "Malignant cells in the vulvar epidermis (PAS+, Keratin+) that do not usually indicate an underlying cancer."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cause of Granulomatous <b>Orchitis</b>?",
                                      "a": "Tuberculosis (caseating) or idiopathic (non-caseating)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Classic hormone finding in <b>Choriocarcinoma</b>?",
                                      "a": "Extremely high Beta-hCG levels."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Identifying feature of Endodermal Sinus (Yolk Sac) tumor in males?",
                                      "a": "Schiller-Duval bodies and elevated Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Paratesticular Rhabdomyosarcoma?",
                                      "a": "A highly malignant tumor of the spermatic cord, typically occurring in children."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is \"Paradoxical Undressing\"?",
                                      "a": "A phenomenon in hypothermia where the victim removes clothes due to a false sensation of burning heat."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Forensic significance of \"Post-mortem Teeth Pinkness\"?",
                                      "a": "Often associated with <b>carbon monoxide</b> poisoning, drowning, or hypothermia."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Excited Delirium Syndrome\"?",
                                      "a": "A state of extreme agitation, hyperthermia, and superhuman strength, often leading to sudden death during restraint."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Finding in \"Air Embolism\" at autopsy?",
                                      "a": "Air bubbles in the coronary arteries or frothy blood in the right heart."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What are \"Lines of Zahn\"?",
                                      "a": "Laminated patterns in a thrombus formed by alternating layers of platelets/fibrin and RBCs."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Agonal Thrombus\"?",
                                      "a": "A thrombus formed in the heart during the process of dying."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is \"Embalming Artifact\"?",
                                      "a": "Changes to the body (e.g., tissue firmness, chemical odors) that interfere with post-mortem toxicology and histology."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Significance of \"Vitreous Glucose\" post-mortem?",
                                      "a": "High levels suggest poorly controlled diabetes or diabetic ketoacidosis before death."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Typical finding in Ethylene Glycol poisoning?",
                                      "a": "Calcium oxalate crystals in the renal tubules."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is \"Methanol\" poisoning hallmark?",
                                      "a": "Putaminal <b>necrosis</b> and retinal damage (formic acid toxicity)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Forensic finding in Chronic Lead Poisoning?",
                                      "a": "Lead lines on gums (Burton lines) and \"lead lines\" on epiphyses of long bones in X-rays."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Pathergy\" in forensics?",
                                      "a": "An exaggerated skin response to minor trauma, occasionally seen in specific systemic diseases."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is \"Kiernan\u2019s Sign\"?",
                                      "a": "Hemorrhages in the anterior pituitary stalk, often seen in hanging."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Characteristic of \"Shotgun\" entrance at close range?",
                                      "a": "A single large hole (\"cookie cutter\" appearance) with charred edges."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is \"Rat-hole\" entrance?",
                                      "a": "An irregular shotgun entry wound where the individual pellets begin to diverge (intermediate range)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Significance of the \"Pelvis\" in age determination?",
                                      "a": "Changes in the pubic symphyseal surface are the most reliable skeletal indicators of age in adults."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"<b>Livor Mortis</b>\" fixation?",
                                      "a": "Occurs when blood becomes trapped in the capillaries and no longer shifts with body movement."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Finding in \"Ligature Strangulation\"?",
                                      "a": "A horizontal mark on the neck, usually below the larynx, often encircling the entire neck."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Distinction between \"Hanging\" and \"Ligature Strangulation\" marks?",
                                      "a": "Hanging marks are usually inverted \"V\" shapes and incomplete; Ligature marks are horizontal and continuous."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is \"Post-mortem Caloric Production\"?",
                                      "a": "A temporary rise in body temperature after death due to continued metabolic activity or infection."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Individualization\" in forensics?",
                                      "a": "The process of proving that a sample came from a unique source (e.g., fingerprints, DNA)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Finding in \"Aspiration of Gastric Contents\"?",
                                      "a": "Food particles and acidic fluid found in the terminal bronchioles."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Indicator of \"Premortem\" Bruising?",
                                      "a": "Microscopic evidence of inflammatory cells (neutrophils) and fibrin."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Vagal Inhibition\" death?",
                                      "a": "Sudden cardiac arrest caused by stimulation of the vagus nerve (e.g., pressure on the neck/carotid sinus)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is \"Tardieu\u2019s Spots\"?",
                                      "a": "Small petechial hemorrhages beneath the pleura or epicardium in asphyxial deaths."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of \"Stab\" wound death?",
                                      "a": "Internal hemorrhage leading to hypovolemic shock."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Manner of Death: Natural\"?",
                                      "a": "Death caused exclusively by disease or the aging process."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Manner of Death: Accidental\"?",
                                      "a": "Death resulting from an unintentional action or event (e.g., a fall)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Manner of Death: Homicide\"?",
                                      "a": "Death resulting from a volitional act committed by another person."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is \"<b>Cadaveric Spasm</b>\"?",
                                      "a": "Immediate muscle stiffening at death, usually occurring during intense physical or emotional stress."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of <b>Meigs Syndrome</b>?",
                                      "a": "Triad of ovarian fibroma, ascites, and hydrothorax (pleural effusion)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common location for an accessory breast (polymastia)?",
                                      "a": "Along the \"milk line\" (from the axilla to the vulva)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Pathogenesis of Fat <b>Necrosis</b> of the breast?",
                                      "a": "Trauma or surgery leads to <b>saponification</b> of fat and a localized inflammatory response."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Comedocarcinoma?",
                                      "a": "A subtype of DCIS characterized by high-grade cells and central \"cheese-like\" <b>necrosis</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cause of Vaginitis with a \"fishy\" odor?",
                                      "a": "Bacterial vaginosis (Gardnerella vaginalis)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Defining characteristic of \"Clue Cells\"?",
                                      "a": "Vaginal epithelial cells covered in Gardnerella bacteria, giving them a fuzzy appearance."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Brenner Tumor?",
                                      "a": "A surface epithelial tumor of the ovary containing transitional (urothelial) cells."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What are Reinke Crystals?",
                                      "a": "Eosinophilic, rod-shaped inclusions found in Leydig cell tumors."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary difference between Partial and Complete Hydatidiform Moles?",
                                      "a": "Partial moles contain fetal parts and result from 2 sperm fertilizing 1 egg (69,XXX/XXY)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Histological finding in <b>Choriocarcinoma</b>?",
                                      "a": "Proliferation of cytotrophoblasts and syncytiotrophoblasts without chorionic villi."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Endometrial Polyp?",
                                      "a": "Benign growth of endometrial glands and stroma, often associated with Tamoxifen use."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common subtype of Cervical Cancer?",
                                      "a": "Squamous cell carcinoma (80%)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of Koilocytic change?",
                                      "a": "HPV infection causes nuclear enlargement, hyperchromasia, and a clear perinuclear halo."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Spermatocele?",
                                      "a": "A painless, sperm-containing cyst of the epididymis."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cause of Acute Prostatitis in men < 35?",
                                      "a": "Chlamydia trachomatis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Lichtenberg Figures\"?",
                                      "a": "Fern-like skin patterns caused by a lightning strike (not a true burn)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is a \"Dicing\" injury?",
                                      "a": "Small, L-shaped or square abrasions from tempered glass fragments in motor vehicle accidents."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Brush Burn\"?",
                                      "a": "A large, friction-induced abrasion caused by a body sliding across a rough surface."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Difference between \"Primary\" and \"Secondary\" impact in pedestrian accidents?",
                                      "a": "Primary is the initial hit by the car; Secondary is when the pedestrian hits the ground."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Significance of \"Pink Teeth\" in forensics?",
                                      "a": "Post-mortem artifact often seen in drowning or <b>carbon monoxide</b> poisoning due to hemoglobin breakdown."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Somatice Death\"?",
                                      "a": "The irreversible cessation of heart, lung, and brain function."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is \"Molecular (Cellular) Death\"?",
                                      "a": "The death of individual tissues and cells, occurring hours after somatic death."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Adipocere\" (Grave Wax)?",
                                      "a": "A waxy, soap-like substance formed by the anaerobic hydrolysis of body fat in moist environments."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Forensic finding in \"Chronic Arsenic Poisoning\"?",
                                      "a": "Mees\u2019 lines (white transverse lines) on the fingernails and \"raindrop\" skin pigmentation."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Burking\"?",
                                      "a": "A method of homicidal asphyxia involving sitting on the chest while covering the nose and mouth."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is the \"Pugilistic Attitude\"?",
                                      "a": "A defensive-looking posture in fire victims caused by heat-induced shrinkage of muscle fibers."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Finding in \"<b>Carbon Monoxide</b>\" poisoning (Blood)?",
                                      "a": "Carboxyhemoglobin levels > 50% are typically lethal."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Post-mortem Redistribution\"?",
                                      "a": "Movement of drugs from tissues into the blood after death, which can skew toxicology results."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common site of \"Hyoid Bone\" fracture?",
                                      "a": "The greater horns, typically occurring in manual strangulation."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Whiplash\" injury?",
                                      "a": "Acceleration-deceleration injury of the cervical spine."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Difference between \"True\" and \"False\" Aneurysm in trauma?",
                                      "a": "True involves all three layers of the vessel wall; False (pseudoaneurysm) is a hematoma contained by adventitia."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Tache Noire\"?",
                                      "a": "Dark, horizontal bands across the eyes when they remain open after death."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Significance of \"Vitreous Potassium\"?",
                                      "a": "Levels increase at a predictable rate after death, used to estimate the post-mortem interval."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is a \"Joule Burn\"?",
                                      "a": "An electrical entry wound characterized by a central crater and firm, pale edges."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"<b>Sudden Infant Death Syndrome</b>\" (SIDS)?",
                                      "a": "Sudden death of an infant under 1 year that remains unexplained after full autopsy and scene investigation."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Characteristic of \"Hard Contact\" GSW?",
                                      "a": "Muzzle imprint and searing of the skin edges."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Exhumation\"?",
                                      "a": "The legal disinterment of a body for examination or evidence collection."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Finding in \"<b>Cyanide</b>\" poisoning (Internal)?",
                                      "a": "Bright red blood and organs with a bitter almond odor."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Manner of Death\"?",
                                      "a": "The legal classification of death (Natural, Accident, Suicide, Homicide, or Undetermined)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Cause of Death\"?",
                                      "a": "The specific injury or disease that leads to physiological collapse (e.g., Gunshot wound to the head)"
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Fat <b>Necrosis</b> of the breast?",
                                      "a": "A benign, firm mass caused by trauma or surgery, showing necrotic adipocytes and lipid-laden macrophages."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Characteristics of Sclerosing Adenosis?",
                                      "a": "Increased number of acini and intralobular fibrosis, often associated with calcifications."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common benign tumor of the female breast?",
                                      "a": "Fibroadenoma."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Typical behavior of a Fibroadenoma?",
                                      "a": "Estrogen-sensitive; grows during pregnancy and shrinks after menopause."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Defining feature of a Phyllodes Tumor?",
                                      "a": "Large, \"leaf-like\" stromal projections with higher cellularity than a fibroadenoma."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Intraductal Papilloma?",
                                      "a": "A benign growth in a large duct, presenting with serous or bloody nipple discharge in premenopausal women."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Pathological distinction of Ductal Carcinoma In Situ (DCIS)?",
                                      "a": "Malignant proliferation of cells in ducts with no invasion of the basement membrane."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Paget Disease of the Breast?",
                                      "a": "DCIS that extends up the lactiferous ducts to the skin of the nipple, causing a crusty, eczematous lesion."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common location for breast cancer?",
                                      "a": "Upper outer quadrant."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Defining feature of Invasive Lobular Carcinoma?",
                                      "a": "\"Single-file\" pattern of cells due to the loss of E-cadherin expression."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Hormone receptor status often seen in Medullary Carcinoma of the breast?",
                                      "a": "Often \"Triple Negative\" (ER-, PR-, and HER2-)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cause of spontaneous abortion?",
                                      "a": "Fetal chromosomal abnormalities (especially Trisomy 16)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Placenta Previa?",
                                      "a": "Implantation of the placenta over the internal cervical os, presenting as painless third-trimester bleeding."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Abruptio Placentae?",
                                      "a": "Premature separation of the placenta from the uterine wall, presenting as painful third-trimester bleeding."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Classic \"triad\" of Preeclampsia?",
                                      "a": "Hypertension, proteinuria, and edema (though edema is no longer a strict diagnostic criterion)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Paradoxical Undressing\"?",
                                      "a": "A phenomenon in hypothermia where a victim removes clothes due to a false sensation of heat from peripheral vasodilation."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Forensic indicator of Hypothermia?",
                                      "a": "Wischnewski spots (multiple small, dark gastric mucosal hemorrhages)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Defining feature of \"Dry Drowning\"?",
                                      "a": "Death from asphyxia due to intense laryngospasm without significant water aspiration."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Significance of \"Diatoms\" in drowning?",
                                      "a": "Finding silica-shelled algae in closed organs (like bone marrow) suggests the victim was alive during submersion."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common finding in \"Choking on Food\" (Bolus Death)?",
                                      "a": "Sudden cardiac arrest due to vagal inhibition from laryngeal stimulation."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Excited Delirium Syndrome\"?",
                                      "a": "State of extreme agitation, hyperthermia, and superhuman strength, often leading to sudden death during restraint."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Characteristic of \"Lichtenberg Figures\"?",
                                      "a": "Transient, fern-like skin patterns caused by a lightning strike."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Finding in \"<b>Carbon Monoxide</b>\" poisoning?",
                                      "a": "Cherry-red discoloration of the skin, muscles, and blood."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most reliable method for identifying a skeletonized body?",
                                      "a": "Comparison of dental records or DNA profiling."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Antemortem\" injury?",
                                      "a": "An injury sustained before death, characterized by vital reactions like inflammation, bruising, or bleeding."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Postmortem\" injury?",
                                      "a": "An injury sustained after death, typically lacking a vital inflammatory response."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Identifying feature of \"Exit Wounds\" in GSW?",
                                      "a": "Larger, irregular shape, and absence of soot, stippling, or an abrasion collar."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Finding in \"Manual Strangulation\"?",
                                      "a": "Petechial hemorrhages in the eyes, bruising of neck muscles, and potential fracture of the hyoid bone."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Manner of Death: Undetermined\"?",
                                      "a": "A classification used when there is insufficient information or evidence to assign a specific manner (e.g., suicide vs. accident)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is the \"Rule of Nines\"?",
                                      "a": "A clinical tool used in burn forensics to estimate the total body surface area affected."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Typical finding in \"High-Voltage\" electrical injury?",
                                      "a": "Extensive deep-tissue destruction and potential \"blow-out\" wounds at exit points."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Sign of \"Smoke Inhalation\" at autopsy?",
                                      "a": "Soot in the larynx, trachea, and bronchi, indicating the person was alive during the fire."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Ligature Mark\"?",
                                      "a": "A furrow in the neck produced by a cord or wire, typically horizontal in strangulation."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Difference between \"Primary\" and \"Secondary\" blast injuries?",
                                      "a": "Primary is due to the pressure wave (hollow organs); Secondary is due to shrapnel/debris."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"<b>Mummification</b>\"?",
                                      "a": "Drying and shriveling of a body in hot, dry, and airy conditions."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common location for an Ectopic Pregnancy?",
                                      "a": "The Ampulla of the Fallopian Tube."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of the \"Arias-Stella Reaction\"?",
                                      "a": "Benign endometrial nuclear hyperchromasia and pleomorphism associated with pregnancy."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is a Brenner Tumor of the ovary?",
                                      "a": "A rare surface epithelial tumor containing transitional epithelium (urothelium)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Defining feature of a <b>Struma Ovarii</b>?",
                                      "a": "A specialized teratoma composed entirely of mature thyroid tissue."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is the most common germ cell tumor in children under 3?",
                                      "a": "Yolk Sac Tumor (Endodermal Sinus Tumor)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Diagnostic histological feature of <b>Yolk Sac Tumors</b>?",
                                      "a": "Schiller-Duval bodies (glomerulus-like structures)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common testicular tumor in men over age 60?",
                                      "a": "Diffuse Large B-cell Lymphoma (usually metastatic)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary difference between a <b>Seminoma</b> and a <b>Dysgerminoma</b>?",
                                      "a": "<b>Seminoma</b> occurs in the testes; <b>Dysgerminoma</b> occurs in the ovary (histologically identical)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What are Reinke Crystals?",
                                      "a": "Rod-shaped cytoplasmic inclusions found in Leydig cell tumors."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Call-Exner Bodies?",
                                      "a": "Small, gland-like follicles filled with eosinophilic material seen in <b>Granulosa Cell Tumors</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Hormone produced by <b>Granulosa Cell Tumors</b>?",
                                      "a": "Estrogen (often presents with precocious puberty or postmenopausal bleeding)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Bowenoid Papulosis?",
                                      "a": "Multiple reddish-brown papules on the penis caused by HPV 16; histologically CIS but rarely progresses."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Difference between Phimosis and Paraphimosis?",
                                      "a": "Phimosis is inability to retract the foreskin; Paraphimosis is a retracted foreskin that cannot be returned, causing ischemia."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Common cause of \"Non-gonococcal Urethritis\"?",
                                      "a": "Chlamydia trachomatis (serotypes D-K)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Post-mortem Redistribution\"?",
                                      "a": "Changes in drug concentration after death due to diffusion from tissue reservoirs into the blood."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Forensic significance of Vitreous Humor?",
                                      "a": "It is an isolated fluid used to test for electrolytes, glucose, and alcohol post-mortem."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Finding in the stomach indicating Insulin Overdose?",
                                      "a": "Usually none; diagnosis requires testing for elevated insulin/C-peptide ratios at the injection site."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Appearance of \"Lichtenberg Figures\"?",
                                      "a": "Arborescent (fern-like) skin patterns pathognomonic for lightning strikes."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Difference between \"Primary\" and \"Secondary\" blast injury?",
                                      "a": "Primary is due to the blast wave (pressure); Secondary is due to flying shrapnel."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is a \"Tertiary\" blast injury?",
                                      "a": "Injury caused by the body being physically thrown against an object by the blast wind."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Somatice Death\"?",
                                      "a": "The irreversible cessation of the functions of the brain, heart, and lungs."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Molecular Death\"?",
                                      "a": "The death of individual tissues and cells occurring at different rates after somatic death."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Significance of \"Marbling\" in decomposition?",
                                      "a": "Hemolysis of blood in vessels reacting with hydrogen sulfide, creating a visible venous network."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is \"Adipocere\"?",
                                      "a": "A waxy substance (grave wax) formed by the anaerobic hydrolysis of fat in wet environments."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Defining feature of a \"Stellate\" GSW entry?",
                                      "a": "A star-shaped wound caused by gas expansion under the skin in a contact shot over bone."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is \"Sooting\" (Smudging)?",
                                      "a": "Carbon deposits on the skin indicating a near-contact or close-range gunshot wound."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Tattooing\" (Stippling)?",
                                      "a": "Small punctate abrasions caused by unburnt gunpowder hitting the skin in intermediate-range GSWs."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Common autopsy finding in chronic Lead poisoning?",
                                      "a": "\"Lead lines\" (Burton lines) on the gums and basophilic stippling of RBCs."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Post-mortem finding in Ethylene Glycol poisoning?",
                                      "a": "Calcium oxalate crystals in the renal tubules (viewed under polarized light)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Forensic finding in <b>Cyanide</b> poisoning?",
                                      "a": "Bright red \"cherry\" lividity and a bitter almond odor from the cavities."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Significance of \"Hyoid Bone\" fracture?",
                                      "a": "A strong indicator of manual strangulation in adults."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Tache Noire\"?",
                                      "a": "A brown, horizontal band across the sclera occurring when the eyes remain open after death."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is \"<b>Cadaveric Spasm</b>\"?",
                                      "a": "Immediate onset of <b>rigor mortis</b> at the moment of death, usually during violent struggle."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Manner of Death\"?",
                                      "a": "The legal category of death: Natural, Accident, Suicide, Homicide, or Undetermined."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Placenta Accreta?",
                                      "a": "Placenta attaches directly to the myometrium without penetrating it; often due to a prior C-section scar."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Placenta Increta?",
                                      "a": "Placental villi invade into the uterine myometrium."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Placenta Percreta?",
                                      "a": "Placental villi invade through the entire myometrium and may attach to the bladder or rectum."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Risk factor for Placental Abruption?",
                                      "a": "Maternal hypertension, smoking, and cocaine use."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Classic presentation of Vasa Previa?",
                                      "a": "Painless vaginal bleeding and fetal bradycardia following the rupture of membranes."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of Velamentous Cord Insertion?",
                                      "a": "The umbilical cord inserts into the fetal membranes rather than the placenta itself."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cause of Gestational <b>Choriocarcinoma</b>?",
                                      "a": "Preceding <b>Hydatidiform Mole</b> (50% of cases)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Hallmark finding of Preeclampsia on renal biopsy?",
                                      "a": "Glomerular endotheliosis (swelling of endothelial cells)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Distinction between Preeclampsia and Eclampsia?",
                                      "a": "Eclampsia includes the onset of new-onset grand mal seizures."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Hydrops Fetalis\"?",
                                      "a": "Accumulation of fluid (edema) in at least two fetal compartments, often due to severe Rh incompatibility."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Shaken Baby Syndrome\"?",
                                      "a": "A form of abusive head trauma characterized by the triad of subdural hematoma, retinal hemorrhages, and encephalopathy."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Forensic finding in \"Immersion Burns\" (Abuse)?",
                                      "a": "\"Stocking and glove\" pattern with a sharp line of demarcation and no splash marks."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Significance of a \"Spiral Fracture\" in a non-ambulatory infant?",
                                      "a": "Highly suggestive of non-accidental trauma (physical abuse)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"SIDS\" (<b>Sudden Infant Death Syndrome</b>)?",
                                      "a": "Sudden death of an infant < 1 year that remains unexplained after a thorough scene investigation and autopsy."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is \"Munchausen Syndrome by Proxy\"?",
                                      "a": "A form of child abuse where a caregiver feigns or induces illness in a child for attention."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Post-mortem Interval\" (PMI)?",
                                      "a": "The estimated time that has elapsed since a person has died."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most accurate skeletal indicator for determining biological sex?",
                                      "a": "The pelvis (specifically the sub-pubic angle and sciatic notch)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Negligent Homicide\"?",
                                      "a": "A death resulting from criminal negligence rather than a deliberate intent to kill."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is a \"Dying Declaration\"?",
                                      "a": "A statement made by a person who believes they are about to die, often admissible as evidence in court."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Medical Examiner\" vs. \"Coroner\"?",
                                      "a": "A Medical Examiner is typically a forensic pathologist; a Coroner is an elected official who may not have medical training."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is \"Trace Evidence\"?",
                                      "a": "Microscopic materials (hair, fibers, soil) transferred during a crime."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Locard\u2019s Exchange Principle\"?",
                                      "a": "The concept that every contact leaves a trace."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Forensic finding in \"Air Embolism\"?",
                                      "a": "Presence of air in the right ventricle of the heart, often diagnosed by opening the heart under water."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"<b>Exsanguination</b>\"?",
                                      "a": "Death caused by the loss of blood to the point where life cannot be sustained."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cause of \"Sudden Natural Death\" in adults?",
                                      "a": "Ischemic heart disease (Coronary Artery Disease)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is a \"Hesitation Wound\"?",
                                      "a": "Superficial sharp-force injuries (incisions) near a fatal wound, indicative of suicide."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"<b>Livor Mortis</b>\" Blanching?",
                                      "a": "Pressure on the skin that turns the purple area white; indicates that the <b>livor mortis</b> is not yet \"fixed.\""
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Difference between \"Primary\" and \"Secondary\" Flaccidity?",
                                      "a": "Primary occurs immediately after death; Secondary occurs after <b>Rigor Mortis</b> has passed due to decomposition."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"<b>Saponification</b>\"?",
                                      "a": "The process of turning body fat into adipocere."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Defining feature of a \"Near-Contact\" Gunshot Wound?",
                                      "a": "Presence of soot on the skin but no muzzle imprint or stellate tearing."
                            }
                  ]
        },
        {
                  "topic": "Immunology",
                  "icon": "shield-virus",
                  "cards": [
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary lymphoid organs?",
                                      "a": "<b>Bone marrow</b> and <b>Thymus</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Cell type that links innate and adaptive immunity?",
                                      "a": "<b>Dendritic cells</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Toll-like receptor (TLR) that recognizes Lipopolysaccharide (LPS)?",
                                      "a": "TLR4."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Transcription factor activated by TLR signaling to produce cytokines?",
                                      "a": "NF-&kappa;B."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of <b>Neutrophil Extracellular Traps</b> (NETs)?",
                                      "a": "Release of chromatin and antimicrobial proteins following cell death (NETosis)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Component of the MAC (<b>Membrane Attack Complex</b>)?",
                                      "a": "C5b, C6, C7, C8, C9."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most potent anaphylatoxins in the complement cascade?",
                                      "a": "C3a, C4a, and C5a."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary opsonins in the complement system?",
                                      "a": "C3b and IgG."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Function of <b>Decay-Accelerating Factor</b> (CD55)?",
                                      "a": "Prevents formation of C3 convertase on host cells."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Deficiency associated with <b>Paroxysmal Nocturnal Hemoglobinuria</b> (PNH)?",
                                      "a": "CD55 and CD59 (MIRL) deficiency."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Result of C1 esterase inhibitor deficiency?",
                                      "a": "<b>Hereditary Angioedema</b> (due to elevated Bradykinin)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Classic trigger for the Lectin pathway?",
                                      "a": "Mannose-binding lectin (MBL) binding to microbial carbohydrates."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "<b>MHC Class I</b> structure?",
                                      "a": "One heavy chain and one &beta;<sub>2</sub>-microglobulin."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "<b>MHC Class II</b> structure?",
                                      "a": "One &alpha; chain and one &beta; chain."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Cellular distribution of <b>MHC Class I</b>?",
                                      "a": "All nucleated cells and platelets (not RBCs)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Origin of peptides loaded onto <b>MHC Class I</b>?",
                                      "a": "Endogenous (cytosolic) proteins processed by proteasomes."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Origin of peptides loaded onto <b>MHC Class II</b>?",
                                      "a": "Exogenous (extracellular) proteins processed in acidified endosomes."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Function of the <b>Invariant Chain</b> (Ii) in MHC II?",
                                      "a": "Prevents premature binding of endogenous peptides to the MHC II cleft."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "<b>HLA association:</b> B27?",
                                      "a": "Seronegative Spondyloarthropathies (e.g., <b>Ankylosing Spondylitis</b>)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "<b>HLA association:</b> DR3 and DR4?",
                                      "a": "Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "<b>HLA association:</b> DR2?",
                                      "a": "<b>Multiple Sclerosis</b>, Hay Fever, <b>SLE</b>, <b>Goodpasture syndrome</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "<b>HLA association:</b> DQ2/DQ8?",
                                      "a": "<b>Celiac Disease</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Location of T-cell positive selection?",
                                      "a": "Thymic cortex."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Criterion for T-cell positive selection?",
                                      "a": "Ability to bind self-MHC."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Location of T-cell negative selection?",
                                      "a": "Thymic medulla."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Function of the AIRE protein?",
                                      "a": "Expression of self-antigens in the <b>thymus</b> for negative selection."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Result of AIRE deficiency?",
                                      "a": "<b>Autoimmune Polyendocrine Syndrome Type 1</b> (APS-1)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Signal 1 for T-cell activation?",
                                      "a": "MHC-peptide complex binding to TCR."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Signal 2 (Costimulation) for T-cell activation?",
                                      "a": "B7 (CD80/86) on APC binding to CD28 on T-cell."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Cytokine that drives Th1 differentiation?",
                                      "a": "IL-12 and IFN-&gamma;."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Cytokine that drives Th2 differentiation?",
                                      "a": "IL-4."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Cytokine that drives Th17 differentiation?",
                                      "a": "TGF-&beta; and IL-6."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary function of Th1 cells?",
                                      "a": "Activate macrophages and cytotoxic T-cells."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary function of Th2 cells?",
                                      "a": "Activate B-cells and eosinophils (<b>Type I hypersensitivity</b>)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Characteristic markers of <b>Regulatory T-cells</b> (Tregs)?",
                                      "a": "CD4, CD25, and FOXP3."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Cytokines secreted by Tregs to suppress immune response?",
                                      "a": "IL-10 and TGF-&beta;."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Location of B-cell <b>VDJ recombination</b>?",
                                      "a": "<b>Bone marrow</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Enzyme responsible for <b>VDJ recombination</b>?",
                                      "a": "RAG-1 and RAG-2."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Signal 1 for B-cell activation?",
                                      "a": "Antigen binding to surface IgM/IgD."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Signal 2 for B-cell activation (T-cell dependent)?",
                                      "a": "CD40 on B-cell binding to CD40L on Th cell."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Enzyme required for somatic hypermutation and isotype switching?",
                                      "a": "<b>Activation-induced Cytidine Deaminase</b> (AID)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Only antibody isotype that crosses the placenta?",
                                      "a": "IgG."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most abundant antibody produced in the body (secretions)?",
                                      "a": "IgA."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Antibody isotype found as a pentamer in serum?",
                                      "a": "IgM."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Part of the antibody that determines the isotype (class)?",
                                      "a": "Fc region (heavy chain constant region)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Part of the antibody that binds the antigen?",
                                      "a": "Fab region (Variable light and heavy chains)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "\"Hot T-bone stEAK\" mnemonic for IL-1 through IL-6?",
                                      "a": "<b>IL-1:</b> Fever; IL-2: T-cells; IL-3: <b>Bone marrow</b>; IL-4: IgE; IL-5: IgA; IL-6: aKute phase reactants."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary cytokine for neutrophil chemotaxis?",
                                      "a": "IL-8."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Cytokines that inhibit viral replication in neighbor cells?",
                                      "a": "IFN-&alpha; and IFN-&beta;."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Major macrophage-activating cytokine?",
                                      "a": "IFN-&gamma;."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Major proinflammatory cytokine in cachexia?",
                                      "a": "TNF-&alpha;."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Cytokines involved in the \"Isotype switch\" to IgE?",
                                      "a": "IL-4 and IL-13."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "<b>Type I Hypersensitivity</b> mechanism?",
                                      "a": "IgE mediated; mast cell and basophil degranulation."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "<b>Type II Hypersensitivity</b> mechanism?",
                                      "a": "Cytotoxic; IgG/IgM antibodies against cell surface antigens."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "<b>Type III Hypersensitivity</b> mechanism?",
                                      "a": "Immune complex deposition (Ag-Ab complexes) activating complement."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "<b>Type IV Hypersensitivity</b> mechanism?",
                                      "a": "Delayed-type; T-cell mediated (not antibody)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Example of <b>Type II hypersensitivity</b> (non-cytotoxic)?",
                                      "a": "<b>Graves disease</b> or <b>Myasthenia Gravis</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Example of <b>Type III hypersensitivity</b>?",
                                      "a": "<b>SLE</b>, <b>Polyarteritis nodosa</b>, <b>Poststreptococcal glomerulonephritis</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Example of <b>Type IV hypersensitivity</b>?",
                                      "a": "<b>Contact dermatitis</b>, PPD test, <b><b>Graft-versus-host disease</b></b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Defect in <b>X-linked Agammaglobulinemia</b> (Bruton)?",
                                      "a": "Mutation in BTK (Bruton Tyrosine Kinase); no B-cell maturation."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common primary immunodeficiency?",
                                      "a": "<b>Selective IgA deficiency</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Presentation of <b>Common Variable Immunodeficiency</b> (CVID)?",
                                      "a": "Decreased plasma cells and immunoglobulins; increased risk of autoimmune disease and lymphoma."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Triad of <b>DiGeorge Syndrome</b>?",
                                      "a": "Thymic aplasia (T-cell def), Hypocalcemia (Parathyroid aplasia), Conotruncal heart defects."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Chromosomal deletion in <b>DiGeorge Syndrome</b>?",
                                      "a": "22q11."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Defect in <b>Severe Combined Immunodeficiency</b> (SCID) (X-linked)?",
                                      "a": "IL-2R gamma chain defect."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Defect in SCID (Autosomal Recessive)?",
                                      "a": "Adenosine Deaminase (ADA) deficiency."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Triad of <b>Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome</b>?",
                                      "a": "Thrombocytopenia, Eczema, Recurrent pyogenic infections (WATER)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Defect in <b>Hyper-IgM Syndrome</b>?",
                                      "a": "Defective CD40L on Th cells."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Defect in <b>Chronic Granulomatous Disease</b> (CGD)?",
                                      "a": "NADPH oxidase deficiency; failure of respiratory burst."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Pathogens associated with CGD?",
                                      "a": "Catalase-positive organisms (e.g., S. aureus, Aspergillus, Nocardia)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Screening test for CGD?",
                                      "a": "Dihydrorhodamine (DHR) <b>flow cytometry</b> (preferred) or Nitroblue Tetrazolium (NBT) test."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Defect in <b>Leukocyte Adhesion Deficiency</b> (Type 1)?",
                                      "a": "LFA-1 integrin (CD18) defect."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Clinical hallmark of <b>Leukocyte Adhesion Deficiency</b>?",
                                      "a": "Delayed separation of the umbilical cord."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Defect in <b>Ch\u00e9diak-Higashi Syndrome</b>?",
                                      "a": "LYST gene mutation; lysosomal trafficking defect."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Classic biopsy finding in Ch\u00e9diak-Higashi?",
                                      "a": "Giant granules in neutrophils and platelets."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Hyperacute transplant rejection timing?",
                                      "a": "Within minutes."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Hyperacute transplant rejection mechanism?",
                                      "a": "Preformed recipient antibodies against donor graft (<b>Type II hypersensitivity</b>)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Acute transplant rejection mechanism?",
                                      "a": "Host T-cells sensitized against donor HLA (Cellular) or antibodies (Humoral)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Chronic transplant rejection mechanism?",
                                      "a": "T-cell mediated secretion of cytokines leading to vascular smooth muscle proliferation and fibrosis."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of <b>Graft-versus-Host Disease</b> (GVHD)?",
                                      "a": "Grafted T-cells (donor) attack host (recipient) tissues."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Organs commonly affected by GVHD?",
                                      "a": "Skin (rash), Liver (jaundice), and GI tract (diarrhea)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Autoantibody for <b>SLE</b> (Highly Specific)?",
                                      "a": "Anti-dsDNA and Anti-Smith."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Autoantibody for Drug-induced Lupus?",
                                      "a": "Anti-histone."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Autoantibody for <b>Rheumatoid Arthritis</b> (More Specific)?",
                                      "a": "Anti-CCP (Cyclic Citrullinated Peptide)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Autoantibody for <b>Scleroderma</b> (Diffuse)?",
                                      "a": "Anti-Scl-70 (Anti-DNA topoisomerase I)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Autoantibody for <b>CREST Syndrome</b> (Limited <b>Scleroderma</b>)?",
                                      "a": "Anti-centromere."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Autoantibody for <b>Sj\u00f6gren Syndrome</b>?",
                                      "a": "Anti-Ro (SSA) and Anti-La (SSB)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Autoantibody for <b>Mixed Connective Tissue Disease</b>?",
                                      "a": "Anti-U1 RNP."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Autoantibody for <b>Wegener</b> (GPA)?",
                                      "a": "c-ANCA (PR3-ANCA)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Autoantibody for <b>Microscopic Polyangiitis</b> or Churg-Strauss?",
                                      "a": "p-ANCA (MPO-ANCA)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Autoantibody for <b>Hashimoto Thyroiditis</b>?",
                                      "a": "Anti-TPO (Thyroperoxidase) and Anti-thyroglobulin."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Autoantibody for <b>Primary Biliary Cholangitis</b>?",
                                      "a": "Anti-mitochondrial (AMA)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Autoantibody for <b>Autoimmune Hepatitis</b> (Type 1)?",
                                      "a": "Anti-smooth muscle (ASMA)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of <b>Cyclosporine</b>?",
                                      "a": "Binds cyclophilin; inhibits calcineurin; prevents IL-2 transcription."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Major side effect of <b>Cyclosporine</b>?",
                                      "a": "Nephrotoxicity."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of <b>Tacrolimus</b> (FK506)?",
                                      "a": "Binds FKBP; inhibits calcineurin; prevents IL-2 transcription."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of <b>Sirolimus</b> (Rapamycin)?",
                                      "a": "Binds FKBP; inhibits mTOR; prevents response to IL-2 (not production)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of <b>Azathioprine</b>?",
                                      "a": "Antimetabolite precursor of 6-mercaptopurine; inhibits purine synthesis."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Drug interaction with <b>Azathioprine</b>?",
                                      "a": "Allopurinol (inhibits Xanthine Oxidase, increasing toxicity)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of <b>Mycophenolate Mofetil</b>?",
                                      "a": "Reversibly inhibits IMP dehydrogenase; prevents purine synthesis in B/T cells."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Target of <b>Infliximab</b>/Adalimumab?",
                                      "a": "TNF-&alpha;."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Target of <b>Rituximab</b>?",
                                      "a": "CD20 (found on B-cells)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Target of <b>Abatacept</b>?",
                                      "a": "CTLA-4 (binds B7 to block T-cell costimulation)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Target of <b>Natalizumab</b>?",
                                      "a": "&alpha;<sub>4</sub>-integrin."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Risk associated with <b>Natalizumab</b> therapy?",
                                      "a": "Progressive Multifocal Leukoencephalopathy (PML) via JC virus reactivation."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Target of <b>Ustekinumab</b>?",
                                      "a": "IL-12 and IL-23."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Target of <b>Denosumab</b>?",
                                      "a": "RANKL (mimics osteoprotegerin)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Type of vaccine that induces a strong CD8+ T-cell response?",
                                      "a": "Live attenuated vaccines."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Benefit of Conjugated vaccines (e.g., H. influenzae)?",
                                      "a": "Converts polysaccharide to a T-cell dependent antigen to induce memory."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Cell type responsible for the \"Paracortex\" expansion in lymph nodes during infection?",
                                      "a": "T-cells."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Name of the specialized post-capillary venules in the lymph node paracortex?",
                                      "a": "High Endothelial Venules (HEVs)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of <b>Passive Immunity</b>?",
                                      "a": "Receiving preformed antibodies (short-lived, no memory)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "<b>Serum sickness</b> is which type of hypersensitivity?",
                                      "a": "Type III."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "<b>Arthus reaction</b> is which type of hypersensitivity?",
                                      "a": "Type III (local)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Function of Fas-Fas Ligand interaction?",
                                      "a": "Extrinsic pathway of apoptosis (eliminates self-reactive lymphocytes)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Cytokine primarily responsible for stimulating Acute Phase Reactants (e.g., CRP)?",
                                      "a": "IL-6."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Negative acute phase reactant (decreases during inflammation)?",
                                      "a": "Albumin and Transferrin."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Component of the TCR complex responsible for signal transduction?",
                                      "a": "CD3 (consisting of &gamma;, \\delta, &epsilon;, and &zeta; chains)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "The \"Zap-70\" protein associates with which part of the TCR?",
                                      "a": "Phosphorylated ITAMs on the &zeta; (zeta) chains."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Role of Lck in T-cell signaling?",
                                      "a": "Tyrosine kinase that phosphorylates ITAMs upon MHC-TCR binding."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Major inhibitory receptor on T-cells that binds B7 (high affinity)?",
                                      "a": "CTLA-4."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Inhibitory receptor on T-cells that binds PD-L1/PD-L2 on tumor cells?",
                                      "a": "PD-1."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Result of \"Anergy\" in T-cells?",
                                      "a": "Functional unresponsiveness due to Signal 1 (MHC-TCR) without Signal 2 (Costimulation)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "<b>Direct Coombs Test</b> detects what?",
                                      "a": "Antibodies bound directly to the surface of Red Blood Cells."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "<b>Indirect Coombs Test</b> detects what?",
                                      "a": "Unbound antibodies in the patient's serum."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Laboratory technique used to quantify specific cytokines or proteins in a fluid?",
                                      "a": "<b>ELISA</b> (Enzyme-Linked Immunosorbent Assay)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Technique used to determine the size, granularity, and surface markers of individual cells?",
                                      "a": "<b>Flow Cytometry</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Type of test used to confirm a positive HIV <b>ELISA</b>?",
                                      "a": "<b>Western Blot</b> (detects specific proteins/antibodies)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What does \"Titer\" represent in serology?",
                                      "a": "The highest dilution of serum that still yields a positive reaction."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Location of B-cell proliferation and somatic hypermutation in lymph nodes?",
                                      "a": "Germinal Centers (within follicles)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Lymph node structure missing in <b>X-linked Agammaglobulinemia</b>?",
                                      "a": "Germinal centers and follicles."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Specific area of the spleen where T-cells reside?",
                                      "a": "Periarteriolar Lymphoid Sheath (PALS)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Specific area of the spleen where B-cells reside?",
                                      "a": "Splenic follicles (within the White Pulp)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Function of \"M cells\" in Peyer\u2019s Patches?",
                                      "a": "Transcytosis of antigens from the gut lumen to underlying APCs."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Lymphatic drainage of the scrotum?",
                                      "a": "Superficial inguinal lymph nodes."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Lymphatic drainage of the testes?",
                                      "a": "Para-aortic lymph nodes."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "How do Superantigens (e.g., TSST-1) activate T-cells?",
                                      "a": "Cross-link the &beta;-variable region of TCR to the MHC II molecule (outside the peptide groove)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Percentage of T-cells activated by a typical antigen vs. a superantigen?",
                                      "a": "<0.001% (Antigen) vs. up to 20% (Superantigen)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of \"Antigenic Variation\" in Trypanosomes?",
                                      "a": "Periodic changes in surface glycoproteins to evade antibody recognition."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of \"Antigenic Shift\" in Influenza?",
                                      "a": "Reassortment of genomic segments (causes pandemics)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of \"Antigenic Drift\" in Influenza?",
                                      "a": "Point mutations in Hemagglutinin or Neuraminidase (causes epidemics)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary immune cell responsible for destroying Helminths?",
                                      "a": "Eosinophils (via Antibody-Dependent Cellular Cytotoxicity)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Major basic protein (MBP) is released by which cell?",
                                      "a": "Eosinophils."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Defect in <b>Ataxia-Telangiectasia</b>?",
                                      "a": "Defect in the ATM gene (failure to repair DNA double-strand breaks)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Clinical triad of <b>Ataxia-Telangiectasia</b>?",
                                      "a": "Cerebellar ataxia, Telangiectasias (spider angiomas), and IgA deficiency."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Findings in <b>Job Syndrome</b> (Hyper-IgE Syndrome)?",
                                      "a": "<b>FATED:</b> coarse Facies, Cold staph Abscesses, retained primary Teeth, increased IgE, Dermatologic (eczema)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Genetic defect in <b>Job Syndrome</b>?",
                                      "a": "STAT3 mutation (leads to deficiency in Th17 cells)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary concern in patients with Terminal Complement Deficiency (C5-C9)?",
                                      "a": "Recurrent Neisseria infections."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Why are asplenic patients susceptible to encapsulated organisms?",
                                      "a": "Loss of splenic macrophages and decreased opsonization (IgM/complement)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Pathogenesis of <b>Goodpasture Syndrome</b>?",
                                      "a": "Autoantibodies against Type IV Collagen in glomerular and alveolar basement membranes."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Immunofluorescence pattern in <b>Goodpasture Syndrome</b>?",
                                      "a": "Linear IgG deposition."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Immunofluorescence pattern in <b>Poststreptococcal Glomerulonephritis</b>?",
                                      "a": "Granular (\"Lumpy-Bumpy\") deposition (<b>Type III Hypersensitivity</b>)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Pathogenesis of <b>Pemphigus Vulgaris</b>?",
                                      "a": "Antibodies against Desmoglein (<b>Type II Hypersensitivity</b>)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Pathogenesis of <b>Bullous Pemphigoid</b>?",
                                      "a": "Antibodies against Hemidesmosomes (<b>Type II Hypersensitivity</b>)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary mediator of \"Late-phase\" <b>Type I Hypersensitivity</b>?",
                                      "a": "Leukotrienes."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Syngeneic graft (Isograft) definition?",
                                      "a": "Graft between genetically identical individuals (e.g., monozygotic twins)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Xenograft definition?",
                                      "a": "Graft between different species."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of <b>Rituximab</b> in B-cell lymphoma?",
                                      "a": "Monoclonal antibody against CD20 leads to B-cell lysis via ADCC and complement."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Function of CAR-T cell therapy?",
                                      "a": "Patient's T-cells are engineered to express a chimeric receptor targeting a specific tumor antigen (e.g., CD19)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Tumor marker associated with yolk sac tumors and hepatoma?",
                                      "a": "&alpha;-fetoprotein (AFP)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Target of <b>Omalizumab</b>?",
                                      "a": "IgE (prevents binding to Fc&epsilon;RI)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Target of <b>Palivizumab</b>?",
                                      "a": "RSV F protein (used for prophylaxis in high-risk infants)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Target of <b>Eculizumab</b>?",
                                      "a": "Complement C5 (used in PNH)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of <b>Abciximab</b>?",
                                      "a": "Antagonist of GPIIb/IIIa receptors (antiplatelet)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Target of <b>Vedolizumab</b>?",
                                      "a": "&alpha;<sub>4</sub>&beta;<sub>7</sub> integrin (gut-specific immunosuppression for IBD)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary cytokine that stimulates liver synthesis of fibrinogen?",
                                      "a": "IL-6."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary receptor for HIV on T-cells?",
                                      "a": "CD4."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Coreceptors required for HIV entry?",
                                      "a": "CCR5 (early/macrophages) or CXCR4 (late/T-cells)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Protective mutation against HIV infection?",
                                      "a": "CCR5 &Delta;32 mutation."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of \"Central Tolerance\" in B-cells?",
                                      "a": "Receptor editing (re-expression of RAG genes to change light chain)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Process by which B-cells change the light chain to avoid self-reactivity?",
                                      "a": "Receptor Editing."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "The two primary proteins required for TCR gene rearrangement?",
                                      "a": "RAG-1 and RAG-2."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary marker used to distinguish Hematopoietic Stem Cells?",
                                      "a": "CD34."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Surface marker found on all Mature T-cells but not B-cells?",
                                      "a": "CD3."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Surface marker for the LPS receptor on Macrophages?",
                                      "a": "CD14."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Co-receptor on B-cells that enhances signaling (part of the CD19/CD21/CD81 complex)?",
                                      "a": "CD21 (also the receptor for Epstein-Barr Virus)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Transcription factor required for Th17 development?",
                                      "a": "ROR&gamma;t."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Transcription factor required for Th2 development?",
                                      "a": "GATA-3."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Transcription factor required for Th1 development?",
                                      "a": "T-bet."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Molecule that prevents the assembly of the MAC on host cells?",
                                      "a": "CD59 (Protectin)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Factor that cleaves C3b into its inactive form (iC3b)?",
                                      "a": "Factor I (with Factor H as a cofactor)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Clinical finding in Factor H deficiency?",
                                      "a": "Atypical Hemolytic Uremic Syndrome (aHUS)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Major chemotactic factor for Eosinophils?",
                                      "a": "Eotaxin (and IL-5)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Enzyme in neutrophils that produces Hypochlorous acid (bleach)?",
                                      "a": "Myeloperoxidase (MPO)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Identifying feature of <b>Natural Killer</b> (NK) cells?",
                                      "a": "CD16 (Fc&gamma;RIII) and CD56; lack of CD3."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism by which NK cells recognize \"missing self\"?",
                                      "a": "Detection of downregulated <b>MHC Class I</b> on virally infected or tumor cells."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "<b>Prozone Effect</b> in serology?",
                                      "a": "False negative result due to antibody excess (prevents cross-linking)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Test used to diagnose <b>Chronic Granulomatous Disease</b> via fluorescence?",
                                      "a": "Dihydrorhodamine (DHR) 123 <b>flow cytometry</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Type of hypersensitivity involved in the <b>Arthus Reaction</b>?",
                                      "a": "Type III (local immune complex vasculitis)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of the \"Wheal and Flare\" reaction?",
                                      "a": "<b>Type I hypersensitivity</b> (immediate mast cell degranulation)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Target antigen in <b>Bullous Pemphigoid</b>?",
                                      "a": "BP180 or BP230 (Hemidesmosomes)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Target antigen in <b>Pemphigus Vulgaris</b>?",
                                      "a": "Desmoglein 1 and 3 (Desmosomes)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Viral protein produced by HSV to block TAP transporters?",
                                      "a": "ICP47 (prevents peptide loading onto MHC I)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism used by Mycobacteria to survive inside macrophages?",
                                      "a": "Inhibition of phagosome-lysosome fusion."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Method used by Neisseria gonorrhoeae to evade antibodies?",
                                      "a": "Pilin antigenic variation and IgA protease secretion."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary immune defense against Giardia lamblia?",
                                      "a": "Secretory IgA."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Immune cell type that forms the core of a granuloma?",
                                      "a": "Epithelioid macrophages (activated by Th1 IFN-&gamma;)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Deficiency associated with <b>SLE</b>-like symptoms and recurrent infections?",
                                      "a": "Early complement component deficiency (C1q, C2, C4)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Genetics of Agammaglobulinemia (Bruton)?",
                                      "a": "X-linked recessive."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Gene defect in IPEX Syndrome?",
                                      "a": "FOXP3 mutation (leads to lack of Tregs)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Clinical triad of IPEX Syndrome?",
                                      "a": "Immune dysregulation, Polyendocrinopathy, Enteropathy, X-linked."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Genetic defect in Hyper-IgE (Job) Syndrome?",
                                      "a": "STAT3 mutation."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of Autoimmune Lymphoproliferative Syndrome (ALPS)?",
                                      "a": "Defect in Fas/FasL (impaired apoptosis of self-reactive lymphocytes)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Target of Basiliximab?",
                                      "a": "IL-2 receptor (CD25)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Target of Bortezomib?",
                                      "a": "26S Proteasome (used in Multiple Myeloma)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Target of Cetuximab?",
                                      "a": "EGFR (Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Target of Trastuzumab?",
                                      "a": "HER2/neu."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Target of Bevacizumab?",
                                      "a": "VEGF (inhibits angiogenesis)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Target of Secukinumab?",
                                      "a": "IL-17A (used in Psoriasis)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Target of Dupilumab?",
                                      "a": "IL-4 receptor &alpha; (inhibits both IL-4 and IL-13 signaling)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of Methotrexate in immunology?",
                                      "a": "Dihydrofolate reductase inhibitor; inhibits lymphocyte proliferation."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "The \"<b>Direct Pathway</b>\" of allorecognition?",
                                      "a": "Recipient T-cells recognize intact donor MHC on donor APCs."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "The \"<b>Indirect Pathway</b>\" of allorecognition?",
                                      "a": "Recipient APCs process donor MHC and present it to recipient T-cells."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Major cytokine responsible for the fibrosis in chronic graft rejection?",
                                      "a": "TGF-&beta;."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Type of vaccine that should never be given to a SCID patient?",
                                      "a": "Live attenuated vaccines (e.g., Rotavirus, MMR, Varicella)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary antibody isotype in Breast Milk?",
                                      "a": "IgA."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What is \"<b>Clonal Ignorance</b>\"?",
                                      "a": "Self-reactive T-cells that ignore antigens because they are sequestered (e.g., Blood-Brain Barrier)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"<b>Molecular Mimicry</b>\"?",
                                      "a": "Microbial antigens resembling self-antigens (e.g., Rheumatic Fever)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Marker for Follicular <b>Dendritic Cells</b> (FDCs)?",
                                      "a": "CD21 (used to trap immune complexes for B-cells)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Function of the \"Poly-Ig Receptor\"?",
                                      "a": "Transports IgA across epithelial cells into the lumen."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Major component of the \"Secretory Component\" of IgA?",
                                      "a": "A fragment of the Poly-Ig receptor."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Difference between \"Affinity\" and \"Avidity\"?",
                                      "a": "Affinity is the strength of a single bond; Avidity is the overall strength of multiple bonds (e.g., IgM has high avidity)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Major protein that initiates the Classical Complement Pathway?",
                                      "a": "C1 complex (C1q, C1r, C1s)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Number of IgG molecules required to activate C1?",
                                      "a": "Two (must be in close proximity)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Number of IgM molecules required to activate C1?",
                                      "a": "One (in the \"staple\" conformation)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary signaling pathway used by most cytokine receptors?",
                                      "a": "JAK/STAT pathway."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "STAT protein activated by IL-12 to drive Th1 differentiation?",
                                      "a": "STAT4."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "STAT protein activated by IL-4 to drive Th2 differentiation?",
                                      "a": "STAT6."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "STAT protein activated by IL-6 to drive Th17 differentiation?",
                                      "a": "STAT3."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Function of ITIMs (Immunoreceptor Tyrosine-based Inhibitory Motifs)?",
                                      "a": "Recruit phosphatases (like SHP-1) to turn off lymphocyte activation."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Which inhibitory receptors contain ITIMs?",
                                      "a": "CTLA-4 and PD-1."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Main mechanism of Central Tolerance for T-cells?",
                                      "a": "Apoptosis (Negative Selection) in the <b>Thymus</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Two main mechanisms of Peripheral Tolerance?",
                                      "a": "Anergy and Suppression by Tregs."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Fate of a B-cell that recognizes soluble self-antigen in the <b>bone marrow</b>?",
                                      "a": "It becomes anergic (unresponsive)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Fate of a B-cell that recognizes multivalent self-antigen on a cell surface in the <b>bone marrow</b>?",
                                      "a": "Receptor Editing (altering the light chain) or Apoptosis."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary function of Plasmacytoid <b>Dendritic Cells</b> (pDCs)?",
                                      "a": "Massive production of Type I Interferons (&alpha; and &beta;) during viral infections."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Only lymphocyte that does not require clonal expansion/differentiation to function?",
                                      "a": "<b>Natural Killer</b> (NK) cells."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Cell type that expresses the receptor \"RANK\"?",
                                      "a": "Osteoclast precursors (activated by RANKL from T-cells/Osteoblasts)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary cell responsible for the \"Alternative\" activation of macrophages (M2)?",
                                      "a": "Th2 cells (via IL-4 and IL-13)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Role of M2 (Alternatively Activated) macrophages?",
                                      "a": "Tissue repair, fibrosis, and anti-inflammatory effects."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Genetic defect in <b>Bare Lymphocyte Syndrome</b> Type II?",
                                      "a": "Mutations in transcription factors for <b>MHC Class II</b> expression (e.g., CIITA)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Result of <b>Bare Lymphocyte Syndrome</b> Type II?",
                                      "a": "Profound CD4+ T-cell deficiency (SCID-like presentation)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Genetic defect in <b>Bare Lymphocyte Syndrome</b> Type I?",
                                      "a": "TAP1 or TAP2 deficiency (leads to low <b>MHC Class I</b> and low CD8+ T-cells)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Characteristic lab finding in <b>Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome</b>?",
                                      "a": "Small platelets and low platelet count (thrombocytopenia)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Cytokine deficiency that leads to \"Disseminated Mycobacterial Infections\"?",
                                      "a": "IL-12 receptor deficiency or IFN-&gamma; receptor deficiency."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Immunodeficiency associated with a 1q21.1 or 22q11.2 deletion?",
                                      "a": "<b>DiGeorge Syndrome</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Purpose of the CH50 assay?",
                                      "a": "Measures the total hemolytic complement activity (tests the Classical Pathway)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "What does a low CH50 with normal C3/C4 suggest?",
                                      "a": "Deficiency in the terminal complement components (C5-C9)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Meaning of \"Antinuclear Antibody (ANA)\" pattern: Peripheral/Rim?",
                                      "a": "Highly suggestive of <b>SLE</b> (Anti-dsDNA)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Meaning of \"Antinuclear Antibody (ANA)\" pattern: Nucleolar?",
                                      "a": "Suggestive of Systemic Sclerosis (<b>Scleroderma</b>)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Test used to detect <b>Chronic Granulomatous Disease</b> before DHR became standard?",
                                      "a": "Nitroblue Tetrazolium (NBT) test."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Target of Belimumab?",
                                      "a": "B-cell activating factor (BAFF/BLyS); used in <b>SLE</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Target of Tocilizumab?",
                                      "a": "IL-6 receptor; used in <b>Rheumatoid Arthritis</b> and Cytokine Release Syndrome."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Target of Canakinumab?",
                                      "a": "IL-1&beta;; used in periodic fever syndromes and Gout."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Target of <b>Abciximab</b>?",
                                      "a": "Glycoprotein IIb/IIIa on platelets."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Target of <b>Denosumab</b>?",
                                      "a": "RANKL; used to treat Osteoporosis."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mnemonic for the 4 Ts of <b>Type IV Hypersensitivity</b>?",
                                      "a": "T-cells, Transplant (Rejection), TB (PPD), Touching (<b>Contact Dermatitis</b>)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Differentiation between \"Opsonization\" and \"Neutralization\"?",
                                      "a": "Opsonization marks for phagocytosis; Neutralization blocks binding to host cells."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Part of the IgA molecule that prevents its degradation by proteases?",
                                      "a": "Secretory Component."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Major antibody isotype found in the primary (early) immune response?",
                                      "a": "IgM."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Major antibody isotype found in the secondary (amnestic) immune response?",
                                      "a": "IgG."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Location of \"Margination\" in the inflammatory process?",
                                      "a": "Blood vessel wall (leukocytes move to the periphery)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Proteins responsible for leukocyte \"Rolling\"?",
                                      "a": "E-selectin and P-selectin on endothelium; Sialyl-Lewis X on leukocytes."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Proteins responsible for leukocyte \"Tight Adhesion\"?",
                                      "a": "ICAM-1 on endothelium; LFA-1 (Integrin) on leukocytes."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Protein responsible for leukocyte \"Diapedesis\" (Transmigration)?",
                                      "a": "PECAM-1 (CD31)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Name for the specialized macrophages in the Liver?",
                                      "a": "<b>Kupffer cells</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Name for the specialized macrophages in the Brain?",
                                      "a": "<b>Microglia</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Name for the specialized macrophages in the Lung?",
                                      "a": "<b>Alveolar macrophages</b> (Dust cells)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Function of \"<b>Properdin</b>\" in the complement system?",
                                      "a": "Stabilizes C3 convertase (C3bBb) in the Alternative pathway."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Result of \"C3 deficiency\"?",
                                      "a": "Increased susceptibility to <b>Type III hypersensitivity</b> and recurrent pyogenic infections."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary cytokine involved in \"Class Switching\" to IgA?",
                                      "a": "TGF-&beta; (and IL-5 in some species)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Cytokine that induces the production of \"Acute Phase Reactants\" from the liver?",
                                      "a": "IL-6."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "<b>HLA association:</b> B51?",
                                      "a": "Behcet disease."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "<b>HLA association:</b> DR4?",
                                      "a": "<b><b>Rheumatoid arthritis</b> (Mnemonic:</b> 4 walls in a \"Rheum\")."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "<b>HLA association:</b> A3?",
                                      "a": "Hemochromatosis."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "<b>HLA association:</b> B*5701?",
                                      "a": "Abacavir hypersensitivity."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "<b>HLA association:</b> DR5?",
                                      "a": "<b>Hashimoto thyroiditis</b> and Pernicious anemia."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Protein that acts as a \"molecular switch\" in TCR signaling by cycling between GTP and GDP?",
                                      "a": "Ras."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "The three primary pathways activated by the TCR complex?",
                                      "a": "MAP kinase (Ras/Raf), Calcium-Calmodulin (NFAT), and PKC-\\theta (NF-&kappa;B)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Enzyme inhibited by <b>Cyclosporine</b> and <b>Tacrolimus</b>?",
                                      "a": "Calcineurin (a phosphatase)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Target of the drug <b>Sirolimus</b> (Rapamycin) within the cell?",
                                      "a": "mTOR (Mammalian Target of Rapamycin)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Role of DAG (Diacylglycerol) in T-cell activation?",
                                      "a": "Activates Protein Kinase C (PKC)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Specialized epithelial cells that transport IgA across the basement membrane?",
                                      "a": "Enterocytes (via the Poly-Ig receptor)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary antibody isotype in the urogenital and respiratory tracts?",
                                      "a": "Secretory IgA."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Major cytokine responsible for \"Oral Tolerance\"?",
                                      "a": "TGF-&beta;."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Name of the <b>dendritic cells</b> found specifically in the epidermis?",
                                      "a": "<b>Langerhans cells</b>."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Characteristic electron microscopy finding in <b>Langerhans cells</b>?",
                                      "a": "Birbeck granules (tennis racket shape)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of \"Immune Subversion\" by tumors via TGF-&beta;?",
                                      "a": "Induction of Tregs and inhibition of CTL/NK cell activity."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Two common tumor-associated antigens used in clinical monitoring (not screening)?",
                                      "a": "CEA (Carcinoembryonic antigen) and PSA (Prostate-specific antigen)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Class of drugs that include Ipilimumab?",
                                      "a": "Checkpoint inhibitors (specifically Anti-CTLA-4)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Target of Nivolumab and Pembrolizumab?",
                                      "a": "PD-1 (Programmed Cell Death Protein 1)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary cytokine used in the treatment of Renal Cell Carcinoma and Melanoma?",
                                      "a": "IL-2 (Aldesleukin)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Test used to identify the specific HLA alleles of a transplant patient?",
                                      "a": "HLA Typing (PCR or <b>Flow Cytometry</b>)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Significance of a \"Positive Crossmatch\" in transplantation?",
                                      "a": "Recipient has preformed antibodies against donor HLA; contraindication to transplant."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Difference between \"Direct\" and \"Indirect\" Immunofluorescence?",
                                      "a": "Direct uses a labeled primary antibody; Indirect uses a labeled secondary antibody."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Diagnostic use of Nitroblue Tetrazolium (NBT) reduction?",
                                      "a": "Assessment of the oxidative burst in neutrophils (remains yellow in CGD)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Mechanism of \"<b>Molecular Mimicry</b>\" in Rheumatic Fever?",
                                      "a": "Antibodies against Group A Strep M-protein cross-react with cardiac myosin."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Why do patients with IgA deficiency have an increased risk of anaphylaxis during blood transfusions?",
                                      "a": "They may have anti-IgA antibodies (IgE class) that react to IgA in the donor blood."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Definition of \"Hapten\"?",
                                      "a": "A small molecule that is only immunogenic when attached to a large carrier protein."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Why are patients with \"Early\" complement deficiencies (C1, C2, C4) at risk for <b>SLE</b>?",
                                      "a": "Impaired clearance of apoptotic cells and immune complexes."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary immune response to a \"Superantigen\" (toxic shock)?",
                                      "a": "Massive release of IFN-&gamma; and IL-2 (T-cells) and TNF-&alpha; and IL-1 (Macrophages)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Location of \"T-cell independent\" B-cell activation?",
                                      "a": "Splenic marginal zone (responses to polysaccharide capsules)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Most common cause of death in patients with SCID (if untreated)?",
                                      "a": "Opportunistic infections (e.g., Pneumocystis jirovecii or Candida) in the first year of life."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary mediator of the \"Anamnestic\" (memory) response?",
                                      "a": "Memory B and T cells."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Enzyme that creates the \"N-nucleotide\" diversity during <b>VDJ recombination</b>?",
                                      "a": "TdT (Terminal Deoxynucleotidyl Transferase)."
                            },
                            {
                                      "q": "Primary clinical use of Rho(D) Immune Globulin (RhoGAM)?",
                                      "a": "Prevents Hemolytic Disease of the Newborn by opsonizing fetal RBCs in maternal circulation."
                            }
                  ]
        }],
      'year3': [
{
          section: 'Internal Medicine',
          topic: "Cardiology — ACS, heart failure, arrhythmias, valvular disease",
          icon: 'heart',
          cards: [
            { q: "Most common cause of ACS?", a: "Rupture of an unstable atherosclerotic plaque with subsequent thrombus formation." },
            { q: "Definition of Unstable Angina (UA)?", a: "Ischemic symptoms at rest, new-onset severe angina, or increasing intensity/frequency without cardiac biomarker elevation." },
            { q: "Difference between NSTEMI and STEMI on EKG?", a: "NSTEMI shows ST-depression or T-wave inversion; STEMI shows ST-segment elevation or new LBBB." },
            { q: "Gold standard biomarker for myocardial necrosis?", a: "Cardiac Troponin I or T." },
            { q: "Timeframe for Troponin elevation after MI?", a: "Elevates in 3–12 hours, peaks at 24 hours, stays elevated for 5–14 days." },
            { q: "Primary goal for \"Door-to-Balloon\" time in STEMI?", a: "Less than 90 minutes." },
            { q: "Indication for Fibrinolytics in STEMI?", a: "PCI unavailable within 120 minutes of medical contact." },
            { q: "Absolute contraindication for Fibrinolytics?", a: "Prior intracranial hemorrhage, active internal bleeding, or ischemic stroke within 3 months." },
            { q: "Standard \"MONA\" therapy for ACS?", a: "Morphine, Oxygen (if <90%), Nitroglycerin, Aspirin." },
            { q: "Role of Beta-blockers in ACS?", a: "Reduce myocardial oxygen demand by decreasing heart rate and contractility." },
            { q: "Contraindications for Nitroglycerin in ACS?", a: "Right ventricular infarction, hypotension, or recent PDE-5 inhibitor use (e.g., Sildenafil)." },
            { q: "Post-MI complication occurring 2–7 days later with a new holosystolic murmur?", a: "Ventricular septal rupture or Papillary muscle rupture." },
            { q: "Clinical sign of Right Ventricular (RV) Infarction?", a: "Hypotension, clear lungs, and JVD (Kussmaul’s sign)." },
            { q: "Treatment for RV Infarction?", a: "Aggressive IV fluid resuscitation; avoid nitrates." },
            { q: "Definition of Dressler Syndrome?", a: "Post-MI autoimmune pericarditis occurring 2–10 weeks after the event." },
            { q: "First-line long-term antiplatelet therapy post-ACS?", a: "Dual Antiplatelet Therapy (DAPT) with Aspirin and a P2Y12 inhibitor (e.g., Clopidogrel, Ticagrelor)." },
            { q: "Primary distinction between HFrEF and HFpEF?", a: "HFrEF (Systolic) has LVEF ≤40%; HFpEF (Diastolic) has LVEF ≥50%." },
            { q: "Most common cause of Left-sided Heart Failure?", a: "Ischemic heart disease and Hypertension." },
            { q: "Most common cause of Right-sided Heart Failure?", a: "Left-sided heart failure." },
            { q: "Key physical exam finding for Left HF?", a: "Pulmonary rales, S3 gallop, and displaced apical impulse." },
            { q: "Key physical exam finding for Right HF?", a: "Jugular Venous Distention (JVD), hepatomegaly, and peripheral edema." },
            { q: "Meaning of a Positive Hepatojugular Reflux?", a: "Suggests elevated pulmonary artery wedge pressure (Left heart congestion)." },
            { q: "Diagnostic utility of B-type Natriuretic Peptide (BNP)?", a: "High sensitivity to rule out HF in patients with acute dyspnea." },
            { q: "Four pillars of Guideline-Directed Medical Therapy (GDMT) for HFrEF?", a: "ARNI (or ACEi/ARB), Beta-blocker, MRA (Spironolactone), and SGLT2 inhibitor." },
            { q: "Which Beta-blockers are proven to reduce mortality in HFrEF?", a: "Carvedilol, Metoprolol succinate, and Bisoprolol." },
            { q: "Indication for Spironolactone in HF?", a: "NYHA Class II-IV with LVEF ≤35%." },
            { q: "Common side effect of ACE inhibitors?", a: "Cough and angioedema due to increased bradykinin." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Sacubitril (in ARNI)?", a: "Neprilysin inhibitor that prevents breakdown of natriuretic peptides." },
            { q: "Digoxin’s role in HF management?", a: "Symptom control and reduction of hospitalizations; does not improve mortality." },
            { q: "Sign of Digoxin toxicity on EKG?", a: "Downsloping ST-segment depression (\"Salvador Dali mustache\") and arrhythmias." },
            { q: "Criteria for Cardiac Resynchronization Therapy (CRT)?", a: "LVEF ≤35%, NYHA II-IV, and QRS duration ≥150ms with LBBB morphology." },
            { q: "CXR findings in Acute Decompensated HF?", a: "Cephalization of vessels, Kerley B lines, pleural effusions, and cardiomegaly." },
            { q: "Most common chronic arrhythmia?", a: "Atrial Fibrillation (AFib)." },
            { q: "EKG characteristics of AFib?", a: "Irregularly irregular rhythm with absent P-waves." },
            { q: "Scoring system used to assess stroke risk in AFib?", a: "CHA2DS2-VASc score." },
            { q: "Treatment for hemodynamically unstable AFib?", a: "Immediate synchronized electrical cardioversion." },
            { q: "First-line pharmacological rate control for AFib?", a: "Beta-blockers or Non-dihydropyridine Calcium Channel Blockers (Diltiazem/Verapamil)." },
            { q: "Target INR for AFib patients on Warfarin?", a: "2.0 to 3.0." },
            { q: "Classic EKG finding in Atrial Flutter?", a: "\"Sawtooth\" flutter waves, typically seen in leads II, III, and aVF." },
            { q: "First-line treatment for Paroxysmal Supraventricular Tachycardia (PSVT)?", a: "Vagal maneuvers followed by IV Adenosine." },
            { q: "Characteristic EKG triad of Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW)?", a: "Short PR interval, widened QRS, and a Delta wave." },
            { q: "Avoided drugs in WPW with AFib?", a: "AV nodal blockers (ABCD: Adenosine, Beta-blockers, CCBs, Digoxin)." },
            { q: "Definition of Torsades de Pointes?", a: "Polymorphic Ventricular Tachycardia associated with a prolonged QT interval." },
            { q: "Immediate treatment for Torsades de Pointes?", a: "IV Magnesium Sulfate." },
            { q: "EKG finding in First-degree AV block?", a: "Fixed PR interval prolongation >200ms without dropped beats." },
            { q: "Distinguishing feature of Mobitz Type I (Wenckebach)?", a: "Progressive PR interval lengthening until a QRS is dropped." },
            { q: "Management of Mobitz Type II block?", a: "Permanent pacemaker (due to high risk of progression to 3rd-degree block)." },
            { q: "Definition of Third-degree (Complete) Heart Block?", a: "Complete dissociation between P-waves and QRS complexes." },
            { q: "Classic triad of Aortic Stenosis (AS)?", a: "Angina, Syncope, and Dyspnea (SAD)." },
            { q: "Murmur description for Aortic Stenosis?", a: "Harsh crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur at the right upper sternal border radiating to carotids." },
            { q: "Physical exam sign \"Pulsus parvus et tardus\" indicates?", a: "Severe Aortic Stenosis." },
            { q: "Most common cause of Aortic Stenosis in patients <70 years old?", a: "Bicuspid aortic valve." },
            { q: "Murmur description for Aortic Regurgitation (AR)?", a: "Early diastolic blowing decrescendo murmur at the left sternal border." },
            { q: "What is \"Water-hammer pulse\" (Corrigan pulse)?", a: "Rapidly rising and falling arterial pulse seen in chronic Aortic Regurgitation." },
            { q: "Most common cause of Mitral Stenosis (MS) worldwide?", a: "Rheumatic Heart Disease." },
            { q: "Murmur description for Mitral Stenosis?", a: "Mid-diastolic rumbling murmur with an opening snap at the apex." },
            { q: "Relationship between Opening Snap and MS severity?", a: "A shorter A2-to-opening snap interval indicates more severe stenosis." },
            { q: "Murmur description for Mitral Regurgitation (MR)?", a: "Holosystolic, blowing murmur at the apex radiating to the axilla." },
            { q: "Acute Mitral Regurgitation is a common complication of?", a: "Papillary muscle rupture post-MI or Infective Endocarditis." },
            { q: "Murmur of Mitral Valve Prolapse (MVP)?", a: "Midsystolic click followed by a late systolic murmur." },
            { q: "Maneuver that makes MVP and HOCM murmurs louder?", a: "Valsalva or standing (decreases preload)." },
            { q: "Murmur of Tricuspid Regurgitation?", a: "Holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border that increases with inspiration." },
            { q: "Definitive diagnostic test for all valvular lesions?", a: "Echocardiography." },
            { q: "Most common type of cardiomyopathy?", a: "Dilated Cardiomyopathy." },
            { q: "Characteristic finding on Echo for Hypertrophic Obstructive Cardiomyopathy (HOCM)?", a: "Asymmetric septal hypertrophy and Systolic Anterior Motion (SAM) of the mitral valve." },
            { q: "First-line medication for symptomatic HOCM?", a: "Beta-blockers (to increase diastolic filling time)." },
            { q: "Classic EKG finding in Acute Pericarditis?", a: "Diffuse ST-segment elevation and PR-segment depression." },
            { q: "Beck’s Triad for Cardiac Tamponade?", a: "Hypotension, JVD, and muffled heart sounds." },
            { q: "Definition of Pulsus Paradoxus?", a: "A drop in systolic blood pressure >10 mmHg during inspiration." },
            { q: "EKG finding in Cardiac Tamponade?", a: "Electrical alternans (alternating QRS amplitude)." },
            { q: "Kussmaul’s sign is most associated with which pericardial pathology?", a: "Constrictive Pericarditis." },
            { q: "Treatment of choice for Cardiac Tamponade?", a: "Urgent pericardiocentesis." },
            { q: "Common etiology of Restrictive Cardiomyopathy?", a: "Amyloidosis, Sarcoidosis, or Hemochromatosis." },
            { q: "Preferred treatment for Hypertensive Emergency?", a: "IV Labetalol, Nicardipine, or Nitroprusside (with controlled BP reduction)." },
            { q: "Calculation for Mean Arterial Pressure (MAP)?", a: "$MAP = \\frac{SBP + 2(DBP)}{3}$" },
            { q: "Medication that improves survival in African Americans with HFrEF?", a: "Hydralazine plus Isosorbide Dinitrate." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Statins?", a: "Inhibition of HMG-CoA reductase, the rate-limiting step in cholesterol synthesis." },
            { q: "Most common side effect of Statin therapy?", a: "Myalgia or transaminitis (liver enzyme elevation)." },
            { q: "Treatment for cocaine-induced chest pain?", a: "Benzodiazepines and Nitrates; avoid Beta-blockers (risk of unopposed alpha-agonism)." },
            { q: "Duke Criteria is used for the diagnosis of?", a: "Infective Endocarditis." },
            { q: "Major Duke Criteria?", a: "Positive blood cultures (typical organism) and Evidence of endocardial involvement (Echo showing vegetation)." },
            { q: "Physical signs of Endocarditis?", a: "Roth spots (retina), Janeway lesions (painless palms/soles), Osler nodes (painful fingertips), and Splinter hemorrhages." },
            { q: "First-line treatment for most patients with Pericarditis?", a: "NSAIDs and Colchicine." },
            { q: "Aortic Dissection: Type A vs Type B management?", a: "Type A (ascending) requires surgery; Type B (descending) is typically managed with medical BP control." },
            { q: "Classic CXR finding in Aortic Dissection?", a: "Widened mediastinum." },
            { q: "What is the \"Bainbridge Reflex\"?", a: "Increase in heart rate due to an increase in central venous pressure (atrial stretch)." },
            { q: "Most common cause of sudden cardiac death in young athletes?", a: "Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy (HOCM)." },
            { q: "Screening recommendation for Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm (AAA)?", a: "One-time ultrasound for men aged 65–75 who have ever smoked." },
            { q: "Definitive treatment for sick sinus syndrome?", a: "Permanent Pacemaker." },
            { q: "Primary mechanism of action of Lidocaine as an antiarrhythmic?", a: "Class IB Sodium channel blocker; shortens action potential duration." },
            { q: "Drug of choice for symptomatic Bradycardia?", a: "Atropine." },
            { q: "Most common cause of Myocarditis in the US?", a: "Viral infections (e.g., Coxsackievirus B, Parvovirus B19)." },
            { q: "Gold standard for diagnosing Coronary Artery Disease?", a: "Coronary Angiography." },
            { q: "Treatment for Hyperkalemia-induced EKG changes?", a: "IV Calcium gluconate (to stabilize the cardiac membrane)." },
            { q: "Which valvular lesion is associated with Head Bobbing (De Musset sign)?", a: "Chronic Aortic Regurgitation." },
            { q: "EKG finding of a \"Brugada Pattern\"?", a: "Pseudo-RBBB and persistent ST elevation in leads V1–V2." },
            { q: "Management of a patient with WPW and Atrial Fibrillation?", a: "Procainamide or Cardioversion (Avoid AV nodal blockers)." },
            { q: "Major side effect of Amiodarone?", a: "Pulmonary fibrosis, thyroid dysfunction (hypo/hyper), and hepatotoxicity." },
            { q: "Goal BP for most patients according to ACC/AHA guidelines?", a: "Less than 130/80 mmHg." },
            { q: "Most sensitive EKG finding for Posterior Wall MI?", a: "ST-segment depression in leads V1–V3 with prominent R waves." },
            { q: "What is the \"de Winter\" T-wave pattern?", a: "Upsloping ST-depression with tall, symmetric T-waves in precordial leads; indicates proximal LAD occlusion." },
            { q: "Clinical utility of the HEART score?", a: "Risk stratifies patients with undifferentiated chest pain in the ED to identify those safe for discharge." },
            { q: "Which score predicts 6-month mortality in patients admitted with ACS?", a: "The GRACE score (Global Registry of Acute Cardiac Events)." },
            { q: "Goal time for Fibrinolytics if PCI is unavailable?", a: "Door-to-needle time <30 minutes." },
            { q: "Indication for urgent CABG in ACS?", a: "Failed PCI, cardiogenic shock, or mechanical complications (e.g., VSD, papillary muscle rupture)." },
            { q: "Significance of elevated Troponin in End-Stage Renal Disease (ESRD) without ACS?", a: "Chronic microinjury to the myocardium; however, a rising/falling trend still suggests acute ischemia." },
            { q: "Recommended duration of P2Y12 inhibitor therapy after DES placement in ACS?", a: "At least 12 months." },
            { q: "Standard management for HFrEF patient with persistent symptoms on Triple Therapy?", a: "Add an SGLT2 inhibitor (e.g., Dapagliflozin) or consider ARNI if on ACEi/ARB." },
            { q: "Contraindication for starting ARNI immediately after ACE inhibitor?", a: "Requires a 36-hour washout period to prevent angioedema." },
            { q: "Electrolyte abnormality associated with Spironolactone?", a: "Hyperkalemia and metabolic acidosis." },
            { q: "When to initiate Hydralazine-Isosorbide Dinitrate in HFrEF?", a: "Symptomatic African American patients despite optimal GDMT or patients unable to tolerate ACEi/ARBs due to renal failure." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Ivabradine?", a: "Selective $I_f$ current inhibitor in the SA node; reduces heart rate without affecting contractility." },
            { q: "Indication for Ivabradine?", a: "HFrEF (LVEF ≤35%) in sinus rhythm with resting heart rate ≥70 bpm despite maximally tolerated Beta-blockers." },
            { q: "Key hemodynamic difference between Systolic and Diastolic HF?", a: "Systolic HF involves reduced contractility; Diastolic HF involves impaired ventricular relaxation/compliance." },
            { q: "First-line treatment for a patient with AFib and Wolff-Parkinson-White?", a: "Procainamide (avoid Adenosine, Beta-blockers, CCBs, and Digoxin)." },
            { q: "Pathophysiology of Ashman’s Phenomenon?", a: "Aberrantly conducted QRS (usually RBBB) following a short R-R interval preceded by a long R-R interval, common in AFib." },
            { q: "Definition of \"Holiday Heart Syndrome\"?", a: "Acute AFib triggered by excessive alcohol consumption in a healthy individual." },
            { q: "Classic EKG finding in MAT (Multifocal Atrial Tachycardia)?", a: "HR >100 bpm with ≥3 different P-wave morphologies; often seen in COPD patients." },
            { q: "Initial treatment for MAT?", a: "Oxygenation and treatment of underlying pulmonary disease; CCBs for rate control if needed." },
            { q: "Most specific EKG sign of Digoxin toxicity?", a: "Atrial tachycardia with variable AV block." },
            { q: "Characteristic EKG of Wellens’ Syndrome?", a: "Deeply inverted or biphasic T-waves in V2–V3; indicates critical proximal LAD stenosis." },
            { q: "Most common cause of chronic Aortic Regurgitation in developed countries?", a: "Aortic root dilation (e.g., hypertension, Marfan syndrome, aortic dissection)." },
            { q: "Meaning of \"Austin Flint\" murmur?", a: "Mid-to-late diastolic rumble at the apex heard in severe Aortic Regurgitation (due to regurgitant jet impinging on the mitral valve)." },
            { q: "Management of symptomatic severe Mitral Stenosis with favorable morphology?", a: "Percutaneous Mitral Balloon Valvotomy." },
            { q: "Preferred diagnostic tool to assess for Mitral Valve vegetations or thrombus?", a: "Transesophageal Echocardiography (TEE)." },
            { q: "Characteristic murmur of Tricuspid Stenosis?", a: "Diastolic rumble at the left lower sternal border that increases with inspiration (Carvallo’s sign)." },
            { q: "Key indication for valve replacement in asymptomatic Aortic Stenosis?", a: "LVEF <50% or patient undergoing other cardiac surgery." },
            { q: "Murmur change during Handgrip in Mitral Regurgitation?", a: "Murmur intensity increases due to increased afterload." },
            { q: "First-line treatment for Symptomatic Bradycardia?", a: "Atropine 1mg IV; if ineffective, provide transcutaneous pacing or Dopamine/Epinephrine infusion." },
            { q: "Classic CXR sign of Coarctation of the Aorta?", a: "Rib notching (due to collateral intercostal arteries) and the \"3\" sign." },
            { q: "Most common primary cardiac tumor in adults?", a: "Atrial Myxoma (usually in the left atrium)." },
            { q: "Most common primary cardiac tumor in children?", a: "Rhabdomyoma (highly associated with Tuberous Sclerosis)." },
            { q: "Treatment for Type B Aortic Dissection?", a: "IV Beta-blockers (e.g., Esmolol) to reduce heart rate and shear stress, followed by vasodilators if needed." },
            { q: "ECG sign for Hypercalcemia?", a: "Shortened QT interval." },
            { q: "ECG sign for Hypocalcemia?", a: "Prolonged QT interval." },
            { q: "Definitive management for Pulseless Electrical Activity (PEA)?", a: "CPR and Epinephrine; look for reversible causes (Hs and Ts)." },
            { q: "Effect of Valsalva maneuver on the murmur of Aortic Stenosis?", a: "Decreases (due to decreased preload)." },
            { q: "Effect of Squatting on the murmur of HOCM?", a: "Decreases (increased preload and afterload increase LV volume, reducing outflow obstruction)." },
            { q: "Murmur that increases with Sustained Handgrip?", a: "Mitral Regurgitation, Aortic Regurgitation, and VSD (due to increased afterload)." },
            { q: "Murmur that decreases with Sustained Handgrip?", a: "HOCM and Aortic Stenosis." },
            { q: "Definition of the \"Gallavardin Phenomenon\"?", a: "Dissociation between the noisy and musical components of the Aortic Stenosis murmur, often heard at the apex." },
            { q: "Clinical significance of a prominent 'a' wave in JVP?", a: "Right atrial contraction against a closed or stiff valve (Tricuspid Stenosis, Pulmonary HTN, or Right Ventricular Hypertrophy)." },
            { q: "Clinical significance of \"Cannon a-waves\"?", a: "Atrial contraction against a closed tricuspid valve (AV dissociation: V-Tach or 3rd-degree Heart Block)." },
            { q: "Clinical significance of a prominent 'v' wave in JVP?", a: "Tricuspid Regurgitation (blood flows back into the atrium during ventricular systole)." },
            { q: "Next best step for a patient with chest pain and new Left Bundle Branch Block (LBBB)?", a: "Treat as an emergent STEMI (Immediate reperfusion therapy)." },
            { q: "First-line treatment for Prinzmetal (Vasospastic) Angina?", a: "Calcium Channel Blockers and Nitrates." },
            { q: "Avoided medication in Prinzmetal Angina?", a: "Non-selective Beta-blockers (may worsen vasospasm due to unopposed alpha-1 activity)." },
            { q: "TIMI Risk Score parameters?", a: "Age ≥65, ≥3 CAD risk factors, Known CAD (>50% stenosis), Aspirin use in last 7 days, Severe angina (2+ in 24h), ST deviation, Elevated biomarkers." },
            { q: "Management of a NSTEMI patient with refractory ischemia or hemodynamic instability?", a: "Immediate Cardiac Catheterization (within 2 hours)." },
            { q: "Common EKG finding in Acute Pulmonary Embolism (PE)?", a: "Sinus tachycardia (most common) or S1Q3T3 pattern (specific but rare)." },
            { q: "Most common cause of death within the first few hours of a myocardial infarction?", a: "Ventricular Fibrillation." },
            { q: "Most common cause of Cor Pulmonale?", a: "Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD)." },
            { q: "First-line treatment for Acute Pulmonary Edema?", a: "\"LMNOP\" (Loop diuretics, Morphine, Nitrates, Oxygen, Position/BiPAP)." },
            { q: "Lab test used to differentiate heart failure from primary lung disease?", a: "BNP or NT-proBNP." },
            { q: "Classic EKG finding in Dilated Cardiomyopathy?", a: "Non-specific ST-T wave changes and Left Bundle Branch Block." },
            { q: "Most common cause of Restrictive Cardiomyopathy in the US?", a: "Amyloidosis." },
            { q: "Definitive diagnosis of Myocarditis?", a: "Endomyocardial Biopsy (though Cardiac MRI is often used first)." },
            { q: "Classic EKG in Arrhythmogenic Right Ventricular Cardiomyopathy (ARVC)?", a: "Epsilon wave (small notch at the end of the QRS in V1–V3)." },
            { q: "Preferred treatment for hemodynamically stable Ventricular Tachycardia?", a: "IV Amiodarone or Procainamide." },
            { q: "Next step for Pulseless Ventricular Tachycardia or Ventricular Fibrillation?", a: "Immediate unsynchronized defibrillation." },
            { q: "Treatment for symptomatic Sinus Bradycardia if Atropine fails?", a: "Transcutaneous pacing." },
            { q: "EKG finding of Trifascicular Block?", a: "RBBB, Left axis deviation (LAFB), and 1st-degree AV block." },
            { q: "Medication used for chemical stress testing in patients who cannot exercise?", a: "Adenosine, Regadenoson, or Dobutamine." },
            { q: "What does \"Capture Beats\" and \"Fusion Beats\" on EKG signify?", a: "Diagnostic of Ventricular Tachycardia (helps distinguish from SVT with aberrancy)." },
            { q: "Management of a pregnant patient with symptomatic Mitral Stenosis?", a: "Percutaneous Mitral Balloon Valvotomy." },
            { q: "Most common complication of Mitral Stenosis?", a: "Atrial Fibrillation (due to left atrial enlargement)." },
            { q: "Best indicator of the severity of Mitral Regurgitation?", a: "Presence of an S3 gallop." },
            { q: "Classic presentation of Atrial Myxoma?", a: "Fever, weight loss, and a \"tumor plop\" sound on auscultation (often mimics Mitral Stenosis)." },
            { q: "Indication for surgery in chronic Aortic Regurgitation?", a: "Symptomatic, or asymptomatic with LV dysfunction (EF <50%) or severe LV dilation." },
            { q: "Classic finding on fundoscopy in a patient with Hypertension?", a: "AV nicking, silver/copper wiring, and cotton wool spots." },
            { q: "Definition of Hypertensive Urgency?", a: "BP >180/120 mmHg without evidence of acute end-organ damage." },
            { q: "Definition of Hypertensive Emergency?", a: "BP >180/120 mmHg with acute end-organ damage (e.g., encephalopathy, MI, stroke, renal failure)." },
            { q: "First-line treatment for Hypertriglyceridemia (>500 mg/dL)?", a: "Fibrates (to prevent pancreatitis)." },
            { q: "Most common site of origin for an embolus causing a stroke in a patient with AFib?", a: "Left Atrial Appendage." },
            { q: "Management of Cardiac Tamponade while waiting for pericardiocentesis?", a: "Aggressive IV fluid bolus to increase preload." },
            { q: "Significance of an S4 gallop?", a: "Atrial contraction into a stiff, non-compliant ventricle (e.g., LVH, restrictive cardiomyopathy)." },
            { q: "Next step for a patient with a \"tearing\" chest pain radiating to the back?", a: "CT Angiography of the chest/abdomen or TEE (if unstable)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of action of Ezetimibe?", a: "Inhibits cholesterol absorption at the brush border of the small intestine." },
            { q: "Most common side effect of Niacin and how to prevent it?", a: "Prostaglandin-mediated flushing; prevented by pre-treatment with Aspirin." },
            { q: "Class of antiarrhythmics that exhibit \"Use-Dependence\"?", a: "Class IC (e.g., Flecainide); their effect is stronger at higher heart rates." },
            { q: "Major electrolyte side effect of Thiazide diuretics?", a: "Hypokalemia, Hyponatremia, and Hypercalcemia." },
            { q: "Major electrolyte side effect of Loop diuretics?", a: "Hypokalemia, Hyponatremia, and Hypocalcemia." },
            { q: "Antihypertensive class known to cause peripheral edema?", a: "Dihydropyridine Calcium Channel Blockers (e.g., Amlodipine)." },
            { q: "Drug-induced Lupus is most commonly associated with which cardiac medication?", a: "Hydralazine or Procainamide." },
            { q: "Which anti-lipid medication can lead to gallstones?", a: "Fibrates (by increasing cholesterol excretion into bile)." },
            { q: "Timing of Ventricular Free Wall Rupture post-MI?", a: "5 days to 2 weeks." },
            { q: "Clinical presentation of Ventricular Free Wall Rupture?", a: "Sudden onset of Cardiac Tamponade and PEA." },
            { q: "Post-MI complication presenting with \"Step-up\" in oxygen saturation from RA to RV?", a: "Ventricular Septal Rupture." },
            { q: "EKG finding of a Ventricular Aneurysm?", a: "Persistent ST-segment elevation several weeks after the MI." },
            { q: "Most common cause of death in the \"Late\" phase (weeks) post-MI?", a: "Heart Failure or Ventricular Arrhythmias." },
            { q: "Definition of \"Sick Sinus Syndrome\"?", a: "Tachycardia-Bradycardia Syndrome due to SA node dysfunction." },
            { q: "Standard of care for a patient with Brugada Syndrome and prior syncope?", a: "Implantable Cardioverter Defibrillator (ICD)." },
            { q: "First-line treatment for Vasovagal Syncope?", a: "Reassurance, avoidance of triggers, and physical counter-pressure maneuvers." },
            { q: "Distinguishing feature of Orthostatic Hypotension on vitals?", a: "Drop in SBP >20 mmHg or DBP >10 mmHg upon standing." },
            { q: "Management of symptomatic Carotid Sinus Hypersensitivity?", a: "Permanent pacemaker." },
            { q: "What is \"Quincke’s Pulse\"?", a: "Capillary pulsations in the fingertips/nailbeds seen in Aortic Regurgitation." },
            { q: "What is \"Duroziez’s Sign\"?", a: "A systolic and diastolic bruit heard over the femoral artery when compressed by a stethoscope." },
            { q: "Graham-Steell murmur description?", a: "Early diastolic murmur at the left sternal border due to Pulmonary Regurgitation in the setting of Pulmonary HTN." },
            { q: "Primary valvular lesion associated with IV drug use?", a: "Tricuspid Regurgitation (Infective Endocarditis)." },
            { q: "Which valvular lesion presents with a \"large V-wave\" in the PCWP trace?", a: "Mitral Regurgitation." },
            { q: "Goal LDL for a patient with known CAD or high-risk diabetes?", a: "<70 mg/dL (or <55 mg/dL in very high-risk patients per newest guidelines)." },
            { q: "Components of the ASCVD Risk Calculator?", a: "Age, Sex, Race, BP, Cholesterol levels, Diabetes, and Smoking status." },
            { q: "Indication for high-intensity statin regardless of LDL level?", a: "Clinical ASCVD or Diabetes with a 10-year risk >20%." },
            { q: "First-line antihypertensive in a patient with Diabetes and Proteinuria?", a: "ACE inhibitor or ARB (for renal protection)." },
            { q: "Significance of a \"Pulsus Bisferiens\"?", a: "Two systolic peaks; seen in Aortic Regurgitation or HOCM." },
            { q: "Classic cause of \"Water-Bottle\" Heart on CXR?", a: "Large Pericardial Effusion." },
            { q: "Management of a patient with suspected Aortic Dissection and Hypotension?", a: "Immediate TEE and surgical consult (suggests rupture into pericardium/tamponade)." },
            { q: "Classic EKG finding in Hypothermia?", a: "J-waves (Osborn waves) — an upward deflection at the R-ST junction." },
            { q: "Key finding on Echo for Cardiac Amyloidosis?", a: "Increased wall thickness with \"speckled\" or \"granular\" myocardium." },
            { q: "Treatment for a patient with persistent stable Angina despite Beta-blocker and Nitrate therapy?", a: "Add Ranolazine." },
            { q: "Most common location of a Coarctation of the Aorta?", a: "Just distal to the origin of the left subclavian artery." },
            { q: "Significance of a \"Fixed Splitting of S2\"?", a: "Atrial Septal Defect (ASD)." },
            { q: "First-line treatment for a hemodynamically stable patient with WPW and AFib?", a: "IV Procainamide." },
            { q: "Most common cause of death in patients with Marfan Syndrome?", a: "Aortic Root Dissection." },
            { q: "Mechanism of nitrates in relieving angina?", a: "Systemic venodilation resulting in decreased preload and reduced myocardial wall tension." },
            { q: "Most common congenital heart defect found in adults?", a: "Bicuspid Aortic Valve." },
            { q: "Physical exam finding for a small Ventricular Septal Defect (VSD)?", a: "Loud, harsh holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border; often palpated as a thrill." },
            { q: "Murmur of a Patent Ductus Arteriosus (PDA)?", a: "Continuous \"machine-like\" murmur heard best at the left infraclavicular area." },
            { q: "Management of a large PDA in an adult?", a: "Percutaneous or surgical closure to prevent pulmonary hypertension and endocarditis." },
            { q: "Definition of Eisenmenger Syndrome?", a: "Reversal of a left-to-right shunt to right-to-left due to severe, irreversible pulmonary hypertension." },
            { q: "Clinical signs of Eisenmenger Syndrome?", a: "Cyanosis, clubbing, and polycythemia." },
            { q: "Why is the murmur of VSD or PDA \"missing\" in Eisenmenger Syndrome?", a: "The pressures in the systemic and pulmonary circulations have equalized, eliminating the pressure gradient." },
            { q: "Normal Pulmonary Artery Wedge Pressure (PAWP)?", a: "6–12 mmHg." },
            { q: "Hemodynamic profile of Cardiogenic Shock?", a: "Low Cardiac Output, High PAWP (Preload), and High SVR (Afterload)." },
            { q: "Hemodynamic profile of Hypovolemic Shock?", a: "Low Cardiac Output, Low PAWP, and High SVR." },
            { q: "Hemodynamic profile of Septic (Distributive) Shock?", a: "High Cardiac Output, Low PAWP, and Low SVR." },
            { q: "Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure in non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema (ARDS)?", a: "Normal (typically <18 mmHg)." },
            { q: "Most common cardiac complication during pregnancy in women with Mitral Stenosis?", a: "Acute pulmonary edema or Atrial Fibrillation due to increased plasma volume." },
            { q: "Definition of Peripartum Cardiomyopathy?", a: "Dilated cardiomyopathy/HF occurring in the last month of pregnancy or within 5 months postpartum." },
            { q: "Management of Peripartum Cardiomyopathy with LVEF <25%?", a: "Anticoagulation (high risk of thrombus) and standard HF therapy (avoiding ACEi/ARBs if still pregnant)." },
            { q: "Preferred anticoagulation for a pregnant patient with a mechanical heart valve?", a: "Low Molecular Weight Heparin (LMWH) in the first trimester; Warfarin may be used in the 2nd/3rd trimester." },
            { q: "First-line antihypertensive for a pregnant patient?", a: "Labetalol, Nifedipine, or Methyldopa." },
            { q: "Next step for an asymptomatic 45-year-old with a Grade 1/6 mid-systolic murmur?", a: "Reassurance; no further workup needed for soft, short systolic murmurs in asymptomatic patients." },
            { q: "Next step for any diastolic or continuous murmur found on exam?", a: "Echocardiogram (these are always pathologic until proven otherwise)." },
            { q: "When is S4 considered \"normal\"?", a: "In healthy older adults due to age-related decrease in ventricular compliance." },
            { q: "Best initial test for suspected stable CAD in a patient with a baseline LBBB on EKG?", a: "Stress Echocardiogram or Nuclear Stress Test (exercise EKG is non-diagnostic with LBBB)." },
            { q: "Major advantage of a bioprosthetic (bovine/porcine) valve over a mechanical valve?", a: "No requirement for long-term anticoagulation." },
            { q: "Major disadvantage of a bioprosthetic valve?", a: "Limited durability (typically lasts 10–15 years); prone to calcification." },
            { q: "Preferred valve type for a 30-year-old patient?", a: "Mechanical valve (due to longevity), provided they can tolerate lifelong  Warfarin." }
          ]
        },
        {
          section: 'Internal Medicine',
          topic: "Pulmonology — Pneumonia, COPD, asthma, PE, TB",
          icon: 'wind',
          cards: [
            { q: "Most common cause of Community-Acquired Pneumonia (CAP)?", a: "Streptococcus pneumoniae." },
            { q: "CURB-65 criteria for pneumonia admission?", a: "Confusion, Urea >7 mmol/L, RR ≥30, BP (S <90 or D ≤60), Age ≥65." },
            { q: "Standard outpatient treatment for CAP in a healthy patient (no comorbidities)?", a: "Amoxicillin, Doxycycline, or a Macrolide (if local resistance <25%)." },
            { q: "Empiric inpatient treatment for non-ICU CAP?", a: "Respiratory Fluoroquinolone (e.g., Levofloxacin) OR Beta-lactam + Macrolide." },
            { q: "Common causative agent of pneumonia in alcoholics with \"currant jelly\" sputum?", a: "Klebsiella pneumoniae." },
            { q: "Pneumonia associated with air conditioning or contaminated water sources?", a: "Legionella pneumophila." },
            { q: "Diagnostic test for Legionella?", a: "Urine antigen test." },
            { q: "Physical exam finding: Increased tactile fremitus and dullness to percussion?", a: "Consolidation (Pneumonia)." },
            { q: "Definition of Hospital-Acquired Pneumonia (HAP)?", a: "Pneumonia occurring ≥48 hours after admission." },
            { q: "Treatment for suspected MRSA pneumonia?", a: "Vancomycin or Linezolid." },
            { q: "Management of a parapneumonic effusion with pH <7.2 or glucose <60?", a: "Chest tube drainage (indicates empyema or complicated effusion)." },
            { q: "Primary TB chest X-ray finding?", a: "Ghon complex (calcified parenchymal lesion and hilar node)." },
            { q: "Reactivation TB typically affects which part of the lung?", a: "Apical/Posterior segments of upper lobes." },
            { q: "Gold standard for diagnosing active TB?", a: "Sputum culture (though AFB stain is the rapid initial step)." },
            { q: "Standard 4-drug regimen for active TB (RIPE)?", a: "Rifampin, Isoniazid, Pyrazinamide, Ethambutol." },
            { q: "Side effect of Ethambutol requiring monitoring?", a: "Optic neuritis (decreased visual acuity/red-green color blindness)." },
            { q: "Side effect of Pyrazinamide?", a: "Hyperuricemia (can trigger gout)." },
            { q: "Medication given with Isoniazid to prevent peripheral neuropathy?", a: "Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine)." },
            { q: "Screening test for latent TB in a patient with prior BCG vaccine?", a: "IGRA (Interferon-Gamma Release Assay)." },
            { q: "Definition of a positive PPD in a healthcare worker?", a: "≥10 mm induration. Pulmonology Part 2: Asthma & COPD" },
            { q: "The \"Classic Triad\" of asthma pathophysiology?", a: "Airway inflammation, reversible airway obstruction, and bronchial hyperresponsiveness." },
            { q: "Diagnostic criteria for asthma on spirometry?", a: "Increase in FEV1 >12% and >200 mL after bronchodilator." },
            { q: "Gold standard for diagnosing asthma when spirometry is normal?", a: "Methacholine challenge (PC20 <8 mg/mL)." },
            { q: "Step 1 treatment for intermittent asthma (GINA/NAEPP update)?", a: "PRN low-dose ICS-formoterol." },
            { q: "Characteristic finding in sputum of asthmatics?", a: "Curschmann spirals or Charcot-Leyden crystals." },
            { q: "Definition of \"Moderate Persistent\" asthma?", a: "Daily symptoms or nighttime awakenings >1x/week." },
            { q: "Side effect of inhaled corticosteroids (ICS)?", a: "Oral candidiasis (thrush)." },
            { q: "Definition of Chronic Bronchitis?", a: "Productive cough for ≥3 months in 2 consecutive years." },
            { q: "Major genetic risk factor for COPD in a young non-smoker?", a: "Alpha-1 Antitrypsin deficiency." },
            { q: "Typical FEV1/FVC ratio in COPD?", a: "<0.70 (post-bronchodilator)." },
            { q: "Only two interventions proven to reduce mortality in COPD?", a: "Smoking cessation and Long-term Oxygen Therapy (LTOT)." },
            { q: "Criteria for initiating LTOT in COPD?", a: "PaO2 ≤55 mmHg or SaO2 ≤88% at rest." },
            { q: "Best initial treatment for symptomatic COPD (Group A)?", a: "SABA or SAMA (Short-acting bronchodilator)." },
            { q: "Common CXR findings in emphysema?", a: "Flattened diaphragm, hyperlucency, and increased retrosternal airspace." },
            { q: "Most common cause of COPD exacerbation?", a: "Viral or bacterial infection (e.g., H. influenzae)." },
            { q: "Three cardinal symptoms requiring antibiotics in COPD exacerbation?", a: "Increased dyspnea, increased sputum volume, and increased sputum purulence." },
            { q: "Target oxygen saturation in a COPD exacerbation?", a: "88% to 92%." },
            { q: "First-line ventilation for COPD exacerbation with respiratory acidosis?", a: "Non-invasive positive pressure ventilation (NPPV/BiPAP)." },
            { q: "Characteristic EKG finding in severe COPD/Cor Pulmonale?", a: "Multifocal Atrial Tachycardia (MAT) or Right Axis Deviation." },
            { q: "Lung volume change pathognomonic for air trapping in COPD?", a: "Increased Residual Volume (RV) and Total Lung Capacity (TLC). Pulmonology Part 3: Pulmonary Embolism (PE)" },
            { q: "Most common symptom of Pulmonary Embolism?", a: "Dyspnea." },
            { q: "Most common EKG finding in PE?", a: "Sinus tachycardia." },
            { q: "The \"Classic\" but rare EKG sign of PE?", a: "S1Q3T3 (Deep S in I, Q wave in III, inverted T in III)." },
            { q: "Initial step for a patient with low clinical suspicion of PE?", a: "D-dimer (to rule out)." },
            { q: "Gold standard imaging for diagnosing PE?", a: "CT Angiography (CTPA)." },
            { q: "Alternative imaging for PE if the patient has renal failure or contrast allergy?", a: "V/Q Scan." },
            { q: "Arterial Blood Gas (ABG) finding typically seen in PE?", a: "Respiratory alkalosis (due to hyperventilation) with hypoxemia." },
            { q: "Hampton’s Hump on CXR represents?", a: "Pulmonary infarction (wedge-shaped opacity)." },
            { q: "Westermark sign on CXR represents?", a: "Avascularity distal to the PE (oligemia)." },
            { q: "Treatment for a hemodynamically unstable PE (Massive PE)?", a: "Systemic Thrombolysis (e.g., tPA)." },
            { q: "First-line anticoagulation for a stable PE?", a: "DOACs (e.g., Rivaroxaban, Apixaban) or LMWH bridge to Warfarin." },
            { q: "Indication for an IVC filter?", a: "Documented DVT/PE with absolute contraindication to anticoagulation." },
            { q: "Minimum duration of anticoagulation for a first-time provoked PE?", a: "3 months." },
            { q: "Definition of \"Submassive\" PE?", a: "Hemodynamically stable but with evidence of Right Ventricular (RV) strain." },
            { q: "Echocardiographic sign of RV strain in PE?", a: "McConnell’s sign (RV free wall akinesia with sparing of the apex). Pulmonology Part 4: Advanced Physiology & Mixed Topics" },
            { q: "Flow-volume loop: Scooped-out appearance?", a: "Obstructive lung disease (Asthma/COPD)." },
            { q: "Flow-volume loop: Tall, narrow, \"witch's hat\" appearance?", a: "Restrictive lung disease (IPF/Sarcoidosis)." },
            { q: "West Zone of the lung where PA > Pa > Pv?", a: "Zone 1 (Apex)." },
            { q: "Effect of PE on V/Q ratio?", a: "V/Q = infinity (Dead space ventilation)." },
            { q: "Effect of airway obstruction/mucus plug on V/Q ratio?", a: "V/Q = 0 (Shunt)." },
            { q: "Definition of Type 1 Respiratory Failure?", a: "Hypoxemic (PaO2 <60) with normal or low PaCO2." },
            { q: "Definition of Type 2 Respiratory Failure?", a: "Hypercapnic (PaCO2 >50)." },
            { q: "Light's Criteria for Exudative Effusion?", a: "LDH >2/3 upper limit, Fluid/Serum LDH >0.6, or Fluid/Serum Protein >0.5." },
            { q: "Most common cause of transudative pleural effusion?", a: "Congestive Heart Failure." },
            { q: "Fluid analysis of a Chylothorax?", a: "High triglycerides (>110 mg/dL) and milky appearance." },
            { q: "Management of a primary spontaneous pneumothorax <2 cm in a stable patient?", a: "Observation and supplemental oxygen." },
            { q: "Management of a Tension Pneumothorax?", a: "Immediate needle decompression followed by chest tube." },
            { q: "Location for needle decompression in tension pneumothorax?", a: "2nd intercostal space, mid-clavicular line (or 4th/5th anterior axillary)." },
            { q: "Classic CXR finding in Asbestosis?", a: "Pleural plaques (calcified) involving the diaphragm." },
            { q: "Silicosis increases the risk of which infection?", a: "Tuberculosis (impaired macrophage function)." },
            { q: "\"Eggshell\" calcifications of hilar lymph nodes?", a: "Silicosis." },
            { q: "Bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy and non-caseating granulomas?", a: "Sarcoidosis." },
            { q: "Electrolyte abnormality common in Sarcoidosis?", a: "Hypercalcemia (due to 1-alpha hydroxylase activity in macrophages)." },
            { q: "First-line treatment for symptomatic Sarcoidosis?", a: "Glucocorticoids (Prednisone)." },
            { q: "Meaning of a \"normal\" PaCO2 in a severe asthma attack?", a: "Impending respiratory failure (patient is tiring)." },
            { q: "DLCO in Emphysema vs. Asthma?", a: "Decreased in Emphysema; Normal or Increased in Asthma." },
            { q: "DLCO in Restrictive Lung Disease (Interstial)?", a: "Decreased." },
            { q: "Primary drug for smoking cessation that is a nicotinic receptor partial agonist?", a: "Varenicline (Chantix)." },
            { q: "Contraindication for Bupropion in smoking cessation?", a: "Seizure disorder or eating disorders." },
            { q: "Most common lung cancer in non-smokers?", a: "Adenocarcinoma." },
            { q: "Lung cancer associated with PTHrP and hypercalcemia?", a: "Squamous Cell Carcinoma." },
            { q: "Lung cancer associated with SIADH or Lambert-Eaton?", a: "Small Cell Lung Cancer." },
            { q: "Standard of care for diagnosing a peripheral lung nodule?", a: "Transthoracic needle aspiration or VATS." },
            { q: "Standard of care for diagnosing a central lung lesion?", a: "Bronchoscopy." },
            { q: "Definition of Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS) P/F ratio?", a: "PaO2/FiO2 ≤300 mmHg." },
            { q: "Primary ventilator strategy for ARDS?", a: "Low tidal volume ventilation (6 mL/kg ideal body weight)." },
            { q: "Common CXR finding in ARDS?", a: "Diffuse bilateral \"fluffy\" infiltrates (sparing costophrenic angles)." },
            { q: "PCWP in ARDS?", a: "<18 mmHg (suggests non-cardiogenic origin)." },
            { q: "Characteristic finding on sleep study for Obstructive Sleep Apnea (OSA)?", a: "Apnea-Hypopnea Index (AHI) ≥5 with symptoms or ≥15 without." },
            { q: "First-line treatment for OSA?", a: "CPAP and weight loss." },
            { q: "Most common cause of Cor Pulmonale?", a: "COPD." },
            { q: "Medication of choice for Pulmonary Arterial Hypertension (Group 1)?", a: "PDE-5 inhibitors (Sildenafil), Endothelin antagonists (Bosentan), or Prostanoids." },
            { q: "Initial test for suspected Pulmonary Hypertension?", a: "Echocardiogram (to estimate PASP)." },
            { q: "Gold standard for definitive diagnosis of Pulmonary Hypertension?", a: "Right heart catheterization (mPAP >20 mmHg)." },
            { q: "Physical exam sign of right-sided heart failure?", a: "Jugular Venous Distension (JVD) and peripheral edema." },
            { q: "\"Sudden onset chest pain, tachycardia, and clear lungs\" suggests?", a: "Pulmonary Embolism." },
            { q: "Difference between Hypoxemia and Hypoxia?", a: "Hypoxemia is low arterial O2 tension; Hypoxia is low O2 delivery to tissues." },
            { q: "Effect of high altitude on the oxygen-dissociation curve?", a: "Right shift (increased 2,3-BPG)." },
            { q: "Characteristic of Malignant Pleural Effusion?", a: "Exudative, often bloody, positive cytology." },
            { q: "Treatment for Latent TB (standard)?", a: "4 months of Rifampin OR 9 months of Isoniazid." },
            { q: "Recommended vaccine for adults >65 to prevent pneumonia?", a: "PCV20 (or PCV15 followed by PPSV23)." },
            { q: "Mainstay of treatment for allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis (ABPA)?", a: "Oral corticosteroids and Itraconazole." },
            { q: "Diagnostic finding for ABPA?", a: "High IgE, eosinophilia, and \"finger-in-glove\" opacities on CT." },
            { q: "Common cause of lung abscess?", a: "Aspiration of anaerobic oral flora." },
            { q: "Preferred antibiotic for lung abscess?", a: "Ampicillin-sulbactam or Clindamycin." },
            { q: "\"Honeycombing\" on High-Resolution CT?", a: "Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis (IPF)." },
            { q: "Distinguishing feature of Hypersensitivity Pneumonitis?", a: "Exposure history (e.g., birds, hay) and reversal upon removal of agent." },
            { q: "Carbon Monoxide poisoning effect on SaO2 (pulse ox)?", a: "Often shows 100% (pulse ox cannot distinguish HbO2 from Carboxy-Hb)." },
            { q: "Treatment for Carbon Monoxide poisoning?", a: "100% Oxygen (Hyperbaric if severe)." },
            { q: "Formula for A-a gradient?", a: "$PAO2 - PaO2$ where $PAO2 = (FiO2 \\times (Patm - PH2O)) - (PaCO2 / 0.8)$." },
            { q: "Causes of Hypoxemia with a NORMAL A-a gradient?", a: "Hypoventilation or High Altitude." },
            { q: "Causes of Hypoxemia with an INCREASED A-a gradient?", a: "V/Q mismatch, Shunt, or Diffusion limitation." },
            { q: "Typical CXR finding in Mycoplasma pneumonia?", a: "Interstitial \"patchy\" infiltrates (looks worse than patient feels)." },
            { q: "Most likely diagnosis: Smoker, weight loss, hyponatremia?", a: "Small Cell Lung Cancer (SIADH)." },
            { q: "Superior Vena Cava (SVC) Syndrome is most commonly caused by?", a: "Bronchogenic carcinoma (Small Cell)." },
            { q: "Samter’s Triad?", a: "Asthma, Aspirin sensitivity, Nasal polyps." },
            { q: "Why is Aspirin avoided in Samter's Triad?", a: "Leads to overproduction of leukotrienes." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Albuterol?", a: "Beta-2 agonist (increases cAMP → bronchodilation)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Tiotropium?", a: "Muscarinic antagonist (blocks M3 → prevents bronchoconstriction)." },
            { q: "Management of massive hemoptysis (>600mL)?", a: "Secure airway, place \"bleeding side down,\" and perform bronchoscopy/embolization." },
            { q: "Pneumonia associated with exposure to birds (parrots, turkeys)?", a: "Chlamydia psittaci (Psittacosis)." },
            { q: "Pneumonia associated with exposure to livestock or parturient cats?", a: "Coxiella burnetii (Q fever)." },
            { q: "Most common cause of pneumonia in patients with Cystic Fibrosis (adults)?", a: "Pseudomonas aeruginosa." },
            { q: "Treatment for Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (PCP)?", a: "Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (TMP-SMX)." },
            { q: "When to add steroids to PCP treatment in HIV patients?", a: "PaO2 <70 mmHg or A-a gradient ≥35 mmHg." },
            { q: "Fungal pneumonia associated with bat or bird droppings in the Ohio/Mississippi River valleys?", a: "Histoplasmosis." },
            { q: "Fungal infection associated with \"Broad-based budding yeast\" on microscopy?", a: "Blastomycosis." },
            { q: "Fungal pneumonia common in the Southwest US (desert) presenting with erythema nodosum?", a: "Coccidioidomycosis." },
            { q: "Characteristic finding of Aspergillus on silver stain?", a: "Septate hyphae with 45-degree (dichotomous) branching." },
            { q: "Characteristic finding of Mucor species?", a: "Non-septate hyphae with wide-angle (90-degree) branching. Pulmonology Part 6: Pleural & Mediastinal Disease" },
            { q: "Definition of a \"complicated\" parapneumonic effusion?", a: "Presence of bacteria on Gram stain/culture or pH <7.2." },
            { q: "Treatment for an uncomplicated parapneumonic effusion?", a: "Antibiotics alone (no drainage needed)." },
            { q: "Most common cause of a pleural effusion that is \"milky\" and has high lymphocytes?", a: "Chylothorax (often due to thoracic duct trauma or lymphoma)." },
            { q: "Light's Criteria: Pleural fluid LDH to Serum LDH ratio threshold for exudate?", a: ">0.6." },
            { q: "Light's Criteria: Pleural fluid Protein to Serum Protein ratio threshold for exudate?", a: ">0.5." },
            { q: "Common cause of a very low pleural fluid glucose (<30 mg/dL)?", a: "Rheumatoid Arthritis or Empyema." },
            { q: "Definition of an Empyema?", a: "Frank pus in the pleural space." },
            { q: "Most common cause of an anterior mediastinal mass (The 4 T's)?", a: "Thymoma, Teratoma, Thyroid (ectopic), \"Terrible\" Lymphoma. Pulmonology Part 7: Advanced COPD & Asthma Management" },
            { q: "Next step for a COPD patient with frequent exacerbations despite LAMA + LABA?", a: "Add Inhaled Corticosteroid (Triple Therapy: ICS + LABA + LAMA)." },
            { q: "Role of Roflumilast in COPD?", a: "PDE-4 inhibitor used to reduce exacerbations in patients with chronic bronchitis and severe obstruction." },
            { q: "Common EKG finding in a patient with a massive PE and Right Heart Strain?", a: "New Right Bundle Branch Block (RBBB)." },
            { q: "Definition of \"Pulsus Paradoxus\"?", a: "Drop in systolic BP >10 mmHg during inspiration (seen in severe asthma/COPD or tamponade)." },
            { q: "Indication for Omalizumab in asthma?", a: "Severe persistent asthma with high IgE levels and poor control on ICS/LABA." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Montelukast?", a: "Leukotriene receptor antagonist (CysLT1)." },
            { q: "Management of \"Silent Chest\" in an acute asthma exacerbation?", a: "Immediate intubation (indicates no air movement/impending arrest)." },
            { q: "First-line medication for Exercise-Induced Bronchospasm?", a: "SABA 10-20 minutes before exercise. Pulmonology Part 8: Critical Care & Miscellaneous" },
            { q: "Best initial test for suspected Sleep Apnea?", a: "Polysomnography (Sleep Study)." },
            { q: "Characteristic PFT finding in Vocal Cord Dysfunction?", a: "Flattened inspiratory loop (Extrathoracic obstruction)." },
            { q: "Diagnosis for: Young woman, recurrent pneumothorax, chylous effusion, CT shows thin-walled cysts?", a: "Lymphangioleiomyomatosis (LAM)." },
            { q: "\"Flesh-colored\" nodules on the shins in a patient with respiratory symptoms?", a: "Erythema Nodosum (highly suggestive of Sarcoidosis or Coccidioidomycosis)." },
            { q: "Löfgren Syndrome triad (variant of Sarcoidosis)?", a: "Erythema nodosum, bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, and migratory polyarthritis." },
            { q: "Preferred diagnostic step for a centrally located lung mass?", a: "Flexible Bronchoscopy." },
            { q: "Preferred diagnostic step for a peripherally located lung mass?", a: "Percutaneous (Transthoracic) needle biopsy." },
            { q: "Formula for calculating the PaO2 from SaO2 (The 40-50-60/70-80-90 rule)?", a: "SaO2 70% ≈ PaO2 40; SaO2 80% ≈ PaO2 50; SaO2 90% ≈ PaO2 60." },
            { q: "Most common cause of death in patients with Sarcoidosis?", a: "Pulmonary Fibrosis (Respiratory Failure)." },
            { q: "Primary treatment for Alpha-1 Antitrypsin deficiency?", a: "Smoking cessation and IV Alpha-1 Antitrypsin augmentation therapy." },
            { q: "Characteristic finding on CT in Bronchiectasis?", a: "\"Signet ring\" sign (dilated bronchus larger than its adjacent artery)." },
            { q: "Most common cause of Bronchiectasis in the US?", a: "Cystic Fibrosis." },
            { q: "Best drug to prevent high-altitude pulmonary edema (HAPE)?", a: "Nifedipine (if prophylactic) or gradual ascent." },
            { q: "Standard treatment for Carbon Monoxide (CO) poisoning?", a: "100% Hyperbaric oxygen (if CO levels >25% or neurologic symptoms)." },
            { q: "Cyanide poisoning antidote often paired with CO treatment?", a: "Hydroxocobalamin or Sodium Thiosulfate." },
            { q: "Most common EKG finding in Pulmonary Hypertension?", a: "Right ventricular hypertrophy (Tall R wave in V1, Right Axis Deviation). Pulmonology Part 9: Tuberculosis (Deep Dive)" },
            { q: "Appearance of Tuberculosis on Histology?", a: "Caseating granulomas with central necrosis and Langhans giant cells." },
            { q: "Definition of \"Miliary\" TB?", a: "Hematogenous spread of TB resulting in small \"millet seed\" lesions throughout the lungs and organs." },
            { q: "Site of \"Pott's Disease\"?", a: "Tuberculosis of the spine (vertebrae)." },
            { q: "Most common extrapulmonary site of TB?", a: "Lymph nodes (Scrofula)." },
            { q: "Screening frequency for TB in healthcare workers with a baseline negative test?", a: "Only after an exposure (per latest CDC guidelines; no longer annually for all)." },
            { q: "Minimum time for \"Standard\" TB treatment duration?", a: "6 months (2 months RIPE, 4 months RI)." },
            { q: "Major side effect of Rifampin?", a: "Orange discoloration of body fluids (urine, tears) and CYP450 induction." },
            { q: "Which TB drug is contraindicated in pregnancy?", a: "Pyrazinamide (strictly speaking, RI and E are preferred; P is avoided in some guidelines). Summary Checklist for Students Obstructive: $FEV1/FVC < 0.7$ Restrictive: $TLC < 80\\%$ predicted. Diffusion (DLCO): Differentiates Emphysema (low) from Asthma (normal/high). Admission: Use CURB-65. Antibiotics: Always cover S. pneumo and Legionella/Mycoplasma (atypicals). This brings the total count to roughly 115-120 high-yield points, covering the entire requested section from basic pathophysiology (West's) to clinical management (Harrison's/First Aid)." },
            { q: "Definition of a \"solitary pulmonary nodule\"?", a: "A single, well-circumscribed lesion <3 cm in diameter." },
            { q: "Management of a lung nodule with \"popcorn\" calcification on CT?", a: "Observation (indicates a benign Hamartoma)." },
            { q: "Most important risk factor for lung cancer (besides smoking)?", a: "Radon gas exposure." },
            { q: "Paraneoplastic syndrome associated with Squamous Cell Lung Cancer?", a: "Hypercalcemia due to PTHrP production." },
            { q: "Paraneoplastic syndrome associated with Small Cell Lung Cancer (renal)?", a: "SIADH (causing euvolemic hyponatremia)." },
            { q: "Paraneoplastic syndrome associated with Small Cell Lung Cancer (adrenal)?", a: "Ectopic ACTH production (Cushing Syndrome)." },
            { q: "Classic presentation of Lambert-Eaton Myasthenic Syndrome?", a: "Proximal muscle weakness that improves with use." },
            { q: "Pancoast Tumor (Superior Sulcus Tumor) typically presents with?", a: "Horner Syndrome (Ptosis, Miosis, Anhidrosis) and shoulder pain." },
            { q: "Standard screening protocol for lung cancer (USPSTF)?", a: "Annual Low-Dose CT for adults 50–80 with a 20 pack-year history who currently smoke or quit in the last 15 years." },
            { q: "Which lung cancer is \"central\" and characterized by intercellular bridges on histology?", a: "Squamous Cell Carcinoma. Pulmonology Part 11: Occupational & Environmental Lung Disease" },
            { q: "Pneumoconiosis associated with coal mining?", a: "Coal Worker’s Pneumoconiosis (Black Lung)." },
            { q: "Caplan Syndrome?", a: "Combination of Rheumatoid Arthritis and pneumoconiosis (usually coal or silica) with intrapulmonary nodules." },
            { q: "Occupational exposure associated with Berylliosis?", a: "Aerospace, electronics, or nuclear power industries." },
            { q: "Pathological similarity between Berylliosis and Sarcoidosis?", a: "Both present with non-caseating granulomas." },
            { q: "Primary treatment for Berylliosis?", a: "Systemic Corticosteroids." },
            { q: "Distinction between \"Farmer’s Lung\" and \"Silicosis\"?", a: "Farmer’s Lung is Hypersensitivity Pneumonitis (moldy hay); Silicosis is a fibrotic pneumoconiosis (sandblasting/rock mining)." },
            { q: "Byssinosis is caused by inhalation of?", a: "Cotton dust (\"Monday morning chest tightness\")." },
            { q: "Asbestos exposure significantly increases the risk of which two cancers?", a: "Bronchogenic Carcinoma (more common) and Mesothelioma (more specific). Pulmonology Part 12: Rare but High-Yield Entities" },
            { q: "Pulmonary Alveolar Proteinosis (PAP) is caused by?", a: "Accumulation of surfactant in the alveoli due to anti-GM-CSF antibodies." },
            { q: "Characteristic \"Periodic Acid-Schiff (PAS) positive\" material in alveoli?", a: "Pulmonary Alveolar Proteinosis." },
            { q: "Treatment for Pulmonary Alveolar Proteinosis?", a: "Whole-lung lavage." },
            { q: "Characteristic finding of Goodpasture Syndrome?", a: "Anti-GBM antibodies attacking the lungs (hemoptysis) and kidneys (hematuria)." },
            { q: "Primary treatment for Wegener's (Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis)?", a: "Corticosteroids and Cyclophosphamide (or Rituximab)." },
            { q: "Serological marker for Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis?", a: "c-ANCA (PR3-ANCA)." },
            { q: "Serological marker for Churg-Strauss (Eosinophilic Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis)?", a: "p-ANCA (MPO-ANCA) and high peripheral eosinophil count. Pulmonology Part 13: Pulmonary Critical Care (Mechanical Ventilation)" },
            { q: "Initial Tidal Volume setting for a patient with ARDS?", a: "6 mL/kg of Ideal Body Weight." },
            { q: "Goal for Plateau Pressure in ARDS management?", a: "<30 cm H2O." },
            { q: "Primary purpose of PEEP (Positive End-Expiratory Pressure) in ARDS?", a: "To recruit collapsed alveoli and improve oxygenation." },
            { q: "Potential complication of high PEEP or high Tidal Volumes?", a: "Barotrauma (Pneumothorax) or Volutrauma." },
            { q: "First-line management for a patient with Acute Cardiogenic Pulmonary Edema?", a: "Supplemental O2, Loop Diuretics (Lasix), and Nitroglycerin (if BP allows)." },
            { q: "How does PEEP affect cardiac output?", a: "Decreases cardiac output by increasing intrathoracic pressure and reducing venous return (preload)." },
            { q: "Clinical sign of Carbon Dioxide Narcosis?", a: "Somnolence, confusion, and asterixis in a patient with high PaCO2." },
            { q: "Management of \"Auto-PEEP\" (breath stacking) in an intubated COPD patient?", a: "Disconnect the circuit briefly to allow full exhalation and decrease the respiratory rate." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Roflumilast in COPD management?", a: "Phosphodiesterase-4 (PDE-4) inhibitor that increases cAMP to reduce inflammation." },
            { q: "Side effect of Theophylline toxicity?", a: "Seizures and cardiac arrhythmias (narrow therapeutic index)." },
            { q: "Primary drug class for the treatment of Pulmonary Arterial Hypertension (Group 1)?", a: "Endothelin receptor antagonists (e.g., Bosentan) or PDE-5 inhibitors (e.g., Sildenafil)." },
            { q: "Common side effect of inhaled Tiotropium?", a: "Dry mouth (Anticholinergic effect)." },
            { q: "Best initial antibiotic for a \"Rusty\" sputum pneumonia?", a: "Ceftriaxone plus Azithromycin (covering S. pneumoniae)." },
            { q: "Preferred antibiotic for a \"foul-smelling\" sputum pneumonia?", a: "Ampicillin-sulbactam (covers anaerobes and aerobes in aspiration)." },
            { q: "Mechanism of Omalizumab in severe asthma?", a: "Monoclonal antibody that binds to IgE, preventing it from binding to mast cells." },
            { q: "First-line medication to treat a stable patient with Pneumocystis jirovecii and a sulfa allergy?", a: "Pentamidine or Atovaquone. Pulmonology Part 15: Clinical \"Buzzwords\" & Physical Exam" },
            { q: "Physical exam finding: \"Velcro-like\" inspiratory crackles?", a: "Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis." },
            { q: "Egophony (E to A change) indicates?", a: "Lung consolidation (Pneumonia)." },
            { q: "Definition of \"Whispered Pectoriloquy\"?", a: "Whispered sounds are heard clearly through the stethoscope, indicating consolidation." },
            { q: "Physical exam finding in a large Pleural Effusion?", a: "Decreased tactile fremitus, dullness to percussion, and decreased breath sounds." },
            { q: "Tracheal deviation AWAY from the affected side?", a: "Tension Pneumothorax or Large Pleural Effusion." },
            { q: "Tracheal deviation TOWARD the affected side?", a: "Atelectasis or Pneumonectomy." },
            { q: "Meaning of \"Tactile Fremitus\" in Pneumothorax?", a: "Decreased (air in the pleural space blocks vibration)." },
            { q: "Meaning of \"Tactile Fremitus\" in Pneumonia?", a: "Increased (solid/liquid medium conducts vibration better). Pulmonology Part 16: Sleep Medicine & Ventilation Nuances" },
            { q: "Distinction between OSA and Central Sleep Apnea (CSA)?", a: "CSA lacks respiratory effort (no chest wall movement); OSA has respiratory effort against an obstruction." },
            { q: "\"Cheyne-Stokes\" respiration is commonly seen in?", a: "Heart Failure or Stroke (form of Central Sleep Apnea)." },
            { q: "Primary risk factor for Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome (Pickwickian)?", a: "BMI ≥30." },
            { q: "Definition of Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome?", a: "Daytime hypercapnia (PaCO2 >45 mmHg) in an obese patient not explained by other causes." },
            { q: "What is \"Veno-venous (VV) ECMO\" used for?", a: "Severe ARDS refractory to conventional mechanical ventilation." },
            { q: "Target PaO2 for a patient with ARDS?", a: "55–80 mmHg (avoiding oxygen toxicity)." },
            { q: "Definition of \"Permissive Hypercapnia\" in ARDS?", a: "Allowing high PaCO2 to maintain low tidal volumes and protect the lungs from barotrauma. Pulmonology Part 17: Differential Diagnosis \"Red Flags\"" },
            { q: "Unilateral wheezing in a child/toddler?", a: "Foreign body aspiration." },
            { q: "Unilateral wheezing in an adult smoker?", a: "Endobronchial tumor (Lung cancer)." },
            { q: "\"Sudden onset of dyspnea and pleuritic chest pain in a tall, thin male smoker\"?", a: "Primary Spontaneous Pneumothorax." },
            { q: "\"Chest pain, dyspnea, and friction rub\" 2-3 days after a viral illness?", a: "Pleurisy (Pleuritis)." },
            { q: "Recurrent pneumonia in the SAME lung lobe?", a: "Suggests localized obstruction (e.g., Bronchogenic carcinoma or foreign body)." },
            { q: "\"Bat-wing\" or \"Butterfly\" opacities on CXR?", a: "Pulmonary Edema (CHF)." },
            { q: "\"Kerley B lines\" on CXR indicate?", a: "Increased pulmonary venous pressure (CHF/Pulmonary Edema)." },
            { q: "Common cause of \"Eggshell calcification\" of hilar nodes besides Silicosis?", a: "Sarcoidosis (rarely) or Treated Lymphoma. Pulmonology Part 18: Critical Lab Values" },
            { q: "A pH <7.2 in pleural fluid always necessitates?", a: "Drainage (Chest tube)." },
            { q: "What is a \"Normal\" A-a gradient for a 20-year-old?", a: "~9 mmHg (Formula: Age/4 + 4)." },
            { q: "Meaning of a \"high\" DLCO?", a: "Alveolar hemorrhage (e.g., Goodpasture) or Asthma." },
            { q: "A PaO2/FiO2 ratio of <100 defines?", a: "Severe ARDS." },
            { q: "Gold standard test for Cystic Fibrosis?", a: "Sweat Chloride Test (>60 mmol/L). ===================================================================== ===================================================================== Section: Internal Medicine" }
          ]
        },
        {
          section: 'Internal Medicine',
          topic: "Gastroenterology — IBD, GI bleed, liver disease, pancreatitis",
