            { q: "Laboratory ratio suggestive of UGIB?", a: "Elevated BUN/Creatinine ratio (>20:1)." },
            { q: "Medication to initiate immediately in suspected Variceal Bleed?", a: "Octreotide (somatostatin analog)." },
            { q: "Prophylactic antibiotic class for cirrhotic patients with UGIB?", a: "Fluoroquinolones or Ceftriaxone." },
            { q: "Most common cause of Lower GI Bleed (LGIB) in adults >60?", a: "Diverticulosis." },
            { q: "Presentation of bleeding from Angiodysplasia?", a: "Painless hematochezia in elderly patients (often associated with aortic stenosis)." },
            { q: "First step in managing a hemodynamically unstable GI bleed?", a: "Two large-bore IVs and fluid resuscitation." },
            { q: "Definitive management for refractory esophageal variceal bleeding?", a: "TIPS (Transjugular Intrahepatic Portosystemic Shunt)." },
            { q: "Common cause of UGIB following forceful vomiting?", a: "Mallory-Weiss Tear." },
            { q: "Definition of \"Hematochezia\"?", a: "Bright red blood per rectum, usually indicating a lower GI source." },
            { q: "Definition of \"Melena\"?", a: "Black, tarry stools indicating digested blood, usually from an upper GI source." },
            { q: "Finding on EGD that suggests a high risk of ulcer re-bleeding?", a: "Visible vessel or adherent clot (Forrest classification). Part 3: Liver Disease & Cirrhosis" },
            { q: "Physical exam finding specific to hepatic encephalopathy?", a: "Asterixis (flapping tremor)." },
            { q: "Standard medical treatment for Hepatic Encephalopathy?", a: "Lactulose (to trap ammonia) and Rifaximin." },
            { q: "Common cause of Budd-Chiari Syndrome?", a: "Hepatic vein thrombosis." },
            { q: "SAAG score indicating portal hypertension-related ascites?", a: "SAAG ≥ 1.1 g/dL." },
            { q: "Primary screening test for Hepatocellular Carcinoma (HCC)?", a: "Ultrasound and Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) every 6 months." },
            { q: "Gold standard for diagnosing Wilson’s Disease?", a: "Increased 24-hour urinary copper or Liver biopsy." },
            { q: "Triad of Hemochromatosis?", a: "Cirrhosis, Diabetes (\"Bronze Diabetes\"), and Skin hyperpigmentation." },
