            {
                        "q": "What defines a \"Modified Radical Mastectomy\"?",
                        "a": "Removal of the entire breast tissue, skin-axillary tail complex, and a formal Level I/II axillary lymph node dissection, while sparing the pectoralis major muscle."
            },
            {
                        "q": "What defines a \"Radical Mastectomy\" (Halsted Mastectomy), and is it commonly performed today?",
                        "a": "Removal of the breast, axillary nodes, and pectoralis major/minor muscles; it is virtually never performed today due to equivalent survival with less morbid options."
            },
            {
                        "q": "What is the classic clinical presentation of Inflammatory Breast Cancer?",
                        "a": "Erythema, warmth, and skin edema mimicking mastitis, presenting as \"peau d'orange\" (orange peel skin) due to tumor emboli blocking dermal lymphatics."
            },
            {
                        "q": "What is the initial step in the management of highly suspected or biopsy-proven Inflammatory Breast Cancer?",
                        "a": "Neoadjuvant systemic chemotherapy followed by modified radical mastectomy and adjuvant radiation (never upfront surgery)."
            },
            {
                        "q": "What is Paget's disease of the breast?",
                        "a": "A malignant intraepithelial adenocarcinoma condition presenting as a raw, scaling, or ulcerated lesion of the nipple-areola complex."
            },
            {
                        "q": "Paget's disease of the breast is almost universally an indicator of what underlying pathology?",
                        "a": "An underlying ductal carcinoma in situ (DCIS) or invasive ductal carcinoma deeper within the breast tissue."
            },
