                        "a": "Hampton’s Hump (a wedge-shaped, pleural-based opacification) and Westermark’s Sign (regional oligemia/decreased vascularity distal to the occluded vessel)."
            },
            {
                        "q": "Immediate initial medical treatment for a confirmed or highly suspected acute, non-massive DVT/PE in a stable patient?",
                        "a": "Immediate systemic anticoagulation with therapeutic Low-Molecular-Weight Heparin (LMWH), Fondaparinux, or a Direct Oral Anticoagulant (DOAC like rivaroxaban or apixaban)."
            },
            {
                        "q": "Mechanism of action of Unfractionated Heparin (UFH)?",
                        "a": "Binds to and accelerates the activity of Antithrombin III, which primarily inactivates Thrombin (Factor IIa) and Factor Xa."
            },
            {
                        "q": "Laboratory parameter monitored to guide the dosing of continuous intravenous Unfractionated Heparin?",
                        "a": "Activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) or anti-Factor Xa assay levels."
            },
            {
                        "q": "Primary specific reversal agent for Unfractionated Heparin?",
                        "a": "Protamine sulfate (1 mg reverses approximately 100 units of heparin)."
            },
            {
                        "q": "Mechanism of action of Low-Molecular-Weight Heparin (LMWH, e.g., enoxaparin)?",
                        "a": "Binds to Antithrombin III to selectively inhibit Factor Xa with minimal effect on Thrombin (Factor IIa)."
            },
            {
                        "q": "Clinical scenarios where Unfractionated Heparin is preferred over Low-Molecular-Weight Heparin?",
                        "a": "Patients with severe renal impairment (CrCl <30 mL/min), hemodynamically unstable PE, or an impending requirement for immediate surgical intervention due to UFH's short half-life and easy reversibility."
            },
            {
                        "q": "Serious immune-mediated drug reaction characterized by a paradoxical drop in platelets and a prothrombotic state 5 to 10 days after starting heparin?",
                        "a": "Heparin-Induced Thrombocytopenia (HIT), caused by IgG antibodies directed against the Heparin-Platelet Factor 4 (PF4) complex."
            },
            {
                        "q": "Immediate management actions required upon clinical suspicion of Heparin-Induced Thrombocytopenia (HIT)?",
                        "a": "Stop all forms of heparin immediately and initiate alternative anticoagulation with a direct thrombin inhibitor (e.g., argatroban or bivalirudin); do not transfuse platelets."
            },
            {
                        "q": "Mechanism of action of Warfarin?",
                        "a": "Inhibits the Vitamin K Epoxide Reductase Complex 1 (VKORC1), blocking the gamma-carboxylation and activation of Vitamin K-dependent clotting factors II, VII, IX, and X, along with proteins C and S."
            },
            {
                        "q": "Reason why rapid induction of Warfarin without a concurrent heparin \"bridge\" is strictly contraindicated in acute thrombosis?",
                        "a": "Warfarin depletes Protein C and S (short half-lives) faster than it depletes factors II and X, inducing a transient hypercoagulable state that can lead to skin necrosis."
            },
            {
                        "q": "Standard target International Normalized Ratio (INR) range for a patient on Warfarin therapy for DVT or PE?",
                        "a": "Target INR range of 2.0 to 3.0."
            },
            {
                        "q": "Specific reversal agents for Warfarin therapy depending on the urgency of bleeding?",
                        "a": "Intravenous Vitamin K for non-emergent correction; 4-Factor Prothrombin Complex Concentrate (PCC) or Fresh Frozen Plasma (FFP) for emergency reversal during major hemorrhage."
            },
            {
                        "q": "Target proteins inhibited by Direct Oral Anticoagulants (DOACs) like Rivaroxaban and Apixaban?",
                        "a": "Direct inhibition of free and clot-bound Factor Xa."
            },
            {
                        "q": "Standard recommended duration of anticoagulation therapy for a first-episode DVT/PE secondary to a transient, reversible surgical risk factor?",
                        "a": "3 months."
            },
            {
                        "q": "Core definition of a \"Massive\" Pulmonary Embolism?",
                        "a": "Acute PE presenting with sustained systemic hypotension (systolic BP <90 mmHg for >15 minutes) or requiring inotropic support, independent of right ventricular dysfunction."
            },
            {
                        "q": "Core definition of a \"Submassive\" Pulmonary Embolism?",
                        "a": "Acute PE presenting with normal systemic blood pressure but with objective evidence of right ventricular strain (seen on echocardiogram/CT) or elevated cardiac biomarkers (Troponin/BNP)."
            },
            {
                        "q": "First-line definitive therapeutic intervention for a patient with a hemodynamically unstable, massive Pulmonary Embolism who has no contraindications?",
                        "a": "Systemic thrombolytic therapy (e.g., recombinant tissue plasminogen activator / alteplase)."
            },
            {
                        "q": "Management strategy for a patient with a massive Pulmonary Embolism who has absolute contraindications to systemic thrombolysis?",
                        "a": "Emergent surgical pulmonary embolectomy or catheter-directed mechanical thrombectomy."
            },
            {
                        "q": "Precise clinical indication for the placement of an Inferior Vena Cava (IVC) filter?",
                        "a": "Confirmed acute proximal DVT or PE in a patient with an absolute contraindication to anticoagulation therapy, or recurrent VTE despite documented therapeutic anticoagulation."
